WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
If you want to learn about computer science and the art
of programming, this course is where to start.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.300
CS50 is considered by many to be one of the
best computer science courses in the world.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.000
This is a Harvard University course
taught by Dr. David Malan

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
and we are proud to bring it to
the freeCodeCamp YouTube channel.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Throughout a series of lectures, Dr. Malan will teach you 
how to think algorithmically and solve problems efficiently.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.300
And make sure to check the description for a lot of
extra resources that go along with the course.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:01:44.881
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:50.281
DAVID MALAN: All right, this is CS50,
Harvard University's introduction

00:01:50.281 --> 00:01:52.591
to the intellectual
enterprises of computer science

00:01:52.591 --> 00:01:56.341
and the art of programming, back here
on campus in beautiful Sanders Theatre

00:01:56.341 --> 00:01:58.411
for the first time in quite a while.

00:01:58.411 --> 00:02:01.001
So welcome to the class.

00:02:01.001 --> 00:02:02.381
My name is David--

00:02:02.381 --> 00:02:03.147
OK.

00:02:03.147 --> 00:02:06.549
[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:13.311
So my name is David Malan.

00:02:13.311 --> 00:02:16.911
And I took this class myself
some time ago, but almost didn't.

00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:20.121
It was sophomore fall and I
was sitting in on the class.

00:02:20.121 --> 00:02:22.431
And I was a little curious
but, eh, it didn't really

00:02:22.431 --> 00:02:24.508
feel like the field for me.

00:02:24.508 --> 00:02:26.841
I was definitely a computer
person, but computer science

00:02:26.841 --> 00:02:28.314
felt like something altogether.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.231
And I only got up the
nerve to take the class,

00:02:30.231 --> 00:02:32.871
ultimately, because the professor
at the time, Brian Kernighan,

00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:35.601
allowed me to take the
class pass/fail, initially.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:37.491
And that is what made
all the difference.

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:39.981
I quickly found that
computer science is not just

00:02:39.981 --> 00:02:42.801
about programming and working
in isolation on your computer.

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:45.391
It's really about problem
solving more generally.

00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:48.081
And there was something
about homework, frankly,

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:51.471
that was, like, actually fun for perhaps
the first time in, what, 19 years.

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.996
And there was something
about this ability

00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:56.121
that I discovered, along
with all of my classmates,

00:02:56.121 --> 00:03:00.373
to actually create something and bring
a computer to life to solve a problem,

00:03:00.373 --> 00:03:03.081
and sort of bring to bear something
that I'd been using every day

00:03:03.081 --> 00:03:06.261
but didn't really know how to harness,
that's been gratifying ever since,

00:03:06.261 --> 00:03:08.151
and definitely challenging
and frustrating.

00:03:08.151 --> 00:03:10.753
Like, to this day,
all these years later,

00:03:10.753 --> 00:03:13.461
you're going to run up against
mistakes, otherwise known as bugs,

00:03:13.461 --> 00:03:15.111
in programming, that
just drive you nuts.

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:16.611
And you feel like you've hit a wall.

00:03:16.611 --> 00:03:18.951
But the trick really is
to give it enough time,

00:03:18.951 --> 00:03:21.181
to take a step back, take
a break when you need to.

00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:24.441
And there's nothing better, I daresay,
than that sense of gratification

00:03:24.441 --> 00:03:26.169
and pride, really,
when you get something

00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:28.461
to work, and in a class like
this, present, ultimately,

00:03:28.461 --> 00:03:32.091
at term's end, something like
your very own final project.

00:03:32.091 --> 00:03:35.551
Now, this isn't to say that
I took to it 100% perfectly.

00:03:35.551 --> 00:03:40.761
In fact, just this past week, I looked
in my old CS50 binder, which I still

00:03:40.761 --> 00:03:43.281
have from some 25 years
ago, and took a photo

00:03:43.281 --> 00:03:47.961
of what was apparently the very first
program that I wrote and submitted,

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:50.271
and quickly received minus 2 points on.

00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:53.451
But this is a program that we'll
soon see in the coming days that

00:03:53.451 --> 00:03:57.741
does something quite simply like
print "Hello, CS50," in this case,

00:03:57.741 --> 00:03:58.409
to the screen.

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:00.201
And to be fair, I
technically hadn't really

00:04:00.201 --> 00:04:02.481
followed the directions, which is
why I lost those couple of points.

00:04:02.481 --> 00:04:05.803
But if you just look at this, especially
if you've never programmed before,

00:04:05.803 --> 00:04:07.761
you might have heard
about programming language

00:04:07.761 --> 00:04:09.719
but you've never typed
something like this out,

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:11.481
undoubtedly it's going to look cryptic.

00:04:11.481 --> 00:04:13.521
But unlike human
languages, frankly, which

00:04:13.521 --> 00:04:17.481
were a lot more sophisticated, a
lot more vocabulary, a lot more

00:04:17.481 --> 00:04:21.621
grammatical rules, programming, once
you start to wrap your mind around what

00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:24.734
it is and how it works and what these
various languages are, it's so easy,

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:26.901
you'll see, after a few
months of a class like this,

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:29.001
to start teaching
yourself, subsequently,

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:32.731
other languages, as they may
come, in the coming years as well.

00:04:32.731 --> 00:04:36.051
So what ultimately matters
in this particular course

00:04:36.051 --> 00:04:38.691
is not so much where you end
up relative to your classmates

00:04:38.691 --> 00:04:41.901
but where you end up relative
to yourself when you began.

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:43.381
And indeed, you'll begin today.

00:04:43.381 --> 00:04:46.911
And the only experience that matters
ultimately in this class is your own.

00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:49.041
And so, consider where you are today.

00:04:49.041 --> 00:04:51.531
Consider, perhaps, just how
cryptic something like that

00:04:51.531 --> 00:04:52.851
looked a few seconds ago.

00:04:52.851 --> 00:04:56.181
And take comfort in knowing just
some months from now all of that

00:04:56.181 --> 00:04:58.409
will be within your own grasp.

00:04:58.409 --> 00:05:01.701
And if you're thinking that, OK, surely
the person in front of me, to the left,

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:05.421
to the right, behind me, knows more than
me, that's statistically not the case.

00:05:05.421 --> 00:05:10.101
2/3 of CS50 students have never taken
a CS course before, which is to say,

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:14.731
you're in very good company
throughout this whole term.

00:05:14.731 --> 00:05:16.821
So then, what is computer science?

00:05:16.821 --> 00:05:18.441
I claim that it's problem solving.

00:05:18.441 --> 00:05:20.721
And the upside of that is
that problem solving is

00:05:20.721 --> 00:05:23.031
something we sort of do all the time.

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:25.834
But a computer science
class, learning to program,

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:27.501
I think kind of cleans up your thoughts.

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:31.041
It helps you learn how to think more
methodically, more carefully, more

00:05:31.041 --> 00:05:32.631
correctly, more precisely.

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:34.464
Because, honestly, the
computer is not going

00:05:34.464 --> 00:05:37.671
to do what you want unless you are
correct and precise and methodical.

00:05:37.671 --> 00:05:39.896
And so, as such, there's
these fringe benefits

00:05:39.896 --> 00:05:42.771
of just learning to think like a
computer scientist and a programmer.

00:05:42.771 --> 00:05:45.561
And it doesn't take all
that much to start doing so.

00:05:45.561 --> 00:05:49.221
This, for instance, is perhaps the
simplest picture of computer science,

00:05:49.221 --> 00:05:51.141
sure, but really problem
solving in general.

00:05:51.141 --> 00:05:54.471
Problems are all about taking input,
like the problem you want to solve.

00:05:54.471 --> 00:05:56.361
You want to get the solution, a.k.a.

00:05:56.361 --> 00:05:57.051
output.

00:05:57.051 --> 00:05:59.751
And so, something interesting
has got to be happening in here,

00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:03.241
in here, when you're trying to
get from those inputs to outputs.

00:06:03.241 --> 00:06:05.811
Now, in the world of
computers specifically,

00:06:05.811 --> 00:06:09.681
we need to decide in advance how we
represent these inputs and outputs.

00:06:09.681 --> 00:06:13.724
We all just need to decide, whether
it's Macs or PCs or phones or something

00:06:13.724 --> 00:06:16.641
else, that we're all going to speak
some common language, irrespective

00:06:16.641 --> 00:06:18.841
of our human languages as well.

00:06:18.841 --> 00:06:22.551
And you may very well know that
computers tend to speak only

00:06:22.551 --> 00:06:26.741
what language, so to speak?

00:06:26.741 --> 00:06:29.231
Assembly, one, but binary,
two, might be your go-to.

00:06:29.231 --> 00:06:32.321
And binary, by implying two,
means that the world of computers

00:06:32.321 --> 00:06:35.981
has just two digits at
its disposal, 0 and 1.

00:06:35.981 --> 00:06:40.061
And indeed, we humans have many more
than that, certainly not just zeros

00:06:40.061 --> 00:06:40.691
and ones alone.

00:06:40.691 --> 00:06:43.121
But a computer indeed
only has zeros and ones.

00:06:43.121 --> 00:06:45.341
And yet, somehow they can do so much.

00:06:45.341 --> 00:06:47.711
They can crunch numbers in
Excel, send text messages,

00:06:47.711 --> 00:06:51.081
create images and artwork
and movies and more.

00:06:51.081 --> 00:06:54.791
And so, how do you get from something
as simple as a few zeros, a few ones,

00:06:54.791 --> 00:06:56.921
to all of the stuff
that we're doing today

00:06:56.921 --> 00:06:58.871
in our pockets and laptops and desktops?

00:06:58.871 --> 00:07:01.571
Well, it turns out that
we can start quite simply.

00:07:01.571 --> 00:07:05.471
If a computer were to want to do
something as simple as count, well,

00:07:05.471 --> 00:07:06.191
what could it do?

00:07:06.191 --> 00:07:09.191
Well, in our human world,
we might count doing this,

00:07:09.191 --> 00:07:13.779
like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, using so-called
unitary notation, literally the digits

00:07:13.779 --> 00:07:16.571
on your fingers where one finger
represents one person in the room,

00:07:16.571 --> 00:07:18.521
if I'm, for instance, taking attendance.

00:07:18.521 --> 00:07:22.601
Now, we humans would typically
actually count 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:25.481
And we'd go past just those five
digits and count much higher,

00:07:25.481 --> 00:07:26.951
using zeros through nines.

00:07:26.951 --> 00:07:29.871
But computers, somehow, only
have these zeros and ones.

00:07:29.871 --> 00:07:33.191
So if a computer only somehow
speaks binary, zeros and ones,

00:07:33.191 --> 00:07:36.181
how does it even count
past the number 1?

00:07:36.181 --> 00:07:38.741
Well, here are 3 zeros, of course.

00:07:38.741 --> 00:07:42.251
And if you translate this
number in binary, 000,

00:07:42.251 --> 00:07:46.151
to a more familiar number in decimal,
we would just call this zero.

00:07:46.151 --> 00:07:47.021
Enough said.

00:07:47.021 --> 00:07:49.871
If we were to represent, with
a computer, the number 1,

00:07:49.871 --> 00:07:52.931
it would actually be 001,
which, not surprisingly,

00:07:52.931 --> 00:07:55.991
is exactly the same as we
might do in our human world,

00:07:55.991 --> 00:07:59.501
but we might not bother writing
out the two zeros at the beginning.

00:07:59.501 --> 00:08:02.171
But a computer, now, if it
wants to count as high as two,

00:08:02.171 --> 00:08:03.701
it doesn't have the digit 2.

00:08:03.701 --> 00:08:06.431
And so it has to use a different
pattern of zeros and ones.

00:08:06.431 --> 00:08:08.621
And that happens to be 010.

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:10.811
So this is not 10 with
a zero in front of it.

00:08:10.811 --> 00:08:13.281
It's indeed zero one zero
in the context of binary.

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:15.401
And if we want to count
higher now than two,

00:08:15.401 --> 00:08:19.541
we're going to have to tweak these
zeros and ones further to get 3.

00:08:19.541 --> 00:08:24.011
And then if we want 4
or 5 or 6 or 7, we're

00:08:24.011 --> 00:08:26.921
just kind of toggling these
zeros and ones, a.k.a.

00:08:26.921 --> 00:08:31.408
bits, for binary digits that represent,
via these different patterns,

00:08:31.408 --> 00:08:33.491
different numbers that you
and I, as humans, know,

00:08:33.491 --> 00:08:36.731
of course, as the so-called
decimal system, 0 through 9,

00:08:36.731 --> 00:08:40.391
dec implying 10, 10 digits,
those zeros through nine.

00:08:40.391 --> 00:08:42.761
So why that particular pattern?

00:08:42.761 --> 00:08:44.681
And why these particular zeros and ones?

00:08:44.681 --> 00:08:48.011
Well, it turns out that
representing one thing or the other

00:08:48.011 --> 00:08:50.361
is just really simple for a computer.

00:08:50.361 --> 00:08:50.861
Why?

00:08:50.861 --> 00:08:53.111
At the end of the day, they're
powered by electricity.

00:08:53.111 --> 00:08:56.081
And it's a really simple thing to
just either store some electricity

00:08:56.081 --> 00:08:57.791
or don't store some electricity.

00:08:57.791 --> 00:09:00.911
Like, that's as simple as
the world can get, on or off.

00:09:00.911 --> 00:09:03.111
1 or 0, so to speak.

00:09:03.111 --> 00:09:05.821
So, in fact, inside of a
computer, a phone, anything

00:09:05.821 --> 00:09:07.571
these days that's
electronic, pretty much,

00:09:07.571 --> 00:09:10.763
is some number of switches,
otherwise known as transistors.

00:09:10.763 --> 00:09:11.471
And they're tiny.

00:09:11.471 --> 00:09:14.853
You've got thousands, millions of them
in your Mac or PC or phone these days.

00:09:14.853 --> 00:09:17.811
And these are just tiny little switches
that can get turned on and off.

00:09:17.811 --> 00:09:20.471
And by turning those things
on and off in patterns,

00:09:20.471 --> 00:09:24.274
a computer can count from 0 on up
to 7, and even higher than that.

00:09:24.274 --> 00:09:27.191
And so these switches, really, you
can think of being as like switches

00:09:27.191 --> 00:09:27.691
like this.

00:09:27.691 --> 00:09:29.983
Let me just borrow one of
our little stage lights here.

00:09:29.983 --> 00:09:30.971
Here's a light bulb.

00:09:30.971 --> 00:09:32.261
It's currently off.

00:09:32.261 --> 00:09:34.691
And so, I could just think
of this as representing,

00:09:34.691 --> 00:09:38.111
in my laptop, a transistor,
a switch, representing 0.

00:09:38.111 --> 00:09:43.121
But if I allow some electricity
to flow, now I, in fact, have a 1.

00:09:43.121 --> 00:09:44.731
Well, how do I count higher than 1?

00:09:44.731 --> 00:09:46.341
I, of course, need another light bulb.

00:09:46.341 --> 00:09:48.531
So let me grab another one here.

00:09:48.531 --> 00:09:53.351
And if I put it in that same kind of
pattern, I don't want to just do this.

00:09:53.351 --> 00:09:57.141
That's sort of the old finger
counting way of unary, just 1, 2.

00:09:57.141 --> 00:09:59.141
I want to actually take
into account the pattern

00:09:59.141 --> 00:10:00.681
of these things being on and off.

00:10:00.681 --> 00:10:06.731
So if this was one a moment ago, what I
think I did earlier was I turned it off

00:10:06.731 --> 00:10:10.661
and let the next one over be on, a.k.a.

00:10:10.661 --> 00:10:12.071
010.

00:10:12.071 --> 00:10:15.111
And let me get us a
third bit, if you will.

00:10:15.111 --> 00:10:16.601
And that feels like enough.

00:10:16.601 --> 00:10:20.440
Here is that same pattern now,
starting at the beginning with 3.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.841
So here is 000.

00:10:22.841 --> 00:10:25.721
Here is 001.

00:10:25.721 --> 00:10:32.771
Here is 010, a.k.a., in our
human world of decimal, 2.

00:10:32.771 --> 00:10:35.171
And then we could, of course,
keep counting further.

00:10:35.171 --> 00:10:37.941
This now would be 3 and dot dot dot.

00:10:37.941 --> 00:10:40.871
If this other bulb now goes
on, and that switch is turned

00:10:40.871 --> 00:10:43.361
and all three stay on--
this, again, was what number?

00:10:43.361 --> 00:10:44.036
AUDIENCE: Seven.

00:10:44.036 --> 00:10:45.581
DAVID MALAN: OK, so, seven.

00:10:45.581 --> 00:10:49.811
So it's just as simple,
relatively, as that, if you will.

00:10:49.811 --> 00:10:53.981
But how is it that these
patterns came to be?

00:10:53.981 --> 00:10:56.806
Well, these patterns actually
follow something very familiar.

00:10:56.806 --> 00:10:58.931
You and I don't really
think about it at this level

00:10:58.931 --> 00:11:02.801
anymore because we've probably been
doing math and numbers since grade

00:11:02.801 --> 00:11:04.271
school or whatnot.

00:11:04.271 --> 00:11:09.161
But if we consider something in
decimal, like the number 123,

00:11:09.161 --> 00:11:10.301
I immediately jump to that.

00:11:10.301 --> 00:11:12.971
This looks like 123 in decimal.

00:11:12.971 --> 00:11:13.661
But why?

00:11:13.661 --> 00:11:17.801
It's really just three symbols,
a 1, a 2 with a bit of curve, a 3

00:11:17.801 --> 00:11:20.291
with a couple of curves, that
you and I now instinctively

00:11:20.291 --> 00:11:22.151
just assign meaning to.

00:11:22.151 --> 00:11:27.001
But if we do rewind a few years,
that is one hundred twenty-three

00:11:27.001 --> 00:11:30.481
because you're assigning meaning
to each of these columns.

00:11:30.481 --> 00:11:33.331
The 3 is in the so-called ones place.

00:11:33.331 --> 00:11:36.931
The 2 is in the so-called tens place.

00:11:36.931 --> 00:11:39.841
And the 1 is in the
so-called hundreds place.

00:11:39.841 --> 00:11:41.851
And then the math ensues
quickly in your head.

00:11:41.851 --> 00:11:47.311
This is technically 100 times 1, plus
10 times 2, plus 1 times 3, a.k.a.

00:11:47.311 --> 00:11:48.991
100 plus 20 plus 3.

00:11:48.991 --> 00:11:54.391
And there we get the sort of
mathematical notion we know as 123.

00:11:54.391 --> 00:11:58.571
Well, nicely enough, in binary,
it's actually the same thing.

00:11:58.571 --> 00:12:01.021
It's just these columns mean
a little something different.

00:12:01.021 --> 00:12:05.011
If you use three digits in decimal,
and you have the ones place,

00:12:05.011 --> 00:12:09.331
the tens place, and the hundreds place,
well, why was that 1, 10, and 100?

00:12:09.331 --> 00:12:11.231
They're technically just powers of 10.

00:12:11.231 --> 00:12:13.621
So 10 to the 0, 10 to
the 1, 10 to the 2.

00:12:13.621 --> 00:12:14.521
Why 10?

00:12:14.521 --> 00:12:16.441
Decimal system, "dec" meaning 10.

00:12:16.441 --> 00:12:18.841
You have 8 and 10 digits, 0 through 9.

00:12:18.841 --> 00:12:21.391
In the binary system, if you're
going to use three digits,

00:12:21.391 --> 00:12:24.911
just change the bases if you're
using only zeros and ones.

00:12:24.911 --> 00:12:29.131
So now it's powers of 2, 2 to the
0, 2 to the 1, 2 to the 2, a.k.a.

00:12:29.131 --> 00:12:31.901
1 and 2 and 4, respectively.

00:12:31.901 --> 00:12:36.121
And if you keep going, it's going
to be 8s column, 16s column, 32, 64,

00:12:36.121 --> 00:12:37.511
and so forth.

00:12:37.511 --> 00:12:40.261
So, why did we get these
patterns that we did?

00:12:40.261 --> 00:12:46.291
Here's your 000 because it's 4 times
0, 2 times 0, 1 times 0, obviously 0.

00:12:46.291 --> 00:12:49.471
This is why we got the
decimal number 1 in binary.

00:12:49.471 --> 00:12:53.341
This is why we got the number 2
in binary, because it's 4 times

00:12:53.341 --> 00:13:01.051
0, plus 2 times 1, plus 1 times 0, and
now 3, and now 4, and now 5, and now 6,

00:13:01.051 --> 00:13:01.831
and now 7.

00:13:01.831 --> 00:13:05.373
And, of course, if you wanted to
count as high as 8, to be clear,

00:13:05.373 --> 00:13:06.331
what do you have to do?

00:13:06.331 --> 00:13:08.831
What does a computer need to
do to count even higher than 7?

00:13:08.831 --> 00:13:09.813
AUDIENCE: Add a bit.

00:13:09.813 --> 00:13:10.881
DAVID MALAN: Add a bit.

00:13:10.881 --> 00:13:12.531
Add another light bulb, another switch.

00:13:12.531 --> 00:13:14.811
And, indeed, computers
have standardized just how

00:13:14.811 --> 00:13:17.241
many zeros and ones,
or bits or switches,

00:13:17.241 --> 00:13:19.021
they throw at these kinds of problems.

00:13:19.021 --> 00:13:23.301
And, in fact, most computers would
typically use at least eight at a time.

00:13:23.301 --> 00:13:25.971
And even if you're only counting
as high as three or seven,

00:13:25.971 --> 00:13:28.431
you would still use eight and
have a whole bunch of zeros.

00:13:28.431 --> 00:13:31.551
But that's OK, because the
computers these days certainly

00:13:31.551 --> 00:13:35.301
have so many more, thousands,
millions of transistors and switches

00:13:35.301 --> 00:13:37.921
that that's quite OK.

00:13:37.921 --> 00:13:41.671
All right, so, with that said, if
we can now count as high as seven

00:13:41.671 --> 00:13:44.161
or, frankly, as high
as we want, that only

00:13:44.161 --> 00:13:46.771
seems to make computers
useful for things like Excel,

00:13:46.771 --> 00:13:48.031
like number crunching.

00:13:48.031 --> 00:13:50.281
But computers, of course,
let you send text messages,

00:13:50.281 --> 00:13:52.241
write documents, and so much more.

00:13:52.241 --> 00:13:55.531
So how would a computer represent
something like a letter,

00:13:55.531 --> 00:13:59.861
like the letter A of the English
alphabet, if, at the end of the day,

00:13:59.861 --> 00:14:02.679
all they have is switches?

00:14:02.679 --> 00:14:03.221
Any thoughts?

00:14:03.221 --> 00:14:03.721
Yeah.

00:14:03.721 --> 00:14:05.961
AUDIENCE: You can represent
letters in numbers.

00:14:05.961 --> 00:14:08.571
DAVID MALAN: OK, so we could
represent letters using numbers.

00:14:08.571 --> 00:14:10.004
OK, so what's a proposal?

00:14:10.004 --> 00:14:11.421
What number should represent what?

00:14:11.421 --> 00:14:15.221
AUDIENCE: Say if you were starting
at the beginning of the alphabet,

00:14:15.221 --> 00:14:18.746
you could say 1 is A, 2 is B, 3 is C.

00:14:18.746 --> 00:14:19.621
DAVID MALAN: Perfect.

00:14:19.621 --> 00:14:22.631
Yeah, we just all have to agree
somehow that one number is

00:14:22.631 --> 00:14:23.881
going to represent one letter.

00:14:23.881 --> 00:14:28.491
So 1 is A, 2 is B, 3 is
C, Z is 26, and so forth.

00:14:28.491 --> 00:14:30.991
Maybe we can even take into
account uppercase and lowercase.

00:14:30.991 --> 00:14:34.231
We just have to agree and sort of
write it down in some global standard.

00:14:34.231 --> 00:14:36.461
And humans, indeed, did just that.

00:14:36.461 --> 00:14:38.011
They didn't use 1, 2, 3.

00:14:38.011 --> 00:14:40.111
It turns out they started
a little higher up.

00:14:40.111 --> 00:14:44.131
Capital A has been
standardized as the number 65.

00:14:44.131 --> 00:14:47.581
And capital B has been
standardized as the number 66.

00:14:47.581 --> 00:14:50.371
And you can kind of imagine
how it goes up from there.

00:14:50.371 --> 00:14:53.251
And that's because whatever
you're representing,

00:14:53.251 --> 00:14:57.461
ultimately, can only be stored, at
the end of the day, as zeros and ones.

00:14:57.461 --> 00:15:01.111
And so, some humans in a room before,
decided that capital A shall be 65,

00:15:01.111 --> 00:15:05.101
or, really, this pattern of zeros
and ones inside of every computer

00:15:05.101 --> 00:15:08.791
in the world, 01000001.

00:15:08.791 --> 00:15:12.481
So if that pattern of zeros and
ones ever appears in a computer,

00:15:12.481 --> 00:15:17.911
it might be interpreted then as indeed
a capital letter A, eight of those bits

00:15:17.911 --> 00:15:18.811
at a time.

00:15:18.811 --> 00:15:23.191
But I worry, just to be clear, we
might have now created a problem.

00:15:23.191 --> 00:15:25.681
It might seem, if I play
this naively, that, OK,

00:15:25.681 --> 00:15:28.861
how do I now actually do
math with the number 65?

00:15:28.861 --> 00:15:33.571
If now Excel displays 65 is
an A, let alone Bs and Cs.

00:15:33.571 --> 00:15:37.231
So how might a computer
do as you've proposed,

00:15:37.231 --> 00:15:41.876
have this mapping from numbers to
letters, but still support numbers?

00:15:41.876 --> 00:15:43.501
It feels like we've given something up.

00:15:43.501 --> 00:15:44.001
Yeah?

00:15:44.001 --> 00:15:46.263
AUDIENCE: By having
a prefix for letters?

00:15:46.263 --> 00:15:47.596
DAVID MALAN: By having a prefix?

00:15:47.596 --> 00:15:48.887
AUDIENCE: You could have
prefixes and suffixes.

00:15:48.887 --> 00:15:51.641
DAVID MALAN: OK, so we could
perhaps have some kind of prefix,

00:15:51.641 --> 00:15:53.231
like some pattern of zeros and ones--

00:15:53.231 --> 00:15:56.201
I like this-- that
indicates to the computer

00:15:56.201 --> 00:15:58.871
here comes another pattern
that represents a letter.

00:15:58.871 --> 00:16:02.921
Here comes another pattern that
represents a number or a letter.

00:16:02.921 --> 00:16:03.551
So, not bad.

00:16:03.551 --> 00:16:04.371
I like that.

00:16:04.371 --> 00:16:05.621
Other thoughts?

00:16:05.621 --> 00:16:08.481
How might a computer
distinguish these two?

00:16:08.481 --> 00:16:08.981
Yeah.

00:16:08.981 --> 00:16:11.361
AUDIENCE: Have a
different file format, so,

00:16:11.361 --> 00:16:16.121
like, odd text or just
check the graphic or--

00:16:16.121 --> 00:16:17.841
DAVID MALAN: Indeed, and that's spot-on.

00:16:17.841 --> 00:16:20.781
Nothing wrong with what you suggested,
but the world generally does just that.

00:16:20.781 --> 00:16:23.614
The reason we have all of these
different file formats in the world,

00:16:23.614 --> 00:16:28.521
like JPEG and GIF and PNGs
and Word documents, .docx,

00:16:28.521 --> 00:16:32.271
and Excel files and so forth, is
because a bunch of humans got in a room

00:16:32.271 --> 00:16:35.781
and decided, well, in the context
of this type of file, or really,

00:16:35.781 --> 00:16:38.601
more specifically, in the
context of this type of program,

00:16:38.601 --> 00:16:42.141
Excel versus Photoshop versus
Google Docs or the like,

00:16:42.141 --> 00:16:46.551
we shall interpret any patterns of
zeros and ones as being maybe numbers

00:16:46.551 --> 00:16:51.051
for Excel, maybe letters in, like, a
text messaging program or Google Docs,

00:16:51.051 --> 00:16:54.471
or maybe even colors of the rainbow
in something like Photoshop and more.

00:16:54.471 --> 00:16:55.611
So it's context dependent.

00:16:55.611 --> 00:16:58.491
And we'll see, when we
ourselves start programming,

00:16:58.491 --> 00:17:00.471
you the programmer
will ultimately provide

00:17:00.471 --> 00:17:05.671
some hints to the computer that tells
the computer, interpret it as follows.

00:17:05.671 --> 00:17:08.631
So, similar in spirit to that, but
not quite a standardized with these

00:17:08.631 --> 00:17:09.261
prefixes.

00:17:09.261 --> 00:17:12.651
So this system here actually has a
name ASCII, the American Standard

00:17:12.651 --> 00:17:14.421
Code for Information Interchange.

00:17:14.421 --> 00:17:16.761
And indeed, it began here
in the US, and that's

00:17:16.761 --> 00:17:19.551
why it's actually a little
biased toward A's through Z's

00:17:19.551 --> 00:17:21.301
and a bit of punctuation as well.

00:17:21.301 --> 00:17:22.921
And that quickly became a problem.

00:17:22.921 --> 00:17:26.781
But if we start simply now,
in English, the mapping

00:17:26.781 --> 00:17:28.861
itself is fairly straightforward.

00:17:28.861 --> 00:17:33.291
So if A is 65, B it 66,
and dot dot dot, suppose

00:17:33.291 --> 00:17:36.021
that you received a text
message, an email, from a friend,

00:17:36.021 --> 00:17:39.081
and underneath the hood,
so to speak, if you kind of

00:17:39.081 --> 00:17:42.741
looked inside the computer, what you
technically received in this text

00:17:42.741 --> 00:17:48.081
or this email happened to
be the numbers 72, 73, 33,

00:17:48.081 --> 00:17:50.811
or, really, the underlying
pattern of zeros and ones.

00:17:50.811 --> 00:17:56.377
What might your friend have sent you
as a message, if it's 72, 73, 33?

00:17:56.377 --> 00:17:58.213
AUDIENCE: Hey.

00:17:58.213 --> 00:17:58.921
DAVID MALAN: Hey?

00:17:58.921 --> 00:17:59.281
Close.

00:17:59.281 --> 00:17:59.941
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:17:59.941 --> 00:18:00.781
DAVID MALAN: Hi.

00:18:00.781 --> 00:18:01.781
It's, indeed, hi.

00:18:01.781 --> 00:18:02.281
Why?

00:18:02.281 --> 00:18:06.781
Well, apparently, according to this
little cheat sheet, H is 72, I is 73.

00:18:06.781 --> 00:18:09.421
It's not obvious from
this chart what the 33 is,

00:18:09.421 --> 00:18:11.521
but indeed, this
pattern represents "hi."

00:18:11.521 --> 00:18:13.774
And anyone want to guess,
or if you know, what 33 is?

00:18:13.774 --> 00:18:14.941
AUDIENCE: Exclamation point.

00:18:14.941 --> 00:18:16.021
DAVID MALAN: Exclamation point.

00:18:16.021 --> 00:18:18.563
And this is, frankly, not the
kind of thing most people know.

00:18:18.563 --> 00:18:22.241
But it's easily accessible by a
nice user-friendly chart like this.

00:18:22.241 --> 00:18:23.671
So this is an ASCII chart.

00:18:23.671 --> 00:18:26.431
When I said that we just need to
write down this mapping earlier,

00:18:26.431 --> 00:18:27.511
this is what people did.

00:18:27.511 --> 00:18:29.303
They wrote it down in
a book or in a chart.

00:18:29.303 --> 00:18:33.961
And, for instance, here is our
72 for H, here is our 73 for I,

00:18:33.961 --> 00:18:37.861
and here is our 33
for exclamation point.

00:18:37.861 --> 00:18:41.191
And computers, Macs, PCs,
iPhones, Android devices,

00:18:41.191 --> 00:18:43.721
just know this mapping
by heart, if you will.

00:18:43.721 --> 00:18:46.451
They've been designed to
understand those letters.

00:18:46.451 --> 00:18:48.121
So here, I might have received "hi."

00:18:48.121 --> 00:18:51.750
Technically, what I've received is
these patterns of zeros and ones.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:54.960
But it's important to note that when
you get these patterns of zeros and ones

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:58.021
in any format, be it
email or text or a file,

00:18:58.021 --> 00:19:00.661
they do tend to come
in standard lengths,

00:19:00.661 --> 00:19:04.351
with a certain number of
zeros and ones altogether.

00:19:04.351 --> 00:19:07.200
And this happens to be 8 plus 8, plus 8.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.561
So just to get the message
"hi, exclamation point,"

00:19:10.561 --> 00:19:15.001
you would have received at least,
it would seem, some 24 bits.

00:19:15.001 --> 00:19:18.331
But frankly, bits are so tiny,
literally and mathematically,

00:19:18.331 --> 00:19:21.061
that we don't tend to think or
talk, generally, in terms of bits.

00:19:21.061 --> 00:19:23.371
You're probably more
familiar with bytes.

00:19:23.371 --> 00:19:27.481
B-Y-T-E-S is a byte,
is a byte, is a byte.

00:19:27.481 --> 00:19:29.341
A byte is just 8 bits.

00:19:29.341 --> 00:19:32.374
And even those, frankly, aren't
that useful if we do out the math.

00:19:32.374 --> 00:19:34.291
How high can you count
if you have eight bits?

00:19:34.291 --> 00:19:35.921
Anyone know?

00:19:35.921 --> 00:19:38.131
Say it again?

00:19:38.131 --> 00:19:39.271
Higher than that.

00:19:39.271 --> 00:19:42.571
Unless you want to go
negative, that's fine.

00:19:42.571 --> 00:19:45.151
256, technically 255.

00:19:45.151 --> 00:19:48.631
Long story short, if we actually got
into the weeds of all of these zeros

00:19:48.631 --> 00:19:54.061
and ones, and we figured out what
11111111 mathematically adds up

00:19:54.061 --> 00:19:57.571
to in decimal, it would
indeed be 255, or less

00:19:57.571 --> 00:20:00.281
if you want to represent
negative numbers as well.

00:20:00.281 --> 00:20:04.141
So this is useful because now we can
speak, not just in terms of bytes

00:20:04.141 --> 00:20:06.811
but, if the files are bigger,
kilobytes is thousands of bytes,

00:20:06.811 --> 00:20:10.591
megabytes is millions of bytes,
gigabytes is billions of bytes,

00:20:10.591 --> 00:20:14.081
terabytes are trillions
of bytes, and so forth.

00:20:14.081 --> 00:20:20.521
We have a vocabulary for these
increasingly large quantities of data.

00:20:20.521 --> 00:20:24.351
The problem is that, if you're using
ASCII and, therefore, eight bits or one

00:20:24.351 --> 00:20:27.801
byte per character, and
originally, only seven, you

00:20:27.801 --> 00:20:30.391
can only represent 255 characters.

00:20:30.391 --> 00:20:33.981
And that's actually 256 total
characters, including zero.

00:20:33.981 --> 00:20:37.791
And that's fine if you're using
literally English, in this case,

00:20:37.791 --> 00:20:39.141
plus a bunch of punctuation.

00:20:39.141 --> 00:20:42.081
But there's many human
languages in the world

00:20:42.081 --> 00:20:45.411
that need many more symbols
and, therefore, many more bits.

00:20:45.411 --> 00:20:48.291
So, thankfully, the world
decided that we'll indeed

00:20:48.291 --> 00:20:51.441
support not just the US
English keyboard, but all

00:20:51.441 --> 00:20:54.501
of the accented characters that
you might want for some languages.

00:20:54.501 --> 00:20:57.741
And heck, if we use enough
bits, zeros and ones,

00:20:57.741 --> 00:21:01.731
not only can we represent all
human languages in written form,

00:21:01.731 --> 00:21:03.651
as well as some emotions
along the way, we

00:21:03.651 --> 00:21:06.621
can capture the latter with
these things called emojis.

00:21:06.621 --> 00:21:09.231
And indeed, these are very
much in vogue these days.

00:21:09.231 --> 00:21:12.951
You probably send and/or receive
many of these things any given day.

00:21:12.951 --> 00:21:16.521
These are just characters, like
letters of an alphabet, patterns

00:21:16.521 --> 00:21:20.571
of zeros and ones that you're receiving,
that the world has also standardized.

00:21:20.571 --> 00:21:22.691
For instance, there
are certain emojis that

00:21:22.691 --> 00:21:24.721
are represented with
certain patterns of bits.

00:21:24.721 --> 00:21:28.071
And when you receive them, your
phone, your laptop, your desktop,

00:21:28.071 --> 00:21:30.001
displays them as such.

00:21:30.001 --> 00:21:32.781
And this newer standard
is called Unicode.

00:21:32.781 --> 00:21:35.271
So it's a superset of
what we called ASCII.

00:21:35.271 --> 00:21:39.951
And Unicode is just a mapping of many
more numbers to many more letters

00:21:39.951 --> 00:21:42.321
or characters, more
generally, that might

00:21:42.321 --> 00:21:45.141
use eight bits for
backwards compatibility

00:21:45.141 --> 00:21:49.821
with the old way of doing things with
ASCII, but they might also use 16 bits.

00:21:49.821 --> 00:21:51.681
And if you have 16
bits, you can actually

00:21:51.681 --> 00:21:54.714
represent more than
65,000 possible letters.

00:21:54.714 --> 00:21:55.881
And that's getting up there.

00:21:55.881 --> 00:22:01.341
And heck, Unicode might even use 32
bits to represent letters and numbers

00:22:01.341 --> 00:22:03.231
and punctuation symbols and emojis.

00:22:03.231 --> 00:22:06.411
And that would give you up
to 4 billion possibilities.

00:22:06.411 --> 00:22:09.981
And, I daresay, one of the reasons we
see so many emojis these days is we

00:22:09.981 --> 00:22:11.061
have so much room.

00:22:11.061 --> 00:22:14.061
I mean, we've got room for
billions more, literally.

00:22:14.061 --> 00:22:16.281
So, in fact, just as a
little bit of trivia,

00:22:16.281 --> 00:22:21.501
has anyone ever received this decimal
number, or if you prefer binary now,

00:22:21.501 --> 00:22:25.971
has anyone ever received this pattern
of zeros and ones on your phone,

00:22:25.971 --> 00:22:29.071
in a text or an email,
perhaps this past year?

00:22:29.071 --> 00:22:33.951
Well, if you actually look this up,
this esoteric sequence of zeros and ones

00:22:33.951 --> 00:22:37.131
happens to represent
face with medical mask.

00:22:37.131 --> 00:22:40.941
And notice that if you've got
an iPhone or an Android device,

00:22:40.941 --> 00:22:43.291
you might be seeing different things.

00:22:43.291 --> 00:22:46.581
In fact, this is the Android
version of this, most recently.

00:22:46.581 --> 00:22:49.221
This is the iOS version
of it, most recently.

00:22:49.221 --> 00:22:51.891
And there's bunches of other
interpretations by other companies

00:22:51.891 --> 00:22:53.041
as well.

00:22:53.041 --> 00:22:55.731
So Unicode, as a
consortium, if you will,

00:22:55.731 --> 00:22:58.861
has standardized the descriptions
of what these things are.

00:22:58.861 --> 00:23:02.391
But the companies themselves,
manufacturers out there,

00:23:02.391 --> 00:23:05.001
have generally interpreted
it as you see fit.

00:23:05.001 --> 00:23:08.331
And this can lead to some
human miscommunications.

00:23:08.331 --> 00:23:11.871
In fact, for like, literally,
embarrassingly, like a year or two,

00:23:11.871 --> 00:23:14.781
I started being in the habit of
using the emoji that kind of looks

00:23:14.781 --> 00:23:17.821
like this because I thought it was
like woo, happy face, or whatever.

00:23:17.821 --> 00:23:19.761
I didn't realize this
is the emoji for hug

00:23:19.761 --> 00:23:24.351
because whatever device I was using
sort of looks like this, not like this.

00:23:24.351 --> 00:23:27.171
And that's because of their
interpretation of the data.

00:23:27.171 --> 00:23:31.551
This has happened too when
what was a gun became a water

00:23:31.551 --> 00:23:33.591
pistol in some manufacturers' eyes.

00:23:33.591 --> 00:23:37.971
And so it's an interesting dichotomy
between what information we all

00:23:37.971 --> 00:23:42.501
want to represent and how we
choose, ultimately, to represent it.

00:23:42.501 --> 00:23:45.891
Questions, then, on these
representations of formats,

00:23:45.891 --> 00:23:49.021
be it numbers or letters, or soon more.

00:23:49.021 --> 00:23:49.521
Yeah?

00:23:49.521 --> 00:23:52.141
AUDIENCE: Why is decimal
popular for a computer

00:23:52.141 --> 00:23:54.739
if binary is the basis for everything?

00:23:54.739 --> 00:23:56.531
DAVID MALAN: Sorry,
why is what so popular?

00:23:56.531 --> 00:23:59.311
AUDIENCE: Why is the decimal popular
if binary is the fundamental--

00:23:59.311 --> 00:24:01.901
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, so we'll come
back to this in a few weeks, in fact.

00:24:01.901 --> 00:24:03.811
There are other ways
to represent numbers.

00:24:03.811 --> 00:24:04.921
Binary is one.

00:24:04.921 --> 00:24:06.001
Decimal is another.

00:24:06.001 --> 00:24:07.171
Unary is another.

00:24:07.171 --> 00:24:12.481
And hexadecimal is yet a fourth that
uses 16 total digits, literally 0

00:24:12.481 --> 00:24:15.541
through 9 plus A, B, C,
D, E, F. And somehow,

00:24:15.541 --> 00:24:18.601
you can similarly count
even higher with those.

00:24:18.601 --> 00:24:21.121
We'll see in a few weeks
why this is compelling.

00:24:21.121 --> 00:24:24.781
But hexadecimal, long story
short, uses four bits per digit.

00:24:24.781 --> 00:24:28.291
And so, four bits, if you have two
digits in hex, that gives you eight.

00:24:28.291 --> 00:24:30.811
And it's just a very
convenient unit of measure.

00:24:30.811 --> 00:24:34.141
And it's also human convention in
the world of files and other things.

00:24:34.141 --> 00:24:35.874
But we'll come back to that soon.

00:24:35.874 --> 00:24:36.541
Other questions?

00:24:36.541 --> 00:24:39.924
AUDIENCE: Do the lights on the
stage supposedly say that--

00:24:39.924 --> 00:24:42.591
DAVID MALAN: Do the lights on the
stage supposedly say anything?

00:24:42.591 --> 00:24:46.311
Well, if we had thought in advance
to use maybe 64 light bulbs,

00:24:46.311 --> 00:24:51.651
that would seem to give us 8
total bytes on stage, 8 times 8,

00:24:51.651 --> 00:24:52.761
giving us just that.

00:24:52.761 --> 00:24:54.021
Maybe.

00:24:54.021 --> 00:24:55.171
Good question.

00:24:55.171 --> 00:24:58.447
Other questions on 0's and 1's?

00:24:58.447 --> 00:25:01.131
It's a little bright in here.

00:25:01.131 --> 00:25:02.061
No?

00:25:02.061 --> 00:25:04.911
Oh, yes?

00:25:04.911 --> 00:25:08.931
Where everyone's pointing
somewhere specific.

00:25:08.931 --> 00:25:09.571
There we go.

00:25:09.571 --> 00:25:10.161
Sorry.

00:25:10.161 --> 00:25:11.391
Very bright in this corner.

00:25:11.391 --> 00:25:14.863
AUDIENCE: I was just going
to ask about the 255 bits,

00:25:14.863 --> 00:25:16.347
like with the maximum characters.

00:25:16.347 --> 00:25:16.847
[INAUDIBLE]

00:25:16.847 --> 00:25:19.555
DAVID MALAN: Ah, sure, and we'll
come back to this, in some form,

00:25:19.555 --> 00:25:22.131
in the coming days too,
at a slower pace too,

00:25:22.131 --> 00:25:26.074
we have, with eight bits, two
possible values for the first

00:25:26.074 --> 00:25:28.491
and then two for the next, two
for the next, and so forth.

00:25:28.491 --> 00:25:30.131
So that's 2 times 2 times 2.

00:25:30.131 --> 00:25:32.231
That's 2 to the eighth
power total, which

00:25:32.231 --> 00:25:36.731
means you can have 256 total
possible patterns of zeros and ones.

00:25:36.731 --> 00:25:40.581
But as we'll see soon computer
scientists, programmers,

00:25:40.581 --> 00:25:45.251
software often starts counting at 0 by
convention and if you use one of those

00:25:45.251 --> 00:25:50.981
patterns, 00000000 to represent
the decimal number we know is zero,

00:25:50.981 --> 00:25:56.831
you only have 255 other patterns left
to count as high as therefore 255.

00:25:56.831 --> 00:25:57.711
That's all.

00:25:57.711 --> 00:25:59.421
Good question.

00:25:59.421 --> 00:26:04.421
All right, so what then might we
have besides these emojis and letters

00:26:04.421 --> 00:26:04.991
and numbers?

00:26:04.991 --> 00:26:07.451
Well, we of course have things
like colors and programs

00:26:07.451 --> 00:26:09.683
like Photoshop and pictures and photos.

00:26:09.683 --> 00:26:11.141
Well let me ask the question again.

00:26:11.141 --> 00:26:14.351
How might a computer, do you think,
knowing what you know now, represents

00:26:14.351 --> 00:26:16.001
something like a color?

00:26:16.001 --> 00:26:19.511
Like what are our options if all we've
got are zeros and ones and switches?

00:26:19.511 --> 00:26:20.358
Yeah?

00:26:20.358 --> 00:26:21.821
AUDIENCE: RGB

00:26:21.821 --> 00:26:23.471
DAVID MALAN: RGB.

00:26:23.471 --> 00:26:27.071
RGB indeed is this acronym that
represents some amount of red

00:26:27.071 --> 00:26:29.591
and some amount of green and
blue and indeed computers

00:26:29.591 --> 00:26:32.381
can represent colors by just doing that.

00:26:32.381 --> 00:26:33.893
Remembering, for instance, this dot.

00:26:33.893 --> 00:26:36.851
This yellow dot on the screen that
might be part of any of those emojis

00:26:36.851 --> 00:26:39.611
these days, well that's some amount
of red, some amount of green,

00:26:39.611 --> 00:26:40.444
some amount of blue.

00:26:40.444 --> 00:26:42.341
And if you sort of mix
those colors together,

00:26:42.341 --> 00:26:44.081
you can indeed get a very specific one.

00:26:44.081 --> 00:26:46.661
And we'll see you in
just a moment just that.

00:26:46.661 --> 00:26:51.408
So indeed earlier on, humans
only used seven bits total.

00:26:51.408 --> 00:26:54.491
And it was only once they decided,
well, let's add an eighth bit that they

00:26:54.491 --> 00:26:57.101
got extended ASCII and
that was initially in part

00:26:57.101 --> 00:27:00.941
a solution to the same problem of
not having enough room, if you will,

00:27:00.941 --> 00:27:05.248
in those patterns of zeros and ones
to represent all of the characters

00:27:05.248 --> 00:27:06.081
that you might want.

00:27:06.081 --> 00:27:10.331
But even that wasn't enough and that's
why we've now gone up to 16 and 32

00:27:10.331 --> 00:27:11.891
and long past 7.

00:27:11.891 --> 00:27:16.961
So if we come back now to
this one particular color.

00:27:16.961 --> 00:27:19.331
RGB was proposed as a scheme,
but how might this work?

00:27:19.331 --> 00:27:21.281
Well, consider for instance this.

00:27:21.281 --> 00:27:25.661
If we do indeed decide as a group to
represent any color of the rainbow

00:27:25.661 --> 00:27:28.421
with some mixture of some red,
some green, and some blue,

00:27:28.421 --> 00:27:33.311
we have to decide how to represent
the amount of red and green and blue.

00:27:33.311 --> 00:27:37.031
Well, it turns out if all we have
are zeros and ones, ergo numbers,

00:27:37.031 --> 00:27:38.351
let's do just that.

00:27:38.351 --> 00:27:44.501
For instance, suppose a computer we're
using, these three numbers 72, 73, 33,

00:27:44.501 --> 00:27:47.471
no longer in the context of
an email or a text message,

00:27:47.471 --> 00:27:51.701
but now in the context of something
like Photoshop, a program for editing

00:27:51.701 --> 00:27:55.331
and creating graphical files,
maybe this first number

00:27:55.331 --> 00:28:00.141
could be interpreted as representing
some amount of red, green, and blue,

00:28:00.141 --> 00:28:00.688
respectively.

00:28:00.688 --> 00:28:02.021
And that's exactly what happens.

00:28:02.021 --> 00:28:05.741
You can think of the first digit as
red, second as green, third as blue.

00:28:05.741 --> 00:28:10.031
And so ultimately when you combine that
amount of red, that amount of green,

00:28:10.031 --> 00:28:14.231
that amount of blue, it turns out it's
going to resemble the shade of yellow.

00:28:14.231 --> 00:28:18.041
And indeed, you can come up
with a numbers between 0 and 255

00:28:18.041 --> 00:28:21.461
for each of those colors to mix any
other color that you might want.

00:28:21.461 --> 00:28:23.331
And you can actually
see this in practice.

00:28:23.331 --> 00:28:26.531
Even though our screens,
admittedly, are getting really good

00:28:26.531 --> 00:28:30.739
on our phones and laptops such that you
barely see the dots, they are there.

00:28:30.739 --> 00:28:32.531
You might have heard
the term pixel before.

00:28:32.531 --> 00:28:34.661
Pixel's just a dot on
the screen and you've

00:28:34.661 --> 00:28:38.261
got thousands, millions of them these
days horizontally and vertically.

00:28:38.261 --> 00:28:41.201
If I take even this
emoji, which again happens

00:28:41.201 --> 00:28:46.211
to be one company's interpretation
of a face with medical mask

00:28:46.211 --> 00:28:48.551
and zoom in a bit, maybe
zoom in a bit more,

00:28:48.551 --> 00:28:50.891
you can actually start
to see these pixels.

00:28:50.891 --> 00:28:53.561
Things get pixelated
because what you're seeing

00:28:53.561 --> 00:28:57.281
is each of the individual dots
that compose this particular image.

00:28:57.281 --> 00:28:59.681
And apparently each of
these individual dots

00:28:59.681 --> 00:29:04.961
are probably using 24 bits, eight bits
for red, eight bits for green, eight

00:29:04.961 --> 00:29:07.301
bits for blue, in some pattern.

00:29:07.301 --> 00:29:11.291
This program or some other like
Photoshop is interpreting one pattern

00:29:11.291 --> 00:29:16.271
and it's white or yellow or
black or some brown in between.

00:29:16.271 --> 00:29:19.871
So if you look sort of awkwardly, but
up close to your phone or your laptop

00:29:19.871 --> 00:29:23.673
or maybe your TV, you can
see exactly this, too.

00:29:23.673 --> 00:29:25.631
All right, well, what
about things that we also

00:29:25.631 --> 00:29:27.256
watch every day on YouTube or the like?

00:29:27.256 --> 00:29:28.541
Things like videos.

00:29:28.541 --> 00:29:30.551
How would a computer,
knowing what we know now,

00:29:30.551 --> 00:29:32.546
represent something like a video?

00:29:35.081 --> 00:29:37.931
How might you represent a video
using only zeros and ones?

00:29:37.931 --> 00:29:38.761
Yeah?

00:29:38.761 --> 00:29:43.261
AUDIENCE: As we can see here,
they represent images, right?

00:29:43.261 --> 00:29:47.761
[INAUDIBLE] sounds of
the 0 and 1s as well.

00:29:47.761 --> 00:29:50.761
[INAUDIBLE]

00:29:50.761 --> 00:29:52.041
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, exactly.

00:29:52.041 --> 00:29:55.411
To summarize, what video really
adds is just some notion of time.

00:29:55.411 --> 00:29:58.131
It's not just one image, it's
not just one letter or a number,

00:29:58.131 --> 00:30:01.911
it's presumably some kind of
sequence because time is passing.

00:30:01.911 --> 00:30:05.781
So with a whole bunch of images,
maybe 24 maybe 30 per second,

00:30:05.781 --> 00:30:08.481
if you fly them by the
human's eyes, we can

00:30:08.481 --> 00:30:11.241
interpret them using our eyes
and brain that there is now

00:30:11.241 --> 00:30:13.431
movement and therefore video.

00:30:13.431 --> 00:30:16.041
Similarly with audio or music.

00:30:16.041 --> 00:30:20.211
If we just came up with some convention
for representing those same notes

00:30:20.211 --> 00:30:23.361
on a musical instrument, could we have
the computer synthesize them, too?

00:30:23.361 --> 00:30:25.153
And this might be
actually pretty familiar.

00:30:25.153 --> 00:30:29.901
Let me pull up a quick video here,
which happens to be an old school

00:30:29.901 --> 00:30:31.311
version of the same idea.

00:30:31.311 --> 00:30:32.991
You might remember from childhood.

00:30:32.991 --> 00:30:36.983
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:40.975 --> 00:30:44.468
[CLICKING]

00:30:54.961 --> 00:30:58.861
So granted that particular
video is an actual video

00:30:58.861 --> 00:31:02.311
of a paper-based animation, but
indeed, that's really all you need,

00:31:02.311 --> 00:31:06.271
is some sequence of these images,
which themselves of course

00:31:06.271 --> 00:31:10.321
are just zeros and ones because they're
just this grid of these pixels or dots.

00:31:10.321 --> 00:31:14.004
Now something like musical notes like
these, those of you who are musicians

00:31:14.004 --> 00:31:16.171
might just naturally play
these on physical devices,

00:31:16.171 --> 00:31:19.201
but computers can certainly
represent those sounds, too.

00:31:19.201 --> 00:31:22.051
For instance, a popular
format for audio is

00:31:22.051 --> 00:31:24.781
called MIDI and MIDI
might just represent

00:31:24.781 --> 00:31:29.101
each note that you saw a moment ago
essentially as a sequence of numbers.

00:31:29.101 --> 00:31:32.491
But more generally, you might
think about music as having notes,

00:31:32.491 --> 00:31:35.401
for instance, A through G, maybe
some flats and some sharps,

00:31:35.401 --> 00:31:39.001
you might have the duration like how
long is the note being heard or played

00:31:39.001 --> 00:31:41.041
on a piano or some
other device, and then

00:31:41.041 --> 00:31:43.771
just the volume like how hard
does a human in the real world

00:31:43.771 --> 00:31:46.681
press down on that key and
therefore how loud is that sound?

00:31:46.681 --> 00:31:51.571
It would seem that just remembering
little details like that quantitatively

00:31:51.571 --> 00:31:57.091
we can then represent really all of
these otherwise analog human realities.

00:31:57.091 --> 00:32:00.421
So that then is really
a laundry list of ways

00:32:00.421 --> 00:32:02.551
that we can just represent information.

00:32:02.551 --> 00:32:05.314
Again, computers or digital have
all of these different formats,

00:32:05.314 --> 00:32:07.981
but at the end of the day and as
fancy as those devices in years

00:32:07.981 --> 00:32:11.761
are, it's just zeros and ones, tiny
little switches or light bulbs,

00:32:11.761 --> 00:32:14.941
if you will, represented in some
way and it's up to the software

00:32:14.941 --> 00:32:17.941
that you and I and others
write to use those zeros

00:32:17.941 --> 00:32:21.421
and ones in ways we want to get
the computers to do something

00:32:21.421 --> 00:32:22.861
more powerfully.

00:32:22.861 --> 00:32:27.181
Questions, then, on this representation
of information, which I daresay

00:32:27.181 --> 00:32:30.811
is ultimately what problem solving
is all about, taking in information

00:32:30.811 --> 00:32:35.591
and producing new via
some process in between.

00:32:35.591 --> 00:32:38.921
Any questions there?

00:32:38.921 --> 00:32:40.071
Yeah, in back.

00:32:40.071 --> 00:32:43.999
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so we talked about how
different file formats kind of allow

00:32:43.999 --> 00:32:45.963
you to interpret information.

00:32:45.963 --> 00:32:50.873
How does a file format like .mp4
discriminate between audio and video

00:32:50.873 --> 00:32:52.346
within itself as a value?

00:32:52.346 --> 00:32:53.971
DAVID MALAN: So a really good question.

00:32:53.971 --> 00:32:55.921
There are many other
file formats out there.

00:32:55.921 --> 00:32:58.731
You allude to MP4 for video
and more generally the use

00:32:58.731 --> 00:33:01.341
are these things called
codecs and containers.

00:33:01.341 --> 00:33:04.911
It's not quite as simple when
using larger files, for instance,

00:33:04.911 --> 00:33:08.449
in more modern formats that a
video is just a sequence of images,

00:33:08.449 --> 00:33:08.991
for instance.

00:33:08.991 --> 00:33:09.561
Why?

00:33:09.561 --> 00:33:13.161
If you stored that many images
for like a Hollywood movie,

00:33:13.161 --> 00:33:17.431
like 24 or 30 of them per second,
that's a huge number of images.

00:33:17.431 --> 00:33:19.761
And if you've ever taken
photos on your phone,

00:33:19.761 --> 00:33:23.781
you might know how many megabytes or
larger even individual photographs

00:33:23.781 --> 00:33:24.381
might be.

00:33:24.381 --> 00:33:27.771
So humans have developed over
the years a fancier software

00:33:27.771 --> 00:33:32.481
that uses much more math to represent
the same information more minimally

00:33:32.481 --> 00:33:35.241
just using somehow shorter
patterns of zeros and ones

00:33:35.241 --> 00:33:37.831
than are most simplistic
representation here.

00:33:37.831 --> 00:33:40.161
And they use what might
be called compression.

00:33:40.161 --> 00:33:42.561
If you've ever used a zip
file or something else,

00:33:42.561 --> 00:33:45.171
somehow your computer is
using fewer zeros and ones

00:33:45.171 --> 00:33:47.061
to represent the same
amount of information,

00:33:47.061 --> 00:33:49.523
ideally without losing any information.

00:33:49.523 --> 00:33:52.731
In the world of multimedia, which we'll
touch on a little bit in a few weeks,

00:33:52.731 --> 00:33:56.331
there are both lossy and
lossless formats out there.

00:33:56.331 --> 00:33:59.961
Lossless means you lose
no information whatsoever.

00:33:59.961 --> 00:34:05.361
But more commonly as you're alluding
to one is lossy compression, L-O-S-S-Y,

00:34:05.361 --> 00:34:08.151
where you're actually throwing
away some amount of quality.

00:34:08.151 --> 00:34:10.593
You're getting some amount
of pixelation that might not

00:34:10.593 --> 00:34:13.551
look perfect to the human, but heck
it's a lot cheaper and a lot easier

00:34:13.551 --> 00:34:14.541
to distribute.

00:34:14.541 --> 00:34:17.691
And in the world of multimedia,
you have containers like QuickTime

00:34:17.691 --> 00:34:20.481
and other MPEG containers
that can combine

00:34:20.481 --> 00:34:24.561
different formats of video, different
formats of audio in one file,

00:34:24.561 --> 00:34:26.841
but there, too, do
designers have discretion.

00:34:26.841 --> 00:34:28.911
So more in a few weeks, too.

00:34:28.911 --> 00:34:32.311
Other questions, then, on
information here as well?

00:34:32.311 --> 00:34:32.811
Yeah?

00:34:32.811 --> 00:34:35.523
AUDIENCE: So I know
computers used to be very big

00:34:35.523 --> 00:34:37.511
and taking up like a
whole room and stuff.

00:34:37.511 --> 00:34:41.545
Is the reason they've gotten
smaller because we can store

00:34:41.545 --> 00:34:43.826
this information piecemeal or what?

00:34:43.826 --> 00:34:44.701
DAVID MALAN: Exactly.

00:34:44.701 --> 00:34:47.659
I mean, back in the day you might
have heard of the expression a vacuum

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:50.341
tube, which is like some
physically large device that

00:34:50.341 --> 00:34:53.281
might have only stored some 0 or 1.

00:34:53.281 --> 00:34:55.921
Yes, it is the miniaturization
of hardware these days

00:34:55.921 --> 00:35:00.721
that has allowed us to store as many
and many more zeros and ones much more

00:35:00.721 --> 00:35:01.661
closely together.

00:35:01.661 --> 00:35:03.584
And as we've built more
fancy machines that

00:35:03.584 --> 00:35:06.001
can sort of design this hardware
at an even smaller scale,

00:35:06.001 --> 00:35:08.531
we're just packing more and
more into these devices.

00:35:08.531 --> 00:35:09.841
But there, too, is a trade off.

00:35:09.841 --> 00:35:13.111
For instance, you might know by
using your phone or your laptop

00:35:13.111 --> 00:35:15.811
for quite a while, maybe on
your lap, starts to get warm.

00:35:15.811 --> 00:35:17.941
So there are these literal
physical side effects

00:35:17.941 --> 00:35:20.371
of this where now some
of our devices run hot.

00:35:20.371 --> 00:35:23.251
This is why like a data
center in the real world

00:35:23.251 --> 00:35:25.591
might need more air conditioning
than a typical place,

00:35:25.591 --> 00:35:28.713
because there are these
physical artifacts as well.

00:35:28.713 --> 00:35:31.921
In fact, if you'd like to see one of
the earliest computers from decades ago,

00:35:31.921 --> 00:35:35.311
across the river here in now Allston
in the new engineering building

00:35:35.311 --> 00:35:40.621
is the Harvard Mark 1 computer that
will give you a much better mental model

00:35:40.621 --> 00:35:41.981
of just that.

00:35:41.981 --> 00:35:44.491
Well if we come back now
to this first picture

00:35:44.491 --> 00:35:47.161
being computer science or
really problem solving,

00:35:47.161 --> 00:35:49.111
I daresay we have more
than enough ways now

00:35:49.111 --> 00:35:53.191
to represent information, input and
output, so long as we all just agree

00:35:53.191 --> 00:35:56.251
on something and thankfully all
of those before us have given us

00:35:56.251 --> 00:35:57.961
things like ASCII and Unicode.

00:35:57.961 --> 00:36:01.151
Not to mention MP4s, word
documents, and the like.

00:36:01.151 --> 00:36:05.251
But what's inside of this proverbial
black box into which these inputs are

00:36:05.251 --> 00:36:06.998
going in the outputs are coming?

00:36:06.998 --> 00:36:09.540
Well that's where we get this
term you might have heard, too.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:14.671
An algorithm, which is just step-by-step
instructions for solving some problem

00:36:14.671 --> 00:36:17.941
incarnated in the world
of computers by software.

00:36:17.941 --> 00:36:20.911
When you write software
aka programs, you

00:36:20.911 --> 00:36:26.100
are implementing one or more algorithms,
one or more sets of instructions

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.381
for solving some problem, and maybe
you're using this language or that,

00:36:29.381 --> 00:36:31.423
but at the end of the day,
no matter the language

00:36:31.423 --> 00:36:34.411
you use the computer is going
to represent what you type

00:36:34.411 --> 00:36:37.770
using just zeros and ones.

00:36:37.770 --> 00:36:40.261
So what might be a
representative algorithm?

00:36:40.261 --> 00:36:42.631
Nowadays you might use
your phone quite a bit

00:36:42.631 --> 00:36:45.481
to make calls or send texts
or emails and therefore you

00:36:45.481 --> 00:36:48.001
have a whole bunch of
contacts in your address book.

00:36:48.001 --> 00:36:50.251
Nowadays, of course,
this is very digital,

00:36:50.251 --> 00:36:53.341
but whether on iOS or
Android or the like,

00:36:53.341 --> 00:36:56.011
you might have a whole
bunch of names, first name

00:36:56.011 --> 00:36:58.943
and/or last, as well as numbers
and emails and the like.

00:36:58.943 --> 00:37:01.651
You might be in the habit of like
scrolling through on your phone

00:37:01.651 --> 00:37:05.191
all of those names to find
the person you want to call.

00:37:05.191 --> 00:37:09.001
It's probably sorted alphabetically by
first name or last name, A through Z,

00:37:09.001 --> 00:37:11.431
or some other symbol.

00:37:11.431 --> 00:37:16.764
This is frankly quite the same as
we used to do back in my day, CS50,

00:37:16.764 --> 00:37:18.181
when we just used a physical book.

00:37:18.181 --> 00:37:20.014
In this physical book
might be a whole bunch

00:37:20.014 --> 00:37:22.171
of names alphabetically
sorted from left to right

00:37:22.171 --> 00:37:24.341
corresponding to a
whole bunch of numbers.

00:37:24.341 --> 00:37:27.001
So suppose that in this
old Harvard phone book

00:37:27.001 --> 00:37:29.161
we want to search for John Harvard.

00:37:29.161 --> 00:37:31.441
We might of course start
quite simply at the beginning

00:37:31.441 --> 00:37:36.071
here, looking at one page at a
time, and this is an algorithm.

00:37:36.071 --> 00:37:40.321
This is like literally step-by-step
looking for the solution

00:37:40.321 --> 00:37:41.491
to this problem.

00:37:41.491 --> 00:37:43.711
In that sense, if John
Harvard's in the phone book,

00:37:43.711 --> 00:37:47.664
is this algorithm page-by-page
correct, would you say?

00:37:47.664 --> 00:37:48.413
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:37:48.413 --> 00:37:49.121
DAVID MALAN: Yes.

00:37:49.121 --> 00:37:51.041
Like if John Harvard's
in the phone book,

00:37:51.041 --> 00:37:54.641
obviously I'm eventually going to get to
him, so that's what we mean by correct.

00:37:54.641 --> 00:37:56.141
Is it efficient?

00:37:56.141 --> 00:37:58.341
Is it well designed, would you say?

00:37:58.341 --> 00:37:58.841
No.

00:37:58.841 --> 00:38:01.991
I mean this is going to take forever
even just to get to the Js or the Hs,

00:38:01.991 --> 00:38:03.191
depending how this thing's sorted.

00:38:03.191 --> 00:38:04.931
All right, well let
me go a little faster.

00:38:04.931 --> 00:38:06.431
I'll start like two pages at a time.

00:38:06.431 --> 00:38:11.051
2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, and so forth.

00:38:11.051 --> 00:38:13.721
Sounds faster, is faster, is it correct?

00:38:13.721 --> 00:38:14.411
AUDIENCE: No.

00:38:14.411 --> 00:38:16.421
DAVID MALAN: OK, why is it not correct?

00:38:16.421 --> 00:38:17.236
Yeah?

00:38:17.236 --> 00:38:19.044
AUDIENCE: So if you're
starting on page 1,

00:38:19.044 --> 00:38:22.086
you're only going odd number of pages,
so if it's on an even number page,

00:38:22.086 --> 00:38:22.756
you'll miss it.

00:38:22.756 --> 00:38:23.631
DAVID MALAN: Exactly.

00:38:23.631 --> 00:38:26.298
If I start on an odd number of
pages and I'm going two at a time

00:38:26.298 --> 00:38:28.231
I might miss pages in between.

00:38:28.231 --> 00:38:30.493
And if I therefore conclude
when I get to the back

00:38:30.493 --> 00:38:33.201
of the book there was no John
Harvard, I might have just errored.

00:38:33.201 --> 00:38:35.451
This would be again one of these bugs.

00:38:35.451 --> 00:38:39.261
But if I try a little harder,
I feel like there's a solution.

00:38:39.261 --> 00:38:41.551
We don't have to completely
throw out this algorithm.

00:38:41.551 --> 00:38:44.241
I think we can probably go
roughly twice as fast still.

00:38:44.241 --> 00:38:47.001
But what should we do
instead to fix this?

00:38:47.001 --> 00:38:48.803
Yeah, in back.

00:38:48.803 --> 00:38:52.275
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:58.861 --> 00:38:59.611
DAVID MALAN: Nice.

00:38:59.611 --> 00:39:02.986
So I think what many of us, most of us,
if we even use this technology any more

00:39:02.986 --> 00:39:05.611
these days, we might go roughly
to the middle of the phone book

00:39:05.611 --> 00:39:06.903
just to kind of get us started.

00:39:06.903 --> 00:39:10.351
And now I'm looking down, I'm looking
for J, assuming first name, J Harvard,

00:39:10.351 --> 00:39:13.061
and it looks like I'm in the M section.

00:39:13.061 --> 00:39:15.759
So just to be clear,
what should I do next?

00:39:15.759 --> 00:39:19.663
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:21.658 --> 00:39:23.741
DAVID MALAN: OK, and
presumably it is John Harvard

00:39:23.741 --> 00:39:24.949
would be to the left of this.

00:39:24.949 --> 00:39:27.931
So here's an opportunity to
figuratively and literally tear

00:39:27.931 --> 00:39:32.461
this particular problem in half,
throw half of the problem away.

00:39:32.461 --> 00:39:34.981
It's actually pretty easy
if you just do it that way.

00:39:34.981 --> 00:39:36.791
The hard way is this way.

00:39:36.791 --> 00:39:41.141
But I've now just decreased the
size of this problem really in half.

00:39:41.141 --> 00:39:45.241
So if I started with 1,000 pages
of phone numbers and names, now

00:39:45.241 --> 00:39:46.591
I'm down to 500.

00:39:46.591 --> 00:39:48.601
And already we haven't
found John Harvard,

00:39:48.601 --> 00:39:50.751
but that's a big bite
out of this problem.

00:39:50.751 --> 00:39:54.339
I do think it's correct because if
J is to the left of M, of course,

00:39:54.339 --> 00:39:56.131
he's definitely not
going to be over there.

00:39:56.131 --> 00:40:00.151
I think if I repeat this again
dividing and conquering, if you will,

00:40:00.151 --> 00:40:02.071
here I might have gone a little too far.

00:40:02.071 --> 00:40:03.581
Now I'm in like the E section.

00:40:03.581 --> 00:40:08.131
So let me tear the problem in half
again, throw another 250 pages away,

00:40:08.131 --> 00:40:11.101
and again repeat, dividing
and dividing and conquering

00:40:11.101 --> 00:40:14.551
until finally, presumably, I end
up with just one page of a phone

00:40:14.551 --> 00:40:18.091
book on which John Harvard's
name either is or is not,

00:40:18.091 --> 00:40:21.401
but because of the algorithm
you proposed, step by step,

00:40:21.401 --> 00:40:24.551
I know that he's not in
anything I discarded.

00:40:24.551 --> 00:40:28.201
So traumatic is that
might have been made out

00:40:28.201 --> 00:40:31.716
to be, it's actually just harnessing
pretty good human intuition.

00:40:31.716 --> 00:40:33.841
Indeed, this is what
programming is all about, too.

00:40:33.841 --> 00:40:36.691
It's not about learning
a completely new world,

00:40:36.691 --> 00:40:40.351
but really just how to harness intuition
and ideas that you might already

00:40:40.351 --> 00:40:43.381
have and take naturally
but learning how to express

00:40:43.381 --> 00:40:45.901
them now more succinctly,
more precisely,

00:40:45.901 --> 00:40:49.151
using things called
programming languages.

00:40:49.151 --> 00:40:54.331
Why is an algorithm like that if I found
John Harvard better than, ultimately,

00:40:54.331 --> 00:40:56.821
just doing the first
one or even the second

00:40:56.821 --> 00:40:59.941
and maybe doubling back
to check those even pages?

00:40:59.941 --> 00:41:01.831
Well let's just look
at little charts here.

00:41:01.831 --> 00:41:04.291
Again, we don't have to get
into the nuances of numbers,

00:41:04.291 --> 00:41:07.501
but if we've got like a chart
here, xy plot, on the x-axis

00:41:07.501 --> 00:41:09.431
here I claim as the size of the problem.

00:41:09.431 --> 00:41:12.441
So measured in the numbers
of pages in the phone book.

00:41:12.441 --> 00:41:16.051
So the farther you go out here, the
more pages are in the phone book.

00:41:16.051 --> 00:41:18.641
And here we have time
to solve on the y-axis.

00:41:18.641 --> 00:41:21.331
So the higher you go
up, the more time it's

00:41:21.331 --> 00:41:24.101
going to be taking to solve
that particular problem.

00:41:24.101 --> 00:41:27.751
So let's just arbitrarily say that
the first algorithm, involving

00:41:27.751 --> 00:41:31.511
like n pages, might be
represented graphically like this.

00:41:31.511 --> 00:41:33.931
No matter the slope,
it's a straight line

00:41:33.931 --> 00:41:36.361
because there's presumably
a one to one relationship

00:41:36.361 --> 00:41:40.441
between numbers of pages and number
of seconds or number of page turns.

00:41:40.441 --> 00:41:40.951
Why?

00:41:40.951 --> 00:41:43.291
If the phone company adds
another page next year

00:41:43.291 --> 00:41:45.301
because some new people
move to town, that's

00:41:45.301 --> 00:41:47.161
going to require one
additional page for me.

00:41:47.161 --> 00:41:49.111
One to one.

00:41:49.111 --> 00:41:52.801
If, though, we use the second
algorithm, flawed though it was,

00:41:52.801 --> 00:41:56.431
unless we double back a little bit
to fix someone being in between,

00:41:56.431 --> 00:41:59.281
that's too going to be a
straight line, but it's

00:41:59.281 --> 00:42:02.821
going to be a different slope because
now there's a 2 to 1 or a 1 to 2

00:42:02.821 --> 00:42:06.251
relationship because I'm
going to pages at a time.

00:42:06.251 --> 00:42:10.981
So if the phone company adds
another page or another two pages,

00:42:10.981 --> 00:42:13.008
that still only just one more step.

00:42:13.008 --> 00:42:15.091
You can see the difference
if I kind of draw this.

00:42:15.091 --> 00:42:18.211
If this is the phone book in
question, this number of pages,

00:42:18.211 --> 00:42:20.911
it might take this many
seconds on the yellow line

00:42:20.911 --> 00:42:24.661
to represent or to find
someone like John Harvard.

00:42:24.661 --> 00:42:27.421
But of course on the first
algorithm, the red line,

00:42:27.421 --> 00:42:29.761
it's literally going to
take twice as many steps.

00:42:29.761 --> 00:42:32.311
And what do the n here mean?
n is the go-to variable

00:42:32.311 --> 00:42:36.251
for computer scientist or programmer
just generically representing a number.

00:42:36.251 --> 00:42:38.671
So if the number of pages
in the phone book is n,

00:42:38.671 --> 00:42:41.581
the number of steps the second
algorithm would have taken

00:42:41.581 --> 00:42:44.266
would be in the worst case n over 2.

00:42:44.266 --> 00:42:46.271
Half as many because
you're going twice as fast.

00:42:46.271 --> 00:42:50.611
But the third algorithm, actually
if you recall your logarithms,

00:42:50.611 --> 00:42:52.321
looks a little something like this.

00:42:52.321 --> 00:42:54.751
There's a fundamentally
different relationship

00:42:54.751 --> 00:42:58.261
between the size of the problem and
the amount of time required to solve it

00:42:58.261 --> 00:43:00.151
that technically is
log-based, too, again,

00:43:00.151 --> 00:43:02.911
but it's really the
shape that's different.

00:43:02.911 --> 00:43:06.331
The implication there is that if,
for instance, Cambridge and Allston,

00:43:06.331 --> 00:43:08.911
two different towns here in
Massachusetts, merge next year

00:43:08.911 --> 00:43:11.971
and there's just one phone
book that's twice as big,

00:43:11.971 --> 00:43:14.671
no big deal for that
third and final algorithm.

00:43:14.671 --> 00:43:15.181
Why?

00:43:15.181 --> 00:43:17.941
You just tear the problem
one more time in half,

00:43:17.941 --> 00:43:21.361
taking one more byte,
that's it, not another 1,000

00:43:21.361 --> 00:43:23.761
bytes just to get to the solution.

00:43:23.761 --> 00:43:26.321
Put another way, you
can walk it way, way,

00:43:26.321 --> 00:43:29.521
way out here to a much bigger
phone book and ultimately

00:43:29.521 --> 00:43:32.551
that green line is barely
going to have budged.

00:43:32.551 --> 00:43:35.881
So this then is just a way of
now formalizing and thinking

00:43:35.881 --> 00:43:42.401
about what the performance or
quality of these algorithms might be.

00:43:42.401 --> 00:43:46.071
Before we now make one more
formalization of the algorithm itself,

00:43:46.071 --> 00:43:52.121
any questions then on this notion of
efficiency or now performance of ideas?

00:43:52.121 --> 00:43:52.621
Yeah.

00:43:52.621 --> 00:43:56.193
AUDIENCE: How many phone
books have you got?

00:43:56.193 --> 00:43:58.651
DAVID MALAN: (LAUGHING) A lot
of phone books over the years

00:43:58.651 --> 00:44:02.041
and if you or your parents have any
more still somewhere we could definitely

00:44:02.041 --> 00:44:04.501
use them because they're hard to find.

00:44:04.501 --> 00:44:05.641
Other questions?

00:44:05.641 --> 00:44:07.571
But thanks.

00:44:07.571 --> 00:44:09.771
Other questions here, too?

00:44:09.771 --> 00:44:10.271
No.

00:44:10.271 --> 00:44:11.981
Oh, was that a murmur?

00:44:11.981 --> 00:44:13.008
Yes, over here.

00:44:13.008 --> 00:44:15.341
AUDIENCE: You could get Harry
Potter as a guest speaker.

00:44:15.341 --> 00:44:16.474
DAVID MALAN: Sorry, say again.

00:44:16.474 --> 00:44:18.781
AUDIENCE: You could get Harry
Potter as a guest speaker.

00:44:18.781 --> 00:44:19.801
DAVID MALAN: (LAUGHING) Oh, yeah.

00:44:19.801 --> 00:44:20.301
Hopefully.

00:44:20.301 --> 00:44:23.381
Then we'd have a little
something more to use here.

00:44:23.381 --> 00:44:28.741
So now if we want to formalize
further what it is we just did,

00:44:28.741 --> 00:44:30.361
we can go ahead and introduce this.

00:44:30.361 --> 00:44:33.328
A form of code aka pseudocode.

00:44:33.328 --> 00:44:35.911
Pseudocode is not a specific
language, it's not like something

00:44:35.911 --> 00:44:38.851
we're about to start coding in, it's
just a way of expressing yourself

00:44:38.851 --> 00:44:41.371
in English or any human
language succinctly

00:44:41.371 --> 00:44:45.331
correctly toward an end of getting
your idea for an algorithm across.

00:44:45.331 --> 00:44:47.701
So for instance, here
might be how we could

00:44:47.701 --> 00:44:51.331
formalize the code, the pseudocode
for that same algorithm.

00:44:51.331 --> 00:44:54.211
Step one was pick up the
phone book, as I did.

00:44:54.211 --> 00:44:56.941
Step two might be open to
the middle of the phone book,

00:44:56.941 --> 00:44:58.741
as you proposed that we do first.

00:44:58.741 --> 00:45:01.921
Step three was probably to
look down at the pages, I did.

00:45:01.921 --> 00:45:04.531
And step four gets a
little more interesting

00:45:04.531 --> 00:45:07.771
because I had to quickly make a
decision and ask myself a question.

00:45:07.771 --> 00:45:11.521
If person is on page, then
I should probably just

00:45:11.521 --> 00:45:13.251
go ahead and call that person.

00:45:13.251 --> 00:45:15.751
But that probably wasn't the
case at least for John Harvard,

00:45:15.751 --> 00:45:17.851
and I opened the M section.

00:45:17.851 --> 00:45:19.951
So there's this other
question I should now

00:45:19.951 --> 00:45:22.591
ask else if the person
is earlier in the book,

00:45:22.591 --> 00:45:26.011
then I should tear the problem
in half as I did but go left, so

00:45:26.011 --> 00:45:30.121
to speak, and then not just open to the
middle of the left half of the book,

00:45:30.121 --> 00:45:33.301
but really just go back to
step three, repeat myself.

00:45:33.301 --> 00:45:33.901
Why?

00:45:33.901 --> 00:45:37.651
Because I can just repeat what I
just did, but with a smaller problem

00:45:37.651 --> 00:45:39.011
having taken this big bite.

00:45:39.011 --> 00:45:41.753
But, if the person
was later in the book,

00:45:41.753 --> 00:45:44.461
as might have happened with a
different person than John Harvard,

00:45:44.461 --> 00:45:47.086
then I should open to the middle
of the right half of the book,

00:45:47.086 --> 00:45:49.076
again go back to line
three, but again, I'm

00:45:49.076 --> 00:45:51.451
not going to get sucked doing
something forever like this

00:45:51.451 --> 00:45:54.521
because I keep shrinking
the size of the problem.

00:45:54.521 --> 00:45:56.431
Lastly, the only
possible scenario that's

00:45:56.431 --> 00:45:59.906
left, if John Harvard is not on
the page and he's not to the left

00:45:59.906 --> 00:46:02.281
and he's not to the right,
what should our conclusion be?

00:46:02.281 --> 00:46:03.323
AUDIENCE: He's not there.

00:46:03.323 --> 00:46:04.489
DAVID MALAN: He's not there.

00:46:04.489 --> 00:46:05.391
He's not listed.

00:46:05.391 --> 00:46:08.241
So we need to quit in some other form.

00:46:08.241 --> 00:46:11.751
Now as an aside, it's kind of deliberate
that I buried that last question

00:46:11.751 --> 00:46:16.021
at the end because this is what
happens all too often in programming,

00:46:16.021 --> 00:46:17.991
whether you're new at
it or professional,

00:46:17.991 --> 00:46:22.201
just not considering all possible
cases, corner cases if you will,

00:46:22.201 --> 00:46:25.701
that might not happen that often,
but if you don't anticipate them

00:46:25.701 --> 00:46:28.551
in your own code,
pseudocode or otherwise,

00:46:28.551 --> 00:46:31.101
this is when and why
programs might crash

00:46:31.101 --> 00:46:34.111
or you might say stupid little
spinning beach balls or hourglasses

00:46:34.111 --> 00:46:35.361
or your computer might reboot.

00:46:35.361 --> 00:46:35.931
Why?

00:46:35.931 --> 00:46:38.781
It's doing something
sort of unpredictable

00:46:38.781 --> 00:46:42.411
if a human, maybe myself,
didn't anticipate this.

00:46:42.411 --> 00:46:45.501
Like what does this program do if
John Harvard is not in the phone book

00:46:45.501 --> 00:46:48.109
if I had omitted lines 12 and 13?

00:46:48.109 --> 00:46:48.651
I don't know.

00:46:48.651 --> 00:46:50.651
Maybe it would behave
differently on a Mac or PC

00:46:50.651 --> 00:46:53.331
because it's sort of undefined behavior.

00:46:53.331 --> 00:46:56.511
These are the kinds of omissions
that frankly you're invariably

00:46:56.511 --> 00:46:58.641
going to make, bugs
you're going to introduce,

00:46:58.641 --> 00:47:02.751
mistakes you're going to make early
on, and me, too, 25 years later.

00:47:02.751 --> 00:47:06.111
But you'll get better at
thinking about those corner cases

00:47:06.111 --> 00:47:09.031
and handling anything that can
possibly go wrong and as a result,

00:47:09.031 --> 00:47:11.931
your code will be all the better for it.

00:47:11.931 --> 00:47:15.263
Now the problem ultimately
with learning how to program,

00:47:15.263 --> 00:47:16.971
especially if you've
never had experience

00:47:16.971 --> 00:47:21.411
or even if you do but you
learned one language only,

00:47:21.411 --> 00:47:25.131
is that they all look a little
cryptic at first glance.

00:47:25.131 --> 00:47:27.261
But they do share certain commonalities.

00:47:27.261 --> 00:47:30.141
In fact, we'll use this
pseudocode to define those first.

00:47:30.141 --> 00:47:32.361
Highlighted in yellow
here are what henceforth

00:47:32.361 --> 00:47:34.521
we're going to start calling functions.

00:47:34.521 --> 00:47:37.641
Lots of different programming
languages exist, but most of them

00:47:37.641 --> 00:47:40.821
have what we might call
functions, which are actions

00:47:40.821 --> 00:47:43.798
or verbs that solve
some smaller problem.

00:47:43.798 --> 00:47:46.131
That is to say, you might use
a whole bunch of functions

00:47:46.131 --> 00:47:50.241
to solve a bigger problem
because each function tends to do

00:47:50.241 --> 00:47:52.851
something very specific or precise.

00:47:52.851 --> 00:47:57.141
These then in English might be
translated in code, actual computer

00:47:57.141 --> 00:47:59.361
code, to these things called functions.

00:47:59.361 --> 00:48:02.931
Highlighted in yellow now are
what we might call conditionals.

00:48:02.931 --> 00:48:05.751
Conditionals are things
that you do conditionally

00:48:05.751 --> 00:48:07.443
based on the answer to some question.

00:48:07.443 --> 00:48:09.651
You can think of them kind
of like forks in the road.

00:48:09.651 --> 00:48:12.351
Do you go left or go right
or some other direction

00:48:12.351 --> 00:48:14.809
based on the answer to some question?

00:48:14.809 --> 00:48:16.101
Well, what are those questions?

00:48:16.101 --> 00:48:20.181
Highlighted now in yellow or what we
would call Boolean expressions, named

00:48:20.181 --> 00:48:25.071
after a mathematician last name Bool,
that simply have yes no answers.

00:48:25.071 --> 00:48:31.131
Or, if you prefer, true or false answers
or, heck, if you prefer 1 or 0 answers.

00:48:31.131 --> 00:48:34.311
We just need to distinguish
one scenario from another.

00:48:34.311 --> 00:48:37.101
The last thing manifests
in this pseudocode

00:48:37.101 --> 00:48:39.741
is what I might highlight
now and call loops.

00:48:39.741 --> 00:48:43.521
Some kind of cycle, some kind of
directive that tells us to do something

00:48:43.521 --> 00:48:48.861
again and again so that I don't
need a 1,000-line program to search

00:48:48.861 --> 00:48:53.031
a 1,000-page phone book, I can get
away with a 13-line program but sort

00:48:53.031 --> 00:48:58.161
of repeat myself inherently in order to
solve some problem until I get to that

00:48:58.161 --> 00:48:59.221
last step.

00:48:59.221 --> 00:49:01.581
So this then is what we
might call pseudocode

00:49:01.581 --> 00:49:05.151
and indeed there are
other characteristics

00:49:05.151 --> 00:49:08.541
of programs that we'll touch on before
long, things like arguments and return

00:49:08.541 --> 00:49:13.741
values, variables, and more, but
unfortunately in most languages,

00:49:13.741 --> 00:49:16.371
including some we will very
deliberately use in this class

00:49:16.371 --> 00:49:19.761
and that everyone in the real
world these days still uses,

00:49:19.761 --> 00:49:22.201
its programs tend to look like this.

00:49:22.201 --> 00:49:24.861
This for instance, is a distillation
of that very first program

00:49:24.861 --> 00:49:29.811
I wrote in 1996 in CS50 itself just
to print something on the screen.

00:49:29.811 --> 00:49:34.731
In fact, this version here just tries
to print quote unquote, "Hello, world."

00:49:34.731 --> 00:49:37.731
Which is, dare say, the most
canonical first thing that most

00:49:37.731 --> 00:49:41.601
any programmer ever gets a
computer to say just because,

00:49:41.601 --> 00:49:42.901
but look at this mess.

00:49:42.901 --> 00:49:45.921
I mean, there's a hash symbol,
these angled brackets, parentheses,

00:49:45.921 --> 00:49:49.761
words like int, curly braces, quotes,
parentheses, semicolons, and back

00:49:49.761 --> 00:49:50.421
slashes.

00:49:50.421 --> 00:49:54.021
I mean there's more overhead
and more syntax and clutter

00:49:54.021 --> 00:49:55.941
than there is an actual idea.

00:49:55.941 --> 00:50:00.081
Now that's not to say that you won't
be able to understand this before long,

00:50:00.081 --> 00:50:03.441
because honestly there's not that many
patterns, indeed programming languages

00:50:03.441 --> 00:50:07.701
have typically a much smaller vocabulary
than any actual human language,

00:50:07.701 --> 00:50:10.581
but at first it might
indeed look quite cryptic.

00:50:10.581 --> 00:50:14.361
But you can perhaps infer I have no
idea what these other lines do yet,

00:50:14.361 --> 00:50:18.171
but "Hello, world." is
presumably quote unquote what

00:50:18.171 --> 00:50:19.861
will be printed on the screen.

00:50:19.861 --> 00:50:22.881
But what we'll do today,
after a short break,

00:50:22.881 --> 00:50:24.981
and set the stage for
next week is introduce

00:50:24.981 --> 00:50:27.336
these exact same ideas in
just a bit using Scratch,

00:50:27.336 --> 00:50:29.211
something that you
yourselves might have used

00:50:29.211 --> 00:50:32.631
when you're quite younger but
without the same vocabulary applied

00:50:32.631 --> 00:50:34.041
to those ideas.

00:50:34.041 --> 00:50:38.421
The upside of what we'll soon do using
Scratch, this graphical programming

00:50:38.421 --> 00:50:41.961
language from our friends down the
road at MIT, it'll let us today

00:50:41.961 --> 00:50:46.491
start to drag and drop things that
look like puzzle pieces that interlock

00:50:46.491 --> 00:50:48.471
together if it makes
logical sense to do so,

00:50:48.471 --> 00:50:51.711
but without the distraction
of hashes, parentheses,

00:50:51.711 --> 00:50:54.411
curly braces, angle brackets,
semicolons, and things

00:50:54.411 --> 00:50:56.311
that are quite beside the point.

00:50:56.311 --> 00:50:58.801
But for now, let's go ahead
and take a 10 minute break here

00:50:58.801 --> 00:51:01.101
and when we resume, we
will start programming.

00:51:01.101 --> 00:51:04.561
So this on the screen
is a language called

00:51:04.561 --> 00:51:07.921
C something that will dive
into next week and thankfully

00:51:07.921 --> 00:51:10.981
this now on the screen is
another language called Python

00:51:10.981 --> 00:51:13.921
that we'll also take a look at
in a few weeks before long along

00:51:13.921 --> 00:51:15.691
with other languages along the way.

00:51:15.691 --> 00:51:19.641
Today though, and for this first
week, week zero, so to speak,

00:51:19.641 --> 00:51:21.391
we use Scratch because
again it will allow

00:51:21.391 --> 00:51:23.971
us to explore some of those
programming fundamentals

00:51:23.971 --> 00:51:28.471
that will be in C and in Python and in
JavaScript and other languages, too,

00:51:28.471 --> 00:51:32.521
but in a way where we don't have to
worry about the distractions of syntax.

00:51:32.521 --> 00:51:35.351
So the world of Scratch looks like this.

00:51:35.351 --> 00:51:38.071
It's a web-based or downloadable
programming environment

00:51:38.071 --> 00:51:40.921
that has this layout here
by default. On the left here

00:51:40.921 --> 00:51:45.001
we'll soon see is a palette of puzzle
pieces, programming blocks that

00:51:45.001 --> 00:51:47.521
represent all of those
ideas we just discussed.

00:51:47.521 --> 00:51:50.071
And by dragging and
dropping these puzzle pieces

00:51:50.071 --> 00:51:54.181
or blocks over this big area
and connecting them together,

00:51:54.181 --> 00:51:56.251
if it makes logical
sense to do so, we'll

00:51:56.251 --> 00:51:58.531
start programming in this environment.

00:51:58.531 --> 00:52:01.561
The environment allows you to have
multiple sprites, so to speak.

00:52:01.561 --> 00:52:04.111
Multiple characters, things
like a cat or anything

00:52:04.111 --> 00:52:08.311
else, and those sprites exist
in this rectangular world

00:52:08.311 --> 00:52:11.521
up here that you can full screen to
make bigger and this here by default

00:52:11.521 --> 00:52:16.201
is Scratch, who can move up, down, left,
right and do many more things, too.

00:52:16.201 --> 00:52:19.291
Within its Scratch's
world you can think of it

00:52:19.291 --> 00:52:23.251
as perhaps a familiar
coordinate system with Xs and Ys

00:52:23.251 --> 00:52:27.001
which is helpful only when it comes
time to position things on the screen.

00:52:27.001 --> 00:52:32.281
Right now Scratch is at the default,
0,0, where x equals 0 and y equals 0.

00:52:32.281 --> 00:52:36.061
If you were to move the cat way
up to the top, x would stay zero,

00:52:36.061 --> 00:52:37.891
y would be positive 180.

00:52:37.891 --> 00:52:40.651
If you move the cat all the way
to the bottom, x would stay zero,

00:52:40.651 --> 00:52:42.941
but y would now be negative 180.

00:52:42.941 --> 00:52:47.461
And if you went left, x would become
negative 240 but y would stay 0,

00:52:47.461 --> 00:52:51.691
or to the right x would be
240 and y would stay zero.

00:52:51.691 --> 00:52:55.591
So those numbers generally don't
so much matter because you can just

00:52:55.591 --> 00:52:58.171
move relatively in this
world up, down, left, right,

00:52:58.171 --> 00:53:01.231
but when it comes time
to precisely position

00:53:01.231 --> 00:53:03.601
some of these sprites
or other imagery, it'll

00:53:03.601 --> 00:53:07.051
be helpful just to have that mental
model off up, down, left, and right.

00:53:07.051 --> 00:53:10.151
Well let's go ahead and make perhaps
the simplest of programs here.

00:53:10.151 --> 00:53:13.051
I'm going to switch over to the
same programming environment

00:53:13.051 --> 00:53:15.971
now for a tour of the left hand side.

00:53:15.971 --> 00:53:20.911
So by default selected here are
the category in blue motion,

00:53:20.911 --> 00:53:24.451
which has a whole bunch of puzzle
pieces or blocks that relate to motion.

00:53:24.451 --> 00:53:27.001
And whereas Scratch as
a graphical language

00:53:27.001 --> 00:53:30.688
categorizes things by the type
of things that these pieces do,

00:53:30.688 --> 00:53:32.521
we'll see that throughout
this whole palette

00:53:32.521 --> 00:53:35.461
we'll have functions and
variables and conditionals

00:53:35.461 --> 00:53:38.651
and Boolean expressions and more
each in a different color and shape.

00:53:38.651 --> 00:53:42.181
So for instance, moving 10 steps
or turning one way or the other

00:53:42.181 --> 00:53:45.691
would be functions categorized
here as things like motion.

00:53:45.691 --> 00:53:49.258
Under looks in purple, you
might have speech bubbles

00:53:49.258 --> 00:53:51.091
that you can create by
dragging and dropping

00:53:51.091 --> 00:53:54.361
these that might say "hello" or
whatever for some number of seconds.

00:53:54.361 --> 00:53:58.321
Or you could switch costumes, change
the cat to look like a dog or a bird

00:53:58.321 --> 00:54:00.481
or anything else in between.

00:54:00.481 --> 00:54:01.321
Sounds, too.

00:54:01.321 --> 00:54:05.011
You can play sounds like "meow" or
anything you might import or record,

00:54:05.011 --> 00:54:06.001
yourself.

00:54:06.001 --> 00:54:09.271
Then there's these things Scratch calls
events and the most important of these

00:54:09.271 --> 00:54:11.191
is the first, when green flag clicked.

00:54:11.191 --> 00:54:14.221
Because if we look over to the
right of Scratch's world here,

00:54:14.221 --> 00:54:17.671
this rectangular region has
this green flag and red stop

00:54:17.671 --> 00:54:20.881
sign up above, one of which is
for Play one of which is for Stop

00:54:20.881 --> 00:54:24.511
and so that's going to allow us to
start and stop our actual programs

00:54:24.511 --> 00:54:27.841
when that green flag
is initially clicked.

00:54:27.841 --> 00:54:31.861
But you can listen for other types of
events when the spacebar is pressed

00:54:31.861 --> 00:54:35.911
or something else, when this sprite
is clicked or something else.

00:54:35.911 --> 00:54:39.571
Here you already see like a
programmer's incarnation of things

00:54:39.571 --> 00:54:42.451
you and I take for granted like
every day now on our phones.

00:54:42.451 --> 00:54:46.391
Any time you tap an icon or drag your
finger or hit a button on the side.

00:54:46.391 --> 00:54:48.551
These are what a programmer
would call events,

00:54:48.551 --> 00:54:51.451
things that happen and
are often triggered by us

00:54:51.451 --> 00:54:55.471
humans and things that a program
be it in Scratch or Python

00:54:55.471 --> 00:54:59.281
or C or anything else can
listen for and respond to.

00:54:59.281 --> 00:55:01.981
Indeed, that's why when you tap
the phone icon on your phone,

00:55:01.981 --> 00:55:04.441
the phone application
starts up because someone

00:55:04.441 --> 00:55:08.641
wrote software that's listening for a
finger press on that particular icon.

00:55:08.641 --> 00:55:10.711
So Scratch has these same things, too.

00:55:10.711 --> 00:55:13.591
Under Control in orange,
you can see that we

00:55:13.591 --> 00:55:15.721
can wait for one second
or repeat something

00:55:15.721 --> 00:55:17.671
some number of times,
10 by default, but we

00:55:17.671 --> 00:55:20.801
can change anything in these
white circles to anything else.

00:55:20.801 --> 00:55:22.711
There's another puzzle
piece here forever,

00:55:22.711 --> 00:55:25.591
which implies some kind of loop where
we can do something again and again.

00:55:25.591 --> 00:55:27.061
Even though it seems a
little tight, there's

00:55:27.061 --> 00:55:29.131
not much room to fit
something there, Scratch

00:55:29.131 --> 00:55:31.006
is going to have these
things grow and shrink

00:55:31.006 --> 00:55:33.994
however we want to fill
similarly shaped pieces.

00:55:33.994 --> 00:55:35.161
Here are those conditionals.

00:55:35.161 --> 00:55:40.061
If something is true or false,
then do this next thing.

00:55:40.061 --> 00:55:42.841
And that's how we can put in
this little trapezoid-like shape.

00:55:42.841 --> 00:55:46.771
Some form of Boolean expression, a
question with a yes/no, true/false,

00:55:46.771 --> 00:55:50.131
or one/zero answer and decide
whether to do something or not.

00:55:50.131 --> 00:55:52.441
You can combine these things, too.

00:55:52.441 --> 00:55:55.906
If something is true, do this,
else do this other thing.

00:55:55.906 --> 00:55:57.781
And you can even tuck
one inside of the other

00:55:57.781 --> 00:56:01.321
if you want to ask three
or four or more questions.

00:56:01.321 --> 00:56:03.011
Sensing, too, is going to be a thing.

00:56:03.011 --> 00:56:07.871
You can ask questions aka Boolean
expressions like is the sprite touching

00:56:07.871 --> 00:56:10.161
the mouse pointer, the
arrow on the screen?

00:56:10.161 --> 00:56:12.801
So that you can start to
interact with these programs.

00:56:12.801 --> 00:56:15.299
What is the distance between
a sprite and a mouse pointer?

00:56:15.299 --> 00:56:17.591
You can do simple calculations
just to figure out maybe

00:56:17.591 --> 00:56:20.141
if the enemy is getting
close to the cat.

00:56:20.141 --> 00:56:23.651
Under Operator some lower level
stuff like math, but also the ability

00:56:23.651 --> 00:56:25.851
to pick random numbers,
which for a game is great

00:56:25.851 --> 00:56:27.851
because then you can kind
of vary the difficulty

00:56:27.851 --> 00:56:30.309
or what's happening in a game
without the same game playing

00:56:30.309 --> 00:56:31.991
the same way every time.

00:56:31.991 --> 00:56:33.551
And you can combine ideas.

00:56:33.551 --> 00:56:37.521
Something and something must be true
in order to make that kind of decision

00:56:37.521 --> 00:56:38.021
before.

00:56:38.021 --> 00:56:40.241
Or we can even join two words together.

00:56:40.241 --> 00:56:43.181
Says apple and banana by default,
but you can type in or drag and drop

00:56:43.181 --> 00:56:46.991
whatever you want there to
combine multiple words into full,

00:56:46.991 --> 00:56:48.581
larger sentences.

00:56:48.581 --> 00:56:52.361
Then lastly down here, there's in
orange things called variables.

00:56:52.361 --> 00:56:54.911
In math we've obviously
got x and y and whatnot.

00:56:54.911 --> 00:56:56.921
In programming we'll
have the same ability

00:56:56.921 --> 00:57:03.011
to store in these named symbols,
x or y, values that we care about.

00:57:03.011 --> 00:57:06.701
Numbers or letters or words or
colors or anything, ultimately.

00:57:06.701 --> 00:57:09.971
But in programming you'll see that
it's much more conventional not to just

00:57:09.971 --> 00:57:13.391
use simple letters like x
and y and z, but to actually

00:57:13.391 --> 00:57:20.291
give variables full singular or plural
words to describe what they are.

00:57:20.291 --> 00:57:24.131
Then lastly, if this isn't
enough color blocks for you,

00:57:24.131 --> 00:57:25.961
you can create your own blocks.

00:57:25.961 --> 00:57:29.051
Indeed, this is going to be a
programming principle we'll apply today

00:57:29.051 --> 00:57:32.921
and with the first problem set whereby
once you start to assemble these puzzle

00:57:32.921 --> 00:57:37.661
pieces and you realize, oh, would have
been nice if those several pieces could

00:57:37.661 --> 00:57:40.991
have just been replaced by one
had MIT thought to give me that

00:57:40.991 --> 00:57:44.291
one puzzle piece, you yourself
can make your own blocks

00:57:44.291 --> 00:57:47.111
by connecting these all together,
giving them a name, and boom,

00:57:47.111 --> 00:57:49.251
a new puzzle piece will exist.

00:57:49.251 --> 00:57:51.581
So let's do the simplest,
most canonical programs

00:57:51.581 --> 00:57:53.531
here, starting up with
control, and I'm going

00:57:53.531 --> 00:57:57.881
to click and drag and drop this
thing here when green flag clicked.

00:57:57.881 --> 00:58:01.108
Then I'm going to grab one
more, for instance under Looks,

00:58:01.108 --> 00:58:03.191
and under Looks I'm going
to go ahead and just say

00:58:03.191 --> 00:58:07.901
something like initially not
just Hello but the more canonical

00:58:07.901 --> 00:58:09.551
Hello comma world.

00:58:09.551 --> 00:58:12.251
Now you might guess that in
this programming environment,

00:58:12.251 --> 00:58:15.611
I can go over here now and
click the green flag and voila,

00:58:15.611 --> 00:58:17.091
Hello comma world.

00:58:17.091 --> 00:58:19.006
So that's my first
program and obviously much

00:58:19.006 --> 00:58:21.881
more user friendly than typing out
the much more cryptic text that we

00:58:21.881 --> 00:58:25.041
saw on the screen that you,
too, will type out next week.

00:58:25.041 --> 00:58:28.281
But for now, we'll just focus on
these ideas, in this case, a function.

00:58:28.281 --> 00:58:29.891
So what it is that just happened?

00:58:29.891 --> 00:58:32.391
This purple block here is
Say, that's the function,

00:58:32.391 --> 00:58:37.091
and it seems to take some form of input
in the white oval, specifically Hello

00:58:37.091 --> 00:58:37.996
comma world.

00:58:37.996 --> 00:58:40.121
Well this actually fits
the paradigm that we looked

00:58:40.121 --> 00:58:42.446
at earlier of just inputs and outputs.

00:58:42.446 --> 00:58:45.341
So if I may, if you consider
what this puzzle piece is doing,

00:58:45.341 --> 00:58:47.051
it actually fits this model.

00:58:47.051 --> 00:58:51.011
The input in this case is going
to be Hello comma world in white.

00:58:51.011 --> 00:58:55.346
The algorithm is going to be implemented
as a function by MIT called Say

00:58:55.346 --> 00:58:57.971
and the output of that is going
to be some kind of side effect,

00:58:57.971 --> 00:59:01.271
like the cat and the speech
bubble are saying Hello, world.

00:59:01.271 --> 00:59:03.761
So already even that
simple drag and drop

00:59:03.761 --> 00:59:07.341
mimics exactly this relatively
simple mental model.

00:59:07.341 --> 00:59:08.751
So let's take things further.

00:59:08.751 --> 00:59:11.923
Let's go ahead now and make the
program a little more interactive so

00:59:11.923 --> 00:59:14.381
that it says something like
Hello, David, or Hello, Carter,

00:59:14.381 --> 00:59:16.601
or Hello to you specifically.

00:59:16.601 --> 00:59:18.556
And for this, I'm going
to go under Sensing.

00:59:18.556 --> 00:59:21.431
And you might have to poke around
to find these things the first time

00:59:21.431 --> 00:59:24.473
around, but I've done this a few times
so I kind of know where things are

00:59:24.473 --> 00:59:25.301
and what color.

00:59:25.301 --> 00:59:26.861
There's this function here.

00:59:26.861 --> 00:59:28.753
Ask what's your name,
but that's in white,

00:59:28.753 --> 00:59:30.461
so we can change the
question to anything

00:59:30.461 --> 00:59:34.931
we want, and it's going to wait for
the human to type in their answer.

00:59:34.931 --> 00:59:37.511
This function called Ask
is a little different

00:59:37.511 --> 00:59:41.321
from the Say block, which just had
this side effect of printing a speech

00:59:41.321 --> 00:59:42.461
bubble to the screen.

00:59:42.461 --> 00:59:47.081
The ask function is even more powerful
in that after it asks the human to type

00:59:47.081 --> 00:59:47.831
something in.

00:59:47.831 --> 00:59:50.831
This function is going
to hand you back what

00:59:50.831 --> 00:59:55.091
they typed in in the form of
what's called a return value, which

00:59:55.091 --> 00:59:57.941
is stored ultimately and by
default this thing called Answer.

00:59:57.941 --> 01:00:00.613
This little blue oval here
called Answer is again

01:00:00.613 --> 01:00:02.321
one of these variables
that in math would

01:00:02.321 --> 01:00:05.829
be called just x or y but in
programming we're saying what it does.

01:00:05.829 --> 01:00:07.371
So I'm going to go ahead and do this.

01:00:07.371 --> 01:00:09.204
Let me go ahead and
drag and drop this block

01:00:09.204 --> 01:00:11.631
and I want to ask the question
before saying anything,

01:00:11.631 --> 01:00:13.631
but you'll notice that
Scratch is smart and it's

01:00:13.631 --> 01:00:15.641
going to realize I want to
insert something in between

01:00:15.641 --> 01:00:17.599
and it's just going to
move things up and down.

01:00:17.599 --> 01:00:20.531
I'm going to let go and ask the
default question, what's your name?

01:00:20.531 --> 01:00:23.771
And now if I want to go ahead
and say hello, David or Carter,

01:00:23.771 --> 01:00:26.141
let's just do Hello
comma, because I obviously

01:00:26.141 --> 01:00:28.821
don't know when I'm writing the
program who's going to use it.

01:00:28.821 --> 01:00:35.141
So let me now grab another looks block
up here, say something again, and now

01:00:35.141 --> 01:00:39.281
let me go back to Sensing and now
grab the return value, represented

01:00:39.281 --> 01:00:42.611
by this other puzzle piece, and
let me just drag and drop it here.

01:00:42.611 --> 01:00:45.461
Notice it's the same shape, even
if it's not quite the same size.

01:00:45.461 --> 01:00:47.651
Things will grow or shrink as needed.

01:00:47.651 --> 01:00:49.721
All right, so let's now zoom out.

01:00:49.721 --> 01:00:52.631
Let me go and stop the old version
because I don't want to say Hello,

01:00:52.631 --> 01:00:53.291
world anymore.

01:00:53.291 --> 01:00:55.751
Let me hit the green
flag and what's my name?

01:00:55.751 --> 01:00:56.651
All right, David.

01:00:56.651 --> 01:00:58.403
Enter.

01:00:58.403 --> 01:00:59.339
Huh.

01:00:59.339 --> 01:01:01.881
All right, maybe I just wasn't
paying close enough attention.

01:01:01.881 --> 01:01:03.141
Let me try it again.

01:01:03.141 --> 01:01:06.361
Green flag, D-A-V-I-D, Enter.

01:01:06.361 --> 01:01:09.281
This seems like a bug.

01:01:09.281 --> 01:01:13.301
What's the bug or
mistake might you think?

01:01:13.301 --> 01:01:14.151
Yeah?

01:01:14.151 --> 01:01:17.371
AUDIENCE: Do you need to somehow add
them together in the same text box?

01:01:17.371 --> 01:01:20.761
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, we kind of want
to combine them in the same text box.

01:01:20.761 --> 01:01:23.941
And it's technically a bug because
this just looks kind of stupid.

01:01:23.941 --> 01:01:26.911
It's just saying David
after I asked for my name.

01:01:26.911 --> 01:01:29.971
I'd like it to say
maybe Hello then David,

01:01:29.971 --> 01:01:32.461
but it's just blowing past
the Hello and printing David.

01:01:32.461 --> 01:01:34.781
But let's put our finger
on why this is happening.

01:01:34.781 --> 01:01:38.191
You're right for the solution, but
what's the actual fundamental problem?

01:01:38.191 --> 01:01:39.228
In back.

01:01:39.228 --> 01:01:42.707
AUDIENCE: So it says hello,
but it gets to that last step

01:01:42.707 --> 01:01:43.926
so quickly you can't see it.

01:01:43.926 --> 01:01:44.801
DAVID MALAN: Perfect.

01:01:44.801 --> 01:01:47.441
I mean, computers are
really darn fast these days.

01:01:47.441 --> 01:01:50.351
It is saying Hello, all of us
are just too slow in this room

01:01:50.351 --> 01:01:54.741
to even see it because it's then saying
David on the screen so fast as well.

01:01:54.741 --> 01:01:57.443
So there's a couple of solutions
here, and yours is spot on,

01:01:57.443 --> 01:01:59.651
but just to poke around,
you'll see the first example

01:01:59.651 --> 01:02:03.131
of how many ways in programming be
it Scratch or C or Python or anything

01:02:03.131 --> 01:02:05.441
else, that there are going
to be to solve problems?

01:02:05.441 --> 01:02:07.421
We'll teach you over the
course of these weeks,

01:02:07.421 --> 01:02:10.661
sometimes some ways are
better relatively than others,

01:02:10.661 --> 01:02:13.811
but rarely is there a
best way necessarily,

01:02:13.811 --> 01:02:15.728
because again reasonable
people will disagree.

01:02:15.728 --> 01:02:17.936
And what we'll try to teach
you over the coming weeks

01:02:17.936 --> 01:02:20.111
is how to kind of think
through those nuances.

01:02:20.111 --> 01:02:22.258
And it's not going to be
obvious at first glance,

01:02:22.258 --> 01:02:24.341
but the more programs you
write, the more feedback

01:02:24.341 --> 01:02:26.951
you get, the more bugs
that you introduce,

01:02:26.951 --> 01:02:30.861
the more you'll get your footing with
exactly this kind of problem solving.

01:02:30.861 --> 01:02:33.131
So let me try this in a couple of ways.

01:02:33.131 --> 01:02:35.621
Up here would be one
solution to the problem.

01:02:35.621 --> 01:02:40.286
MIT anticipated this kind of issue,
especially with first-time programmers,

01:02:40.286 --> 01:02:42.161
and I could just use a
puzzle piece that says

01:02:42.161 --> 01:02:44.771
say the following for
two seconds or one second

01:02:44.771 --> 01:02:47.381
or whatever, then do the
same with the next word

01:02:47.381 --> 01:02:50.111
and it might be kind
of a bit of a pause,

01:02:50.111 --> 01:02:54.611
Hello, one second, two seconds, David,
one second, two seconds, but at least

01:02:54.611 --> 01:02:56.761
it would look a little
more grammatically correct.

01:02:56.761 --> 01:02:59.261
But I can do it a little more
elegantly, as you've proposed.

01:02:59.261 --> 01:03:01.386
Let me go ahead and throw
away one of these blocks,

01:03:01.386 --> 01:03:04.181
and you can just drag and let
go and it'll delete itself.

01:03:04.181 --> 01:03:10.211
Let me go down to Operators because
this Join block here is the right shape.

01:03:10.211 --> 01:03:13.721
So even if you're not sure what goes
where, just focus on the shapes first.

01:03:13.721 --> 01:03:15.971
Let me drag this over here.

01:03:15.971 --> 01:03:17.301
It grew to fill that.

01:03:17.301 --> 01:03:20.171
Let me go ahead and
say hello comma space.

01:03:20.171 --> 01:03:22.331
Now it could just say by
default Hello, banana,

01:03:22.331 --> 01:03:27.701
but let me go back to
Sensing, Drag answer,

01:03:27.701 --> 01:03:29.501
and that's going to drag and drop there.

01:03:29.501 --> 01:03:34.161
So now notice we're sort of stacking
or nesting one block on another

01:03:34.161 --> 01:03:38.021
so that the output of one becomes the
input to another, but that's OK here.

01:03:38.021 --> 01:03:42.024
Let me go ahead and zoom
out, hit Stop, and hit Play.

01:03:42.024 --> 01:03:43.191
All right, what's your name?

01:03:43.191 --> 01:03:45.551
D-A-V-I-D, Enter, and voila.

01:03:45.551 --> 01:03:48.771
Now it's presumably
as we first intended.

01:03:48.771 --> 01:03:49.359
[APPLAUSE]

01:03:49.359 --> 01:03:50.401
(LAUGHING) Oh, thank you.

01:03:55.401 --> 01:03:56.061
Thank you.

01:03:56.061 --> 01:03:57.811
No minus 2 this time.

01:03:57.811 --> 01:04:02.728
So consider that even with
this additional example,

01:04:02.728 --> 01:04:05.811
it still fits the same mental model,
but in a little more interesting way.

01:04:05.811 --> 01:04:08.961
Here's that new function
Ask something and wait.

01:04:08.961 --> 01:04:12.471
And notice that in this case too
there's an input, otherwise known

01:04:12.471 --> 01:04:15.621
henceforth as an argument
or a parameter, programming

01:04:15.621 --> 01:04:18.681
speak for just an input in
the context of a function.

01:04:18.681 --> 01:04:21.831
If we use our drawing as before
to represent this thing here,

01:04:21.831 --> 01:04:26.061
we'll see that the input now is going
to be quote unquote "What's your name?"

01:04:26.061 --> 01:04:30.081
The algorithm is going to be implemented
by way of this new puzzle piece,

01:04:30.081 --> 01:04:33.381
the function called Ask, and the
output of that thing this time

01:04:33.381 --> 01:04:36.111
is not going to be the
cat saying anything yet,

01:04:36.111 --> 01:04:39.721
but rather it's going
to be the actual answer.

01:04:39.721 --> 01:04:43.131
So instead of the visual side effect
of the speech bubble appearing,

01:04:43.131 --> 01:04:45.621
now nothing visible is happening yet.

01:04:45.621 --> 01:04:49.671
Thanks to this function it's sort of
handing me back like a scrap of paper

01:04:49.671 --> 01:04:55.701
with whatever I typed in written on it
so I can reuse D-A-V-I-D one or more

01:04:55.701 --> 01:04:57.951
times even like I did.

01:04:57.951 --> 01:05:00.051
Now what did I then do with that value?

01:05:00.051 --> 01:05:04.821
Well consider that with
the subsequent function

01:05:04.821 --> 01:05:08.281
we had this Say block,
too, combined with a join.

01:05:08.281 --> 01:05:11.781
So we have this variable
called Answer, we're joining it

01:05:11.781 --> 01:05:14.203
with that first argument, Hello.

01:05:14.203 --> 01:05:16.161
So already we see that
some functions like Join

01:05:16.161 --> 01:05:20.001
can take not one but two arguments,
or inputs, and that's fine.

01:05:20.001 --> 01:05:24.891
The output of Join is presumably going
to be Hello, David or Hello, Carter

01:05:24.891 --> 01:05:27.171
or whatever the human typed in.

01:05:27.171 --> 01:05:31.581
That output notice is essentially
becoming the input to another function,

01:05:31.581 --> 01:05:33.951
Say, just because we've
kind of stacked things

01:05:33.951 --> 01:05:35.851
or nested them on top of one another.

01:05:35.851 --> 01:05:40.281
But methodically, it's
really the same idea.

01:05:40.281 --> 01:05:44.901
The input now are two things,
Hello comma and the return value

01:05:44.901 --> 01:05:47.301
from the previous Ask function.

01:05:47.301 --> 01:05:51.231
The function now is going to be Join,
the output is going to be Hello, David.

01:05:51.231 --> 01:05:53.061
But that Hello, David
output is now going

01:05:53.061 --> 01:05:57.501
to become the input to another function,
namely that first block called Say,

01:05:57.501 --> 01:06:01.611
and that's then going to have the side
effect of printing out Hello, David

01:06:01.611 --> 01:06:02.671
on the screen.

01:06:02.671 --> 01:06:06.411
So again as sort of sophisticated
as ours as yours as others programs

01:06:06.411 --> 01:06:09.231
are going to get, they really do
fit this very simple mental model

01:06:09.231 --> 01:06:12.741
of inputs and outputs and you just have
to learn to recognize the vocabulary

01:06:12.741 --> 01:06:17.031
and to know what kinds of puzzle
pieces or concepts ultimately to apply.

01:06:17.031 --> 01:06:19.651
But you can ultimately really
kind of spice these things up.

01:06:19.651 --> 01:06:21.321
Let me go back to my
program here that just is

01:06:21.321 --> 01:06:22.911
using the speech bubble at the moment.

01:06:22.911 --> 01:06:26.091
Scratch's inside has some pretty
fancy interactive features, too.

01:06:26.091 --> 01:06:28.971
I click the Extensions button
in the bottom left corner.

01:06:28.971 --> 01:06:33.248
And let me go ahead and choose
the Text to Speech extension.

01:06:33.248 --> 01:06:36.081
This is using a Cloud service, so
if you have an internet connection

01:06:36.081 --> 01:06:39.141
it can actually talk to the
Cloud or a third party service,

01:06:39.141 --> 01:06:42.411
and this one is going to give me a
few new green puzzle pieces, namely

01:06:42.411 --> 01:06:45.171
the ability to speak
something from my speakers

01:06:45.171 --> 01:06:47.091
instead of just saying it textually.

01:06:47.091 --> 01:06:48.574
So let me go ahead and drag this.

01:06:48.574 --> 01:06:51.741
Now notice I don't have to interlock
them if I'm just kind of playing around

01:06:51.741 --> 01:06:52.971
and I want to move some things around.

01:06:52.971 --> 01:06:55.281
I just want to use this as
like a canvas temporarily.

01:06:55.281 --> 01:06:58.141
Let me go ahead and
steal the Join from here,

01:06:58.141 --> 01:07:01.581
put it there, let me throw away
the Say block by just moving it

01:07:01.581 --> 01:07:04.641
left and letting go, and
now let me join this in

01:07:04.641 --> 01:07:08.581
so I've now changed my program
to be a little more interesting.

01:07:08.581 --> 01:07:10.341
So now let me stop the old version.

01:07:10.341 --> 01:07:11.931
Let me start the new.

01:07:11.931 --> 01:07:12.711
What's your name?

01:07:12.711 --> 01:07:13.611
Type in David.

01:07:13.611 --> 01:07:14.721
And voila:

01:07:14.721 --> 01:07:15.696
PROGRAM: Hello, banana.

01:07:18.366 --> 01:07:20.241
DAVID MALAN: (LAUGHING)
OK, minus 2 for real.

01:07:20.241 --> 01:07:27.261
All right, so what I accidentally
threw away there, intentionally

01:07:27.261 --> 01:07:30.171
for instructional purposes,
was the actual answer

01:07:30.171 --> 01:07:32.661
that came back from the ask block.

01:07:32.661 --> 01:07:33.621
That's embarrassing.

01:07:33.621 --> 01:07:37.593
So now if I play this again,
let's click the green icon.

01:07:37.593 --> 01:07:38.301
What's your name?

01:07:38.301 --> 01:07:38.841
David.

01:07:38.841 --> 01:07:40.311
And now:

01:07:40.311 --> 01:07:41.399
PROGRAM: Hello, David.

01:07:41.399 --> 01:07:42.441
DAVID MALAN: There we go.

01:07:42.441 --> 01:07:43.821
Hello, David.

01:07:43.821 --> 01:07:45.416
All right, thank you.

01:07:45.416 --> 01:07:50.271
[APPLAUSE]

01:07:50.271 --> 01:07:54.481
OK, so we have these functions then
in place, but what more can we do?

01:07:54.481 --> 01:07:57.441
Well what about those conditionals
and loops and other constructs?

01:07:57.441 --> 01:07:59.811
How can we bring these programs
to life so it's not just

01:07:59.811 --> 01:08:01.951
clicking a button and voila,
something's happening?

01:08:01.951 --> 01:08:04.251
Let's go ahead and make this
now even more interactive.

01:08:04.251 --> 01:08:06.501
Let me go ahead and throw
away most of these pieces

01:08:06.501 --> 01:08:09.471
and let me just spice things up
with some more audio under Sound.

01:08:09.471 --> 01:08:12.171
I'm going to go to Play
Sound Meow until done.

01:08:12.171 --> 01:08:13.581
Here we go, green flag.

01:08:13.581 --> 01:08:15.251
[MEOW]

01:08:15.251 --> 01:08:18.521
OK, it's a little loud, but it
did exactly do what it said.

01:08:18.521 --> 01:08:19.895
Let's hear it again.

01:08:19.895 --> 01:08:21.291
[QUIETER MEOW]

01:08:21.291 --> 01:08:21.791
OK.

01:08:21.791 --> 01:08:23.491
It's kind of an underwhelming
program eventually

01:08:23.491 --> 01:08:26.551
since you'd like to think that the
cat would just meow on its own, but.

01:08:26.551 --> 01:08:26.941
[MEOW]

01:08:26.941 --> 01:08:28.358
I have to keep hitting the button.

01:08:28.358 --> 01:08:31.386
Well this seems like an opportunity
for doing something again and again.

01:08:31.386 --> 01:08:33.511
So all right, well if I
wanted to meow, meow, meow,

01:08:33.511 --> 01:08:37.171
let me just grab a few of these, or you
can even right click or Control click

01:08:37.171 --> 01:08:39.361
and you can Copy Paste
even in code here.

01:08:39.361 --> 01:08:41.006
Let me play this now.

01:08:41.006 --> 01:08:43.231
[THREE MEOWS]

01:08:43.231 --> 01:08:45.228
All right, so now like
it's not really emoting

01:08:45.228 --> 01:08:46.561
happiness in quite the same way.

01:08:46.561 --> 01:08:47.821
It might be hungry or upset.

01:08:47.821 --> 01:08:49.421
So let's slow it down.

01:08:49.421 --> 01:08:52.481
Let me go to Control, wait
one second in between,

01:08:52.481 --> 01:08:55.861
which might be a little less worrisome.

01:08:55.861 --> 01:08:56.881
Here we go, Play.

01:08:56.881 --> 01:09:01.451
[THREE SLOWER MEOWS]

01:09:01.451 --> 01:09:06.311
OK, so if my goal was to make
the cat meow three times,

01:09:06.311 --> 01:09:10.001
I dare say this code or
algorithm is correct.

01:09:10.001 --> 01:09:12.941
But let's now critique its design.

01:09:12.941 --> 01:09:14.681
Is this well-designed?

01:09:14.681 --> 01:09:17.781
And if not, why not?

01:09:17.781 --> 01:09:19.171
What are your thoughts here?

01:09:19.171 --> 01:09:21.501
Yeah?

01:09:21.501 --> 01:09:27.053
AUDIENCE: You could use the forever
or a repeat to make it more--

01:09:27.053 --> 01:09:28.511
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, so yeah, agreed.

01:09:28.511 --> 01:09:31.301
I could use forever or repeat,
but let me push a little harder.

01:09:31.301 --> 01:09:31.991
But why?

01:09:31.991 --> 01:09:36.071
Like this works, I'm kind of done with
the assignments, what's bad about it?

01:09:36.071 --> 01:09:37.881
AUDIENCE: There's too much repetition.

01:09:37.881 --> 01:09:40.131
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, there's
too much repetition, right?

01:09:40.131 --> 01:09:42.591
If I wanted to change the
sound that the cat is making

01:09:42.591 --> 01:09:46.041
to a different variant of meow or
have it bark instead like a dog,

01:09:46.041 --> 01:09:48.351
I could change it from the
dropdown here apparently,

01:09:48.351 --> 01:09:51.351
but then I'd have to change it here
and then I'd have to change it here,

01:09:51.351 --> 01:09:54.111
and God, if this were even longer
that just gets tedious quickly

01:09:54.111 --> 01:09:56.031
and you're probably
increasing the probability

01:09:56.031 --> 01:09:57.081
that you're going to
screw up and you're going

01:09:57.081 --> 01:10:00.072
to miss one of the dropdowns or
something stupid and introduce a bug.

01:10:00.072 --> 01:10:02.780
Or, if you wanted to change the
number of seconds you're waiting,

01:10:02.780 --> 01:10:05.151
you've got to change it in
two, maybe even more places.

01:10:05.151 --> 01:10:07.581
Again, you're just
creating risk for yourself

01:10:07.581 --> 01:10:09.151
and potential bugs in the program.

01:10:09.151 --> 01:10:13.041
So I do like the repeat or the forever
idea so that I don't repeat myself.

01:10:13.041 --> 01:10:15.801
And indeed, what I
alluded to being possible,

01:10:15.801 --> 01:10:18.651
copy pasting earlier, doesn't
mean it's a good thing.

01:10:18.651 --> 01:10:20.421
And in code, generally
speaking, when you

01:10:20.421 --> 01:10:23.841
start to copy and paste puzzle
pieces or text next week,

01:10:23.841 --> 01:10:26.910
you're probably not doing
something quite well.

01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:30.681
So let me go ahead and throw away most
of these to get rid of the duplication,

01:10:30.681 --> 01:10:33.681
keeping just two of the
blocks that I care about.

01:10:33.681 --> 01:10:37.881
Let me grab the Repeat block for now,
let me move this inside of the Repeat

01:10:37.881 --> 01:10:40.941
block, it's going to grow to fit
it, let me reconnect all this

01:10:40.941 --> 01:10:44.450
and change the 10 just
to a 3, and now, Play.

01:10:44.450 --> 01:10:49.601
[THREE SLOW MEOWS]

01:10:49.601 --> 01:10:50.660
So, better.

01:10:50.660 --> 01:10:51.621
It's the same thing.

01:10:51.621 --> 01:10:53.538
It's still correct, but
now I've set the stage

01:10:53.538 --> 01:10:57.041
to let the cat meow, for instance,
four times by changing one thing,

01:10:57.041 --> 01:11:00.431
40 times by changing one thing, or
it could just use the Forever block

01:11:00.431 --> 01:11:03.160
and just walk away and it
will meow forever instead.

01:11:03.160 --> 01:11:05.231
If that's your goal,
that would be better.

01:11:05.231 --> 01:11:07.570
A better design but still correct.

01:11:07.570 --> 01:11:08.320
But you know what?

01:11:08.320 --> 01:11:10.271
Now that I have a
program that's designed

01:11:10.271 --> 01:11:13.631
to have a cat meow, wow like why?

01:11:13.631 --> 01:11:16.360
I mean, MIT invented
Scratch, Scratch as a cat,

01:11:16.360 --> 01:11:18.371
why is there no puzzle
piece called Meow?

01:11:18.371 --> 01:11:20.081
This feels like a missed opportunity.

01:11:20.081 --> 01:11:22.751
Now to be fair, they gave
us all the building blocks

01:11:22.751 --> 01:11:26.032
with which we could implement that
idea, but a principle of programming

01:11:26.032 --> 01:11:28.240
and really computer science
is to leverage what we're

01:11:28.240 --> 01:11:30.490
going to now start calling Abstraction.

01:11:30.490 --> 01:11:34.481
We have step-by-step instructions
here, the Repeat, the Play,

01:11:34.481 --> 01:11:37.001
and the Wait that collectively
implements this idea

01:11:37.001 --> 01:11:38.771
that we humans would call meowing.

01:11:38.771 --> 01:11:41.831
Wouldn't it be nice to abstract
away those several puzzle

01:11:41.831 --> 01:11:45.701
pieces into just one that literally
just says what it does, meow?

01:11:45.701 --> 01:11:48.281
Well here's where we
can make our own blocks.

01:11:48.281 --> 01:11:52.331
Let me go over here to Scratch
under the pink block category

01:11:52.331 --> 01:11:55.933
here and let me click Make a Block.

01:11:55.933 --> 01:11:57.641
Here I see a slightly
different interface

01:11:57.641 --> 01:12:00.491
where I can choose a name for it
and I'm going to call it Meow.

01:12:00.491 --> 01:12:01.241
I'm going to keep it simple.

01:12:01.241 --> 01:12:01.741
That's it.

01:12:01.741 --> 01:12:03.281
No inputs to meow yet.

01:12:03.281 --> 01:12:05.181
I'm just going to click OK.

01:12:05.181 --> 01:12:08.201
Now I'm just going to
clean this up a bit here.

01:12:08.201 --> 01:12:12.021
Let me drag and drop Play
Sound and Wait over here.

01:12:12.021 --> 01:12:13.031
And you know what?

01:12:13.031 --> 01:12:15.851
I'm just going to drag this
way down here, way down

01:12:15.851 --> 01:12:18.191
here because now that I'm
done implementing Meow,

01:12:18.191 --> 01:12:21.051
I'm going to literally abstract
it away, sort of out of sight,

01:12:21.051 --> 01:12:25.301
out of mind, because now notice at
top left there is a new pink puzzle

01:12:25.301 --> 01:12:26.711
piece called Meow.

01:12:26.711 --> 01:12:31.541
So at this point, I'd argue it doesn't
really matter how Meow is implemented.

01:12:31.541 --> 01:12:35.021
Frankly, I don't know how Ask
or Say was implemented by MIT.

01:12:35.021 --> 01:12:37.211
They abstracted those
things away for us.

01:12:37.211 --> 01:12:40.121
Now I have a brand new puzzle
piece that just says what it is.

01:12:40.121 --> 01:12:44.441
And this is now still correct,
but arguably better design.

01:12:44.441 --> 01:12:45.041
Why?

01:12:45.041 --> 01:12:47.441
Because it's just more
readable to me, to you,

01:12:47.441 --> 01:12:49.691
it's more maintainable
when you look at your code

01:12:49.691 --> 01:12:52.241
a year from now for the first time
because you're sort of finally looking

01:12:52.241 --> 01:12:53.949
back at the very first
program you wrote.

01:12:53.949 --> 01:12:55.541
It says what it does.

01:12:55.541 --> 01:12:59.231
The function itself has semantics,
which conveys what's going on.

01:12:59.231 --> 01:13:01.961
If you really care about
how Meow is implemented,

01:13:01.961 --> 01:13:04.931
you could scroll down and start
to tinker with the underlying

01:13:04.931 --> 01:13:09.701
implementation details, but otherwise
you don't need to care anymore.

01:13:09.701 --> 01:13:13.211
Now I feel like there's an
even additional opportunity

01:13:13.211 --> 01:13:17.711
here for abstraction and to factor
out some of this functionality.

01:13:17.711 --> 01:13:21.161
It's kind of lame that I
have this Repeat block that

01:13:21.161 --> 01:13:24.511
lets me call the Meow function,
so to speak, use the Meow function

01:13:24.511 --> 01:13:25.571
three times.

01:13:25.571 --> 01:13:28.481
Wouldn't it be nice if I could
just call them Meow function,

01:13:28.481 --> 01:13:32.861
aka use the Meow function, and pass
it in input that tells the puzzle

01:13:32.861 --> 01:13:35.111
piece how many times I want it to meow?

01:13:35.111 --> 01:13:37.971
Well let me go ahead and
zoom out and scroll down.

01:13:37.971 --> 01:13:41.561
Let me right click or Control click on
the pink piece here and choose Edit,

01:13:41.561 --> 01:13:44.951
or I could just start from scratch,
no pun intended, with a new one.

01:13:44.951 --> 01:13:48.791
Now here, rather than just give this
thing a name Meow, let me go ahead

01:13:48.791 --> 01:13:50.801
and add an input here.

01:13:50.801 --> 01:13:53.081
I'm going to go ahead and
type in, for instance, n,

01:13:53.081 --> 01:13:56.141
for number of times to
meow, and just to make

01:13:56.141 --> 01:13:58.506
this even more user friendly
and self descriptive,

01:13:58.506 --> 01:14:00.881
I'm going to add a label,
which has no functional impact,

01:14:00.881 --> 01:14:03.071
it's just an aesthetic,
and I'm just going

01:14:03.071 --> 01:14:05.741
to say Times, just to make
it read more like English

01:14:05.741 --> 01:14:08.201
in this case that tells me
what the puzzle piece does.

01:14:08.201 --> 01:14:09.681
Now I'm going to click OK.

01:14:09.681 --> 01:14:12.201
And now I need to refine
this a little bit.

01:14:12.201 --> 01:14:18.481
Let me go ahead and grab
under Control a repeat block,

01:14:18.481 --> 01:14:22.181
let me move the Play, Sound,
and Wait, into the repeat block.

01:14:22.181 --> 01:14:24.721
I don't want 10 and I
also don't want 3 here.

01:14:24.721 --> 01:14:29.881
What I want now is this n that is
my actual variable that Scratch

01:14:29.881 --> 01:14:33.271
is creating for me that represents
whatever input the human programmer

01:14:33.271 --> 01:14:34.026
provides.

01:14:34.026 --> 01:14:35.401
Notice that snaps right in place.

01:14:35.401 --> 01:14:39.451
Let me connect this and now voila, I
have an even fancier version of Meow

01:14:39.451 --> 01:14:40.951
that is parameterized.

01:14:40.951 --> 01:14:44.879
It takes input that affects
its behavior accordingly.

01:14:44.879 --> 01:14:47.671
Now I'm going to scroll back up,
because out of sight, out of mind,

01:14:47.671 --> 01:14:49.021
I just care that Meow exists.

01:14:49.021 --> 01:14:52.561
Now I can tighten up my code, so
to speak, use even fewer lines

01:14:52.561 --> 01:14:55.741
to do the same thing by
throwing away the Repeat block,

01:14:55.741 --> 01:15:00.091
reconnecting this new puzzle piece here
that takes an input like 3 and voila,

01:15:00.091 --> 01:15:02.221
now we're really programming, right?

01:15:02.221 --> 01:15:04.561
We've not made any forward
progress functionally.

01:15:04.561 --> 01:15:06.871
The thing just mouse three times.

01:15:06.871 --> 01:15:09.061
But it's a better design.

01:15:09.061 --> 01:15:11.371
As you program more and
more, these are the kinds

01:15:11.371 --> 01:15:13.861
of instincts still start
to acquire so that one,

01:15:13.861 --> 01:15:16.711
you can start to take a big assignment,
a big problem set, something

01:15:16.711 --> 01:15:20.371
for homework even, that feels kind of
overwhelming at first, like, oh my God

01:15:20.371 --> 01:15:21.541
where do I even begin?

01:15:21.541 --> 01:15:25.441
But if you start to identify what are
the subproblems of a bigger problem?

01:15:25.441 --> 01:15:27.391
Then you can start making progress.

01:15:27.391 --> 01:15:32.071
I do this to this day where if I have to
tackle some programming-related project

01:15:32.071 --> 01:15:36.121
it's so easy to drag my feet and ugh,
it's going to take forever to start,

01:15:36.121 --> 01:15:38.821
until I just start writing
down like a to do list

01:15:38.821 --> 01:15:41.491
and I start to modularize the
program and say, all right, well

01:15:41.491 --> 01:15:42.601
what do I want this thing to do?

01:15:42.601 --> 01:15:43.051
Meowing.

01:15:43.051 --> 01:15:43.681
What's that mean?

01:15:43.681 --> 01:15:45.681
I've got to have it say
something on the screen.

01:15:45.681 --> 01:15:48.151
All right, I need to have it
say something on the screen

01:15:48.151 --> 01:15:49.081
some number of times.

01:15:49.081 --> 01:15:52.421
Like literally a mental or written
checklist, or pseudocode code,

01:15:52.421 --> 01:15:55.621
if you will, in English on a
piece of paper or text file,

01:15:55.621 --> 01:15:57.811
and then you can decide,
OK, the first thing I

01:15:57.811 --> 01:16:00.791
need to do for homework to
solve this real world problem,

01:16:00.791 --> 01:16:02.161
I just need a Meow function.

01:16:02.161 --> 01:16:04.261
I need to use a bunch
of other code, too,

01:16:04.261 --> 01:16:06.661
but I need to create a
Meow function and boom,

01:16:06.661 --> 01:16:10.441
now you have a piece of the problem
solved not unlike we did with the phone

01:16:10.441 --> 01:16:14.851
book there, but in this case, we'll
have presumably other problems to solve.

01:16:14.851 --> 01:16:16.511
All right, so what more can we do?

01:16:16.511 --> 01:16:18.993
Let's add a few more
pieces to the puzzle here.

01:16:18.993 --> 01:16:20.701
Let's actually interact
with the cat now.

01:16:20.701 --> 01:16:24.031
Let me go ahead and now when the
green flag is clicked, let me go ahead

01:16:24.031 --> 01:16:26.821
and ask a question using an event here.

01:16:26.821 --> 01:16:31.081
Let me go ahead and
say, let's see, I want

01:16:31.081 --> 01:16:34.331
to do something like implement
the notion of petting the cat.

01:16:34.331 --> 01:16:39.841
So if the cursor is touching the
cat like here, something like this,

01:16:39.841 --> 01:16:42.473
it'd be cute if the cat meows
like you're petting a cat.

01:16:42.473 --> 01:16:45.181
So I'm going to ask the question,
when the green flag is clicked,

01:16:45.181 --> 01:16:48.371
if let's see I think I need Sensing.

01:16:48.371 --> 01:16:50.761
So if touching mouse
pointer, this is way too big

01:16:50.761 --> 01:16:52.891
but again the shape is
fine, so there goes.

01:16:52.891 --> 01:16:53.731
Grew to fill.

01:16:53.731 --> 01:16:56.281
And then if it's touching
the mouse pointer,

01:16:56.281 --> 01:16:59.761
that is if the cat to whom
this script or this program,

01:16:59.761 --> 01:17:03.031
any time I attach puzzle
pieces MIT calls them a script

01:17:03.031 --> 01:17:07.231
or like a program, if you will, let
me go ahead then and choose a sound

01:17:07.231 --> 01:17:10.219
and say play sound meow until done.

01:17:10.219 --> 01:17:11.761
All right, so here it is to be clear.

01:17:11.761 --> 01:17:13.891
When the green flag is
clicked, ask the question,

01:17:13.891 --> 01:17:18.181
if the cat is touching the mouse
pointer then place sound meow.

01:17:18.181 --> 01:17:18.721
Here we go.

01:17:18.721 --> 01:17:20.734
Play.

01:17:20.734 --> 01:17:23.491
[SILENCE]

01:17:23.491 --> 01:17:25.351
All right, let's try again.

01:17:25.351 --> 01:17:26.041
Play.

01:17:26.041 --> 01:17:28.371
[SILENCE]

01:17:28.371 --> 01:17:29.581
Huh.

01:17:29.581 --> 01:17:34.351
I'm worried it's not Scratch's
fault. Feels like mine.

01:17:34.351 --> 01:17:37.551
What's the bug here?

01:17:37.551 --> 01:17:39.381
Why doesn't this work?

01:17:39.381 --> 01:17:42.231
Yeah, in back, who just turned.

01:17:42.231 --> 01:17:46.087
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:17:47.936 --> 01:17:50.811
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, the problem is
the moment I click that green flag,

01:17:50.811 --> 01:17:54.261
Scratch asks the question, is the
cat touching the mouse pointer?

01:17:54.261 --> 01:17:57.261
And obviously it's not because the
cursor was like up there a moment ago

01:17:57.261 --> 01:17:58.431
and it's not down there.

01:17:58.431 --> 01:18:01.371
It's fine if I move the cursor
down there, but too late.

01:18:01.371 --> 01:18:03.141
The program already asked the question.

01:18:03.141 --> 01:18:06.811
The answer was no or false or zero,
however you want to think about it,

01:18:06.811 --> 01:18:08.191
so no sound was played.

01:18:08.191 --> 01:18:10.341
So what might be the solution here be?

01:18:10.341 --> 01:18:12.891
I could move my cursor
quickly, but that feels

01:18:12.891 --> 01:18:14.901
like never going to work out right.

01:18:14.901 --> 01:18:16.941
Other solutions here?

01:18:16.941 --> 01:18:18.051
Yeah, in way back?

01:18:18.051 --> 01:18:20.521
Could you use the forever loop?

01:18:20.521 --> 01:18:21.341
The Forever loop.

01:18:21.341 --> 01:18:24.601
So I could indeed use this Forever
loop because if I want my program

01:18:24.601 --> 01:18:28.081
to just constantly listen to me, well
let's literally do something forever,

01:18:28.081 --> 01:18:30.151
or at least forever as
long as the program is

01:18:30.151 --> 01:18:32.318
running until I explicitly hit Stop.

01:18:32.318 --> 01:18:33.151
So let me grab that.

01:18:33.151 --> 01:18:36.481
Let me go to Control, let
me grab the Forever block,

01:18:36.481 --> 01:18:40.261
let me move the If inside of this
Forever block, reconnect this,

01:18:40.261 --> 01:18:43.951
go back up here, click the green
flag, and now nothing's happened yet,

01:18:43.951 --> 01:18:45.721
but let me try moving my cursor now.

01:18:45.721 --> 01:18:47.221
[MEOW]

01:18:47.221 --> 01:18:47.821
Oh.

01:18:47.821 --> 01:18:48.721
So now.

01:18:48.721 --> 01:18:49.321
[MEOW]

01:18:49.321 --> 01:18:50.381
That's kind of cute.

01:18:50.381 --> 01:18:52.298
So now the cat is actually
responding and it's

01:18:52.298 --> 01:18:54.841
going to keep doing
this again and again.

01:18:54.841 --> 01:18:58.651
So now we have this idea of taking these
different ideas, these different puzzle

01:18:58.651 --> 01:19:01.501
pieces, assembling them into
something more complicated.

01:19:01.501 --> 01:19:03.621
I could definitely put a name to this.

01:19:03.621 --> 01:19:05.371
I could create a custom
block, but for now

01:19:05.371 --> 01:19:08.074
let's just consider what kind
of more interactivity we can do.

01:19:08.074 --> 01:19:09.241
Let me go ahead and do this.

01:19:09.241 --> 01:19:12.931
By again grabbing a,
when green flag clicked,

01:19:12.931 --> 01:19:16.004
let me go ahead and
click the video sensing,

01:19:16.004 --> 01:19:18.421
and I'm going to rotate the
laptop because otherwise we're

01:19:18.421 --> 01:19:21.463
going to get a little inception thing
here where the camera is picking up

01:19:21.463 --> 01:19:22.471
the camera is up there.

01:19:22.471 --> 01:19:25.981
So I'm going to go reveal to
you what's inside the lectern

01:19:25.981 --> 01:19:29.911
here while we rotate this.

01:19:29.911 --> 01:19:34.981
Now that we have a non video
backdrop, I'm going to say this.

01:19:34.981 --> 01:19:37.111
Instead of the green flag
clicked, actually, I'm

01:19:37.111 --> 01:19:40.771
going to say when the video motion
is greater than some arbitrary

01:19:40.771 --> 01:19:47.233
measurement of motion, I'm going to go
ahead and play sound meow until done.

01:19:47.233 --> 01:19:48.941
And then I'm going to
get out of the way.

01:19:48.941 --> 01:19:50.511
So here's the cat.

01:19:50.511 --> 01:19:53.341
We'll put them on top of there.

01:19:53.341 --> 01:19:54.471
[MEOW]

01:19:54.471 --> 01:19:55.791
OK.

01:19:55.791 --> 01:19:57.459
All right, and here we go.

01:19:57.459 --> 01:20:00.451
[MEOW]

01:20:00.451 --> 01:20:03.171
So my hand is moving faster
than 50 something or other,

01:20:03.171 --> 01:20:05.021
whatever the unit of measure is.

01:20:05.021 --> 01:20:06.339
[MEOW]

01:20:06.339 --> 01:20:06.881
AUDIENCE: Aw.

01:20:06.881 --> 01:20:08.298
DAVID MALAN: (LAUGHING) Thank you.

01:20:08.298 --> 01:20:10.961
So now we have an even
more interactive version.

01:20:10.961 --> 01:20:12.081
[MEOW]

01:20:12.081 --> 01:20:15.721
But I think if I sort of slowly.

01:20:15.721 --> 01:20:17.761
[LAUGHING]

01:20:17.761 --> 01:20:18.781
(LAUGHING) Right?

01:20:18.781 --> 01:20:23.571
It's completely creepy, but I'm
not like exceeding the threshold--

01:20:23.571 --> 01:20:24.361
[MEOW]

01:20:24.361 --> 01:20:27.161
Until finally my hand
moves as fast as that.

01:20:27.161 --> 01:20:29.341
And so here actually is
an opportunity to show you

01:20:29.341 --> 01:20:31.261
something a former student did.

01:20:31.261 --> 01:20:32.531
Let me go ahead here and--

01:20:32.531 --> 01:20:33.031
[MEOW TWICE]

01:20:33.031 --> 01:20:35.501
OK, got to stop this.

01:20:35.501 --> 01:20:38.391
Let me go ahead and zoom out
of this in just a moment.

01:20:38.391 --> 01:20:38.891
[MEOW]

01:20:38.891 --> 01:20:39.931
If someone would be--

01:20:39.931 --> 01:20:40.611
[LAUGHING]

01:20:40.611 --> 01:20:42.361
(LAUGHING) If someone
would be comfortable

01:20:42.361 --> 01:20:44.871
coming up not only masked but
also on camera on the internet

01:20:44.871 --> 01:20:48.751
I thought we'd play one of your former
classmate's projects here up on stage.

01:20:48.751 --> 01:20:51.401
Would anyone like to volunteer
here and be up on stage?

01:20:51.401 --> 01:20:51.901
Who's that?

01:20:51.901 --> 01:20:52.401
Yeah.

01:20:52.401 --> 01:20:52.951
Come on down.

01:20:52.951 --> 01:20:53.701
What's your name?

01:20:53.701 --> 01:20:54.661
AUDIENCE: Sahar.

01:20:54.661 --> 01:20:55.501
DAVID MALAN: Sahar.

01:20:55.501 --> 01:20:57.011
All right, come on down.

01:20:57.011 --> 01:20:58.981
Let me get it set up for you here.

01:20:58.981 --> 01:20:59.953
[MEOW]

01:20:59.953 --> 01:21:05.299
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:05.299 --> 01:21:10.181
[MEOW]

01:21:10.181 --> 01:21:13.241
All right, let me go ahead
and full screen this here.

01:21:13.241 --> 01:21:17.641
So this is whack-a-mole by one
of your firmer predecessors.

01:21:17.641 --> 01:21:20.671
It's going to use the camera focusing
on your head, which will have

01:21:20.671 --> 01:21:22.328
to position inside of this rectangle.

01:21:22.328 --> 01:21:24.661
Have you ever played the
whack-a-mole game at an arcade?

01:21:24.661 --> 01:21:25.231
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:21:25.231 --> 01:21:25.471
DAVID MALAN: OK.

01:21:25.471 --> 01:21:27.618
So for those who haven't,
these little moles pop up

01:21:27.618 --> 01:21:29.701
and with a very fuzzy
hammer you sort of hit down.

01:21:29.701 --> 01:21:31.493
You though, if you
don't mind, you're going

01:21:31.493 --> 01:21:34.121
to use your head to do this virtually.

01:21:34.121 --> 01:21:39.121
So let's line up your head with
this red rectangle, if you could,

01:21:39.121 --> 01:21:40.021
we'll do beginner.

01:21:40.021 --> 01:21:41.551
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:21:41.551 --> 01:21:42.761
All right, here we go.

01:21:42.761 --> 01:21:44.521
Sahar.

01:21:44.521 --> 01:21:45.991
Give it a moment.

01:21:45.991 --> 01:21:47.221
OK, come a little closer.

01:21:47.221 --> 01:21:48.211
[DINGING]

01:21:48.211 --> 01:21:50.401
And now hit the moles with your head.

01:21:50.401 --> 01:21:51.841
[DING]

01:21:51.841 --> 01:21:53.126
There we go, one point.

01:21:53.126 --> 01:21:53.626
[DING]

01:21:53.626 --> 01:21:56.113
One point.

01:21:56.113 --> 01:21:58.011
[DINGING]

01:21:58.011 --> 01:22:00.021
Nice.

01:22:00.021 --> 01:22:01.101
15 seconds to go.

01:22:01.101 --> 01:22:01.711
There we go.

01:22:01.711 --> 01:22:02.211
Oh yeah.

01:22:02.211 --> 01:22:04.211
One point.

01:22:04.211 --> 01:22:06.211
[LAUGHING]

01:22:06.211 --> 01:22:07.696
[DINGING]

01:22:07.696 --> 01:22:09.471
Six seconds.

01:22:09.471 --> 01:22:10.919
AUDIENCE: Oh no.

01:22:10.919 --> 01:22:11.961
DAVID MALAN: There we go.

01:22:11.961 --> 01:22:12.681
Quick!

01:22:12.681 --> 01:22:14.931
[DINGING]

01:22:14.931 --> 01:22:16.851
All right, a round of
applause for Sahar.

01:22:16.851 --> 01:22:18.128
Thank you.

01:22:18.128 --> 01:22:22.104
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:24.601 --> 01:22:26.733
So beyond having a
little bit of fun here,

01:22:26.733 --> 01:22:28.441
the goal was to
demonstrate that by using

01:22:28.441 --> 01:22:31.891
some fairly simple, primitive,
some basic building blocks

01:22:31.891 --> 01:22:34.261
but assembling them in a fun
way with some music, maybe

01:22:34.261 --> 01:22:37.781
some new costumes or artwork, you
can really bring programs to life.

01:22:37.781 --> 01:22:40.841
But at the end of the day, the
only puzzle pieces really involved

01:22:40.841 --> 01:22:43.591
were ones like the ones I just
dragged and dropped and a few more,

01:22:43.591 --> 01:22:45.311
because there were
clearly lots of moles.

01:22:45.311 --> 01:22:49.291
So the student probably created a few
different sprites, not a single cap,

01:22:49.291 --> 01:22:50.708
but at least four different moles.

01:22:50.708 --> 01:22:53.458
They had like some kind of graphic
on the screen that showed Sahar

01:22:53.458 --> 01:22:54.601
where to position her head.

01:22:54.601 --> 01:22:57.091
There were some kind of
timer, maybe a variable

01:22:57.091 --> 01:22:59.676
that every second was counting down.

01:22:59.676 --> 01:23:02.551
So you can imagine taking what looks
like a pretty impressive project

01:23:02.551 --> 01:23:04.561
at first glance, and
perhaps overwhelming

01:23:04.561 --> 01:23:07.981
to solve yourself, but just think about
what are the basic building blocks?

01:23:07.981 --> 01:23:12.361
And pluck off one piece of the
puzzle, so to speak, at a time.

01:23:12.361 --> 01:23:15.101
So indeed if we rewind a little bit.

01:23:15.101 --> 01:23:17.881
Let me go ahead here
and introduce a program

01:23:17.881 --> 01:23:20.521
that I myself made
back in graduate school

01:23:20.521 --> 01:23:23.081
when Scratch was first
being developed by MIT.

01:23:23.081 --> 01:23:26.401
Let me go ahead and open
here, give me just one second,

01:23:26.401 --> 01:23:30.631
something that I called back
in the day Oscar Time that

01:23:30.631 --> 01:23:32.761
looks a little something like this.

01:23:32.761 --> 01:23:34.591
If I fullscreen it and hit Play.

01:23:34.591 --> 01:23:38.166
[MUSIC - SESAME STREET, "I LOVE TRASH"]

01:23:38.166 --> 01:23:40.041
OSCAR THE GROUCH:
(SINGING) Oh, I love trash.

01:23:40.041 --> 01:23:42.458
DAVID MALAN: So you'll notice
a piece of trash is falling.

01:23:42.458 --> 01:23:45.931
I can click on it and drag and as I get
close and close to the trash can notice

01:23:45.931 --> 01:23:47.931
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING)
Anything ragged or--

01:23:47.931 --> 01:23:49.821
DAVID MALAN: It wants
to go in, it seems.

01:23:49.821 --> 01:23:50.901
And if I let go--

01:23:50.901 --> 01:23:52.401
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING) Yes, I--

01:23:52.401 --> 01:23:54.144
DAVID MALAN: One point.

01:23:54.144 --> 01:23:54.936
Here comes another.

01:23:54.936 --> 01:23:56.601
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING) If you
really want to see something trashy--

01:23:56.601 --> 01:23:57.741
DAVID MALAN: I'll do
the same, two points.

01:23:57.741 --> 01:24:00.141
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING) I have here
a sneaker that's tattered and worn--

01:24:00.141 --> 01:24:01.821
DAVID MALAN: There's a
sneaker falling from the sky,

01:24:01.821 --> 01:24:03.289
so another sprite of some sort.

01:24:03.289 --> 01:24:05.247
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING)
The laces are torn.

01:24:05.247 --> 01:24:07.071
A gift from my mother--

01:24:07.071 --> 01:24:09.291
DAVID MALAN: I can also
get just a little lazy

01:24:09.291 --> 01:24:13.234
and just let them fall into the
trash themself if I want to.

01:24:13.234 --> 01:24:15.651
So you can see it doesn't have
to do with my mouse cursor,

01:24:15.651 --> 01:24:18.271
it has to do apparently
with the distance here.

01:24:18.271 --> 01:24:19.731
Let's listen a little further.

01:24:19.731 --> 01:24:23.301
I think some additional trash
is about to make its appearance.

01:24:23.301 --> 01:24:26.871
Presumably there's some kind of variable
that's keeping track of this score.

01:24:26.871 --> 01:24:28.371
OSCAR THE GROUCH: (SINGING) I love--

01:24:28.371 --> 01:24:30.704
DAVID MALAN: OK, let's see
what the last chorus here is.

01:24:30.704 --> 01:24:32.721
OSCAR THE GROUCH:
(SINGING) Rotten stuff.

01:24:32.721 --> 01:24:35.811
I have here some newspaper, crusty and

01:24:35.811 --> 01:24:37.791
DAVID MALAN: OK, and thus he continues.

01:24:37.791 --> 01:24:40.701
And the song actually
goes on and on and on

01:24:40.701 --> 01:24:43.371
and I do not have fond memories
of implementing this and hearing

01:24:43.371 --> 01:24:46.401
this song for like 10
straight hours, but it's

01:24:46.401 --> 01:24:50.056
a good example to just consider
how was this program composed?

01:24:50.056 --> 01:24:52.431
How did I go about implementing
it the first time around?

01:24:52.431 --> 01:24:54.756
And let me go ahead and
open up some programs now

01:24:54.756 --> 01:24:56.631
that I wrote in advance
just so that we could

01:24:56.631 --> 01:24:58.911
see how these things are assembled.

01:24:58.911 --> 01:25:02.331
Honestly, the first thing
I probably did was probably

01:25:02.331 --> 01:25:04.731
to do something a little like this.

01:25:04.731 --> 01:25:07.161
Here is just a version
of the program where

01:25:07.161 --> 01:25:09.861
I set out to solve
just one problem first

01:25:09.861 --> 01:25:12.121
of planting a lamp post in the program.

01:25:12.121 --> 01:25:12.621
Right?

01:25:12.621 --> 01:25:14.391
I kind of had a vision of what I wanted.

01:25:14.391 --> 01:25:15.981
You know, it evolved
over time, certainly,

01:25:15.981 --> 01:25:17.421
but I knew I wanted
trash to fall, I wanted

01:25:17.421 --> 01:25:19.129
a cute little Oscar
the Grouch to pop out

01:25:19.129 --> 01:25:22.041
of the trashcan, and some other
stuff, but wow that's a lot

01:25:22.041 --> 01:25:23.691
to just tackle all at once.

01:25:23.691 --> 01:25:26.571
I'm going to start easy, download
a picture of a lamp post,

01:25:26.571 --> 01:25:30.951
and then drag and drop it into the
stage as a costume and boom, that's

01:25:30.951 --> 01:25:31.971
version one.

01:25:31.971 --> 01:25:33.741
It doesn't functionally do anything.

01:25:33.741 --> 01:25:36.831
I mean, literally that's the
code that I wrote to do this.

01:25:36.831 --> 01:25:38.991
All I did was use like
the Backdrops feature

01:25:38.991 --> 01:25:41.041
and drag and drop and
move things around,

01:25:41.041 --> 01:25:44.181
but it got me to version
one of my program.

01:25:44.181 --> 01:25:46.011
Then what might version two be?

01:25:46.011 --> 01:25:48.591
Well I considered what
piece of functionality

01:25:48.591 --> 01:25:52.083
frankly might be the easiest to
pluck off next and the trash can.

01:25:52.083 --> 01:25:54.291
That seems like a pretty
core piece of functionality.

01:25:54.291 --> 01:25:56.551
It just needs to sit
there most of the time.

01:25:56.551 --> 01:25:59.931
So the next thing I
probably did was to open up,

01:25:59.931 --> 01:26:05.601
for instance, the trash can version
here that looks a little something now

01:26:05.601 --> 01:26:06.114
like this.

01:26:06.114 --> 01:26:08.031
So this time I'll show
you what's inside here.

01:26:08.031 --> 01:26:10.401
There is some code, but not much.

01:26:10.401 --> 01:26:14.421
Notice at bottom right I change the
default cat to a picture of a trashcan,

01:26:14.421 --> 01:26:17.451
instead, but it's the same
principle that I can control.

01:26:17.451 --> 01:26:20.121
And then over here I added this code.

01:26:20.121 --> 01:26:23.031
When the green flag is
clicked, switch the costume

01:26:23.031 --> 01:26:25.111
to something I arbitrarily
called Oscar 1.

01:26:25.111 --> 01:26:26.871
So I found a couple
of different pictures

01:26:26.871 --> 01:26:29.991
of a trash can, one that looks
closed, one that looks partly open,

01:26:29.991 --> 01:26:32.151
and eventually one that
has Oscar coming out,

01:26:32.151 --> 01:26:33.811
and I just gave them different names.

01:26:33.811 --> 01:26:36.981
So I said Switch to Oscar 1, which
is the closed one by default,

01:26:36.981 --> 01:26:40.791
then forever do the following:
if touching the mouse pointer,

01:26:40.791 --> 01:26:45.711
then switch the costume to
Oscar 2, else switch to Oscar 1.

01:26:45.711 --> 01:26:49.311
That is to say, I just wanted to
implement this idea of the can opening

01:26:49.311 --> 01:26:52.131
and closing, even if it's not
exactly what I wanted ultimately,

01:26:52.131 --> 01:26:54.181
I just wanted to make
some forward progress.

01:26:54.181 --> 01:26:59.941
So here, when I run this program by
clicking Play, notice what happens.

01:26:59.941 --> 01:27:03.441
Nothing yet, but if I get
closer to the trash can,

01:27:03.441 --> 01:27:07.311
it indeed pops open because
it's forever listening

01:27:07.311 --> 01:27:10.501
for whether the sprite,
the trash can in this case,

01:27:10.501 --> 01:27:11.751
is touching the mouse pointer.

01:27:11.751 --> 01:27:12.441
And that's it.

01:27:12.441 --> 01:27:15.501
That was version 2, if you will.

01:27:15.501 --> 01:27:18.651
If I went in now and added the lamp
post and compose the program together,

01:27:18.651 --> 01:27:20.151
now we're starting to make progress.

01:27:20.151 --> 01:27:20.361
Right?

01:27:20.361 --> 01:27:22.941
Now it would look a little
something more like the program

01:27:22.941 --> 01:27:25.131
I intended ultimately to create.

01:27:25.131 --> 01:27:27.961
What piece did I probably
bite off after that?

01:27:27.961 --> 01:27:30.231
Well, I think what I did
is I probably decided

01:27:30.231 --> 01:27:33.741
let me implement one of the pieces of
trash, not the shoe in the newspaper

01:27:33.741 --> 01:27:34.351
all at once.

01:27:34.351 --> 01:27:37.881
Let's just get one piece of
trash working correctly first.

01:27:37.881 --> 01:27:40.281
So let me go ahead and open this one.

01:27:40.281 --> 01:27:43.851
And again, all of these examples will
be available on the course's website

01:27:43.851 --> 01:27:46.041
so you can see all of
these examples, too.

01:27:46.041 --> 01:27:48.441
It's not terribly long, I
just implement it in advance

01:27:48.441 --> 01:27:50.811
so we could flip
through kind of quickly.

01:27:50.811 --> 01:27:52.341
Here's what I did here.

01:27:52.341 --> 01:27:56.151
On the right hand side, I turned
my sprite into a piece of trash

01:27:56.151 --> 01:27:58.771
this time instead of a cat,
instead of a trash can,

01:27:58.771 --> 01:28:04.131
and I also created, with Carter's help,
a second sprite, this one a floor.

01:28:04.131 --> 01:28:07.191
It's literally just a black line
because I just wanted initially

01:28:07.191 --> 01:28:09.621
to have some notion of a
floor so I could detect

01:28:09.621 --> 01:28:12.141
if the trash is touching the floor.

01:28:12.141 --> 01:28:15.411
Now without seeing the code yet,
just hearing that description,

01:28:15.411 --> 01:28:20.301
why might I have wanted the second
sprite and this black line for a floor

01:28:20.301 --> 01:28:22.748
with the trash intending
to fall from the sky?

01:28:22.748 --> 01:28:24.081
What might I have been thinking?

01:28:24.081 --> 01:28:26.041
Like what problem might
I be trying to solve?

01:28:26.041 --> 01:28:26.271
Yeah?

01:28:26.271 --> 01:28:28.731
AUDIENCE: You don't want the
first sprite to go through it.

01:28:28.731 --> 01:28:31.689
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, you don't want
the first sprite to start at the top,

01:28:31.689 --> 01:28:34.281
go through, and then boom,
you completely lose it.

01:28:34.281 --> 01:28:36.933
That would not be a very useful thing.

01:28:36.933 --> 01:28:39.891
Or it would seem to maybe eat up more
and more of the computer's memory

01:28:39.891 --> 01:28:42.561
if the trash is just endlessly
falling and I can't grab it.

01:28:42.561 --> 01:28:44.331
It might be a little traumatic
if you tried to get it

01:28:44.331 --> 01:28:46.873
and you can't pull it back out
and you can't fix the program.

01:28:46.873 --> 01:28:48.549
So I just wanted the thing to stop.

01:28:48.549 --> 01:28:50.091
So how might I have implemented this?

01:28:50.091 --> 01:28:51.741
Let's look at the code at left.

01:28:51.741 --> 01:28:56.361
Here I have a bit of randomness,
like I proposed earlier exists.

01:28:56.361 --> 01:28:59.241
There's this blue
function called Go To x,

01:28:59.241 --> 01:29:03.051
y that lets me move a
sprite to any position,

01:29:03.051 --> 01:29:07.731
up, down, left, right, I picked a random
x location, either here or over here,

01:29:07.731 --> 01:29:12.471
negative 240 to positive 240, and then
a y value of 180, which is the top.

01:29:12.471 --> 01:29:14.221
This just makes the
game more interesting.

01:29:14.221 --> 01:29:18.411
It's kind of lame pretty quickly if the
trash always falls from the same spot.

01:29:18.411 --> 01:29:21.651
Here's this a little bit of randomness,
like most any game would have,

01:29:21.651 --> 01:29:23.121
that spices things up.

01:29:23.121 --> 01:29:26.694
So now if I click the green flag,
you'll see that it just falls,

01:29:26.694 --> 01:29:28.611
nothing interesting is
going to happen, but it

01:29:28.611 --> 01:29:33.051
does stop when it touches the black
line because notice what we did here.

01:29:33.051 --> 01:29:37.821
I'm forever asking the question if
the distance of the sprite, the trash,

01:29:37.821 --> 01:29:41.551
is to the floor is greater
than zero, that's fine.

01:29:41.551 --> 01:29:44.361
Change the y location by negative 3.

01:29:44.361 --> 01:29:48.861
So move it down 3 pixels, down 3
pixels, until the distance to the floor

01:29:48.861 --> 01:29:52.701
is not greater than zero, it is zero
or even negative, at which point

01:29:52.701 --> 01:29:54.428
it should just stop moving altogether.

01:29:54.428 --> 01:29:56.511
There's other ways we could
have implemented this,

01:29:56.511 --> 01:29:58.731
but this felt like a nice,
clean way that logically, just

01:29:58.731 --> 01:29:59.523
made it make sense.

01:29:59.523 --> 01:30:03.711
OK, now I got some trash falling, I
got a trash can that opens and closes,

01:30:03.711 --> 01:30:08.211
I have a lamp post, now I'm a
good three steps into the program.

01:30:08.211 --> 01:30:09.411
We're making progress.

01:30:09.411 --> 01:30:12.411
If we consider one or two
final pieces, something

01:30:12.411 --> 01:30:17.571
like the dragging of the trash, let me
go ahead and open up this version 2.

01:30:17.571 --> 01:30:21.171
Dragging the trash requires
a different type of question.

01:30:21.171 --> 01:30:22.371
Let me zoom in here.

01:30:22.371 --> 01:30:24.121
Here's the piece of trash.

01:30:24.121 --> 01:30:26.901
I only need one sprite, no
floor here because I just

01:30:26.901 --> 01:30:29.721
want the human to move it up,
down, left, right and the human's

01:30:29.721 --> 01:30:32.751
not going to physically be able
to move it outside of the world.

01:30:32.751 --> 01:30:36.481
If we zoom in on this code, the way
we've solved this is as follows.

01:30:36.481 --> 01:30:40.671
We're using that And conjunction
that we glimpsed earlier because when

01:30:40.671 --> 01:30:44.301
the green flag is clicked, we're
forever asking this question or really

01:30:44.301 --> 01:30:47.571
these questions, plural,
if the mouse is down

01:30:47.571 --> 01:30:53.211
and the trash is touching the
mouse pointer, that's equivalent

01:30:53.211 --> 01:30:55.521
logically to clicking on the trash.

01:30:55.521 --> 01:30:58.831
Go ahead and move the
trash to the mouse pointer.

01:30:58.831 --> 01:31:00.891
So again it takes this
very familiar idea

01:31:00.891 --> 01:31:04.011
that you and I take for granted
every day on Macs and PCs of clicking

01:31:04.011 --> 01:31:05.271
and dragging and dropping.

01:31:05.271 --> 01:31:06.621
How is that implemented?

01:31:06.621 --> 01:31:10.821
Well Mac OS or Windows are
probably asking a question.

01:31:10.821 --> 01:31:15.321
For every icon, is the mouse down
and is the icon touching the mouse?

01:31:15.321 --> 01:31:19.461
If so, go to the location
of the mouse forever

01:31:19.461 --> 01:31:21.561
while the mouse button is clicked down.

01:31:21.561 --> 01:31:23.721
So how does this work in reality now?

01:31:23.721 --> 01:31:25.971
Let me go ahead and click on the Play.

01:31:25.971 --> 01:31:30.031
Nothing happens at first, but if
I click on it, I can move it up,

01:31:30.031 --> 01:31:31.371
down, left, right.

01:31:31.371 --> 01:31:33.121
It doesn't move thereafter.

01:31:33.121 --> 01:31:36.871
So I now need to kind of combine
this idea of dragging with falling,

01:31:36.871 --> 01:31:39.604
but I bet I could just start
to use just one single program.

01:31:39.604 --> 01:31:42.021
Right now I'm using separate
ones to show different ideas,

01:31:42.021 --> 01:31:45.171
but now that's another
bite out of the problem.

01:31:45.171 --> 01:31:48.028
If we do one last one,
something like the scorekeeping

01:31:48.028 --> 01:31:51.111
is interesting, because recall that
every time we dragged a piece of trash

01:31:51.111 --> 01:31:54.661
into the can, Oscar popped out
and told us the current score.

01:31:54.661 --> 01:31:59.241
So let me go ahead and find
this one, Oscar variables,

01:31:59.241 --> 01:32:01.221
and let me zoom in on this one.

01:32:01.221 --> 01:32:04.424
This one is longer because we
combined all of these elements.

01:32:04.424 --> 01:32:07.341
So this is the kind of thing that
if you looked at first glance, like,

01:32:07.341 --> 01:32:09.861
I have no idea how I would
have implemented this

01:32:09.861 --> 01:32:12.141
from nothing, from scratch literally.

01:32:12.141 --> 01:32:16.491
But again, if you take your
vision and componenitize it

01:32:16.491 --> 01:32:18.591
into these smaller,
bite-sized problems, you

01:32:18.591 --> 01:32:21.021
could take these baby
steps, so to speak, and then

01:32:21.021 --> 01:32:22.591
solve everything collectively.

01:32:22.591 --> 01:32:25.761
So what's new here is this bottom one.

01:32:25.761 --> 01:32:30.411
Forever do the following:
if the trash is touching

01:32:30.411 --> 01:32:33.861
Oscar, the other sprite that
we've now added to the program,

01:32:33.861 --> 01:32:35.841
change the score by 1.

01:32:35.841 --> 01:32:37.851
This is an orange and
indeed if we poke around

01:32:37.851 --> 01:32:42.411
we'll see that orange is a variable,
like an x or y but with a better name,

01:32:42.411 --> 01:32:46.401
changing it means to add 1 or
if it's negative subtract 1.

01:32:46.401 --> 01:32:51.471
Then go ahead and have the
trash go to pick random.

01:32:51.471 --> 01:32:53.091
What is this all about?

01:32:53.091 --> 01:32:56.941
Well, let me show you what it's doing
and then we can infer backwards.

01:32:56.941 --> 01:32:58.341
Let me go ahead and hit Play.

01:32:58.341 --> 01:33:01.491
All right, it's falling, I'm clicking
and dragging it, I'm moving it over,

01:33:01.491 --> 01:33:02.774
and I'm letting go.

01:33:02.774 --> 01:33:04.191
All right, let me do it once more.

01:33:04.191 --> 01:33:06.831
Letting go, let me stop.

01:33:06.831 --> 01:33:13.371
Why do I have this function at the
end called Go To x and y randomly?

01:33:13.371 --> 01:33:16.041
Like what problem is this solving here?

01:33:16.041 --> 01:33:18.133
Yeah, in way back.

01:33:18.133 --> 01:33:21.570
AUDIENCE: Just the same
track teleported to the top

01:33:21.570 --> 01:33:23.386
after you put it in the trash can.

01:33:23.386 --> 01:33:24.511
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, exactly.

01:33:24.511 --> 01:33:27.303
Even though the human perceives
this as like a lot of trash falling

01:33:27.303 --> 01:33:29.271
from the sky, it's
actually the same piece

01:33:29.271 --> 01:33:32.151
of trash, just kind of being
magically moved back to the top

01:33:32.151 --> 01:33:33.651
as though it's a new one.

01:33:33.651 --> 01:33:36.531
There, too, you have this
idea of reusable code.

01:33:36.531 --> 01:33:40.161
If you were constantly copying
and pasting your pieces of trash

01:33:40.161 --> 01:33:43.191
and creating 20 pieces of trash, 30
pieces of trash, just because you

01:33:43.191 --> 01:33:46.821
want the game to have that many
levels, probably doing something wrong.

01:33:46.821 --> 01:33:49.911
Reuse the code that you wrote,
reuse the sprites that you wrote,

01:33:49.911 --> 01:33:54.681
and that would give you not just
correctness, but also a better design.

01:33:54.681 --> 01:33:57.666
Well let's take a look at one
final set of building blocks

01:33:57.666 --> 01:33:59.541
that we can compose
ultimately into something

01:33:59.541 --> 01:34:02.011
particularly interactive as follows.

01:34:02.011 --> 01:34:03.921
Let me go ahead and
zoom out here and let

01:34:03.921 --> 01:34:08.571
me propose that we implement something
like some kind of maze-based game.

01:34:08.571 --> 01:34:09.991
Let me go ahead here.

01:34:09.991 --> 01:34:13.311
So I want to implement
some maze-based game that

01:34:13.311 --> 01:34:15.061
looks at first glance like this.

01:34:15.061 --> 01:34:15.736
Let me hit Play.

01:34:15.736 --> 01:34:18.111
It's not a very fun game yet,
but here's a little Harvard

01:34:18.111 --> 01:34:22.191
shield, a couple of black lines, this
time vertical instead of horizontal,

01:34:22.191 --> 01:34:24.231
but notice you can't
quite see my hand here,

01:34:24.231 --> 01:34:28.949
but I'm using my arrow keys to go down,
to go up, to go left, to go right,

01:34:28.949 --> 01:34:31.491
but if I keep going right, right,
right, right, right, right,

01:34:31.491 --> 01:34:32.841
right it's not going anywhere.

01:34:32.841 --> 01:34:35.321
And left, left, left, left, left, left,
left, left, left, left, left, left,

01:34:35.321 --> 01:34:36.741
left it eventually stops.

01:34:36.741 --> 01:34:41.241
So before we look at the code,
how might this be working?

01:34:41.241 --> 01:34:44.991
What kinds of scripts,
collections of puzzle pieces,

01:34:44.991 --> 01:34:47.491
might collectively
help us implement this?

01:34:47.491 --> 01:34:48.531
What do you think?

01:34:48.531 --> 01:34:52.371
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:34:56.546 --> 01:34:57.671
DAVID MALAN: Perfect, yeah.

01:34:57.671 --> 01:35:00.839
There's probably some question being
asked, if touching the black line,

01:35:00.839 --> 01:35:03.131
and it happens to be a couple
of sprites, each of which

01:35:03.131 --> 01:35:06.381
is just literally a vertical black line
we're probably asking a question like,

01:35:06.381 --> 01:35:07.451
are you touching it?

01:35:07.451 --> 01:35:10.451
Is the distance to it
zero or close to zero?

01:35:10.451 --> 01:35:16.131
And if so, we just ignore the left
or the right arrow at that point.

01:35:16.131 --> 01:35:16.871
So that works.

01:35:16.871 --> 01:35:18.941
But otherwise, if we're
not touching a wall,

01:35:18.941 --> 01:35:22.481
what are we probably doing
instead forever here?

01:35:22.481 --> 01:35:24.821
How is the movement working presumably?

01:35:24.821 --> 01:35:26.831
Yeah and back.

01:35:26.831 --> 01:35:28.621
Oh are you scratching?

01:35:28.621 --> 01:35:29.641
OK, sure.

01:35:29.641 --> 01:35:30.733
Let's go on.

01:35:30.733 --> 01:35:33.691
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:35:33.691 --> 01:35:35.565
DAVID MALAN: Sorry, say a little louder.

01:35:35.565 --> 01:35:38.799
AUDIENCE: Presumably it's continually
looking for you to hit the arrow keys

01:35:38.799 --> 01:35:40.191
and then moving when you do.

01:35:40.191 --> 01:35:41.066
DAVID MALAN: Exactly.

01:35:41.066 --> 01:35:44.931
It's continually, forever listening for
the arrow keys up, down, left, right,

01:35:44.931 --> 01:35:47.271
and if the up arrow is
pressed, we're probably

01:35:47.271 --> 01:35:49.801
changing the y by a positive value.

01:35:49.801 --> 01:35:52.401
If the down arrow is pressed,
we're going down by y,

01:35:52.401 --> 01:35:54.091
and left and right accordingly.

01:35:54.091 --> 01:35:55.671
So let's actually take a quick look.

01:35:55.671 --> 01:35:59.001
If I zoom out here and take a look
at the code that implements this,

01:35:59.001 --> 01:36:01.556
there's a lot going on at
first glance, but let's see.

01:36:01.556 --> 01:36:03.681
First of all, let me drag
some stuff out of the way

01:36:03.681 --> 01:36:05.841
because it's kind of
overwhelming at first glance,

01:36:05.841 --> 01:36:09.291
especially if you, for instance, were
poking around online as for problem set

01:36:09.291 --> 01:36:11.934
0 just to get inspiration,
most projects out there

01:36:11.934 --> 01:36:13.851
are going to look
overwhelming at first glance

01:36:13.851 --> 01:36:16.321
until you start to wrap your
mind around what's going on.

01:36:16.321 --> 01:36:19.581
But in this case, we've
implemented some abstractions

01:36:19.581 --> 01:36:22.731
from the get go to explain to
ourselves and to anyone else looking

01:36:22.731 --> 01:36:24.681
at the code what's going on.

01:36:24.681 --> 01:36:29.101
This is that program with the two black
lines and the Harvard shield going up,

01:36:29.101 --> 01:36:30.081
down, left, and right.

01:36:30.081 --> 01:36:33.801
It initially puts the shield
in the middle, 0,0, then

01:36:33.801 --> 01:36:37.251
forever listens for keyboard,
as I think you were describing,

01:36:37.251 --> 01:36:40.461
and it feels for the walls, as
I think you were describing.

01:36:40.461 --> 01:36:42.021
Now how is that implemented?

01:36:42.021 --> 01:36:42.861
Don't know yet.

01:36:42.861 --> 01:36:46.401
These are custom blocks we created
as abstractions to kind of hide

01:36:46.401 --> 01:36:48.651
those implementation details
because honestly that's

01:36:48.651 --> 01:36:50.131
all I need to know right now.

01:36:50.131 --> 01:36:52.851
But, as aspiring programmers,
if we're curious now,

01:36:52.851 --> 01:36:55.461
let's scroll down to the
actual implementation

01:36:55.461 --> 01:36:57.261
of listening for keyboard.

01:36:57.261 --> 01:36:59.701
This is the one on the left
and it is a little long,

01:36:59.701 --> 01:37:02.461
but it's a lot of similar structure.

01:37:02.461 --> 01:37:07.371
We're doing the following, if the up
arrow is pressed, then change y by 1.

01:37:07.371 --> 01:37:08.031
Go up.

01:37:08.031 --> 01:37:11.601
If the down arrow is pressed,
then change y by negative 1.

01:37:11.601 --> 01:37:12.341
Go down.

01:37:12.341 --> 01:37:15.361
Right arrow, left arrow, and that's it.

01:37:15.361 --> 01:37:17.991
So it just assembles all
of those ideas, combines it

01:37:17.991 --> 01:37:20.451
into one new block just because
it's kind of overwhelming,

01:37:20.451 --> 01:37:22.681
let's just implement it
once and tuck it away.

01:37:22.681 --> 01:37:26.721
And if we scroll now over to
the Feel for Walls function,

01:37:26.721 --> 01:37:30.081
this now is asking the
question as hypothesized,

01:37:30.081 --> 01:37:35.001
if I'm touching the left wall, change my
x value by 1, sort of move away from it

01:37:35.001 --> 01:37:35.721
a little bit.

01:37:35.721 --> 01:37:38.661
If I'm touching the right
wall, then move x by negative 1

01:37:38.661 --> 01:37:40.593
to move a little bit away from it.

01:37:40.593 --> 01:37:42.051
So it kind of bounces off the wall.

01:37:42.051 --> 01:37:47.451
Just in case it slightly went over, we
keep the crest within those two walls.

01:37:47.451 --> 01:37:51.231
All right, then a couple of
more pieces here to introduce.

01:37:51.231 --> 01:37:54.441
What if we want to actually add
some kind of adversary or opponent

01:37:54.441 --> 01:37:55.311
to this game?

01:37:55.311 --> 01:38:02.031
Well, let me go ahead to maybe this one
here where the adversary in this game

01:38:02.031 --> 01:38:05.459
might, for instance, be designed to
be bouncing to stand in your way.

01:38:05.459 --> 01:38:08.751
This is like a maze and you're trying to
get the Harvard shield from the bottom

01:38:08.751 --> 01:38:09.811
to the top or vice versa.

01:38:09.811 --> 01:38:14.361
Uh oh, Yale is in the way and it
seems to be automatically bouncing

01:38:14.361 --> 01:38:15.541
back and forth here.

01:38:15.541 --> 01:38:16.791
Well, let me ask someone else.

01:38:16.791 --> 01:38:17.451
Hypothesize.

01:38:17.451 --> 01:38:19.251
How is this working?

01:38:19.251 --> 01:38:21.381
This is an idea you have,
this as an idea you see.

01:38:21.381 --> 01:38:26.491
Let's reverse engineer in
your head how it works.

01:38:26.491 --> 01:38:27.691
How might this be working?

01:38:27.691 --> 01:38:28.936
Yeah, in back.

01:38:28.936 --> 01:38:32.656
AUDIENCE: If the Yale symbol is
touching a right wall or left wall,

01:38:32.656 --> 01:38:34.639
then have it bounce.

01:38:34.639 --> 01:38:36.431
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, so
if the Yale symbol is

01:38:36.431 --> 01:38:39.131
touching the left wall or the right
wall, we somehow have it bounce.

01:38:39.131 --> 01:38:41.756
And indeed we'll see there's a
puzzle piece that can do exactly

01:38:41.756 --> 01:38:44.066
that technically off
the edge, as we'll see,

01:38:44.066 --> 01:38:45.691
but there's another way we can do this.

01:38:45.691 --> 01:38:46.751
Let's look at the code.

01:38:46.751 --> 01:38:49.271
The way we ourselves
can implement exactly

01:38:49.271 --> 01:38:52.011
that idea bounce is just
with a little bit of logic.

01:38:52.011 --> 01:38:54.581
So here's what this version
of the program is doing.

01:38:54.581 --> 01:38:58.721
It's moving Yale by default to 0,0
just to arbitrarily put it somewhere,

01:38:58.721 --> 01:39:03.251
pointing it direction 90 degrees, which
means just horizontally, essentially,

01:39:03.251 --> 01:39:06.491
and then it's forever doing
this: if touching the left wall

01:39:06.491 --> 01:39:10.451
or touching the right wall,
here's our translation of bounce.

01:39:10.451 --> 01:39:11.953
We're just turning 180 degrees.

01:39:11.953 --> 01:39:13.661
And the nice thing
about that is we don't

01:39:13.661 --> 01:39:16.369
have to worry if we're going from
right to left or left to right.

01:39:16.369 --> 01:39:19.481
180 degrees is going to
work on both of the walls.

01:39:19.481 --> 01:39:21.311
And that's it.

01:39:21.311 --> 01:39:24.671
After we do that, we just move
one step, one pixel, at a time

01:39:24.671 --> 01:39:28.151
but we're doing it forever so
something is happening continually

01:39:28.151 --> 01:39:30.641
and the Yale icon is
bouncing back and forth.

01:39:30.641 --> 01:39:33.551
Well one final piece
here, what if now we

01:39:33.551 --> 01:39:39.711
want another adversary, a more advanced
