a little more traditional,
a little more text-based,

01:46:45.921 --> 01:46:48.501
not puzzle piece- or
block-based, known as C.

01:46:48.501 --> 01:46:49.771
This is an older language.

01:46:49.771 --> 01:46:50.979
It's been around for decades.

01:46:50.979 --> 01:46:54.501
But it's a language that underlies so
many of today's more modern languages,

01:46:54.501 --> 01:46:57.261
among them something called
Python that we'll also

01:46:57.261 --> 01:46:58.821
come to in a few weeks' time.

01:46:58.821 --> 01:47:00.921
Indeed, at the end of
the semester, the goal

01:47:00.921 --> 01:47:02.661
is for you to feel that
you've not learned Scratch,

01:47:02.661 --> 01:47:04.911
you've not learned C, or
even Python, for that matter,

01:47:04.911 --> 01:47:07.371
but fundamentally that you've
learned how to program.

01:47:07.371 --> 01:47:09.801
Unfortunately, when you
learn how to program

01:47:09.801 --> 01:47:13.681
with a more traditional language like
this, there's just so much distraction.

01:47:13.681 --> 01:47:17.091
Last week I described all of the
syntax, all of the weird punctuation

01:47:17.091 --> 01:47:19.881
that you see in this, like the
hash symbol, these angled brackets,

01:47:19.881 --> 01:47:22.761
parentheses, curly braces,
backslash n, and more.

01:47:22.761 --> 01:47:26.791
Well, today we're not going to reveal
what all of those little particulars

01:47:26.791 --> 01:47:27.291
mean.

01:47:27.291 --> 01:47:31.461
But by next week, will this no
longer look like the proverbial Greek

01:47:31.461 --> 01:47:35.391
to you, a language that, presumably,
you've never actually seen or typed

01:47:35.391 --> 01:47:36.321
before.

01:47:36.321 --> 01:47:40.201
But to do that, we'll explore some
of the very same topics as last week.

01:47:40.201 --> 01:47:43.221
So recall that, via Scratch-- and
presumably via problem set 1--

01:47:43.221 --> 01:47:46.581
we took a look at things called
functions that are actions or verbs.

01:47:46.581 --> 01:47:49.311
And related to functions
were arguments like inputs.

01:47:49.311 --> 01:47:52.761
And related to some functions
were returned values like outputs.

01:47:52.761 --> 01:47:56.151
Then we talked a bit about conditionals,
forks in the road, so to speak,

01:47:56.151 --> 01:47:59.541
Boolean expressions, which are
just yes/no questions or true/false

01:47:59.541 --> 01:48:03.141
questions, loops, which let you do
things again and again, variables,

01:48:03.141 --> 01:48:05.991
like in math, that let you
store values temporarily,

01:48:05.991 --> 01:48:07.671
and then even other topics still.

01:48:07.671 --> 01:48:11.421
So if you were comfortable on the
heels of problem set 0 and last week,

01:48:11.421 --> 01:48:14.491
realize that all of these topics
are going to remain with us.

01:48:14.491 --> 01:48:18.231
So really, today is just about acquiring
all the more of a mental model for how

01:48:18.231 --> 01:48:22.911
you translate those ideas into,
presumably, a very cryptic new syntax--

01:48:22.911 --> 01:48:25.699
a new syntax, frankly,
that's actually more

01:48:25.699 --> 01:48:27.741
simple in some ways than
your own human language,

01:48:27.741 --> 01:48:31.423
be it English or something else, because
there's far fewer vocabulary words.

01:48:31.423 --> 01:48:33.381
There's actually far less
syntax that you might

01:48:33.381 --> 01:48:35.451
have in, say, a typical human language.

01:48:35.451 --> 01:48:39.601
But you need to be with these computer
languages all the more precise

01:48:39.601 --> 01:48:41.851
so that you're most,
ultimately, correct,

01:48:41.851 --> 01:48:46.041
and ultimately will see to your code
is successful along a few other lines

01:48:46.041 --> 01:48:46.621
as well.

01:48:46.621 --> 01:48:49.791
So if you think about the last time
you kind of wandered around not really

01:48:49.791 --> 01:48:51.981
knowing what you were doing
or encountered something new--

01:48:51.981 --> 01:48:54.856
might not have been that long ago,
entering Harvard Yard for the very

01:48:54.856 --> 01:48:58.461
first time, or Old Campus or the like,
be it in Cambridge or New Haven--

01:48:58.461 --> 01:49:01.823
you didn't really need to know how
to do everything as a first year.

01:49:01.823 --> 01:49:03.531
You didn't need to
know who everyone was,

01:49:03.531 --> 01:49:07.231
where everything was, how Harvard or
Yale, or anything else for that matter,

01:49:07.231 --> 01:49:07.731
worked.

01:49:07.731 --> 01:49:10.856
You sort of got by day to day by just
focusing on those things that matter.

01:49:10.856 --> 01:49:12.606
And anything you didn't
really understand,

01:49:12.606 --> 01:49:14.961
you sort of turned a blind
eye to until it's important.

01:49:14.961 --> 01:49:16.761
And that's, indeed, what
we're going to do today.

01:49:16.761 --> 01:49:18.636
And really, for the next
several weeks, we'll

01:49:18.636 --> 01:49:21.171
focus on details that
are initially important

01:49:21.171 --> 01:49:24.343
and try to wave our hands, so to speak,
at details that, yeah, eventually

01:49:24.343 --> 01:49:25.801
we'll get to, might be interesting.

01:49:25.801 --> 01:49:27.468
But for now, they might be distractions.

01:49:27.468 --> 01:49:29.901
And by distractions, I really
mean some of that syntax

01:49:29.901 --> 01:49:31.501
to which I alluded earlier.

01:49:31.501 --> 01:49:34.671
So by the end of today-- and
really, by the end of problem set 1,

01:49:34.671 --> 01:49:37.401
your first foray, presumably,
into this language called C--

01:49:37.401 --> 01:49:39.171
you'll have written some code.

01:49:39.171 --> 01:49:41.781
And you'll be asking yourself--
we'll be asking yourselves--

01:49:41.781 --> 01:49:43.161
just how good is that code?

01:49:43.161 --> 01:49:46.041
Well, first and foremost, per
last week, be it in Scratch

01:49:46.041 --> 01:49:51.591
or phone book form, code ultimately
needs to be correct, to be well done.

01:49:51.591 --> 01:49:53.541
You want the problem
to be solved correctly.

01:49:53.541 --> 01:49:55.491
So that one sort of goes without saying.

01:49:55.491 --> 01:49:59.511
And along the way this term, we'll
provide you with tools and techniques

01:49:59.511 --> 01:50:02.746
so you don't have to just sit there
sort of endlessly trying an input,

01:50:02.746 --> 01:50:05.371
checking the output, trying
another input, checking the output.

01:50:05.371 --> 01:50:07.161
There's a lot of automation
tools in the real world--

01:50:07.161 --> 01:50:09.201
and in this class and
others like it-- that

01:50:09.201 --> 01:50:13.011
will help facilitate you answering
that question for yourself, is my code

01:50:13.011 --> 01:50:15.901
correct, according to our
specifications or the like.

01:50:15.901 --> 01:50:18.441
But then something that's
going to take more time

01:50:18.441 --> 01:50:21.861
and you're probably not going to feel
100% comfortable with the first week,

01:50:21.861 --> 01:50:24.891
the first weeks, is just how
well designed your code is.

01:50:24.891 --> 01:50:27.614
It's one thing to speak
English or write English,

01:50:27.614 --> 01:50:30.031
but it's another thing-- or
any language, for that matter.

01:50:30.031 --> 01:50:32.191
But it's another thing to
speak it or write it well.

01:50:32.191 --> 01:50:35.149
And we spend all these years in middle
school, high school, presumably,

01:50:35.149 --> 01:50:38.601
writing papers and other documents,
getting grades and feedback on them

01:50:38.601 --> 01:50:41.811
as to how well formulated your
arguments were, how well structured

01:50:41.811 --> 01:50:43.020
your paper was, and the like.

01:50:43.020 --> 01:50:44.971
And there's that same
idea in programming.

01:50:44.971 --> 01:50:49.591
It doesn't matter necessarily that
you've just solved a problem correctly.

01:50:49.591 --> 01:50:53.616
If your code is a complete visual
mess, or if it's crazy long,

01:50:53.616 --> 01:50:55.491
it's going to be really
hard for someone else

01:50:55.491 --> 01:50:59.121
to wrap their mind around what your code
is doing and, indeed, to be confident

01:50:59.121 --> 01:51:00.201
if it is correct.

01:51:00.201 --> 01:51:04.011
And honestly, you-- the
next morning, the next year,

01:51:04.011 --> 01:51:07.551
the next time you look at
that code-- might have no idea

01:51:07.551 --> 01:51:09.381
what you yourself were even thinking.

01:51:09.381 --> 01:51:13.731
But you will if you focus,
too, on designing good code,

01:51:13.731 --> 01:51:16.641
getting your algorithms efficient,
getting your code nice and clean,

01:51:16.641 --> 01:51:19.161
and even making sure your
code looks pretty, which

01:51:19.161 --> 01:51:20.881
we'd describe as a matter of style.

01:51:20.881 --> 01:51:24.514
So in the written human world, having
punctuation in the right place,

01:51:24.514 --> 01:51:27.181
capitalization and the like-- the
sort of way you write an essay

01:51:27.181 --> 01:51:29.221
but not necessarily
send a text message--

01:51:29.221 --> 01:51:31.421
relates to style, for instance.

01:51:31.421 --> 01:51:33.361
And so good style in
code is going to have

01:51:33.361 --> 01:51:36.961
a few of these characteristics that are
pretty easily taught and remembered.

01:51:36.961 --> 01:51:41.981
But you just have to start to get in the
habit of writing code in a certain way.

01:51:41.981 --> 01:51:44.941
So these three axes, so to speak,
correctness, design, and style,

01:51:44.941 --> 01:51:48.121
are really the overarching
goals when writing code that

01:51:48.121 --> 01:51:50.201
ultimately is going to look like this.

01:51:50.201 --> 01:51:52.201
So this program we
conjectured last week does

01:51:52.201 --> 01:51:56.864
what if you run it on a Mac or
PC or somewhere else, presumably?

01:51:56.864 --> 01:51:57.531
What does it do?

01:51:57.531 --> 01:51:59.301
Yeah?

01:51:59.301 --> 01:52:00.261
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:52:00.261 --> 01:52:02.136
DAVID J. MALAN: It just
prints, Hello, world.

01:52:02.136 --> 01:52:04.311
And honestly, that's kind
of atrocious that you

01:52:04.311 --> 01:52:08.061
need to hit your keyboard keys this
many times with this cryptic syntax just

01:52:08.061 --> 01:52:09.741
to get a program to say, Hello, world.

01:52:09.741 --> 01:52:11.991
So a spoiler-- in a
few weeks' time when we

01:52:11.991 --> 01:52:14.451
introduce other, more modern
languages, like Python,

01:52:14.451 --> 01:52:18.631
you can distill this same logic
into literally one line of code.

01:52:18.631 --> 01:52:20.301
And so we're getting there, ultimately.

01:52:20.301 --> 01:52:23.294
But it's helpful to understand
what it is that's going on here,

01:52:23.294 --> 01:52:25.461
because even though this
is a pretty cryptic syntax,

01:52:25.461 --> 01:52:28.101
there's nothing after this week and,
really, next week that you shouldn't

01:52:28.101 --> 01:52:30.921
be able to understand even about
something that right now looks

01:52:30.921 --> 01:52:32.311
a little something like this.

01:52:32.311 --> 01:52:33.644
So how do you write code?

01:52:33.644 --> 01:52:35.811
Well, I've given us sort
of the answer to a problem.

01:52:35.811 --> 01:52:37.728
How do you print, Hello,
world, on the screen?

01:52:37.728 --> 01:52:39.178
So what do I do with this code?

01:52:39.178 --> 01:52:42.261
Well, we're in the habit of typically
writing things with, like, Microsoft

01:52:42.261 --> 01:52:43.911
Word or Google documents.

01:52:43.911 --> 01:52:48.031
And yeah, I could open up Word or
Google Docs or Pages or the like

01:52:48.031 --> 01:52:50.781
and just literally transcribe
that character for character,

01:52:50.781 --> 01:52:53.391
save it, and boom, I've got a program.

01:52:53.391 --> 01:52:56.631
But the problem, per last week, is
that computers only understand or speak

01:52:56.631 --> 01:52:59.164
what other language, so to speak?

01:52:59.164 --> 01:53:00.081
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:53:00.081 --> 01:53:02.311
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so
binary, zeros and ones.

01:53:02.311 --> 01:53:04.311
And so this, obviously,
is not zeros and ones.

01:53:04.311 --> 01:53:07.041
So it doesn't matter if I put it in
a Word doc, Google Doc, Pages file,

01:53:07.041 --> 01:53:07.633
or the like.

01:53:07.633 --> 01:53:10.050
The computer is not going to
understand it until I somehow

01:53:10.050 --> 01:53:11.571
translate it to zeros and ones.

01:53:11.571 --> 01:53:14.121
And honestly, none of those
tools that I rattled off

01:53:14.121 --> 01:53:15.981
are really appropriate for programming.

01:53:15.981 --> 01:53:16.551
Why?

01:53:16.551 --> 01:53:19.011
Well, they come with features
like bold facing and italics

01:53:19.011 --> 01:53:22.881
and sort of fluffy, aesthetic stuff
that has no functional impact on what

01:53:22.881 --> 01:53:24.631
you're trying to do with your code.

01:53:24.631 --> 01:53:26.661
And they don't have
the ability, it would

01:53:26.661 --> 01:53:29.661
seem, to convert that code
ultimately to zeros and ones.

01:53:29.661 --> 01:53:32.451
But tools that do have
this capability might

01:53:32.451 --> 01:53:36.351
be called Integrated Development
Environments, or IDEs,

01:53:36.351 --> 01:53:38.181
or, more simply, text editors.

01:53:38.181 --> 01:53:42.561
A text editor is a tool that a
programmer uses perhaps every day

01:53:42.561 --> 01:53:44.031
to write their code.

01:53:44.031 --> 01:53:46.851
And it's a simple program-- here,
for instance, a very popular one

01:53:46.851 --> 01:53:49.543
called Visual Studio Code, or VS Code.

01:53:49.543 --> 01:53:51.501
And at the top here, you
see that I've actually

01:53:51.501 --> 01:53:56.391
created in advance before class a very
simple empty file called "hello.c."

01:53:56.391 --> 01:53:57.021
Why?

01:53:57.021 --> 01:54:00.741
Well, .c indicates by convention that
this is going to be a file in which

01:54:00.741 --> 01:54:01.641
there is C code.

01:54:01.641 --> 01:54:06.051
It's not .docx, which would mean in
this file is a Microsoft Word document,

01:54:06.051 --> 01:54:08.061
or .pages is a Pages file.

01:54:08.061 --> 01:54:12.621
This is .c, which means in this file is
going to be text in the language called

01:54:12.621 --> 01:54:16.071
C. This number 1 here is just an
automatic line number that's going help

01:54:16.071 --> 01:54:18.441
me keep track of how long
or short this program is.

01:54:18.441 --> 01:54:21.051
And the cursor is just
blinking there, waiting

01:54:21.051 --> 01:54:23.031
for me to start typing some code.

01:54:23.031 --> 01:54:26.031
Well, let me go ahead and type
out exactly the same code.

01:54:26.031 --> 01:54:28.261
For me, it comes pretty
comfortably from memory.

01:54:28.261 --> 01:54:31.791
So I'm going to go ahead and include
something called standardio.h--

01:54:31.791 --> 01:54:32.961
more on that later.

01:54:32.961 --> 01:54:37.221
I'm going to magically type int
main(void), whatever that means--

01:54:37.221 --> 01:54:38.611
we'll come back to that later--

01:54:38.611 --> 01:54:43.561
one of these curly braces and then a
sibling there that closes the same.

01:54:43.561 --> 01:54:46.611
Then I'm going to hit Tab
to indent a few spaces.

01:54:46.611 --> 01:54:52.851
And then I'm going to type not print,
but printf, then "Hello, world," /n,

01:54:52.851 --> 01:54:55.851
close quote, close
parenthesis, semicolon.

01:54:55.851 --> 01:55:00.051
And I dare say this was essentially
the very first program I wrote some

01:55:00.051 --> 01:55:01.220
25 years ago.

01:55:01.220 --> 01:55:03.291
I wrote it to say, "Hi, CS50."

01:55:03.291 --> 01:55:06.201
Now it just says the more canonical,
conventional, "Hello, world."

01:55:06.201 --> 01:55:07.100
But that's it.

01:55:07.100 --> 01:55:08.391
That's my very first program.

01:55:08.391 --> 01:55:11.241
And all I need to now do is
maybe hit Command-S or Control-S

01:55:11.241 --> 01:55:12.111
to save the file.

01:55:12.111 --> 01:55:15.081
And voila, I am a programmer.

01:55:15.081 --> 01:55:17.828
The catch though, is,
OK, how do I run this?

01:55:17.828 --> 01:55:19.911
Like, on your Mac or PC,
how do you run a program?

01:55:19.911 --> 01:55:21.411
Well, usually double-click an icon.

01:55:21.411 --> 01:55:23.121
On your phone, you tap an icon.

01:55:23.121 --> 01:55:26.811
In this environment that we're using
and that many programmers-- dare

01:55:26.811 --> 01:55:31.671
say most programmers-- use, you don't
have immediately a nice, pretty icon

01:55:31.671 --> 01:55:32.511
to double-click on.

01:55:32.511 --> 01:55:35.734
That's very user friendly,
but it's not very necessary.

01:55:35.734 --> 01:55:38.151
Especially when you get more
comfortable with programming,

01:55:38.151 --> 01:55:41.031
you're going to want to type commands
because it's just faster than pointing

01:55:41.031 --> 01:55:42.021
and clicking a mouse.

01:55:42.021 --> 01:55:43.854
And you're going to
want to automate things,

01:55:43.854 --> 01:55:46.791
which is a lot easier if it's all
command or text-based, as opposed

01:55:46.791 --> 01:55:49.131
to mouse and muscular movements.

01:55:49.131 --> 01:55:52.011
And so here I have my program.

01:55:52.011 --> 01:55:54.591
It lives in this file called "hello.c."

01:55:54.591 --> 01:55:58.131
I need to now convert it,
though, to zeros and ones.

01:55:58.131 --> 01:56:02.331
Well, how do I go about doing this,
and how am I going to get from this

01:56:02.331 --> 01:56:03.681
so-called code--

01:56:03.681 --> 01:56:06.471
or source code, as it's
conventionally called--

01:56:06.471 --> 01:56:11.041
to this, these zeros and ones that
we'll now start calling machine code.

01:56:11.041 --> 01:56:13.491
The zeros and ones from last
week can be used not only

01:56:13.491 --> 01:56:18.751
to represent numbers and letters,
colors, audio, video, and more.

01:56:18.751 --> 01:56:24.051
It can also represent instructions to a
computer, like print, or play a sound,

01:56:24.051 --> 01:56:26.091
or delete a file, or save a file.

01:56:26.091 --> 01:56:28.761
All the sort of basics
of a computer somehow

01:56:28.761 --> 01:56:32.361
can be represented by other
patterns of zeros and ones.

01:56:32.361 --> 01:56:34.934
And just like last week,
it depends on the context

01:56:34.934 --> 01:56:36.351
in which these numbers are stored.

01:56:36.351 --> 01:56:39.681
Sometimes they're interpreted as
numbers, like in a spreadsheet.

01:56:39.681 --> 01:56:41.391
Sometimes they're interpreted as colors.

01:56:41.391 --> 01:56:46.041
Sometimes they're interpreted as
instructions, commands to your computer

01:56:46.041 --> 01:56:50.821
to do very low-level operations,
like print something on the screen.

01:56:50.821 --> 01:56:55.611
So fortunately, last week's definition
of computer science of problem solving

01:56:55.611 --> 01:56:58.021
is a nice mental model for
exactly the goal at hand.

01:56:58.021 --> 01:57:00.801
I have some input, AKA source code.

01:57:00.801 --> 01:57:05.001
I want to output ultimately
machine code, those zeros and ones.

01:57:05.001 --> 01:57:07.581
I certainly don't want to do
this kind of process by hand.

01:57:07.581 --> 01:57:11.181
So hopefully there's an algorithm
implemented by some special program

01:57:11.181 --> 01:57:12.626
that does exactly that.

01:57:12.626 --> 01:57:14.751
And those of you who do
have some prior experience,

01:57:14.751 --> 01:57:16.947
this program might be called a?

01:57:16.947 --> 01:57:18.151
A compiler.

01:57:18.151 --> 01:57:20.151
So a few of you have,
indeed, programmed before.

01:57:20.151 --> 01:57:21.831
Not all languages use compilers.

01:57:21.831 --> 01:57:24.351
C, in fact, is a language
that does use a compiler.

01:57:24.351 --> 01:57:27.681
And so I just need to find myself--

01:57:27.681 --> 01:57:31.281
on my computer somewhere,
presumably-- a so-called compiler,

01:57:31.281 --> 01:57:35.731
a program whose purpose in life is
to convert one language to another.

01:57:35.731 --> 01:57:40.461
And source code written textually
in C, like we saw a moment ago,

01:57:40.461 --> 01:57:41.661
is source code.

01:57:41.661 --> 01:57:44.491
The machine code is the
corresponding zeros and ones.

01:57:44.491 --> 01:57:48.261
So let me go back to the same
programming environment called

01:57:48.261 --> 01:57:50.421
Visual Studio Code or VS Code.

01:57:50.421 --> 01:57:53.541
This is typically a program you
or any programmer on the internet

01:57:53.541 --> 01:57:57.351
can download onto their own Mac or
PC and be on their way with whatever

01:57:57.351 --> 01:57:59.751
computer you own writing some code.

01:57:59.751 --> 01:58:02.181
A downside, though, of that
approach is that all of us

01:58:02.181 --> 01:58:04.791
have slightly different
versions of Macs or PCs.

01:58:04.791 --> 01:58:07.371
We have slightly different
versions of operating systems.

01:58:07.371 --> 01:58:08.931
They may or may not be up to date.

01:58:08.931 --> 01:58:13.021
It's just a technical support nightmare
to create a uniform environment,

01:58:13.021 --> 01:58:15.831
especially for an introductory
class, where everyone should ideally

01:58:15.831 --> 01:58:19.201
be on the same page so we can
get you up and running quickly.

01:58:19.201 --> 01:58:23.181
And so I'm actually using a cloud-based
version of VS Code, something

01:58:23.181 --> 01:58:25.971
that you only need a browser to access.

01:58:25.971 --> 01:58:28.761
And then you can be on any
computer, today or tomorrow.

01:58:28.761 --> 01:58:32.341
By the end of the semester, we're
going to get you out of the cloud,

01:58:32.341 --> 01:58:36.021
so to speak, as best we can and
get you onto your own Mac or PC,

01:58:36.021 --> 01:58:39.531
so that after this class, especially if
it's the only CS class you ever take,

01:58:39.531 --> 01:58:43.131
you feel like you can continue
programming in any number of languages,

01:58:43.131 --> 01:58:45.271
even with CS50 behind you.

01:58:45.271 --> 01:58:47.961
But for now, wonderfully, the
browser version of VS Code

01:58:47.961 --> 01:58:51.793
should pretty much be identical
to what the eventual downloadable

01:58:51.793 --> 01:58:53.001
version of the same would be.

01:58:53.001 --> 01:58:55.521
And you'll see in problem
set 1 how to access this

01:58:55.521 --> 01:58:58.791
and how to get going yourself
with your first programs.

01:58:58.791 --> 01:59:01.641
But I haven't mentioned this
bottom part of the screen,

01:59:01.641 --> 01:59:03.141
this bottom part of the screen.

01:59:03.141 --> 01:59:06.571
And this is an area where we have
what's called a terminal window.

01:59:06.571 --> 01:59:10.401
So this is sort of old-school technology
that allows you, with a keyboard,

01:59:10.401 --> 01:59:15.381
to interact with a computer, wherever
it may be-- on your lap, in your pocket,

01:59:15.381 --> 01:59:17.601
or even, in this case, in the cloud.

01:59:17.601 --> 01:59:20.151
So on the top-hand
portion of this screen

01:59:20.151 --> 01:59:24.741
is my text editor, like tabbed
windows, like in many programs, where

01:59:24.741 --> 01:59:26.931
I can just create files and write code.

01:59:26.931 --> 01:59:30.051
The bottom of the screen here,
my so-called terminal window,

01:59:30.051 --> 01:59:33.141
gives me the ability to
run commands on a server

01:59:33.141 --> 01:59:35.601
that currently I have
exclusive access to.

01:59:35.601 --> 01:59:39.681
So because I logged into VS
Code with my account online,

01:59:39.681 --> 01:59:44.451
I have my own sort of virtual
server, if you will, in the cloud--

01:59:44.451 --> 01:59:46.701
otherwise known as, in
this context, a container.

01:59:46.701 --> 01:59:49.771
This has its own operating system
for me, its own hard drive,

01:59:49.771 --> 01:59:52.641
if you will, where I can save
and create files of my own,

01:59:52.641 --> 01:59:54.991
separate from yours and vice versa.

01:59:54.991 --> 01:59:57.381
And it's at this very
simple prompt, which

01:59:57.381 --> 02:00:00.218
is conventionally-- but not always--
abbreviated by a dollar sign,

02:00:00.218 --> 02:00:01.551
has nothing to do with currency.

02:00:01.551 --> 02:00:03.644
It just means, type your commands here.

02:00:03.644 --> 02:00:05.811
This is where I'm going to
be able to type commands,

02:00:05.811 --> 02:00:09.871
like compile my source
code into machine code.

02:00:09.871 --> 02:00:15.523
So it's a Command Line Interface, or
CLI, on top of an operating system

02:00:15.523 --> 02:00:18.231
that you might not have ever used
or seen, but it's very popular,

02:00:18.231 --> 02:00:19.221
called Linux.

02:00:19.221 --> 02:00:23.731
Odds are almost all of us in this room
are using Mac OS or Windows right now,

02:00:23.731 --> 02:00:26.991
but we're all going to start using an
operating system called Linux, which

02:00:26.991 --> 02:00:30.141
is in a family of operating systems
that offer not only this command

02:00:30.141 --> 02:00:33.231
line interface, but are used not
just for programming, but for serving

02:00:33.231 --> 02:00:35.851
websites and developing
applications and the like.

02:00:35.851 --> 02:00:40.291
And it's, indeed, a familiar and very
powerful interface, as we'll see.

02:00:40.291 --> 02:00:45.801
So how do I go about making this
file, hello.c, into a program?

02:00:45.801 --> 02:00:48.741
There's no icon to double-click,
but there is a command.

02:00:48.741 --> 02:00:54.141
I can type, make hello, at this dollar
sign prompt, go ahead and hit Enter,

02:00:54.141 --> 02:00:56.301
and nothing appears to happen.

02:00:56.301 --> 02:00:57.533
But that's a good thing.

02:00:57.533 --> 02:00:59.491
And as we'll see in
programming, almost always,

02:00:59.491 --> 02:01:02.661
if you don't see anything go wrong,
that means everything went right.

02:01:02.661 --> 02:01:04.401
So this is going to
be a rarity at first,

02:01:04.401 --> 02:01:07.491
but this is a good thing that
it just seems to do nothing.

02:01:07.491 --> 02:01:11.661
But now there is in the
folder in my accounts

02:01:11.661 --> 02:01:15.261
in this on the cloud
a file called "hello."

02:01:15.261 --> 02:01:18.771
And it's a bit of a weird command,
but you'll get familiar with it

02:01:18.771 --> 02:01:19.581
before long.

02:01:19.581 --> 02:01:22.581
. just means go into my current folder.

02:01:22.581 --> 02:01:28.371
/hello means run the program called
"hello" in this current folder.

02:01:28.371 --> 02:01:32.871
So ./hello, and then Enter, and voila,
now I'm actually not just programming,

02:01:32.871 --> 02:01:35.791
but running my actual code.

02:01:35.791 --> 02:01:38.301
So what have I just done?

02:01:38.301 --> 02:01:40.111
Let me go ahead and do this.

02:01:40.111 --> 02:01:42.771
I'm going to go ahead and open
up the sidebar of this program,

02:01:42.771 --> 02:01:44.911
and you'll see in problem
set 1 how to do this.

02:01:44.911 --> 02:01:48.051
And this might look a little different
based on your own configuration.

02:01:48.051 --> 02:01:51.191
Even the color scheme I'm using might
ultimately look different from yours,

02:01:51.191 --> 02:01:52.941
because it supports a
nice colorful theme.

02:01:52.941 --> 02:01:56.721
So you can have different colors
and brightnesses depending

02:01:56.721 --> 02:01:58.251
on your mood or the time of day.

02:01:58.251 --> 02:02:01.641
What I've opened here, though, is
what is called in VS Code Explorer,

02:02:01.641 --> 02:02:04.279
and this is just all of the
files in my cloud account.

02:02:04.279 --> 02:02:05.571
And there's not many right now.

02:02:05.571 --> 02:02:06.576
There's only two.

02:02:06.576 --> 02:02:09.081
One is the file called
hello.c, and it's highlighted

02:02:09.081 --> 02:02:10.851
because I've got it open right there.

02:02:10.851 --> 02:02:14.331
And the other is a file called
"hello," which is brand new

02:02:14.331 --> 02:02:17.331
and was created when I ran that command.

02:02:17.331 --> 02:02:21.431
And what's now worth noting is that
now things are getting a little more

02:02:21.431 --> 02:02:22.781
like Mac OS and Windows.

02:02:22.781 --> 02:02:26.141
Like on the left-hand side, you have
a GUI, a Graphical User Interface.

02:02:26.141 --> 02:02:30.276
But on the bottom here, again, you
have a CLI, Command Line Interface.

02:02:30.276 --> 02:02:32.651
These are just different ways
to interact with computers,

02:02:32.651 --> 02:02:34.193
and you'll get comfortable with both.

02:02:34.193 --> 02:02:37.451
And honestly, you're certainly familiar
and comfortable with GUIs already,

02:02:37.451 --> 02:02:40.911
so it's the command line one
with which we'll spend some time.

02:02:40.911 --> 02:02:43.601
Now suppose that I just
wanted to do something

02:02:43.601 --> 02:02:45.439
more than compile this program.

02:02:45.439 --> 02:02:47.231
Suppose I wanted to go
ahead and remove it.

02:02:47.231 --> 02:02:48.701
Like, uh-uh, no, I made a mistake.

02:02:48.701 --> 02:02:51.071
I want to say, "Hello,
CS50," not "Hello, world."

02:02:51.071 --> 02:02:55.121
I could just hover up here, like in
any software, and I could right-click,

02:02:55.121 --> 02:02:57.881
and I could poke around, and
there, delete permanently.

02:02:57.881 --> 02:03:00.371
So most of us might have
that instinct on a Mac or PC.

02:03:00.371 --> 02:03:02.801
You right-click or Control-click,
and you poke around.

02:03:02.801 --> 02:03:05.951
But in a command line interface,
let me do this instead.

02:03:05.951 --> 02:03:08.441
The command for removing
or deleting a file

02:03:08.441 --> 02:03:12.101
in the world of Linux, this
other operating system,

02:03:12.101 --> 02:03:16.331
is just a type rm for remove,
and then "hello," Enter.

02:03:16.331 --> 02:03:19.091
It's a somewhat cryptic confirmation
message, but this just means,

02:03:19.091 --> 02:03:19.961
are you sure?

02:03:19.961 --> 02:03:21.761
I'm going to go ahead
and type Y for Yes.

02:03:21.761 --> 02:03:24.191
And now when I hit
Enter, watch what happens

02:03:24.191 --> 02:03:28.241
at top left in the Explorer, the
GUI, the graphical interface.

02:03:28.241 --> 02:03:30.790
Voila, it disappears.

02:03:30.790 --> 02:03:32.981
Not terribly exciting,
but this just means

02:03:32.981 --> 02:03:35.540
this is a graphical version
of what we're seeing here.

02:03:35.540 --> 02:03:38.800
And in fact, if you want to
never use the GUI again--

02:03:38.800 --> 02:03:41.691
I'll go ahead and close it
with a keyboard shortcut here--

02:03:41.691 --> 02:03:45.431
you can forever just type
ls for list and hit Enter.

02:03:45.431 --> 02:03:48.101
And you will see in the
command line interface

02:03:48.101 --> 02:03:50.618
all of the files in your current folder.

02:03:50.618 --> 02:03:52.451
So anything you can do
with a mouse, you can

02:03:52.451 --> 02:03:54.040
do with this command line interface.

02:03:54.040 --> 02:03:57.411
And indeed, we'll see many more
things that you can do as well.

02:03:57.411 --> 02:04:01.091
But the inventors of this, this
operating system and its predecessors,

02:04:01.091 --> 02:04:02.291
were very succinct.

02:04:02.291 --> 02:04:04.361
Like, the command is rm for remove.

02:04:04.361 --> 02:04:06.131
The command is ls for list.

02:04:06.131 --> 02:04:06.891
It's very terse.

02:04:06.891 --> 02:04:07.391
Why?

02:04:07.391 --> 02:04:10.481
Because it's just faster to type.

02:04:10.481 --> 02:04:14.261
So before we forge ahead with making
something more interesting than

02:04:14.261 --> 02:04:16.181
just "Hello, world,"
let me pause here to see

02:04:16.181 --> 02:04:19.421
if there's questions on
source code or machine

02:04:19.421 --> 02:04:24.221
code or compiler or this
command line interface.

02:04:24.221 --> 02:04:25.094
Yeah?

02:04:25.094 --> 02:04:26.629
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:04:26.629 --> 02:04:28.921
DAVID J. MALAN: Really good
question, and let me recap.

02:04:28.921 --> 02:04:30.751
If I were to make
changes to the program,

02:04:30.751 --> 02:04:33.871
run it, and then maybe make other
changes and try to rerun it,

02:04:33.871 --> 02:04:37.028
would those changes be reflected,
even though I've reworded slightly.

02:04:37.028 --> 02:04:37.861
Well, let's do this.

02:04:37.861 --> 02:04:39.961
I already removed the old version.

02:04:39.961 --> 02:04:43.531
So let me go ahead and point
out that if I do ./hello now,

02:04:43.531 --> 02:04:47.761
I'm going to see some kind of error
because I just deleted the file.

02:04:47.761 --> 02:04:50.431
No such file or directory, so
it's not terribly user friendly,

02:04:50.431 --> 02:04:51.961
but it's saying what the problem is.

02:04:51.961 --> 02:04:55.231
Let me go ahead and remake
it by typing make hello.

02:04:55.231 --> 02:04:59.911
Now if I type ls, I'll see not one
but two files again, and one of them

02:04:59.911 --> 02:05:03.616
is even green with a little asterisk
to indicate that it's executable.

02:05:03.616 --> 02:05:05.491
It's sort of the textual
version of something

02:05:05.491 --> 02:05:07.251
you could double-click
in our human world.

02:05:07.251 --> 02:05:10.501
So now, of course, if I run hello, we're
back where I started, "Hello, world."

02:05:10.501 --> 02:05:15.811
But now suppose I change it to
"Hello, CS50," like I did years ago.

02:05:15.811 --> 02:05:21.001
Let me go ahead and save the file with
Command-S or Control-S. Down here now,

02:05:21.001 --> 02:05:23.681
let me run ./hello again, and voila.

02:05:23.681 --> 02:05:25.171
Huh.

02:05:25.171 --> 02:05:27.431
So let me ask someone else
to answer that question.

02:05:27.431 --> 02:05:29.011
What's the missing step?

02:05:29.011 --> 02:05:31.051
Why did it not say, "Hello, CS50."

02:05:31.051 --> 02:05:32.254
Yeah?

02:05:32.254 --> 02:05:33.171
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:05:33.171 --> 02:05:35.241
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so
I didn't compile it again.

02:05:35.241 --> 02:05:38.421
So sort of newbie mistake, you're going
to make this mistake and many others

02:05:38.421 --> 02:05:38.991
before long.

02:05:38.991 --> 02:05:42.051
But now let me go ahead
and remake hello, enter.

02:05:42.051 --> 02:05:45.171
It's going to seemingly
make the same program.

02:05:45.171 --> 02:05:49.431
But this time when I run
it, it's, "Hello, CS50."

02:05:49.431 --> 02:05:53.181
Any other questions on some
of these building blocks?

02:05:53.181 --> 02:05:56.581
And we'll come back to all the
crazy syntax I typed before long.

02:05:56.581 --> 02:05:58.881
But for now, we're focusing
on just the output.

02:05:58.881 --> 02:05:59.831
Yeah?

02:05:59.831 --> 02:06:00.853
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:06:00.853 --> 02:06:02.561
DAVID J. MALAN: When
I keep running make,

02:06:02.561 --> 02:06:05.511
it creates a new version
of the machine code.

02:06:05.511 --> 02:06:09.491
So it keeps changing the hello program
and the hello file, and that's it.

02:06:09.491 --> 02:06:12.599
There's no make file, per se.

02:06:12.599 --> 02:06:13.634
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:06:13.634 --> 02:06:15.051
DAVID J. MALAN: Good question, no.

02:06:15.051 --> 02:06:18.141
If I open up that directory, you'll
see that there's just the one.

02:06:18.141 --> 02:06:21.231
And it doesn't matter how
many times I run make hello--

02:06:21.231 --> 02:06:25.251
three, four, five-- it just
keeps overwriting the original.

02:06:25.251 --> 02:06:28.753
So it's kind of like just saving in
the world of Google Docs or Microsoft

02:06:28.753 --> 02:06:29.461
Word or the like.

02:06:29.461 --> 02:06:31.003
But there's an additional step today.

02:06:31.003 --> 02:06:36.281
We have to then convert my words to
the computer's, the zeros and ones.

02:06:36.281 --> 02:06:37.594
Yeah, in front.

02:06:37.594 --> 02:06:38.541
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:06:38.541 --> 02:06:40.731
DAVID J. MALAN: Oh, what
happens if I run hello.c?

02:06:40.731 --> 02:06:45.081
So let me go ahead and do ./hello.c,
which is a mistake you'll invariably

02:06:45.081 --> 02:06:46.521
make early on.

02:06:46.521 --> 02:06:47.821
Permission denied.

02:06:47.821 --> 02:06:48.801
So what does that mean?

02:06:48.801 --> 02:06:50.961
This is where the error
messages mean something

02:06:50.961 --> 02:06:53.661
to the people who designed the operating
system, but it's a little cryptic.

02:06:53.661 --> 02:06:55.701
It's not that you don't
have access to the file.

02:06:55.701 --> 02:06:57.411
It means that it's not executable.

02:06:57.411 --> 02:07:00.201
This is not something you
have permission to run,

02:07:00.201 --> 02:07:04.743
but you do have permission to read
or write it-- that is, change it.

02:07:04.743 --> 02:07:06.021
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

02:07:06.521 --> 02:07:08.229
DAVID J. MALAN: Oh,
really good question.

02:07:08.229 --> 02:07:11.801
So if I have named my file, hello
dot C, or more generally something

02:07:11.801 --> 02:07:15.761
dot C, of the things that
Make does is it automatically

02:07:15.761 --> 02:07:17.381
picks the file name for me.

02:07:17.381 --> 02:07:18.911
And we'll discuss a bit--

02:07:18.911 --> 02:07:20.711
we'll discuss this a bit more next week.

02:07:20.711 --> 02:07:23.711
Make itself-- is kind of the
first of white lies today--

02:07:23.711 --> 02:07:25.481
itself is not a compiler.

02:07:25.481 --> 02:07:30.821
It's a program that knows how to find
and use the compiler on the system

02:07:30.821 --> 02:07:33.161
and automatically create the program.

02:07:33.161 --> 02:07:37.091
If I use, as we'll discuss next
week, the actual compiler myself,

02:07:37.091 --> 02:07:41.081
I have to type a much longer sequence
of commands to specify explicitly

02:07:41.081 --> 02:07:43.271
what do I want the name
of my program to be.

02:07:43.271 --> 02:07:45.311
Make is a nice program,
especially in week 1,

02:07:45.311 --> 02:07:47.661
because it just automates
all of that for us.

02:07:47.661 --> 02:07:51.221
And so here, we have now a
program that very simply prints

02:07:51.221 --> 02:07:52.221
something on the screen.

02:07:52.221 --> 02:07:54.791
So let's not put this
into the context of where

02:07:54.791 --> 02:07:58.871
we left off last time in the context
of Scratch and inputs and outputs.

02:07:58.871 --> 02:08:01.961
So we discuss the last time, of
course, functions and arguments.

02:08:01.961 --> 02:08:07.011
Functions, again, are those actions and
verbs like say, or ask, or the like.

02:08:07.011 --> 02:08:09.403
And the arguments were the
inputs to those functions,

02:08:09.403 --> 02:08:11.861
generally in those little white
ovals that, in Scratch, you

02:08:11.861 --> 02:08:13.961
could type words or numbers into.

02:08:13.961 --> 02:08:16.961
We'll see, in all of the languages
we're going to see this term,

02:08:16.961 --> 02:08:18.283
have that same capability.

02:08:18.283 --> 02:08:20.991
And let's just start to translate
one of these things to another.

02:08:20.991 --> 02:08:24.911
So for instance, let's put
this same program in C,

02:08:24.911 --> 02:08:26.111
in the context of Scratch.

02:08:26.111 --> 02:08:29.621
This is what Hello, World looked like
last week in the form of one function.

02:08:29.621 --> 02:08:32.531
This week, of course,
it looks like print.

02:08:32.531 --> 02:08:34.991
And then the parentheses,
notice, are kind of

02:08:34.991 --> 02:08:38.679
deliberately designed in the world of
Scratch to resemble that same shape.

02:08:38.679 --> 02:08:40.721
Even though this is a
white oval, you kind of get

02:08:40.721 --> 02:08:45.401
that it's kind of evoking that
same idea with the parentheses.

02:08:45.401 --> 02:08:49.181
Technically the function
in C, it's not called say.

02:08:49.181 --> 02:08:50.471
It's not even called print.

02:08:50.471 --> 02:08:51.671
It's called printf.

02:08:51.671 --> 02:08:55.061
The F stands for formatted, but we'll
see what that means in a moment.

02:08:55.061 --> 02:08:57.611
But printf is the closest
analogous function

02:08:57.611 --> 02:09:00.881
for say in the world of
C. Notice if, though, you

02:09:00.881 --> 02:09:05.501
want to print something like
Hello, World or Hello CS50 in C,

02:09:05.501 --> 02:09:08.981
you don't just write the
words as we did last week.

02:09:08.981 --> 02:09:11.411
You also had an add what,
if you notice already

02:09:11.411 --> 02:09:13.361
what's missing from this version.

02:09:13.361 --> 02:09:15.681
Yeah, so the double quotes
on the left and the right.

02:09:15.681 --> 02:09:20.999
So, that's necessary in C whenever
you have a string of words.

02:09:20.999 --> 02:09:22.541
And I'm using that word deliberately.

02:09:22.541 --> 02:09:26.831
Whenever you have multiple words
like this, this is known as a string

02:09:26.831 --> 02:09:27.461
as we'll see.

02:09:27.461 --> 02:09:30.971
And you have to put it in double
quotes, not single quotes.

02:09:30.971 --> 02:09:32.681
You have to put it in double quotes.

02:09:32.681 --> 02:09:36.881
There's one other stupid thing
that we need to have in my C code

02:09:36.881 --> 02:09:40.971
in order to get this function to do
something ultimately, which is what?

02:09:40.971 --> 02:09:41.841
Semicolon.

02:09:41.841 --> 02:09:44.241
So just like in our human
world, you eventually

02:09:44.241 --> 02:09:47.271
got into the habit of using, at
least in formal writing, periods.

02:09:47.271 --> 02:09:50.691
Semicolon is generally what
you use to finish your thought

02:09:50.691 --> 02:09:53.781
in the world of programming with C.

02:09:53.781 --> 02:09:55.911
All right, so we have
that function in place.

02:09:55.911 --> 02:09:59.661
Now, what does this really fit
into in terms of the mental model?

02:09:59.661 --> 02:10:01.071
Well, functions take arguments.

02:10:01.071 --> 02:10:04.761
And it turns out functions can
have different types of outputs.

02:10:04.761 --> 02:10:07.071
And we've actually seen
both already last week.

02:10:07.071 --> 02:10:11.361
One type of output from a function
can be something called a side effect.

02:10:11.361 --> 02:10:13.431
And it generally refers
to something visual,

02:10:13.431 --> 02:10:17.571
like something appearing on the screen
or a sound playing from your computer.

02:10:17.571 --> 02:10:20.211
It's sort of a side effect of
the function doing its thing.

02:10:20.211 --> 02:10:24.231
And indeed, last week we saw this in
the context of passing in something

02:10:24.231 --> 02:10:27.181
like Hello, World as
input to the say function.

02:10:27.181 --> 02:10:31.041
And we saw on the screen Hello,
World, but it was kind of a one off.

02:10:31.041 --> 02:10:32.931
It's one and done.

02:10:32.931 --> 02:10:35.841
You can't actually do anything
with that visual output

02:10:35.841 --> 02:10:38.581
other than consume it,
visually, with your human eyes.

02:10:38.581 --> 02:10:43.251
But sometimes, recall last week, we
had functions like the ask block that

02:10:43.251 --> 02:10:44.961
actually returned me some value.

02:10:44.961 --> 02:10:47.001
Remember the ask, what's your name.

02:10:47.001 --> 02:10:50.684
It handed me back whatever
answer the human typed in.

02:10:50.684 --> 02:10:52.851
It didn't just arbitrarily
display it on the screen.

02:10:52.851 --> 02:10:55.191
The cat didn't necessarily
say it on the screen.

02:10:55.191 --> 02:11:01.641
It was stored, instead, in that special
variable that was called answer.

02:11:01.641 --> 02:11:05.511
Because some functions have not
side effects but return values.

02:11:05.511 --> 02:11:09.321
They hand you back an output
that you can use and reuse,

02:11:09.321 --> 02:11:11.961
unlike the side effect, which,
again displays and that's it.

02:11:11.961 --> 02:11:14.461
You can't sort of catch
it and hold on to it.

02:11:14.461 --> 02:11:17.601
So, in the context of last
week, we had the ask block.

02:11:17.601 --> 02:11:20.271
And that had this special
answer return value.

02:11:20.271 --> 02:11:22.971
In C, we're going to
see in just a moment,

02:11:22.971 --> 02:11:25.401
we could translate this as follows.

02:11:25.401 --> 02:11:29.001
The closest match I can
propose for the ask block

02:11:29.001 --> 02:11:31.581
is a function that we're going
to start calling get string.

02:11:31.581 --> 02:11:34.731
String is, again, a word, a
set of words, like a phrase

02:11:34.731 --> 02:11:36.711
or a sentence in programming.

02:11:36.711 --> 02:11:41.331
It, too, is a function insofar as
it takes input and pretty much--

02:11:41.331 --> 02:11:43.551
this isn't always true--
but very often when

02:11:43.551 --> 02:11:48.261
you have a word in C followed by an open
parenthesis and a closed parenthesis,

02:11:48.261 --> 02:11:50.961
it's most likely the name of a function.

02:11:50.961 --> 02:11:53.461
And we're going to see that
there's some exceptions to that.

02:11:53.461 --> 02:11:55.791
But for now this indeed
looks like a function

02:11:55.791 --> 02:11:57.201
because it matches that pattern.

02:11:57.201 --> 02:12:00.669
If I want to ask the question,
what's your name, question mark--

02:12:00.669 --> 02:12:03.711
and I'm even going to deliberately
put a space there just to kind of move

02:12:03.711 --> 02:12:07.161
the cursor a little bit over so that
the human isn't typing literally

02:12:07.161 --> 02:12:08.191
after the question mark.

02:12:08.191 --> 02:12:10.201
So that's just the nitpicky aesthetic.

02:12:10.201 --> 02:12:14.481
This is perhaps the closest analog
to just asking that question.

02:12:14.481 --> 02:12:18.681
But because the ask
block returns a value,

02:12:18.681 --> 02:12:22.021
the analog here forget string is
that it, too, returns a value.

02:12:22.021 --> 02:12:23.961
It doesn't just print the human's input.

02:12:23.961 --> 02:12:28.131
It hands it back to you in the form
of a variable, a.k.a. return value,

02:12:28.131 --> 02:12:30.501
that I can then use and reuse.

02:12:30.501 --> 02:12:33.471
Now ideally it would be as
simple as this literally

02:12:33.471 --> 02:12:36.891
saying answer on the left equals.

02:12:36.891 --> 02:12:38.781
And this is where
things start to diverge

02:12:38.781 --> 02:12:40.641
from math and sort of our human world.

02:12:40.641 --> 02:12:44.211
This equal sign, henceforth,
is not the equal sign.

02:12:44.211 --> 02:12:46.521
It is the assignment operator.

02:12:46.521 --> 02:12:50.031
To assign a value means to
store a value in some variable.

02:12:50.031 --> 02:12:53.641
And you read these things,
weirdly, right to left.

02:12:53.641 --> 02:12:55.821
So here is a function called get string.

02:12:55.821 --> 02:12:58.311
I claim that it's going
to return to you whatever

02:12:58.311 --> 02:13:00.411
the human types in as their name.

02:13:00.411 --> 02:13:03.321
It's going to get stored
over here on the left because

02:13:03.321 --> 02:13:06.331
of this so-called assignment
operator, that yes is an equal sign.

02:13:06.331 --> 02:13:08.581
But it doesn't mean
equality in this context.

02:13:08.581 --> 02:13:10.171
It makes things equal.

02:13:10.171 --> 02:13:14.961
But it does so by copying the value on
the right into the thing on the left.

02:13:14.961 --> 02:13:16.941
Unfortunately, we're not
quite done yet with C.

02:13:16.941 --> 02:13:19.671
And this is where, again, it
gets a little annoying at first

02:13:19.671 --> 02:13:23.781
where Scratch just let us express
our ideas without so much syntax.

02:13:23.781 --> 02:13:27.111
In C when you have a
variable you don't just

02:13:27.111 --> 02:13:29.001
give it a name like you did in Scratch.

02:13:29.001 --> 02:13:33.051
You also have to tell the computer
in advance what type of value

02:13:33.051 --> 02:13:34.311
it is storing.

02:13:34.311 --> 02:13:37.341
String is one such type of value.

02:13:37.341 --> 02:13:40.071
Int, for integer, is
going to be another.

02:13:40.071 --> 02:13:42.861
And there's even more than that
we'll see today and beyond.

02:13:42.861 --> 02:13:46.071
And this is partly an answer to the
question that came up one or more times

02:13:46.071 --> 02:13:49.971
last week, which was how does a computer
distinguish this pattern of zeros

02:13:49.971 --> 02:13:51.321
and ones from this.

02:13:51.321 --> 02:13:55.518
Like is this a letter, a number,
a color, a piece of video.

02:13:55.518 --> 02:13:58.351
And I just claimed last week that
it totally depends on the program.

02:13:58.351 --> 02:13:59.434
It depends on the context.

02:13:59.434 --> 02:14:00.871
And that's true.

02:14:00.871 --> 02:14:04.191
But within those
programs, it often depends

02:14:04.191 --> 02:14:08.811
on what the human programmer
said the type of the value is.

02:14:08.811 --> 02:14:10.851
If this specifies that
the string, which means

02:14:10.851 --> 02:14:12.891
interpret the following
zeros and ones that

02:14:12.891 --> 02:14:16.641
are stored in my program as
words or letters, more generally.

02:14:16.641 --> 02:14:20.961
If it's an int for integer, it would
be implying, by the programmer,

02:14:20.961 --> 02:14:24.981
treat the following zeros and
ones in my program as a number,

02:14:24.981 --> 02:14:26.791
an integer, not a string.

02:14:26.791 --> 02:14:29.661
So here's where this
week, unlike with Scratch,

02:14:29.661 --> 02:14:33.081
which is kind of figures out what you
mean, with C in a lot of languages

02:14:33.081 --> 02:14:36.201
you have to be this pedantic
and tell it what you mean.

02:14:36.201 --> 02:14:39.721
There's still one stupid thing
missing from my code here.

02:14:39.721 --> 02:14:40.951
What's still missing here?

02:14:40.951 --> 02:14:41.251
Yeah.

02:14:41.251 --> 02:14:42.151
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:14:42.151 --> 02:14:44.231
DAVID J. MALAN: And we still
need the stupid semicolon.

02:14:44.231 --> 02:14:45.648
And I'm sort of impugning it here.

02:14:45.648 --> 02:14:48.061
Because honestly, these are
the kinds of stupid mistakes

02:14:48.061 --> 02:14:50.701
you're going to make today,
tomorrow, this weekend, next week,

02:14:50.701 --> 02:14:54.481
a few weeks from now, until you
start to notice this and recognize it

02:14:54.481 --> 02:14:58.441
as well as you do English or
whatever your spoken language is.

02:14:58.441 --> 02:14:59.928
Yeah, question.

02:14:59.928 --> 02:15:00.511
Good question.

02:15:00.511 --> 02:15:03.031
Suppose I mix apples and
oranges, so to speak,

02:15:03.031 --> 02:15:06.421
and I try to put a string in
an int or an int in a string,

02:15:06.421 --> 02:15:08.581
the compiler is going to complain.

02:15:08.581 --> 02:15:10.841
So when I run that make
command as I did earlier,

02:15:10.841 --> 02:15:14.893
it's not going to be nice and blissfully
quiet and just give me another prompt.

02:15:14.893 --> 02:15:17.101
It's going to yell at me
with honestly a very cryptic

02:15:17.101 --> 02:15:20.351
looking error message until we get
the muscle memory for reading it.

02:15:20.351 --> 02:15:23.101
Other questions.

02:15:23.101 --> 02:15:24.991
Ah, what happened to the backslash n.

02:15:24.991 --> 02:15:26.821
So, we'll come back to that
in just a moment, if we may.

02:15:26.821 --> 02:15:29.779
Because I have deliberately omitted
it here but we did have it earlier.

02:15:29.779 --> 02:15:32.311
And we'll see the different
behavior in a sec.

02:15:32.311 --> 02:15:36.061
Other questions.

02:15:36.061 --> 02:15:37.471
Yeah, not at all nitpicky.

02:15:37.471 --> 02:15:39.721
These are the kinds of
things that just matter.

02:15:39.721 --> 02:15:43.711
And it's going to take time to recognize
and develop this muscle memory.

02:15:43.711 --> 02:15:48.306
Everything I've typed here except,
for the W at the moment, is lowercase.

02:15:48.306 --> 02:15:50.431
And the W is capitalized
just because it's English.

02:15:50.431 --> 02:15:51.901
Everything else is lowercase.

02:15:51.901 --> 02:15:54.511
And this kind of varies by
language and also context.

02:15:54.511 --> 02:15:58.621
So, in many languages the convention
is to use all lowercase letters

02:15:58.621 --> 02:16:00.061
for your variable names.

02:16:00.061 --> 02:16:02.441
Other languages might use
some capitals, as well.

02:16:02.441 --> 02:16:04.051
But we'll talk about that before long.

02:16:04.051 --> 02:16:05.951
But this is the kind
of thing that matters

02:16:05.951 --> 02:16:09.481
and is hard to see at first, especially
when a little S doesn't look that

02:16:09.481 --> 02:16:12.931
different when it's on your tiny
laptop screen from a capital S.

02:16:12.931 --> 02:16:15.851
But you'll start to
develop these instincts.

02:16:15.851 --> 02:16:18.061
All right, so besides
this particular block,

02:16:18.061 --> 02:16:22.181
let's go ahead and consider how we can
go about implementing this now in code.

02:16:22.181 --> 02:16:24.301
So let me switch back to VS Code here.

02:16:24.301 --> 02:16:26.081
This was the program I had earlier.

02:16:26.081 --> 02:16:28.891
And let me go ahead and
undo my CS50 change.

02:16:28.891 --> 02:16:30.721
And this time just rerun it.

02:16:30.721 --> 02:16:35.251
Rerun Make on Hello with the original
version with the backslash n.

02:16:35.251 --> 02:16:37.231
Enter, nothing bad
seems to have happened.

02:16:37.231 --> 02:16:40.741
So dot slash Hello, enter Hello, World.

02:16:40.741 --> 02:16:43.141
Now, if you're curious,
this is a good instinct

02:16:43.141 --> 02:16:45.559
to start to acquire what
happens if I get rid of this.

02:16:45.559 --> 02:16:47.851
Well, I'm probably not going
to break things too badly.

02:16:47.851 --> 02:16:48.781
So let's try.

02:16:48.781 --> 02:16:51.241
Let me go ahead now and do Make Hello.

02:16:51.241 --> 02:16:52.331
Still compile.

02:16:52.331 --> 02:16:53.981
So it's not a really bad mistake.

02:16:53.981 --> 02:16:56.701
So let me go ahead and
run dot slash Hello.

02:16:56.701 --> 02:16:58.921
What's the difference here?

02:16:58.921 --> 02:17:01.561
Yeah, what do you see that's different?

02:17:01.561 --> 02:17:05.091
Yeah, the dollar sign, my so-called
prompt, stayed on the same line.

02:17:05.091 --> 02:17:05.631
Why?

02:17:05.631 --> 02:17:08.781
Well, we can presumably
infer now that the backslash

02:17:08.781 --> 02:17:12.531
n is some fancy notation for
saying create a new line,

02:17:12.531 --> 02:17:14.881
move the cursor, so to
speak, to the next line.

02:17:14.881 --> 02:17:18.771
Notice that the cursor will move to
the next line in my terminal window.

02:17:18.771 --> 02:17:20.781
If I keep hitting it,
it just automatically,

02:17:20.781 --> 02:17:22.371
by nature of hitting enter, does it.

02:17:22.371 --> 02:17:25.311
But it'd be kind of stupid if when
you run a program in this world,

02:17:25.311 --> 02:17:28.311
simple as it is, if
the next command is now

02:17:28.311 --> 02:17:31.771
weirdly spaced in the middle of
the terminal with the dollar sign,

02:17:31.771 --> 02:17:33.111
it just looks sloppy.

02:17:33.111 --> 02:17:35.061
It's really just an aesthetic argument.

02:17:35.061 --> 02:17:40.941
And notice that it's not acceptable or
correct to do this, to hit enter there.

02:17:40.941 --> 02:17:43.441
Let me go ahead and save that,
though, and see what happens.

02:17:43.441 --> 02:17:47.001
Let me go ahead now and
run Make Hello enter.

02:17:47.001 --> 02:17:49.161
Oh my god, like four errors.

02:17:49.161 --> 02:17:53.391
This is like, what, 10 lines of
errors for a one line program.

02:17:53.391 --> 02:17:56.391
And this is where, again, you'll start
to develop the instincts for just

02:17:56.391 --> 02:17:57.381
reading this stuff.

02:17:57.381 --> 02:18:00.531
These kinds of tools, like
the compiler tool we're using,

02:18:00.531 --> 02:18:03.861
were not designed necessarily
with user friendliness in mind.

02:18:03.861 --> 02:18:06.081
That's changed over the
decades, but certainly early

02:18:06.081 --> 02:18:09.811
on it's really just meant to be
correct and precise with its errors.

02:18:09.811 --> 02:18:11.151
So what did I do here?

02:18:11.151 --> 02:18:13.941
Missing terminating
close quote character,

02:18:13.941 --> 02:18:17.061
long story short, when
you have a string in C,

02:18:17.061 --> 02:18:20.511
your double quotes just have to
be on the same line just because.

02:18:20.511 --> 02:18:22.049
Now, there's the slight white lie.

02:18:22.049 --> 02:18:23.091
There's ways around this.

02:18:23.091 --> 02:18:29.631
But the best way around it is to
use this so-called escape sequence.

02:18:29.631 --> 02:18:32.391
To escape something means generally
to put a backslash, and then

02:18:32.391 --> 02:18:34.711
a special symbol like n for new line.

02:18:34.711 --> 02:18:39.511
And this is just the agreed upon way
that humans, decades ago, decided,

02:18:39.511 --> 02:18:42.651
OK you don't just hit your enter key.

02:18:42.651 --> 02:18:46.941
You instead put backslash n
and that tells the computer

02:18:46.941 --> 02:18:49.471
to move the cursor to the new line.

02:18:49.471 --> 02:18:50.978
So again, kind of cryptic.

02:18:50.978 --> 02:18:52.311
But once you know it, that's it.

02:18:52.311 --> 02:18:55.471
It's just another word
in our vocabulary.

02:18:55.471 --> 02:18:58.551
So now let me transition to making
my program a little more interactive.

02:18:58.551 --> 02:19:00.141
Instead of just saying
Hello, world, let me

02:19:00.141 --> 02:19:02.016
change it like last week
to say Hello, David,

02:19:02.016 --> 02:19:04.321
or whoever is interacting
with the program.

02:19:04.321 --> 02:19:07.851
So I'm going to do string
answer gets, get string,

02:19:07.851 --> 02:19:11.256
quote unquote, what's your name.

02:19:11.256 --> 02:19:13.131
I'm not going to bother
with a new line here.

02:19:13.131 --> 02:19:13.731
I could.

02:19:13.731 --> 02:19:15.181
This is now just a judgment call.

02:19:15.181 --> 02:19:17.889
I deliberately want the human to
type their name on the same line

02:19:17.889 --> 02:19:18.951
just because.

02:19:18.951 --> 02:19:20.791
And how do I now print this?

02:19:20.791 --> 02:19:22.971
Well last week recall we used say.

02:19:22.971 --> 02:19:25.251
And then we use the
other block called join.

02:19:25.251 --> 02:19:27.681
So the idea here is the same.

02:19:27.681 --> 02:19:30.181
But the syntax this week is
going to be a little different.

02:19:30.181 --> 02:19:33.871
It's going to be printf, which
prints something on the screen.

02:19:33.871 --> 02:19:38.091
I'm going to go ahead
and say Hello comma.

02:19:38.091 --> 02:19:43.071
And let me just go with this initially
with the backslash n, semicolon.

02:19:43.071 --> 02:19:46.701
Let me go ahead and recompile my code.

02:19:46.701 --> 02:19:50.293
Whoops, damn doesn't work still.

02:19:50.293 --> 02:19:51.501
And look at all these errors.

02:19:51.501 --> 02:19:54.441
There's more errors than code I wrote.

02:19:54.441 --> 02:19:56.431
But what's going on here?

02:19:56.431 --> 02:19:58.881
Well, this is actually
something, a mistake you'll see,

02:19:58.881 --> 02:20:01.131
somewhat often, at least initially.

02:20:01.131 --> 02:20:03.271
And let's start to glean
what's going on here.

02:20:03.271 --> 02:20:06.921
So here, if I look at the very first
line of output after the dollar sign--

02:20:06.921 --> 02:20:09.921
so even though it jumped
down the screen pretty fast,

02:20:09.921 --> 02:20:12.831
I wrote Make Hello at
the dollar sign, prompt.

02:20:12.831 --> 02:20:14.361
And then here's the first error.

02:20:14.361 --> 02:20:17.121
On Hello dot C, line 5--

02:20:17.121 --> 02:20:21.021
technically character 5, but generally
line is enough to get you going--

02:20:21.021 --> 02:20:24.951
there's an error, use of
undeclared identifier string.

02:20:24.951 --> 02:20:27.121
Did you mean standard in?

02:20:27.121 --> 02:20:28.101
So, I didn't.

02:20:28.101 --> 02:20:30.361
And this is not an
obvious solution at first.

02:20:30.361 --> 02:20:34.161
But you'll start to recognize
these patterns in error messages.

02:20:34.161 --> 02:20:39.741
It turns out that if I want to use
string, I actually have to do this.

02:20:39.741 --> 02:20:43.880
I have to include another library
up here, another line of code,

02:20:43.880 --> 02:20:46.461
rather, called CS50
dot H. We'll come back

02:20:46.461 --> 02:20:48.301
to what this means in just a moment.

02:20:48.301 --> 02:20:54.561
But if I now retroactively say,
all right, what does standard I/O

02:20:54.561 --> 02:20:55.670
do for us up here.

02:20:55.670 --> 02:20:59.721
Before I added that new line,
what is standard I/O doing?

02:20:59.721 --> 02:21:01.670
Well, if you think
back to Scratch, there

02:21:01.670 --> 02:21:07.761
were a few examples with the camera and
with the speech to-- the text to voice.

02:21:07.761 --> 02:21:10.281
Remember I had to poke around
in the extensions button.

02:21:10.281 --> 02:21:12.110
And then I had to load it into Scratch.

02:21:12.110 --> 02:21:14.241
It didn't come natively with Scratch.

02:21:14.241 --> 02:21:15.920
C is quite like that.

02:21:15.920 --> 02:21:18.170
Some functions come with the language.

02:21:18.170 --> 02:21:22.161
But for the most part, if you want to
use a function, an action or a verb

02:21:22.161 --> 02:21:26.571
like printf, you have to load
that extension, so to speak,

02:21:26.571 --> 02:21:29.361
that more traditionally
is called a library.

02:21:29.361 --> 02:21:35.121
So there is a standard I/O
library, STD I/O, standard I/O,

02:21:35.121 --> 02:21:37.351
where I/O just means input and output.

02:21:37.351 --> 02:21:39.441
Which means, just like
in MIT's World, there

02:21:39.441 --> 02:21:43.161
was an extension for doing text
to voice or for using your camera.

02:21:43.161 --> 02:21:45.471
In C, there's an extension, a.k.a.

02:21:45.471 --> 02:21:49.231
a library, for doing
standard input and output.

02:21:49.231 --> 02:21:53.451
And so if you want to use any functions
related to standard input and output,

02:21:53.451 --> 02:21:58.131
like text from a keyboard, you
have to include standard I/O dot

02:21:58.131 --> 02:22:02.361
H. And then can you use printf.

02:22:02.361 --> 02:22:03.891
Same goes here.

02:22:03.891 --> 02:22:08.691
Get string, it turns out, is a
function that CS50 wrote some time ago.

02:22:08.691 --> 02:22:10.971
And as we'll see over
the coming weeks, it just

02:22:10.971 --> 02:22:14.781
makes it way easier to
get input from a user.

02:22:14.781 --> 02:22:18.111
C is very good with printf at
printing output on the screen.

02:22:18.111 --> 02:22:21.411
C makes it really annoying and
hard, as we'll see in a few weeks,

02:22:21.411 --> 02:22:23.191
to just get input from the user.

02:22:23.191 --> 02:22:25.821
So we wrote a function
called get_string,

02:22:25.821 --> 02:22:29.241
but the only way you can use
that is to load the extension,

02:22:29.241 --> 02:22:32.271
a.k.a. load the library called CS50.

02:22:32.271 --> 02:22:35.841
And we'll come back in time, like,
why is it .h, why is it a hash symbol.

02:22:35.841 --> 02:22:39.291
But for now, standard
I/O is a library that

02:22:39.291 --> 02:22:42.541
gives you access to printf and
input- and output-related stuff.

02:22:42.541 --> 02:22:45.111
CS50 is a second library
that provides you

02:22:45.111 --> 02:22:48.441
with access to functions
that don't come with C

02:22:48.441 --> 02:22:51.501
that include something like get_string.

02:22:51.501 --> 02:22:55.191
So with that said, we've
now kind of teased apart

02:22:55.191 --> 02:22:58.171
at a high level what lines
2 and now 1 are doing.

02:22:58.171 --> 02:23:00.381
Let me go ahead and rerun make hello.

02:23:00.381 --> 02:23:01.611
Now it worked.

02:23:01.611 --> 02:23:05.099
So all those crazy error messages
were resolved by just one fix,

02:23:05.099 --> 02:23:07.641
so key takeaway is not to get
overwhelmed by the sheer number

02:23:07.641 --> 02:23:08.581
of errors.

02:23:08.581 --> 02:23:13.521
Let me now do ./hello and if I type
in my name, what am I going to say?

02:23:16.521 --> 02:23:19.471
What do you think?

02:23:19.471 --> 02:23:24.091
Yeah, hello answer, because the
computer is going to take me literally.

02:23:24.091 --> 02:23:26.671
And it turns out that if
you just write "hello,

02:23:26.671 --> 02:23:29.611
answer" all in the double quotes,
you're really just passing

02:23:29.611 --> 02:23:32.551
English as the input
to the printf function,

02:23:32.551 --> 02:23:34.471
you're not actually
passing in the variable.

02:23:34.471 --> 02:23:37.321
And unfortunately in
C, it's not quite as

02:23:37.321 --> 02:23:40.861
easy to plug things in to
other things that you've typed.

02:23:40.861 --> 02:23:43.111
Remember in Scratch, there
was not just the Save block

02:23:43.111 --> 02:23:45.661
but the Join block,
which was kind of pretty,

02:23:45.661 --> 02:23:47.461
you can combine apples and oranges--

02:23:47.461 --> 02:23:48.901
or was it apple and banana?

02:23:48.901 --> 02:23:52.641
Then we changed it to hello and then
the answer that the human typed in.

02:23:52.641 --> 02:23:54.766
In C, the syntax is going
to be a little different.

02:23:54.766 --> 02:23:59.851
You tell the computer inside of your
double quotes that you want to have

02:23:59.851 --> 02:24:05.191
a placeholder there, a so-called
format code. %s means, hey, computer,

02:24:05.191 --> 02:24:08.041
put a string here eventually.

02:24:08.041 --> 02:24:12.661
Then outside of your quotes, you
just add a comma and then you type

02:24:12.661 --> 02:24:18.421
in whatever variable you want the
computer to plug in at that %s location

02:24:18.421 --> 02:24:19.231
for you.

02:24:19.231 --> 02:24:23.671
So %s is a format code which
serves as a placeholder.

02:24:23.671 --> 02:24:26.431
And now the printf function
was designed by humans years

02:24:26.431 --> 02:24:28.651
ago to figure out how to
do the apple and banana

02:24:28.651 --> 02:24:30.973
thing of joining two words together.

02:24:30.973 --> 02:24:33.181
It's not nearly as user-friendly
as it is in Scratch,

02:24:33.181 --> 02:24:35.701
but it's a very common paradigm.

02:24:35.701 --> 02:24:38.881
So let me try and rerun
this now. make hello.

02:24:38.881 --> 02:24:40.441
No errors, that's good.

02:24:40.441 --> 02:24:41.521
./hello.

02:24:41.521 --> 02:24:42.631
What's my name, David?

02:24:42.631 --> 02:24:45.781
If I type Enter now, now it's hello.

02:24:45.781 --> 02:24:46.351
David.

02:24:46.351 --> 02:24:48.811
And the printf, here's the F in printf.

02:24:48.811 --> 02:24:53.581
It formats its input for you by using
these placeholders for things like

02:24:53.581 --> 02:24:58.201
strings, represented again by %s.

02:24:58.201 --> 02:25:02.651
So a quick question then, if I focus
here on line 7 for just a moment

02:25:02.651 --> 02:25:09.481
and even zoom in here, how many
inputs is printf taking as a function?

02:25:09.481 --> 02:25:13.501
A moment ago, I'll admit that it was
taking one input, "hello, world,"

02:25:13.501 --> 02:25:14.791
quote unquote.

02:25:14.791 --> 02:25:19.981
How many inputs might you
infer printf is taking now?

02:25:19.981 --> 02:25:20.521
2.

02:25:20.521 --> 02:25:25.201
And it's implied by this comma here,
which is separating the first one,

02:25:25.201 --> 02:25:29.311
quote, unquote, "hello, %s"
from the second one, answer.

02:25:29.311 --> 02:25:32.731
And then just as a quick safety
check here, why is it not 3?

02:25:32.731 --> 02:25:35.321
Because there's obviously
two commas here.

02:25:35.321 --> 02:25:38.281
Why is it not actually
3 arguments or inputs?

02:25:38.281 --> 02:25:42.001
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:25:42.001 --> 02:25:43.001
DAVID J. MALAN: Exactly.

02:25:43.001 --> 02:25:46.211
The comma to the left is actually
part of my English grammar,

02:25:46.211 --> 02:25:47.694
that's all, so same syntax.

02:25:47.694 --> 02:25:50.111
And, again, here's where
programming can just be confusing

02:25:50.111 --> 02:25:52.901
early on because we're using the
same special punctuation to mean

02:25:52.901 --> 02:25:55.731
different things, it just
depends on the context.

02:25:55.731 --> 02:25:57.971
And so now is actually
a good time to point out

02:25:57.971 --> 02:26:01.751
all of the somewhat pretty colors that
have been popping up on the screen

02:26:01.751 --> 02:26:02.291
here--

02:26:02.291 --> 02:26:06.041
even though I wasn't going to a format
menu, I wasn't boldfacing things,

02:26:06.041 --> 02:26:08.991
I certainly wasn't changing
things to red or blue or whatnot--

02:26:08.991 --> 02:26:13.901
that's because a text editor like
VS Code syntax highlights for you.

02:26:13.901 --> 02:26:17.051
This is a feature of so many different
programming environments nowadays,

02:26:17.051 --> 02:26:18.641
VS Code does it as well.

02:26:18.641 --> 02:26:23.021
If your text editor understands the
language that you're programming in--

02:26:23.021 --> 02:26:24.321
C, in this case--

02:26:24.321 --> 02:26:28.701
it highlights in different colors the
different types of ideas in your code.

02:26:28.701 --> 02:26:31.871
So, for instance, string and
answer here are in black,

02:26:31.871 --> 02:26:35.828
but get_string a function is in
this sort of nasty brown-yellow

02:26:35.828 --> 02:26:38.411
here right now, but that's just
how it displays on the screen.

02:26:38.411 --> 02:26:41.111
The string, though, here in red
is kind of jumping out at me,

02:26:41.111 --> 02:26:42.791
and that's marginally useful.

02:26:42.791 --> 02:26:44.114
The %s is in blue.

02:26:44.114 --> 02:26:46.281
That's kind of nice, because
it's jumping out at me.

02:26:46.281 --> 02:26:49.601
And so it's just using different colors
to make different things on the screen

02:26:49.601 --> 02:26:53.021
pop so you can focus on
how these ideas interrelate

02:26:53.021 --> 02:26:55.121
and, honestly, when you
might make a mistake.

02:26:55.121 --> 02:26:58.031
For instance, let me accidentally
leave off this quote here.

02:26:58.031 --> 02:27:03.011
And now all of a sudden,
notice if I delete the quote,

02:27:03.011 --> 02:27:05.711
the colors start to get a little awry.

02:27:05.711 --> 02:27:09.641
But if I go back there and put it
back, now everything's back in place.

02:27:09.641 --> 02:27:11.471
What's another feature
of this text editor?

02:27:11.471 --> 02:27:15.251
Notice when my cursor is next
to this parenthesis, which

02:27:15.251 --> 02:27:18.111
demarcates the end of the
inputs to the function,

02:27:18.111 --> 02:27:21.611
notice that highlighted in green
here is the opening parenthesis.

02:27:21.611 --> 02:27:22.151
Why?

02:27:22.151 --> 02:27:24.238
It's just a visually useful
thing, especially when

02:27:24.238 --> 02:27:26.321
you start writing more and
more code, just to make

02:27:26.321 --> 02:27:28.241
sure your parentheses are lining up.

02:27:28.241 --> 02:27:31.391
And that's true for these curly braces
over here on the left and the right.

02:27:31.391 --> 02:27:33.011
We'll come back to those in a moment.

02:27:33.011 --> 02:27:36.821
If I put my cursor there, you can
see that these things correspond

02:27:36.821 --> 02:27:37.701
to one another.

02:27:37.701 --> 02:27:40.601
So it's nothing in your code
fundamentally, it's just the editor

02:27:40.601 --> 02:27:42.911
trying to help you, the human, program.

02:27:42.911 --> 02:27:45.251
And you can even see it,
though it's a little subtle--

02:27:45.251 --> 02:27:48.131
see these four dots here
and these four dots here?

02:27:48.131 --> 02:27:49.691
That's my indentation.

02:27:49.691 --> 02:27:53.531
I configured VS Code to
indent by four spaces, which

02:27:53.531 --> 02:27:54.761
is a very common convention.

02:27:54.761 --> 02:27:58.091
Any time I hit the Tab key, this
too can help you make sure--

02:27:58.091 --> 02:28:00.941
once we have more interesting
and longer programs--

02:28:00.941 --> 02:28:04.121
that everything lines
up nice and neatly.

02:28:04.121 --> 02:28:05.381
Phew.

02:28:05.381 --> 02:28:07.391
All right, any questions
then on printf or more?

02:28:07.391 --> 02:28:08.653
Yeah.

02:28:08.653 --> 02:28:11.123
AUDIENCE: [? Would ?]
the printf [INAUDIBLE]??

02:28:11.123 --> 02:28:12.831
DAVID J. MALAN: Short
answer, yes. printf

02:28:12.831 --> 02:28:16.131
can handle more than one
type of variable or value.

02:28:16.131 --> 02:28:17.241
%s is one.

02:28:17.241 --> 02:28:20.671
We're going to see %i is another
for plugging in an integer.

02:28:20.671 --> 02:28:24.261
You can have multiple i's, multiple
s's, and even other symbols too.

02:28:24.261 --> 02:28:26.151
We'll come back to that
in just a little bit.

02:28:26.151 --> 02:28:29.841
printf can take many more
arguments than just these two.

02:28:29.841 --> 02:28:32.151
This is just meant to be representative.

02:28:32.151 --> 02:28:34.191
Yeah, over here.

02:28:34.191 --> 02:28:36.201
Can you declare variables
within the printf?

02:28:36.201 --> 02:28:37.611
No.

02:28:37.611 --> 02:28:39.651
The only variable I'm
using right now is answer,

02:28:39.651 --> 02:28:43.251
and it's got to be done outside
the context of printf in this case.

02:28:43.251 --> 02:28:46.311
Good question, we'll see
more of that before long.

02:28:46.311 --> 02:28:47.953
Yeah, in back.

02:28:47.953 --> 02:28:49.744
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:28:49.744 --> 02:28:51.911
DAVID J. MALAN: How do we
download the CS50 library?

02:28:51.911 --> 02:28:55.091
So we will show you in problems
set 1 exactly how to do that.

02:28:55.091 --> 02:28:58.421
It's automatically done for you in
our version of VS Code in the cloud.

02:28:58.421 --> 02:29:01.811
If, ultimately, you program on your own
Mac or PC, either initially or later

02:29:01.811 --> 02:29:03.701
on, it's also installable online.

02:29:03.701 --> 02:29:07.074
But if you want to ask that
via online or afterward,

02:29:07.074 --> 02:29:08.741
we can point you in the right direction.

02:29:08.741 --> 02:29:10.391
But PSet 1 will itself.

02:29:10.391 --> 02:29:11.232
Yeah.

02:29:11.232 --> 02:29:13.341
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:29:13.341 --> 02:29:16.971
DAVID J. MALAN: String is the type
of the variable or, more properly,

02:29:16.971 --> 02:29:19.101
the data type of the variable.

02:29:19.101 --> 02:29:22.281
int is another keyword I alluded
to earlier, I haven't used it yet.

02:29:22.281 --> 02:29:26.191
int, for integer, is going to be
another type, or data type, of variable.

02:29:26.191 --> 02:29:27.441
AUDIENCE: OK. [? Thank you. ?]

02:29:27.441 --> 02:29:28.316
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

02:29:28.316 --> 02:29:29.691
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:29:29.691 --> 02:29:31.108
DAVID J. MALAN: Oh, good question.

02:29:31.108 --> 02:29:34.971
Could I go ahead and just
plug in this function,

02:29:34.971 --> 02:29:39.261
kind of like we did in Scratch,
getting rid of the variable altogether

02:29:39.261 --> 02:29:42.471
and just do this, which
recall, is reminiscent of what

02:29:42.471 --> 02:29:45.921
I did in Scratch by plopping
block on top of block on block?

02:29:45.921 --> 02:29:48.271
Am I answering that right?

02:29:48.271 --> 02:29:50.231
Can I put string in front of get_string?

02:29:50.231 --> 02:29:50.731
No.

02:29:50.731 --> 02:29:53.701
You only put the word string
in front of a variable

02:29:53.701 --> 02:29:55.201
that you want to make string.

02:29:55.201 --> 02:29:57.871
And even though I'm apparently
answering the wrong question,

02:29:57.871 --> 02:30:01.711
let me go ahead and zoom out,
save this, do make hello again.

02:30:01.711 --> 02:30:03.451
Seems to compile OK.

02:30:03.451 --> 02:30:06.181
If I run ./hello, type in David, voila.

02:30:06.181 --> 02:30:07.451
That, too, works.

02:30:07.451 --> 02:30:10.391
And so, actually, let's go down
this rabbit hole for just a moment.

02:30:10.391 --> 02:30:12.211
Clearly, it's still correct--

02:30:12.211 --> 02:30:14.611
at least, based on my limited testing.

02:30:14.611 --> 02:30:18.001
Is this better designed
or worse designed?

02:30:18.001 --> 02:30:20.131
Let's open that question
like we did last week.

02:30:20.131 --> 02:30:21.481
Yeah?

02:30:21.481 --> 02:30:23.821
Yeah, I kind of agree with that.

02:30:23.821 --> 02:30:26.011
Reasonable people could
disagree, but I do

02:30:26.011 --> 02:30:29.971
agree that this seems harder to
read because I start reading here,

02:30:29.971 --> 02:30:32.438
but wait a minute. get_string
is going to get used first,

02:30:32.438 --> 02:30:34.271
and then it's going to
give me back a value.

02:30:34.271 --> 02:30:37.501
So, yeah, it just feels like it
was nicer to read top to bottom,

02:30:37.501 --> 02:30:38.161
I would say.

02:30:38.161 --> 02:30:38.791
Your thoughts?

02:30:38.791 --> 02:30:39.741
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:30:39.741 --> 02:30:40.616
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

02:30:40.616 --> 02:30:44.611
And so this is useful if I only want
to print out the person's name once.

02:30:44.611 --> 02:30:47.774
If I want to use it later in a
longer program, I'm out of luck,

02:30:47.774 --> 02:30:49.441
and so I haven't saved it in a variable.

02:30:49.441 --> 02:30:53.041
So I think, long story short, we
could debate this all day long.

02:30:53.041 --> 02:30:56.151
But in this case, eh, if you can
make a reasonable argument one

02:30:56.151 --> 02:30:59.241
way or the other, that's a
pretty solid ground to stand on.

02:30:59.241 --> 02:31:01.101
But, invariably,
reasonable people are going

02:31:01.101 --> 02:31:05.641
to disagree, whether first-time
programmers or many years after that.

02:31:05.641 --> 02:31:09.741
So let's frame this one last example
in the context of the same process

02:31:09.741 --> 02:31:11.381
of taking inputs and outputs.

02:31:11.381 --> 02:31:13.131
The functions we've
been talking about all

02:31:13.131 --> 02:31:17.721
take inputs, otherwise now known as
arguments, or parameters, pretty much

02:31:17.721 --> 02:31:18.531
synonymous.

02:31:18.531 --> 02:31:21.741
That's just the fancy word
for an input to a function.

02:31:21.741 --> 02:31:25.046
And some functions have either
side effects, like we saw--

02:31:25.046 --> 02:31:27.171
printing something, saying
something on the screen,

02:31:27.171 --> 02:31:28.911
sort of visually or audibly--

02:31:28.911 --> 02:31:33.921
or they return a value, which is a
reusable value, like name or answer,

02:31:33.921 --> 02:31:35.251
in this case.

02:31:35.251 --> 02:31:39.261
If we look then at what we did last
time in the world of Scratch last week,

02:31:39.261 --> 02:31:41.811
the input was what's your
name, the function was ask,

02:31:41.811 --> 02:31:44.781
and the return value was answer.

02:31:44.781 --> 02:31:49.521
And now let's take a look at this block,
which is honestly a more user-friendly

02:31:49.521 --> 02:31:51.267
version of what we just did with the %s.

02:31:51.267 --> 02:31:54.921
Last week we said save, then
join, then hello and answer.

02:31:54.921 --> 02:31:58.581
But the interesting takeaway there
was not how to say hello anything.

02:31:58.581 --> 02:32:03.021
It was the fact that in Scratch
2, the output of one function,

02:32:03.021 --> 02:32:08.151
like the green join, could become
the input to another function,

02:32:08.151 --> 02:32:09.501
the purple say.

02:32:09.501 --> 02:32:12.471
The syntax in C is
admittedly pretty different,

02:32:12.471 --> 02:32:14.481
but the idea is essentially the same.

02:32:14.481 --> 02:32:18.381
Here, though, we have
hello, a placeholder,

02:32:18.381 --> 02:32:21.891
but we have to, in this
world of C, tell printf

02:32:21.891 --> 02:32:25.111
what we want to plug in
for that placeholder.

02:32:25.111 --> 02:32:25.981
It's just different.

02:32:25.981 --> 02:32:27.148
But that's the way to do it.

02:32:27.148 --> 02:32:29.691
When we get to Python and other
languages later in the term,

02:32:29.691 --> 02:32:31.521
there's actually easier ways to do this.

02:32:31.521 --> 02:32:34.311
But this is a very common
paradigm, particularly when

02:32:34.311 --> 02:32:37.491
you want to format
your data in some way.

02:32:37.491 --> 02:32:40.051
All right, let's then take a
step back to where we began,

02:32:40.051 --> 02:32:43.701
which was with that whole
program, which had the include

02:32:43.701 --> 02:32:47.631
and it had int main(void) and
all of this other cryptic syntax.

02:32:47.631 --> 02:32:51.351
This Scratch piece last week
was kind of like the go-to

02:32:51.351 --> 02:32:53.869
whenever you want to have a
main part of your program.

02:32:53.869 --> 02:32:55.911
It's not the only way to
start a Scratch program.

02:32:55.911 --> 02:32:59.181
You could listen for clicks or other
things, not just the green flag.

02:32:59.181 --> 02:33:03.831
But this was probably the most popular
place to start a program in Scratch.

02:33:03.831 --> 02:33:07.479
In C, the closest analog is
to literally write this out.

02:33:07.479 --> 02:33:10.521
So just like last week, if you were
in the habit of dragging and dropping

02:33:10.521 --> 02:33:13.101
when green flag clicked,
as a C programmer,

02:33:13.101 --> 02:33:15.771
the first thing you would do is
after creating an empty file,

02:33:15.771 --> 02:33:18.621
like I did with hello.c,
you'd probably type int

02:33:18.621 --> 02:33:22.041
main(void) open curly
brace, closed curly brace,

02:33:22.041 --> 02:33:26.101
and then you can put all of your
code inside of those curly braces.

02:33:26.101 --> 02:33:29.211
So just like Scratch had
this sort of magnetic nature

02:33:29.211 --> 02:33:33.531
to it where the puzzle pieces would snap
together, C, as a text-based language,

02:33:33.531 --> 02:33:38.241
tends to use these curly braces, one
of them opened, the other one closed.

02:33:38.241 --> 02:33:41.481
And anything inside of
those braces, so to speak,

02:33:41.481 --> 02:33:44.571
is part of this puzzle piece, a.k.a.

02:33:44.571 --> 02:33:45.891
main.

02:33:45.891 --> 02:33:47.451
So what was atop them?

02:33:47.451 --> 02:33:50.841
We went down this rabbit hole moment ago
with these things called header files,

02:33:50.841 --> 02:33:52.674
even though I didn't
call them by this name.

02:33:52.674 --> 02:33:56.856
But, indeed, when we have a whole
program in Scratch, super easy.

02:33:56.856 --> 02:33:59.481
Just have the one green flag
clicked and then say hello, world.

02:33:59.481 --> 02:34:00.681
There's no special syntax.

02:34:00.681 --> 02:34:03.351
After all, it's meant to be very
user-friendly and graphical.

02:34:03.351 --> 02:34:09.531
In C, though, you technically can't just
put int main(void) printf hello, world.

02:34:09.531 --> 02:34:10.701
You also need this.

02:34:10.701 --> 02:34:16.341
Because, again, you need to tell
the compiler to load the library--

02:34:16.341 --> 02:34:22.041
code that someone else wrote-- so that
the compiler knows what printf even is.

02:34:22.041 --> 02:34:24.981
You have to load the
CS50 library whenever

02:34:24.981 --> 02:34:28.281
you want to use get_string or
other functions, like get_int,

02:34:28.281 --> 02:34:29.631
as we'll soon see.

02:34:29.631 --> 02:34:32.691
Otherwise, the compiler won't
know what get_string is.

02:34:32.691 --> 02:34:35.011
You just have to do it this way.

02:34:35.011 --> 02:34:37.341
The specific file name
I'm mentioning here,

02:34:37.341 --> 02:34:44.311
stdio.h, cs50.h, is what C programmers
called a call a header file.

02:34:44.311 --> 02:34:46.831
We'll see eventually what's
inside of those files.

02:34:46.831 --> 02:34:51.061
But long story short, it's like a menu
of all of the available functions.

02:34:51.061 --> 02:34:54.561
So in cs50.h, there's a menu
mentioning get_string, get_int,

02:34:54.561 --> 02:34:56.151
and a bunch of other stuff.

02:34:56.151 --> 02:35:01.791
And in stdio.h, there's a menu of
functions, among which are printf.

02:35:01.791 --> 02:35:04.641
And that menu is what
prepares the compiler

02:35:04.641 --> 02:35:08.946
to know how to implement
those same functions.

02:35:08.946 --> 02:35:10.311
All right, let me pause here.

02:35:10.311 --> 02:35:11.372
Question.

02:35:11.372 --> 02:35:15.878
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:35:15.878 --> 02:35:16.961
DAVID J. MALAN: Not quite.

02:35:16.961 --> 02:35:20.561
A library provides all of the
functionality we're talking about.

02:35:20.561 --> 02:35:25.721
A header file is the very specific
mechanism via which you include it.

02:35:25.721 --> 02:35:27.771
And we'll discuss this more next week.

02:35:27.771 --> 02:35:30.161
For now, they're essentially
the same, but we'll discuss

02:35:30.161 --> 02:35:32.801
nuances between the two next week.

02:35:32.801 --> 02:35:36.461
Yeah, the library would be standard
I/O. The library would CS50.

02:35:36.461 --> 02:35:41.721
The corresponding header
file is stdio.h, cs50.h.

02:35:41.721 --> 02:35:42.221
Indeed.

02:35:42.221 --> 02:35:42.941
Other questions.

02:35:42.941 --> 02:35:43.677
Yeah.

02:35:43.677 --> 02:35:45.861
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:35:45.861 --> 02:35:46.891
DAVID J. MALAN: Indeed.

02:35:46.891 --> 02:35:48.081
That, too, is on the menu.

02:35:48.081 --> 02:35:49.081
We'll come back to that.

02:35:49.081 --> 02:35:50.481
But the word string--

02:35:50.481 --> 02:35:54.231
incredibly common in the world of
programming, it's not a CS50 idea--

02:35:54.231 --> 02:35:59.301
but in C, there's technically no
such data type as string by default.

02:35:59.301 --> 02:36:02.216
We have sort of conjured it up
to simplify the first few weeks.

02:36:02.216 --> 02:36:05.091
That's a training wheel that we'll
very deliberately, in a few weeks,

02:36:05.091 --> 02:36:09.411
take away, and we'll see why we've
been using get_string and string.

02:36:09.411 --> 02:36:14.161
Because C otherwise makes things
quite more challenging early on,

02:36:14.161 --> 02:36:16.191
which then gets besides
the point for us.

02:36:16.191 --> 02:36:17.457
Yeah.

02:36:17.457 --> 02:36:19.738
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:36:19.738 --> 02:36:20.571
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes.

02:36:20.571 --> 02:36:23.481
Early on, you will have to use whatever
is prescribed by the specification.

02:36:23.481 --> 02:36:24.951
That will include CS50's functions.

02:36:24.951 --> 02:36:28.191
Long story short, you referred, I
think, a moment ago to another function

02:36:28.191 --> 02:36:30.981
called scanf, we won't
talk about for a few weeks.

02:36:30.981 --> 02:36:36.711
Long story short, in C, it's pretty easy
and possible to get input from a user.

02:36:36.711 --> 02:36:40.311
The catch is that it's really
easy to do it dangerously.

02:36:40.311 --> 02:36:45.111
And C, because it's an older,
lower-level language, so to speak,

02:36:45.111 --> 02:36:49.431
that gives you pretty much ultimate
control over your computer's hardware.

02:36:49.431 --> 02:36:51.621
It's very easy to make mistakes.

02:36:51.621 --> 02:36:55.101
And, indeed, that's too
why we use the library,

02:36:55.101 --> 02:36:58.541
so your code won't crash unintendedly.

02:36:58.541 --> 02:37:01.621
All right, so with this in
mind, we have this now mapping

02:37:01.621 --> 02:37:03.371
between the Scratch
version and the other.

02:37:03.371 --> 02:37:06.538
Let me just give you a quick tour of
some of the other placeholders and data

02:37:06.538 --> 02:37:10.241
types that students will start seeing as
we assemble more interesting programs.

02:37:10.241 --> 02:37:13.511
In the world of Linux, here
is a non-exhaustive list

02:37:13.511 --> 02:37:16.811
of commands with which you'll get
familiar over the next few weeks

02:37:16.811 --> 02:37:18.041
by playing with problem sets.

02:37:18.041 --> 02:37:22.784
We've only seen two of these so
far, ls for list, rm for others.

02:37:22.784 --> 02:37:24.701
But I mention them now
just so that it doesn't

02:37:24.701 --> 02:37:30.341
feel too foreign when you see them
on screen or online in a problem set.

02:37:30.341 --> 02:37:32.411
cp is going to stand for copy.

02:37:32.411 --> 02:37:35.711
mkdir is going to stand
for make directory. mv is

02:37:35.711 --> 02:37:38.471
going to stand for move or rename.

02:37:38.471 --> 02:37:44.471
rmdir is going to be remove directory,
and cd is going to be for change /

02:37:44.471 --> 02:37:46.961
and let me show you this
last one here first,

02:37:46.961 --> 02:37:49.511
only because it's something
you'll use so commonly.

02:37:49.511 --> 02:37:53.681
If I go back to my code here on
the screen, I'm going to go ahead

02:37:53.681 --> 02:37:58.091
and re-open the little GUI on the
left-hand side, the so-called Explorer,

02:37:58.091 --> 02:38:01.451
revealing that I've got two
files, hello and hello.c

02:38:01.451 --> 02:38:02.951
so nothing has changed since there.

02:38:02.951 --> 02:38:05.801
Suppose now that it's
a few weeks into class

02:38:05.801 --> 02:38:07.661
and I want to start
organizing the code I'm

02:38:07.661 --> 02:38:10.511
writing so that I have a folder
for this week or next week,

02:38:10.511 --> 02:38:13.421
or maybe a folder for
problem set 1, problem set 2.

02:38:13.421 --> 02:38:15.131
I can do this in a few ways.

02:38:15.131 --> 02:38:18.191
In the GUI, I can go up
here and do what most of you

02:38:18.191 --> 02:38:19.961
would do instinctively on a Mac or PC.

02:38:19.961 --> 02:38:22.451
You look for like a
folder icon, you click it,

02:38:22.451 --> 02:38:25.961
and then you name a
folder like PSet1, Enter.

02:38:25.961 --> 02:38:28.691
Voila, you've got a folder called PSet1.

02:38:28.691 --> 02:38:34.701
I can confirm as much with my command
line interface by typing what command?

02:38:34.701 --> 02:38:36.891
How can I list what's in my folder?

02:38:36.891 --> 02:38:38.391
Yeah, so ls for list.

02:38:38.391 --> 02:38:39.504
And now I see hello--

02:38:39.504 --> 02:38:41.421
and it's green with an
asterisk because that's

02:38:41.421 --> 02:38:43.581
my executable, my runnable program--

02:38:43.581 --> 02:38:46.701
hello.c, which is my source
code, and now PSet1 with a slash

02:38:46.701 --> 02:38:50.031
at the end, which just implies
that it's indeed a folder.

02:38:50.031 --> 02:38:52.321
All right, I didn't really
want to do it that way.

02:38:52.321 --> 02:38:53.871
I'd like to do it more advanced.

02:38:53.871 --> 02:38:57.531
So let me go ahead and right-click
on PSet1, delete permanently.

02:38:57.531 --> 02:38:59.711
I get a scary irreversible
error message.

02:38:59.711 --> 02:39:01.461
But there's nothing
in it, so that's fine.

02:39:01.461 --> 02:39:03.561
Now I've deleted it using the GUI.

02:39:03.561 --> 02:39:08.851
But now let me go ahead and start doing
the same thing from the command line.

02:39:08.851 --> 02:39:11.061
And if you're wondering how
things keep disappearing,

02:39:11.061 --> 02:39:15.291
if you hit Control-L in your terminal
window or explicitly type clear,

02:39:15.291 --> 02:39:18.471
it will delete everything you previously
typed just to clean things up.

02:39:18.471 --> 02:39:20.596
In practice, you don't need
to be doing this often.

02:39:20.596 --> 02:39:23.481
I'm doing it just to keep our
focus on my latest commands.

02:39:23.481 --> 02:39:26.963
If I do-- what was the command
to make a new directory?

02:39:26.963 --> 02:39:28.321
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:39:28.321 --> 02:39:30.541
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so
mkdir, make directory.

02:39:30.541 --> 02:39:32.251
Let me create PSet1, Enter.

02:39:32.251 --> 02:39:34.621
And notice at left, there's my PSet1.

02:39:34.621 --> 02:39:37.211
If I want to get a little
overzealous, plan for next week,

02:39:37.211 --> 02:39:39.121
here's my PSet2 directory.

02:39:39.121 --> 02:39:44.723
Suppose now I want to open those
folders on a Mac or PC or in this GUI,

02:39:44.723 --> 02:39:46.681
I could double-click on
it like this, and you'd

02:39:46.681 --> 02:39:48.408
see this little arrow is moving.

02:39:48.408 --> 02:39:51.491
It's not doing anything because there's
nothing in there, but that's fine.

02:39:51.491 --> 02:39:55.051
But suppose again I want to get more
comfortable with my command line.

02:39:55.051 --> 02:39:59.131
Notice if I type ls now, I
see all four same things.

02:39:59.131 --> 02:40:05.821
Let me change directories
with cd space PSet1 Enter.

02:40:05.821 --> 02:40:08.461
And now notice two things
will have happened.

02:40:08.461 --> 02:40:14.071
One, my prompt has changed
slightly to remind me where I am,

02:40:14.071 --> 02:40:17.731
just to keep me sane so that I don't
forget what folder I'm actually in.

02:40:17.731 --> 02:40:21.611
So here is just a visual reminder
of what folder I'm currently in.

02:40:21.611 --> 02:40:26.911
If I type ls now, what should
I see after hitting Enter?

02:40:26.911 --> 02:40:29.591
Nothing, because I've only
created empty folders so far.

02:40:29.591 --> 02:40:31.051
And, indeed, I see nothing.

02:40:31.051 --> 02:40:35.491
If I wanted to create a folder called
Mario for a program that might be

02:40:35.491 --> 02:40:37.891
called Mario this week, I can do that.

02:40:37.891 --> 02:40:40.531
Now if I type ls, there is Mario.

02:40:40.531 --> 02:40:42.871
Now if I do cd Mario,
notice my prompt's going

02:40:42.871 --> 02:40:44.521
to change to be a little more precise.

02:40:44.521 --> 02:40:47.191
Now I'm in PSet1/Mario.

02:40:47.191 --> 02:40:49.051
And notice what's happening at top left.

02:40:49.051 --> 02:40:51.151
Nothing now, because these
folders are collapsed.

02:40:51.151 --> 02:40:54.391
But if I click the little
triangle, there I see Mario.

02:40:54.391 --> 02:40:56.911
Nothing's going on in there
because there's no files yet.

02:40:56.911 --> 02:41:00.811
But suppose now I want to
create a file called mario.c.

02:41:00.811 --> 02:41:04.771
I could go up here, I could click the
little plus icon, and use the GUI.

02:41:04.771 --> 02:41:07.591
Or I can just type code mario.c.

02:41:07.591 --> 02:41:08.221
Voila.

02:41:08.221 --> 02:41:10.083
That creates a new tab for me.

02:41:10.083 --> 02:41:13.291
I'm not going to write any code in here
yet, but I am going to save the file.

02:41:13.291 --> 02:41:16.951
And now at top left, you'll
see that mario.c appears.

02:41:16.951 --> 02:41:19.493
So at some point, you can
eventually just close the Explorer.

02:41:19.493 --> 02:41:22.159
Because, again, it's not providing
you with any new information.

02:41:22.159 --> 02:41:23.971
It's maybe more
user-friendly, but there's

02:41:23.971 --> 02:41:27.961
nothing you can't do at the command
line that you could do with the GUI.

02:41:27.961 --> 02:41:29.881
All right, but now I'm kind of stuck.

02:41:29.881 --> 02:41:32.221
How do I get out of this folder?

02:41:32.221 --> 02:41:34.021
In my Mac or PC world,
I'd probably click

02:41:34.021 --> 02:41:37.321
the Back button or something like that
or just close it and start all over.

02:41:37.321 --> 02:41:42.181
In the terminal window,
I can do cd dot dot.

02:41:42.181 --> 02:41:46.651
Dot dot is a nickname, if you
will, for the parent directory.

02:41:46.651 --> 02:41:48.101
That is, the previous directory.

02:41:48.101 --> 02:41:52.601
So if I hit Enter now, notice I'm
going to close the Mario folder,

02:41:52.601 --> 02:41:56.131
a.k.a. directory, and
now I'm back in PSet1.

02:41:56.131 --> 02:42:00.241
Or, if I want to be fancy, let me
go back into Mario temporarily.

02:42:00.241 --> 02:42:03.301
If I type ls, there's
mario.c, just to orient us.

02:42:03.301 --> 02:42:06.459
If I want to do multiple things
at a time, I could do cd../..

02:42:09.271 --> 02:42:13.021
which goes to my parent to my
grandparent all in one breath.

02:42:13.021 --> 02:42:16.541
And voila, now I'm back in my
default folder, if you will.

02:42:16.541 --> 02:42:20.911
And one last little trick of the
trade, if I'm in PSet1/Mario like I

02:42:20.911 --> 02:42:24.181
was a moment ago, and you're
just tired of all the navigation,

02:42:24.181 --> 02:42:26.881
if you just type cd and
hit Enter, it'll whisk you

02:42:26.881 --> 02:42:29.071
away back to your default
folder, and you don't have

02:42:29.071 --> 02:42:31.321
to worry about getting there manually.

02:42:31.321 --> 02:42:38.521
Recall a bit ago, though, that I
was running hello as this, ./hello.

02:42:38.521 --> 02:42:42.751
If dot refers to my parent,
perhaps infer here syntactically,

02:42:42.751 --> 02:42:46.721
what does a single dot mean instead?

02:42:46.721 --> 02:42:49.481
It means this directory,
your current directory.

02:42:49.481 --> 02:42:50.901
Why is that necessary?

02:42:50.901 --> 02:42:52.993
It just makes super
explicit to the computer

02:42:52.993 --> 02:42:55.451
that I want the program called
hello that's installed here,

02:42:55.451 --> 02:42:59.501
not in some random other folder
on my hard drive, so to speak.

02:42:59.501 --> 02:43:02.426
I want the one that's
right here instead.

02:43:02.426 --> 02:43:04.301
All right, so besides
these commands, there's

02:43:04.301 --> 02:43:06.453
going to be others that
we encounter over time.

02:43:06.453 --> 02:43:07.661
Those are kind of the basics.

02:43:07.661 --> 02:43:11.711
That allows you to wean yourself off
of a GUI, Graphical User Interface,

02:43:11.711 --> 02:43:14.471
and start using more comfortably,
with practice and time,

02:43:14.471 --> 02:43:16.361
a command line interface instead.

02:43:16.361 --> 02:43:19.631
Well, what about those other
types, now back in the world of C?

02:43:19.631 --> 02:43:23.951
Those commands were not C. Those are
just command-specific to a command line

02:43:23.951 --> 02:43:28.301
interface, like in Linux, which,
again, we're using in the cloud.

02:43:28.301 --> 02:43:30.311
It's an alternative
to Mac OS and Windows.

02:43:30.311 --> 02:43:34.811
Back in the world of C now, we've
seen strings, which are words.

02:43:34.811 --> 02:43:38.351
I mentioned int or integer,
but there's others as well.

02:43:38.351 --> 02:43:42.581
In the world of C, we've
seen string, we will see int.

02:43:42.581 --> 02:43:46.031
If you want a bigger integer, there's
something literally called a long.

02:43:46.031 --> 02:43:49.451
If you want a single character,
there's something called a char.

02:43:49.451 --> 02:43:53.681
If you want a Boolean value,
true or false, there is a bool.

02:43:53.681 --> 02:43:56.111
And if you want a floating-point value--

02:43:56.111 --> 02:43:59.781
a fancy way of saying a real number,
something with a decimal point in it--

02:43:59.781 --> 02:44:03.201
that is what C and other
languages call a float.

02:44:03.201 --> 02:44:06.611
And if you want even more numbers
after the decimal point that

02:44:06.611 --> 02:44:10.211
is more precision, you can
use something called a double.

02:44:10.211 --> 02:44:14.021
That is to say, here is, again,
an example in programming

02:44:14.021 --> 02:44:17.681
where it's up to you now to provide
the computer with hints, essentially,

02:44:17.681 --> 02:44:21.641
that it will rely on to know what
is this pattern of zeros and ones.

02:44:21.641 --> 02:44:23.331
Is it a number, a letter?

02:44:23.331 --> 02:44:26.621
Is it a sound, an image,
a color, or the like?

02:44:26.621 --> 02:44:30.971
These are the types of data types
that provide exactly those hints.

02:44:30.971 --> 02:44:35.801
What are the functions that come in
the menu that is the CS50 library?

02:44:35.801 --> 02:44:39.251
We talked about standard I/O, and
that's just one function so far, printf.

02:44:39.251 --> 02:44:42.911
In the CS50 library, you can
see that it follows a pattern.

02:44:42.911 --> 02:44:45.581
The C50 library exists largely
for the first few weeks

02:44:45.581 --> 02:44:50.998
of the class to make our lives easier
when you just want to get user input.

02:44:50.998 --> 02:44:53.831
So if you want to get a string,
like a word or words from the human,

02:44:53.831 --> 02:44:54.701
you use get_string.

02:44:54.701 --> 02:44:57.743
If you want to get an integer from
the user, you're going to use get_int.

02:44:57.743 --> 02:45:01.091
When you want to get any of those
other data types, for the most part,

02:45:01.091 --> 02:45:03.861
you use get_ something else.

02:45:03.861 --> 02:45:06.221
And they're indeed all
lowercase by convention.

02:45:06.221 --> 02:45:07.331
What about printf?

02:45:07.331 --> 02:45:10.631
If we have the ability now to
store different types of data

02:45:10.631 --> 02:45:13.751
and we have functions with which
to get different types of data,

02:45:13.751 --> 02:45:16.841
how might you go about printing
different types of data?

02:45:16.841 --> 02:45:23.801
Well, we've seen %s for string,
%i for integer, %c for char,

02:45:23.801 --> 02:45:30.131
%f for a float or a double, those
real numbers I described earlier,

02:45:30.131 --> 02:45:33.761
and then %li for a long integer.

02:45:33.761 --> 02:45:36.281
So here's the first
example of inconsistencies.

02:45:36.281 --> 02:45:38.981
In an ideal world, that would
just be %l and we'd move on.

02:45:38.981 --> 02:45:42.941
It's %li instead in this case.

02:45:42.941 --> 02:45:45.701
That's printf and some
of its format codes.

02:45:45.701 --> 02:45:47.261
What more might we do?

02:45:47.261 --> 02:45:49.511
Well, in C, as we'll see--

02:45:49.511 --> 02:45:51.858
no pun intended-- there is
a whole bunch of operators.

02:45:51.858 --> 02:45:54.191
And, indeed, computers, one
of the first things they did

02:45:54.191 --> 02:45:57.941
was a lot of math and calculations, so
there's a lot of operators like these.

02:45:57.941 --> 02:46:01.161
Computers, and in turn, C, really
good at addition, subtraction,

02:46:01.161 --> 02:46:04.191
multiplication, division,
and even the percent sign,

02:46:04.191 --> 02:46:05.741
which is the remainder operator.

02:46:05.741 --> 02:46:08.651
There's a special symbol
in C and other languages

02:46:08.651 --> 02:46:13.161
just for getting the remainder, when
you divide one number by another.

02:46:13.161 --> 02:46:18.911
There are other features in the world
of C, like variables, as we've seen.

02:46:18.911 --> 02:46:22.931
And there's also what is of
playfully called syntactic sugar that

02:46:22.931 --> 02:46:27.371
makes it easier over time
to write fewer characters

02:46:27.371 --> 02:46:29.091
but express your thoughts the same.

02:46:29.091 --> 02:46:33.471
So just as a single example
of this, as a single example,

02:46:33.471 --> 02:46:37.331
consider this use of
a variable last week.

02:46:37.331 --> 02:46:41.741
Here in Scratch is how you might
set a variable called counter to 0.

02:46:41.741 --> 02:46:43.961
In C, it's going to be similar.

02:46:43.961 --> 02:46:46.241
If you want the variable
to be called counter,

02:46:46.241 --> 02:46:49.541
you literally write the word counter,
or whatever you want it to be called.

02:46:49.541 --> 02:46:53.351
You then use the assignment
operator, a.k.a. the equals sign,

02:46:53.351 --> 02:46:56.931
and you assign it whatever its initial
value should be here on the right.

02:46:56.931 --> 02:47:01.001
So, again, the 0 is going to get copied
from right to left into the variable

02:47:01.001 --> 02:47:02.991
because of that single equal sign.

02:47:02.991 --> 02:47:05.831
But this isn't sufficient
in C. What else

02:47:05.831 --> 02:47:08.891
is missing on the right-hand
side, instinctively now?

02:47:08.891 --> 02:47:11.486
Even if you've never
programmed in this before.

02:47:11.486 --> 02:47:12.111
Yeah, in front.

02:47:12.111 --> 02:47:13.151
AUDIENCE: Semicolon.

02:47:13.151 --> 02:47:14.776
DAVID J. MALAN: A semicolon at the end.

02:47:14.776 --> 02:47:16.941
And one other thing, I
think, is probably missing.

02:47:16.941 --> 02:47:17.441
Again.

02:47:17.441 --> 02:47:18.414
AUDIENCE: A data type.

02:47:18.414 --> 02:47:19.581
DAVID J. MALAN: A data type.

02:47:19.581 --> 02:47:22.671
So if we can keep going
back and forth here,

02:47:22.671 --> 02:47:26.321
what data type seems appropriate
intuitively for counter?

02:47:26.321 --> 02:47:27.431
int for integer.

02:47:27.431 --> 02:47:29.861
So, indeed, we need to
tell the computer when

02:47:29.861 --> 02:47:32.531
creating a variable what
type of data we want,

02:47:32.531 --> 02:47:35.721
and we need to finish our
thought with the semicolon.

02:47:35.721 --> 02:47:38.141
So there might be a counterpart there.

02:47:38.141 --> 02:47:43.271
What about in Scratch if we wanted
to increment that counter variable?

02:47:43.271 --> 02:47:45.671
We had this very user-friendly
puzzle piece last time

02:47:45.671 --> 02:47:49.741
that was change counter
by 1, or add 1 to counter.

02:47:49.741 --> 02:47:54.121
In C, here's where things get
a little more interesting.

02:47:54.121 --> 02:47:58.111
And pretty commonly done, you might
do this. counter = counter + 1;

02:47:58.111 --> 02:47:59.311
with a semicolon.

02:47:59.311 --> 02:48:02.221
And this is where, again, it's
important to note, the equal sign,

02:48:02.221 --> 02:48:03.361
it's not equality.

02:48:03.361 --> 02:48:05.341
Otherwise, this makes no sense.

02:48:05.341 --> 02:48:08.251
counter cannot equal
counter plus 1, right?

02:48:08.251 --> 02:48:11.171
That just doesn't work if we're
talking about integers here.

02:48:11.171 --> 02:48:13.411
That's because the equal
sign is assignment.

02:48:13.411 --> 02:48:15.751
So it can certainly be the
case that you calculate

02:48:15.751 --> 02:48:19.921
counter plus 1, whatever that is,
then you update the value of counter

02:48:19.921 --> 02:48:22.871
from right to left to be that new value.

02:48:22.871 --> 02:48:25.201
This, as we'll see,
is a very common thing

02:48:25.201 --> 02:48:29.101
to do in programming just to kind of
count upward, for whatever reason.

02:48:29.101 --> 02:48:30.931
You can write this more succinctly.

02:48:30.931 --> 02:48:34.711
This code here is what we'll
call syntactic sugar, sort

02:48:34.711 --> 02:48:39.541
of a fancy way of saying the same thing
with fewer words or fewer characters

02:48:39.541 --> 02:48:40.321
on the screen.

02:48:40.321 --> 02:48:44.221
This also adds 1, or whatever
number you type over here,

02:48:44.221 --> 02:48:46.081
to the variable on the left.

02:48:46.081 --> 02:48:49.771
And there's one other form of syntactic
sugar we're going to start seeing too,

02:48:49.771 --> 02:48:51.601
and it's even more terse than this.

02:48:51.601 --> 02:48:56.851
That too will increment counter by 1
by literally changing its value by 1.

02:48:56.851 --> 02:48:59.701
Or if you change it to minus
minus, subtracting 1 from it.

02:48:59.701 --> 02:49:02.311
You can't do that with
2 and 3 and 4, but you

02:49:02.311 --> 02:49:07.861
can do it by default with just plus plus
or minus minus adding or subtracting 1.

02:49:07.861 --> 02:49:08.762
Yeah.

02:49:08.762 --> 02:49:12.611
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:49:12.611 --> 02:49:15.641
DAVID J. MALAN: Ah, so when you are
changing a variable that already

02:49:15.641 --> 02:49:20.201
has been created, as we did with
the code that looked like this,

02:49:20.201 --> 02:49:23.591
you no longer need to remind the
computer what the data type is.

02:49:23.591 --> 02:49:27.221
Thankfully, the computer is
at least as smart as that.

02:49:27.221 --> 02:49:31.991
It will remember the type of
the data that you intended.

02:49:31.991 --> 02:49:34.941
Other questions or comments on this?

02:49:34.941 --> 02:49:36.191
All right, that's quite a lot.

02:49:36.191 --> 02:49:38.571
Why don't we go ahead and
here take a 10-minute break?

02:49:38.571 --> 02:49:41.771
And we'll be back, we'll
start writing some code.

02:49:41.771 --> 02:49:44.981
All right, so we are back.

02:49:44.981 --> 02:49:48.581
We've just looked at some
of the basics of compiling,

02:49:48.581 --> 02:49:50.336
even if it doesn't
quite feel that basic.

02:49:50.336 --> 02:49:52.211
But now, let's actually
start focusing really

02:49:52.211 --> 02:49:55.511
on writing more and more code,
more and more interesting

02:49:55.511 --> 02:49:58.701
code, kind of like we dove
into Scratch last week.

02:49:58.701 --> 02:50:00.611
So here I have these code open.

02:50:00.611 --> 02:50:01.571
I've closed the GUI.

02:50:01.571 --> 02:50:04.361
I'm going to focus more on my
terminal window and my code editors.

02:50:04.361 --> 02:50:07.444
Many different ways I can create new
files, but I want to create something

02:50:07.444 --> 02:50:08.511
called a calculator.

02:50:08.511 --> 02:50:11.501
So, again, within this
environment of VS Code,

02:50:11.501 --> 02:50:15.581
I can literally write the code
command which is VS Code specific,

02:50:15.581 --> 02:50:18.371
and it just creates a new
file for me automatically.

02:50:18.371 --> 02:50:20.411
Or I could do that in the GUI.

02:50:20.411 --> 02:50:23.383
I'm going to go ahead and create
this file called calculator.c

02:50:23.383 --> 02:50:25.841
and I'm going to go ahead and
include some familiar things.

02:50:25.841 --> 02:50:30.851
So I'm just going to go ahead and
proactively include cs50.h, stdio.h.

02:50:30.851 --> 02:50:33.911
I'm going to go ahead from
memory and do the int void main--

02:50:33.911 --> 02:50:38.249
more on that next week, why it's
int, why it's void, and so forth.

02:50:38.249 --> 02:50:40.541
And now let me just implement
a very simple calculator.

02:50:40.541 --> 02:50:44.361
We saw some mathematical
operators, like plus and the like.

02:50:44.361 --> 02:50:45.861
So let's actually use this.

02:50:45.861 --> 02:50:48.671
So let me go ahead and
first give myself a variable

02:50:48.671 --> 02:50:52.481
called x, sort of like grade
school math or algebra.

02:50:52.481 --> 02:50:55.571
Let me go ahead then and
get an int, which is new,

02:50:55.571 --> 02:50:57.251
but I mentioned this exists.

02:50:57.251 --> 02:51:00.641
And then let me just ask the user
for whatever their x value is.

02:51:00.641 --> 02:51:03.984
The thing in the quotes is
just the English, or the string

02:51:03.984 --> 02:51:06.651
that I'm printing on the screen.
so I could say anything I want.

02:51:06.651 --> 02:51:09.931
I'm just going to say x colon
to prompt the user accordingly.

02:51:09.931 --> 02:51:12.431
Now I'm going to go ahead and
get another variable called y.

02:51:12.431 --> 02:51:13.631
I'm going to get int again.

02:51:13.631 --> 02:51:16.271
And now, I'm going to
prompt the user for y.

02:51:16.271 --> 02:51:18.551
And I'm just very nitpickly
using a space just

02:51:18.551 --> 02:51:22.031
to move the cursor so it doesn't
look too messy on the screen.

02:51:22.031 --> 02:51:27.221
And then lastly, let me go ahead and
just print out the sum of x and y.

02:51:27.221 --> 02:51:31.721
In an ideal world, I would just
say something like printf x + y.

02:51:31.721 --> 02:51:36.641
But that is not valid in C. The
first argument, recall, in printf

02:51:36.641 --> 02:51:39.261
has to be a string in double quotes.

02:51:39.261 --> 02:51:43.061
So if I want to print out
the value of an integer,

02:51:43.061 --> 02:51:47.321
I need to put something in quotes
here, maybe followed by a newline,

02:51:47.321 --> 02:51:49.221
if I want to move the cursor as well.

02:51:49.221 --> 02:51:51.641
So, again, we only glimpsed
it briefly, but what

02:51:51.641 --> 02:51:55.481
do I replace these question marks with
if I want a placeholder for an integer?

02:51:55.481 --> 02:51:56.571
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:51:56.571 --> 02:51:57.981
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so %i.

02:51:57.981 --> 02:52:00.591
Just like %s was string, %i is integer.

02:52:00.591 --> 02:52:02.361
So I change this %i.

02:52:02.361 --> 02:52:06.351
And now if I want to add x and y, for
instance, super-- simple calculator,

02:52:06.351 --> 02:52:09.771
doesn't do much of anything other
than addition of two integers--

02:52:09.771 --> 02:52:11.341
I think this works.

02:52:11.341 --> 02:52:14.091
And, again, it looks definitely
cryptic at first glance.

02:52:14.091 --> 02:52:16.491
It would be if programming
weren't this cryptic.

02:52:16.491 --> 02:52:18.511
Other languages will
clean this up for us.

02:52:18.511 --> 02:52:22.191
But, again, if you focus on the
basics, printf takes one input first--

02:52:22.191 --> 02:52:26.151
which is a format string with
English or whatever language,

02:52:26.151 --> 02:52:27.591
some placeholders, maybe--

02:52:27.591 --> 02:52:31.551
then it takes potentially more
arguments after the comma,

02:52:31.551 --> 02:52:33.921
like the value of x plus y.

02:52:33.921 --> 02:52:36.801
All right, let me go ahead
now and make calculator,

02:52:36.801 --> 02:52:41.301
which, again, compiles
my source code in C,

02:52:41.301 --> 02:52:44.481
pictured above, and converts
it into corresponding machine

02:52:44.481 --> 02:52:46.431
code, or zeros and ones.

02:52:46.431 --> 02:52:47.481
No error messages.

02:52:47.481 --> 02:52:48.771
so that's already good.

02:52:48.771 --> 02:52:50.721
Now I do ./calculator.

02:52:50.721 --> 02:52:53.841
Let's do 1 plus 1 and Enter.

02:52:53.841 --> 02:52:54.681
Voila.

02:52:54.681 --> 02:52:57.531
Now I have the makings of a calculator.

02:52:57.531 --> 02:53:00.421
Now let's start to tinker
with this a little bit.

02:53:00.421 --> 02:53:02.271
What if I instead had done this?

02:53:02.271 --> 02:53:08.151
int z = x + y and then plug-in z here.

02:53:08.151 --> 02:53:14.841
If I rerun make calculator, Enter,
rerun ./calculator, type in 1 plus 1,

02:53:14.841 --> 02:53:20.791
still equals 2, and let me claim that
it will work for other values as well--

02:53:20.791 --> 02:53:22.821
which of these versions
is better designed?

02:53:22.821 --> 02:53:27.651
If both seem to be correct at very
cursory glance, is this version better

02:53:27.651 --> 02:53:30.711
or is the previous one without the z?

02:53:30.711 --> 02:53:34.191
OK, so this one is arguably better
because I've now got a reusable

02:53:34.191 --> 02:53:36.501
variable called z that I
cannot only print but, heck,

02:53:36.501 --> 02:53:39.301
if my program is longer,
I can use it elsewhere.

02:53:39.301 --> 02:53:40.523
Counterthoughts?

02:53:40.523 --> 02:53:41.398
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:53:41.398 --> 02:53:42.273
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

02:53:42.273 --> 02:53:44.681
Debatable, like before, because
it depends on my intent.

02:53:44.681 --> 02:53:46.556
And, honestly, I think
a pretty good argument

02:53:46.556 --> 02:53:48.001
can be made for the first version.

02:53:48.001 --> 02:53:50.851
Because if I have no
intention of-- as you note--

02:53:50.851 --> 02:53:53.801
using that variable
again, you know what?

02:53:53.801 --> 02:53:55.801
Maybe I might as well do
this, just because it's

02:53:55.801 --> 02:53:57.331
one less thing to think about.

02:53:57.331 --> 02:53:58.441
It's one less distraction.

02:53:58.441 --> 02:54:00.901
It's one less line of code
to have to understand.

02:54:00.901 --> 02:54:02.041
It's just a little tighter.

02:54:02.041 --> 02:54:04.771
So here, again, it does
depend on your intention.

02:54:04.771 --> 02:54:07.021
But this field is pretty reasonable.

02:54:07.021 --> 02:54:09.301
And I think, as someone
noted earlier, when

02:54:09.301 --> 02:54:13.411
I did the same thing with get_string,
that, yeah, maybe kind of crossed

02:54:13.411 --> 02:54:16.336
s line because get_string and the
what's your name inside of it,

02:54:16.336 --> 02:54:17.461
it was just so much longer.

02:54:17.461 --> 02:54:20.851
But x + y, eh, it's not that hard
to wrap our mind around what's

02:54:20.851 --> 02:54:23.061
going on inside of the printf argument.

02:54:23.061 --> 02:54:25.831
So, again, these are the kinds
of thoughts that hopefully you'll

02:54:25.831 --> 02:54:28.531
acquire the instinct for
on not necessarily reaching

02:54:28.531 --> 02:54:30.991
the same answer as someone
else, but, again, the thought

02:54:30.991 --> 02:54:33.221
process is what matters here.

02:54:33.221 --> 02:54:36.181
All right, so how might I enhance
this program a little bit?

02:54:36.181 --> 02:54:38.471
Let's just talk about
style for just a moment.

02:54:38.471 --> 02:54:42.991
So x and y, at least in this case,
are pretty reasonable variable names.

02:54:42.991 --> 02:54:43.500
Why?

02:54:43.500 --> 02:54:45.750
Because that's the go-to
variable names in math

02:54:45.750 --> 02:54:47.375
when you're adding two things together.

02:54:47.375 --> 02:54:48.910
So x and y seem pretty reasonable.

02:54:48.910 --> 02:54:53.160
I could have done something like,
well, maybe my first variable

02:54:53.160 --> 02:54:56.731
should be called first
number and my next variable

02:54:56.731 --> 02:54:58.553
should be called second number.

02:54:58.553 --> 02:55:00.511
And then down here, I
would have to change this

02:55:00.511 --> 02:55:04.330
to first number plus second number.

02:55:04.330 --> 02:55:07.051
Like, eh, this isn't really
adding anything semantically

02:55:07.051 --> 02:55:08.341
to help my comprehension.

02:55:08.341 --> 02:55:11.231
But that would be one other
direction we could have taken things.

02:55:11.231 --> 02:55:14.761
So if you have very simple
ideas that are conventionally

02:55:14.761 --> 02:55:18.790
expressed with common variable names
like x and y, totally fine here.

02:55:18.790 --> 02:55:22.591
What if I want to annotate this program
and remind myself what it is it does?

02:55:22.591 --> 02:55:25.381
Well, I can add in C
what are called comments.

02:55:25.381 --> 02:55:30.031
With a slash slash, two forward slashes,
you can write a note to yourself,

02:55:30.031 --> 02:55:32.580
like prompt user for x.

02:55:32.580 --> 02:55:35.401
And then down here, I could
do something like prompt user

02:55:35.401 --> 02:55:37.620
for y, just to remind
myself what I'm doing there.

02:55:37.620 --> 02:55:40.171
And down here, perform addition.

02:55:40.171 --> 02:55:42.991
Now, in this case, I'm not
sure these commands are really

02:55:42.991 --> 02:55:44.101
adding all that much.

02:55:44.101 --> 02:55:47.821
Because in the time it took me to write
and eventually read these comments,

02:55:47.821 --> 02:55:49.951
I could have just read
the three lines of code.

02:55:49.951 --> 02:55:52.891
But as our programs
get more sophisticated

02:55:52.891 --> 02:55:55.549
and you start to learn more syntax--

02:55:55.549 --> 02:55:58.091
that, honestly, you might forget
the next day, the next week,

02:55:58.091 --> 02:56:01.891
the next month-- might be useful
to have these notes to self that

02:56:01.891 --> 02:56:04.681
reminds you of what your
code is doing or maybe even

02:56:04.681 --> 02:56:06.421
how it is doing that thing.

02:56:06.421 --> 02:56:09.330
With these early programs,
not really necessary,

02:56:09.330 --> 02:56:11.671
doesn't really add all that
much to our comprehension,

02:56:11.671 --> 02:56:14.160
but it is a mechanism
you have in place that

02:56:14.160 --> 02:56:18.061
can help you actually remind
yourself or remind someone

02:56:18.061 --> 02:56:20.279
else what it is that's going on.

02:56:20.279 --> 02:56:23.071
Well, let me go ahead and rerun
this again in this current version,

02:56:23.071 --> 02:56:24.431
make calculator.

02:56:24.431 --> 02:56:27.001
And here, too, you might
think I'm typing crazy fast--

02:56:27.001 --> 02:56:27.691
not really.

02:56:27.691 --> 02:56:28.771
I'm hitting Tab a lot.

02:56:28.771 --> 02:56:32.201
So it turns out that
Linux, the operating system

02:56:32.201 --> 02:56:33.511
we're using here in the cloud--

02:56:33.511 --> 02:56:36.961
but, actually, Windows and Mac
OS nowadays support this too--

02:56:36.961 --> 02:56:38.801
supports autocomplete.

02:56:38.801 --> 02:56:42.301
So if you only have one
program that starts with C-A-L,

02:56:42.301 --> 02:56:45.211
you don't have to finish writing
calculator, you can just hit Tab,

02:56:45.211 --> 02:56:47.771
and the computer will
finish your thought for you.

02:56:47.771 --> 02:56:51.931
The other thing you can do is
if you hit Up and keep going up,

02:56:51.931 --> 02:56:54.424
you'll scroll through your
entire history of commands.

02:56:54.424 --> 02:56:56.341
So there too, I've been
saving some keystrokes

02:56:56.341 --> 02:56:59.174
by hitting Up quickly rather than
retyping the same darn thing again

02:56:59.174 --> 02:56:59.831
and again.

02:56:59.831 --> 02:57:02.131
So, again, just another
little convenience

02:57:02.131 --> 02:57:05.911
to make programming and interacting with
the command line interface even faster.

02:57:05.911 --> 02:57:09.281
All right, let me go ahead and just make
sure it's compiled in the current form.

02:57:09.281 --> 02:57:11.131
The comments have no functional impact.

02:57:11.131 --> 02:57:13.231
These green things are
just notes to self.

02:57:13.231 --> 02:57:15.421
Let me run calculator with
maybe-- how about this?

02:57:15.421 --> 02:57:19.441
Instead of 1 plus 1,
how about 1 billion--

02:57:22.004 --> 02:57:23.171
whoops, let's do that again.

02:57:23.171 --> 02:57:24.461
Wa, da, da.

02:57:24.461 --> 02:57:30.176
1 million, 1 billion, and another 1
billion, and that answer is 2 billion.

02:57:30.176 --> 02:57:31.551
All right, so that seems correct.

02:57:31.551 --> 02:57:33.093
Let's run this program one more time.

02:57:33.093 --> 02:57:39.281
How about 2 billion
plus another 2 billion?

02:57:39.281 --> 02:57:42.121
Did you know that?

02:57:42.121 --> 02:57:45.121
So, apparently, it's not so correct.

02:57:45.121 --> 02:57:49.591
And, clearly, running 1 plus 1 was
not the most robust testing of my code

02:57:49.591 --> 02:57:50.551
here.

02:57:50.551 --> 02:57:53.236
What might have gone wrong?

02:57:53.236 --> 02:57:54.361
What might have gone wrong?

02:57:54.361 --> 02:57:55.246
Yeah.

02:57:55.246 --> 02:57:57.466
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:57:57.466 --> 02:57:58.341
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

02:57:58.341 --> 02:58:00.511
The computer probably ran
out of space with bits.

02:58:00.511 --> 02:58:05.271
So it turns out with these data types--
we've been talking about string and int

02:58:05.271 --> 02:58:09.651
and also float and char and those
other things-- they all use a specific,

02:58:09.651 --> 02:58:13.281
and, most importantly, finite
number of bits to represent them.

02:58:13.281 --> 02:58:14.961
It can vary by computer.

02:58:14.961 --> 02:58:18.891
Newer computers use more bits, older
computers tended to use fewer bits.

02:58:18.891 --> 02:58:21.841
It's not necessarily standardized
for all of these data types.

02:58:21.841 --> 02:58:28.161
But in this case, in this environment,
it is using 32 bits for an integer.

02:58:28.161 --> 02:58:28.836
That's a lot.

02:58:28.836 --> 02:58:30.981
So with 32 bits, you
can count pretty high.

02:58:30.981 --> 02:58:34.161
This is 64 light bulbs on the
stage and could count even higher.

02:58:34.161 --> 02:58:38.331
An int is only using half of these, or
we have two integers here on the stage.

02:58:38.331 --> 02:58:42.351
Now, if you think back to last week,
we talked about 8 bits at one point.

02:58:42.351 --> 02:58:47.371
And if you have 8 bits, 8 zeros and
ones, you can count as high as 256--

02:58:47.371 --> 02:58:49.701
just a good number to
generally remember as trivia.

02:58:49.701 --> 02:58:53.541
8 bits gives you 256
permutations of zeros and ones.

02:58:53.541 --> 02:58:57.441
32 gives you roughly how
many, if anyone knows?

02:58:57.441 --> 02:58:59.841
It's 2 to the 32 power.

02:58:59.841 --> 02:59:02.511
So it's roughly 4 billion, 2 to the 32.

02:59:02.511 --> 02:59:04.311
If you don't know that, it's fine.

02:59:04.311 --> 02:59:07.311
Most programmers, though, eventually
remember these kinds of heuristics.

02:59:07.311 --> 02:59:08.811
So it's roughly 4 billion.

02:59:08.811 --> 02:59:10.791
So that feels like enough.

02:59:10.791 --> 02:59:13.881
2 billion plus 2 billion
is exactly 4 billion.

02:59:13.881 --> 02:59:17.961
And that actually should
fit in a 32-bit integer.

02:59:17.961 --> 02:59:20.751
The catch is that my Mac,
your PC, and the like

02:59:20.751 --> 02:59:22.791
also like to support negative numbers.

02:59:22.791 --> 02:59:26.151
And if you want to support both positive
and negative numbers, that technically

02:59:26.151 --> 02:59:29.271
means with 32-bit integers,
you can count as high

02:59:29.271 --> 02:59:33.543
as roughly 2 billion positive
or 2 billion negative

02:59:33.543 --> 02:59:34.501
in the other direction.

02:59:34.501 --> 02:59:38.061
That's still 4 billion, give or
take, but it's only half as many

02:59:38.061 --> 02:59:39.661
in one direction or the other.

02:59:39.661 --> 02:59:44.211
So how could I go about implementing
a correct calculator here?

02:59:44.211 --> 02:59:47.601
What might the solution be?

02:59:47.601 --> 02:59:50.181
Yeah, so not just li,
which was for long integer.

02:59:50.181 --> 02:59:54.631
I have to make one more change,
which is to the data type itself.

02:59:54.631 --> 02:59:59.091
So let me go back up here and change
x from an int to a long, a.k.a.

02:59:59.091 --> 03:00:00.231
long integer.

03:00:00.231 --> 03:00:02.401
And then let me change y as well.

03:00:02.401 --> 03:00:05.901
And then let me change the format code
per the little cheat sheet we had up

03:00:05.901 --> 03:00:07.971
a few minutes ago to li.

03:00:07.971 --> 03:00:10.581
Let me recompile the calculator--

03:00:10.581 --> 03:00:11.571
seems to work OK.

03:00:11.571 --> 03:00:12.631
Let's rerun it.

03:00:12.631 --> 03:00:14.061
Now let's do 1 plus 1.

03:00:14.061 --> 03:00:15.601
That's should obviously be the same.

03:00:15.601 --> 03:00:20.701
Now let's do 2 billion
and another 2 billion

03:00:20.701 --> 03:00:22.331
and cross our fingers this time.

03:00:22.331 --> 03:00:24.721
Now we're counting as high as 4 billion.

03:00:24.721 --> 03:00:27.871
And we can go way higher than
4 billion, but we're only

03:00:27.871 --> 03:00:29.761
kicking the can down the street a bit.

03:00:29.761 --> 03:00:31.261
Even though we're now using--

03:00:31.261 --> 03:00:32.431
with a long--

03:00:32.431 --> 03:00:37.781
64 bits, which is as long as this
stage now, that's still a finite value.

03:00:37.781 --> 03:00:40.381
It might be a really big
value, but it's still finite.

03:00:40.381 --> 03:00:42.811
And we'll come back at the
end of today to these kinds

03:00:42.811 --> 03:00:44.041
of fundamental limitations.

03:00:44.041 --> 03:00:48.311
Because arguably now, my calculator
is correct for like millions,

03:00:48.311 --> 03:00:51.281
billions of possible inputs but not all.

03:00:51.281 --> 03:00:53.401
And that's problematic
if you actually want

03:00:53.401 --> 03:00:58.741
to use my calculator for any
possible inputs, not just

03:00:58.741 --> 03:01:03.301
ones that are roughly less than,
say, 2 billion, as in this case.

03:01:03.301 --> 03:01:05.293
All right, any questions then on that?

03:01:05.293 --> 03:01:07.501
But it's really just a
precursor for all the problems

03:01:07.501 --> 03:01:10.506
that we're going to have to
eventually deal with later on.

03:01:10.506 --> 03:01:15.688
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:01:15.688 --> 03:01:17.021
DAVID J. MALAN: A good question.

03:01:17.021 --> 03:01:17.261
Yes.

03:01:17.261 --> 03:01:20.351
If we were still using z, we would
also have to change it to a long.

03:01:20.351 --> 03:01:23.471
Otherwise, we'd be ignoring
32 of the bits that

03:01:23.471 --> 03:01:25.991
had been added together via the longs.

03:01:25.991 --> 03:01:27.831
Good question.

03:01:27.831 --> 03:01:32.351
All right, so how about we spice things
up with maybe not just addition here,

03:01:32.351 --> 03:01:35.711
how about something
with some conditions?

03:01:35.711 --> 03:01:37.631
Let's start to ask
some actual questions.

03:01:37.631 --> 03:01:44.091
So a moment ago, recall that we had
just the declaration of variables.

03:01:44.091 --> 03:01:46.091
Now let's look back at
something in Scratch that

03:01:46.091 --> 03:01:48.633
looked a little something like
this, a bunch of puzzle pieces

03:01:48.633 --> 03:01:50.711
asking questions by way
of these conditionals

03:01:50.711 --> 03:01:53.711
and then these Boolean expressions
here in green, maybe saying something

03:01:53.711 --> 03:01:55.241
like x is less than y.

03:01:55.241 --> 03:01:58.301
In C, this actually maps pretty cleanly.

03:01:58.301 --> 03:02:02.291
It's much cleaner from left to right
than it was with printf and join.

03:02:02.291 --> 03:02:04.991
Here, we have just code
that looks like this.

03:02:04.991 --> 03:02:09.251
If, a space, two parentheses
and then x less than y,

03:02:09.251 --> 03:02:12.224
and then we have something like
printf there in the middle.

03:02:12.224 --> 03:02:14.141
So here, it's actually
kind of a nice mapping.

03:02:14.141 --> 03:02:16.511
Notice that, just as the
yellow puzzle piece in Scratch

03:02:16.511 --> 03:02:18.644
is kind of hugging the
purple puzzle piece,

03:02:18.644 --> 03:02:21.311
that's effectively the role that
these curly braces are playing.

03:02:21.311 --> 03:02:24.701
They're sort of encapsulating
all of the code on the inside.

03:02:24.701 --> 03:02:27.851
The parentheses represent
the Boolean expression

03:02:27.851 --> 03:02:32.411
that needs to be asked and answered to
decide whether or not to do this thing.

03:02:32.411 --> 03:02:34.841
And here's an exception to
what I alluded to earlier.

03:02:34.841 --> 03:02:38.891
Usually, when you see a word and
then a parenthesis, something,

03:02:38.891 --> 03:02:42.131
and then closed parenthesis, I
claimed that's usually a function.

03:02:42.131 --> 03:02:44.321
And I'm still feeling pretty
good about that claim.

03:02:44.321 --> 03:02:45.761
But there are exceptions.

03:02:45.761 --> 03:02:48.281
And the word if is not a function.

03:02:48.281 --> 03:02:50.501
It's just a programming construct.

03:02:50.501 --> 03:02:55.421
It's a feature of the C language
that similarly uses parentheses, just

03:02:55.421 --> 03:02:58.001
for different purposes
for a Boolean expression.

03:02:58.001 --> 03:02:59.411
How about something like this?

03:02:59.411 --> 03:03:02.501
Last week, if you wanted to
have a two-way fork in the road,

03:03:02.501 --> 03:03:05.501
go this way or that way,
you can have if and else.

03:03:05.501 --> 03:03:08.091
In C, that would look a
little something like this.

03:03:08.091 --> 03:03:11.111
And if we add in the printf's,
it now looks quite like the same,

03:03:11.111 --> 03:03:15.041
but it adds, of course, the word else
and then a couple of more curly braces.

03:03:15.041 --> 03:03:21.191
As an aside, in C, It's not strictly
necessary to have curly braces

03:03:21.191 --> 03:03:25.781
if you have only one line
of code indented underneath.

03:03:25.781 --> 03:03:30.068
For best practice, though, do so anyway,
because it makes super clear to you

03:03:30.068 --> 03:03:31.901
and ultimately anyone
else reading your code

03:03:31.901 --> 03:03:35.411
that you intend for just that one
or more line of code to execute.

03:03:35.411 --> 03:03:37.061
How about this from last week?

03:03:37.061 --> 03:03:38.681
Here was a three-way fork in the road.

03:03:38.681 --> 03:03:45.073
If x is less than y, else if x is
greater than y, else if x equals y.

03:03:45.073 --> 03:03:47.531
Now, here's where you have some
disparities between Scratch

03:03:47.531 --> 03:03:52.751
and C. Scratch uses an equals sign
for equality, to compare two values.

03:03:52.751 --> 03:03:55.841
C uses a single equals sign
for assignment from right

03:03:55.841 --> 03:03:58.611
to left, minor difference
between the two worlds.

03:03:58.611 --> 03:04:02.831
In C, we could implement the same
code like this, the addition being

03:04:02.831 --> 03:04:04.331
just this additional else if.

03:04:04.331 --> 03:04:08.696
And if we add in the printf's, it
looks a little something now like this.

03:04:08.696 --> 03:04:12.461
This is correct both in the
Scratch world and in the C world.

03:04:12.461 --> 03:04:16.421
But could someone make a claim that
this is not, again, well-designed?

03:04:16.421 --> 03:04:17.141
Exactly.

03:04:17.141 --> 03:04:18.581
We don't need the last if.

03:04:18.581 --> 03:04:21.731
We need the else, at least,
but we don't need the last if.

03:04:21.731 --> 03:04:24.221
Because, at least in the
world of comparing integers,

03:04:24.221 --> 03:04:27.221
it's either going to be less
than, greater than, or equal to.

03:04:27.221 --> 03:04:28.731
There is no other case.

03:04:28.731 --> 03:04:31.331
So you can save a few
seconds, if you will,

03:04:31.331 --> 03:04:35.381
of your program running-- a blink of
the eye-- by only asking two questions

03:04:35.381 --> 03:04:37.871
and then inferring what
the answer to the third

03:04:37.871 --> 03:04:41.481
must be just by nature of
your own human logic here.

03:04:41.481 --> 03:04:42.821
Now, why is that a good thing?

03:04:42.821 --> 03:04:46.571
If, for instance, x and y
happen to equal each other--

03:04:46.571 --> 03:04:51.341
I type in 1 and 1 for both values,
either in Scratch or in the C world--

03:04:51.341 --> 03:04:55.571
in the case of this version,
you're sort of stupidly

03:04:55.571 --> 03:04:59.021
asking three questions, all of
which are going to get asked

03:04:59.021 --> 03:05:02.321
even though the answer is no, no, yes.

03:05:02.321 --> 03:05:05.801
That is false, false, true.

03:05:05.801 --> 03:05:09.641
That seems to be unnecessary because
if we instead optimize this code,

03:05:09.641 --> 03:05:13.451
get rid of the unnecessary if and
just do as you proposed logically--

03:05:13.451 --> 03:05:16.481
else print that x is equal to y--

03:05:16.481 --> 03:05:20.961
now if x indeed equals y because
they're both 1 or some other value,

03:05:20.961 --> 03:05:26.201
now you're only going to ask two
questions, so 2/3 as many questions,

03:05:26.201 --> 03:05:29.481
and then you're going to get
your same correct result.

03:05:29.481 --> 03:05:32.141
So, again, a minor detail,
but, again, the kinds of things

03:05:32.141 --> 03:05:33.849
you should be thinking
about, not only as

03:05:33.849 --> 03:05:36.431
you write your code to
be correct but also write

03:05:36.431 --> 03:05:38.831
it to be well-designed as well.

03:05:38.831 --> 03:05:41.081
All right, so why don't we
go ahead and translate this

03:05:41.081 --> 03:05:44.651
into the context of an
actual program here?

03:05:44.651 --> 03:05:46.571
I'll create a blank window here.

03:05:46.571 --> 03:05:50.441
And let's do something with points,
like points on my own very first CS50

03:05:50.441 --> 03:05:51.521
problem set.

03:05:51.521 --> 03:05:54.641
Let me go ahead and
run code of points.c.

03:05:54.641 --> 03:05:56.711
That's just going to
give me a new text file.

03:05:56.711 --> 03:06:00.761
And then up here, I'm going to
do my usual, include cs50.h.

03:06:00.761 --> 03:06:03.161
include stdio.h.

03:06:03.161 --> 03:06:04.781
int main void.

03:06:04.781 --> 03:06:08.231
So a lot of boilerplate, so to
speak, in these early programs.

03:06:08.231 --> 03:06:09.521
And now, let's see.

03:06:09.521 --> 03:06:12.821
Let's ask the user, how
many points did they

03:06:12.821 --> 03:06:15.061
lose on their most recent CS50 PSet?

03:06:15.061 --> 03:06:19.041
So sort of evoke my photograph of
my own very first PSet last week

03:06:19.041 --> 03:06:20.691
where I lost a couple of points myself.

03:06:20.691 --> 03:06:23.801
So int points = get_int.

03:06:23.801 --> 03:06:27.741
Then I'll ask a question in English
like, how many points did you lose,

03:06:27.741 --> 03:06:29.301
question mark, space?

03:06:29.301 --> 03:06:33.601
And then once I have this answer,
let's now ask some questions of it.

03:06:33.601 --> 03:06:36.861
So if points is less than 2--

03:06:36.861 --> 03:06:40.521
borrowing the syntax that we
saw on the screen a moment ago--

03:06:40.521 --> 03:06:42.921
let's go ahead and print
out something explanatory

03:06:42.921 --> 03:06:49.191
like you lost fewer points
than me, backslash n.

03:06:49.191 --> 03:06:52.221
else if points greater than 2--

03:06:52.221 --> 03:06:53.991
which is, again how many I lost--

03:06:53.991 --> 03:06:59.661
I'm going to go ahead and print out you
lost more points than me, backslash n.

03:06:59.661 --> 03:07:03.848
else if-- wait a minute, else seems
to be sufficient logically here.

03:07:03.848 --> 03:07:05.931
I'm just going to go ahead
and print out something

03:07:05.931 --> 03:07:12.621
like you lost the same number
of points as me, backslash n.

03:07:12.621 --> 03:07:16.791
So, really, just a straightforward
application of that simple idea

03:07:16.791 --> 03:07:18.961
but to a concrete scenario here.

03:07:18.961 --> 03:07:21.091
So let me go ahead and save this.

03:07:21.091 --> 03:07:24.141
Let me go ahead and
run make points, Enter.

03:07:24.141 --> 03:07:26.031
No errors, that's good.

03:07:26.031 --> 03:07:27.021
Run points.

03:07:27.021 --> 03:07:29.331
And then, how many points did you lose?

03:07:29.331 --> 03:07:31.103
How about, it's 1 point?

03:07:31.103 --> 03:07:32.811
All right, you lost
fewer points than me.

03:07:32.811 --> 03:07:34.341
How about 0 points?

03:07:34.341 --> 03:07:34.941
Even better.

03:07:34.941 --> 03:07:36.171
How about 3 points?

03:07:36.171 --> 03:07:37.081
And so forth.

03:07:37.081 --> 03:07:40.851
So, again, we have the ability to
express in C now pretty basic idea

03:07:40.851 --> 03:07:44.841
from last week in reality, which is
this notion of conditionals and asking

03:07:44.841 --> 03:07:46.111
questions.

03:07:46.111 --> 03:07:50.811
There's something subtle here, though,
that's maybe not super well-designed

03:07:50.811 --> 03:07:53.391
that someone might call a magic number.

03:07:53.391 --> 03:07:56.581
This is programming speak
for something I've done here.

03:07:56.581 --> 03:08:01.881
There's a bit of redundancy unrelated
to the if and the else and the else.

03:08:01.881 --> 03:08:07.471
But is there something I typed twice
just to ask, perhaps, for the obvious?

03:08:07.471 --> 03:08:11.431
Exactly, I've hard-coded, so to speak,
manually typed out the number 2--

03:08:11.431 --> 03:08:13.771
in two locations, in this case--

03:08:13.771 --> 03:08:15.701
that did not come from the user.

03:08:15.701 --> 03:08:18.601
So, apparently, once I
compile this, this is it.

03:08:18.601 --> 03:08:21.671
You're always comparing
yourself to me in like, 1996,

03:08:21.671 --> 03:08:24.631
which for better or for worse,
is all the program can do.

03:08:24.631 --> 03:08:27.931
But this is an example too of
a magic number in the sense

03:08:27.931 --> 03:08:31.691
like, wait, where did that 2 come
from, and why is it in two places?

03:08:31.691 --> 03:08:35.251
It feels like we are setting the
stage for just a higher probability

03:08:35.251 --> 03:08:36.481
of screwing up down the road.

03:08:36.481 --> 03:08:39.931
Because the longer this code gets,
suppose I'm comparing against 2 points

03:08:39.931 --> 03:08:40.681
elsewhere--

03:08:40.681 --> 03:08:42.901
2, 3, 4, 5 places--

03:08:42.901 --> 03:08:45.031
am I going to keep typing the number 2?

03:08:45.031 --> 03:08:46.291
Like, yeah, that's fine.

03:08:46.291 --> 03:08:46.981
It's correct.

03:08:46.981 --> 03:08:47.911
It's going to work.

03:08:47.911 --> 03:08:49.861
But, honestly, eventually,
you're going to screw up,

03:08:49.861 --> 03:08:52.411
and you're going to miss one of the
2's, you're going to change it to a 3,

03:08:52.411 --> 03:08:54.911
because maybe I did worse the
next year, or 1, I did better.

03:08:54.911 --> 03:08:57.551
And you don't want these
numbers to get out of sync.

03:08:57.551 --> 03:09:01.051
So what would be a logical
improvement to this design,

03:09:01.051 --> 03:09:04.111
rather than hard-coding the
same number sort of magically

03:09:04.111 --> 03:09:07.011
in two or more places?

03:09:07.011 --> 03:09:09.689
Yeah, why don't I make a
variable that I can use in there?

03:09:09.689 --> 03:09:11.481
So, for instance, I
could create a variable

03:09:11.481 --> 03:09:14.541
like this, another integer called mine.

03:09:14.541 --> 03:09:16.371
And I'm just going to
initialize it to 2.

03:09:16.371 --> 03:09:19.371
And then I'm going to change
mentions of 2 to this.

03:09:19.371 --> 03:09:23.001
And mine is a pretty reasonable
name for a variable insofar

03:09:23.001 --> 03:09:27.261
as it refers to exactly
whose points are in question.

03:09:27.261 --> 03:09:29.781
There's a risk here,
though, minor though it is.

03:09:29.781 --> 03:09:32.361
I could accidentally
change mine at some point.

03:09:32.361 --> 03:09:36.451
Maybe I forget what mine represents,
and I do some addition or subtraction.

03:09:36.451 --> 03:09:39.021
So there's a way to tell the
computer "don't trust me,

03:09:39.021 --> 03:09:40.731
because I'm going to
screw up eventually"

03:09:40.731 --> 03:09:43.311
by making a variable constant too.

03:09:43.311 --> 03:09:45.781
So a constant in a
programming language--

03:09:45.781 --> 03:09:47.421
this did not exist in Scratch--

03:09:47.421 --> 03:09:50.541
is just an additional hint to the
computer that essentially enables

03:09:50.541 --> 03:09:52.131
you to program more defensively.

03:09:52.131 --> 03:09:55.341
If you don't trust
yourself necessarily to not

03:09:55.341 --> 03:09:57.651
screw up later, or
honestly, in practice,

03:09:57.651 --> 03:10:01.161
if you know that number should
never change, make it constant

03:10:01.161 --> 03:10:02.821
and never think about it again.

03:10:02.821 --> 03:10:08.091
This tells the compiler to make sure
that even you later in your code cannot

03:10:08.091 --> 03:10:09.741
change the number 2.

03:10:09.741 --> 03:10:14.241
And another convention in C and other
languages, when you have a constant,

03:10:14.241 --> 03:10:16.693
it's often common to just
capitalize the variable.

03:10:16.693 --> 03:10:18.651
Kind of like you're
yelling, but it really just

03:10:18.651 --> 03:10:20.003
visually makes it stand out.

03:10:20.003 --> 03:10:21.711
So it's kind of like
a nice rule of thumb

03:10:21.711 --> 03:10:24.591
that helps you realize, oh,
that must be a constant.

03:10:24.591 --> 03:10:27.291
Capitalization alone does
not make it constant.

03:10:27.291 --> 03:10:28.881
The word const does.

03:10:28.881 --> 03:10:31.131
But the capitalization
is just a visual reminder

03:10:31.131 --> 03:10:35.041
that this is somewhere,
somehow a constant.

03:10:35.041 --> 03:10:37.491
So just a minor refinement,
but, again, we're

03:10:37.491 --> 03:10:41.451
sort of getting better at
programming just by instilling

03:10:41.451 --> 03:10:43.701
these kinds of heuristics.

03:10:43.701 --> 03:10:48.021
Questions, then, on conditionals
in C or these constants?

03:10:48.021 --> 03:10:48.757
Yeah.

03:10:48.757 --> 03:10:53.811
AUDIENCE: Why do you not use
semicolons after line 9 and line 13?

03:10:53.811 --> 03:10:58.761
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, why do you
not use a semicolon in lines 9, 13?

03:10:58.761 --> 03:10:59.361
Just because.

03:10:59.361 --> 03:11:01.401
This is the way the
language was designed.

03:11:01.401 --> 03:11:03.501
And it's confusing early on.

03:11:03.501 --> 03:11:07.221
Generally speaking, when you're
using conditionals-- and eventually,

03:11:07.221 --> 03:11:08.181
we'll see loops--

03:11:08.181 --> 03:11:09.861
there's no semicolons involved.

03:11:09.861 --> 03:11:14.781
For now, assume that semicolons usually
finish your thought after a function.

03:11:14.781 --> 03:11:18.411
That's not 100% reliable of a heuristic,
but it'll get you most of the way

03:11:18.411 --> 03:11:19.941
there.

03:11:19.941 --> 03:11:20.721
And just because.

03:11:20.721 --> 03:11:23.721
Left hand was not talking to the right
hand when some of these languages

03:11:23.721 --> 03:11:25.191
were designed.

03:11:25.191 --> 03:11:27.391
All right, so let's do something else.

03:11:27.391 --> 03:11:28.681
How about this?

03:11:28.681 --> 03:11:31.761
If I have the ability to ask
something conditionally--

03:11:31.761 --> 03:11:33.741
is this thing true or
is this other thing--

03:11:33.741 --> 03:11:36.651
could I write a very simple program
that does something basic like,

03:11:36.651 --> 03:11:39.981
tells me if a number the
human types is even or odd?

03:11:39.981 --> 03:11:42.391
Well, let me just get the
framework for that in place.

03:11:42.391 --> 03:11:44.991
Let me go ahead and
write code of a parity--

03:11:44.991 --> 03:11:47.061
is a fancy way of saying even or odd.

03:11:47.061 --> 03:11:53.511
And let me go ahead and include cs50.h,
include stdio.h, int main void--

03:11:53.511 --> 03:11:55.521
again, more on those down the road.

03:11:55.521 --> 03:11:59.781
But, for now, I'm going to go ahead
and get a number n from the user

03:11:59.781 --> 03:12:03.351
by calling get_int and asking
them for whatever n is.

03:12:03.351 --> 03:12:07.171
And then now I'm going to
introduce some pseudocode.

03:12:07.171 --> 03:12:08.931
So here's the first
example of a program,

03:12:08.931 --> 03:12:12.001
honestly, that I'm not
really sure how to proceed.

03:12:12.001 --> 03:12:14.691
So let me just resort to some
pseudocode using comments.

03:12:14.691 --> 03:12:17.221
Eventually, I'll get rid of
this and write actual code.

03:12:17.221 --> 03:12:22.676
But if n is even, then print--

03:12:22.676 --> 03:12:24.051
actually, let me just print that.

03:12:24.051 --> 03:12:27.411
Let me just go ahead and say
printf, quote unquote, "even",

03:12:27.411 --> 03:12:29.631
because I know how to use printf.

03:12:29.631 --> 03:12:33.231
else-- all right, I
know how to printf odd,

03:12:33.231 --> 03:12:35.901
so let me just say printf,
quote unquote, "odd".

03:12:35.901 --> 03:12:39.631
So here, I've sort of taken a bite
out of the problem, if you will.

03:12:39.631 --> 03:12:42.681
And let me go ahead and put
in my little placeholders.

03:12:42.681 --> 03:12:44.581
I want to do some kind of conditions.

03:12:44.581 --> 03:12:49.401
So if, question marks now, let me go
ahead and fill in the blanks here.

03:12:49.401 --> 03:12:51.511
else I'll put this here.

03:12:51.511 --> 03:12:53.541
So I think I'm OK now.

03:12:53.541 --> 03:12:55.641
I'm getting closer to solving this.

03:12:55.641 --> 03:12:59.371
But I still have this
question mark here.

03:12:59.371 --> 03:13:06.091
How, using syntax we've seen, might
I determine if n is even or odd?

03:13:06.091 --> 03:13:07.621
What do you think?

03:13:07.621 --> 03:13:08.121
Nice.

03:13:08.121 --> 03:13:12.051
There's this little operator I
mentioned by name earlier, the remainder

03:13:12.051 --> 03:13:14.101
operator, that will let
you do exactly that.

03:13:14.101 --> 03:13:16.663
If you divide any number by
2, that mathematical heuristic

03:13:16.663 --> 03:13:19.371
is going to tell you if it's even
or odd based on whether there's

03:13:19.371 --> 03:13:21.291
a remainder of 0 or 1.

03:13:21.291 --> 03:13:25.041
And that's nice because the alternative
would seem to be doing something stupid

03:13:25.041 --> 03:13:33.441
like if n == 0 or if n
equals 2 or n equals 4--

03:13:33.441 --> 03:13:37.071
your code would be infinitely long if
you had to ask all possible questions.

03:13:37.071 --> 03:13:43.911
But if I do n divided by 2
and look at the remainder--

03:13:43.911 --> 03:13:47.281
it's a little cryptic, but
this will indeed do the trick.

03:13:47.281 --> 03:13:51.471
So the percent sign is
the remainder operator.

03:13:51.471 --> 03:13:56.791
It does numerator divided by denominator
and returns not the result of that

03:13:56.791 --> 03:13:58.981
but, rather, the remainder of that.

03:13:58.981 --> 03:14:02.511
So if you divide anything by 2,
it's going to be a 0 or 1 remainder.

03:14:02.511 --> 03:14:06.681
And if, indeed, 2 divides
into n evenly, giving you 0,

03:14:06.681 --> 03:14:08.061
then you're going to print even.

03:14:08.061 --> 03:14:10.071
Else, it's got to be odd.

03:14:10.071 --> 03:14:14.751
But there is something odd-- pun
intended-- in this highlighted line.

03:14:14.751 --> 03:14:20.641
What is another new piece of syntax,
apparently, besides the percent sign?

03:14:20.641 --> 03:14:21.901
What's a little off there?

03:14:21.901 --> 03:14:24.011
Yeah.

03:14:24.011 --> 03:14:25.541
Yeah, so that's not a typo.

03:14:25.541 --> 03:14:28.091
And I even caught myself
verbally saying it a moment ago,

03:14:28.091 --> 03:14:29.471
just because it's so ingrained.

03:14:29.471 --> 03:14:32.981
What must this mean here?

03:14:32.981 --> 03:14:33.706
Yeah.

03:14:33.706 --> 03:14:34.581
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:14:34.581 --> 03:14:36.331
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, if
something's equivalent to the other.

03:14:36.331 --> 03:14:38.481
So now this is the equality operator.

03:14:38.481 --> 03:14:40.281
It's not assignment from right to left.

03:14:40.281 --> 03:14:42.561
And this one too is an
example of, literally,

03:14:42.561 --> 03:14:45.441
humans not really planning
ahead, perhaps, left hand

03:14:45.441 --> 03:14:47.541
not talking to right hand
in that someone decided,

03:14:47.541 --> 03:14:49.161
let's use the equals
sign for assignment.

03:14:49.161 --> 03:14:52.036
And then some number of minutes or
days later, people are like, damn,

03:14:52.036 --> 03:14:53.841
how do we now compare for equality?

03:14:53.841 --> 03:14:55.054
Well, let's just use two.

03:14:55.054 --> 03:14:58.221
And if you think this is a little weird,
in some languages, like JavaScript,

03:14:58.221 --> 03:15:01.051
there's a third version where
you use three equal signs.

03:15:01.051 --> 03:15:03.331
So, again, it's humans that
design these languages.

03:15:03.331 --> 03:15:06.528
So if you're ever frustrated by them,
confused by them, eh, admittedly,

03:15:06.528 --> 03:15:08.361
it might just not have
been the best design.

03:15:08.361 --> 03:15:11.191
But we just kind of have
to live with it ever since.

03:15:11.191 --> 03:15:12.891
So let me go ahead and zoom out here.

03:15:12.891 --> 03:15:15.751
Let me go ahead and make parity here.

03:15:15.751 --> 03:15:20.571
So make parity-- and, again, parity
is just the name of my file, parity.c.

03:15:20.571 --> 03:15:23.301
./parity, type in a number like 2.

03:15:23.301 --> 03:15:24.381
That's indeed even.

03:15:24.381 --> 03:15:26.421
4, that's indeed even.

03:15:26.421 --> 03:15:29.721
3, that's indeed odd, and so forth.

03:15:29.721 --> 03:15:32.871
If we continue testing, presumably,
we'll get the same kinds of answers.

03:15:32.871 --> 03:15:34.171
How about something else?

03:15:34.171 --> 03:15:37.701
Let me go ahead now and let me start
copying and pasting some of this code

03:15:37.701 --> 03:15:40.761
because, admittedly, it's getting
a little tedious to keep typing out

03:15:40.761 --> 03:15:42.681
all of that boilerplate at the top.

03:15:42.681 --> 03:15:46.011
Let me create a program
called agree.c that's

03:15:46.011 --> 03:15:48.111
reminiscent of any of
those forms you have

03:15:48.111 --> 03:15:52.291
to agree to online with a checkbox
or typing in yes or no or the like.

03:15:52.291 --> 03:15:55.461
So let me throw away all the
guts of this main program

03:15:55.461 --> 03:15:57.781
and now ask something like this.

03:15:57.781 --> 03:16:01.621
Let me go ahead and prompt
user to agree to something.

03:16:01.621 --> 03:16:06.801
I'm going to go ahead and say, how
about get_string do you agree--

03:16:06.801 --> 03:16:12.111
whatever the question might be--
and I want the human to type y or n

03:16:12.111 --> 03:16:13.951
for yes or no, respectively.

03:16:13.951 --> 03:16:16.971
So if it's only a single
character, actually, I

03:16:16.971 --> 03:16:18.611
can actually get by with just get_char.

03:16:18.611 --> 03:16:20.361
Not used it before,
but it was on our menu

03:16:20.361 --> 03:16:22.341
of functions from the CS50 library.

03:16:22.341 --> 03:16:25.431
And if I want to get
the user's response,

03:16:25.431 --> 03:16:28.501
the return value should be
a char also on the left.

03:16:28.501 --> 03:16:31.281
So now we've seen strings,
ints, and now chars,

03:16:31.281 --> 03:16:33.171
if we only care about a single letter.

03:16:33.171 --> 03:16:38.151
And now let's go ahead,
check whether user agreed.

03:16:38.151 --> 03:16:47.751
So how about if c == "y", then let me
go ahead and, inside of my curly braces,

03:16:47.751 --> 03:16:51.901
print out agreed or some
such sentence like that.

03:16:51.901 --> 03:16:54.561
else if they did not type
c-- or you know what?

03:16:54.561 --> 03:16:56.391
Let's be explicit here,
just so they can't

03:16:56.391 --> 03:16:59.061
type z or b or some random letter.

03:16:59.061 --> 03:17:07.011
else if c=="n" n for no, then let me
go ahead and print out not agreed,

03:17:07.011 --> 03:17:07.979
or something like that.

03:17:07.979 --> 03:17:10.521
And I'm just going to ignore
the user if they don't cooperate

03:17:10.521 --> 03:17:14.781
and they type z or b or
something that's not y or n.

03:17:14.781 --> 03:17:21.141
All right, let me go ahead now and
compile this code, make agree, ./agree.

03:17:21.141 --> 03:17:22.071
All right, do I agree?

03:17:22.071 --> 03:17:22.571
Yes.

03:17:22.571 --> 03:17:25.251
Let's go with the default.
OK, so that seems to work.

03:17:25.251 --> 03:17:27.201
No, I don't agree this time.

03:17:27.201 --> 03:17:28.411
That seems to work.

03:17:28.411 --> 03:17:33.921
How about my caps lock key is on or
I'm just really yelling, capital Y?

03:17:33.921 --> 03:17:34.911
It ignores me.

03:17:34.911 --> 03:17:37.973
Capital N, it ignores me.

03:17:37.973 --> 03:17:41.181
So, obviously, a bug, at least if I want
to tolerate uppercase and lowercase,

03:17:41.181 --> 03:17:43.021
which is kind of reasonable.

03:17:43.021 --> 03:17:48.481
So what would be the possible
solutions here, do you think?

03:17:48.481 --> 03:17:51.621
How do I solve this and tolerate
both capital and lowercase?

03:17:51.621 --> 03:17:54.336
Maybe what's the simplest,
most naive implementation?

03:17:54.336 --> 03:17:56.491
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:17:56.491 --> 03:17:58.991
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so why
don't I just ask two questions?

03:17:58.991 --> 03:18:03.551
Or you know what, even more simplistic
based only on what we've seen before--

03:18:03.551 --> 03:18:06.911
if you will, let me just copy
and paste some of this code.

03:18:06.911 --> 03:18:11.781
Change this to an else-- whoops,
not in caps-- else if "Y".

03:18:11.781 --> 03:18:13.781
And then I bet I could
do the same thing with n.

03:18:13.781 --> 03:18:15.641
But here too, just like
with Scratch, as soon

03:18:15.641 --> 03:18:17.724
as you start to find
yourself copying and pasting,

03:18:17.724 --> 03:18:19.481
you're probably doing something wrong.

03:18:19.481 --> 03:18:22.811
And what you said verbally,
if I may, was actually better.

03:18:22.811 --> 03:18:29.501
Because you're implying that I could
just say something like OR c == "Y"

03:18:29.501 --> 03:18:32.841
or, down here, c == "N".

03:18:32.841 --> 03:18:39.711
The catch is, you can't use the word OR
in C. It's actually two vertical bars.

03:18:39.711 --> 03:18:43.181
So you can express one
question or another.

03:18:43.181 --> 03:18:46.301
You only need one of the
answers to be yes or true,

03:18:46.301 --> 03:18:48.221
and you use two vertical bars.

03:18:48.221 --> 03:18:50.981
By contrast, just so
you've seen it, if you

03:18:50.981 --> 03:18:54.341
wanted to check if something is equal
to something AND something else,

03:18:54.341 --> 03:18:56.891
you could use two ampersands.

03:18:56.891 --> 03:18:59.411
This logically would make
no sense here, though.

03:18:59.411 --> 03:19:02.651
Certainly, what the human typed can't
both be lowercase and uppercase.

03:19:02.651 --> 03:19:03.921
That just makes no sense.

03:19:03.921 --> 03:19:05.711
So in this case, we do want OR.

03:19:05.711 --> 03:19:07.476
But that allows me to
tighten my code up.

03:19:07.476 --> 03:19:09.851
I don't have to start copying
and pasting whole branches.

03:19:09.851 --> 03:19:13.561
I can now ask two questions at once.

03:19:13.561 --> 03:19:16.871
Questions, then, on this variation?

03:19:16.871 --> 03:19:17.869
Really good question.

03:19:17.869 --> 03:19:19.661
Can you convert the
input to all lowercase?

03:19:19.661 --> 03:19:20.831
Absolutely, you could.

03:19:20.831 --> 03:19:22.481
We don't have the capability yet.

03:19:22.481 --> 03:19:24.941
It turns out that's going to require--

03:19:24.941 --> 03:19:27.166
to be easy, another library,
though we could do it

03:19:27.166 --> 03:19:30.041
ourselves knowing a little bit about
ASCII or Unicode from last week.

03:19:30.041 --> 03:19:34.114
But, yes, that would be an alternative,
but more on that a different time.

03:19:34.114 --> 03:19:34.781
Other questions?

03:19:34.781 --> 03:19:37.141
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:19:37.141 --> 03:19:38.391
DAVID J. MALAN: Good question.

03:19:38.391 --> 03:19:41.988
Unfortunately, you have to be explicit
in C. You can't just say this,

03:19:41.988 --> 03:19:44.321
even though that's kind of
how you might think about it.

03:19:44.321 --> 03:19:49.241
You have to ask a complete question
using the equality sign twice

03:19:49.241 --> 03:19:50.231
in this case.

03:19:50.231 --> 03:19:51.941
Let me ask a question now too.

03:19:51.941 --> 03:19:53.051
It's not a typo.

03:19:53.051 --> 03:19:57.761
I deliberately used single quotes
around all of my single letters here.

03:19:57.761 --> 03:19:59.351
Why might that be?

03:19:59.351 --> 03:20:03.521
Previously, we used double quotes
for anything that looked like text.

03:20:03.521 --> 03:20:05.911
Yeah.

03:20:05.911 --> 03:20:08.971
Correct, string is double quotes for
multiple characters-- or even one,

03:20:08.971 --> 03:20:10.051
technically, but yes.

03:20:10.051 --> 03:20:14.211
And single quotes for single characters.

03:20:14.211 --> 03:20:15.801
Because my data type is different.

03:20:15.801 --> 03:20:18.771
I chose the simple route of
just using a single char.

03:20:18.771 --> 03:20:21.351
In fact, this program
won't work with Y-E-S

03:20:21.351 --> 03:20:25.041
or N-O. That's not supported at the
moment-- more on that another time.

03:20:25.041 --> 03:20:27.831
I had to use single quotes
because that's how C does it.

03:20:27.831 --> 03:20:29.791
If you're dealing with
single characters,

03:20:29.791 --> 03:20:31.851
a.k.a. chars, use single quotes.

03:20:31.851 --> 03:20:32.781
If it's a string--

03:20:32.781 --> 03:20:36.591
even if it's one single
character in a string

03:20:36.591 --> 03:20:39.624
as though you're starting to write
out a longer word or sentence--

03:20:39.624 --> 03:20:40.791
that would be double quotes.

03:20:40.791 --> 03:20:42.861
And we'll see why this
is before long too.

03:20:42.861 --> 03:20:46.551
But, again, just things to keep
in mind whenever writing code

03:20:46.551 --> 03:20:48.741
in this particular language.

03:20:48.741 --> 03:20:50.971
Yeah, down here.

03:20:50.971 --> 03:20:56.061
So, short answer, if I'm understanding
correctly, this would be incorrect.

03:20:56.061 --> 03:20:58.271
And this would be even more incorrect.

03:20:58.271 --> 03:21:00.771
But if you don't mind, let me
kick the can a couple of weeks

03:21:00.771 --> 03:21:02.481
on this as to why this doesn't work.

03:21:02.481 --> 03:21:06.853
The most pleasant way to do this would
indeed be to do something like this.

03:21:06.853 --> 03:21:08.811
But even this is a slippery
slope, because what

03:21:08.811 --> 03:21:12.179
if the user does something weird,
like they capitalize just the Y?

03:21:12.179 --> 03:21:13.971
You can imagine this
getting messy quickly.

03:21:13.971 --> 03:21:16.311
I like your idea earlier
about just forcing everything

03:21:16.311 --> 03:21:18.561
to lowercase just to standardize things.

03:21:18.561 --> 03:21:22.701
Unfortunately, you cannot compare
strings for equality like this

03:21:22.701 --> 03:21:24.781
for, again, reasons will
come to before long.

03:21:24.781 --> 03:21:27.681
So for today, we're keeping it
simple, even though, arguably, it's

03:21:27.681 --> 03:21:31.641
not nearly as user-friendly to
only tolerate individual letters.

03:21:31.641 --> 03:21:34.821
And there's a question over here.

03:21:34.821 --> 03:21:36.981
On the US English keyboard
it's shift and then

03:21:36.981 --> 03:21:40.081
the backslash key above Return,
but depending on your keyboard,

03:21:40.081 --> 03:21:42.321
it will vary.

03:21:42.321 --> 03:21:45.141
All right, so let's
actually now look back

03:21:45.141 --> 03:21:47.181
at something we did a
little bit of last week.

03:21:47.181 --> 03:21:49.971
Let me go ahead and open
a file called meow.c,

03:21:49.971 --> 03:21:52.461
because, recall, that's what
we had Scratch do initially.

03:21:52.461 --> 03:21:55.251
Let me include not the
C50 library this time,

03:21:55.251 --> 03:21:59.151
but just stdio.h because I
only want printf for this demo.

03:21:59.151 --> 03:22:02.721
Let me go ahead now and
just print out meow.

03:22:02.721 --> 03:22:06.411
And then if I want the cat to meow
three times, like it did last week,

03:22:06.411 --> 03:22:07.791
meow, meow, meow.

03:22:07.791 --> 03:22:08.631
Save it.

03:22:08.631 --> 03:22:10.711
make meow, ./meow.

03:22:10.711 --> 03:22:11.211
Voila.

03:22:11.211 --> 03:22:14.121
The program is written--
correct, I claim.

03:22:14.121 --> 03:22:14.751
It ran.

03:22:14.751 --> 03:22:15.591
It compiled OK.

03:22:15.591 --> 03:22:17.841
But, again, this was the
beginning of our conversation

03:22:17.841 --> 03:22:20.541
last week of not being
particularly well-designed.

03:22:20.541 --> 03:22:23.631
And if someone wants to maybe
point out the now obvious,

03:22:23.631 --> 03:22:28.381
why is this not
well-designed, necessarily?

03:22:28.381 --> 03:22:29.821
Yeah, it's just repetition, right?

03:22:29.821 --> 03:22:31.894
Again, I literally
resorted to copy-paste.

03:22:31.894 --> 03:22:33.811
That should be the signal
that you're probably

03:22:33.811 --> 03:22:37.871
doing something wrong or, at best,
just lazy of you, in this case.

03:22:37.871 --> 03:22:40.196
So the solution, as you
might glean from last week,

03:22:40.196 --> 03:22:42.571
is probably going to be one
of those things called loops.

03:22:42.571 --> 03:22:45.271
So let's just take a look at some
of the syntax for loops in C.

03:22:45.271 --> 03:22:48.084
But, again, no new ideas,
it's just some new syntax

03:22:48.084 --> 03:22:49.501
that'll take some getting used to.

03:22:49.501 --> 03:22:53.131
In Scratch, if you wanted to meow
forever with something like this,

03:22:53.131 --> 03:22:57.881
there's not a forever keyword in C, so
this one's a little weird to look at.

03:22:57.881 --> 03:22:59.221
But this is the best we can do.

03:22:59.221 --> 03:23:03.331
It turns out there is a
keyword called while in C.

03:23:03.331 --> 03:23:05.551
And that kind of has
the right semantics,

03:23:05.551 --> 03:23:08.741
because it's like while I do
something again and again,

03:23:08.741 --> 03:23:10.231
that's the best I can do.

03:23:10.231 --> 03:23:14.791
But just like an if condition
or an else if condition,

03:23:14.791 --> 03:23:18.061
those took a Boolean
expression in parentheses,

03:23:18.061 --> 03:23:21.131
a while loop also takes a Boolean
expression in parentheses.

03:23:21.131 --> 03:23:22.531
So I have to ask a question.

03:23:22.531 --> 03:23:26.041
Now, if I want to do something
forever, I could kind of stupidly just

03:23:26.041 --> 03:23:30.541
say while 2 is greater than
1, while 3 is greater than 2,

03:23:30.541 --> 03:23:32.431
or just something completely arbitrary.

03:23:32.431 --> 03:23:36.241
But that should rub you the wrong
way, because like, why 2 versus 1?

03:23:36.241 --> 03:23:41.131
Why 3-- if you want true, just say true.

03:23:41.131 --> 03:23:45.781
So it turns out in C, there are
special keywords, true and false,

03:23:45.781 --> 03:23:48.901
that are literally true
and false, respectively.

03:23:48.901 --> 03:23:53.341
I could also put the number 1 for
true and the number 0 for false,

03:23:53.341 --> 03:23:56.081
but most people would just
say true to be explicit.

03:23:56.081 --> 03:23:59.431
So it's a little hackish, if
you will, but very conventional.

03:23:59.431 --> 03:24:03.781
There's no forever keyword in C. If
I want to then print meow forever,

03:24:03.781 --> 03:24:06.191
I'm going to just use
something like printf here.

03:24:06.191 --> 03:24:08.731
So, again, not perfect
translation from one

03:24:08.731 --> 03:24:11.708
to the other, but absolutely
possible in C. What about this?

03:24:11.708 --> 03:24:14.041
This is a little more common
if you want to do something

03:24:14.041 --> 03:24:17.281
a finite number of times, like repeat 3.

03:24:17.281 --> 03:24:21.901
There's a few different ways we can
do this in C. Here's one approach.

03:24:21.901 --> 03:24:25.471
And here's where C-- like a
lot of text-based languages,

03:24:25.471 --> 03:24:28.921
you kind of have to whip out that
toolkit of all of the basic building

03:24:28.921 --> 03:24:31.171
blocks and think about,
all right, how can I

03:24:31.171 --> 03:24:35.881
build a little machine in software that
does something some number of times?

03:24:35.881 --> 03:24:40.381
Well, let me give myself a variable
called counter, set it equal to 0.

03:24:40.381 --> 03:24:46.741
Let me create a loop whose Boolean
expression is counter less than 3,

03:24:46.741 --> 03:24:50.311
the idea being here, why don't
I just kind of count 1, 2, 3?

03:24:50.311 --> 03:24:53.791
So how do I implement
this physicality in code?

03:24:53.791 --> 03:24:57.181
I give myself a variable,
set it to 0, 0 fingers up.

03:24:57.181 --> 03:25:00.001
Now, I ask the question,
is counter less than 3?

03:25:00.001 --> 03:25:02.971
If so, go ahead and print out meow.

03:25:02.971 --> 03:25:06.331
And just intuitively, even if
you've never seen C code or any code

03:25:06.331 --> 03:25:09.481
before Scratch, what
more do I need to do?

03:25:09.481 --> 03:25:12.421
I've left room here for
one more line of logic.

03:25:12.421 --> 03:25:13.991
Yeah.

03:25:13.991 --> 03:25:15.221
We have to increase counter.

03:25:15.221 --> 03:25:19.151
So I need code like I showed earlier,
like counter equals counter plus 1.

03:25:19.151 --> 03:25:21.401
And so here's where
programming sometimes

03:25:21.401 --> 03:25:22.921
becomes a bit more like plumbing.

03:25:22.921 --> 03:25:25.421
You can't just say what you
mean, like you couldn't Scratch.

03:25:25.421 --> 03:25:28.001
You have to build a little
sort of software machine

03:25:28.001 --> 03:25:31.181
that initializes a value, does
something, increments it, checks it.

03:25:31.181 --> 03:25:34.091
And so it's kind of like
this software-based machine,

03:25:34.091 --> 03:25:37.211
but together, that's just using
some familiar building blocks.

03:25:37.211 --> 03:25:38.381
But this is pretty common.

03:25:38.381 --> 03:25:40.673
Just like in Scratch, you
might have used loops a bunch

03:25:40.673 --> 03:25:42.161
of times, pretty common in C.

03:25:42.161 --> 03:25:44.051
So can we tighten this code up?

03:25:44.051 --> 03:25:48.581
This is correct, but here are
some conventions that are popular.

03:25:48.581 --> 03:25:51.026
If you're going to count, just say i.

03:25:51.026 --> 03:25:52.901
A convention in
programming-- with, at least,

03:25:52.901 --> 03:25:57.491
languages like C-- is just use i
as an integer if all its purpose is

03:25:57.491 --> 03:25:59.651
is to count from like, 0 on up.

03:25:59.651 --> 03:26:01.041
Counter is not wrong.

03:26:01.041 --> 03:26:01.901
It's not bad.

03:26:01.901 --> 03:26:04.781
It's just more verbose
than you need to be.

03:26:04.781 --> 03:26:05.741
Just call it i.

03:26:05.741 --> 03:26:07.533
You don't need more semantics than that.

03:26:07.533 --> 03:26:08.991
All right, what else can I do here?

03:26:08.991 --> 03:26:12.178
There's another opportunity
to tighten up this code.

03:26:12.178 --> 03:26:12.761
Do you recall?

03:26:12.761 --> 03:26:14.721
Yeah.

03:26:14.721 --> 03:26:17.721
Yeah, that syntactic sugar
that does nothing new,

03:26:17.721 --> 03:26:19.521
but it does it more succinctly.

03:26:19.521 --> 03:26:23.991
I can change this to either the
intermediate format or even tighter

03:26:23.991 --> 03:26:25.701
format of just i++.

03:26:25.701 --> 03:26:27.921
Now, this is pretty canonical.

03:26:27.921 --> 03:26:31.941
This is how most people
would implement something

03:26:31.941 --> 03:26:34.611
three times using a loop in C--

03:26:34.611 --> 03:26:36.336
using a while loop, that is.

03:26:36.336 --> 03:26:39.351
Turns out that it's so common
in C and other languages

03:26:39.351 --> 03:26:43.161
to do something finitely many times,
there's a couple of ways to do it.

03:26:43.161 --> 03:26:46.021
In this model, to be
clear, the logic, though,

03:26:46.021 --> 03:26:49.221
is that we start by initializing the
variable, like I've highlighted here.

03:26:49.221 --> 03:26:52.311
We then ask the question,
is i less than 0?

03:26:52.311 --> 03:26:56.031
If so, everything that's
indented inside the curly braces

03:26:56.031 --> 03:26:59.241
gets executed-- namely,
meow then the update.

03:26:59.241 --> 03:27:02.931
Then the computer is going to
have to recheck the condition

03:27:02.931 --> 03:27:06.531
to make sure that i hasn't gotten
so big that it's greater than 3.

03:27:06.531 --> 03:27:09.621
But if not, it then does this
again and it does this again.

03:27:09.621 --> 03:27:12.046
And then it repeats, constantly
checking the condition

03:27:12.046 --> 03:27:14.421
and executing what's in the
block, checking the condition

03:27:14.421 --> 03:27:15.838
and executing what's in the block.

03:27:15.838 --> 03:27:20.391
After three times of that, the condition
is going to be false, or a no answer,

03:27:20.391 --> 03:27:21.621
and that's it for the code.

03:27:21.621 --> 03:27:24.741
It just proceeds to whatever's
down here, just like with Scratch.

03:27:24.741 --> 03:27:27.391
It jumps to the next blocks down below.

03:27:27.391 --> 03:27:30.181
All right, what's another
way, though, to do this?

03:27:30.181 --> 03:27:32.181
Well, I've deliberately
been counting from 0--

03:27:32.181 --> 03:27:33.973
and that's a programming
convention, right?

03:27:33.973 --> 03:27:36.688
We started last week with all
the light bulbs off, which was 0.

03:27:36.688 --> 03:27:39.021
So it's pretty reasonable to
start counting at 0's, just

03:27:39.021 --> 03:27:39.854
like you would here.

03:27:39.854 --> 03:27:41.871
Like, no fingers are up, this is 0--

03:27:41.871 --> 03:27:43.561
fingers on your hand.

03:27:43.561 --> 03:27:47.841
But if you prefer, you could
start counting at i equals 1.

03:27:47.841 --> 03:27:51.141
But then you don't want to
do it while i is less than 3,

03:27:51.141 --> 03:27:54.201
you want to do i is
less than or equal to 3.

03:27:54.201 --> 03:27:58.641
On most keyboards, there's no symbol for
less than or equal to or greater than

03:27:58.641 --> 03:28:02.691
or equal to, so in C, you
use two characters, less than

03:28:02.691 --> 03:28:05.811
and then an equals sign
with no spaces in between.

03:28:05.811 --> 03:28:08.251
That just means less than or equal to.

03:28:08.251 --> 03:28:12.771
We could change it to set i to 2
and make this condition be less than

03:28:12.771 --> 03:28:13.821
or equal to 4.

03:28:13.821 --> 03:28:18.901
We could make this be a 10
and less than or equal to 12.

03:28:18.901 --> 03:28:20.811
But, again, just stick with the basics.

03:28:20.811 --> 03:28:23.931
Start at 0 and count on up
would be the convention.

03:28:23.931 --> 03:28:27.291
Or if you prefer to count
down, that's fine too.

03:28:27.291 --> 03:28:31.701
Set i to 3 and then do this so
long as i is greater than 0,

03:28:31.701 --> 03:28:34.553
but you have to decrement
instead of increment.

03:28:34.553 --> 03:28:36.261
So, again, we could
do this all day long.

03:28:36.261 --> 03:28:39.396
There's literally an infinite number
of ways to implement this idea.

03:28:39.396 --> 03:28:41.271
And that's why I keep
emphasizing convention.

03:28:41.271 --> 03:28:43.761
Call the variable i for
something like this,

03:28:43.761 --> 03:28:47.271
initialize it to 0 for something like
this, and just generally count up,

03:28:47.271 --> 03:28:49.161
unless you really prefer to count down.

03:28:49.161 --> 03:28:51.921
Again, just certain human conventions.

03:28:51.921 --> 03:28:55.111
All right, how about
another way to do this?

03:28:55.111 --> 03:28:59.071
This is what's called a for
loop in C, also very common.

03:28:59.071 --> 03:29:02.091
It's not quite as straightforward
in that it doesn't really read top

03:29:02.091 --> 03:29:04.011
to bottom in exactly the same way.

03:29:04.011 --> 03:29:07.401
This kind of has a lot more
logic tucked into its first line.

03:29:07.401 --> 03:29:09.921
But it does exactly the same thing.

03:29:09.921 --> 03:29:11.631
What happens here is--

03:29:11.631 --> 03:29:15.351
notice that inside the
parentheses, next to the word for,

03:29:15.351 --> 03:29:18.523
there's two semicolons-- which
is another weird use of syntax.

03:29:18.523 --> 03:29:20.481
They're not at the end
of the line, now they're

03:29:20.481 --> 03:29:21.856
in the middle of the parentheses.

03:29:21.856 --> 03:29:24.031
But that's what the
humans chose years ago.

03:29:24.031 --> 03:29:30.951
The first thing before the semicolons
initializes your variable, int i = 0.

03:29:30.951 --> 03:29:34.041
The next thing is the condition
that's going to constantly get

03:29:34.041 --> 03:29:36.561
checked every cycle through this loop.

03:29:36.561 --> 03:29:41.361
And the last thing is going to be
what you do after each loop, which

03:29:41.361 --> 03:29:42.991
in this case is going to be count up.

03:29:42.991 --> 03:29:45.711
So, again, if I rewind
we initialize i to 0.

03:29:45.711 --> 03:29:48.351
We then ask the question,
is i less than 3?

03:29:48.351 --> 03:29:52.611
If so, execute what's
inside of the loop.

03:29:52.611 --> 03:29:58.491
Then the computer does this, it does
the update, incrementing i by 1.

03:29:58.491 --> 03:30:01.221
And then it's not going
to blindly meow again.

03:30:01.221 --> 03:30:04.581
It's going to check again the
condition, is i less than 3?

03:30:04.581 --> 03:30:06.351
Then it's going to meow if so.

03:30:06.351 --> 03:30:10.761
Then it might go ahead and increment
i and check the condition again.

03:30:10.761 --> 03:30:14.449
So, again, this does not read quite
the same simple fashion top to bottom.

03:30:14.449 --> 03:30:16.741
You kind of read it left to
right and then jump around.

03:30:16.741 --> 03:30:21.891
But, again, the initialization,
the constant Boolean expression

03:30:21.891 --> 03:30:24.681
being checked, and the
update after each time

03:30:24.681 --> 03:30:32.551
does the exact same thing as what we saw
a moment ago in this while loop format.

03:30:32.551 --> 03:30:34.101
Which one is better?

03:30:34.101 --> 03:30:35.011
Eh, they're the same.

03:30:35.011 --> 03:30:37.053
I think most people would
probably eventually use

03:30:37.053 --> 03:30:42.571
a for loop once comfortable, but just
because is really the answer there.

03:30:42.571 --> 03:30:45.671
All right, any questions, then, on
loops as we've translated them to C?

03:30:45.671 --> 03:30:46.171
Yeah.

03:30:46.171 --> 03:30:47.923
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:30:47.923 --> 03:30:49.631
DAVID J. MALAN: A for
loop and while loop

03:30:49.631 --> 03:30:53.141
can both be used to do
exactly the same thing.

03:30:53.141 --> 03:30:56.733
There are subtle differences
with issues of scope,

03:30:56.733 --> 03:30:58.691
which we'll discuss before
long, where when you

03:30:58.691 --> 03:31:00.921
create a variable in a for loop--

03:31:00.921 --> 03:31:04.391
notice that it was, again, inside
of those parentheses, which

03:31:04.391 --> 03:31:08.831
technically means it's only going to
exist in these four lines of code.

03:31:08.831 --> 03:31:12.581
By contrast, with the while loop,
I declared my variable outside

03:31:12.581 --> 03:31:13.211
of the loop.

03:31:13.211 --> 03:31:17.031
That variable is going to continue
to exist elsewhere in my program.

03:31:17.031 --> 03:31:20.008
So that's one of the
minor differences there.

03:31:20.008 --> 03:31:20.591
Good question.

03:31:20.591 --> 03:31:22.871
But you'll see some others over time.

03:31:22.871 --> 03:31:26.091
All right, so we claim then
that it's better in some form

03:31:26.091 --> 03:31:27.291
to do this with loops.

03:31:27.291 --> 03:31:29.331
So let's actually jump back to the code.

03:31:29.331 --> 03:31:33.831
Let me go ahead and now re-implement
meowing with a for loop, for instance.

03:31:33.831 --> 03:31:39.701
So how about for int i
= 0, i less than 3, i++.

03:31:39.701 --> 03:31:44.441
Then inside my curly braces, let me go
ahead and print out with printf, meow,

03:31:44.441 --> 03:31:46.911
with a newline and a semicolon.

03:31:46.911 --> 03:31:50.661
So I did it pretty quickly just because
I've long acquired the muscle memory.

03:31:50.661 --> 03:31:53.591
But if I now make meow, no errors there.

03:31:53.591 --> 03:31:54.851
Run ./meow.

03:31:54.851 --> 03:31:57.101
And I see meow, meow, meow.

03:31:57.101 --> 03:31:59.231
Well, let's do now what
we did last week, which

03:31:59.231 --> 03:32:03.501
was to begin to make our own
custom functions, if you will,

03:32:03.501 --> 03:32:09.431
by using our own in C. So here's
where the syntax gets a little funky,

03:32:09.431 --> 03:32:13.841
but we'll explain over time what
each of these keywords is doing.

03:32:13.841 --> 03:32:17.201
If I want to create a
function called meow--

03:32:17.201 --> 03:32:21.281
because the authors of C did not create
a function called meow decades ago--

03:32:21.281 --> 03:32:23.951
I need to give it a name, like meow.

03:32:23.951 --> 03:32:26.531
I need to specify if
it takes any inputs.

03:32:26.531 --> 03:32:28.391
For now, I'm going to say no.

03:32:28.391 --> 03:32:34.201
And I'm going to explicitly say no
by writing the special word void.

03:32:34.201 --> 03:32:37.081
It's also necessary when
implementing a function in C--

03:32:37.081 --> 03:32:38.851
which was not necessary in Scratch--

03:32:38.851 --> 03:32:41.411
to specify what its return type is.

03:32:41.411 --> 03:32:45.061
But for now, I'm just going to say
that meow is the name of the function,

03:32:45.061 --> 03:32:46.591
it takes no inputs--

03:32:46.591 --> 03:32:49.531
and that's what the void
in parentheses means--

03:32:49.531 --> 03:32:54.091
and it does not return
anything like ask did,

03:32:54.091 --> 03:32:56.221
or like get_string or get_int does.

03:32:56.221 --> 03:32:59.941
meow's purpose in life is just to
have side effects, visual side effects

03:32:59.941 --> 03:33:01.991
by printing something on the screen.

03:33:01.991 --> 03:33:04.261
So what is meow going to do?

03:33:04.261 --> 03:33:06.481
I'm going to have it
quite simply say printf,

03:33:06.481 --> 03:33:10.291
quote unquote, "meow", backslash n.

03:33:10.291 --> 03:33:14.671
And now, just like in
Scratch, I can now just call

03:33:14.671 --> 03:33:16.831
a brand new function called meow.

03:33:16.831 --> 03:33:19.531
And here's where too, if you
really don't like the curly braces,

03:33:19.531 --> 03:33:22.591
technically speaking, you can
get rid of them when there's

03:33:22.591 --> 03:33:24.811
only one line of code inside your loop.

03:33:24.811 --> 03:33:27.511
But, again, stylistically,
I would encourage

03:33:27.511 --> 03:33:30.511
you to preserve them to make
super clear to yourself and others

03:33:30.511 --> 03:33:32.471
what it is that's going on.

03:33:32.471 --> 03:33:35.101
Let me go ahead and save
this and do make meow.

03:33:35.101 --> 03:33:36.531
Whoops.

03:33:36.531 --> 03:33:37.031
Darn.

03:33:37.031 --> 03:33:38.073
All right, what did I do?

03:33:38.073 --> 03:33:39.096
Something stupid.

03:33:39.096 --> 03:33:39.971
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:33:39.971 --> 03:33:42.141
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so
0 does not belong there.

03:33:42.141 --> 03:33:44.561
I meant to hit parenthesis.

03:33:44.561 --> 03:33:46.991
So let me rerun make meow.

03:33:46.991 --> 03:33:48.521
OK, fixed.

03:33:48.521 --> 03:33:49.601
My mistake.

03:33:49.601 --> 03:33:51.621
All right, it's still working OK.

03:33:51.621 --> 03:33:54.771
But recall what I did in Scratch,
kind of out of sight, out of mind.

03:33:54.771 --> 03:33:57.821
And just to make a point, let me
just highlight this and move it

03:33:57.821 --> 03:33:59.261
way down in the file.

03:33:59.261 --> 03:34:02.291
Because, again, now that meow
exists, it's an abstraction.

03:34:02.291 --> 03:34:04.601
I just know a meow function exists.

03:34:04.601 --> 03:34:06.111
I want to be able to use it.

03:34:06.111 --> 03:34:08.021
So let me scroll back up.

03:34:08.021 --> 03:34:09.731
My main function is the same.

03:34:09.731 --> 03:34:12.281
Let me go ahead and make meow again.

03:34:12.281 --> 03:34:17.531
And now, just by moving that function,
I've created all these lines of errors.

03:34:17.531 --> 03:34:18.851
And let's look at the first.

03:34:18.851 --> 03:34:21.018
Again, the rule of thumb
here-- it's a little small,

03:34:21.018 --> 03:34:25.061
but it says meow.c in bold-- which is
the name of the file where the bug is--

03:34:25.061 --> 03:34:27.911
5 is the line number,
and 20 is the character.

03:34:27.911 --> 03:34:30.741
So line number is enough alone.

03:34:30.741 --> 03:34:32.501
Let's see.

03:34:32.501 --> 03:34:36.611
Oh, this is what happens
when I scrolled up too far.

03:34:36.611 --> 03:34:37.211
Sorry.

03:34:37.211 --> 03:34:39.761
This is the error we're
now looking at, line 7.

03:34:39.761 --> 03:34:43.691
I was looking at the old error message
from earlier before I fixed the 0.

03:34:43.691 --> 03:34:45.551
meow.c line 7.

03:34:45.551 --> 03:34:49.781
All right, apparently, C does not
know what the meow function is.

03:34:49.781 --> 03:34:53.441
Implicit declaration of
function meow is invalid in C99.

03:34:53.441 --> 03:34:54.581
Well, what does that mean?

03:34:54.581 --> 03:34:57.941
Declaration of function means
your creation of a function.

03:34:57.941 --> 03:35:01.511
Like, I'm declaring that meow
exists, but I haven't apparently

03:35:01.511 --> 03:35:02.501
defined it yet.

03:35:02.501 --> 03:35:06.231
And then C99 is the version
of C from the year 1999,

03:35:06.231 --> 03:35:09.191
which we generally use here, it's
one of the more recent versions.

03:35:09.191 --> 03:35:12.461
So why is that the case?

03:35:12.461 --> 03:35:16.031
Can you infer from the mere fact
that I just moved meow to the bottom

03:35:16.031 --> 03:35:19.041
of the file-- which was fine
in Scratch but now is bad--

03:35:19.041 --> 03:35:20.419
why is that?

03:35:20.419 --> 03:35:21.866
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:35:21.866 --> 03:35:23.991
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, C is
just kind of old school.

03:35:23.991 --> 03:35:25.681
It reads your code top to bottom.

03:35:25.681 --> 03:35:30.001
And if it does not know what meow
is when you first try to use it,

03:35:30.001 --> 03:35:32.911
it just freaks out and prints
out these error messages.

03:35:32.911 --> 03:35:38.511
So the solution is, quite simply, don't
do that, just leave it where it was.

03:35:38.511 --> 03:35:42.471
But you can imagine this getting a
little annoying over time, if only

03:35:42.471 --> 03:35:46.761
because main is, by name, the
main part of your program.

03:35:46.761 --> 03:35:49.971
And, honestly, it would just
be nice if main were always

03:35:49.971 --> 03:35:51.099
at the top of your code.

03:35:51.099 --> 03:35:53.391
Because if you want to
understand what a file is doing,

03:35:53.391 --> 03:35:55.401
it makes sense to just
read it top to bottom.

03:35:55.401 --> 03:35:57.576
Well, there is a solution to this.

03:35:57.576 --> 03:36:02.721
You can put functions in different
orders with main at the top so long

03:36:02.721 --> 03:36:07.501
as you-- and this is perhaps the
only time copy paste is appropriate--

03:36:07.501 --> 03:36:10.671
so long as you leave a little
breadcrumb for the compiler

03:36:10.671 --> 03:36:13.131
at the very top of your
file that literally

03:36:13.131 --> 03:36:17.091
repeats the return value,
the name, and the arguments

03:36:17.091 --> 03:36:19.681
to that function, semicolon.

03:36:19.681 --> 03:36:22.791
This is, so to speak,
declaring your function--

03:36:22.791 --> 03:36:25.261
and the real fancy way
is this is a prototype.

03:36:25.261 --> 03:36:27.541
It's like, what is this
thing going to look like?

03:36:27.541 --> 03:36:30.481
But the semicolon means I'm not
going to deal with this yet.

03:36:30.481 --> 03:36:32.331
I'm going to actually
define the function

03:36:32.331 --> 03:36:34.701
or implement it down below here.

03:36:34.701 --> 03:36:36.681
This is kind of a stupid detail.

03:36:36.681 --> 03:36:40.221
More recent languages
get rid of this need,

03:36:40.221 --> 03:36:41.994
you can put your functions in any order.

03:36:41.994 --> 03:36:43.911
But, again, if you just
think about the basics

03:36:43.911 --> 03:36:46.231
of programming languages
like this one here--

03:36:46.231 --> 03:36:47.211
and as you noted--

03:36:47.211 --> 03:36:49.351
it must just be reading
your code top to bottom.

03:36:49.351 --> 03:36:53.061
So annoying, yes, but
explained, yes too.

03:36:53.061 --> 03:36:58.281
So let me go ahead and make meow one
more time, ./meow, still working OK.

03:36:58.281 --> 03:37:02.331
And let me make one final enhancement
to this meow program here.

03:37:02.331 --> 03:37:05.221
Let me go ahead now and
say something like this.

03:37:05.221 --> 03:37:07.191
Let me go ahead and say,
all right, wouldn't it

03:37:07.191 --> 03:37:12.941
be nice if my meow function could do
something for me some number of times?

03:37:12.941 --> 03:37:14.701
So suppose I want to do this.

03:37:14.701 --> 03:37:17.871
This meow function at the moment
is going to meow three times.

03:37:17.871 --> 03:37:21.111
But suppose I want to meow
n times, where n is just

03:37:21.111 --> 03:37:23.181
some number provided by the user.

03:37:23.181 --> 03:37:27.561
Well, just like in Scratch,
custom functions can take inputs,

03:37:27.561 --> 03:37:30.121
I just presently am saying void.

03:37:30.121 --> 03:37:34.551
But if I change this to int n,
thereby telling the compiler,

03:37:34.551 --> 03:37:38.151
hey, meow still doesn't
return something,

03:37:38.151 --> 03:37:40.581
but it does take something as input.

03:37:40.581 --> 03:37:43.581
It takes an integer,
and I want to call it n.

03:37:43.581 --> 03:37:46.071
So this is another way
of declaring a variable

03:37:46.071 --> 03:37:48.891
but a way of declaring a
variable that gets handed into,

03:37:48.891 --> 03:37:50.431
as input, the function.

03:37:50.431 --> 03:37:55.101
So now if I tighten up main here, now
I can actually do something really cool

03:37:55.101 --> 03:37:58.551
just like in Scratch, which is this.

03:37:58.551 --> 03:38:01.041
If I now look at this
code-- let me Zoom in here--

03:38:01.041 --> 03:38:04.311
now my main program is really
well-written in the sense

03:38:04.311 --> 03:38:07.191
that it just says what it
does, meow three times.

03:38:07.191 --> 03:38:11.031
This works, though, because I
defined meow as now taking an input,

03:38:11.031 --> 03:38:17.691
an integer called n, and then using
n in my now familiar for loop.

03:38:17.691 --> 03:38:18.771
There's one change.

03:38:18.771 --> 03:38:21.271
You might have caught my one mistake.

03:38:21.271 --> 03:38:25.101
I also have to remind myself up
here to make that change too.

03:38:25.101 --> 03:38:27.891
Again, this is one of the only
redundancies or copy-paste

03:38:27.891 --> 03:38:29.511
that's sort of reasonable.

03:38:29.511 --> 03:38:32.031
But there, I have now a better version.

03:38:32.031 --> 03:38:36.381
So let me go ahead and rerun
this, make meow, ./meow.

03:38:36.381 --> 03:38:36.981
Voila.

03:38:36.981 --> 03:38:39.531
So, again, no change
in correctness but now,

03:38:39.531 --> 03:38:41.391
again, we're sort of
modularizing our code.

03:38:41.391 --> 03:38:44.721
And, heck, what you could do now-- and
this is just a tease about a feature

03:38:44.721 --> 03:38:45.501
down the road--

03:38:45.501 --> 03:38:48.321
those header files we talked
about early, those libraries,

03:38:48.321 --> 03:38:51.051
this is the kind of modularization
we're talking about.

03:38:51.051 --> 03:38:54.921
We, the staff, wrote a function called
get_string, get_int, and so forth,

03:38:54.921 --> 03:39:01.191
we put it in a file called CS50, and we
put little breadcrumbs-- specifically,

03:39:01.191 --> 03:39:03.381
these things called prototypes--

03:39:03.381 --> 03:39:05.571
in cs50.h.

03:39:05.571 --> 03:39:10.251
So that when you all, as aspiring
programmers, include cs50.h,

03:39:10.251 --> 03:39:14.301
you are sort of secretly telling the
compiler at the very top of your code

03:39:14.301 --> 03:39:16.291
what the menu of available functions is.

03:39:16.291 --> 03:39:16.791
Why?

03:39:16.791 --> 03:39:21.561
Because in CS50 is lines like
these-- obviously, not for meow,

03:39:21.561 --> 03:39:24.141
but for get_string,
get_int, and so forth.

03:39:24.141 --> 03:39:29.541
And stdio.h is the same lines
of code for things like printf.

03:39:29.541 --> 03:39:31.741
So that's all that's going on there.

03:39:31.741 --> 03:39:38.041
It's just a way of telling the computer
in advance what functions to expect.

03:39:38.041 --> 03:39:40.921
All right, any questions,
then, on these here?

03:39:43.801 --> 03:39:44.311
Correct.

03:39:44.311 --> 03:39:47.131
So if you don't mind, I
want to continue to wave

03:39:47.131 --> 03:39:49.051
my hand at that detail for today.

03:39:49.051 --> 03:39:53.581
Indeed, int main void is a little weird,
because what would the input domain be?

03:39:53.581 --> 03:39:55.668
We have no mechanism
for providing input yet.

03:39:55.668 --> 03:39:57.751
And what does it mean for
main to return anything?

03:39:57.751 --> 03:39:59.251
Like, who is it returning to?

03:39:59.251 --> 03:40:00.378
For another day, if we may.

03:40:00.378 --> 03:40:02.461
They're going to come into
play but that, for now,

03:40:02.461 --> 03:40:05.101
today is just something you
should take at face value,

03:40:05.101 --> 03:40:08.321
as necessary copy-paste
to begin programs.

03:40:08.321 --> 03:40:11.491
So meow is a function that takes an
input, the number of times to meow,

03:40:11.491 --> 03:40:14.971
but it didn't actually have a
return value, hence the void.

03:40:14.971 --> 03:40:17.521
But what if we actually want
to create our own function that

03:40:17.521 --> 03:40:20.431
not only takes 0 or
more inputs as arguments

03:40:20.431 --> 03:40:24.061
but also returns some value, maybe an
int, maybe a float, maybe something

03:40:24.061 --> 03:40:24.991
else altogether?

03:40:24.991 --> 03:40:27.641
Well, it turns out, in C,
we can do that as well.

03:40:27.641 --> 03:40:31.081
Let me go ahead and create a
new file here called discount.

03:40:31.081 --> 03:40:33.121
And let's implement a
quick program via which

03:40:33.121 --> 03:40:35.641
we can discount some regular
price by some percentage,

03:40:35.641 --> 03:40:37.741
as though there's a sale
going on in a store.

03:40:37.741 --> 03:40:44.491
Let me go ahead and include our usual
cs50.h followed by stdio.h at the top.

03:40:44.491 --> 03:40:47.551
Let me give myself int
main void as before.

03:40:47.551 --> 03:40:50.381
And inside of main, let's go
ahead and do something simple.

03:40:50.381 --> 03:40:52.471
Let's give ourselves a
float called regular,

03:40:52.471 --> 03:40:55.351
representing the regular
price of something in a store.

03:40:55.351 --> 03:40:58.111
Let's go ahead and get a float
from the user asking them

03:40:58.111 --> 03:41:00.481
what that regular price is.

03:41:00.481 --> 03:41:04.741
Then, next, let's go ahead and declare
a second variable-- also a float--

03:41:04.741 --> 03:41:07.951
called sale, ultimately
representing the sale price

03:41:07.951 --> 03:41:09.811
after some percentage discount off.

03:41:09.811 --> 03:41:13.021
And let's go ahead and simply
calculate whatever regular is.

03:41:13.021 --> 03:41:15.851
And, say, 15% off is a
pretty good discount.

03:41:15.851 --> 03:41:20.471
So let's go ahead and discount
regular, whatever it is, by 15%,

03:41:20.471 --> 03:41:23.911
which is equivalent, of course, to
multiplying it with the asterisk

03:41:23.911 --> 03:41:25.681
by 0.85.

03:41:25.681 --> 03:41:30.126
Of course, if we're taking off 15%,
we multiply the regular price by 0.85.

03:41:30.126 --> 03:41:32.251
Now, let's go ahead and
print out the results here.

03:41:32.251 --> 03:41:36.271
Let me go ahead and say
printf sale price, colon--

03:41:36.271 --> 03:41:38.941
let me go ahead and %f,
but, more specifically,

03:41:38.941 --> 03:41:43.951
%.2f because, at least in US currency
we typically show cents to two decimal

03:41:43.951 --> 03:41:44.611
places--

03:41:44.611 --> 03:41:45.751
followed by a newline.

03:41:45.751 --> 03:41:48.631
And then let me go ahead and
plug in the value of sale.

03:41:48.631 --> 03:41:52.231
All right, let's go down here
and do make discount, Enter.

03:41:52.231 --> 03:41:54.781
So far, so good-- ./discount.

03:41:54.781 --> 03:41:57.001
And the regular price is maybe $100.

03:41:57.001 --> 03:41:59.561
So the sale price should be $85.

03:41:59.561 --> 03:42:01.483
So our arithmetic seems
to be correct here.

03:42:01.483 --> 03:42:02.941
But let's fast-forward now in time.

03:42:02.941 --> 03:42:04.801
Suppose that we find
ourselves discounting

03:42:04.801 --> 03:42:07.728
a lot of prices in an
application, maybe a website

03:42:07.728 --> 03:42:10.561
like Amazon where they're offering
some kind of percentage discount.

03:42:10.561 --> 03:42:13.201
And it'd be nice to have
a reusable function that

03:42:13.201 --> 03:42:16.421
just does this arithmetic for
us, simple though it may be.

03:42:16.421 --> 03:42:18.511
So let's go ahead and
modify discount this time

03:42:18.511 --> 03:42:22.051
to give ourselves our own
function called discount,

03:42:22.051 --> 03:42:23.778
for instance, that takes an input--

03:42:23.778 --> 03:42:25.861
like the regular price
that you want to discount--

03:42:25.861 --> 03:42:28.073
and then it also returns a value.

03:42:28.073 --> 03:42:29.281
It doesn't just print it out.

03:42:29.281 --> 03:42:34.081
It returns a value, namely, a float
that represents what the sale price is.

03:42:34.081 --> 03:42:37.231
So let me go down
below main and go ahead

03:42:37.231 --> 03:42:39.901
and define a function that's
going to return a float,

03:42:39.901 --> 03:42:42.118
because we're dealing
with dollar amount still.

03:42:42.118 --> 03:42:43.951
The function is going
to be called discount.

03:42:43.951 --> 03:42:47.971
And it's going to take one input, like
the price that we want to discount.

03:42:47.971 --> 03:42:50.171
In here, I'm going to do
something very simple.

03:42:50.171 --> 03:42:55.811
I'm going to say float sale equals
whatever that price is times 0.85.

03:42:55.811 --> 03:42:58.053
And then I'm going to go
ahead and return sale.

03:42:58.053 --> 03:43:00.511
Now, for that matter, I can
actually tighten this up a bit.

03:43:00.511 --> 03:43:04.081
If I'm only declaring a variable
to store a value that I'm then

03:43:04.081 --> 03:43:09.191
returning with this keyword return, I
actually don't even need that variable.

03:43:09.191 --> 03:43:11.041
So I can delete the second line.

03:43:11.041 --> 03:43:13.681
And I can actually just go ahead
and get rid of that variable

03:43:13.681 --> 03:43:16.291
altogether and immediately
return whatever the arithmetic

03:43:16.291 --> 03:43:20.161
result is of taking the price input,
the argument that's being passed in,

03:43:20.161 --> 03:43:21.901
times 0.85.

03:43:21.901 --> 03:43:25.681
So very simple function that
simply does the discounting for me.

03:43:25.681 --> 03:43:29.011
As always, let me go
ahead and copy-paste--

03:43:29.011 --> 03:43:32.251
the only time it's OK to copy-paste--
the prototype of that function, so

03:43:32.251 --> 03:43:35.341
the top of the file, so that
when compiling this code,

03:43:35.341 --> 03:43:38.611
main has already seen
the word discount before.

03:43:38.611 --> 03:43:40.271
And now let me go into the code here.

03:43:40.271 --> 03:43:43.411
And instead of doing
the math myself in main,

03:43:43.411 --> 03:43:46.471
let me presume that we
have some function already

03:43:46.471 --> 03:43:50.791
in our toolkit called discount that
lets me discount the regular price

03:43:50.791 --> 03:43:52.846
and return that value.

03:43:52.846 --> 03:43:54.971
And then down here, my code
doesn't need to change.

03:43:54.971 --> 03:43:58.201
I'm still going to print out
sale the variable in which I'm

03:43:58.201 --> 03:44:00.441
storing that result. But
notice what I've done here.

03:44:00.441 --> 03:44:02.191
I've sort of abstracted
the way the notion

03:44:02.191 --> 03:44:06.091
of taking a discount by creating my
own function that takes a float called

03:44:06.091 --> 03:44:07.951
price, or anything else as input.

03:44:07.951 --> 03:44:10.441
It does a little bit of math,
simple though it is here,

03:44:10.441 --> 03:44:12.011
and then it returns a value.

03:44:12.011 --> 03:44:14.941
But notice that discount
is not printing that value.

03:44:14.941 --> 03:44:17.191
It's literally using
this other keyword called

03:44:17.191 --> 03:44:21.811
return so that I can hand back that
value, just like get_string hands

03:44:21.811 --> 03:44:25.411
back a value, just like get_int
back an integer without printing it

03:44:25.411 --> 03:44:29.851
for you-- so that I up here on
line 9 can go ahead and store

03:44:29.851 --> 03:44:33.721
that value in a variable if I want
and then actually print it out.

03:44:33.721 --> 03:44:38.441
Let me go ahead now and recompile
this code with make discount.

03:44:38.441 --> 03:44:40.381
Let me go ahead and do ./discount.

03:44:40.381 --> 03:44:43.041
And let's, again, do $100.

03:44:43.041 --> 03:44:46.531
Sale price is going to be $85 as well.

03:44:46.531 --> 03:44:50.721
Now, it turns out that functions don't
have to take just 0 or 1 argument

03:44:50.721 --> 03:44:51.261
as input.

03:44:51.261 --> 03:44:53.241
They can actually take 2 or 3 or more.

03:44:53.241 --> 03:44:57.201
So, in fact, suppose we wanted to now
enhance this version of my program

03:44:57.201 --> 03:45:01.401
and take in as input to the discount
function, not just the price

03:45:01.401 --> 03:45:03.891
that I want to discount but
also the percentage off,

03:45:03.891 --> 03:45:08.151
thereby allowing us to support not just
15% off but any number of percentage

03:45:08.151 --> 03:45:08.871
points off.

03:45:08.871 --> 03:45:13.611
Well, let me go up here and declare
an int, say, and call it percent_off.

03:45:13.611 --> 03:45:15.951
And let me ask the user
for how many percentage

03:45:15.951 --> 03:45:17.811
points they want to take off.

03:45:17.811 --> 03:45:21.501
So I'm going to say percent_off
inside of the prompt here,

03:45:21.501 --> 03:45:23.751
get that int called percent_off.

03:45:23.751 --> 03:45:26.871
And now in addition to
passing in regular as an input

03:45:26.871 --> 03:45:30.801
to the discount function, I'm
also going to pass in percent_off.

03:45:30.801 --> 03:45:34.821
But I need to tell the computer
that it is taking now two arguments,

03:45:34.821 --> 03:45:37.131
and the way I do this
is just with a comma

03:45:37.131 --> 03:45:39.381
down here in the
function's own definition.

03:45:39.381 --> 03:45:43.611
Here is going to be a percentage
argument, a second argument,

03:45:43.611 --> 03:45:44.511
per the comma.

03:45:44.511 --> 03:45:50.091
And I'm now going to use that
percentage in a slightly familiar way.

03:45:50.091 --> 03:45:53.481
I don't want to just do percentage
like this, because, of course,

03:45:53.481 --> 03:45:56.744
that's going to increase
the size of the total price.

03:45:56.744 --> 03:45:59.661
I actually need to do a little bit
of real-world math where if this is

03:45:59.661 --> 03:46:03.381
a percentage off, like the number
15 for 15 percentage points,

03:46:03.381 --> 03:46:06.591
I need to do 100 minus that
many percentage points,

03:46:06.591 --> 03:46:08.871
thereby giving me 100 minus 15--

03:46:08.871 --> 03:46:09.711
85.

03:46:09.711 --> 03:46:13.131
And then I need to divide
that by 100 in order now

03:46:13.131 --> 03:46:18.031
to give myself 0.85 times
the price that was passed in.

03:46:18.031 --> 03:46:22.911
But if I go ahead now and save this,
run, make discount one last time,

03:46:22.911 --> 03:46:24.891
I notice that I've
actually got an error here.

03:46:24.891 --> 03:46:26.301
What have I done wrong?

03:46:26.301 --> 03:46:28.221
Well, I need to change
that prototype too.

03:46:28.221 --> 03:46:30.719
And, again, this is admittedly
an annoying aspect of C

03:46:30.719 --> 03:46:32.511
that you have to maintain
consistency here.

03:46:32.511 --> 03:46:33.178
But that's fine.

03:46:33.178 --> 03:46:35.391
I'm just going to go up
here, change this to int

03:46:35.391 --> 03:46:37.641
percentage-- spelling incorrectly.

03:46:37.641 --> 03:46:40.581
And now let me retry
compilation, make discount,

03:46:40.581 --> 03:46:42.111
crossing my fingers this time.

03:46:42.111 --> 03:46:46.701
Worked OK. ./discount, and voila, $100.

03:46:46.701 --> 03:46:49.221
And percent off, say, 15 points.

03:46:49.221 --> 03:46:52.981
And, voila, $85.

03:46:52.981 --> 03:46:55.311
Now, it's worth noting
that I've deliberately

03:46:55.311 --> 03:46:58.401
returned the results of my
math from this function.

03:46:58.401 --> 03:47:01.791
I haven't just done the math on the
original variable that's being passed.

03:47:01.791 --> 03:47:03.951
In fact, if we take a look
at this second version

03:47:03.951 --> 03:47:07.771
where discount is now taking a price
argument and a percentage argument,

03:47:07.771 --> 03:47:10.041
notice that I'm not doing
something like this.

03:47:10.041 --> 03:47:14.421
I'm not just saying price
equals price times 100

03:47:14.421 --> 03:47:18.261
minus percentage divided
by 100 and leaving at that.

03:47:18.261 --> 03:47:21.951
The problem there is that
this variable price is going

03:47:21.951 --> 03:47:24.291
to be scoped to that discount function.

03:47:24.291 --> 03:47:27.231
And we'll encounter this again
before long, but this notion of scope

03:47:27.231 --> 03:47:32.091
just refers to where in which a
variable actually lives or exists

03:47:32.091 --> 03:47:33.211
or is accessible.

03:47:33.211 --> 03:47:36.201
So it turns out if I change price
in the context of this discount

03:47:36.201 --> 03:47:38.601
function, that's not going
to have a lasting effect.

03:47:38.601 --> 03:47:40.311
If I actually want to
get the result back

03:47:40.311 --> 03:47:43.761
to the function that used the
discount function, namely, main,

03:47:43.761 --> 03:47:47.001
I actually do need to take this
approach of actually returning

03:47:47.001 --> 03:47:51.721
the value explicitly so that ultimately
I'm handing back the discounted price.

03:47:51.721 --> 03:47:52.221
All right.

03:47:52.221 --> 03:47:54.346
Well, let's go ahead and
maybe how about let's just

03:47:54.346 --> 03:47:57.801
use these primitives in
just a few different ways.

03:47:57.801 --> 03:48:02.301
How about a little game of
yesteryear, Super Mario Brothers?

03:48:02.301 --> 03:48:05.661
And in the original Super Mario
Brothers and in bunches of variants,

03:48:05.661 --> 03:48:08.061
so you have these
side-scrolling worlds that

03:48:08.061 --> 03:48:11.471
look like this where there's some coins
in the sky hidden behind these question

03:48:11.471 --> 03:48:11.971
marks.

03:48:11.971 --> 03:48:15.231
So let's just use this as a
visual to consider how in C could

03:48:15.231 --> 03:48:17.061
I start to make
something semi-graphical.

03:48:17.061 --> 03:48:20.271
Like, not actual colors or fanciness,
that feels like too much too soon--

03:48:20.271 --> 03:48:23.011
just something like printing
out some question marks.

03:48:23.011 --> 03:48:26.031
Well, if I go back over here,
let me create that actual file

03:48:26.031 --> 03:48:27.421
that I alluded to earlier.

03:48:27.421 --> 03:48:30.291
So let me code up mario.c.

03:48:30.291 --> 03:48:34.191
Let me go ahead and include
stdio.h, int main void, again,

03:48:34.191 --> 03:48:36.321
which we'll continue to
copy-paste for today.

03:48:36.321 --> 03:48:40.431
And then let me just go ahead and
do something simple like 1, 2, 3, 4,

03:48:40.431 --> 03:48:41.421
and a newline.

03:48:41.421 --> 03:48:44.609
All right, this is what we
might call ASCII art, which

03:48:44.609 --> 03:48:47.401
just means graphics but really just
implemented with your keyboard.

03:48:47.401 --> 03:48:52.131
And if I make mario and do ./mario,
it's not nearly as engaging visually

03:48:52.131 --> 03:48:55.911
as this, but it's the beginning
of this kind of map for a game.

03:48:55.911 --> 03:49:00.051
Well, if I wanted to now print
out of those things dynamically,

03:49:00.051 --> 03:49:01.731
let me go back to my code here.

03:49:01.731 --> 03:49:03.831
And instead of printing
out for all at once,

03:49:03.831 --> 03:49:08.451
I could do something like four int i
gets 0, i less than 4, i plus plus.

03:49:08.451 --> 03:49:13.231
And then inside here, I could just
print out one of them at a time.

03:49:13.231 --> 03:49:15.891
Let me save that, make mario.

03:49:15.891 --> 03:49:20.631
And, at the risk of
disappointing, so close

03:49:20.631 --> 03:49:23.801
but I made a mistake,
just a stupid aesthetic.

03:49:23.801 --> 03:49:25.971
The prompt is not on the new line.

03:49:25.971 --> 03:49:27.476
How could I move it?

03:49:27.476 --> 03:49:29.061
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:49:29.061 --> 03:49:31.811
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, I need an
escape character, the backslash n.

03:49:31.811 --> 03:49:35.541
But should I put it here?

03:49:35.541 --> 03:49:37.791
OK, no, because that's going
to put it after everyone,

03:49:37.791 --> 03:49:40.499
and it's going to make this thing
vertical instead of horizontal.

03:49:40.499 --> 03:49:44.031
So, logically, just like in Scratch, put
it at the end of the loop, so something

03:49:44.031 --> 03:49:44.751
out here.

03:49:44.751 --> 03:49:48.301
And just print out, for instance,
only, quote unquote, new line.

03:49:48.301 --> 03:49:51.351
And now if I do make
mario again, ./mario, OK.

03:49:51.351 --> 03:49:52.338
We're back in business.

03:49:52.338 --> 03:49:54.171
But a little better
designed in that now I'm

03:49:54.171 --> 03:49:57.391
not repeating myself multiple times,
I'm doing this again and again.

03:49:57.391 --> 03:50:00.711
But let's do one other
thing here with mario.

03:50:00.711 --> 03:50:05.961
Let me go ahead and ask the user how
many question marks or coins to print.

03:50:05.961 --> 03:50:09.711
The catch here is that there's another
type of loop that's helpful for this,

03:50:09.711 --> 03:50:12.171
and it's called a do
while loop, generally.

03:50:12.171 --> 03:50:15.711
A do while loop is
similar to a while loop,

03:50:15.711 --> 03:50:19.221
but it checks the condition
last instead of first.

03:50:19.221 --> 03:50:21.039
Recall earlier on the
slide, we had while,

03:50:21.039 --> 03:50:22.581
open parenthesis, closed parenthesis.

03:50:22.581 --> 03:50:25.941
And I kept claiming that we check
whether i is less than-- whatever

03:50:25.941 --> 03:50:29.071
it was, 3 in advance again and again.

03:50:29.071 --> 03:50:32.631
A do while loop just inverts the
logic so that you can actually

03:50:32.631 --> 03:50:34.041
do something like this.

03:50:34.041 --> 03:50:36.381
At the top of this program,
I'm going to go ahead now

03:50:36.381 --> 03:50:40.461
and give myself a variable
n like this of type integer.

03:50:40.461 --> 03:50:44.871
And then I'm going to do, literally,
the following with the keyword do.

03:50:44.871 --> 03:50:48.591
n equals get_int-- and I'm going
to ask the user for the width,

03:50:48.591 --> 03:50:51.441
like the number of
dollar signs to print.

03:50:51.441 --> 03:50:56.031
And I'm going to do this
while n is less than, say, 1.

03:50:56.031 --> 03:50:59.031
So this is a little cryptic,
but the salient differences

03:50:59.031 --> 03:51:04.191
are the Boolean expression is now
at the bottom of my block of code,

03:51:04.191 --> 03:51:05.901
not at the top.

03:51:05.901 --> 03:51:07.381
Now, why is this?

03:51:07.381 --> 03:51:11.391
Well, the difference
here if I make mario is--

03:51:11.391 --> 03:51:12.891
whoops.

03:51:12.891 --> 03:51:16.621
I need to add cs50.h, because
I'm now using get_int.

03:51:16.621 --> 03:51:22.051
If I now compile this version
of Mario and do ./mario,

03:51:22.051 --> 03:51:26.641
a do while loop is helpful when you want
to do something no matter what first

03:51:26.641 --> 03:51:30.931
and then check some condition or some
Boolean expression to see if maybe,

03:51:30.931 --> 03:51:32.461
in this case, the user cooperated.

03:51:32.461 --> 03:51:35.773
It would make no sense if
the user typed in, say, 0,

03:51:35.773 --> 03:51:37.231
because there's no work to be done.

03:51:37.231 --> 03:51:39.451
It'd be really weird if
they said negative 100,

03:51:39.451 --> 03:51:41.221
because that makes no sense logically.

03:51:41.221 --> 03:51:46.261
So with this simple construct
here, I am doing the following

03:51:46.261 --> 03:51:48.811
while n is less than 1.

03:51:48.811 --> 03:51:53.078
The implication is that as soon
as n equals 1 or is bigger than 1,

03:51:53.078 --> 03:51:55.411
I'm going to break out of
this loop, and I've got myself

03:51:55.411 --> 03:52:00.631
a variable called n containing,
essentially, a positive value, 1

03:52:00.631 --> 03:52:03.211
through 2 billion or so.

03:52:03.211 --> 03:52:07.321
And I can now use this, for
instance, here, change the 4 to an n

03:52:07.321 --> 03:52:09.811
so now my program is completely dynamic.

03:52:09.811 --> 03:52:13.711
Let me go ahead and do
make mario, ./mario again.

03:52:13.711 --> 03:52:15.871
And I'll do 4, still works.

03:52:15.871 --> 03:52:18.751
I'll do 40, still works.

03:52:18.751 --> 03:52:22.141
And the difference here with the
do while is if something like this

03:52:22.141 --> 03:52:25.321
involves getting user input,
well, there's no question to ask.

03:52:25.321 --> 03:52:27.161
The user hasn't given you anything yet.

03:52:27.161 --> 03:52:31.111
So you have to do something first,
then check, and break out of the loop

03:52:31.111 --> 03:52:35.808
if the human has, for instance,
cooperated, in this case.

03:52:35.808 --> 03:52:37.891
All right, well why don't
we escalate to something

03:52:37.891 --> 03:52:41.941
more like this in the same game,
where you're underground as Mario,

03:52:41.941 --> 03:52:45.481
and this is like a two-dimensional
wall that's popping up here?

03:52:45.481 --> 03:52:48.481
It looks like a 3 by 3, for
instance, for the sake of discussion.

03:52:48.481 --> 03:52:51.931
And it's like, made of bricks, so
I'll use maybe hash symbols this time.

03:52:51.931 --> 03:52:53.761
Well, it turns out that we can nest--

03:52:53.761 --> 03:52:57.311
that is, combine-- some of
these same ideas as follows.

03:52:57.311 --> 03:53:01.111
Let me go ahead now and
change back to this code.

03:53:01.111 --> 03:53:05.501
And I'm going to keep the
do while loop from before.

03:53:05.501 --> 03:53:07.441
And I'm going to ask,
though, this question,

03:53:07.441 --> 03:53:09.091
what's the size of this square?

03:53:09.091 --> 03:53:13.841
I'm going to assume it's n by
n, so 3 by 3, 4 by 4, whatever.

03:53:13.841 --> 03:53:16.621
So I'm just going to ask for the
size of this square of bricks.

03:53:16.621 --> 03:53:18.374
And now, how do I do this?

03:53:18.374 --> 03:53:20.791
Well, I'm going to go ahead,
for instance, and print out--

03:53:20.791 --> 03:53:25.908
how about for int i =
0, i less than n, i++.

03:53:25.908 --> 03:53:27.991
Let me just keep it simple
and print out something

03:53:27.991 --> 03:53:32.791
like this, just a single
hash symbol that is a brick,

03:53:32.791 --> 03:53:34.801
and a newline after it.

03:53:34.801 --> 03:53:36.271
All right, let's make mario.

03:53:36.271 --> 03:53:38.401
Run mario of 3.

03:53:38.401 --> 03:53:40.321
OK, that's close to being it.

03:53:40.321 --> 03:53:41.431
I've got a column.

03:53:41.431 --> 03:53:43.181
All right, but I need it to be wider.

03:53:43.181 --> 03:53:45.751
So the solution last time
was to get rid of the newline

03:53:45.751 --> 03:53:49.921
and then maybe put the
newline here, after the loop.

03:53:49.921 --> 03:53:55.951
All right, so let's do make mario,
./mario, and type in 3 and huh.

03:53:55.951 --> 03:54:00.481
All right, so I kind of need to
combine these two ideas somehow.

03:54:00.481 --> 03:54:04.351
So how might we solve this problem?

03:54:04.351 --> 03:54:10.591
I want to print rows and
columns, not row or column.

03:54:10.591 --> 03:54:12.121
How do I do this?

03:54:12.121 --> 03:54:12.631
Yeah.

03:54:12.631 --> 03:54:15.016
AUDIENCE: Add another
loop in the for loop.

03:54:15.016 --> 03:54:15.891
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

03:54:15.891 --> 03:54:17.571
Add another loop in the for loop, right?

03:54:17.571 --> 03:54:21.891
If you use one loop conceptually
to kind of count the rows from top

03:54:21.891 --> 03:54:24.471
to bottom, and then
within each row, you then

03:54:24.471 --> 03:54:26.871
sort of typewriter style--
old school typewriter--

03:54:26.871 --> 03:54:30.021
do like, character, character,
character, character horizontally,

03:54:30.021 --> 03:54:32.521
I think we could do exactly
what we want to achieve here.

03:54:32.521 --> 03:54:33.421
So how about this?

03:54:33.421 --> 03:54:36.441
Let me get rid of this line and
get rid of this line for now.

03:54:36.441 --> 03:54:39.201
And let me just give myself
another loop on the inside.

03:54:39.201 --> 03:54:42.921
And since I'm already using i,
another reasonable convention

03:54:42.921 --> 03:54:45.021
here would be to say something like j.

03:54:45.021 --> 03:54:49.311
So j also gets 0, j is less than n, j++.

03:54:49.311 --> 03:54:51.621
And now, what's going to happen?

03:54:51.621 --> 03:54:56.181
Let me go ahead and print out just
one of these things at a time.

03:54:56.181 --> 03:54:58.441
And let me save and let me run this.

03:54:58.441 --> 03:54:59.991
Let me see how close we are.

03:54:59.991 --> 03:55:01.971
Make mario 3.

03:55:01.971 --> 03:55:06.421
OK, three, that's clearly wrong, but
I see nine things there on the screen.

03:55:06.421 --> 03:55:07.581
So we're close.

03:55:07.581 --> 03:55:12.891
What's the one fix I need now to
move the old school typewriter head

03:55:12.891 --> 03:55:15.201
down to the next row when appropriate?

03:55:15.201 --> 03:55:17.141
What do you think?

03:55:17.141 --> 03:55:19.091
Yeah, I need one of these backslash n's.

03:55:19.091 --> 03:55:23.921
And let me add some comments now to
help everyone visualize what I've done.

03:55:23.921 --> 03:55:30.971
For each row, for each column,
how about print a brick--

03:55:30.971 --> 03:55:33.101
just to kind of explain the logic?

03:55:33.101 --> 03:55:37.811
And so I add that because
now move to next row,

03:55:37.811 --> 03:55:40.391
I could do something like
this with a backslash n.

03:55:40.391 --> 03:55:43.751
So here is where the comments,
really, my pseudocode

03:55:43.751 --> 03:55:46.731
actually kind of illuminates
the situation a bit.

03:55:46.731 --> 03:55:51.354
Let me go ahead and recompile
mario, ./mario 3, now we're talking.

03:55:51.354 --> 03:55:53.771
It's not a perfect square,
just because these hash symbols

03:55:53.771 --> 03:55:57.551
are a little taller than they are wide,
but that's just a font detail here.

03:55:57.551 --> 03:56:03.751
Now I've done something that's quite
more akin to something like this.

03:56:03.751 --> 03:56:08.244
All right, so let me pause here
and see if there are any questions.

03:56:08.244 --> 03:56:10.411
Again, the code's getting
a little more complicated,

03:56:10.411 --> 03:56:14.116
but we're just building more
complicated programs like in Scratch,

03:56:14.116 --> 03:56:15.991
with familiar puzzle
pieces-- some variables,

03:56:15.991 --> 03:56:17.341
some loops, some conditionals.

03:56:17.341 --> 03:56:19.411
It's all the same as before.

03:56:19.411 --> 03:56:20.881
Yeah.

03:56:20.881 --> 03:56:22.141
Can you multiply strings in C?

03:56:22.141 --> 03:56:22.891
No.

03:56:22.891 --> 03:56:25.808
But ask that same question again in
a few weeks when we get to Python,

03:56:25.808 --> 03:56:27.774
and the answer will be yes.

03:56:27.774 --> 03:56:28.441
Other questions.

03:56:28.441 --> 03:56:29.881
Yeah.

03:56:29.881 --> 03:56:32.521
In C, you must specify
the return type, the name

03:56:32.521 --> 03:56:34.581
of the function, and the
inputs, or arguments,

03:56:34.581 --> 03:56:35.831
to the function in that order.

03:56:35.831 --> 03:56:39.181
And if none of them are applicable,
you write the word void.

03:56:39.181 --> 03:56:42.061
So same question as earlier, let
me kick that can a week or so,

03:56:42.061 --> 03:56:44.261
and we'll come back to
that and we'll see why.

03:56:44.261 --> 03:56:47.281
But for now, just take on faith
that you need to do that with main.

03:56:47.281 --> 03:56:49.651
Because main is a little
special, similar to the

03:56:49.651 --> 03:56:51.031
when green flag is clicked.

03:56:51.031 --> 03:56:54.271
It too was a little special as well.

03:56:54.271 --> 03:56:55.249
Yeah

03:56:55.249 --> 03:57:01.578
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:57:01.578 --> 03:57:02.411
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes.

03:57:02.411 --> 03:57:05.961
If you want to get out of a
loop early, you could do this.

03:57:05.961 --> 03:57:08.321
So let me answer this question this way.

03:57:08.321 --> 03:57:14.391
An alternative to a do while loop
would be to do something like this.

03:57:14.391 --> 03:57:15.881
How about while true--

03:57:15.881 --> 03:57:18.291
so do the following forever--

03:57:18.291 --> 03:57:23.471
let me go ahead and get an inch from
the user for the size of this thing.

03:57:23.471 --> 03:57:26.771
If n is greater than 0--

03:57:26.771 --> 03:57:28.611
that is, a positive integer--

03:57:28.611 --> 03:57:32.411
then go ahead and use a
new keyword called break.

03:57:32.411 --> 03:57:35.691
This is identical to what we just did.

03:57:35.691 --> 03:57:37.131
It's just a little longer.

03:57:37.131 --> 03:57:39.671
It's like a couple extra
lines, a lot of them are blank.

03:57:39.671 --> 03:57:41.111
And so it's just an alternative.

03:57:41.111 --> 03:57:43.856
But a do while does the same
thing but a little tighter--

03:57:43.856 --> 03:57:47.001
if that's in answer to your question.

03:57:47.001 --> 03:57:51.434
All right, so let's now introduce,
finally, a sequence of problems

03:57:51.434 --> 03:57:53.351
that I've kind of been
brushing under the rug,

03:57:53.351 --> 03:57:55.781
though we did see a little bit
of evidence of this earlier

03:57:55.781 --> 03:57:57.881
when we tried to add 2
billion and 2 billion,

03:57:57.881 --> 03:58:02.181
and it overflowed the number
of bits in an int, so to speak.

03:58:02.181 --> 03:58:06.431
Let me go ahead and code up a
program called calculator again.

03:58:06.431 --> 03:58:09.221
But I'm going to go ahead now
and change this to floats.

03:58:09.221 --> 03:58:12.821
So I'm going to change x to a float,
and I'm going to use get_float.

03:58:12.821 --> 03:58:15.651
And a float, again, is just
a floating point value,

03:58:15.651 --> 03:58:19.071
which is a fancy way of saying a real
number with a decimal point in it.

03:58:19.071 --> 03:58:22.481
And down here, I'm going to
go ahead and use %f for float.

03:58:22.481 --> 03:58:24.851
And I'm going to go ahead
now and do one more thing.

03:58:24.851 --> 03:58:27.643
Instead of addition, I want to do
something fancier, like division,

03:58:27.643 --> 03:58:29.501
so divide x by y.

03:58:29.501 --> 03:58:32.051
And I'm going to give myself
another third float called z,

03:58:32.051 --> 03:58:33.731
as we did at the beginning of today.

03:58:33.731 --> 03:58:37.331
And I'm going to print out z
instead of x and y explicitly.

03:58:37.331 --> 03:58:42.011
So I'm going to go ahead now and
do make calculator, ./calculator.

03:58:42.011 --> 03:58:44.321
And let's do something like, oh, 2/3.

03:58:44.321 --> 03:58:47.711
2 divided by 3 is 0.66667.

03:58:47.711 --> 03:58:49.601
So that's what you would rather expect.

03:58:49.601 --> 03:58:52.311
Let me run it again, 1/10.

03:58:52.311 --> 03:58:54.501
All right, so 0.1, and a bunch of zeros.

03:58:54.501 --> 03:58:56.521
That too is what you
would rather expect.

03:58:56.521 --> 03:58:58.191
But now let me get a little curious.

03:58:58.191 --> 03:59:02.271
It turns out that in C, you can
modify the behavior of these format

03:59:02.271 --> 03:59:03.231
codes a little bit.

03:59:03.231 --> 03:59:05.391
By default, you get 6 or so digits.

03:59:05.391 --> 03:59:07.851
Suppose that you want
to get exactly 2 digits.

03:59:07.851 --> 03:59:11.816
You can more succinctly say 0.2
before the f and after the percent.

03:59:11.816 --> 03:59:14.691
This is the kind of thing that's
hard to remember, but you Google it,

03:59:14.691 --> 03:59:16.911
and you find that, OK,
format code for floats

03:59:16.911 --> 03:59:19.531
uses 0.2 to do two decimal points.

03:59:19.531 --> 03:59:23.061
So let me do make calculator
again, ./calculator.

03:59:23.061 --> 03:59:24.411
How about 2/3?

03:59:24.411 --> 03:59:25.501
0.67.

03:59:25.501 --> 03:59:28.491
So it handles the display of
significant digits for us here.

03:59:28.491 --> 03:59:32.181
And now let me go ahead
and do 1/10 and 0.10.

03:59:32.181 --> 03:59:33.494
So it's adhering to that.

03:59:33.494 --> 03:59:35.661
Well, maybe I really want
a lot of precision, right?

03:59:35.661 --> 03:59:37.161
I've got a really powerful computer.

03:59:37.161 --> 03:59:39.681
Let me see 50 numbers
after the decimal point.

03:59:39.681 --> 03:59:41.551
That's a lot of significant digits.

03:59:41.551 --> 03:59:44.341
Let me remake the
calculator-- whoops, typo.

03:59:44.341 --> 03:59:48.981
Let me remake the calculator,
./mario calculator.

03:59:48.981 --> 03:59:51.731
And how about 2/3 again?

03:59:51.731 --> 03:59:54.401
Well, that's interesting.

03:59:54.401 --> 03:59:58.391
Pretty sure it's supposed to be
a 0.6 with a line over it, right?

03:59:58.391 --> 03:59:59.531
In grade school math.

03:59:59.531 --> 04:00:01.239
All right, well, maybe
that's just a bug.

04:00:01.239 --> 04:00:02.291
How about 1/10?

04:00:02.291 --> 04:00:05.051
OK, that's really getting funky.

04:00:05.051 --> 04:00:06.401
So what's going on?

04:00:06.401 --> 04:00:10.448
It seems that my program cannot
only not do addition very well--

04:00:10.448 --> 04:00:12.281
we eventually hit
problems in the billions--

04:00:12.281 --> 04:00:16.861
we can't even do very
precise numbers here.

04:00:16.861 --> 04:00:19.321
What's going on?

04:00:19.321 --> 04:00:19.861
Exactly.

04:00:19.861 --> 04:00:22.201
In a nutshell, the computer's
approximating the answer

04:00:22.201 --> 04:00:25.143
using that many numbers
after the decimal point.

04:00:25.143 --> 04:00:26.851
But the problem
fundamentally is actually

04:00:26.851 --> 04:00:30.101
very similar to that integer
overflow from before.

04:00:30.101 --> 04:00:31.801
And I'm using that now as a term of art.

04:00:31.801 --> 04:00:36.211
Integers can overflow if you're trying
to use more bits than you actually

04:00:36.211 --> 04:00:37.128
have available to you.

04:00:37.128 --> 04:00:40.378
You sort of change them all to ones, and
then you're out of bits, so to speak.

04:00:40.378 --> 04:00:42.761
Same thing here, but in the
different context of floats--

04:00:42.761 --> 04:00:45.061
if you only have 32
bits-- or, heck, if we

04:00:45.061 --> 04:00:48.721
change to double and only have 64
bits, that's a lot of precision,

04:00:48.721 --> 04:00:50.071
but it's not infinite.

04:00:50.071 --> 04:00:54.071
And, yet, pretty sure there's an
infinite number of real numbers.

04:00:54.071 --> 04:00:59.041
In the world, which is to say a computer
with finite memory cannot possibly

04:00:59.041 --> 04:01:01.414
represent all possible
numbers in the world.

04:01:01.414 --> 04:01:03.331
Because, again, there's
not an infinite number

04:01:03.331 --> 04:01:06.571
of permutations of 32 or 64 bits.

04:01:06.571 --> 04:01:10.351
It might be a lot, in the billions
or more, but it's still finite.

04:01:10.351 --> 04:01:13.771
And so, indeed, this is the
computer's closest approximation

04:01:13.771 --> 04:01:15.971
to what's actually going on there.

04:01:15.971 --> 04:01:18.991
And so this is an example of what
we would actually generally call

04:01:18.991 --> 04:01:21.001
floating-point imprecision.

04:01:21.001 --> 04:01:26.341
Floating-point imprecision refers to the
inability for computers fundamentally

04:01:26.341 --> 04:01:29.341
to represent all possible
real numbers 100%

04:01:29.341 --> 04:01:33.118
precisely, at least by default
in languages like C. Thankfully,

04:01:33.118 --> 04:01:35.201
in the world of scientific
computing and so forth,

04:01:35.201 --> 04:01:38.821
there are solutions to this problem
that just give you more digits.

04:01:38.821 --> 04:01:42.071
But the problem fundamentally
is still going to be there.

04:01:42.071 --> 04:01:44.791
So there's a reason I
changed x and y to floats.

04:01:44.791 --> 04:01:47.051
Let's see what would
happen if we rewound a bit.

04:01:47.051 --> 04:01:52.501
And instead of using floats for x and y,
again, you say integer, so int x and y.

04:01:52.501 --> 04:01:56.161
And let's go far back
and use get_int as well,

04:01:56.161 --> 04:01:59.138
thereby giving us integers x and y.

04:01:59.138 --> 04:02:01.721
Let's still leave z as a float,
because at the end of the day,

04:02:01.721 --> 04:02:04.441
we want to be able to handle
fractions or floating-point values.

04:02:04.441 --> 04:02:07.381
But let's go ahead now and
print out this value of z

04:02:07.381 --> 04:02:09.721
having changed x and y now to ints.

04:02:09.721 --> 04:02:15.391
make calculator, ./calculator, and
let's do, say, 2 for the numerator,

04:02:15.391 --> 04:02:16.711
3 for the denominator.

04:02:16.711 --> 04:02:21.961
And it's not 0.666, and it's
not even rounding oddly.

04:02:21.961 --> 04:02:23.761
It's just all zeros this time.

04:02:23.761 --> 04:02:24.971
So why is that?

04:02:24.971 --> 04:02:28.771
Well, it turns out that C, when
dividing an integer by an integer,

04:02:28.771 --> 04:02:31.771
is always going to give you
back an integer, an int.

04:02:31.771 --> 04:02:34.951
The problem is that floating-point
values don't fit in ints.

04:02:34.951 --> 04:02:37.651
Only the integral part to the
left of the decimal point does.

04:02:37.651 --> 04:02:41.491
Everything at and beyond the decimal
point itself get thrown away,

04:02:41.491 --> 04:02:44.281
known as a feature in
C called truncation.

04:02:44.281 --> 04:02:47.591
When dividing an integer by an
integer, you get back an integer.

04:02:47.591 --> 04:02:51.181
But if you're trying to then store
what's actually a floating point

04:02:51.181 --> 04:02:54.631
result in that integer, C is just
going to throw away everything

04:02:54.631 --> 04:02:57.421
at and beyond the decimal point,
leaving us with this case,

04:02:57.421 --> 04:03:03.521
in just the 0 from what should
have been 0.666666 and so forth.

04:03:03.521 --> 04:03:05.230
So let's see one more example, in fact.

04:03:05.230 --> 04:03:06.841
Let me go back to my terminal here.

04:03:06.841 --> 04:03:08.551
Let me do ./calculator again.

04:03:08.551 --> 04:03:09.751
And let's do 4/3.

04:03:09.751 --> 04:03:13.751
This time, It should be
1.33333 and so forth.

04:03:13.751 --> 04:03:20.730
But let's see, 4 divided by 3, both as
integers, this time gives us 1.0000,

04:03:20.730 --> 04:03:23.761
but there too the
answer should be 1.333.

04:03:23.761 --> 04:03:27.871
But the floating-point part is
getting truncated or thrown away,

04:03:27.871 --> 04:03:29.171
leaving us with just 1.

04:03:29.171 --> 04:03:30.371
So how do we solve this?

04:03:30.371 --> 04:03:33.971
Well, certainly, we could just use
floats from the get-go, as I did.

04:03:33.971 --> 04:03:37.591
But if, by nature of your program,
you only have access to integers--

04:03:37.591 --> 04:03:40.801
or maybe even longs, for which
the same problem would occur--

04:03:40.801 --> 04:03:44.070
what we can actually do
is called type conversion.

04:03:44.070 --> 04:03:47.041
And we can explicitly tell
the computer that we actually

04:03:47.041 --> 04:03:50.230
want to treat this int as though
it's a floating-point value.

04:03:50.230 --> 04:03:52.021
And we can do that for both x and y.

04:03:52.021 --> 04:03:55.871
So let me go back to my code here, and
I have a couple of options, in fact.

04:03:55.871 --> 04:04:01.261
I can convert y to a float by
doing this, I can cast y to a float

04:04:01.261 --> 04:04:04.261
by literally writing the type
float inside of parentheses

04:04:04.261 --> 04:04:05.431
right before the y.

04:04:05.431 --> 04:04:08.881
And if I really want to be explicit,
I can also do the same to x.

04:04:08.881 --> 04:04:12.791
But, strictly speaking, it suffices
to just change one or the other,

04:04:12.791 --> 04:04:14.070
not necessarily both.

04:04:14.070 --> 04:04:19.111
Let me go ahead now and do make
calculator again, ./calculator,

04:04:19.111 --> 04:04:21.900
and let's try 2 divided by 3.

04:04:21.900 --> 04:04:25.140
And now, we're back to an
answer that's closer to correct.

04:04:25.140 --> 04:04:27.841
But, indeed, we're still having
some rounding issues there.

04:04:27.841 --> 04:04:31.621
Let's run it one more
time for 4 divided by 3.

04:04:31.621 --> 04:04:33.990
There too we're closer to
the right answer, at least.

04:04:33.990 --> 04:04:36.451
But we still have that
floating-point imprecision,

04:04:36.451 --> 04:04:39.301
but that's going to be another
problem altogether to solve.

04:04:39.301 --> 04:04:41.221
And here in a little
more detail is that issue

04:04:41.221 --> 04:04:44.400
of integer overflow, which
is in the context of ints.

04:04:44.400 --> 04:04:48.390
Suppose that we think back to
last week when we had three bits,

04:04:48.390 --> 04:04:53.971
and we counted from 0 to
7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.

04:04:53.971 --> 04:04:56.221
I think I asked the question,
how would we count to 8?

04:04:56.221 --> 04:04:58.291
Someone proposed, well,
we need a fourth bit.

04:04:58.291 --> 04:05:01.560
That's fine if you have a
fourth bit, if you have access

04:05:01.560 --> 04:05:03.480
to another light bulb or transistor.

04:05:03.480 --> 04:05:09.240
If you don't, though, the next number
after this is technically 1000.

04:05:09.240 --> 04:05:13.111
But if you don't have space for
or hardware for that fourth bit,

04:05:13.111 --> 04:05:16.341
you might as well just be
representing the number 0.

04:05:16.341 --> 04:05:19.640
So in the world of integers, if
you're only using three bits,

04:05:19.640 --> 04:05:23.361
those three bits eventually
overflow when you count past 7.

04:05:23.361 --> 04:05:28.011
Because what should be 8 can't fit, so
to speak, so it rolls back over to 0.

04:05:28.011 --> 04:05:30.841
And as arcane as this
problem might seem,

04:05:30.841 --> 04:05:33.051
we humans have done
this a couple of times.

04:05:33.051 --> 04:05:35.328
You might recall
knowing about or reading

04:05:35.328 --> 04:05:37.161
about the Y2K problem,
where a lot of people

04:05:37.161 --> 04:05:38.461
thought the world was going to end.

04:05:38.461 --> 04:05:38.961
Why?

04:05:38.961 --> 04:05:43.911
Because on January 1st of
2000, a lot of computers,

04:05:43.911 --> 04:05:48.501
presumably, were going to update their
clocks from 1999 to the year 2000.

04:05:48.501 --> 04:05:51.831
The problem is, though, for
decades, for efficiency, we humans

04:05:51.831 --> 04:05:54.711
were honestly in the habit of
not storing years as four digits.

04:05:54.711 --> 04:05:55.233
Why?

04:05:55.233 --> 04:05:58.191
Because that's just a lot of space
to waste, especially since centuries

04:05:58.191 --> 04:05:59.461
don't happen that often.

04:05:59.461 --> 04:06:02.541
So a lot of computer systems,
especially early on when

04:06:02.541 --> 04:06:05.781
hardware was very expensive
and memory was very tight,

04:06:05.781 --> 04:06:08.390
just stored the last
two digits of any year.

04:06:08.390 --> 04:06:14.271
The problem, of course, on January 1st
of 2000 is that 99 rolls over to 100.

04:06:14.271 --> 04:06:19.341
But if you don't have room for
another digit it's just 00.

04:06:19.341 --> 04:06:23.721
And if your code assumes a prefix of
19, well, we just went from the year

04:06:23.721 --> 04:06:26.863
1999 back to the year 1900.

04:06:26.863 --> 04:06:29.571
Thankfully, long story short, a
lot of people wrote a lot of code

04:06:29.571 --> 04:06:32.881
in a lot of old languages and
mostly warded off this problem,

04:06:32.881 --> 04:06:34.401
so the world did not end.

04:06:34.401 --> 04:06:39.981
The next time the world might end
though, is on January 19, 2038.

04:06:39.981 --> 04:06:42.561
Now, that might feel
like a long time away,

04:06:42.561 --> 04:06:44.931
but so did the year 2000, at one point.

04:06:44.931 --> 04:06:51.351
Why might clocks again break in
today's modern computers in 2038,

04:06:51.351 --> 04:06:52.221
might you think?

04:06:52.221 --> 04:06:54.023
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:06:54.023 --> 04:06:54.981
DAVID J. MALAN: Indeed.

04:06:54.981 --> 04:06:57.151
So this refers to some
number of seconds.

04:06:57.151 --> 04:07:00.561
So it turns out that the way
computers generally keep track of time

04:07:00.561 --> 04:07:04.131
is they count the total number
of seconds since the epoch, which

04:07:04.131 --> 04:07:06.621
is defined as January 1, 1970.

04:07:06.621 --> 04:07:07.221
Why?

04:07:07.221 --> 04:07:09.891
It was just a good year
to start counting at,

04:07:09.891 --> 04:07:11.881
when computers really
came onto the scene.

04:07:11.881 --> 04:07:16.401
Unfortunately, most computers used 32
bits to count the number of seconds

04:07:16.401 --> 04:07:20.331
since January 1, 1970, the
implication of which is we

04:07:20.331 --> 04:07:23.211
can only count up to
roughly 2 billion seconds.

04:07:23.211 --> 04:07:29.811
2 billion seconds is going to
happen in 2038, at which 30 11's

04:07:29.811 --> 04:07:31.701
are going to roll over as follows.

04:07:31.701 --> 04:07:34.899
That number 2 billion,
which is the max--

04:07:34.899 --> 04:07:37.441
because if you're representing
positive and negative numbers,

04:07:37.441 --> 04:07:39.941
recall that you can only count
as high as positive 2 billion

04:07:39.941 --> 04:07:41.181
or negative 2 billion--

04:07:41.181 --> 04:07:42.381
looks like this.

04:07:42.381 --> 04:07:44.421
This is roughly the number
2 billion in binary.

04:07:44.421 --> 04:07:47.041
It's all ones with one
zero way over here.

04:07:47.041 --> 04:07:50.991
If I count one second past that
2 billion number, give or take--

04:07:50.991 --> 04:07:54.051
that means, all right,
I add 1, I carry the 1--

04:07:54.051 --> 04:07:57.291
it's just like 9's
becoming 0's in decimal.

04:07:57.291 --> 04:08:01.161
If I keep this sort of simple
animation and I keep carrying the 1,

04:08:01.161 --> 04:08:05.451
carrying the 1, carrying the 1, 1 second
after 2 billion seconds, give or take,

04:08:05.451 --> 04:08:08.221
I have this number in
the computer's memory.

04:08:08.221 --> 04:08:11.781
So there's still 1 bit that's
a 1 all the way to the left.

04:08:11.781 --> 04:08:16.041
Unfortunately, that bit
often represents negativity,

04:08:16.041 --> 04:08:20.721
whereby if that first bit is negative,
that represents that the rest of it

04:08:20.721 --> 04:08:22.281
somehow represents a negative number.

04:08:22.281 --> 04:08:23.331
It's not negative 0.

04:08:23.331 --> 04:08:24.831
There's a fancier representation.

04:08:24.831 --> 04:08:27.801
But a very big, positive
number very suddenly

04:08:27.801 --> 04:08:29.691
becomes a very big, negative number.

04:08:29.691 --> 04:08:32.671
And that number is roughly
negative 2 billion.

04:08:32.671 --> 04:08:35.541
That means computers
in 2038 on that date

04:08:35.541 --> 04:08:37.971
are going to accidentally
think that it's

04:08:37.971 --> 04:08:43.611
been negative 2 billion seconds since
January 1, 1970, which is going to make

04:08:43.611 --> 04:08:46.731
computers potentially think it's 1901.

04:08:46.731 --> 04:08:50.991
So what is the solution to
the 2038 problem, perhaps?

04:08:50.991 --> 04:08:53.601
Y2K was because we were
using two digits for years.

04:08:53.601 --> 04:08:55.161
What about 2038?

04:08:55.161 --> 04:08:55.971
More bits.

04:08:55.971 --> 04:09:00.591
And, thankfully, we're getting a
little better at lessons learned here,

04:09:00.591 --> 04:09:03.321
and computers now are
increasingly using 64 bits.

04:09:03.321 --> 04:09:05.716
And all of us will be long
gone by the time we run out

04:09:05.716 --> 04:09:08.091
of that number of seconds, so
it's someone else's problem

04:09:08.091 --> 04:09:09.841
many, many years from now.

04:09:09.841 --> 04:09:11.774
But that's really the
fundamental solution.

04:09:11.774 --> 04:09:13.941
If you're running up against
something finite, well,

04:09:13.941 --> 04:09:16.371
just kick the can further and
just give yourself more bits.

04:09:16.371 --> 04:09:18.711
And, frankly, because hardware
is so much cheaper these days,

04:09:18.711 --> 04:09:21.201
computers are so much faster,
it's not as big of a deal

04:09:21.201 --> 04:09:22.731
as it might have been decades ago.

04:09:22.731 --> 04:09:24.501
But that's indeed the solution.

04:09:24.501 --> 04:09:27.291
But this arises in very common contexts.

04:09:27.291 --> 04:09:32.121
In fact, let me go ahead and write a
real quick program here called pennies.

04:09:32.121 --> 04:09:35.151
You might think that just converting
dollars to pennies in US currency

04:09:35.151 --> 04:09:37.731
might be simple, but let
me go ahead and do this.

04:09:37.731 --> 04:09:41.331
In pennies.c, I'm going to
go ahead and include cs50.h.

04:09:41.331 --> 04:09:48.271
And I'm going to include stdio.h,
int main void as my starting point.

04:09:48.271 --> 04:09:50.401
And now down here, I'm going to do this.

04:09:50.401 --> 04:09:52.371
I'm going to get a float
called amount, and I'm

04:09:52.371 --> 04:09:56.541
going to ask the user for some amount
of dollars, so a dollar amount,

04:09:56.541 --> 04:09:59.271
and I'm going to store that
in a variable called amount.

04:09:59.271 --> 04:10:07.461
Then I'm going to simply convert that
amount to pennies by doing, say, how

04:10:07.461 --> 04:10:10.111
about amount times 100?

04:10:10.111 --> 04:10:16.281
And then I'm going to go ahead and print
out that the number of pennies is %i--

04:10:16.281 --> 04:10:18.231
because that's just an
integer in pennies--

04:10:18.231 --> 04:10:22.491
backslash n, quote
unquote, comma, pennies.

04:10:22.491 --> 04:10:26.181
All right, so if I didn't make any
mistakes here, let me make pennies,

04:10:26.181 --> 04:10:27.801
./pennies.

04:10:27.801 --> 04:10:31.701
And suppose I have, say, $0.99, so 0.99.

04:10:31.701 --> 04:10:32.991
That's 99 pennies.

04:10:32.991 --> 04:10:35.799
Suppose I have $1.23.

04:10:35.799 --> 04:10:36.591
That's pretty good.

04:10:36.591 --> 04:10:39.851
Suppose I have $4.20.

04:10:39.851 --> 04:10:41.311
Huh.

04:10:41.311 --> 04:10:42.931
There's that imprecision issue.

04:10:42.931 --> 04:10:45.241
And this isn't even
that big of an amount.

04:10:45.241 --> 04:10:49.303
Now, not a big deal if the cashier gives
you one penny less than you're owed,

04:10:49.303 --> 04:10:50.761
but you can imagine this adding up.

04:10:50.761 --> 04:10:54.241
You can imagine this being worrisome
for financial implications,

04:10:54.241 --> 04:10:57.631
for financial transactions, for
scientific measurements and the like.

04:10:57.631 --> 04:10:59.861
My program can't even handle this.

04:10:59.861 --> 04:11:01.981
Well, there are some solutions here.

04:11:01.981 --> 04:11:04.171
And it looks like what's
really happening--

04:11:04.171 --> 04:11:08.731
if I print it out using the %f with a
0.50 or whatever to see more decimal

04:11:08.731 --> 04:11:09.451
points--

04:11:09.451 --> 04:11:14.281
presumably, the computer is struggling
to represent $4.20 precisely.

04:11:14.281 --> 04:11:20.761
It's probably storing 4 dollars
and 19.9999-something cents.

04:11:20.761 --> 04:11:23.551
So it's close, but it's not quite there.

04:11:23.551 --> 04:11:28.081
So I could at least solve this
by rounding up, for instance.

04:11:28.081 --> 04:11:31.081
And it turns out there is
a round function out there.

04:11:31.081 --> 04:11:33.841
And it turns out that it's in a
library called the math library.

04:11:33.841 --> 04:11:36.841
And you would know this by looking
at online documentation and the like,

04:11:36.841 --> 04:11:37.951
as we'll point you to.

04:11:37.951 --> 04:11:43.801
And if I now make pennies again and
do ./pennies, I can now do $4.20.

04:11:43.801 --> 04:11:44.701
And, voila.

04:11:44.701 --> 04:11:46.171
Now it's correct.

04:11:46.171 --> 04:11:49.531
So at least in this context, it
seems like a solvable problem.

04:11:49.531 --> 04:11:53.461
But it's certainly something I
need to be mindful of, nonetheless.

04:11:53.461 --> 04:11:57.181
Unfortunately, even professional,
full-time programmers over the years

04:11:57.181 --> 04:12:00.073
have not been particularly
attentive to these kinds of details.

04:12:00.073 --> 04:12:03.031
And in a class like this, the goal
is not just to teach you programming

04:12:03.031 --> 04:12:06.551
but to really teach you what's going
on underneath the hood, so to speak,

04:12:06.551 --> 04:12:09.601
so that you have a bottom-up
understanding of how data

04:12:09.601 --> 04:12:11.981
is represented, how computers
are manipulating it,

04:12:11.981 --> 04:12:16.061
so that you are not on the failing
end of some program having some bug.

04:12:16.061 --> 04:12:19.361
And so that we as a society are not
beholden to those kinds of mistakes

04:12:19.361 --> 04:12:19.861
too.

04:12:19.861 --> 04:12:22.231
And this happens,
unfortunately, all of the time.

04:12:22.231 --> 04:12:26.011
This is a Boeing airplane
that a few years ago needed

04:12:26.011 --> 04:12:29.621
to be rebooted after every 248 days.

04:12:29.621 --> 04:12:30.121
Why?

04:12:30.121 --> 04:12:34.741
Because this Boeing airplane software
was using a 32-bit integer counting up

04:12:34.741 --> 04:12:36.991
tenths of a second to keep
track of something or other

04:12:36.991 --> 04:12:38.761
related to its electrical power.

04:12:38.761 --> 04:12:43.471
And, unfortunately, after 248 days of
the airplane being continuously on--

04:12:43.471 --> 04:12:45.691
which in the airline
industry is apparently not

04:12:45.691 --> 04:12:49.381
uncommon to make every dollar count,
keeping the planes up and running

04:12:49.381 --> 04:12:50.581
all the time--

04:12:50.581 --> 04:12:54.511
the 32-bit number would
roll over and the power

04:12:54.511 --> 04:12:57.601
would shut off on the airplane
as a side effect because of sort

04:12:57.601 --> 04:12:59.681
of undefined behavior in that case.

04:12:59.681 --> 04:13:02.941
The temporary solution by Boeing at
the time was apparently, essentially,

04:13:02.941 --> 04:13:06.151
sort of operating system style,
well, have you rebooted your plane?

04:13:06.151 --> 04:13:09.841
And that was indeed the fix until they
rolled out an actual software patch.

04:13:09.841 --> 04:13:11.351
This stuff really matters.

04:13:11.351 --> 04:13:14.401
And the more hardware we carry
around and the more we as a society

04:13:14.401 --> 04:13:17.551
use these kinds of devices,
the more of these problems

04:13:17.551 --> 04:13:20.431
we're going to run into down the road.

04:13:20.431 --> 04:13:21.971
That's it for CS50.

04:13:21.971 --> 04:13:24.261
We'll see you next time.

04:13:24.261 --> 04:13:25.261
[MUSIC PLAYING]

04:14:42.261 --> 04:14:45.086
DAVID MALAN: This is
CS50 and this is week 2.

04:14:45.086 --> 04:14:47.711
Now that you have some programming
experience under your belts,

04:14:47.711 --> 04:14:50.171
in this more arcane language called c.

04:14:50.171 --> 04:14:53.051
Among our goals today is to help
you understand exactly what you have

04:14:53.051 --> 04:14:54.911
been doing these past several days.

04:14:54.911 --> 04:14:58.216
Wrestling with your first programs in
C, so that you have more of a bottom

04:14:58.216 --> 04:15:00.341
up understanding of what
some of these commands do.

04:15:00.341 --> 04:15:02.841
And, ultimately, what more
we can do with this language.

04:15:02.841 --> 04:15:06.011
So this recall was the very
first program you wrote,

04:15:06.011 --> 04:15:09.131
I wrote in this language
called C, much more textual,

04:15:09.131 --> 04:15:11.231
certainly, than the Scratch equivalent.

04:15:11.231 --> 04:15:15.461
But at the end of the day,
computers, your Mac, your PC,

04:15:15.461 --> 04:15:18.816
VS Code doesn't understand
this actual code.

04:15:18.816 --> 04:15:21.941
What's the format into which we need
to get any program that we write, just

04:15:21.941 --> 04:15:22.441
to recap?

04:15:22.441 --> 04:15:23.463
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:15:23.463 --> 04:15:26.051
DAVID MALAN: So binary,
otherwise known as machine code.

04:15:26.051 --> 04:15:26.551
Right?

04:15:26.551 --> 04:15:30.131
The 0s and 1s that your computer
actually does understand.

04:15:30.131 --> 04:15:32.291
So somehow we need to
get to this format.

04:15:32.291 --> 04:15:34.991
And up until now, we've been
using this command called make,

04:15:34.991 --> 04:15:37.931
which is aptly named, because
it lets you make programs.

04:15:37.931 --> 04:15:40.691
And the invocation of that
has been pretty simple.

04:15:40.691 --> 04:15:44.711
Make hello looks in your current
directory or folder for a file called

04:15:44.711 --> 04:15:49.361
hello.c, implicitly, and then it
compiles that into a file called hello,

04:15:49.361 --> 04:15:51.911
which itself is executable,
which just means runnable,

04:15:51.911 --> 04:15:54.161
so that you can then do ./hello.

04:15:54.161 --> 04:15:58.451
But it turns out that make is
actually not a compiler itself.

04:15:58.451 --> 04:16:00.101
It does help you make programs.

04:16:00.101 --> 04:16:04.781
But make is this utility that comes on
a lot of systems that makes it easier

04:16:04.781 --> 04:16:08.321
to actually compile code by
using an actual compiler,

04:16:08.321 --> 04:16:12.551
the program that converts source code
to machine code, on your own Mac, or PC,

04:16:12.551 --> 04:16:14.921
or whatever cloud environment
you might be using.

04:16:14.921 --> 04:16:17.591
In fact, what make is
doing for us, is actually,

04:16:17.591 --> 04:16:21.491
running a command automatically
known as clang, for C language.

04:16:21.491 --> 04:16:25.851
And, so here, for instance, in VS
Code, is that very first program again,

04:16:25.851 --> 04:16:27.731
this time in the context
of a text editor,

04:16:27.731 --> 04:16:30.941
and I could compile
this with make hello.

04:16:30.941 --> 04:16:33.828
Let me go ahead and use the
compiler itself manually.

04:16:33.828 --> 04:16:36.911
And we'll see in a moment why we've
been automating the process with make.

04:16:36.911 --> 04:16:39.321
I'm going to run clang instead.

04:16:39.321 --> 04:16:41.601
And then I'm going to run hello.c.

04:16:41.601 --> 04:16:43.751
So it's a little different
how the compiler's used.

04:16:43.751 --> 04:16:46.421
It needs to know, explicitly,
what the file is called.

04:16:46.421 --> 04:16:49.541
I'll go ahead and run
clang, hello.c, Enter.

04:16:49.541 --> 04:16:52.676
Nothing seems to happen, which,
generally speaking, is a good thing.

04:16:52.676 --> 04:16:54.051
Because no errors have popped up.

04:16:54.051 --> 04:17:00.401
And if I do ls for list, you'll see
there is not a file called hello.

04:17:00.401 --> 04:17:03.491
But there is a curiously-named
file called a.out.

04:17:03.491 --> 04:17:06.881
This is a historical convention,
stands for assembler output.

04:17:06.881 --> 04:17:09.641
And this is, just, the default
file name for a program

04:17:09.641 --> 04:17:13.661
that you might compile yourself,
manually, using clang itself.

04:17:13.661 --> 04:17:16.091
Let me go ahead now and
point out that that's

04:17:16.091 --> 04:17:17.601
kind of a stupid name for a program.

04:17:17.601 --> 04:17:20.696
Even though it works,
./a.out would work.

04:17:20.696 --> 04:17:23.321
But if you actually want to
customize the name of your program,

04:17:23.321 --> 04:17:26.981
we could just resort to make,
or we could do explicitly

04:17:26.981 --> 04:17:28.181
what make is doing for us.

04:17:28.181 --> 04:17:31.031
It turns out, some
programs, among them make,

04:17:31.031 --> 04:17:33.251
support what are called
command line arguments,

04:17:33.251 --> 04:17:34.571
and more on those later today.

04:17:34.571 --> 04:17:37.931
But these are literally words or
numbers that you type at your prompt

04:17:37.931 --> 04:17:41.591
after the name of a program that just
influences its behavior in some way.

04:17:41.591 --> 04:17:44.301
It modifies its behavior.

04:17:44.301 --> 04:17:47.201
And it turns out, if you read
the documentation for clang,

04:17:47.201 --> 04:17:52.301
you can actually pass a -o, for
output, command line argument, that

04:17:52.301 --> 04:17:54.521
lets you specify,
explicitly what do you want

04:17:54.521 --> 04:17:56.056
your outputted program to be called?

04:17:56.056 --> 04:17:58.931
And then you go ahead and type the
name of the file that you actually

04:17:58.931 --> 04:18:01.371
want to compile, from
source code to machine code.

04:18:01.371 --> 04:18:02.981
Let me hit Enter now.

04:18:02.981 --> 04:18:06.251
Again, nothing seems to happen,
and I type ls and voila.

04:18:06.251 --> 04:18:09.271
Now we still have the old a.out,
because I didn't delete it yet.

04:18:09.271 --> 04:18:10.271
And I do have hello now.

04:18:10.271 --> 04:18:14.681
So ./hello, voila, runs
hello, world again.

04:18:14.681 --> 04:18:16.421
And let me go ahead
and remove this file.

04:18:16.421 --> 04:18:20.854
I could, of course, resort to using
the Explorer, on the left hand side.

04:18:20.854 --> 04:18:23.771
Which, I am in the habit of closing,
just to give us more room to see.

04:18:23.771 --> 04:18:26.501
But I could go ahead and right-click
or control-click on a.out

04:18:26.501 --> 04:18:27.626
if I want to get rid of it.

04:18:27.626 --> 04:18:30.561
Or again, let me focus on
the command line interface.

04:18:30.561 --> 04:18:31.511
And I can use--

04:18:31.511 --> 04:18:32.291
anyone recall?

04:18:32.291 --> 04:18:35.261
We didn't really use it much,
but what command removes a file?

04:18:35.261 --> 04:18:36.926
AUDIENCE: rm.

04:18:36.926 --> 04:18:40.691
DAVID MALAN: So rm for
remove. rm, a.out, Enter.

04:18:40.691 --> 04:18:44.321
Remove regular file,
a.out, y for yes, enter.

04:18:44.321 --> 04:18:46.901
And now, if I do ls
again, voila, it's gone.

04:18:46.901 --> 04:18:48.911
All right, so, let's
now enhance this program

04:18:48.911 --> 04:18:54.551
to do the second version we ever did,
which was to also include cs50.h,

04:18:54.551 --> 04:18:57.410
so that we have access to functions
like, get string, and the like.

04:18:57.410 --> 04:19:04.601
Let me do string, name, gets,
get string, what's your name,

04:19:04.601 --> 04:19:05.811
question mark.

04:19:05.811 --> 04:19:10.271
And now, let me go ahead and say hello
to that name with our %s placeholder,

04:19:10.271 --> 04:19:11.181
comma, name.

04:19:11.181 --> 04:19:13.421
So this was version 2 of
our program last time,

04:19:13.421 --> 04:19:17.561
that very easily compiled with make
hello, but notice the difference now.

04:19:17.561 --> 04:19:20.621
If I want to compile this
thing myself with clang, using

04:19:20.621 --> 04:19:22.781
that same lesson learned,
all right, let's do it.

04:19:22.781 --> 04:19:29.561
clang-o, hello, just so I get a better
name for the program, hello.c, Enter.

04:19:29.561 --> 04:19:34.011
And a new error pops up that some of
you might have encountered on your own.

04:19:34.011 --> 04:19:37.841
So it's a bit arcane here, and there's
this mention of a cryptic-looking path

04:19:37.841 --> 04:19:39.591
with temp for temporary there.

04:19:39.591 --> 04:19:42.821
But somehow, my issue's in
main, as we can see here.

04:19:42.821 --> 04:19:44.518
It somehow relates to hello.c.

04:19:44.518 --> 04:19:47.351
Even though we might not have seen
this language last time in class,

04:19:47.351 --> 04:19:50.231
but there's an undefined
reference to get string.

04:19:50.231 --> 04:19:52.061
As though get string doesn't exist.

04:19:52.061 --> 04:19:55.601
Now, your first instinct might be, well
maybe I forgot cs50.h, but of course,

04:19:55.601 --> 04:19:56.441
I didn't.

04:19:56.441 --> 04:19:58.571
That's the very first
line of my program.

04:19:58.571 --> 04:20:02.171
But it turns out, make is doing
something else for us, all this time.

04:20:02.171 --> 04:20:06.191
Just putting cs50.h, or any header
file at the top of your code,

04:20:06.191 --> 04:20:10.991
for that matter, just teaches the
compiler that a function will exist.

04:20:10.991 --> 04:20:13.571
It, sort of, asks the compiler
to-- it asks the compiler

04:20:13.571 --> 04:20:16.871
to trust that I will, eventually,
get around to implementing functions,

04:20:16.871 --> 04:20:22.391
like get string, and cs50.h,
and stdio.h, printf, therein.

04:20:22.391 --> 04:20:28.091
But this error here, some kind of
linker command, relates to the fact

04:20:28.091 --> 04:20:30.221
that there's a separate
process for actually

04:20:30.221 --> 04:20:34.541
finding the 0s and 1s that
cs50 compiled long ago for you.

04:20:34.541 --> 04:20:38.111
That authors of this operating
system compiled for you, long ago,

04:20:38.111 --> 04:20:39.161
in the form of printf.

04:20:39.161 --> 04:20:42.101
We need to, somehow,
tell the compiler that we

04:20:42.101 --> 04:20:44.711
need to link in code
that someone else wrote,

04:20:44.711 --> 04:20:48.011
the actual machine code that someone
else wrote and then compiled.

04:20:48.011 --> 04:20:51.758
So to do that, you'd have to
type -lcs50, for instance,

04:20:51.758 --> 04:20:52.841
at the end of the command.

04:20:52.841 --> 04:20:55.809
So additionally, telling clang
that, not only do you want to output

04:20:55.809 --> 04:20:58.601
a file called hello, and you want
to compile a file called hello.c,

04:20:58.601 --> 04:21:03.461
you also want to quote-unquote
link in a bunch of 0s and 1s

04:21:03.461 --> 04:21:07.271
that collectively implement
get string and printf.

04:21:07.271 --> 04:21:11.481
So now, if I hit enter,
this time it compiled OK.

04:21:11.481 --> 04:21:17.403
And now if I run ./hello, it works
as it did last week, just like that.

04:21:17.403 --> 04:21:20.361
But honestly, this is just going to
get really tedious, really quickly.

04:21:20.361 --> 04:21:22.191
Notice, already, just
to compile my code,

04:21:22.191 --> 04:21:25.678
I have to run clang-o,
hello, hello.c, lcs50,

04:21:25.678 --> 04:21:27.761
and you're going to have
to type more things, too.

04:21:27.761 --> 04:21:31.151
If you wanted to use the math library,
like, to use that round function,

04:21:31.151 --> 04:21:33.701
you would also have
to do -lm, typically,

04:21:33.701 --> 04:21:37.151
to specify give me the math
bits that someone else compiled.

04:21:37.151 --> 04:21:39.231
And the commands just
get longer and longer.

04:21:39.231 --> 04:21:43.781
So moving forward, we won't have
to resort to running clang itself,

04:21:43.781 --> 04:21:45.591
but clang is, indeed, the compiler.

04:21:45.591 --> 04:21:48.641
That is the program that converts
from source code to machine code.

04:21:48.641 --> 04:21:52.699
But we'll continue to use make because
it just automates that process.

04:21:52.699 --> 04:21:54.491
And the commands are
only going to get more

04:21:54.491 --> 04:21:58.901
cryptic the more sophisticated and
more feature full year programs get.

04:21:58.901 --> 04:22:03.881
And make, again, is just a tool
that makes all that happen.

04:22:03.881 --> 04:22:08.561
Let me pause there to see if
there's any questions before then we

04:22:08.561 --> 04:22:10.151
take a look further under the hood.

04:22:10.151 --> 04:22:11.446
Yeah, in front.

04:22:11.446 --> 04:22:14.446
AUDIENCE: Can you explain again what
the -lcs50-- just why you put that?

04:22:14.446 --> 04:22:16.779
DAVID MALAN: Sure, let me
come back to that in a moment.

04:22:16.779 --> 04:22:18.011
What does the -lcs50 mean?

04:22:18.011 --> 04:22:20.178
We'll come back to that,
visually, in just a moment.

04:22:20.178 --> 04:22:23.111
But it means to link in the
0s and 1s that collectively

04:22:23.111 --> 04:22:24.696
implement get string and printf.

04:22:24.696 --> 04:22:26.321
But we'll see that, visually, in a sec.

04:22:26.321 --> 04:22:27.602
Yeah, behind you.

04:22:27.602 --> 04:22:31.334
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

04:22:31.334 --> 04:22:32.751
DAVID MALAN: Really good question.

04:22:32.751 --> 04:22:35.111
How come I didn't have
to link in standard I/O?

04:22:35.111 --> 04:22:37.211
Because I used printf in version 1.

04:22:37.211 --> 04:22:40.541
Standard I/O is just, literally,
so standard that it's built in,

04:22:40.541 --> 04:22:41.741
it just works for free.

04:22:41.741 --> 04:22:43.061
CS50, of course, is not.

04:22:43.061 --> 04:22:45.341
It did not come with the
language C or the compiler.

04:22:45.341 --> 04:22:46.511
We ourselves wrote it.

04:22:46.511 --> 04:22:50.861
And other libraries, even though
they might come with the language C,

04:22:50.861 --> 04:22:54.861
they might not be enabled by default,
generally for efficiency purposes.

04:22:54.861 --> 04:22:57.731
So you're not loading more 0s
and 1s into the computer's memory

04:22:57.731 --> 04:22:58.541
than you need to.

04:22:58.541 --> 04:23:01.511
So standard I/O is special, if you will.

04:23:01.511 --> 04:23:02.771
Other questions?

04:23:02.771 --> 04:23:03.761
Yeah?

04:23:03.761 --> 04:23:05.681
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:23:05.681 --> 04:23:07.421
DAVID MALAN: Oh, what does the -o mean?

04:23:07.421 --> 04:23:10.451
So -o is shorthand for
the English word output,

04:23:10.451 --> 04:23:15.521
and so -o is telling clang to
please output a file called hello,

04:23:15.521 --> 04:23:18.111
because the next thing I
wrote after the command line

04:23:18.111 --> 04:23:24.190
recall was clang -o hello, then
the name of the file, then -lcs50.

04:23:24.190 --> 04:23:27.668
And this is where these commands
do get and stay fairly arcane.

04:23:27.668 --> 04:23:29.501
It's just through muscle
memory and practice

04:23:29.501 --> 04:23:31.871
that you'll start to remember, oh
what are the other commands that you--

04:23:31.871 --> 04:23:34.538
what are the command line arguments
you can provide to programs?

04:23:34.538 --> 04:23:35.831
But we've seen this before.

04:23:35.831 --> 04:23:39.041
Technically, when you run make
hello, the program is called make,

04:23:39.041 --> 04:23:41.241
hello is the command line argument.

04:23:41.241 --> 04:23:43.301
It's an input to the
make function, albeit,

04:23:43.301 --> 04:23:46.511
typed at the prompt, that tells
make what you want to make.

04:23:46.511 --> 04:23:50.441
Even when I used rm a moment
ago, and did rm of a.out,

04:23:50.441 --> 04:23:52.541
the command line argument
there was called a.out

04:23:52.541 --> 04:23:55.001
and it's telling rm what to delete.

04:23:55.001 --> 04:23:59.531
It is entirely dependent on the programs
to decide what their conventions are,

04:23:59.531 --> 04:24:02.351
whether you use dash this
or dash that, but we'll

04:24:02.351 --> 04:24:05.066
see over time, which ones
actually matter in practice.

04:24:05.066 --> 04:24:10.481
So to come back to the first question
about what actually is happening there,

04:24:10.481 --> 04:24:12.823
let's consider the code more closely.

04:24:12.823 --> 04:24:14.531
So here is that first
version of the code

04:24:14.531 --> 04:24:18.851
again, with stdio.h and only
printf, so no cs50 stuff yet.

04:24:18.851 --> 04:24:21.101
Until we add it back in
and had the second version,

04:24:21.101 --> 04:24:23.891
where we actually get the human's name.

04:24:23.891 --> 04:24:27.044
When you run this command,
there's a few things

04:24:27.044 --> 04:24:28.961
that are happening
underneath the hood, and we

04:24:28.961 --> 04:24:30.911
won't dwell on these
kinds of details, indeed,

04:24:30.911 --> 04:24:33.131
we'll abstract it away by using make.

04:24:33.131 --> 04:24:35.201
But it's worth understanding
from the get-go,

04:24:35.201 --> 04:24:38.141
how much automation is going on, so
that when you run these commands,

04:24:38.141 --> 04:24:39.111
it's not magic.

04:24:39.111 --> 04:24:42.201
You have this bottom-up
understanding of what's going on.

04:24:42.201 --> 04:24:45.791
So when we say you've been
compiling your code with make,

04:24:45.791 --> 04:24:47.861
that's a bit of an oversimplification.

04:24:47.861 --> 04:24:51.041
Technically, every time
you compile your code,

04:24:51.041 --> 04:24:53.831
you're having the computer do
four distinct things for you.

04:24:53.831 --> 04:24:57.281
And this is not four distinct things
that you need to memorize and remember

04:24:57.281 --> 04:24:59.441
every time you run your
program, what's happening,

04:24:59.441 --> 04:25:02.081
but it helps to break it
down into building blocks,

04:25:02.081 --> 04:25:06.371
as to how we're getting from source
code, like C, into 0s and 1s.

04:25:06.371 --> 04:25:10.901
It turns out, that when you compile,
quote-unquote, "your code," technically

04:25:10.901 --> 04:25:14.771
speaking, you're doing four things
automatically, and all at once.

04:25:14.771 --> 04:25:18.221
Preprocessing it, compiling it,
assembling it, and linking it.

04:25:18.221 --> 04:25:21.611
Just humans decided, let's just
call the whole process compiling.

04:25:21.611 --> 04:25:24.491
But for a moment, let's
consider what these steps are.

04:25:24.491 --> 04:25:26.951
So preprocessing refers to this.

04:25:26.951 --> 04:25:30.971
If we look at our source code,
version 2 that uses the cs50 library

04:25:30.971 --> 04:25:34.703
and therefore get string, notice that
we have these include lines at top.

04:25:34.703 --> 04:25:36.911
And they're kind of special
versus all the other code

04:25:36.911 --> 04:25:39.971
we've written, because they start
with hash symbols, specifically.

04:25:39.971 --> 04:25:41.921
And that's sort of a
special syntax that means

04:25:41.921 --> 04:25:44.861
that these are, technically,
called preprocessor directives.

04:25:44.861 --> 04:25:49.551
Fancy way of saying they're handled
special versus the rest of your code.

04:25:49.551 --> 04:25:54.131
In fact, if we focus on
cs50.h, recall from last week

04:25:54.131 --> 04:26:00.131
that I provided a hint as to what's
actually in cs50.h, among other things.

04:26:00.131 --> 04:26:04.841
What was the one salient thing that
I said was in cs50.h and therefore,

04:26:04.841 --> 04:26:07.736
why we were including
it in the first place?

04:26:07.736 --> 04:26:08.611
AUDIENCE: Get string?

04:26:08.611 --> 04:26:11.111
DAVID MALAN: So get
string, specifically,

04:26:11.111 --> 04:26:13.421
the prototype for get string.

04:26:13.421 --> 04:26:15.671
We haven't made many of
our own functions yet,

04:26:15.671 --> 04:26:18.101
but recall that any time
we've made our own functions,

04:26:18.101 --> 04:26:20.591
and we've written them
below main in a file,

04:26:20.591 --> 04:26:23.051
we've also had to, somewhat
stupidly, copy paste

04:26:23.051 --> 04:26:25.631
the prototype of the function
at the top of the file,

04:26:25.631 --> 04:26:29.471
just to teach the compiler that
this function doesn't exist, yet,

04:26:29.471 --> 04:26:31.691
it does down there, but it will exist.

04:26:31.691 --> 04:26:32.561
Just trust me.

04:26:32.561 --> 04:26:35.241
So again, that's what these
prototypes are doing for us.

04:26:35.241 --> 04:26:37.601
So therefore, in my
code, If I want to use

04:26:37.601 --> 04:26:41.021
a function like get string,
or printf, for that matter,

04:26:41.021 --> 04:26:43.411
they're not implemented
clearly in the same file,

04:26:43.411 --> 04:26:44.661
they're implemented elsewhere.

04:26:44.661 --> 04:26:46.953
So I need to tell the compiler
to trust me that they're

04:26:46.953 --> 04:26:48.261
implemented somewhere else.

04:26:48.261 --> 04:26:51.071
And so technically,
inside of cs50.h, which

04:26:51.071 --> 04:26:54.671
is installed somewhere in the
cloud's hard drive, so to speak,

04:26:54.671 --> 04:26:59.081
that you all are accessing via VS Code,
there's a line that looks like this.

04:26:59.081 --> 04:27:03.131
A prototype for the get string function
that says the name of the functions

04:27:03.131 --> 04:27:07.091
get string, it takes one input,
or argument, called prompt,

04:27:07.091 --> 04:27:09.971
and that type of that
prompt is a string.

04:27:09.971 --> 04:27:15.411
Get string, not surprisingly, has a
return value and it returns a string.

04:27:15.411 --> 04:27:19.061
So literally, that line and a
bunch of others, are in cs50.h.

04:27:19.061 --> 04:27:22.541
So rather than you all having
to copy paste the prototype,

04:27:22.541 --> 04:27:25.421
you can just trust that
cs50 figured out what it is.

04:27:25.421 --> 04:27:29.231
You can include cs50.h
and the compiler is going

04:27:29.231 --> 04:27:31.681
to go find that prototype for you.

04:27:31.681 --> 04:27:33.741
Same thing in standard
I/O. Someone else-- what

04:27:33.741 --> 04:27:37.881
must clearly be in stdio.h,
among other stuff, that

04:27:37.881 --> 04:27:41.851
motivates our including stdio.h, too?

04:27:41.851 --> 04:27:42.351
Yeah?

04:27:42.351 --> 04:27:43.059
AUDIENCE: Printf.

04:27:43.059 --> 04:27:45.291
DAVID MALAN: Printf, the
prototype for printf,

04:27:45.291 --> 04:27:48.271
and I'll just change it here
in yellow, to be the same.

04:27:48.271 --> 04:27:49.671
And it turns out, the format--

04:27:49.671 --> 04:27:52.851
the prototype for printf
is, actually, pretty fancy,

04:27:52.851 --> 04:27:56.001
because, as you might have noticed,
printf can take one argument, just

04:27:56.001 --> 04:28:00.171
something to print, 2, if you want
to plug a value into it, 3 or more.

04:28:00.171 --> 04:28:02.881
So the dot dot dot just
represents exactly that.

04:28:02.881 --> 04:28:06.591
It's not quite as simple a prototype
as get strain, but more on that

04:28:06.591 --> 04:28:07.376
another time.

04:28:07.376 --> 04:28:10.311
So what does it mean to
preprocess your code?

04:28:10.311 --> 04:28:14.121
The very first thing the
compiler, clang, in this case,

04:28:14.121 --> 04:28:18.531
is doing for you when it reads your
code top-to-bottom, left-to-right, is it

04:28:18.531 --> 04:28:22.221
notices, oh, here is hash include,
oh, here's another hash include.

04:28:22.221 --> 04:28:27.351
And it, essentially, finds those files
on the hard drive, cs50.h, stdio.h,

04:28:27.351 --> 04:28:31.251
and does the equivalent of copying
and pasting them automatically

04:28:31.251 --> 04:28:33.621
into your code at the very top.

04:28:33.621 --> 04:28:36.711
Thereby teaching the compiler
that gets string and printf

04:28:36.711 --> 04:28:38.691
will eventually exist somewhere.

04:28:38.691 --> 04:28:42.741
So that's the preprocessing
step, whereby, again, it's

04:28:42.741 --> 04:28:46.341
just doing a find-and-replace of
anything that starts with hash include.

04:28:46.341 --> 04:28:48.771
It's plugging in the files
there so that you, essentially,

04:28:48.771 --> 04:28:52.041
get all the prototypes
you need automatically.

04:28:52.041 --> 04:28:53.091
OK.

04:28:53.091 --> 04:28:55.491
What does it mean, then,
to compile the results?

04:28:55.491 --> 04:28:57.711
Because at this point
in the story, your code

04:28:57.711 --> 04:28:59.939
now looks like this in
the computer's memory.

04:28:59.939 --> 04:29:01.731
It doesn't change your
file, it's doing all

04:29:01.731 --> 04:29:04.251
of this in the computer's
memory, or RAM, for you.

04:29:04.251 --> 04:29:06.331
But it, essentially, looks like this.

04:29:06.331 --> 04:29:09.861
Well the next step is what's,
technically, really compiling.

04:29:09.861 --> 04:29:12.681
Even though again, we use
compile as an umbrella term.

04:29:12.681 --> 04:29:15.771
Compiling code in C
means to take code that

04:29:15.771 --> 04:29:18.001
now looks like this in
the computer's memory

04:29:18.001 --> 04:29:21.151
and turn it into something
that looks like this.

04:29:21.151 --> 04:29:22.611
Which is way more cryptic.

04:29:22.611 --> 04:29:25.251
But it was just a few
decades ago that, if you

04:29:25.251 --> 04:29:28.191
were taking a class like
CS50 in its earlier form,

04:29:28.191 --> 04:29:32.001
we wouldn't be using C it didn't exist
yet, we would actually be using this,

04:29:32.001 --> 04:29:33.951
something called assembly language.

04:29:33.951 --> 04:29:37.491
And there's different types of,
or flavors of, assembly language.

04:29:37.491 --> 04:29:41.271
But this is about as low level as
you can get to what a computer really

04:29:41.271 --> 04:29:43.671
understands, be it a
Mac, or PC, or a phone,

04:29:43.671 --> 04:29:46.911
before you start getting
into actual 0s and 1s.

04:29:46.911 --> 04:29:48.274
And most of this is cryptic.

04:29:48.274 --> 04:29:51.441
I couldn't tell you what this is doing
unless I thought it through carefully

04:29:51.441 --> 04:29:54.561
and rewound mentally, years
ago, from having studied it,

04:29:54.561 --> 04:29:57.141
but let's highlight a
few key words in yellow.

04:29:57.141 --> 04:30:01.641
Notice that this assembly language
that the computer is outputting

04:30:01.641 --> 04:30:04.791
for you automatically,
still has mention of main

04:30:04.791 --> 04:30:07.551
and it has mention of get string,
and it has mention of printf.

04:30:07.551 --> 04:30:10.619
So there's some relationship to
the C code we saw a moment ago.

04:30:10.619 --> 04:30:12.411
And then if I highlight
these other things,

04:30:12.411 --> 04:30:14.691
these are what are called
computer instructions.

04:30:14.691 --> 04:30:17.001
At the end of the day,
your Mac, your PC,

04:30:17.001 --> 04:30:20.601
your phone actually only
understands very basic instructions,

04:30:20.601 --> 04:30:25.281
like addition, subtraction, division,
multiplication, move into memory,

04:30:25.281 --> 04:30:30.451
load from memory, print something to
the screen, very basic operations.

04:30:30.451 --> 04:30:32.016
And that's what you're seeing here.

04:30:32.016 --> 04:30:37.011
These assembly instructions
are what the computer actually

04:30:37.011 --> 04:30:41.131
feeds into the brains of the computer,
the CPU, the central processing unit.

04:30:41.131 --> 04:30:44.031
And it's that Intel CPU,
or whatever you have,

04:30:44.031 --> 04:30:47.481
that understands this instruction, and
this one, and this one, and this one.

04:30:47.481 --> 04:30:50.121
And collectively, long
story short, all they do

04:30:50.121 --> 04:30:52.881
is print hello, world on
the screen, but in a way

04:30:52.881 --> 04:30:56.171
that the machine understands how to do.

04:30:56.171 --> 04:30:58.761
So let me pause here.

04:30:58.761 --> 04:31:01.271
Are there any questions on
what we mean by preprocessing?

04:31:01.271 --> 04:31:05.111
Which finds and replaces the hash
includes symbols, among others,

04:31:05.111 --> 04:31:08.711
and compiling, which technically
takes your source code,

04:31:08.711 --> 04:31:12.431
once preprocessed, and converts it to
that stuff called assembly language.

04:31:12.431 --> 04:31:14.603
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] each CPU has--

04:31:14.603 --> 04:31:15.551
DAVID MALAN: Correct.

04:31:15.551 --> 04:31:18.971
Each type of CPU has
its own instruction set.

04:31:18.971 --> 04:31:19.541
Indeed.

04:31:19.541 --> 04:31:23.231
And as a teaser, this is why,
at least back in the day, when

04:31:23.231 --> 04:31:27.161
we used to install software from
CD-ROMs, or some other type of media,

04:31:27.161 --> 04:31:32.483
this is why you can't take a program
that was sold for a Windows computer

04:31:32.483 --> 04:31:33.941
and run it on a Mac, or vice-versa.

04:31:33.941 --> 04:31:38.681
Because the commands, the instructions
that those two products understand,

04:31:38.681 --> 04:31:39.761
are actually different.

04:31:39.761 --> 04:31:44.411
Now Microsoft, or any company, could
generally write code in one language,

04:31:44.411 --> 04:31:48.370
like C or another, and they can
compile it twice, saving a PC version

04:31:48.370 --> 04:31:50.051
and saving a Mac version.

04:31:50.051 --> 04:31:54.370
It's twice as much work and sometimes
you get into some incompatibilities,

04:31:54.370 --> 04:31:57.401
but that's why these steps
are somewhat distinct.

04:31:57.401 --> 04:32:00.971
You can now use the same code and
support even different platforms,

04:32:00.971 --> 04:32:02.201
or systems, if you'd want.

04:32:02.201 --> 04:32:02.701
All right.

04:32:02.701 --> 04:32:03.911
Assembly, assembling.

04:32:03.911 --> 04:32:07.061
Thankfully, this part is fairly
straightforward, at least, in concept.

04:32:07.061 --> 04:32:10.511
To assemble code, which is step
three of four, that is just

04:32:10.511 --> 04:32:14.621
happening for you every time
you run make or, in turn, clang,

04:32:14.621 --> 04:32:17.831
this assembly language, which the
computer generated automatically

04:32:17.831 --> 04:32:21.341
for you from your source code,
is turned into 0s and 1s.

04:32:21.341 --> 04:32:25.044
So that's the step that, last
week, I simplified and said,

04:32:25.044 --> 04:32:28.211
when you compile your code, you convert
it to source code-- from source code

04:32:28.211 --> 04:32:29.231
to machine code.

04:32:29.231 --> 04:32:31.946
Technically, that happens
when you assemble your code.

04:32:31.946 --> 04:32:35.201
But no one in normal
conversations says that, they just

04:32:35.201 --> 04:32:37.541
say compile for all of these terms.

04:32:37.541 --> 04:32:38.571
All right.

04:32:38.571 --> 04:32:41.711
So that's assembling.

04:32:41.711 --> 04:32:43.331
There's one final step.

04:32:43.331 --> 04:32:46.661
Even in this simple program
of getting the user's name

04:32:46.661 --> 04:32:51.381
and then plugging it into printf, I'm
using three different people's code,

04:32:51.381 --> 04:32:51.881
if you will.

04:32:51.881 --> 04:32:54.461
My own, which is in hello.c.

04:32:54.461 --> 04:32:59.861
Some of CS50s, which is
in hello.c, sorry-- which

04:32:59.861 --> 04:33:03.341
is in cs50.c, which is not
a file I've mentioned, yet,

04:33:03.341 --> 04:33:07.481
but it stands to reason, that if
there's a cs50.h that has prototypes,

04:33:07.481 --> 04:33:09.641
turns out, the actual
implementation of get string

04:33:09.641 --> 04:33:11.861
and other things are in cs50.c.

04:33:11.861 --> 04:33:15.551
And there's a third file
somewhere on the hard drive

04:33:15.551 --> 04:33:18.521
that's involved in compiling
even this simple program.

04:33:18.521 --> 04:33:24.232
hello.c, cs50.c, and by that
logic, what might the other be?

04:33:24.232 --> 04:33:24.732
Yeah?

04:33:24.732 --> 04:33:26.536
AUDIENCE: stdio?

04:33:26.536 --> 04:33:27.861
DAVID MALAN: Stdio.c.

04:33:27.861 --> 04:33:30.951
And that's a bit of a white lie,
because that's such a big, fancy library

04:33:30.951 --> 04:33:34.011
that there's actually multiple files
that compose it, but the same idea,

04:33:34.011 --> 04:33:35.641
and we'll take the simplification.

04:33:35.641 --> 04:33:40.461
So when I have this code,
and I compile my code,

04:33:40.461 --> 04:33:45.561
I get those 0s and 1s that end up taking
hello.c and turning it, effectively,

04:33:45.561 --> 04:33:51.091
into 0s and 1s that are combined with
cs50.c, followed by stdio.c as well.

04:33:51.091 --> 04:33:52.101
So let me rewind here.

04:33:52.101 --> 04:33:57.561
Here might be the 0s and 1s for my code,
the two lines of code that I wrote.

04:33:57.561 --> 04:34:02.181
Here might be the 0s and 1s for what
cs50 wrote some years ago in cs50.c.

04:34:02.181 --> 04:34:06.471
Here might be the 0s and 1s that someone
wrote for standard I/O decades ago.

04:34:06.471 --> 04:34:09.981
The last and final step
is that linking command

04:34:09.981 --> 04:34:12.591
that links all of these
0s and 1s together,

04:34:12.591 --> 04:34:18.081
essentially stitches them together
into one single file called hello,

04:34:18.081 --> 04:34:20.646
or called a.out, whatever you name it.

04:34:20.646 --> 04:34:25.911
That last step is what combines all of
these different programmers' 0s and 1s.

04:34:25.911 --> 04:34:28.311
And my God, now we're
really in the weeds.

04:34:28.311 --> 04:34:31.281
Who wants to even think about
running code at this level?

04:34:31.281 --> 04:34:32.421
You shouldn't need to.

04:34:32.421 --> 04:34:33.441
But it's not magic.

04:34:33.441 --> 04:34:36.009
When you're running make,
there's some very concrete steps

04:34:36.009 --> 04:34:38.551
that are happening that humans
have developed over the years,

04:34:38.551 --> 04:34:41.961
over the decades, that breakdown
this big problem of source code going

04:34:41.961 --> 04:34:46.671
to 0s and 1s, or machine code,
into these very specific steps.

04:34:46.671 --> 04:34:50.361
But henceforth, you can
call all of this compiling.

04:34:50.361 --> 04:34:51.381
Questions?

04:34:51.381 --> 04:34:52.041
Or confusion?

04:34:52.041 --> 04:34:52.857
Yeah?

04:34:52.857 --> 04:34:55.065
AUDIENCE: Can you explain
again what a.out signifies?

04:34:55.065 --> 04:34:56.031
DAVID MALAN: Sure.

04:34:56.031 --> 04:34:57.531
What does a.out signify?

04:34:57.531 --> 04:35:02.151
a.out is just the conventional,
default file name for any program

04:35:02.151 --> 04:35:05.541
that you compile directly
with a compiler, like clang.

04:35:05.541 --> 04:35:07.941
It's a meaningless name, though.

04:35:07.941 --> 04:35:11.511
It stands for assembler output, and
assembler might now sound familiar

04:35:11.511 --> 04:35:12.951
from this assembling process.

04:35:12.951 --> 04:35:15.411
It's a lame name for a
computer program, and we

04:35:15.411 --> 04:35:20.711
can override it by outputting
something like hello, instead.

04:35:20.711 --> 04:35:21.578
Yeah?

04:35:21.578 --> 04:35:27.687
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:35:27.687 --> 04:35:32.121
DAVID MALAN: To recap, there are
other prototypes in those files,

04:35:32.121 --> 04:35:36.171
cs50.h, stdio.h, technically, they're
all included on top of your file,

04:35:36.171 --> 04:35:38.721
even though you, strictly
speaking, don't need most of them,

04:35:38.721 --> 04:35:42.451
but they are there, just in
case you might want them.

04:35:42.451 --> 04:35:43.921
And finally, any other questions?

04:35:43.921 --> 04:35:44.421
Yeah?

04:35:44.421 --> 04:35:48.139
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:35:48.139 --> 04:35:51.181
DAVID MALAN: Does it matter what order
we're telling the computer to run?

04:35:51.181 --> 04:35:53.401
Sometimes with libraries,
yes, it matters

04:35:53.401 --> 04:35:55.781
what order they are linked in together.

04:35:55.781 --> 04:35:58.591
But for our purposes, it's
really not going to matter.

04:35:58.591 --> 04:36:03.011
It's going to-- make is going to take
care of automating that process for us.

04:36:03.011 --> 04:36:03.511
All right.

04:36:03.511 --> 04:36:06.056
So with that said, henceforth,
compiling, technically,

04:36:06.056 --> 04:36:06.931
is these four things.

04:36:06.931 --> 04:36:10.951
But we'll focus on it as a higher
level concept, an abstraction,

04:36:10.951 --> 04:36:14.141
known as compiling itself.

04:36:14.141 --> 04:36:16.771
So another process that we'll
now begin to focus on all the

04:36:16.771 --> 04:36:19.951
more this week because, invariably,
this past week you ran against--

04:36:19.951 --> 04:36:21.421
ran up against some challenges.

04:36:21.421 --> 04:36:24.811
You probably created your very first
bugs, or mistakes, in a program

04:36:24.811 --> 04:36:28.201
and so let's focus for a moment on
actual techniques for debugging.

04:36:28.201 --> 04:36:31.321
As you spend more time
this semester, in the years

04:36:31.321 --> 04:36:34.531
to come If you continue to program,
you're never, frankly, probably,

04:36:34.531 --> 04:36:37.838
going to write bug
free code, ultimately.

04:36:37.838 --> 04:36:40.921
Though your programs are going to get
more featureful, more sophisticated,

04:36:40.921 --> 04:36:44.491
and we're all going to start to
make more sophisticated mistakes.

04:36:44.491 --> 04:36:46.831
And to this day, I write
buggy code all the time.

04:36:46.831 --> 04:36:48.781
And I'm always horrified
when I do it up here.

04:36:48.781 --> 04:36:50.881
But hopefully, that
won't happen too often.

04:36:50.881 --> 04:36:54.361
But when it does, it's a process,
now, of debugging, trying

04:36:54.361 --> 04:36:56.491
to find the mistakes in your program.

04:36:56.491 --> 04:36:59.861
You don't have to stare at your code,
or shake your fist at your code.

04:36:59.861 --> 04:37:02.851
There are actual tools
that real world programmers

04:37:02.851 --> 04:37:06.121
use to help debug their
code and find these faults.

04:37:06.121 --> 04:37:08.716
So what are some of the techniques
and tools that folks use?

04:37:08.716 --> 04:37:13.701
Well as an aside, if you've ever--

04:37:13.701 --> 04:37:17.101
a bug in a program is a mistake,
that's been around for some time.

04:37:17.101 --> 04:37:22.271
If you've ever heard this tale,
some 50 plus years ago, in 1947.

04:37:22.271 --> 04:37:27.031
This is an entry in a log book written
by a famous computer scientist known

04:37:27.031 --> 04:37:29.491
as-- named Grace Hopper,
who happened to be the one

04:37:29.491 --> 04:37:33.606
to record the very first discovery of a
quote-unquote actual bug in a computer.

04:37:33.606 --> 04:37:36.121
This was like a moth
that had flown into,

04:37:36.121 --> 04:37:41.341
at the time, a very sophisticated system
known as the Harvard Mark II computer,

04:37:41.341 --> 04:37:44.311
very large, refrigerator-sized
type systems,

04:37:44.311 --> 04:37:48.421
in which an actual bug caused an issue.

04:37:48.421 --> 04:37:51.451
The etymology of bug though,
predates this particular instance,

04:37:51.451 --> 04:37:54.841
but here you have, as any computer
scientists might know, the example

04:37:54.841 --> 04:37:57.106
of a first physical bug in a computer.

04:37:57.106 --> 04:37:59.583
How, though, do you go
about removing such a thing?

04:37:59.583 --> 04:38:02.041
Well, let's consider a very
simple scenario from last time,

04:38:02.041 --> 04:38:05.041
for instance, when we were trying to
print out various aspects of Mario,

04:38:05.041 --> 04:38:07.231
like this column of 3 bricks.

04:38:07.231 --> 04:38:10.921
Let's consider how I might go about
implementing a program like this.

04:38:10.921 --> 04:38:15.391
Let me switch back over to VS
Code here, and I'm going to run--

04:38:15.391 --> 04:38:17.011
write a program.

04:38:17.011 --> 04:38:18.901
And I'm not going to
trust myself, so I'm

04:38:18.901 --> 04:38:20.768
going to call it
buggy.c from the get-go,

04:38:20.768 --> 04:38:22.601
knowing that I'm going
to mess something up.

04:38:22.601 --> 04:38:25.411
But I'm going to go ahead
and include stdio.h.

04:38:25.411 --> 04:38:28.201
And I'm going to define main, as usual.

04:38:28.201 --> 04:38:30.211
So hopefully, no mistakes just yet.

04:38:30.211 --> 04:38:32.971
And now, I want to print those
3 bricks on the screen using

04:38:32.971 --> 04:38:34.531
just hashes for bricks.

04:38:34.531 --> 04:38:40.681
So how about 4 int i get 0, i less
than or equal to 3, i plus plus.

04:38:40.681 --> 04:38:42.541
Now, inside of my
curly braces, I'm going

04:38:42.541 --> 04:38:48.221
to go ahead and print out a hash
followed by a backslash n, semicolon.

04:38:48.221 --> 04:38:52.236
All right, saving the file, doing
make, buggy, Enter, it compiles.

04:38:52.236 --> 04:38:57.601
So there's no syntactical errors,
my code is syntactically correct.

04:38:57.601 --> 04:39:00.901
But some of you have probably
seen the logical error already,

04:39:00.901 --> 04:39:03.631
because when I run this
program I don't get

04:39:03.631 --> 04:39:09.691
this picture, which was 3 bricks
high, I seem to have 4 bricks instead.

04:39:09.691 --> 04:39:12.191
Now, this might be jumping out
at you, why it's happening,

04:39:12.191 --> 04:39:14.191
but I've kept the program
simple just so that we

04:39:14.191 --> 04:39:18.271
don't have to find an actual bug, we can
use a tool to find one that we already

04:39:18.271 --> 04:39:20.231
know about, in this case.

04:39:20.231 --> 04:39:23.311
What might be the first strategy
for finding a bug like this,

04:39:23.311 --> 04:39:27.553
rather than staring at your code,
asking a question, trying to think

04:39:27.553 --> 04:39:28.386
through the problem?

04:39:28.386 --> 04:39:31.951
Well, let's actually try to diagnose
the problem more proactively.

04:39:31.951 --> 04:39:34.681
And the simplest way to do
this now, and years from now,

04:39:34.681 --> 04:39:38.131
is, honestly, going to be to
use a function like printf.

04:39:38.131 --> 04:39:40.051
Printf is a wonderfully
useful function, not

04:39:40.051 --> 04:39:42.811
for formatting-- printing
formatted strings and all that, for

04:39:42.811 --> 04:39:45.691
just looking inside
the values of variables

04:39:45.691 --> 04:39:48.613
that you might be curious
about to see what's going on.

04:39:48.613 --> 04:39:49.321
So you know what?

04:39:49.321 --> 04:39:50.581
Let me do this.

04:39:50.581 --> 04:39:53.371
I see that there's 4 coming
out, but I intended 3.

04:39:53.371 --> 04:39:56.001
So clearly, something's
wrong with my i variables.

04:39:56.001 --> 04:39:58.351
So let me be a little more pedantic.

04:39:58.351 --> 04:40:01.561
Let me go inside of this
loop and, temporarily,

04:40:01.561 --> 04:40:04.741
say something explicit, like, i is--

04:40:04.741 --> 04:40:09.461
&amp;i /n, and then just
plug in the value of i.

04:40:09.461 --> 04:40:09.961
Right?

04:40:09.961 --> 04:40:13.231
This is not the program I want to
write, it's the program I'm temporarily

04:40:13.231 --> 04:40:18.661
writing, because now I'm going
to say make buggy, ./buggy.

04:40:18.661 --> 04:40:20.761
And if I look, now,
at the output, I have

04:40:20.761 --> 04:40:25.351
some helpful diagnostic information.
i is 0, and I get a hash, i is 1,

04:40:25.351 --> 04:40:27.871
and I get a hash, 2 and I
get a hash, 3 and I get hash.

04:40:27.871 --> 04:40:28.788
OK, wait a minute.

04:40:28.788 --> 04:40:30.871
I'm clearly going too many
steps because, maybe, I

04:40:30.871 --> 04:40:33.511
forgot that computers are,
essentially, counting from 0,

04:40:33.511 --> 04:40:35.711
and now, oh, it's less than or equal to.

04:40:35.711 --> 04:40:37.291
Now you see it, right?

04:40:37.291 --> 04:40:40.201
Again, trivial example,
but just by using printf,

04:40:40.201 --> 04:40:43.171
you can see inside of
the computer's memory

04:40:43.171 --> 04:40:45.391
by just printing stuff out like this.

04:40:45.391 --> 04:40:50.031
And now, once you've figured it out, oh,
so this should probably be less than 3,

04:40:50.031 --> 04:40:52.401
or I should start
counting from 1, there's

04:40:52.401 --> 04:40:53.901
any number of ways I could fix this.

04:40:53.901 --> 04:40:56.916
But the most conventional is
probably just to say less than 3.

04:40:56.916 --> 04:41:03.441
Now, I can delete my temporary print
statement, rerun make buggy, ./buggy.

04:41:03.441 --> 04:41:06.051
And, voila, problem solved.

04:41:06.051 --> 04:41:08.091
All right, and to this day, I do this.

04:41:08.091 --> 04:41:11.121
Whether it's making a command line
application, or a web application,

04:41:11.121 --> 04:41:13.311
or mobile application,
It's very common to use

04:41:13.311 --> 04:41:15.531
printf, or some equivalent
in any language,

04:41:15.531 --> 04:41:19.611
just to poke around and see what's
inside the computer's memory.

04:41:19.611 --> 04:41:22.831
Thankfully, there's more
sophisticated tools than this.

04:41:22.831 --> 04:41:25.191
Let me go ahead and
reintroduce the bug here.

04:41:25.191 --> 04:41:28.881
And let me reopen my
sidebar at left here.

04:41:28.881 --> 04:41:32.811
Let me now recompile the code
to make sure it's current.

04:41:32.811 --> 04:41:35.571
And I'm going to run a
command called debug50.

04:41:35.571 --> 04:41:39.351
Which is a command that's
representative of a type of program

04:41:39.351 --> 04:41:41.001
known as a debugger.

04:41:41.001 --> 04:41:43.941
And this debugger is
actually built into VS Code.

04:41:43.941 --> 04:41:47.961
And all debug50 is doing for us is
automating the process of starting

04:41:47.961 --> 04:41:49.911
VS Code's built-in debugger.

04:41:49.911 --> 04:41:52.521
So this isn't even a
CS50-specific tool, we've

04:41:52.521 --> 04:41:55.431
just given you a debug50
command to make it easier

04:41:55.431 --> 04:41:57.116
to start it up from the get-go.

04:41:57.116 --> 04:42:01.821
And the way you run this debugger
is you say debug50, space, and then

04:42:01.821 --> 04:42:04.381
the name of the program
that you want to debug.

04:42:04.381 --> 04:42:06.471
So, in this case, . /buggy.

04:42:06.471 --> 04:42:08.271
So you don't mention your c-file.

04:42:08.271 --> 04:42:10.911
You mention your already-compiled code.

04:42:10.911 --> 04:42:16.491
And what this debugger is going
to let me do is, most powerfully,

04:42:16.491 --> 04:42:19.191
walk through my code step-by-step.

04:42:19.191 --> 04:42:23.191
Because every program we've written
thus far, runs from start to finish,

04:42:23.191 --> 04:42:26.586
even if I'm not done thinking
through each step at a time.

04:42:26.586 --> 04:42:30.111
With a debugger, I can
actually click on a line number

04:42:30.111 --> 04:42:33.441
and say pause execution
here, and the debugger

04:42:33.441 --> 04:42:38.391
will let me walk through my code one
step at a time, one second at a time,

04:42:38.391 --> 04:42:41.001
one minute at a time,
at my own human pace.

04:42:41.001 --> 04:42:43.731
Which is super compelling when
the programs get more complicated

04:42:43.731 --> 04:42:46.861
and they might, otherwise,
fly by on the screen.

04:42:46.861 --> 04:42:50.121
So I'm going to click
to the left of line 5.

04:42:50.121 --> 04:42:52.231
And notice that these
little red dots appear.

04:42:52.231 --> 04:42:55.551
And if I click on one it
stays, and gets even redder.

04:42:55.551 --> 04:42:58.491
And I'm going to run debug50 on ./buggy.

04:42:58.491 --> 04:43:03.351
And in just a moment, you'll see that a
new panel opens on the left hand side.

04:43:03.351 --> 04:43:06.171
It's doing some
configuration of the screen.

04:43:06.171 --> 04:43:10.951
Let me zoom out a little bit here so
we can see more on the screen at once.

04:43:10.951 --> 04:43:14.701
And sometimes, you'll see in VS
Code that debug console opens up,

04:43:14.701 --> 04:43:18.741
which looks very cryptic, just go back
to terminal window if that happens.

04:43:18.741 --> 04:43:22.136
Because at the terminal window is where
you can still interact with your code.

04:43:22.136 --> 04:43:24.381
And let's now take a
look at what's going on.

04:43:24.381 --> 04:43:28.911
If I zoom in on my
buggy.c code here, you'll

04:43:28.911 --> 04:43:35.151
notice that we have the same program
as before, but highlighted in yellow

04:43:35.151 --> 04:43:36.081
is line 5.

04:43:36.081 --> 04:43:39.921
Not a coincidence, that's the line
I set a so-called breakpoint at.

04:43:39.921 --> 04:43:44.661
The little red dot means break
here, pause execution here.

04:43:44.661 --> 04:43:47.977
And the yellow line has
not yet been executed.

04:43:47.977 --> 04:43:51.861
But if I, now, at the top of my
screen, notice these little arrows.

04:43:51.861 --> 04:43:53.011
There's one for Play.

04:43:53.011 --> 04:43:55.011
There's one for this,
which, if I hover over it,

04:43:55.011 --> 04:43:58.401
says Step Over, there's another
that's going to say Step Into,

04:43:58.401 --> 04:44:00.081
there's a third that says Step Out.

04:44:00.081 --> 04:44:02.781
I'm just going to use the
first of these, Step Over.

04:44:02.781 --> 04:44:05.841
And I'm going to do this, and
you'll see that the yellow highlight

04:44:05.841 --> 04:44:09.921
moved from line 5 to line
7 because now it's ready,

04:44:09.921 --> 04:44:12.216
but hasn't yet printed out that hash.

04:44:12.216 --> 04:44:16.078
But the most powerful thing here,
notice, is that top left here.

04:44:16.078 --> 04:44:18.411
It's a little cryptic, because
there's a bunch of things

04:44:18.411 --> 04:44:21.171
going on that will make more
sense over time, but at the top

04:44:21.171 --> 04:44:22.731
there's a section called variables.

04:44:22.731 --> 04:44:25.011
Below that, something
called locals, which means

04:44:25.011 --> 04:44:27.081
local to my current function, main.

04:44:27.081 --> 04:44:31.671
And notice, there's my variable
called i, and its current value is 0.

04:44:31.671 --> 04:44:37.071
So now, once I click Step Over
again, watch what happens.

04:44:37.071 --> 04:44:39.921
We go from line 7 back to line 5.

04:44:39.921 --> 04:44:43.716
But look in the terminal window,
one of the hashes has printed.

04:44:43.716 --> 04:44:46.311
But now, it's printed at my own pace.

04:44:46.311 --> 04:44:48.291
I can think through this step-by-step.

04:44:48.291 --> 04:44:50.601
Notice that i has not changed, yet.

04:44:50.601 --> 04:44:53.961
It's still 0 because the yellow
highlighted line hasn't yet executed.

04:44:53.961 --> 04:44:58.401
But the moment I click Step Over,
it's going to execute line 5.

04:44:58.401 --> 04:45:05.271
Now, notice at top left, i has become
1, and nothing has printed, yet,

04:45:05.271 --> 04:45:07.551
because now, highlighted is line 7.

04:45:07.551 --> 04:45:12.261
So if I click Step Over
again, we'll see the hash.

04:45:12.261 --> 04:45:16.191
If I repeat this process at my
own human, comfortable pace,

04:45:16.191 --> 04:45:21.301
I can see my variables changing, I
can see output changing on the screen,

04:45:21.301 --> 04:45:24.163
and I can just think about
should that have just happened.

04:45:24.163 --> 04:45:26.121
I can pause and give
thought to what's actually

04:45:26.121 --> 04:45:30.501
going on without trying to race the
computer and figure it all out at once.

04:45:30.501 --> 04:45:32.751
I'm going to go ahead and
stop here because we already

04:45:32.751 --> 04:45:35.691
know what this particular problem
is, and that brings me back

04:45:35.691 --> 04:45:36.981
to my default terminal window.

04:45:36.981 --> 04:45:40.441
But this debugger, let me
disable the breakpoint now

04:45:40.441 --> 04:45:42.831
so it doesn't keep
breaking, this debugger

04:45:42.831 --> 04:45:45.021
will be your friend
moving forward in order

04:45:45.021 --> 04:45:49.551
to step through your code step-by-step,
at your own pace to figure out

04:45:49.551 --> 04:45:51.081
where something has gone wrong.

04:45:51.081 --> 04:45:54.658
Printf is great, but it gets annoying if
you have to constantly add print this,

04:45:54.658 --> 04:45:57.741
print this, print this, print this,
recompile, rerun it, oh wait a minute,

04:45:57.741 --> 04:45:59.241
print this, print this.

04:45:59.241 --> 04:46:04.041
The debugger lets you do the
equivalent, but automatically.

04:46:04.041 --> 04:46:10.221
Questions on this debugger, which you'll
see all the more hands-on over time?

04:46:10.221 --> 04:46:11.691
Questions on debugger?

04:46:11.691 --> 04:46:12.815
Yeah?

04:46:12.815 --> 04:46:14.821
AUDIENCE: You were using
a Step Over feature.

04:46:14.821 --> 04:46:17.564
What do the other
features in the debugger--

04:46:17.564 --> 04:46:18.981
DAVID MALAN: Really good question.

04:46:18.981 --> 04:46:21.981
We'll see this before long, but those
other buttons that I glossed over,

04:46:21.981 --> 04:46:26.721
step into and step out of, actually
let you step into specific functions

04:46:26.721 --> 04:46:28.461
if I had any more than main.

04:46:28.461 --> 04:46:31.221
So if main called a
function called something,

04:46:31.221 --> 04:46:34.641
and something called a function
called something else, instead of just

04:46:34.641 --> 04:46:38.991
stepping over the entire execution of
that function, I could step into it

04:46:38.991 --> 04:46:41.366
and walk through its
lines of code one by one.

04:46:41.366 --> 04:46:43.281
So any time you have
a problem set you're

04:46:43.281 --> 04:46:46.401
working on that has multiple functions,
you can set a breakpoint in main,

04:46:46.401 --> 04:46:50.511
if you want, or you can set it inside
of one of your additional functions

04:46:50.511 --> 04:46:53.391
to focus your attention only on that.

04:46:53.391 --> 04:46:56.901
And we'll see examples
of that over time.

04:46:56.901 --> 04:46:58.041
All right, so what else?

04:46:58.041 --> 04:47:02.361
And what's the sort of, elephant
in the room, so to speak,

04:47:02.361 --> 04:47:04.011
is actually a duck in this case.

04:47:04.011 --> 04:47:06.421
Why is there this duck and
all of these ducks here?

04:47:06.421 --> 04:47:10.701
Well, it turns out, a third, genuinely
recommended, debugging technique

04:47:10.701 --> 04:47:14.316
is talking through problems, talking
through code with someone else.

04:47:14.316 --> 04:47:16.881
Now, in the absence of having
a family member, or a friend,

04:47:16.881 --> 04:47:20.781
or a roommate who actually wants to
hear you talk about code, of all things,

04:47:20.781 --> 04:47:25.581
generally, programmers turn to a
rubber duck, or other inanimate objects

04:47:25.581 --> 04:47:27.621
if something animate is not available.

04:47:27.621 --> 04:47:31.021
The idea behind rubber duck
debugging, so to speak,

04:47:31.021 --> 04:47:37.011
is that simply by looking at your code
and talking it through, OK, on line 3,

04:47:37.011 --> 04:47:41.301
I'm starting a 4 loop and
I'm initializing i to 0.

04:47:41.301 --> 04:47:43.251
OK, then, I'm printing out a hash.

04:47:43.251 --> 04:47:48.373
Just by talking through your
code, step-by-step, invariably,

04:47:48.373 --> 04:47:51.081
finds you having the proverbial
light bulb go off over your head,

04:47:51.081 --> 04:47:53.301
because you realize, wait a minute
I just said something stupid,

04:47:53.301 --> 04:47:54.771
or I just said something wrong.

04:47:54.771 --> 04:47:58.761
And this is really just a proxy for any
other human, teaching fellow, teacher

04:47:58.761 --> 04:48:00.321
or friend, colleague.

04:48:00.321 --> 04:48:02.701
But in the absence of any
of those people in the room,

04:48:02.701 --> 04:48:04.618
you're welcome to take,
on your way out today.

04:48:04.618 --> 04:48:08.541
One of these little, rubber ducks and
consider using it, for real, any time

04:48:08.541 --> 04:48:12.081
you want to talk through one
of your problems in CS50,

04:48:12.081 --> 04:48:13.401
or maybe life more generally.

04:48:13.401 --> 04:48:15.741
But having it there on
your desk is just a way

04:48:15.741 --> 04:48:19.401
to help you hear illogic
in what you think

04:48:19.401 --> 04:48:22.051
might, otherwise, be logical code.

04:48:22.051 --> 04:48:26.661
So printf, debugging, rubber-duck
debugging are just three of the ways,

04:48:26.661 --> 04:48:29.468
you'll see over time, to
get to the source of code

04:48:29.468 --> 04:48:31.051
that you will write that has mistakes.

04:48:31.051 --> 04:48:33.141
Which is going to happen,
but it will empower you

04:48:33.141 --> 04:48:36.261
all the more to solve those mistakes.

04:48:36.261 --> 04:48:41.701
All right, any questions on debugging,
in general, or these three techniques?

04:48:41.701 --> 04:48:42.201
Yeah?

04:48:42.201 --> 04:48:44.001
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

04:48:44.001 --> 04:48:46.911
DAVID MALAN: What's the difference
between Step Over and Step Into?

04:48:46.911 --> 04:48:50.241
At the moment, the only one that's
applicable to the code I just wrote

04:48:50.241 --> 04:48:53.601
is Step Over, because it means
step over each line of code.

04:48:53.601 --> 04:48:58.311
If, though, I had other functions
that I had written in this program,

04:48:58.311 --> 04:49:03.561
maybe lower down in the file, I
could step into those function calls

04:49:03.561 --> 04:49:05.730
and walk through them one at a time.

04:49:05.730 --> 04:49:07.911
So we'll come back to this
with an actual example,

04:49:07.911 --> 04:49:10.491
but step into will allow
me to do exactly that.

04:49:10.491 --> 04:49:13.471
In fact, this is a perfect segue to
doing a little something like this.

04:49:13.471 --> 04:49:15.893
Let me go ahead and open
up another file here.

04:49:15.893 --> 04:49:17.601
And, actually, we'll
use the same, buggy.

04:49:17.601 --> 04:49:20.581
And we're going to write one
other thing that's buggy, as well.

