06:00:08.781 --> 06:00:11.751
to where we began today where we
were talking about those command line

06:00:11.751 --> 06:00:12.351
arguments.

06:00:12.351 --> 06:00:16.071
Recall that we talked about rm
taking command line argument.

06:00:16.071 --> 06:00:18.731
The file you want to delete,
we talked about clang

06:00:18.731 --> 06:00:20.481
taking command line
arguments, that again,

06:00:20.481 --> 06:00:22.401
modify the behavior of the program.

06:00:22.401 --> 06:00:25.941
How is it that maybe you and I
can start to write programs that

06:00:25.941 --> 06:00:28.101
actually take command line arguments?

06:00:28.101 --> 06:00:31.881
Well here is where I
can finally explain why

06:00:31.881 --> 06:00:35.001
we've been typing int
main(void) for the past week

06:00:35.001 --> 06:00:38.751
and just asking that you take on faith
that it's just the way you do things.

06:00:38.751 --> 06:00:45.081
Well, by default in C, at least
the most recent versions thereof,

06:00:45.081 --> 06:00:48.271
there's only two official
ways to write main functions.

06:00:48.271 --> 06:00:50.721
You might see other formats
online, but they're generally

06:00:50.721 --> 06:00:53.131
not consistent with the
current specification.

06:00:53.131 --> 06:00:56.421
This, again, was sort of a
boilerplate for the simplest

06:00:56.421 --> 06:00:59.031
function we might write last
week, and recall that we've

06:00:59.031 --> 06:01:00.471
been doing this the whole time.

06:01:00.471 --> 06:01:05.251
(Void) What that (void) means, for all
of the programs I have written thus far

06:01:05.251 --> 06:01:08.151
and you have written thus far,
is that none of our programs

06:01:08.151 --> 06:01:11.301
that we've written take
command line arguments.

06:01:11.301 --> 06:01:13.371
That's what the void there means.

06:01:13.371 --> 06:01:18.211
It turns out that main is the way you
can specify that your program does,

06:01:18.211 --> 06:01:20.001
in fact, take command
line arguments, that

06:01:20.001 --> 06:01:24.021
is words after the command
in your terminal window.

06:01:24.021 --> 06:01:26.481
If you want to actually not
use get int or get string,

06:01:26.481 --> 06:01:30.231
you want the human to be able to
say something, like hello, David

06:01:30.231 --> 06:01:31.101
and hit Enter.

06:01:31.101 --> 06:01:34.201
And just run-- print
hello, David on the screen.

06:01:34.201 --> 06:01:38.721
You can use command line arguments,
words after the program name

06:01:38.721 --> 06:01:41.011
on your command line.

06:01:41.011 --> 06:01:44.721
So we're going to change this in a
moment to be something more verbose,

06:01:44.721 --> 06:01:48.191
but something that's now a bit
more familiar syntactically.

06:01:48.191 --> 06:01:52.701
If you change that (void) in main
to be this incantation instead,

06:01:52.701 --> 06:01:57.741
int, argc, comma, string, argv,
open bracket, close bracket,

06:01:57.741 --> 06:02:00.891
you are now giving yourself
access to writing programs

06:02:00.891 --> 06:02:03.171
that take command line arguments.

06:02:03.171 --> 06:02:06.381
Argc, which stands for
argument count is going

06:02:06.381 --> 06:02:10.671
to be an integer that stores how many
words the human typed at the prompt.

06:02:10.671 --> 06:02:13.311
The C automatically gives that to you.

06:02:13.311 --> 06:02:16.971
String argv stands for
argument vector, that's

06:02:16.971 --> 06:02:21.361
going to be an array of all of the words
that the human typed at the prompt.

06:02:21.361 --> 06:02:23.391
So with today's building
block of an array,

06:02:23.391 --> 06:02:26.241
we have the ability now to let
the humans type as many words,

06:02:26.241 --> 06:02:28.161
or as few words, as
they want at the prompt.

06:02:28.161 --> 06:02:31.161
C is going to automatically put
them in an array called argv,

06:02:31.161 --> 06:02:36.621
and it's going to tell us how many
words there are in an int called argc.

06:02:36.621 --> 06:02:40.321
The int, as the return type here,
we'll come back to in just a moment.

06:02:40.321 --> 06:02:43.611
Let's use this definition
to make, maybe,

06:02:43.611 --> 06:02:45.231
just a couple of simple programs.

06:02:45.231 --> 06:02:47.331
But in problem set 2
will we actually use

06:02:47.331 --> 06:02:50.731
this to control the
behavior of your own code.

06:02:50.731 --> 06:02:57.381
Let me code up a file called
argv.0 just to keep it aptly named.

06:02:57.381 --> 06:02:59.961
Let me include cs50.h.

06:02:59.961 --> 06:03:01.501
Let me go ahead and include--

06:03:01.501 --> 06:03:02.001
oops.

06:03:02.001 --> 06:03:05.211
That is not the right name of a
program, let's start that over.

06:03:05.211 --> 06:03:09.711
Let's go ahead and code up argv.c.

06:03:09.711 --> 06:03:11.061
And here we have--

06:03:11.061 --> 06:03:17.151
include cs50.h, include
stdio.h, int, main, not void,

06:03:17.151 --> 06:03:24.286
let's actually say int, argc, string,
argv, open bracket, close bracket.

06:03:24.286 --> 06:03:26.661
No numbers in between because
you don't know, in advance,

06:03:26.661 --> 06:03:29.571
how many words the human's
going to type at their prompt.

06:03:29.571 --> 06:03:31.021
Now let's go ahead and do this.

06:03:31.021 --> 06:03:35.061
Let's write a very simple program that
just says, hello, David, hello, Carter,

06:03:35.061 --> 06:03:36.921
whoever the name is that gets typed.

06:03:36.921 --> 06:03:40.521
But not using get string, let's
instead have the human just

06:03:40.521 --> 06:03:44.151
type their name at the prompt, just like
rm, just like clang, just like make,

06:03:44.151 --> 06:03:46.431
so it's just one and
done when you hit Enter.

06:03:46.431 --> 06:03:47.871
No additional prompts.

06:03:47.871 --> 06:03:52.641
Let me go ahead then and do this,
printf, quote-unquote, hello,

06:03:52.641 --> 06:03:55.761
comma, and instead of world
today, I want to print out

06:03:55.761 --> 06:03:57.631
whatever the human typed in.

06:03:57.631 --> 06:04:03.111
So let's go ahead and do
this, argv, bracket 0 for now.

06:04:03.111 --> 06:04:07.341
But I don't think this is quite
what I want because, of course,

06:04:07.341 --> 06:04:12.631
that's going to literally print
out argv, bracket, 0, bracket.

06:04:12.631 --> 06:04:16.771
I need a placeholder, so let me
put %s here and then put that here.

06:04:16.771 --> 06:04:20.781
So if argv is an array, but
it's an array of strings,

06:04:20.781 --> 06:04:24.741
then argv bracket 0 is
itself a single string.

06:04:24.741 --> 06:04:27.711
And so it can be plugged
into that %s placeholder.

06:04:27.711 --> 06:04:30.001
Let me go ahead and save my program.

06:04:30.001 --> 06:04:33.601
And compile argv, so far, so good.

06:04:33.601 --> 06:04:37.431
Let me now type in my name
after the name of the program.

06:04:37.431 --> 06:04:38.241
So no get string.

06:04:38.241 --> 06:04:42.541
I'm literally typing an extra word,
my own name at the prompt, Enter.

06:04:42.541 --> 06:04:45.551
OK, it's apparently a little
buggy in a couple of ways.

06:04:45.551 --> 06:04:48.761
I forgot my /n but
that's not a huge deal.

06:04:48.761 --> 06:04:53.221
But apparently, inside of
argv is literally everything

06:04:53.221 --> 06:04:55.531
that humans typed in including
the name of the program.

06:04:55.531 --> 06:05:00.511
So logically, how do I print out hello,
David, or hello so-and-so and not

06:05:00.511 --> 06:05:01.981
the actual name of the program?

06:05:01.981 --> 06:05:03.221
What needs to change here?

06:05:03.221 --> 06:05:03.721
Yeah?

06:05:03.721 --> 06:05:05.311
AUDIENCE: Change the index to 1.

06:05:05.311 --> 06:05:06.061
DAVID MALAN: Yeah.

06:05:06.061 --> 06:05:10.201
So presumably index to 1, if that's
the second thing I, or whichever human,

06:05:10.201 --> 06:05:11.201
has typed at the prompt.

06:05:11.201 --> 06:05:15.671
So let's do make argv
again, ./argv, Enter.

06:05:15.671 --> 06:05:16.351
Huh.

06:05:16.351 --> 06:05:17.891
Hello, nul.

06:05:17.891 --> 06:05:19.951
So this is another form of nul.

06:05:19.951 --> 06:05:23.581
But this is user error, now, on my part.

06:05:23.581 --> 06:05:25.331
I didn't do exactly what I said I would.

06:05:25.331 --> 06:05:25.831
Yeah?

06:05:25.831 --> 06:05:26.791
AUDIENCE: You forgot the parameter.

06:05:26.791 --> 06:05:28.691
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, I
forgot the parameter.

06:05:28.691 --> 06:05:29.961
So that's actually, hm.

06:05:29.961 --> 06:05:31.711
I should probably deal
with that, somehow,

06:05:31.711 --> 06:05:33.553
so that people aren't
breaking my program

06:05:33.553 --> 06:05:35.261
and printing out random
things, like nul.

06:05:35.261 --> 06:05:39.031
But if I do say argv, David,
now you see hello, David.

06:05:39.031 --> 06:05:42.331
I can get a little curious,
like what's at location 2?

06:05:42.331 --> 06:05:47.671
Well we can see, make argv,
bracket, ./argv, David, Enter.

06:05:47.671 --> 06:05:49.171
All right, so just nothing is there.

06:05:49.171 --> 06:05:52.463
But it turns out, in a couple of weeks,
we'll start really poking around memory

06:05:52.463 --> 06:05:54.571
and see if we can't crash
programs deliberately

06:05:54.571 --> 06:05:57.061
because nothing is
stopping me from saying,

06:05:57.061 --> 06:06:00.731
oh what's at location 2
million, for instance?

06:06:00.731 --> 06:06:02.611
We could really start to get curious.

06:06:02.611 --> 06:06:04.681
But for now, we'll do the right thing.

06:06:04.681 --> 06:06:08.621
But let's now make sure the human has
typed in the right number of words.

06:06:08.621 --> 06:06:15.181
So let's say this, if argc equals
2, that is the name of the program

06:06:15.181 --> 06:06:19.021
and one more word after that, go
ahead and trust that in argv 1,

06:06:19.021 --> 06:06:21.241
as you proposed, is the person's name.

06:06:21.241 --> 06:06:26.071
Else, let's go ahead and default
here to something simple and basic,

06:06:26.071 --> 06:06:30.121
like, well, if we don't get a name
from the user, just say hello, world,

06:06:30.121 --> 06:06:31.561
like always.

06:06:31.561 --> 06:06:34.306
So now we're programming defensively.

06:06:34.306 --> 06:06:37.351
This time the human, even if they
screw up, they don't give us a name

06:06:37.351 --> 06:06:40.226
or they give us too many names,
we're just going to say hello, world,

06:06:40.226 --> 06:06:42.151
because I now have some
error handling here.

06:06:42.151 --> 06:06:46.291
Because, again, argc is argument
count, the number of words, total,

06:06:46.291 --> 06:06:48.251
typed at the command line.

06:06:48.251 --> 06:06:51.001
So make, argv, ./argv.

06:06:51.001 --> 06:06:52.801
Let me make the same mistake as before.

06:06:52.801 --> 06:06:53.311
OK.

06:06:53.311 --> 06:06:55.171
I don't get this weird nul behavior.

06:06:55.171 --> 06:06:56.611
I get something well-defined.

06:06:56.611 --> 06:06:57.871
I could now do David.

06:06:57.871 --> 06:07:01.111
I could do David Malan, but
that's not currently supported.

06:07:01.111 --> 06:07:05.551
I would need to alter my logic to
support more than just two words

06:07:05.551 --> 06:07:06.606
after the prompt.

06:07:06.606 --> 06:07:08.031
So what's the point of this?

06:07:08.031 --> 06:07:09.781
At the moment, it's
just a simple exercise

06:07:09.781 --> 06:07:14.963
to actually give myself a way of taking
user input when they run the program.

06:07:14.963 --> 06:07:16.921
Because, consider, it's
just more convenient in

06:07:16.921 --> 06:07:18.931
this new, command-line-interface world.

06:07:18.931 --> 06:07:23.118
If you had to use get string
every time you compile your code,

06:07:23.118 --> 06:07:24.451
it'd be kind of annoying, right?

06:07:24.451 --> 06:07:28.201
You type make, then you might get a
prompt, what would you like to make?

06:07:28.201 --> 06:07:31.951
Then you type in hello, or cash, or
something else, then you hit Enter,

06:07:31.951 --> 06:07:33.591
it just really slows the process.

06:07:33.591 --> 06:07:35.701
But in this
command-line-interface world,

06:07:35.701 --> 06:07:39.031
if you support command line arguments,
then you can use these little tricks.

06:07:39.031 --> 06:07:42.431
Like, scrolling up and down in
your history with your arrow keys.

06:07:42.431 --> 06:07:46.691
You can just type commands more quickly
because you can do it all at once.

06:07:46.691 --> 06:07:49.261
And you don't have to keep
prompting the user, more

06:07:49.261 --> 06:07:52.021
pedantically, for more and more info.

06:07:52.021 --> 06:07:54.541
So any questions then on
command line arguments?

06:07:54.541 --> 06:07:58.261
Which, finally, reveals why
we had (void) initially,

06:07:58.261 --> 06:08:00.871
but what more we can now put in main.

06:08:00.871 --> 06:08:03.331
That's how you take
command line arguments.

06:08:03.331 --> 06:08:04.761
Yeah?

06:08:04.761 --> 06:08:06.871
AUDIENCE: If you were to put--

06:08:06.871 --> 06:08:11.581
if you were to use argv, and you
were to put integers inside of it,

06:08:11.581 --> 06:08:14.184
would it still give you, like, a string?

06:08:14.184 --> 06:08:15.767
Would that still be considered string?

06:08:15.767 --> 06:08:17.184
Or would you consider [INAUDIBLE]?

06:08:17.184 --> 06:08:18.021
DAVID MALAN: Yes.

06:08:18.021 --> 06:08:20.811
If you were to type at
the command line something

06:08:20.811 --> 06:08:24.921
like, not a word, but
something like the number 42,

06:08:24.921 --> 06:08:27.711
that would actually be
treated as a string.

06:08:27.711 --> 06:08:28.551
Why?

06:08:28.551 --> 06:08:30.481
Because again, context matters.

06:08:30.481 --> 06:08:33.201
So if your program is
currently manipulating memory

06:08:33.201 --> 06:08:36.771
as though its characters or strings,
whatever those patterns of 0s and 1s

06:08:36.771 --> 06:08:41.061
are, they will be interpreted
as ASCII text, or Unicode text.

06:08:41.061 --> 06:08:44.901
If we therefore go to the chart here,
that might make you wonder, well,

06:08:44.901 --> 06:08:48.771
then how do you distinguish numbers
from letters in the context of something

06:08:48.771 --> 06:08:50.151
like chars and strings?

06:08:50.151 --> 06:08:58.641
Well, notice 65 is a, 97 is a,
but also 49 is 1, and 50 is 2.

06:08:58.641 --> 06:09:01.761
So the designers of ASCII,
and then later Unicode,

06:09:01.761 --> 06:09:04.941
realized well wait a minute,
if we want to support programs

06:09:04.941 --> 06:09:07.701
that let you type things
that look like numbers,

06:09:07.701 --> 06:09:10.611
even though they're not
technically ints or floats,

06:09:10.611 --> 06:09:14.881
we need a way in ASCII and
Unicode to represent even numbers.

06:09:14.881 --> 06:09:16.131
So here are your numbers.

06:09:16.131 --> 06:09:19.471
And it's a little silly that we have
numbers representing other numbers.

06:09:19.471 --> 06:09:22.124
But again, if you're in the
world of letters and characters,

06:09:22.124 --> 06:09:24.291
you've got to come up with
a mapping for everything.

06:09:24.291 --> 06:09:26.051
And notice here, here's the dot.

06:09:26.051 --> 06:09:30.651
Even if you were to represent 1.23
as a string, or as characters,

06:09:30.651 --> 06:09:35.101
even the dot now is going to be
represented as an ASCII character.

06:09:35.101 --> 06:09:37.191
So again, context here matters.

06:09:37.191 --> 06:09:41.631
All right, one final example
to tease apart what this int is

06:09:41.631 --> 06:09:44.101
and what it's been
doing here for so long.

06:09:44.101 --> 06:09:49.041
So I'm going to add one
bit of logic to a new file

06:09:49.041 --> 06:09:52.011
that I'm going to call exit.c.

06:09:52.011 --> 06:09:53.391
So an exit.c.

06:09:53.391 --> 06:09:57.141
We're going to introduce something that
are generally known as exit status.

06:09:57.141 --> 06:09:59.241
It turns out this is not
a feature we've used yet,

06:09:59.241 --> 06:10:01.501
but it's just useful to know about.

06:10:01.501 --> 06:10:04.611
Especially when automating
tests of your own code.

06:10:04.611 --> 06:10:08.376
When it comes to figuring out if
a program succeeded or failed.

06:10:08.376 --> 06:10:13.131
It turns out that main has one
more feature we haven't leveraged.

06:10:13.131 --> 06:10:18.591
An ability to signal to the user
whether something was successful or not.

06:10:18.591 --> 06:10:22.021
And that's by way of
main's return value.

06:10:22.021 --> 06:10:26.321
So I'm going modify this
program as follows, like this.

06:10:26.321 --> 06:10:29.181
Suppose I want to write
a similar program that

06:10:29.181 --> 06:10:32.161
requires that the user
type a word at the prompt.

06:10:32.161 --> 06:10:36.711
So that argc has to be 2
for whatever design purpose.

06:10:36.711 --> 06:10:43.251
If argc does not equal 2, I want to
quit out of my program prematurely.

06:10:43.251 --> 06:10:46.851
I want to insist that the user
operate the program correctly.

06:10:46.851 --> 06:10:53.061
So I might give them an error message
like, missing command line argument /n.

06:10:53.061 --> 06:10:55.441
But now I want to quit
out of the program.

06:10:55.441 --> 06:10:56.571
Now how can I do that?

06:10:56.571 --> 06:11:01.521
The right way, quote-unquote, to do
that is to return a value from main.

06:11:01.521 --> 06:11:04.851
Now it's a little weird
because no one called main yet,

06:11:04.851 --> 06:11:07.251
right, main just gets
called automatically,

06:11:07.251 --> 06:11:09.561
but the convention is
anytime something goes

06:11:09.561 --> 06:11:14.361
wrong in a program you should
return a non-zero value from main.

06:11:14.361 --> 06:11:16.041
1 is fine as a go-to.

06:11:16.041 --> 06:11:19.731
We don't need to get into the weeds of
having many different exit statuses,

06:11:19.731 --> 06:11:20.481
so to speak.

06:11:20.481 --> 06:11:26.031
But if you return 1, that is a clue to
the system, the Mac, the PC, the cloud

06:11:26.031 --> 06:11:27.691
device that's something went wrong.

06:11:27.691 --> 06:11:28.191
Why?

06:11:28.191 --> 06:11:29.931
Because 1 is not 0.

06:11:29.931 --> 06:11:35.721
If everything works fine, like, let's go
ahead and print out hello comma %s like

06:11:35.721 --> 06:11:40.881
before, quote-unquote argv bracket 1.

06:11:40.881 --> 06:11:43.341
So this is just a version of
the program without an else.

06:11:43.341 --> 06:11:45.651
So this is the same
as doing, essentially,

06:11:45.651 --> 06:11:47.841
an else here like I did earlier.

06:11:47.841 --> 06:11:51.001
I want to signal to the
computer that all is well.

06:11:51.001 --> 06:11:52.551
And so I return 0.

06:11:52.551 --> 06:11:55.911
But strictly speaking, if
I'm already returning here,

06:11:55.911 --> 06:11:58.821
I don't technically need, if
I really want to be nit picky,

06:11:58.821 --> 06:12:01.131
I don't technically need the
else because the only way

06:12:01.131 --> 06:12:05.747
I'm going to get to line 11
is if I didn't already return.

06:12:05.747 --> 06:12:07.441
So what's going on here?

06:12:07.441 --> 06:12:10.791
The only new thing here logically,
is that for the first time ever,

06:12:10.791 --> 06:12:13.071
I'm returning a value from main.

06:12:13.071 --> 06:12:14.991
That's something I
could always have done

06:12:14.991 --> 06:12:19.551
because main has always been defined by
us as taking an int as a return value.

06:12:19.551 --> 06:12:24.141
By default, main automatically,
sort of secretly, returns 0 for you.

06:12:24.141 --> 06:12:27.111
If you've never once use the
return keyword, which you probably

06:12:27.111 --> 06:12:29.631
haven't in main, it just
automatically returns 0

06:12:29.631 --> 06:12:31.556
and the system assumes
that all went well.

06:12:31.556 --> 06:12:33.651
But now that we're starting
to get a little more

06:12:33.651 --> 06:12:35.781
sophisticated with our
code, and you know,

06:12:35.781 --> 06:12:39.741
the programmer, something went
wrong, you can abort programs early.

06:12:39.741 --> 06:12:44.871
You can exit out of them by returning
some other value, besides 0, from main.

06:12:44.871 --> 06:12:47.301
And this is fortuitous
that it's an int, right?

06:12:47.301 --> 06:12:49.371
0 means everything worked.

06:12:49.371 --> 06:12:53.511
Unfortunately, in programming, there are
seemingly, an infinite number of things

06:12:53.511 --> 06:12:54.501
that can go wrong.

06:12:54.501 --> 06:12:57.471
And int gives you 4
billion possible codes

06:12:57.471 --> 06:13:00.716
that you can use, a.k.a. exit
statuses, to signify errors.

06:13:00.716 --> 06:13:04.191
So if you've ever on your Mac
or PC gotten some weird pop up

06:13:04.191 --> 06:13:07.581
that an error happened, sometimes,
there's a cryptic number in it.

06:13:07.581 --> 06:13:09.681
Maybe it's positive,
maybe it's negative.

06:13:09.681 --> 06:13:14.431
It might say error code 123, or
negative 49, or something like that.

06:13:14.431 --> 06:13:18.571
What you're generally seeing, are
these exit statuses, these return

06:13:18.571 --> 06:13:21.871
values from main in a program
that someone at Microsoft,

06:13:21.871 --> 06:13:25.381
or Apple, or somewhere else
wrote, something went wrong,

06:13:25.381 --> 06:13:30.241
they are unnecessarily showing you,
the user what the error code is.

06:13:30.241 --> 06:13:33.361
If only, so that when you call
customer support or submit a ticket,

06:13:33.361 --> 06:13:36.451
you can tell them what exit
status you encountered,

06:13:36.451 --> 06:13:39.331
what error code you encounter.

06:13:39.331 --> 06:13:43.651
All right, any questions
on exit statuses,

06:13:43.651 --> 06:13:48.841
which is the last of our new
building blocks, for now?

06:13:48.841 --> 06:13:49.801
Any questions at all?

06:13:49.801 --> 06:13:50.301
Yeah?

06:13:50.301 --> 06:13:57.801
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] You know how
if you have get string or get int,

06:13:57.801 --> 06:13:59.679
if you want to make [INAUDIBLE]

06:13:59.679 --> 06:14:00.346
DAVID MALAN: No.

06:14:00.346 --> 06:14:03.526
The question is can you
do things again and again

06:14:03.526 --> 06:14:06.151
at the command line like you
could with get string and get int.

06:14:06.151 --> 06:14:08.131
Which, by default,
recall are automatically

06:14:08.131 --> 06:14:10.681
designed to keep prompting
the user in their own loop

06:14:10.681 --> 06:14:14.221
until they give you an int, or a
float, or the like with command line

06:14:14.221 --> 06:14:15.001
arguments, no.

06:14:15.001 --> 06:14:16.471
You're going to get an
error message but then

06:14:16.471 --> 06:14:18.263
you're going to be
returned to your prompt.

06:14:18.263 --> 06:14:21.648
And it's up to you to type
it correctly the next time.

06:14:21.648 --> 06:14:22.231
Good question.

06:14:22.231 --> 06:14:22.731
Yeah?

06:14:22.731 --> 06:14:27.696
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
automatically for you.

06:14:27.696 --> 06:14:29.571
DAVID MALAN: If you
do not return a value

06:14:29.571 --> 06:14:32.991
explicitly main will
automatically return 0 for you,

06:14:32.991 --> 06:14:36.901
that is the way C simply works
so it's not strictly necessary.

06:14:36.901 --> 06:14:39.771
But now that we're starting
to return values explicitly,

06:14:39.771 --> 06:14:42.351
if something goes wrong,
it would be good practice

06:14:42.351 --> 06:14:45.741
to also start returning a value
for main when something goes right

06:14:45.741 --> 06:14:48.036
and there are no errors.

06:14:48.036 --> 06:14:52.071
So let's now get out of
the weeds and contextualize

06:14:52.071 --> 06:14:55.461
this for some actual problems that
we'll be solving in the coming days

06:14:55.461 --> 06:14:57.391
by way of problems set 2 and beyond.

06:14:57.391 --> 06:15:00.001
So here for instance--

06:15:00.001 --> 06:15:04.251
So here for instance, is a
problem that you might think back

06:15:04.251 --> 06:15:08.241
to when you were a kid the
readability of some text or some book,

06:15:08.241 --> 06:15:10.491
the grade level in which
some book is written.

06:15:10.491 --> 06:15:14.001
If you're a young student, you
might read at first-grade level

06:15:14.001 --> 06:15:15.501
or third-grade level in the US.

06:15:15.501 --> 06:15:17.293
Or, if you're in college
presumably, you're

06:15:17.293 --> 06:15:19.206
reading at a university-level of text.

06:15:19.206 --> 06:15:22.334
But what does it mean
for text, like in a book,

06:15:22.334 --> 06:15:24.501
or in an essay, or something
like that to correspond

06:15:24.501 --> 06:15:25.851
to some kind of grade level?

06:15:25.851 --> 06:15:29.211
Well, here's a quote-- a
title of a childhood book.

06:15:29.211 --> 06:15:31.851
One Fish, Two Fish, Red Fish, Blue Fish.

06:15:31.851 --> 06:15:35.101
What might the grade level be for
a book that has words like this?

06:15:35.101 --> 06:15:37.851
Maybe, when you were a kid or if
you have a siblings still reading

06:15:37.851 --> 06:15:40.521
these things, what might the
grade level of this thing be?

06:15:43.061 --> 06:15:43.851
Any guesses?

06:15:43.851 --> 06:15:44.351
Yeah?

06:15:44.351 --> 06:15:45.518
AUDIENCE: Before grade 1.

06:15:45.518 --> 06:15:46.601
DAVID MALAN: Sorry, again?

06:15:46.601 --> 06:15:47.643
AUDIENCE: Before grade 1.

06:15:47.643 --> 06:15:49.911
DAVID MALAN: Before grade
1 is, in fact, correct.

06:15:49.911 --> 06:15:51.551
So that's for really young kids?

06:15:51.551 --> 06:15:52.491
Why is that?

06:15:52.491 --> 06:15:53.441
Well, let's consider.

06:15:53.441 --> 06:15:56.471
These are pretty simple phrases, right?

06:15:56.471 --> 06:15:57.761
One fish, two fish, red--

06:15:57.761 --> 06:16:00.221
I mean there's not even
verbs in these sentences,

06:16:00.221 --> 06:16:04.301
they're just nouns and adjectives,
and very short sentences.

06:16:04.301 --> 06:16:06.461
And so that might be a
heuristic we could use.

06:16:06.461 --> 06:16:09.071
When analyzing text, well if
the words are kind of short,

06:16:09.071 --> 06:16:11.501
the sentences are kind of
short, everything's very simple,

06:16:11.501 --> 06:16:14.511
that's probably a very
young, or early, grade level.

06:16:14.511 --> 06:16:17.926
And so by one formulation, it might
indeed be even before grade 1,

06:16:17.926 --> 06:16:18.926
for someone quite young.

06:16:18.926 --> 06:16:19.931
How about this?

06:16:19.931 --> 06:16:22.283
Mr and Mrs. Dursley, of
number 4, Privet Drive,

06:16:22.283 --> 06:16:25.241
were proud to say that they were
perfectly normal, thank you very much.

06:16:25.241 --> 06:16:27.221
They were the last
people you would expect

06:16:27.221 --> 06:16:29.381
to be involved in anything
strange or mysterious

06:16:29.381 --> 06:16:32.111
because they just didn't
hold with such nonsense.

06:16:32.111 --> 06:16:33.043
And, onward.

06:16:33.043 --> 06:16:34.751
All right, what grade
level is this book?

06:16:34.751 --> 06:16:36.039
AUDIENCE: Third.

06:16:36.039 --> 06:16:37.331
DAVID MALAN: OK, I heard third.

06:16:37.331 --> 06:16:38.846
AUDIENCE: What?

06:16:38.846 --> 06:16:40.241
DAVID MALAN: Seventh, fifth.

06:16:40.241 --> 06:16:41.411
OK, all over the place.

06:16:41.411 --> 06:16:44.801
But grade 7, according to
one particular measure.

06:16:44.801 --> 06:16:49.063
And whether or not we can debate exactly
what age you were when you read this,

06:16:49.063 --> 06:16:51.521
and maybe you're feeling ahead
of your time, or behind now.

06:16:51.521 --> 06:16:55.731
But here, we have a snippet of text.

06:16:55.731 --> 06:17:00.821
What makes this text assume an older
audience, a more mature audience,

06:17:00.821 --> 06:17:03.951
a higher grade level, would you think?

06:17:03.951 --> 06:17:04.451
Yeah?

06:17:04.451 --> 06:17:06.676
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:17:06.676 --> 06:17:09.371
DAVID MALAN: Yeah, it's longer,
different types of words,

06:17:09.371 --> 06:17:11.774
there's commas now in
phrases, and so forth.

06:17:11.774 --> 06:17:13.941
So there's just some kind
of sophistication to this.

06:17:13.941 --> 06:17:16.541
So it turns out for the
upcoming problem set,

06:17:16.541 --> 06:17:19.631
among the things you'll do is
take, as input, texts like this

06:17:19.631 --> 06:17:20.771
and analyze them.

06:17:20.771 --> 06:17:23.333
Considering , well, how
many words are in the text?

06:17:23.333 --> 06:17:24.791
How many sentences are in the text?

06:17:24.791 --> 06:17:26.636
How many letters are in the text?

06:17:26.636 --> 06:17:30.431
And use those according to a
well-defined formula to prescribe what,

06:17:30.431 --> 06:17:33.941
exactly, the grade level of some
actual text-- there's the third--

06:17:33.941 --> 06:17:34.843
might actually be.

06:17:34.843 --> 06:17:37.051
Well what else are we going
to do in the coming days?

06:17:37.051 --> 06:17:39.671
Well I've alluded to this notion
of cryptography in the past.

06:17:39.671 --> 06:17:42.611
This notion of scrambling
information in such a way

06:17:42.611 --> 06:17:45.683
that you can hide the
contents of a message

06:17:45.683 --> 06:17:47.891
from someone who might
otherwise intercept it, right?

06:17:47.891 --> 06:17:50.391
The earliest form of this might
also be when you're younger,

06:17:50.391 --> 06:17:53.651
and you're in class, and you're passing
a note from one person to another,

06:17:53.651 --> 06:17:54.911
from yourself to someone else.

06:17:54.911 --> 06:17:57.221
You don't want to necessarily
write a note in English,

06:17:57.221 --> 06:17:59.381
or some other written,
language you might want

06:17:59.381 --> 06:18:01.691
to scramble it somehow, or encrypt it.

06:18:01.691 --> 06:18:04.721
Maybe you change the As
to a B, and the Bs to a C.

06:18:04.721 --> 06:18:07.031
So that if the teacher snaps
it up and intercepts it,

06:18:07.031 --> 06:18:09.461
they can't actually
understand what it is you've

06:18:09.461 --> 06:18:11.421
written because it's encrypted.

06:18:11.421 --> 06:18:13.871
So long as your friend,
the recipient of this note,

06:18:13.871 --> 06:18:16.151
knows how you manipulated it.

06:18:16.151 --> 06:18:19.901
How you added or subtracted
letters to each other,

06:18:19.901 --> 06:18:23.111
they can decrypt it, which
is to reverse that process.

06:18:23.111 --> 06:18:26.331
So formally, in the world of
cryptography and computer science,

06:18:26.331 --> 06:18:28.391
this is another problem to solve.

06:18:28.391 --> 06:18:31.434
Your input, though, when you have a
message you want to send securely,

06:18:31.434 --> 06:18:33.101
is what's generally known as plain text.

06:18:33.101 --> 06:18:37.241
There's some algorithm that's
going to then encipher, or encrypt

06:18:37.241 --> 06:18:40.361
that information, into what's
called ciphertext, which

06:18:40.361 --> 06:18:42.911
is the scrambled version that
theoretically can get safely

06:18:42.911 --> 06:18:45.371
intercepted and your message
has not been spoiled,

06:18:45.371 --> 06:18:48.881
unless that intercept
actually knows what algorithm

06:18:48.881 --> 06:18:51.411
you used inside of this process.

06:18:51.411 --> 06:18:53.981
So that would be generally
known as a cipher.

06:18:53.981 --> 06:18:57.341
The ciphers typically take,
though, not one input, but two.

06:18:57.341 --> 06:19:01.946
If, for instance, your cipher
is as simple as A becomes B,

06:19:01.946 --> 06:19:05.681
B becomes C, C becomes D,
dot dot dot, Z becomes A,

06:19:05.681 --> 06:19:09.401
you're essentially adding one to
every letter and encrypting it.

06:19:09.401 --> 06:19:12.011
Now that would be,
what we call, the key.

06:19:12.011 --> 06:19:15.731
You and the recipient both have to
agree, presumably, before class,

06:19:15.731 --> 06:19:19.541
in advance, what number you're
going to use that day to rotate,

06:19:19.541 --> 06:19:21.221
or change all of these letters by.

06:19:21.221 --> 06:19:24.671
Because when you add 1, they
upon receiving your ciphertext

06:19:24.671 --> 06:19:27.351
have to subtract 1 to
get back the answer.

06:19:27.351 --> 06:19:31.991
For instance, if the input,
plaintext, is hi, as before,

06:19:31.991 --> 06:19:37.271
and the key is 1, the ciphertext using
this simple rotational algorithm,

06:19:37.271 --> 06:19:41.981
otherwise known as the Caesar cipher,
might be ij exclamation point.

06:19:41.981 --> 06:19:45.669
So it's similar, but it's at
least scrambled at first glance.

06:19:45.669 --> 06:19:47.711
And unless the teacher
really cares to figure out

06:19:47.711 --> 06:19:50.681
what algorithm are they using today,
or what key are they using today,

06:19:50.681 --> 06:19:53.961
it's probably sufficiently
secure for your purposes.

06:19:53.961 --> 06:19:55.421
How do you reverse the process?

06:19:55.421 --> 06:19:58.451
Well, your friend gets this
and reverses it by negative 1.

06:19:58.451 --> 06:20:02.891
So I becomes H, J becomes I,
and things like punctuation

06:20:02.891 --> 06:20:05.321
remain untouched at
least in this scheme.

06:20:05.321 --> 06:20:07.841
So let's consider one
final example here.

06:20:07.841 --> 06:20:15.341
If the input to the algorithm
is Uijtxbtdt50, and the key

06:20:15.341 --> 06:20:17.351
this time is negative 1.

06:20:17.351 --> 06:20:23.771
Such that now B should become A, and C
should become B, and A should become A.

06:20:23.771 --> 06:20:25.391
So we're going in the other direction.

06:20:25.391 --> 06:20:27.291
How might we analyze this?

06:20:27.291 --> 06:20:30.261
Well if we spread all the letters
out, and we start from left to right,

06:20:30.261 --> 06:20:36.041
and we start subtracting one letter,
U becomes T, I becomes H, J becomes I,

06:20:36.041 --> 06:20:41.481
T becomes S, X becomes W, A, was, D, T--

06:20:41.481 --> 06:20:42.531
this was CS50.

06:20:42.531 --> 06:20:43.731
We'll see you next time.

06:20:43.731 --> 06:20:44.361
[APPLAUSE]

06:20:44.361 --> 06:22:01.706
[MUSIC PLAYING]

06:22:01.706 --> 06:22:06.531
DAVID J. MALAN: This is CS50, and
this is already week three.

06:22:06.531 --> 06:22:10.056
And even as we've gotten much more
into the minutia of programming

06:22:10.056 --> 06:22:11.931
and some of the C stuff
that we've been doing

06:22:11.931 --> 06:22:15.201
is all the more cryptic looking,
recall that at the end of the day,

06:22:15.201 --> 06:22:19.008
like, everything we've been doing
ultimately fits into to this model.

06:22:19.008 --> 06:22:20.841
So keep that in mind,
particularly as things

06:22:20.841 --> 06:22:23.591
seem like they're getting more
complicated and more sophisticated.

06:22:23.591 --> 06:22:26.211
It's just a process of learning
a new language that ultimately

06:22:26.211 --> 06:22:28.791
lets us express this process.

06:22:28.791 --> 06:22:31.851
And of course, last week we really
went into the weeds of like how

06:22:31.851 --> 06:22:33.601
inputs and outputs are represented.

06:22:33.601 --> 06:22:37.941
And this thing here, a photograph
thereof, is called what?

06:22:37.941 --> 06:22:39.103
This is what?

06:22:39.103 --> 06:22:39.989
AUDIENCE: RAM.

06:22:39.989 --> 06:22:41.031
DAVID J. MALAN: RAM, I heard--

06:22:41.031 --> 06:22:44.031
Random Access Memory or just
generally known as memory.

06:22:44.031 --> 06:22:46.641
And recall that we looked at
one of these little black chips

06:22:46.641 --> 06:22:48.861
that contains all of the bytes--

06:22:48.861 --> 06:22:50.151
all of the bits, ultimately.

06:22:50.151 --> 06:22:52.761
It's just kind of a grid,
sort of an artist grid, that

06:22:52.761 --> 06:22:55.881
allows us to think about every
one of these memory locations

06:22:55.881 --> 06:22:58.401
as just having a number or
an address, so to speak.

06:22:58.401 --> 06:23:01.131
Like, this might be byte
number 0 and then 1 and then 2

06:23:01.131 --> 06:23:04.041
and then, maybe way down here
again, something like 2 billion

06:23:04.041 --> 06:23:06.441
if you have 2 gigabytes of memory.

06:23:06.441 --> 06:23:10.431
And so as we did that, we started to
explore how we could use this canvas

06:23:10.431 --> 06:23:14.121
to create kind of our own information,
our own inputs and outputs, not

06:23:14.121 --> 06:23:16.741
just the basics like ints
and floats and so forth.

06:23:16.741 --> 06:23:18.831
But we also talked about strings.

06:23:18.831 --> 06:23:21.441
And what is a string as you now know it?

06:23:21.441 --> 06:23:24.561
How would you describe in
layperson's terms a string?

06:23:24.561 --> 06:23:25.371
Yeah, over there.

06:23:25.371 --> 06:23:26.351
AUDIENCE: I was gonna say--

06:23:26.351 --> 06:23:26.841
[AUDIO OUT]

06:23:26.841 --> 06:23:28.258
DAVID J. MALAN: An array of characters.

06:23:28.258 --> 06:23:30.051
And an array, meanwhile--
let's go there.

06:23:30.051 --> 06:23:34.941
How might someone else define an
array in more familiar now terms?

06:23:34.941 --> 06:23:37.521
What would be an array?

06:23:37.521 --> 06:23:38.031
Yeah.

06:23:38.031 --> 06:23:41.431
AUDIENCE: Kind of like
an indexed set of things.

06:23:41.431 --> 06:23:43.421
DAVID J. MALAN: An indexed
set of things-- not bad.

06:23:43.421 --> 06:23:46.571
And I think a key characteristic to
keep in mind with an array is that it

06:23:46.571 --> 06:23:48.251
does actually pertain to memory.

06:23:48.251 --> 06:23:50.171
And it's contiguous memory.

06:23:50.171 --> 06:23:53.561
Byte after byte after byte
is what constitutes an array.

06:23:53.561 --> 06:23:56.171
And we'll see in a couple of
weeks time that there's actually

06:23:56.171 --> 06:24:00.131
more interesting ways to use this same
primitive Canvas to stitch together

06:24:00.131 --> 06:24:03.601
things that are sort of two directional
even that have some kind of shape

06:24:03.601 --> 06:24:04.101
to them.

06:24:04.101 --> 06:24:07.331
But for now, all we've talked about
is arrays and just using these things

06:24:07.331 --> 06:24:11.601
from left to right, top to bottom,
contiguously to represent information.

06:24:11.601 --> 06:24:14.261
So today, we'll consider still an array.

06:24:14.261 --> 06:24:17.381
But we won't focus so
much on representation

06:24:17.381 --> 06:24:18.821
of strings or other data types.

06:24:18.821 --> 06:24:21.279
We'll actually now focus on
the other part of that process,

06:24:21.279 --> 06:24:24.311
of inputs becoming outputs,
namely the thing in the middle--

06:24:24.311 --> 06:24:25.301
algorithms.

06:24:25.301 --> 06:24:29.561
But we have to keep in mind, even though
every time we've looked at an array

06:24:29.561 --> 06:24:32.971
thus far, certainly on the board
like this, you as a human certainly

06:24:32.971 --> 06:24:34.721
have the luxury of
just kind of eyeballing

06:24:34.721 --> 06:24:38.181
the whole thing with a bird's eye view
and seeing where all of those numbers

06:24:38.181 --> 06:24:38.681
are.

06:24:38.681 --> 06:24:40.514
If I asked you where a
particular number is,

06:24:40.514 --> 06:24:43.511
like zero, odds are your eyes
would go right to where it is,

06:24:43.511 --> 06:24:46.511
and boom, problem solved
in sort of one step.

06:24:46.511 --> 06:24:51.621
But the catch is, with a computer
that has this memory, even though you,

06:24:51.621 --> 06:24:55.091
the human, can [INAUDIBLE] see
everything at once, a computer cannot.

06:24:55.091 --> 06:24:58.451
It's better to think of your
computer's memory, your phone's memory,

06:24:58.451 --> 06:25:01.361
or more specifically an
array of memory like this

06:25:01.361 --> 06:25:05.861
as really being a set of closed
doors, not unlike lockers in a school.

06:25:05.861 --> 06:25:08.621
And only by opening
each of those doors can

06:25:08.621 --> 06:25:10.451
the computer actually
see what's in there,

06:25:10.451 --> 06:25:12.971
which is to say that the
computer, unlike you, doesn't

06:25:12.971 --> 06:25:16.841
have this bird's eye view of all
of the data in all these locations.

06:25:16.841 --> 06:25:19.761
It has to much more
methodically look here,

06:25:19.761 --> 06:25:23.951
maybe look here, maybe look here, and
so forth in order to find something.

06:25:23.951 --> 06:25:28.031
Now fortunately, we already have some
building blocks-- loops, conditions,

06:25:28.031 --> 06:25:29.571
Boolean expressions, and the like--

06:25:29.571 --> 06:25:31.481
where you could imagine
writing some code

06:25:31.481 --> 06:25:36.041
that very methodically goes from left
to right or right to left or something

06:25:36.041 --> 06:25:39.851
more sophisticated that actually
finds something you're looking for.

06:25:39.851 --> 06:25:43.211
And just remember that the
conventions we've had since last week

06:25:43.211 --> 06:25:47.681
now is that these arrays are
zero indexed, so to speak.

06:25:47.681 --> 06:25:52.511
To be zero indexed just means that the
data type starts counting from zero.

06:25:52.511 --> 06:25:56.291
So this is location 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

06:25:56.291 --> 06:25:59.681
And notice even though there
are seven total doors here,

06:25:59.681 --> 06:26:02.291
the right-most one,
of course, is called 6

06:26:02.291 --> 06:26:04.301
just because we've
started counting at 0.

06:26:04.301 --> 06:26:09.041
So in the general case, if you
had n doors or n bytes of memory,

06:26:09.041 --> 06:26:13.841
0 would always be at the left, and n
minus 1 would always be at the right.

06:26:13.841 --> 06:26:18.221
That's sort of a generalization of just
thinking about this kind of convention.

06:26:18.221 --> 06:26:22.151
All right, so let's revisit the problem
that we started the whole term off

06:26:22.151 --> 06:26:24.884
with in week zero, which was
this notion of searching.

06:26:24.884 --> 06:26:26.801
And what does it mean
to search for something?

06:26:26.801 --> 06:26:29.579
Well, to find information-- and
this, of course, is omnipresent.

06:26:29.579 --> 06:26:32.621
Anytime you take out your phone, you're
searching for a friend's contact.

06:26:32.621 --> 06:26:35.501
Any time you pull up a browser,
you're googling for this or that.

06:26:35.501 --> 06:26:39.671
So search is kind of one of the
most omnipresent topics and features

06:26:39.671 --> 06:26:41.238
of any device these days.

06:26:41.238 --> 06:26:44.321
So let's consider how the Googles, the
Apples, the Microsofts of the world

06:26:44.321 --> 06:26:48.021
are implementing something as
seemingly familiar as this.

06:26:48.021 --> 06:26:50.381
So here might be the problem statement.

06:26:50.381 --> 06:26:52.811
We want some input to
become some output.

06:26:52.811 --> 06:26:54.161
What's that input going to be?

06:26:54.161 --> 06:26:57.881
Maybe it's a bunch of closed doors
like this out of which we want

06:26:57.881 --> 06:27:00.611
to get back an answer, true or false.

06:27:00.611 --> 06:27:03.146
Is something we're
looking for there or not?

06:27:03.146 --> 06:27:05.771
You can imagine taking this one
step further and trying to find

06:27:05.771 --> 06:27:07.881
where is the thing you're looking for.

06:27:07.881 --> 06:27:10.301
But for now, let's just take
one bite out of the problem.

06:27:10.301 --> 06:27:14.921
Can we tell ourselves, true or
false, is some number behind one

06:27:14.921 --> 06:27:17.751
of these doors or lockers in memory?

06:27:17.751 --> 06:27:22.121
But before we go there and start
talking about ways to do that-- that is,

06:27:22.121 --> 06:27:22.941
algorithms.

06:27:22.941 --> 06:27:27.041
Let's consider how we might
lay the foundation of, like,

06:27:27.041 --> 06:27:30.469
comparing whether one algorithm
is better than another.

06:27:30.469 --> 06:27:32.261
We talked about
correctness, and it sort of

06:27:32.261 --> 06:27:35.801
goes without saying that any code you
write, any algorithm you implement,

06:27:35.801 --> 06:27:36.821
had better be correct.

06:27:36.821 --> 06:27:39.701
Otherwise, what's the point if it
doesn't give you the right answers?

06:27:39.701 --> 06:27:41.621
But we also talked about design.

06:27:41.621 --> 06:27:45.641
And in your own words, what do we
mean when we say a program is better

06:27:45.641 --> 06:27:48.831
designed at this stage than another?

06:27:48.831 --> 06:27:52.241
How do you think about
this notion of design now?

06:27:52.241 --> 06:27:53.411
Yeah, in the middle?

06:27:53.411 --> 06:27:55.661
AUDIENCE: Easier to understand
or easier to institute.

06:27:55.661 --> 06:27:57.351
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, so easier to understand.

06:27:57.351 --> 06:27:58.186
I like that.

06:27:58.186 --> 06:27:58.811
Other thoughts?

06:27:58.811 --> 06:27:59.311
Yeah.

06:27:59.311 --> 06:28:00.341
AUDIENCE: Efficiency.

06:28:00.341 --> 06:28:03.174
DAVID J. MALAN: Efficiency, and what do
you mean by efficiency precisely?

06:28:03.174 --> 06:28:06.524
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:28:06.524 --> 06:28:07.191
DAVID J. MALAN: Nice.

06:28:07.191 --> 06:28:09.751
It doesn't use up too much
memory, and it isn't redundant.

06:28:09.751 --> 06:28:11.481
So you can think about
design along a few

06:28:11.481 --> 06:28:13.439
of these axes-- sort of
the quality of the code

06:28:13.439 --> 06:28:15.631
but also the quality of the performance.

06:28:15.631 --> 06:28:20.179
And as our programs get bigger and
more sophisticated and just longer,

06:28:20.179 --> 06:28:22.221
those kinds of things are
really going to matter.

06:28:22.221 --> 06:28:24.221
And in the real world,
if you start writing code

06:28:24.221 --> 06:28:26.991
not just by yourself but with
someone else, getting the design

06:28:26.991 --> 06:28:30.501
right is just going to make it easier
to collaborate and ultimately produce,

06:28:30.501 --> 06:28:33.031
write code, with just
higher probability.

06:28:33.031 --> 06:28:36.651
So let's consider how we might focus
on exactly the second characteristic,

06:28:36.651 --> 06:28:38.751
the efficiency, of an algorithm.

06:28:38.751 --> 06:28:42.591
And the way we might talk about the
efficiency of algorithms, just how fast

06:28:42.591 --> 06:28:45.711
or how slow they are, is in
terms of their running time.

06:28:45.711 --> 06:28:49.261
That is to say, when they're
running, how much time do they take?

06:28:49.261 --> 06:28:52.371
And we might measure this in
seconds or milliseconds or minutes

06:28:52.371 --> 06:28:54.711
or just some number of
steps in the general case

06:28:54.711 --> 06:28:58.921
because presumably fewer steps, to
your point, is better than more steps.

06:28:58.921 --> 06:29:00.771
So how might we think
about running times?

06:29:00.771 --> 06:29:03.831
Well, there's one general
notation we should define today.

06:29:03.831 --> 06:29:07.971
So computer scientists tend to describe
the running time of an algorithm

06:29:07.971 --> 06:29:12.201
or a piece of code, for that matter, in
terms of what's called big O notation.

06:29:12.201 --> 06:29:14.991
This is literally a
capitalized O, a big O.

06:29:14.991 --> 06:29:18.471
And this generally means that the
running time of some algorithm

06:29:18.471 --> 06:29:21.920
is on the order of such and such,
where such and such, we'll see,

06:29:21.920 --> 06:29:24.530
is just going to be a very
simple mathematical formula.

06:29:24.530 --> 06:29:26.811
It's kind of a way of waving
your hands mathematically

06:29:26.811 --> 06:29:31.131
to convey the idea of just how fast
or how slow some algorithm or code is

06:29:31.131 --> 06:29:32.961
without getting into
the weeds of like, it

06:29:32.961 --> 06:29:36.691
took this many milliseconds or
this many specific number of steps.

06:29:36.691 --> 06:29:40.641
So you might recall then from week
zero, I even introduced this picture

06:29:40.641 --> 06:29:41.721
but without much context.

06:29:41.721 --> 06:29:45.021
At the time, we just use this to
compare those phone book algorithms.

06:29:45.021 --> 06:29:47.961
Recall that this red straight
line was the first algorithm,

06:29:47.961 --> 06:29:49.281
one page at a time.

06:29:49.281 --> 06:29:54.231
The yellow line that's still
straight differed how if you recall?

06:29:54.231 --> 06:29:58.731
That line represented what
alternative algorithm?

06:29:58.731 --> 06:29:59.731
Looking out and back.

06:29:59.731 --> 06:30:00.981
What is that second algorithm?

06:30:00.981 --> 06:30:01.791
Yeah, over there.

06:30:01.791 --> 06:30:03.291
AUDIENCE: Like, two pages at a time.

06:30:03.291 --> 06:30:06.441
DAVID J. MALAN: Two pages at a time, which
was almost correct so long as we

06:30:06.441 --> 06:30:09.701
potentially double back a page if maybe
we go a little too far in the phone

06:30:09.701 --> 06:30:10.201
book.

06:30:10.201 --> 06:30:12.733
So it had a potential bug
but arguably solvable.

06:30:12.733 --> 06:30:15.441
This last algorithm, though, was
the so-called divide and conquer

06:30:15.441 --> 06:30:18.951
strategy where I sort of unnecessarily
tore the phone book in half

06:30:18.951 --> 06:30:20.961
and then in half and
then in half, which,

06:30:20.961 --> 06:30:25.251
as dramatic as that was unnecessarily,
it actually took significantly bigger

06:30:25.251 --> 06:30:27.781
bites out of the
problem-- like 500 pages

06:30:27.781 --> 06:30:33.721
the first time, another 250, another
125 versus just 1 or 2 bytes at a time.

06:30:33.721 --> 06:30:36.944
And so we described its
running time as this picture

06:30:36.944 --> 06:30:39.861
there, though I didn't use that
expression at the time, running times.

06:30:39.861 --> 06:30:42.591
But indeed, time to solve
might be measured just

06:30:42.591 --> 06:30:44.511
abstractly in some unit of measure--

06:30:44.511 --> 06:30:47.781
seconds, milliseconds, minutes, pages--

06:30:47.781 --> 06:30:49.471
via this y-axis here.

06:30:49.471 --> 06:30:52.191
So let's now slap some numbers on this.

06:30:52.191 --> 06:30:55.611
If we had n pages in that
phone book, n just representing

06:30:55.611 --> 06:30:57.981
a generic number, the
first algorithm here

06:30:57.981 --> 06:30:59.811
we might describe as taking n steps.

06:30:59.811 --> 06:31:03.231
Second algorithm we might describe
as taking n divided by 2 steps,

06:31:03.231 --> 06:31:05.871
maybe give or take one if we
have to double back but generally

06:31:05.871 --> 06:31:06.843
n divided by 2.

06:31:06.843 --> 06:31:09.051
And then this thing, if you
remember your logarithms,

06:31:09.051 --> 06:31:11.009
was sort of a fundamentally
different formula--

06:31:11.009 --> 06:31:14.461
log base 2 of n or just
log of n for short.

06:31:14.461 --> 06:31:17.151
So this is of a fundamentally
different formula.

06:31:17.151 --> 06:31:20.791
But what's noteworthy is that
these first two algorithms,

06:31:20.791 --> 06:31:24.411
even though, yes, the second
algorithm was hands down faster--

06:31:24.411 --> 06:31:26.271
I mean, literally twice as fast--

06:31:26.271 --> 06:31:30.921
when you start to zoom out and if
I increase my y-axis and x-axis,

06:31:30.921 --> 06:31:36.921
these first two start to look
awfully similar to one another.

06:31:36.921 --> 06:31:39.171
And if we keep zooming out
and zooming out and zooming

06:31:39.171 --> 06:31:41.451
out as n gets really large--

06:31:41.451 --> 06:31:43.581
that is, the x-axis gets really long--

06:31:43.581 --> 06:31:47.611
these first two algorithms start
to become essentially the same.

06:31:47.611 --> 06:31:50.751
And so this is where computer
scientists use big O notation.

06:31:50.751 --> 06:31:54.781
Instead of saying specifically,
this algorithm takes any steps.

06:31:54.781 --> 06:31:57.981
And this one n divided by 2, a
computer scientist would say,

06:31:57.981 --> 06:32:01.371
eh, each of those algorithms
takes on the order of n steps

06:32:01.371 --> 06:32:03.381
or on the order of n over 2.

06:32:03.381 --> 06:32:04.131
But you know what?

06:32:04.131 --> 06:32:07.851
On the order of n over 2
is pretty much the same

06:32:07.851 --> 06:32:13.981
when n gets really large as being
equivalent to big O of n itself.

06:32:13.981 --> 06:32:18.841
So yes, in practice, it's obviously
fewer steps to move twice as fast.

06:32:18.841 --> 06:32:22.311
But in the big picture, when n
becomes a million, a billion,

06:32:22.311 --> 06:32:24.981
the numbers are already
so darn big at that point

06:32:24.981 --> 06:32:28.041
that these are as, the
shapes of these curves imply,

06:32:28.041 --> 06:32:30.291
pretty much functionally equivalent.

06:32:30.291 --> 06:32:33.441
But this one still
looks better and better

06:32:33.441 --> 06:32:36.831
as n gets large because it's
rising so much less quickly.

06:32:36.831 --> 06:32:39.021
And so here, a computer
scientist would say

06:32:39.021 --> 06:32:43.679
that that third algorithm was on the
order of-- that is, big O of-- log n.

06:32:43.679 --> 06:32:45.471
And they don't have to
bother with the base

06:32:45.471 --> 06:32:49.341
because it's a smaller mathematical
detail that is also just in some sense

06:32:49.341 --> 06:32:52.251
a constant, multiplicative factor.

06:32:52.251 --> 06:32:54.201
So in short, what are
the takeaways here?

06:32:54.201 --> 06:32:56.631
This is just a new
vocabulary that we'll start

06:32:56.631 --> 06:33:00.381
to use when we just want to describe
the running time of an algorithm.

06:33:00.381 --> 06:33:02.961
To make this more real, if
any of you have implemented

06:33:02.961 --> 06:33:08.651
a for loop at this point in any of your
code and that for loop iterated n times

06:33:08.651 --> 06:33:11.951
where maybe in was the height
of your pyramid or maybe n

06:33:11.951 --> 06:33:15.911
was something else that you wanted
to do n times, you wrote code

06:33:15.911 --> 06:33:20.801
or you implemented an algorithm
that operated in big O of n time,

06:33:20.801 --> 06:33:21.561
if you will.

06:33:21.561 --> 06:33:23.711
So this is just a way now
to retroactively start

06:33:23.711 --> 06:33:27.851
describing with somewhat
mathematical notation what we've

06:33:27.851 --> 06:33:30.141
been doing in practice for a while now.

06:33:30.141 --> 06:33:35.861
So here's a list of commonly seen
running times in the real world.

06:33:35.861 --> 06:33:39.371
This is not a thorough list
because you could come up

06:33:39.371 --> 06:33:42.071
with an infinite number of
mathematical formulas, certainly.

06:33:42.071 --> 06:33:45.761
But the common ones we'll discuss
and you will see in your own code

06:33:45.761 --> 06:33:48.431
probably reduce to this list here.

06:33:48.431 --> 06:33:50.681
And if you were to study
more computer science theory,

06:33:50.681 --> 06:33:52.264
this list would get longer and longer.

06:33:52.264 --> 06:33:56.291
But for now, these are sort of the
most familiar ones that we'll soon see.

06:33:56.291 --> 06:33:59.061
All right, two other pieces
of vocabulary, if you will,

06:33:59.061 --> 06:34:00.521
before we start to use this stuff--

06:34:00.521 --> 06:34:03.791
so this, a big omega,
capital omega symbol,

06:34:03.791 --> 06:34:09.531
is used now to describe a lower bound
on the running time of an algorithm.

06:34:09.531 --> 06:34:12.971
So to be clear, big O is
on the order of-- that

06:34:12.971 --> 06:34:16.271
is, an upper bound-- on how
many steps an algorithm might

06:34:16.271 --> 06:34:19.061
take, on the order of so many steps.

06:34:19.061 --> 06:34:21.941
If you want to talk, though,
from the other perspective, well,

06:34:21.941 --> 06:34:24.071
how few steps my algorithm take?

06:34:24.071 --> 06:34:26.681
Maybe in the so-called
best case, it'd be nice

06:34:26.681 --> 06:34:29.471
if we had a notation to
just describe what a lower

06:34:29.471 --> 06:34:32.201
bound is because some
algorithms might be super fast

06:34:32.201 --> 06:34:34.251
in these so-called best cases.

06:34:34.251 --> 06:34:38.021
So the symbology is almost the
same, but we replace the big O

06:34:38.021 --> 06:34:39.141
with the big omega.

06:34:39.141 --> 06:34:42.941
So to be clear, big O describes
an upper bound and omega

06:34:42.941 --> 06:34:44.471
describes a lower bound.

06:34:44.471 --> 06:34:46.901
And we'll see examples
of this before long.

06:34:46.901 --> 06:34:52.781
And then lastly, last one here, big
theta, is used by a computer scientist

06:34:52.781 --> 06:34:57.221
when you have a case where both
the upper bound on an algorithm's

06:34:57.221 --> 06:35:00.251
running time is the
same as the lower bound.

06:35:00.251 --> 06:35:03.941
You can then describe it in one breath
as being in theta of such and such

06:35:03.941 --> 06:35:08.021
instead of saying it's in big O
and in omega of something else.

06:35:08.021 --> 06:35:12.281
All right, so out of context, sort
of just seemingly cryptic symbols,

06:35:12.281 --> 06:35:15.824
but all they refer to is upper
bounds, lower bounds, or when

06:35:15.824 --> 06:35:17.241
they happen to be one in the same.

06:35:17.241 --> 06:35:20.801
And we'll now introduce over time
examples of how we might actually

06:35:20.801 --> 06:35:23.351
apply these to concrete problems.

06:35:23.351 --> 06:35:27.581
But first, let me pause to
see if there's any questions.

06:35:27.581 --> 06:35:30.481
Any questions here?

06:35:30.481 --> 06:35:32.791
Any questions?

06:35:32.791 --> 06:35:35.241
I see pointing somewhere.

06:35:35.241 --> 06:35:37.461
Where are you pointing to?

06:35:37.461 --> 06:35:39.001
Over here-- there we go.

06:35:39.001 --> 06:35:40.341
OK, sorry-- very bright.

06:35:40.341 --> 06:35:43.921
AUDIENCE: So, um, smaller--

06:35:43.921 --> 06:35:45.881
DAVID J. MALAN: Smaller n
functions move faster.

06:35:45.881 --> 06:35:50.621
So yes, if you have something
like n, that takes only steps.

06:35:50.621 --> 06:35:53.626
If you have a formula like n
squared, just by nature of the math,

06:35:53.626 --> 06:35:56.261
that take more steps
and therefore be slower.

06:35:56.261 --> 06:35:58.171
So the larger the
mathematical expression,

06:35:58.171 --> 06:36:02.641
the slower your algorithm is because the
more time or more steps that it takes.

06:36:05.161 --> 06:36:07.531
AUDIENCE: So you want your
n function to be small?

06:36:07.531 --> 06:36:10.771
DAVID J. MALAN: You want your n function,
so to speak, to be small, yes.

06:36:10.771 --> 06:36:12.581
And in fact, the Holy
Grail, so to speak,

06:36:12.581 --> 06:36:16.051
would be this last one here either
in big O notation or even theta,

06:36:16.051 --> 06:36:19.261
when an algorithm is on
the order of a single step.

06:36:19.261 --> 06:36:23.731
That means it literally takes constant
time, one step, or maybe 10 steps,

06:36:23.731 --> 06:36:26.851
100 steps, but a fixed,
constant number of steps.

06:36:26.851 --> 06:36:30.481
That's the best because even
as the phone book gets bigger,

06:36:30.481 --> 06:36:34.711
even as the data set you're
searching gets larger and larger,

06:36:34.711 --> 06:36:37.861
if something only takes a finite
number of steps constantly,

06:36:37.861 --> 06:36:42.001
then it doesn't matter how big
the data set actually gets.

06:36:42.001 --> 06:36:46.201
Questions as well on these notations--
yep, thank you for the pointing.

06:36:46.201 --> 06:36:47.671
This is actually very helpful.

06:36:47.671 --> 06:36:49.531
I'm seeing pointing this way?

06:36:49.531 --> 06:36:52.588
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:36:52.588 --> 06:36:54.921
DAVID J. MALAN: What is the input
to each of these functions?

06:36:54.921 --> 06:36:58.071
It is an expression of how
many steps an algorithm takes.

06:36:58.071 --> 06:37:00.441
So in fact, let me go
ahead and make this

06:37:00.441 --> 06:37:03.581
more concrete with an actual
example here if we could.

06:37:03.581 --> 06:37:06.711
So on stage here, we have
seven lockers which represent,

06:37:06.711 --> 06:37:08.661
if you will, an array of memory.

06:37:08.661 --> 06:37:10.761
And this array of
memory is maybe storing

06:37:10.761 --> 06:37:14.511
seven integers, seven integers that
we might actually want to search for.

06:37:14.511 --> 06:37:17.451
And if we want to search
for these values, how might

06:37:17.451 --> 06:37:18.409
we go about doing this?

06:37:18.409 --> 06:37:20.618
Well, for this, why don't
we make things interesting?

06:37:20.618 --> 06:37:22.171
Would a volunteer like to come on up?

06:37:22.171 --> 06:37:25.281
Have to be masked and on the
internet if you are comfortable.

06:37:25.281 --> 06:37:28.551
Both of-- oh, there's someone putting
their friend's hand up and back?

06:37:28.551 --> 06:37:29.301
Yes, OK.

06:37:29.301 --> 06:37:30.021
Come on down.

06:37:34.741 --> 06:37:37.441
And in just a moment,
our brave volunteer

06:37:37.441 --> 06:37:41.431
is going to help me find a
specific number in the data set

06:37:41.431 --> 06:37:42.881
that we have here on the screen.

06:37:42.881 --> 06:37:46.591
So come on down, and I'll get things
ready for you in advance here.

06:37:46.591 --> 06:37:53.084
Come on down nice to meet.

06:37:53.084 --> 06:37:54.001
And what is your name?

06:37:54.001 --> 06:37:54.751
AUDIENCE: [? Nomira. ?]

06:37:54.751 --> 06:37:55.006
DAVID J. MALAN: Minera?

06:37:55.006 --> 06:37:55.891
AUDIENCE: [? Nomira. ?]

06:37:55.891 --> 06:37:57.474
DAVID J. MALAN: [? Nomira. ?] Nice to meet.

06:37:57.474 --> 06:37:58.061
Come on over.

06:37:58.061 --> 06:38:02.011
So here we have for Nomira seven
lockers or an array of memory.

06:38:02.011 --> 06:38:03.901
And behind each of
these doors is a number.

06:38:03.901 --> 06:38:06.930
And the goal, quite simply,
is, given this array of memory

06:38:06.930 --> 06:38:12.041
as input, to return, true or false, is
the number I care about actually there?

06:38:12.041 --> 06:38:14.611
So suppose I care about the number 0.

06:38:14.611 --> 06:38:18.091
What would be the simplest,
most correct algorithm you could

06:38:18.091 --> 06:38:22.561
apply in order to find us the number 0?

06:38:22.561 --> 06:38:25.969
OK, try opening the first one.

06:38:25.969 --> 06:38:28.511
All right, and maybe just step
aside so the audience can see.

06:38:28.511 --> 06:38:30.521
I think you have not found 0 yet.

06:38:30.521 --> 06:38:31.841
OK, so keep the door open.

06:38:31.841 --> 06:38:34.481
Let's move on to your next choice.

06:38:34.481 --> 06:38:35.441
Second door, sure.

06:38:35.441 --> 06:38:36.359
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:38:36.359 --> 06:38:37.901
DAVID J. MALAN: Oh, go ahead, second door.

06:38:37.901 --> 06:38:38.776
Let's keep it simple.

06:38:38.776 --> 06:38:41.918
Let's just move from left to
right, sort of searching our way.

06:38:41.918 --> 06:38:43.001
And what do you see there?

06:38:43.001 --> 06:38:44.131
Oh, 6, not 0.

06:38:44.131 --> 06:38:45.131
How about the next door?

06:38:48.561 --> 06:38:51.591
All right, also not working
out so well yet, but that's OK.

06:38:51.591 --> 06:38:55.971
If you want to go on to the
next, we're still looking for 0.

06:38:55.971 --> 06:38:57.291
All right, I see a 2.

06:38:57.291 --> 06:38:58.863
All right, it's not so good yet.

06:38:58.863 --> 06:38:59.571
Let's keep going.

06:38:59.571 --> 06:39:01.221
Next door.

06:39:01.221 --> 06:39:02.901
2, 7-- no.

06:39:02.901 --> 06:39:04.461
OK, next door.

06:39:04.461 --> 06:39:10.231
No, that's a-- all
right, very well done.

06:39:10.231 --> 06:39:10.731
Oh.

06:39:13.391 --> 06:39:16.691
All right, so I kind of set you
up for a fairly slow algorithm,

06:39:16.691 --> 06:39:18.911
but let me just ask you
to describe what is it

06:39:18.911 --> 06:39:21.895
you did by following
the steps I gave you.

06:39:21.895 --> 06:39:24.021
AUDIENCE: I just went one
by one to each character.

06:39:24.021 --> 06:39:26.021
DAVID J. MALAN: You went one
by one to each character

06:39:26.021 --> 06:39:27.741
if you want to talk into here.

06:39:27.741 --> 06:39:29.501
So you went one by
one by each character.

06:39:29.501 --> 06:39:32.981
And would you say that algorithm
left or right is correct?

06:39:32.981 --> 06:39:34.151
AUDIENCE: No.

06:39:34.151 --> 06:39:35.321
DAVID J. MALAN: No?

06:39:35.321 --> 06:39:37.361
AUDIENCE: Or, yes, in the scenario.

06:39:37.361 --> 06:39:38.861
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, yes in this scenario.

06:39:38.861 --> 06:39:39.881
Why are you hesitating?

06:39:39.881 --> 06:39:40.421
What's going through your mind?

06:39:40.421 --> 06:39:42.461
AUDIENCE: Because it's not the
most efficient way to do it.

06:39:42.461 --> 06:39:43.341
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, good.

06:39:43.341 --> 06:39:45.971
So we see a contrast here
between correctness and design.

06:39:45.971 --> 06:39:48.721
I mean, I do think it was correct
because even though it was slow,

06:39:48.721 --> 06:39:50.321
you eventually found zero.

06:39:50.321 --> 06:39:52.851
But it took some number of steps.

06:39:52.851 --> 06:39:54.641
So in fact, this would be an algorithm.

06:39:54.641 --> 06:39:56.711
It has a name, called linear search.

06:39:56.711 --> 06:39:59.201
And, [? Nomira, ?] as you
did, you kind of walked along

06:39:59.201 --> 06:40:01.096
a line going from left to right.

06:40:01.096 --> 06:40:01.721
Now let me ask.

06:40:01.721 --> 06:40:04.241
If you had gone from right
to left, would the algorithm

06:40:04.241 --> 06:40:07.391
have been fundamentally better?

06:40:07.391 --> 06:40:08.141
AUDIENCE: Yes.

06:40:08.141 --> 06:40:09.246
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, and why?

06:40:09.246 --> 06:40:11.621
AUDIENCE: Because the zero is
here in the first scenario.

06:40:11.621 --> 06:40:15.221
But if it was like, the zero is in
the middle, it wouldn't have been.

06:40:15.221 --> 06:40:19.381
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, and so here is
where the right way to do things

06:40:19.381 --> 06:40:20.631
becomes a little less obvious.

06:40:20.631 --> 06:40:23.068
You would absolutely have
given yourself a better result

06:40:23.068 --> 06:40:25.151
if you would just happened
to start from the right

06:40:25.151 --> 06:40:27.141
or if I had pointed you
to start over there.

06:40:27.141 --> 06:40:30.268
But the catch is if I asked her to
find another number, like the number 8,

06:40:30.268 --> 06:40:31.601
well, that would have backfired.

06:40:31.601 --> 06:40:33.309
And this time, it
would have taken longer

06:40:33.309 --> 06:40:35.811
to find that number because
it's way over here instead.

06:40:35.811 --> 06:40:40.331
And so in the general case, going
left to right or, heck, right to left

06:40:40.331 --> 06:40:43.901
is probably as correct as you can
get because if you know nothing

06:40:43.901 --> 06:40:47.878
about the order of these numbers-- and
indeed, they seem to be fairly random.

06:40:47.878 --> 06:40:49.961
Some of them are smaller,
some of them are bigger.

06:40:49.961 --> 06:40:51.669
There doesn't seem to
be rhyme or reason.

06:40:51.669 --> 06:40:55.691
Linear search is about as good as you
can do when you don't know anything

06:40:55.691 --> 06:40:57.414
a priori about the numbers.

06:40:57.414 --> 06:41:00.081
So I have a little thank you gift
here, a little CS stress ball.

06:41:00.081 --> 06:41:03.041
Round of applause for
our first volunteer.

06:41:03.041 --> 06:41:03.911
Thank you so much.

06:41:08.031 --> 06:41:12.051
Let's try to formalize what I
just described as linear search

06:41:12.051 --> 06:41:15.299
because indeed, no matter which
end [? Nomira ?] had started on,

06:41:15.299 --> 06:41:17.091
I could have kind of
changed up the problem

06:41:17.091 --> 06:41:19.431
to make sure that it
appears to be running slow.

06:41:19.431 --> 06:41:20.451
But it is correct.

06:41:20.451 --> 06:41:23.631
If zero were among those doors,
she absolutely would have found it

06:41:23.631 --> 06:41:24.621
and indeed did.

06:41:24.621 --> 06:41:30.231
So let's now try to translate what
we did into what we might call again

06:41:30.231 --> 06:41:32.571
pseudo code as from week zero.

06:41:32.571 --> 06:41:35.091
So with pseudo code, we
just need a terse English

06:41:35.091 --> 06:41:38.131
like, or any language, syntax
to describe what we did.

06:41:38.131 --> 06:41:40.641
So here might be one formulation
of what [? Nomira ?] did.

06:41:40.641 --> 06:41:45.051
For each door, from left to right,
if the number is behind the door,

06:41:45.051 --> 06:41:46.641
return true.

06:41:46.641 --> 06:41:51.991
Else, at the very end of the program,
you would return false by default.

06:41:51.991 --> 06:41:53.161
And now you got lucky.

06:41:53.161 --> 06:41:55.161
And by the seventh door,
[? Nomira ?] had indeed

06:41:55.161 --> 06:41:58.791
returned true by saying,
well, there is the zero.

06:41:58.791 --> 06:42:01.431
But let's consider if this
pseudo code is now correct,

06:42:01.431 --> 06:42:02.511
an accurate translation.

06:42:02.511 --> 06:42:06.721
First of all, normally, when we've
seen ifs, we might see an if else.

06:42:06.721 --> 06:42:10.461
And yet down here, return
false is aligned with the for.

06:42:10.461 --> 06:42:14.061
Why did I not indent the return
false, or put another way,

06:42:14.061 --> 06:42:21.141
why did I not do if number is behind
door, return true, else return false?

06:42:21.141 --> 06:42:24.216
Why would that version of this
code have been problematic?

06:42:24.216 --> 06:42:25.679
Way in back.

06:42:25.679 --> 06:42:34.681
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:42:34.681 --> 06:42:37.051
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, I'm not sure
it's because of redundancy.

06:42:37.051 --> 06:42:39.361
Let me go ahead and
just make this explicit.

06:42:39.361 --> 06:42:42.871
If I had instead done
else return false, I

06:42:42.871 --> 06:42:47.303
don't think it's so much redundancy
that I'd be worried about.

06:42:47.303 --> 06:42:48.511
Let me bounce somewhere else.

06:42:48.511 --> 06:42:49.171
Yeah, in front?

06:42:49.171 --> 06:42:52.711
AUDIENCE: Um, maybe
[INAUDIBLE] for the entire list

06:42:52.711 --> 06:42:54.091
after just checking one number.

06:42:54.091 --> 06:42:56.281
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, it would
be returning falls for--

06:42:56.281 --> 06:42:58.156
even though I'd only
looked at-- [? Nomira ?]

06:42:58.156 --> 06:42:59.461
had only looked at one element.

06:42:59.461 --> 06:43:02.503
And it would have been as though if
all of these doors were still closed,

06:43:02.503 --> 06:43:05.971
she opens this up and says, nope,
this is not zero, return false.

06:43:05.971 --> 06:43:09.069
That would give me an incorrect
result because obviously,

06:43:09.069 --> 06:43:11.611
at that stage in the algorithm,
she wouldn't have even looked

06:43:11.611 --> 06:43:13.081
through any of the other doors.

06:43:13.081 --> 06:43:16.871
So just the original indentation
of this, if you will,

06:43:16.871 --> 06:43:19.561
without the [? else, ?]
is correct because only

06:43:19.561 --> 06:43:23.311
if I get to the bottom of this
algorithm or the pseudo code does

06:43:23.311 --> 06:43:26.101
it make sense to conclude
at that point, once she's

06:43:26.101 --> 06:43:29.581
gone through all of the doors,
that nope, there's in fact--

06:43:29.581 --> 06:43:32.911
the number I'm looking for is,
in fact, not actually there.

06:43:32.911 --> 06:43:37.151
So how might we consider now the
running time of this algorithm?

06:43:37.151 --> 06:43:40.291
We have a few different
types of vocabulary now.

06:43:40.291 --> 06:43:43.441
And if we consider now how
we might think about this,

06:43:43.441 --> 06:43:46.981
let's start to translate it from
sort of higher level pseudo code

06:43:46.981 --> 06:43:48.781
to something a little lower level.

06:43:48.781 --> 06:43:52.181
We've been writing code using
n and loops and the like.

06:43:52.181 --> 06:43:56.701
So let's take this higher level
pseudo code and now just kind of

06:43:56.701 --> 06:43:59.251
get a middle ground
between English and C.

06:43:59.251 --> 06:44:03.271
Let me propose that we think about
this version of the same algorithm

06:44:03.271 --> 06:44:05.041
as being a little more pedantic.

06:44:05.041 --> 06:44:13.181
For i from 0 to n minus 1, if number
behind doors bracket i return true.

06:44:13.181 --> 06:44:15.881
Otherwise, at the end of
the program, return false.

06:44:15.881 --> 06:44:17.881
Now I'm kind of mixing
English and C here,

06:44:17.881 --> 06:44:20.311
but that's reasonable if the
reader is familiar with C

06:44:20.311 --> 06:44:21.901
or some similar language.

06:44:21.901 --> 06:44:23.891
And notice this pattern here.

06:44:23.891 --> 06:44:29.011
This is a way of just saying in pseudo
code, give myself a variable called i.

06:44:29.011 --> 06:44:33.721
Start at 0 and then just
count up to n minus 1.

06:44:33.721 --> 06:44:37.771
And recall n minus 1 is not one
shy of the end of the array.

06:44:37.771 --> 06:44:40.531
N minus 1 is the end of
the array because again, we

06:44:40.531 --> 06:44:41.941
started counting at 0.

06:44:41.941 --> 06:44:45.241
So this is a very common way
of expressing this kind of loop

06:44:45.241 --> 06:44:48.271
from the left all the way
to the right of an array.

06:44:48.271 --> 06:44:51.571
Doors I'm kind of implicitly
treating as the name of this array,

06:44:51.571 --> 06:44:54.361
like it's a variable from last
week that I defined as being

06:44:54.361 --> 06:44:56.141
an array of integers in this case.

06:44:56.141 --> 06:45:01.051
So doors bracket i means that
when i is 0, it's this location.

06:45:01.051 --> 06:45:02.581
When i is 1, it's this.

06:45:02.581 --> 06:45:06.151
When i is 7 or, more generally n minus--

06:45:06.151 --> 06:45:10.631
sorry, 6 or, more generally, n
minus 1, that's this location here.

06:45:10.631 --> 06:45:13.061
So same idea but a translation of it.

06:45:13.061 --> 06:45:17.281
So now let's consider what the
running time of this algorithm is.

06:45:17.281 --> 06:45:20.371
If we have this menu of possible
answers to this question,

06:45:20.371 --> 06:45:23.291
how efficient or inefficient
is this algorithm,

06:45:23.291 --> 06:45:26.011
let's take a look in the
context of this pseudo code.

06:45:26.011 --> 06:45:28.861
We don't even have to bother
going all the way to C.

06:45:28.861 --> 06:45:32.081
How do we go about analyzing
each of these steps?

06:45:32.081 --> 06:45:33.961
Well, let's consider this.

06:45:33.961 --> 06:45:39.901
This outermost loop here for i from
0 to n minus 1, that line of code

06:45:39.901 --> 06:45:42.151
is going to execute how many times?

06:45:42.151 --> 06:45:45.781
How many times will that loop execute?

06:45:45.781 --> 06:45:48.901
Let me give folks this
moment to think on it.

06:45:48.901 --> 06:45:51.691
How many times is that
going to loop here?

06:45:51.691 --> 06:45:52.945
Yeah, over there.

06:45:52.945 --> 06:45:54.721
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:45:54.721 --> 06:45:55.891
DAVID J. MALAN: n times, right?

06:45:55.891 --> 06:45:58.081
Because it's from 0 to n minus 1.

06:45:58.081 --> 06:46:00.691
And if it's a little weird to
think in from 0 to n minus 1,

06:46:00.691 --> 06:46:04.193
this is essentially the same
mathematically as from 1 to n.

06:46:04.193 --> 06:46:06.151
And that's perhaps a
little more obviously more

06:46:06.151 --> 06:46:08.051
intuitively n total steps.

06:46:08.051 --> 06:46:12.541
So I might just make a note to myself
this loop is going to operate n times.

06:46:12.541 --> 06:46:14.131
What about these inner steps?

06:46:14.131 --> 06:46:17.521
Well, how many steps or seconds
does it take to ask a question?

06:46:17.521 --> 06:46:19.651
If the number behind--

06:46:19.651 --> 06:46:23.101
if the number you're looking
for is behind doors bracket i,

06:46:23.101 --> 06:46:25.931
well, as [? Nomira ?] did,
that's kind of like one step.

06:46:25.931 --> 06:46:27.181
So you open the door and boom.

06:46:27.181 --> 06:46:30.461
All right, maybe it's two steps,
but it's a constant number of steps.

06:46:30.461 --> 06:46:32.681
So this is some constant
number of steps.

06:46:32.681 --> 06:46:34.441
Let's just call it one for simplicity.

06:46:34.441 --> 06:46:37.861
How many steps or seconds
does it take to return true?

06:46:37.861 --> 06:46:40.224
I don't know exactly in
the computer's memory

06:46:40.224 --> 06:46:41.641
but that feels like a single step.

06:46:41.641 --> 06:46:43.301
Just return true.

06:46:43.301 --> 06:46:46.321
So if this takes one
step, this takes one step

06:46:46.321 --> 06:46:49.321
but only if the condition
is true, it looks

06:46:49.321 --> 06:46:53.731
like you're doing a constant
number of things n times.

06:46:53.731 --> 06:46:56.891
Or maybe you're doing
one additional step.

06:46:56.891 --> 06:46:59.371
So in short, the only thing
that really matters here

06:46:59.371 --> 06:47:02.581
in terms of the efficiency or
inefficiency of the algorithm

06:47:02.581 --> 06:47:05.856
is what are you doing again and again
and again because that's obviously

06:47:05.856 --> 06:47:07.231
the thing that's going to add up.

06:47:07.231 --> 06:47:10.441
Doing one thing or two things
a constant number of times?

06:47:10.441 --> 06:47:11.371
Not a big deal.

06:47:11.371 --> 06:47:16.411
But looping, that's going to add up over
time because the more doors there are,

06:47:16.411 --> 06:47:19.781
the bigger n is going to be and the
more steps that's going to take,

06:47:19.781 --> 06:47:22.681
which is all to say if you
were to describe roughly

06:47:22.681 --> 06:47:27.481
how many steps does this
algorithm take in big O notation,

06:47:27.481 --> 06:47:30.421
what might your instincts say?

06:47:30.421 --> 06:47:35.711
How many steps is this algorithm on the
order of given n doors or n integers?

06:47:35.711 --> 06:47:36.211
Yeah?

06:47:36.211 --> 06:47:37.166
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:47:37.166 --> 06:47:38.041
DAVID J. MALAN: Say again?

06:47:38.041 --> 06:47:39.031
AUDIENCE: O n.

06:47:39.031 --> 06:47:40.471
DAVID J. MALAN: Big O of n.

06:47:40.471 --> 06:47:42.581
And indeed, that's going
to be the case here.

06:47:42.581 --> 06:47:42.811
Why?

06:47:42.811 --> 06:47:44.894
Because you're essentially,
at the end of the day,

06:47:44.894 --> 06:47:48.353
doing n things as an upper
bound on running time.

06:47:48.353 --> 06:47:50.311
And that's, in fact, what
exactly what happened

06:47:50.311 --> 06:47:52.861
with [? Nomira. ?] She had
to look at all n lockers

06:47:52.861 --> 06:47:55.811
before finally getting
to the right answer.

06:47:55.811 --> 06:47:58.831
But what if she got
lucky and the number we

06:47:58.831 --> 06:48:01.741
were looking for was not
at the end of the array

06:48:01.741 --> 06:48:04.441
but was at the beginning of the array?

06:48:04.441 --> 06:48:06.011
How might we think about that?

06:48:06.011 --> 06:48:09.481
Well, have a nomenclature for this
too, of course-- omega notation.

06:48:09.481 --> 06:48:12.091
Remember, omega notation
is a lower bound.

06:48:12.091 --> 06:48:18.601
So given this menu of possible running
times for lower bounds on an algorithm,

06:48:18.601 --> 06:48:23.258
what might the omega notation be
for [? Nomira's ?] linear search?

06:48:23.258 --> 06:48:24.631
AUDIENCE: Omega 1.

06:48:24.631 --> 06:48:26.611
DAVID J. MALAN: Omega of 1, and why that?

06:48:26.611 --> 06:48:28.334
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:48:28.334 --> 06:48:30.751
DAVID J. MALAN: Right, because if
just by chance she gets lucky

06:48:30.751 --> 06:48:33.661
and the number she's looking
for is right there where

06:48:33.661 --> 06:48:35.851
she begins the algorithm, that's it.

06:48:35.851 --> 06:48:36.901
It's one step.

06:48:36.901 --> 06:48:39.571
Maybe it's two steps if you have
to unlock the door and open it,

06:48:39.571 --> 06:48:41.101
but it's a constant number of steps.

06:48:41.101 --> 06:48:43.171
And the way we describe
constant number of steps

06:48:43.171 --> 06:48:45.391
is just with a single number like 1.

06:48:45.391 --> 06:48:49.351
So the omega notation for linear
search might be omega of 1

06:48:49.351 --> 06:48:53.011
because in the best case, she might just
get the number right from the get go.

06:48:53.011 --> 06:48:56.221
But in the worst case, we need to
talk about the upper bound, which

06:48:56.221 --> 06:48:58.351
might indeed be big O of n.

06:48:58.351 --> 06:49:01.051
So again there's this way
now of talking symbolically

06:49:01.051 --> 06:49:06.511
about best cases and worst cases
or lower bounds and upper bounds.

06:49:06.511 --> 06:49:09.241
Theta notation, just
as a little trivia now,

06:49:09.241 --> 06:49:12.326
is it applicable based on the
definition I gave earlier?

06:49:12.326 --> 06:49:13.201
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:49:13.201 --> 06:49:15.991
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, no, because you
only take out the theta notation

06:49:15.991 --> 06:49:18.481
when those two bounds,
upper and lower, happen

06:49:18.481 --> 06:49:21.101
to be the same for shorthand
notation, if you will.

06:49:21.101 --> 06:49:25.891
So it suffices here to talk about
just big O and omega notation.

06:49:25.891 --> 06:49:28.241
Well, what if we are a
