06:51:38.578 --> 06:51:40.681
DAVID J. MALAN: Right, and
voila, the number six.

06:51:40.681 --> 06:51:42.201
All right, so very nicely done.

06:51:44.981 --> 06:51:46.681
A little stressful for you as well.

06:51:46.681 --> 06:51:47.571
Thank you again.

06:51:47.571 --> 06:51:50.151
So here we see by nature
of the locker door

06:51:50.151 --> 06:51:54.711
still being open sort of an
artifact of the greater efficiency,

06:51:54.711 --> 06:51:57.921
it would seem, of this
algorithm because now that Rave

06:51:57.921 --> 06:52:00.831
was given the assumption that
these numbers are sorted from small

06:52:00.831 --> 06:52:04.671
on the left to large on the right,
she was able to apply that same divide

06:52:04.671 --> 06:52:07.971
and conquer algorithm from week zero
which we're now going to give a name--

06:52:07.971 --> 06:52:09.561
binary search.

06:52:09.561 --> 06:52:13.011
And simply by starting in
the middle and realizing,

06:52:13.011 --> 06:52:17.031
OK, too small, then by going to
the right half and realizing, oh,

06:52:17.031 --> 06:52:20.181
went a little too far, then by
going to the left half, which,

06:52:20.181 --> 06:52:24.021
Rave able to find in just
three steps instead of seven

06:52:24.021 --> 06:52:28.081
the number six in this case that
we were actually searching for.

06:52:28.081 --> 06:52:32.251
So you can see that this would
seem to be more efficient.

06:52:32.251 --> 06:52:35.061
Let's consider for just
a moment is it correct.

06:52:35.061 --> 06:52:40.611
If I had used different numbers but
still sorted them from left to right,

06:52:40.611 --> 06:52:43.739
would it still have
worked this algorithm?

06:52:43.739 --> 06:52:44.781
You're nodding your head.

06:52:44.781 --> 06:52:45.531
Can I call on you?

06:52:45.531 --> 06:52:48.281
Like, why would it still
have worked, do you think?

06:52:48.281 --> 06:52:51.061
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

06:52:51.061 --> 06:52:52.811
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so
so long as the numbers

06:52:52.811 --> 06:52:55.121
are always in the same
order from left to right

06:52:55.121 --> 06:52:58.331
or, heck, they could even be in reverse
order, so long as it's consistent,

06:52:58.331 --> 06:53:02.771
the decisions that Rave was making--
if greater than, else, if less than--

06:53:02.771 --> 06:53:05.181
would guide us to the
solution no matter what.

06:53:05.181 --> 06:53:07.821
And it would seem to take fewer steps.

06:53:07.821 --> 06:53:10.581
So if we consider now the
pseudo code for this algorithm,

06:53:10.581 --> 06:53:12.891
let's take a look how we
might describe binary search.

06:53:12.891 --> 06:53:15.761
So binary search we might
describe with something like this.

06:53:15.761 --> 06:53:19.001
If the number is behind the middle
door, which is where Rave began,

06:53:19.001 --> 06:53:21.071
then we can just return true.

06:53:21.071 --> 06:53:24.651
Else if the number is
less than the middle door,

06:53:24.651 --> 06:53:27.161
so if six is less than whatever
is behind the middle door,

06:53:27.161 --> 06:53:29.501
then Rave would have
searched the left half.

06:53:29.501 --> 06:53:32.051
Else if the number is
greater than the middle door,

06:53:32.051 --> 06:53:34.061
Rave would have searched the right half.

06:53:34.061 --> 06:53:38.201
Else, if there are no doors-- and
we'll see in a moment why I put

06:53:38.201 --> 06:53:40.071
this up top just to keep things clean.

06:53:40.071 --> 06:53:43.751
If there's no doors, what should Rave
have presumably returned immediately

06:53:43.751 --> 06:53:47.231
if I gave her no lockers to work with?

06:53:47.231 --> 06:53:48.281
Just returned false.

06:53:48.281 --> 06:53:50.381
But this is an important
case to consider

06:53:50.381 --> 06:53:54.341
because if in the process of
searching by locker by locker,

06:53:54.341 --> 06:53:58.991
we might have whittled down the
problem from seven doors to three doors

06:53:58.991 --> 06:54:01.731
to one door to zero
doors-- and at that point,

06:54:01.731 --> 06:54:03.591
we might have had no
doors left to search.

06:54:03.591 --> 06:54:06.401
So we have to naturally have a
scenario for just considering

06:54:06.401 --> 06:54:07.481
if there were no doors.

06:54:07.481 --> 06:54:11.271
So it's not to say that maybe I don't
give Rave any doors to begin with.

06:54:11.271 --> 06:54:13.271
But as she divides and
divides and divides,

06:54:13.271 --> 06:54:17.081
if she runs out of lockers to ask those
questions of-- or a few weeks ago,

06:54:17.081 --> 06:54:20.021
if I ran out of phone book
pages to tear in half,

06:54:20.021 --> 06:54:23.571
I too might have had to
return false as in this case.

06:54:23.571 --> 06:54:26.861
So how can we now describe
this a little more like C

06:54:26.861 --> 06:54:30.071
just to give ourselves a variable
to start thinking and talking about?

06:54:30.071 --> 06:54:33.291
Well, I might talk about
doors as being an array.

06:54:33.291 --> 06:54:36.851
And so if I want to express the middle
door, I could just, in pseudo code,

06:54:36.851 --> 06:54:38.831
say doors bracket middle.

06:54:38.831 --> 06:54:40.631
I'm assuming that
someone has done the math

06:54:40.631 --> 06:54:43.811
to figure out what the middle door
is, but that's easy enough to do.

06:54:43.811 --> 06:54:46.301
And then doors, if the
number we're looking for

06:54:46.301 --> 06:54:49.831
is less than doors bracket
middle, then search door

06:54:49.831 --> 06:54:53.591
zero through doors middle minus 1.

06:54:53.591 --> 06:54:57.971
So again, this is a more pedantic way of
taking what's a pretty intuitive idea--

06:54:57.971 --> 06:55:00.520
search the left half,
search the right half--

06:55:00.520 --> 06:55:07.151
but start to now describe it in
terms of actual indices or indexes

06:55:07.151 --> 06:55:08.954
like we did with our array notation.

06:55:08.954 --> 06:55:10.871
The last scenario, of
course, is if the number

06:55:10.871 --> 06:55:13.121
is greater than the
door's bracket middle,

06:55:13.121 --> 06:55:16.421
then Rave would have wanted to
search the middle door plus 1--

06:55:16.421 --> 06:55:21.611
so 1 over-- through doors n minus 1--

06:55:21.611 --> 06:55:22.691
through n minus 1.

06:55:22.691 --> 06:55:25.750
So again, just a way of sort of
describing a little more syntactically

06:55:25.750 --> 06:55:27.350
what it is that's going on.

06:55:27.350 --> 06:55:31.181
So how might we translate
this now into big O notation?

06:55:31.181 --> 06:55:38.231
Well, in the worst case, how many
steps total might Rave's binary search

06:55:38.231 --> 06:55:39.431
algorithm have taken?

06:55:39.431 --> 06:55:43.391
Given seven doors or given
more generically n doors,

06:55:43.391 --> 06:55:47.981
how many times could she go left or go
right before finding herself with one

06:55:47.981 --> 06:55:50.471
or no doors left?

06:55:50.471 --> 06:55:53.148
What's the way to think about that?

06:55:53.148 --> 06:55:53.981
Yeah, in the middle?

06:55:53.981 --> 06:55:55.511
AUDIENCE: Log n.

06:55:55.511 --> 06:55:56.351
DAVID J. MALAN: Log n.

06:55:56.351 --> 06:55:57.641
So there's log n again.

06:55:57.641 --> 06:56:00.516
And even if you're not feeling wholly
comfortable with your logarithm

06:56:00.516 --> 06:56:03.611
still, pretty much in programming and
in computer science more generally,

06:56:03.611 --> 06:56:06.791
any time we talk about some algorithm
that's dividing and conquering

06:56:06.791 --> 06:56:10.541
in half, in half, in half,
or any other multiple,

06:56:10.541 --> 06:56:12.941
it's probably involving
logarithms in some sense.

06:56:12.941 --> 06:56:15.761
And log base n essentially
refers to the number

06:56:15.761 --> 06:56:21.521
of times you can divide n by 2 until
you bottom out at just a single door

06:56:21.521 --> 06:56:23.771
or equivalently zero doors left.

06:56:23.771 --> 06:56:24.731
So log n.

06:56:24.731 --> 06:56:28.631
So we might say that indeed,
binary search is in big O of log n

06:56:28.631 --> 06:56:32.801
because the door that Rave
opened last, this one,

06:56:32.801 --> 06:56:34.721
happened to be three doors away.

06:56:34.721 --> 06:56:37.151
And actually, if you do
the math here, that roughly

06:56:37.151 --> 06:56:38.961
works out to be exactly that case.

06:56:38.961 --> 06:56:43.001
If we add one, that's sort of out
of seven doors or roughly eight,

06:56:43.001 --> 06:56:46.301
we were able to search it
in just three total steps.

06:56:46.301 --> 06:56:48.341
What about omega notation, though?

06:56:48.341 --> 06:56:51.581
Like, in the best case, Rave
might have gotten lucky.

06:56:51.581 --> 06:56:53.531
She opened the door, and there it is.

06:56:53.531 --> 06:56:59.331
So how might we describe a lower bound
on the running time of binary search.

06:56:59.331 --> 06:56:59.831
Yeah.

06:56:59.831 --> 06:57:00.486
AUDIENCE: 1.

06:57:00.486 --> 06:57:01.361
DAVID J. MALAN: Say again?

06:57:01.361 --> 06:57:02.201
AUDIENCE: 1.

06:57:02.201 --> 06:57:03.581
DAVID J. MALAN: Omega of 1.

06:57:03.581 --> 06:57:08.171
So here too, we see that in some cases
binary search and linear search, eh,

06:57:08.171 --> 06:57:10.061
like, they're pretty equivalent.

06:57:10.061 --> 06:57:15.191
And so this is why sometimes
compelling to consider both the best

06:57:15.191 --> 06:57:17.651
case in the worst case
because honestly, in general,

06:57:17.651 --> 06:57:19.961
who really cares if you just
get lucky once in a while

06:57:19.961 --> 06:57:21.641
and your algorithm is super fast?

06:57:21.641 --> 06:57:24.611
What you probably care about
is what's the worst case.

06:57:24.611 --> 06:57:25.751
How long are my users--

06:57:25.751 --> 06:57:29.531
how long am I going to be sitting
there watching some spinning hourglass

06:57:29.531 --> 06:57:35.168
or beach ball trying to give myself
an answer to a pretty big problem?

06:57:35.168 --> 06:57:38.001
Well, odds are, you're going to
generally care about big O notation.

06:57:38.001 --> 06:57:39.791
So indeed, moving
forward, will generally

06:57:39.791 --> 06:57:43.091
talk about the running time of
algorithms often in terms of big O,

06:57:43.091 --> 06:57:45.141
a little less so in terms of omega.

06:57:45.141 --> 06:57:47.501
But understanding the
range can be important

06:57:47.501 --> 06:57:53.061
depending on the nature of the data that
you're going to actually be given here.

06:57:53.061 --> 06:57:55.871
All right let me pause and
see if there is any questions.

06:57:58.561 --> 06:58:00.781
Any questions here?

06:58:00.781 --> 06:58:03.211
Yes, thank you.

06:58:03.211 --> 06:58:05.791
AUDIENCE: So this method
is clearly more efficient,

06:58:05.791 --> 06:58:10.801
but it requires that the information
is all compiled in a certain order.

06:58:10.801 --> 06:58:14.131
How do you ensure that you
can compile information

06:58:14.131 --> 06:58:15.626
in a particular order at scale?

06:58:15.626 --> 06:58:17.501
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, it's
a really good question.

06:58:17.501 --> 06:58:20.376
And if I can generalize it, how do
you guarantee that you can do this

06:58:20.376 --> 06:58:22.921
at scale, which algorithm is better?

06:58:22.921 --> 06:58:25.801
I've sort of led us down
this road of implying

06:58:25.801 --> 06:58:27.901
that Rave's second
algorithm, binary search,

06:58:27.901 --> 06:58:29.941
is better because it's so much faster.

06:58:29.941 --> 06:58:33.961
It's log of n in the worst
case instead of big O of n.

06:58:33.961 --> 06:58:37.591
But Rave was given an advantage when
she came up here in that the doors were

06:58:37.591 --> 06:58:38.583
already sorted.

06:58:38.583 --> 06:58:40.291
And so that sort of
invites the question,

06:58:40.291 --> 06:58:42.031
well, given a whole
bunch of random data,

06:58:42.031 --> 06:58:45.071
either a small data set or, heck,
something Google sized with millions,

06:58:45.071 --> 06:58:48.901
billions of pieces of data,
should you sort it first

06:58:48.901 --> 06:58:51.511
from smallest to
largest and then search?

06:58:51.511 --> 06:58:56.281
Or should you just dive right
in and search it linearly?

06:58:56.281 --> 06:58:57.999
Like, how might you think about that?

06:58:57.999 --> 06:58:59.791
If you are Google, for
instance, and you've

06:58:59.791 --> 06:59:03.451
got millions, billions of web pages,
should they just go with linear search

06:59:03.451 --> 06:59:06.211
because it's always going to work
even though it might be slow?

06:59:06.211 --> 06:59:09.181
Or should they invest the time
in sorting all of that data--

06:59:09.181 --> 06:59:10.991
we'll see how in a bit--

06:59:10.991 --> 06:59:13.261
and then search it more efficiently?

06:59:13.261 --> 06:59:15.799
Like, how do you decide
between those options?

06:59:15.799 --> 06:59:18.091
AUDIENCE: If you're sorting
the data, then wouldn't you

06:59:18.091 --> 06:59:20.934
have to go through all of the data?

06:59:20.934 --> 06:59:23.101
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, if you had
to sort the data first--

06:59:23.101 --> 06:59:25.061
and we don't yet formally
know how to do this.

06:59:25.061 --> 06:59:27.478
But obviously, as humans, we
could probably figure it out.

06:59:27.478 --> 06:59:29.641
You do have to look at
all of the data anyway.

06:59:29.641 --> 06:59:33.121
And so you're sort of wasting your
time if you're sorting it only

06:59:33.121 --> 06:59:35.041
then to go in search it.

06:59:35.041 --> 06:59:37.259
But maybe it depends a bit more.

06:59:37.259 --> 06:59:39.301
Like, that's absolutely
right, and if you're just

06:59:39.301 --> 06:59:42.421
searching for one thing in life,
then that's probably a waste of time

06:59:42.421 --> 06:59:46.081
to sort it and then search it because
you're just adding to the process.

06:59:46.081 --> 06:59:48.241
But what's another scenario
in which you might not

06:59:48.241 --> 06:59:53.771
worry about that whereby it might
make sense to sort it and then search?

06:59:53.771 --> 06:59:54.271
Yeah.

06:59:54.271 --> 07:00:00.941
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] you can go
and use the other values as a way

07:00:00.941 --> 07:00:02.171
to find out what's happening.

07:00:02.171 --> 07:00:03.213
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, exactly.

07:00:03.213 --> 07:00:05.753
So if your problem is a
Google-like problem where

07:00:05.753 --> 07:00:08.711
you have more than just one user
who's searching for more than just one

07:00:08.711 --> 07:00:11.141
website page, probably you
should incur the cost up front

07:00:11.141 --> 07:00:14.981
and sort the whole thing because
every subsequent request thereafter

07:00:14.981 --> 07:00:17.171
is going to be faster,
faster, faster because it's

07:00:17.171 --> 07:00:20.801
going to [INAUDIBLE] algorithm of binary
search, binary search, binary search

07:00:20.801 --> 07:00:23.681
that's going to add up
to be way fewer steps

07:00:23.681 --> 07:00:25.971
than doing linear search multiple times.

07:00:25.971 --> 07:00:27.851
So again, kind of
depends on the use case

07:00:27.851 --> 07:00:29.711
and kind of depends on
how important it is.

07:00:29.711 --> 07:00:32.411
And this happens even
in real world contexts.

07:00:32.411 --> 07:00:35.261
I think back always to graduate
school, when I was writing some code

07:00:35.261 --> 07:00:36.971
to analyze some large data set.

07:00:36.971 --> 07:00:39.761
And honestly, it was actually
easier at the time for me

07:00:39.761 --> 07:00:42.671
to write pretty inefficient
but hopefully correct

07:00:42.671 --> 07:00:43.898
code because you know what?

07:00:43.898 --> 07:00:47.231
I could just go to sleep for eight hours
and let it analyze this really big data

07:00:47.231 --> 07:00:47.731
set.

07:00:47.731 --> 07:00:50.696
I didn't have to bother writing
more complex code to sort it just

07:00:50.696 --> 07:00:51.821
to run it more efficiently.

07:00:51.821 --> 07:00:52.321
Why?

07:00:52.321 --> 07:00:55.881
Because I was the only user, and I
only needed to run these queries once.

07:00:55.881 --> 07:00:58.061
And so this was kind of
a reasonable approach,

07:00:58.061 --> 07:01:01.911
reasonable until I woke up eight
hours later and my code was incorrect.

07:01:01.911 --> 07:01:05.321
And now I had to spend another eight
hours rerunning it after fixing it.

07:01:05.321 --> 07:01:07.271
But even there, you
see an example where,

07:01:07.271 --> 07:01:09.251
what is your most precious resource?

07:01:09.251 --> 07:01:11.081
Is it time to run the code?

07:01:11.081 --> 07:01:13.331
Is it time to write the code?

07:01:13.331 --> 07:01:15.459
Is it the amount of memory
the computer is using?

07:01:15.459 --> 07:01:18.251
These are all resources we'll start
to talk about because it really

07:01:18.251 --> 07:01:20.441
depends on what your goals are.

07:01:20.441 --> 07:01:23.411
Any questions, then, on
upper bounds, lower bounds,

07:01:23.411 --> 07:01:26.621
or each of these two
searches, linear or binary?

07:01:26.621 --> 07:01:27.352
Yeah.

07:01:27.352 --> 07:01:29.941
AUDIENCE: So just, when you're
calculating running time,

07:01:29.941 --> 07:01:34.678
does the sorting step
count for that time?

07:01:34.678 --> 07:01:37.511
DAVID J. MALAN: When analyzing running
time, does the sorting step count?

07:01:37.511 --> 07:01:39.671
If you want it to if you actually do it.

07:01:39.671 --> 07:01:41.261
At the moment, it did not apply.

07:01:41.261 --> 07:01:45.461
I just gave Rave the luxury of
knowing that the data was sorted.

07:01:45.461 --> 07:01:48.881
But if I really wanted to charge
her for the amount of time

07:01:48.881 --> 07:01:52.091
it took to find that number six,
I should have added the time

07:01:52.091 --> 07:01:54.073
to sort plus the time to search.

07:01:54.073 --> 07:01:55.781
And in fact, that's
a road we'll go down.

07:01:55.781 --> 07:01:57.531
Why don't we go ahead and
pace ourselves as before?

07:01:57.531 --> 07:01:58.948
Let's take a 10 minute break here.

07:01:58.948 --> 07:02:01.491
And when we come back, we'll
write some actual code.

07:02:01.491 --> 07:02:05.399
So we've seen a couple of searches--
linear search and binary search, which,

07:02:05.399 --> 07:02:06.941
to be fair, we saw back in week zero.

07:02:06.941 --> 07:02:10.151
But let's actually translate at
least one of those now to some code

07:02:10.151 --> 07:02:13.331
using this building block from
last week where we can actually

07:02:13.331 --> 07:02:17.181
define an array if we want, like an
array of integers called numbers.

07:02:17.181 --> 07:02:18.911
So let me switch over to VS Code here.

07:02:18.911 --> 07:02:22.271
Let me go ahead and start
a program called numbers.c.

07:02:22.271 --> 07:02:25.301
And in numbers.c, let me go ahead here.

07:02:25.301 --> 07:02:29.201
And how about let's include
our familiar header files?

07:02:29.201 --> 07:02:31.031
So css50.h.

07:02:31.031 --> 07:02:35.691
I'll include standardio.h that we can
get input and print input if we want.

07:02:35.691 --> 07:02:38.771
And now I'm going to go ahead
and give myself int main void.

07:02:38.771 --> 07:02:40.461
No command line arguments today.

07:02:40.461 --> 07:02:41.593
So I'll leave that as void.

07:02:41.593 --> 07:02:43.301
And I'm going to go
ahead and give myself

07:02:43.301 --> 07:02:45.771
an array of how about seven numbers?

07:02:45.771 --> 07:02:48.581
So I'll call it int number 7.

07:02:48.581 --> 07:02:50.621
And then I can fill
this array with numbers.

07:02:50.621 --> 07:02:54.461
Like, numbers brackets 0 can be
the number 4, and numbers bracket 1

07:02:54.461 --> 07:02:58.669
could be the number 6, and numbers
bracket 2 can be the number 8.

07:02:58.669 --> 07:03:01.211
And this is the same list that
we saw with [? Nomira ?] a bit

07:03:01.211 --> 07:03:03.531
ago where it was 4, then 6, then 8.

07:03:03.531 --> 07:03:04.281
But you know what?

07:03:04.281 --> 07:03:06.701
There's actually another
syntax I can show you here.

07:03:06.701 --> 07:03:09.581
If you know in advance
in a C program that you

07:03:09.581 --> 07:03:14.751
want an array of certain values and you
know therefore how many of those values

07:03:14.751 --> 07:03:17.771
you want, you can actually do this
little trick using curly braces.

07:03:17.771 --> 07:03:20.921
You can say, don't worry
about how big this is.

07:03:20.921 --> 07:03:23.981
It's going to be implicit by
way of these curly braces.

07:03:23.981 --> 07:03:28.971
Here, I can do 4, 6, 8, 2,
7, 5, 0, close curly brace.

07:03:28.971 --> 07:03:31.091
So it's a somewhat new
use of curly braces.

07:03:31.091 --> 07:03:35.141
But this has the effect of giving
me an array called numbers inside

07:03:35.141 --> 07:03:36.801
of which are a whole bunch of integers.

07:03:36.801 --> 07:03:37.391
How many?

07:03:37.391 --> 07:03:41.321
The compiler can infer it from what's
ever inside these curly braces.

07:03:41.321 --> 07:03:44.501
And it seems to be of
size 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.

07:03:44.501 --> 07:03:49.871
And all seven elements will be
initialized with 4, 6, 8, 2, 7, 5, 0

07:03:49.871 --> 07:03:50.691
respectively.

07:03:50.691 --> 07:03:53.141
So just a minor optimization
code wise to tighten up

07:03:53.141 --> 07:03:56.451
what would have otherwise been
like eight separate lines of code.

07:03:56.451 --> 07:03:59.441
Now let's go ahead and implement
linear search, as we called it.

07:03:59.441 --> 07:04:02.483
And you can do this in a bunch of
ways, but I'm going to do it like this.

07:04:02.483 --> 07:04:09.191
For int i get 0, i is
less than 7 i plus plus.

07:04:09.191 --> 07:04:12.161
Then inside of my loop, I'm
going to ask the question, well,

07:04:12.161 --> 07:04:17.381
if the numbers at location i
equals equals, as we asked of

07:04:17.381 --> 07:04:21.041
[? Nomira, ?] the number 0, then I'm
going to go ahead and do something

07:04:21.041 --> 07:04:25.811
like printf found backslash n.

07:04:25.811 --> 07:04:27.641
And then I'm going to return 0.

07:04:27.641 --> 07:04:30.401
Just because of last week's
discussion of returning

07:04:30.401 --> 07:04:34.511
a value for main when all is well,
I'm going to return 0 by convention

07:04:34.511 --> 07:04:37.421
just to signal that indeed,
I found what I'm looking for.

07:04:37.421 --> 07:04:44.921
Otherwise, on what line do I want to
go and add a printf, like, not found

07:04:44.921 --> 07:04:46.961
and return something other than 0?

07:04:46.961 --> 07:04:51.221
Right, I don't think I want an else
here per our pseudo code earlier.

07:04:51.221 --> 07:04:55.391
So on what line would you prefer I
