07:06:44.839 --> 07:06:47.631
Int main void because I'm not going
to bother with any command line

07:06:47.631 --> 07:06:48.841
arguments for now.

07:06:48.841 --> 07:06:53.691
And now if I want an array of strings,
I could do something like this--

07:06:53.691 --> 07:06:56.481
string names bracket 7.

07:06:56.481 --> 07:06:58.461
And then I could start
doing like before.

07:06:58.461 --> 07:07:01.941
Names bracket 0 could be someone
like Bill, and names bracket 1

07:07:01.941 --> 07:07:05.101
could be someone like
Charlie and so forth.

07:07:05.101 --> 07:07:08.713
But there's this new
improvement I can make.

07:07:08.713 --> 07:07:11.421
Let me just let the compiler figure
out how many names there are.

07:07:11.421 --> 07:07:16.911
And using curly braces, I'll do Bill
and then Charlie and then Fred and then

07:07:16.911 --> 07:07:24.051
George and then Ginny and then Percy and
then Ron if there's the pattern there.

07:07:24.051 --> 07:07:27.291
All right, so now I have
these seven names as strings.

07:07:27.291 --> 07:07:28.591
Let's do something similar.

07:07:28.591 --> 07:07:32.091
So for int, i get 0.

07:07:32.091 --> 07:07:35.691
i is less than 7 as before,
i plus plus as before.

07:07:35.691 --> 07:07:39.261
And inside of the, loop lets this
time check for the string in question,

07:07:39.261 --> 07:07:41.991
and suppose we're searching
for Ron arbitrarily.

07:07:41.991 --> 07:07:44.451
He is there, so we should
eventually find him.

07:07:44.451 --> 07:07:51.891
Let me go ahead and say if names bracket
i equals quote unquote Ron, then inside

07:07:51.891 --> 07:07:55.701
of my if condition, I'm going to
say printf found just like before.

07:07:55.701 --> 07:07:57.951
And I'm going to return 0
just because all is well.

07:07:57.951 --> 07:08:00.751
And I'm going to take your
advice from the get go this time

07:08:00.751 --> 07:08:04.921
and, at the end of the loop, print out
not found because if I get this far,

07:08:04.921 --> 07:08:08.031
I have not printed found, and
I have not returned already.

07:08:08.031 --> 07:08:12.201
So I'm just going to go ahead and
return 1 after printing not found.

07:08:12.201 --> 07:08:14.781
All right, let me go ahead and
cross my fingers as always.

07:08:14.781 --> 07:08:17.671
Make names this time.

07:08:17.671 --> 07:08:20.661
And it doesn't seem
to like my code here.

07:08:20.661 --> 07:08:22.731
This is perhaps a new
error that you might not

07:08:22.731 --> 07:08:25.441
have seen yet in names.c line 11.

07:08:25.441 --> 07:08:28.281
So that's this line
here, my if condition.

07:08:28.281 --> 07:08:32.331
Result of comparison against a
string literal is unspecified.

07:08:32.331 --> 07:08:34.753
Use an explicit string
comparison function instead.

07:08:34.753 --> 07:08:37.461
I mean, that's kind of a mouthful,
and the first time you see it,

07:08:37.461 --> 07:08:39.961
you're probably not going to
know how to make sense of that.

07:08:39.961 --> 07:08:43.491
But it does kind of draw our
attention to something being awry

07:08:43.491 --> 07:08:48.201
with the equality checking
here, with equal equals and Ron.

07:08:48.201 --> 07:08:50.601
And here's where again
we've been telling

07:08:50.601 --> 07:08:53.061
sort of a white lie for
the past couple of weeks.

07:08:53.061 --> 07:08:57.261
Strings are a thing in C. Strings
are a thing in programming.

07:08:57.261 --> 07:08:59.031
But recall from last
week, I did disclaim

07:08:59.031 --> 07:09:01.011
there's no such thing
as a string data type

07:09:01.011 --> 07:09:05.031
technically because it's not
a primitive in the way an int

07:09:05.031 --> 07:09:08.571
and a float and a bool are that are
sort of built into the language.

07:09:08.571 --> 07:09:12.531
You can't just use equation
equals to compare two strings.

07:09:12.531 --> 07:09:15.471
You actually have to use
a special function that's

07:09:15.471 --> 07:09:18.501
in this header file we talked
briefly about last week.

07:09:18.501 --> 07:09:21.141
In that header file was
string length or strlen.

07:09:21.141 --> 07:09:23.851
But there's other
functions instead as well.

07:09:23.851 --> 07:09:27.441
Let me, in fact, go ahead
and open up the manual pages.

07:09:27.441 --> 07:09:30.561
And if we go to string.h--

07:09:30.561 --> 07:09:32.121
let me scroll down a bit.

07:09:32.121 --> 07:09:37.161
In string.h you can perhaps infer
what function will probably take

07:09:37.161 --> 07:09:40.973
the place of equals equals for today.

07:09:40.973 --> 07:09:41.931
What do we want to use?

07:09:41.931 --> 07:09:42.291
Yeah.

07:09:42.291 --> 07:09:43.041
AUDIENCE: Strcmp?

07:09:43.041 --> 07:09:47.666
DAVID J. MALAN: So strcmp, S-T-R-C-M-P,
which apparently compares two strings.

07:09:47.666 --> 07:09:49.791
And if I click on that,
we'll see more information.

07:09:49.791 --> 07:09:53.871
And indeed, if I click on strcmp,
we'll see under the synopsis

07:09:53.871 --> 07:09:58.851
that, OK, I need to use the CS50 header
file and string.h, as I already have.

07:09:58.851 --> 07:10:02.211
Here is its prototype,
which is telling me

07:10:02.211 --> 07:10:05.721
that strcmp takes two
strings, S1 and S2, that

07:10:05.721 --> 07:10:07.251
are presumably going to be compared.

07:10:07.251 --> 07:10:09.591
And it returns an integer,
which is interesting.

07:10:09.591 --> 07:10:10.791
So let's read on.

07:10:10.791 --> 07:10:14.091
The description of this function is
that it compares two strings case

07:10:14.091 --> 07:10:15.231
sensitively.

07:10:15.231 --> 07:10:18.471
So uppercase or lowercase
matters, just FYI.

07:10:18.471 --> 07:10:20.931
And then let's look it
the return value here.

07:10:20.931 --> 07:10:25.221
The return value of this function
returns an int less than 0

07:10:25.221 --> 07:10:32.841
if S1 comes before S2, 0 if S1 is the
same as S2, or an int greater than 0

07:10:32.841 --> 07:10:35.301
if S1 comes after S2.

07:10:35.301 --> 07:10:39.141
So the reason that this function
returns an integer and not just a

07:10:39.141 --> 07:10:41.751
bool, true or false, is
that it actually will

07:10:41.751 --> 07:10:45.111
allow us to sort these things
eventually because if you can tell me

07:10:45.111 --> 07:10:49.341
if two strings come in this order or
in this order or they're the same,

07:10:49.341 --> 07:10:51.441
you need three possible return values.

07:10:51.441 --> 07:10:53.271
And a bool, of course,
only gives you two,

07:10:53.271 --> 07:10:56.841
but an int gives you like 4 billion
even though we just need the 3.

07:10:56.841 --> 07:11:01.881
So 0 or a positive number or a negative
number is what this function returns.

07:11:01.881 --> 07:11:06.321
And the documentation goes on to explain
what we mean by ASCIIbetical order.

07:11:06.321 --> 07:11:09.621
Recall that capital A
is 65, capital B is 66,

07:11:09.621 --> 07:11:12.021
and it's those underlying
ASCII or Unicode

07:11:12.021 --> 07:11:15.411
numbers that a computer uses to figure
out whether something comes before it

07:11:15.411 --> 07:11:17.541
or after it like in the dictionary.

07:11:17.541 --> 07:11:20.311
But for our purposes now,
we only care about equality.

07:11:20.311 --> 07:11:22.041
So I'm going to go ahead and do this.

07:11:22.041 --> 07:11:26.181
If I want to compare names
bracket i against Ron,

07:11:26.181 --> 07:11:33.681
I use stir compare or strcmp, names
bracket i comma, quote unquote, Ron.

07:11:33.681 --> 07:11:35.871
So it's a little more
involved than actually

07:11:35.871 --> 07:11:40.191
using equals equals, which
does work for integers, longs,

07:11:40.191 --> 07:11:41.361
and certain other values.

07:11:41.361 --> 07:11:45.211
But for strings, it turns out we
need to use a more powerful function.

07:11:45.211 --> 07:11:45.711
Why?

07:11:45.711 --> 07:11:47.991
Well, last week, recall
what a string really is.

07:11:47.991 --> 07:11:50.581
It's an array of characters.

07:11:50.581 --> 07:11:54.051
And so whereas you can use equals
equals for single characters,

07:11:54.051 --> 07:11:56.961
strcmp, as we'll
eventually see, is going

07:11:56.961 --> 07:11:58.799
to compare multiple characters for us.

07:11:58.799 --> 07:11:59.841
There's more logic there.

07:11:59.841 --> 07:12:03.931
There's a loop needed, and that's
why it comes with the string library.

07:12:03.931 --> 07:12:06.651
But it doesn't just work out of
the box with equals equals alone.

07:12:06.651 --> 07:12:10.628
That would literally be comparing
two things, not two arrays of things.

07:12:10.628 --> 07:12:12.711
And we'll come back to
this next week as to what's

07:12:12.711 --> 07:12:14.281
really going on under the hood.

07:12:14.281 --> 07:12:18.501
So let me go ahead and fix one
bug that I just realized I made.

07:12:18.501 --> 07:12:23.811
I want to check if the return
value of str compare is equal to 0

07:12:23.811 --> 07:12:27.021
because per the documentation,
that meant they're the same.

07:12:27.021 --> 07:12:30.001
All right, let me go ahead
and make names this time.

07:12:30.001 --> 07:12:31.071
Now it compiles.

07:12:31.071 --> 07:12:34.131
Dot slash names, Enter, found.

07:12:34.131 --> 07:12:39.651
And just as a sanity check, let's
check someone outside the family.

07:12:39.651 --> 07:12:43.371
Searching now for Hermione
after recompiling the code,

07:12:43.371 --> 07:12:44.931
after rerunning the code.

07:12:44.931 --> 07:12:46.641
And she's not, in fact, found.

07:12:46.641 --> 07:12:49.581
So here's just a similar
implementation of linear search

07:12:49.581 --> 07:12:53.931
not for integers this time
but instead for strings,

07:12:53.931 --> 07:12:57.501
the subtlety really being we need
a helper function, str compare,

07:12:57.501 --> 07:13:02.001
to actually do the legwork for us of
comparing two arrays of characters.

07:13:02.001 --> 07:13:05.569
All right, questions on either of these
implementations-- yeah, in the middle?

07:13:05.569 --> 07:13:07.253
AUDIENCE: So, if I do [INAUDIBLE]

07:13:07.253 --> 07:13:08.461
DAVID J. MALAN: Ah, good question.

07:13:08.461 --> 07:13:12.621
If I had not fixed what I claimed was
a mistake earlier and I did this--

07:13:12.621 --> 07:13:15.201
and we saw an example of
this last week, actually.

07:13:15.201 --> 07:13:21.831
If a function returns an integer,
be it negative or positive or 0,

07:13:21.831 --> 07:13:25.101
when you get back 0, the
expression, the Boolean expression,

07:13:25.101 --> 07:13:26.401
will be considered false.

07:13:26.401 --> 07:13:29.301
So 0 equals false always.

07:13:29.301 --> 07:13:33.831
If a function returns any positive
number, or any negative number,

07:13:33.831 --> 07:13:37.131
that's going to be
interpreted as true even

07:13:37.131 --> 07:13:41.871
if it's positive or negative, whether
it's 1, negative 1, 2, negative 2.

07:13:41.871 --> 07:13:45.931
And so if I did this, this
would be saying the opposite.

07:13:45.931 --> 07:13:51.291
So if I were to say this, if str compare
of names bracket i and Hermione, that's

07:13:51.291 --> 07:13:57.754
implicitly like saying this does not
equal 0, or it means sort of is true,

07:13:57.754 --> 07:14:00.171
but you don't want to check
for true because, again, we're

07:14:00.171 --> 07:14:01.711
comparing integers here.

07:14:01.711 --> 07:14:05.361
So the reason I did 0
here in this case is

07:14:05.361 --> 07:14:08.971
that it explicitly checks for the return
value that means they're the same.

07:14:08.971 --> 07:14:09.471
And yeah.

07:14:09.471 --> 07:14:10.416
Follow up?

07:14:10.416 --> 07:14:15.309
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:14:15.309 --> 07:14:17.351
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes, you might
not have seen this yet,

07:14:17.351 --> 07:14:20.941
but you can express the
equivalent because if you

07:14:20.941 --> 07:14:24.661
want to check if this is
false, you can actually

07:14:24.661 --> 07:14:27.661
use an exclamation point,
known as a bang in programming,

07:14:27.661 --> 07:14:29.321
that inverts the meaning.

07:14:29.321 --> 07:14:32.524
So false becomes true,
true becomes false.

07:14:32.524 --> 07:14:34.441
So this would be another
way of expressing it.

07:14:34.441 --> 07:14:39.301
This is arguably a worse design, though,
because the documentation explicitly

07:14:39.301 --> 07:14:43.021
says you should be checking
for 0 or a positive value

07:14:43.021 --> 07:14:46.111
or a negative value, and this
little trick, while correct,

07:14:46.111 --> 07:14:49.861
and I think you can make a reasonable
case for it, sort of hides that detail.

07:14:49.861 --> 07:14:51.781
And I would argue instead
for the first way,

07:14:51.781 --> 07:14:53.968
checking for equals equals 0 instead.

07:14:53.968 --> 07:14:55.801
And if that's a little
subtle, not to worry.

07:14:55.801 --> 07:15:00.601
We'll come back to little syntactic
tricks like that before long.

07:15:00.601 --> 07:15:05.131
Other questions on linear
search in these two forms.

07:15:05.131 --> 07:15:06.811
Is there another hand or hands?

07:15:06.811 --> 07:15:08.431
Two hands?

07:15:08.431 --> 07:15:08.971
No?

07:15:08.971 --> 07:15:10.261
OK, just holler if I missed.

07:15:10.261 --> 07:15:12.373
So let's now actually take
this one step further.

07:15:12.373 --> 07:15:15.331
Suppose that we want to write a
