Let me include cs50.h.

07:15:52.741 --> 07:15:58.681
Let me include standardio.h,
and let me include string.h.

07:15:58.681 --> 07:16:01.741
I'm going to again do int main void.

07:16:01.741 --> 07:16:05.071
And then inside of my program, I'm
going to give myself two arrays--

07:16:05.071 --> 07:16:06.871
the efficient way this time.

07:16:06.871 --> 07:16:09.361
String names will be
just two of us this time.

07:16:09.361 --> 07:16:12.751
How about Carter and me?

07:16:12.751 --> 07:16:15.241
And then I'll give myself--
oops, typo already.

07:16:15.241 --> 07:16:18.151
If I want this to be an array, I
don't have to specify the number.

07:16:18.151 --> 07:16:19.741
The compiler can count for me.

07:16:19.741 --> 07:16:21.661
But I do need the square brackets.

07:16:21.661 --> 07:16:28.111
Then for numbers, I'm again going to
use a string array specifying with

07:16:28.111 --> 07:16:33.871
the curly braces that how about
Carter can be at 1-617-495-1000.

07:16:33.871 --> 07:16:35.641
And how about my own number here--

07:16:35.641 --> 07:16:39.361
1-949-468-- oh pattern appearing--

07:16:39.361 --> 07:16:42.121
2750 will be mine.

07:16:42.121 --> 07:16:42.961
Why mine?

07:16:42.961 --> 07:16:44.891
Well, I'm just kind of lined things up.

07:16:44.891 --> 07:16:47.821
So Carter's number is
apparently first in this array,

07:16:47.821 --> 07:16:51.161
and I'm claiming that he'll be
first in this array, respectively.

07:16:51.161 --> 07:16:53.971
I, David, will be the first--
the second in the names array

07:16:53.971 --> 07:16:56.628
and second in the numbers array.

07:16:56.628 --> 07:16:59.461
If you want to have a little fun
with programming, feel free to text

07:16:59.461 --> 07:17:01.631
or call me some time at that number.

07:17:01.631 --> 07:17:05.311
So now let's actually use
this data in some way.

07:17:05.311 --> 07:17:08.729
Let's go ahead and actually search
for my own name and number here.

07:17:08.729 --> 07:17:09.271
So let me do.

07:17:09.271 --> 07:17:11.851
For int i, get 0.

07:17:11.851 --> 07:17:16.451
There's two of us this time-- so i less
than 2 and then i plus plus as before.

07:17:16.451 --> 07:17:18.841
And now I'm going to practice
what I preached earlier,

07:17:18.841 --> 07:17:22.801
and I'm going to use str compare
to find my name in this case.

07:17:22.801 --> 07:17:29.461
And I'm going to say if strcmp of names
bracket i equals quote unquote David

07:17:29.461 --> 07:17:33.101
and that equals 0,
meaning they're the same,

07:17:33.101 --> 07:17:35.971
then just as before, I'm going to
go ahead and print something out.

07:17:35.971 --> 07:17:37.681
But this time, I'm going to
make the program more useful

07:17:37.681 --> 07:17:39.461
and not just say found or not found.

07:17:39.461 --> 07:17:43.411
Now I'm implementing a phone book, like
the contacts app on iOS or Android.

07:17:43.411 --> 07:17:46.741
So I'm going to say something
like, quote unquote, found percent

07:17:46.741 --> 07:17:53.191
s backslash n and then actually
plug in numbers bracket i

07:17:53.191 --> 07:17:56.731
to correspond to the
current name bracket i.

07:17:56.731 --> 07:17:58.586
And then I'll return 0 as before.

07:17:58.586 --> 07:18:00.961
And then down here if we get
all the way through the loop

07:18:00.961 --> 07:18:04.481
and David's not there for some reason,
I'm going to print as before not found

07:18:04.481 --> 07:18:05.941
and then return 1.

07:18:05.941 --> 07:18:10.951
So let me go ahead and compile this
with make phone dot slash phonebook,

07:18:10.951 --> 07:18:13.601
and it seems to have found the number.

07:18:13.601 --> 07:18:17.491
So this code I'm going
to claim is correct.

07:18:17.491 --> 07:18:20.551
It's kind of stupid because I've
just made a phone book or a contacts

07:18:20.551 --> 07:18:22.051
app that only supports two people.

07:18:22.051 --> 07:18:23.864
They're only going to be me and Carter.

07:18:23.864 --> 07:18:26.281
This would be like downloading
the contacts app on a phone

07:18:26.281 --> 07:18:28.198
and you can only call
two people in the world.

07:18:28.198 --> 07:18:30.181
There's no ability to
add names or edit things.

07:18:30.181 --> 07:18:33.221
That, of course, could come later
using get string or something else.

07:18:33.221 --> 07:18:35.054
But for now for the
sake of discussion, I've

07:18:35.054 --> 07:18:37.811
just hardcoded two
names and two numbers.

07:18:37.811 --> 07:18:40.651
But for what it does, I
claim this is correct.

07:18:40.651 --> 07:18:43.951
It's going to find me
and print out my number.

07:18:43.951 --> 07:18:45.841
But is it well-designed?

07:18:45.841 --> 07:18:49.921
Let's start to now consider if
we're not just using arrays,

07:18:49.921 --> 07:18:52.213
but are we using them, well?

07:18:52.213 --> 07:18:55.171
We started to use them last week,
but are we using them well this week?

07:18:55.171 --> 07:18:59.041
And what might I even mean by
using an array well or designing

07:18:59.041 --> 07:19:01.001
this program well?

07:19:01.001 --> 07:19:06.301
Any critiques or concerns
with why this might not

07:19:06.301 --> 07:19:08.461
be the best road for us
to be going down when

07:19:08.461 --> 07:19:12.901
I want to implement something like a
phone book with pieces of information?

07:19:12.901 --> 07:19:15.901
It seems all too vulnerable
to just mistakes.

07:19:15.901 --> 07:19:19.981
For instance, if I screw up the actual
number of names in the names array

07:19:19.981 --> 07:19:24.551
such that it's now more or less than
is in the numbers array or vise versa,

07:19:24.551 --> 07:19:27.781
it feels like there's not a tight
relationship between those pieces

07:19:27.781 --> 07:19:31.501
of data, and it's just sort of
is trusting on the honor system

07:19:31.501 --> 07:19:37.801
that any time I use names bracket i
that it lines up with numbers bracket i.

07:19:37.801 --> 07:19:38.521
And that's fine.

07:19:38.521 --> 07:19:40.441
If you're the one writing
the code, you're probably

07:19:40.441 --> 07:19:42.001
not going to really screw this up.

07:19:42.001 --> 07:19:44.626
But if you start collaborating
with someone else or the program

07:19:44.626 --> 07:19:48.061
is getting much, much longer, the
odds that you or your colleagues

07:19:48.061 --> 07:19:52.471
remember that you're sort of just
trusting that names and numbers line up

07:19:52.471 --> 07:19:54.781
like this is going to fail eventually.

07:19:54.781 --> 07:19:57.901
Someone's not going to realize that,
and just, the code is going to break.

07:19:57.901 --> 07:20:00.901
And you're going to start out putting
the wrong numbers for names, which

07:20:00.901 --> 07:20:05.071
is to say it'd be much nicer if
we could somehow couple these two

07:20:05.071 --> 07:20:09.211
pieces of data, names and numbers,
a little more tightly together so

07:20:09.211 --> 07:20:13.261
that you're not just trusting that
these two independent variables, names

07:20:13.261 --> 07:20:16.991
and numbers, have this kind of
relationship with themselves.

07:20:16.991 --> 07:20:19.561
So let's consider how
we might solve this.

07:20:19.561 --> 07:20:23.761
A new feature today that we'll introduce
is generally known as a data structure.

07:20:23.761 --> 07:20:27.641
In C, we have the ability to
invent our own data types,

07:20:27.641 --> 07:20:30.916
if you will-- data types
that the authors of C decades

07:20:30.916 --> 07:20:32.791
ago just didn't envision
or just didn't think

07:20:32.791 --> 07:20:36.241
were necessary because we can implement
them ourselves-- similar to Scratch

07:20:36.241 --> 07:20:38.641
just as you could create
custom puzzle pieces,

07:20:38.641 --> 07:20:40.721
or in C, you can create
custom functions.

07:20:40.721 --> 07:20:45.121
So in C, can you create
your own types of data

07:20:45.121 --> 07:20:49.261
that go beyond the built in ints
and floats and even strings?

07:20:49.261 --> 07:20:54.901
You can make, for instance, a
person data type or a candidate data

07:20:54.901 --> 07:20:57.451
type in the context of
elections or a person data type

07:20:57.451 --> 07:21:00.311
more generically that might
have a name and a number.

07:21:00.311 --> 07:21:02.081
So how might we do this?

07:21:02.081 --> 07:21:07.831
Well, let me go here and propose
that if we want to define a person,

07:21:07.831 --> 07:21:11.281
wouldn't it be nice if we
could have a person data type,

07:21:11.281 --> 07:21:13.831
and then we could have
an array called people?

07:21:13.831 --> 07:21:17.131
And maybe that array is our
only array with two things

07:21:17.131 --> 07:21:19.561
in it, two persons in it.

07:21:19.561 --> 07:21:22.501
But somehow, those data
types, these persons,

07:21:22.501 --> 07:21:25.519
would have both a name and a
number associated with them.

07:21:25.519 --> 07:21:27.061
So we don't need two separate arrays.

07:21:27.061 --> 07:21:31.601
We need one array of persons,
a brand new data type.

07:21:31.601 --> 07:21:33.251
So how might we do this?

07:21:33.251 --> 07:21:35.431
Well, if we want every
person in the world

07:21:35.431 --> 07:21:37.681
or in this program to
have a name and a number,

07:21:37.681 --> 07:21:40.741
we literally right out
first those two data types.

07:21:40.741 --> 07:21:42.221
Give me a string called name.

07:21:42.221 --> 07:21:45.211
Give me a string called
number semicolon, after each.

07:21:45.211 --> 07:21:48.391
And then we wrap that,
those two lines of code,

07:21:48.391 --> 07:21:51.091
with this syntax, which at first
glance is a little cryptic.

07:21:51.091 --> 07:21:52.771
It's a lot of words all of a sudden.

07:21:52.771 --> 07:21:57.091
But typedef is a new keyword today
that defines a new data type.

07:21:57.091 --> 07:22:00.871
This is the C key word that
lets you create your own data

07:22:00.871 --> 07:22:02.461
type for the very first time.

07:22:02.461 --> 07:22:07.201
Struct is another related key word that
tells the compiler that this isn't just

07:22:07.201 --> 07:22:11.671
a simple data type, like an int or a
float renamed or something like that.

07:22:11.671 --> 07:22:13.301
It actually is a structure.

07:22:13.301 --> 07:22:17.071
It's got some dimensions to it, like
two things in it or three things in it

07:22:17.071 --> 07:22:19.621
or even 50 things inside of it.

07:22:19.621 --> 07:22:23.671
The last word down here is the name
that you want to give your data type,

07:22:23.671 --> 07:22:26.341
and it weirdly goes
after the curly braces.

07:22:26.341 --> 07:22:30.121
But this is how you invent
a data type called person.

07:22:30.121 --> 07:22:33.031
And what this code is
implying is that henceforth,

07:22:33.031 --> 07:22:38.341
the compiler clang will know that a
person is composed of a name that's

07:22:38.341 --> 07:22:41.131
a string and a number that's a string.

07:22:41.131 --> 07:22:44.131
And you don't have to worry
about having multiple arrays now.

07:22:44.131 --> 07:22:48.311
You can just have an array
of people moving forward.

07:22:48.311 --> 07:22:50.279
So how can we go about using this?

07:22:50.279 --> 07:22:52.321
Well, let me go back to
my code from before where

07:22:52.321 --> 07:22:53.701
I was implementing a phone book.

07:22:53.701 --> 07:22:56.076
And why don't we enhance the
phone book code a little bit

07:22:56.076 --> 07:22:58.591
by borrowing some of that new syntax?

07:22:58.591 --> 07:23:01.081
Let me go to the top of
my program above main

07:23:01.081 --> 07:23:04.021
and define a type that's
a structure or a data

07:23:04.021 --> 07:23:08.861
structure that has a name inside of
it and that has a number inside of it.

07:23:08.861 --> 07:23:12.511
And the name of this new structure
again is going to be called person.

07:23:12.511 --> 07:23:17.911
Inside of my code now, let me go ahead
and delete this old stuff temporarily.

07:23:17.911 --> 07:23:21.871
Let me give myself an array
called people of size 2.

07:23:21.871 --> 07:23:25.358
And I'm going to use the
non-terse way to do this.

07:23:25.358 --> 07:23:26.941
I'm not going to use the curly braces.

07:23:26.941 --> 07:23:31.501
I'm going to more pedantic spell out
what I want in this array of size 2

07:23:31.501 --> 07:23:35.221
at location 0, which is the
first person in an array

07:23:35.221 --> 07:23:37.051
because you always start counting at 0.

07:23:37.051 --> 07:23:40.861
I'm going to give that person
a name of quote unquote Carter.

07:23:40.861 --> 07:23:44.341
And the dot is admittedly one
new piece of syntax today too.

07:23:44.341 --> 07:23:46.771
The dot means go inside
of that structure

07:23:46.771 --> 07:23:50.551
and access the variable called
name and give it this value Carter.

07:23:50.551 --> 07:23:52.711
Similarly, if I'm going
to give Carter a number,

07:23:52.711 --> 07:23:57.391
I can go into people bracket 0 dot
number and give that the same thing

07:23:57.391 --> 07:24:02.311
as before plus 1-617-495-1000.

07:24:02.311 --> 07:24:04.591
And then I can do the
same for myself here--

07:24:04.591 --> 07:24:08.506
people bracket-- where should I go?

07:24:08.506 --> 07:24:10.468
OK, one because again, two elements.

07:24:10.468 --> 07:24:11.801
But we started counting at zero.

07:24:11.801 --> 07:24:13.781
Bracket name equals quote unquote David.

07:24:13.781 --> 07:24:18.701
And then lastly, people bracket 1
dot number equals quote unquote plus

07:24:18.701 --> 07:24:24.731
1-949-468-2750.

07:24:24.731 --> 07:24:27.611
So now if I scroll
down here to my logic,

07:24:27.611 --> 07:24:30.491
I don't think this part
needs to change too much.

07:24:30.491 --> 07:24:35.041
I'm still, for the sake of discussion,
going to iterate 2 times from i

07:24:35.041 --> 07:24:37.991
is 0 on up to but not through 2.

07:24:37.991 --> 07:24:41.031
But I think this line
of code needs to change.

07:24:41.031 --> 07:24:49.271
How should I now refer to the
i-th person's name as I iterate?

07:24:49.271 --> 07:24:52.611
What should I compare quote
unquote David to this time?

07:24:52.611 --> 07:24:53.111
Let me see.

07:24:53.111 --> 07:24:54.411
On the end here?

07:24:54.411 --> 07:24:57.439
AUDIENCE: People bracket i dot name.

07:24:57.439 --> 07:24:59.231
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, people
bracket i dot name.

07:24:59.231 --> 07:24:59.531
Why?

07:24:59.531 --> 07:25:01.198
Because people is the name of the array.

07:25:01.198 --> 07:25:04.841
Bracket i is the i-th person that we're
iterating over in the current loop--

07:25:04.841 --> 07:25:07.601
first zero, then one, maybe
higher if it had more people.

07:25:07.601 --> 07:25:10.931
Then dot is our new syntax for
going inside of a data structure

07:25:10.931 --> 07:25:14.231
and accessing a variable therein
which in this case is name.

07:25:14.231 --> 07:25:16.641
And so I can compare
David just as before.

07:25:16.641 --> 07:25:21.251
So it's a little more verbose, but
now arguably this is a better program

07:25:21.251 --> 07:25:26.831
because now these people are full
fledged data types unto themselves.

07:25:26.831 --> 07:25:29.171
There's no more honor
system inside of my loop

07:25:29.171 --> 07:25:31.463
that this is going to line
up because in just a moment,

07:25:31.463 --> 07:25:34.228
I'm going to fix this one last
remnant of the previous version.

07:25:34.228 --> 07:25:36.311
And if I can call back on
you again, what should I

07:25:36.311 --> 07:25:39.191
change numbers bracket i to this time?

07:25:39.191 --> 07:25:45.121
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] dot number.

07:25:45.121 --> 07:25:46.751
DAVID J. MALAN: Dot number, exactly.

07:25:46.751 --> 07:25:49.301
So gone is the honor
system that just assumes

07:25:49.301 --> 07:25:52.421
that bracket i in this array lines up
with bracket i in this other array.

07:25:52.421 --> 07:25:53.171
Now why?

07:25:53.171 --> 07:25:54.791
There's only one array.

07:25:54.791 --> 07:25:56.351
It's an array called people.

07:25:56.351 --> 07:25:58.421
The things it stores are persons.

07:25:58.421 --> 07:26:00.161
A person has a name and a number.

07:26:00.161 --> 07:26:02.578
And so even though it's kind
of marginal admittedly given

07:26:02.578 --> 07:26:05.411
that this is a short program and
given that this kind of made things

07:26:05.411 --> 07:26:07.661
look more complicated
at first glance, we're

07:26:07.661 --> 07:26:10.901
now laying the foundation for just
a better design because you really

07:26:10.901 --> 07:26:13.541
can't screw up now the
association of names

07:26:13.541 --> 07:26:17.141
with numbers because every person's
name and number is, so to speak,

07:26:17.141 --> 07:26:21.071
encapsulated inside
of the same data type.

07:26:21.071 --> 07:26:22.631
And that's a term of art in CS.

07:26:22.631 --> 07:26:26.081
Encapsulation means to
encapsulate-- that is, contain--

07:26:26.081 --> 07:26:28.191
related pieces of information.

07:26:28.191 --> 07:26:34.031
And thus, we have a person that
encapsulates two other data types, name

07:26:34.031 --> 07:26:34.811
and number.

07:26:34.811 --> 07:26:36.671
And this just sets
the foundation for all

07:26:36.671 --> 07:26:39.551
of the cool stuff we've talked
about and you use every day.

07:26:39.551 --> 07:26:40.354
What is an image?

07:26:40.354 --> 07:26:43.271
Well, recall that an image is a bunch
of pixels or dots on the screen.

07:26:43.271 --> 07:26:46.931
Every one of those dots
has RGB values associated

07:26:46.931 --> 07:26:48.791
with it-- red, green, and blue.

07:26:48.791 --> 07:26:52.121
You could imagine now creating
a structure in C probably where

07:26:52.121 --> 07:26:55.901
maybe you have three values,
three variables-- one called red,

07:26:55.901 --> 07:26:57.761
one called green, one called blue.

07:26:57.761 --> 07:27:00.341
And then you could name the
thing not person but pixel.

07:27:00.341 --> 07:27:04.271
And now you could store in C three
different colors-- some amount of red,

07:27:04.271 --> 07:27:08.339
some green, some blue-- and collectively
treat it as the color of a pixel.

07:27:08.339 --> 07:27:11.381
And you could imagine doing something
similar perhaps for video or music.

07:27:11.381 --> 07:27:14.801
Music, you might have three
variables-- one for the musical note,

07:27:14.801 --> 07:27:17.021
the duration, the loudness of it.

07:27:17.021 --> 07:27:20.481
And you can imagine coming up with
your own data type for music as well.

07:27:20.481 --> 07:27:21.731
So this is a little low level.

07:27:21.731 --> 07:27:24.281
We're just using like a
familiar contacts application.

07:27:24.281 --> 07:27:28.631
But we now have the way in code
to express most any type of data

07:27:28.631 --> 07:27:32.621
that we might want to implement
or discuss ultimately.

07:27:32.621 --> 07:27:37.871
So any questions now on struct
or defining our own types,

07:27:37.871 --> 07:27:42.471
the purposes for which are to use
arrays but use them more responsibly

07:27:42.471 --> 07:27:45.641
now in a better design but
also to lay the foundation

07:27:45.641 --> 07:27:50.241
for implementing cooler and cooler
stuff per our week zero discussion?

07:27:50.241 --> 07:27:50.741
Yeah.

07:27:50.741 --> 07:27:52.074
AUDIENCE: What's the [INAUDIBLE]

07:27:52.074 --> 07:27:55.074
DAVID J. MALAN: What's the difference
between this and an object in an object

07:27:55.074 --> 07:27:55.911
oriented language?

07:27:55.911 --> 07:27:58.751
So slight side note, C
is not object-oriented.

07:27:58.751 --> 07:28:02.351
Languages like Java and C++ and
others which you might have heard

07:28:02.351 --> 07:28:05.561
of, programmed yourself, had friends
program in, are object oriented

07:28:05.561 --> 07:28:09.791
languages in those languages they have
things called classes or objects which

07:28:09.791 --> 07:28:10.811
are interrelated.

07:28:10.811 --> 07:28:14.381
And objects can store not
just data, like variables.

07:28:14.381 --> 07:28:18.851
Objects can also store functions, and
you can kind of sort of do this in C.

07:28:18.851 --> 07:28:20.741
But it's not sort of conventional.

07:28:20.741 --> 07:28:24.131
In C, you have data
structures that store data.

07:28:24.131 --> 07:28:29.141
In languages like Java and C+, you have
objects that store data and functions

07:28:29.141 --> 07:28:29.771
together.

07:28:29.771 --> 07:28:32.151
Python is an object-oriented
language as well.

07:28:32.151 --> 07:28:35.631
So we'll see this issue in a few weeks,
but let me wave my hands at it for now.

07:28:35.631 --> 07:28:36.131
Yeah.

07:28:36.131 --> 07:28:38.116
AUDIENCE: Could you
use this [INAUDIBLE]??

07:28:38.116 --> 07:28:38.741
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes.

07:28:38.741 --> 07:28:41.381
Could you use this struct to
redefine how an int is defined?

07:28:41.381 --> 07:28:42.611
Short answer, yes.

07:28:42.611 --> 07:28:46.061
We talked a couple of times
now about integer overflow.

07:28:46.061 --> 07:28:50.261
And most recently, you might have seen
me mention the bug in iOS and Mac OS

07:28:50.261 --> 07:28:52.841
that was literally related
to an int overflow.

07:28:52.841 --> 07:28:57.251
That's the result of ints only
storing 4 bytes or 32 bits

07:28:57.251 --> 07:29:00.161
or even as long as 64 bits or 8 bytes.

07:29:00.161 --> 07:29:01.241
But it's finite.

07:29:01.241 --> 07:29:03.881
But if you want to implement
some financial software

07:29:03.881 --> 07:29:06.461
or some scientific or
mathematical software that

07:29:06.461 --> 07:29:10.331
allows you to count way bigger
than a typical int or a long,

07:29:10.331 --> 07:29:13.496
you could imagine John coming
up with your own structure.

07:29:13.496 --> 07:29:15.621
And in fact, in some
languages there is a structure

07:29:15.621 --> 07:29:19.731
called big int, which allows you
to express even bigger numbers.

07:29:19.731 --> 07:29:20.331
How?

07:29:20.331 --> 07:29:24.771
Well, maybe you store inside of
a big ant an array of values.

07:29:24.771 --> 07:29:27.853
And you somehow allow yourself
to store more and more bits

07:29:27.853 --> 07:29:29.811
based on how high you
want to be able to count.

07:29:29.811 --> 07:29:30.831
So in short, yes.

07:29:30.831 --> 07:29:34.201
We now have the ability now to do
most anything we want in the language

07:29:34.201 --> 07:29:36.561
even if it's not built in for us.

07:29:36.561 --> 07:29:37.461
Other questions.

07:29:37.461 --> 07:29:43.123
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:29:43.123 --> 07:29:45.831
DAVID J. MALAN: Could you define a name
and a number in the same line?

07:29:45.831 --> 07:29:46.430
Sort of.

07:29:46.430 --> 07:29:48.347
It starts to get
syntactically a little messy,

07:29:48.347 --> 07:29:51.590
so I did it a little more
pedantic line by line.

07:29:51.590 --> 07:29:52.231
Good question.

07:29:52.231 --> 07:29:52.791
Over here.

07:29:52.791 --> 07:29:57.271
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] function
you use for the function

07:29:57.271 --> 07:29:59.701
at the bottom of the [INAUDIBLE].

07:29:59.701 --> 07:30:03.390
Could you do something
like that [INAUDIBLE]??

07:30:03.390 --> 07:30:05.760
DAVID J. MALAN: Prototypes--
you have to do A and C. You

07:30:05.760 --> 07:30:09.390
have to define anything you're going
to use or declare anything you're going

07:30:09.390 --> 07:30:11.140
to use before you actually use it.

07:30:11.140 --> 07:30:15.191
So it is deliberate that I put it
at the top of my code in this file.

07:30:15.191 --> 07:30:19.231
Otherwise, the compiler would not know
what I mean by person when I first

07:30:19.231 --> 07:30:22.111
use it here on what's line 14.

07:30:22.111 --> 07:30:25.840
So it has to come first, or it has to
be put into something like a header file

07:30:25.840 --> 07:30:29.340
so that you include it at
the very top of your code.

07:30:29.340 --> 07:30:31.140
Other questions over here.

07:30:31.140 --> 07:30:32.023
Yeah.

07:30:32.023 --> 07:30:37.643
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:30:37.643 --> 07:30:39.351
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, good
question, and we'll

07:30:39.351 --> 07:30:42.861
come back to this later in the term when
we talk about SQL, a database language,

07:30:42.861 --> 07:30:44.991
and storing things in actual databases.

07:30:44.991 --> 07:30:48.681
Generally speaking, even though we
humans call things phone numbers,

07:30:48.681 --> 07:30:52.161
or in the US, we have social security
numbers, those types of numbers

07:30:52.161 --> 07:30:56.421
often have other punctuation in it,
like dashes, parentheses, pluses,

07:30:56.421 --> 07:30:57.741
and so forth.

07:30:57.741 --> 07:31:01.701
You could not store any of that syntax
or that punctuation inside of an int.

07:31:01.701 --> 07:31:03.241
You could only store numbers.

07:31:03.241 --> 07:31:05.301
So one motivation for
using a string is just

07:31:05.301 --> 07:31:08.931
I can store whatever the human wanted
me to store, including parentheses

07:31:08.931 --> 07:31:10.051
and so forth.

07:31:10.051 --> 07:31:13.789
Another reason for
storing things as strings,

07:31:13.789 --> 07:31:15.831
even if they look like
numbers, is in the context

07:31:15.831 --> 07:31:17.449
of zip codes in the United States.

07:31:17.449 --> 07:31:18.741
Again, we'll come back to this.

07:31:18.741 --> 07:31:21.051
But long story short--
years ago, actually--

07:31:21.051 --> 07:31:24.093
I was using Microsoft
Outlook for my email client.

07:31:24.093 --> 07:31:25.551
And eventually I switched to Gmail.

07:31:25.551 --> 07:31:27.261
And this is like 10 plus years ago now.

07:31:27.261 --> 07:31:31.791
And Outlook at the time lets you export
all of your contacts as a CSV file--

07:31:31.791 --> 07:31:33.141
Comma Separated Values.

07:31:33.141 --> 07:31:34.971
More on that in the weeks to come too.

07:31:34.971 --> 07:31:36.763
And that just means I
could download a text

07:31:36.763 --> 07:31:40.071
file with all of my friends and
family and their numbers inside of it.

07:31:40.071 --> 07:31:44.173
Unfortunately, I open that same CSV
file with Excel, I think, at the time

07:31:44.173 --> 07:31:46.131
just to kind of spot
check it and see if what's

07:31:46.131 --> 07:31:47.761
in there was what it was expected.

07:31:47.761 --> 07:31:51.231
And I must have instinctively hit,
like, Command or Control-S to save it.

07:31:51.231 --> 07:31:54.261
And Excel at least has this habit
of sort of reformatting your data.

07:31:54.261 --> 07:31:56.991
If things look like numbers,
it treats them as numbers.

07:31:56.991 --> 07:31:58.401
And Apple Numbers does this too.

07:31:58.401 --> 07:32:00.441
Google Spreadsheets
does this to nowadays.

07:32:00.441 --> 07:32:07.401
But long story short, I then imported
my mildly saved CSV file into Gmail.

07:32:07.401 --> 07:32:11.121
And now 10 plus years later, I'm still
occasionally finding friends and family

07:32:11.121 --> 07:32:17.001
members whose zip codes are in
Cambridge, Massachusetts 2138,

07:32:17.001 --> 07:32:20.661
which is missing the 0 because
we here in Cambridge are 02138.

07:32:20.661 --> 07:32:23.781
And that's because I
treated or I let Excel

07:32:23.781 --> 07:32:26.991
treat what looks like a number
as an actual number or int,

07:32:26.991 --> 07:32:29.901
and now leading zeros become a
problem because mathematically, they

07:32:29.901 --> 07:32:33.261
mean nothing, but in the
mail system, they do--

07:32:33.261 --> 07:32:34.431
sending envelopes and such.

07:32:34.431 --> 07:32:36.014
All right, other final questions here.

07:32:36.014 --> 07:32:39.021
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:32:39.021 --> 07:32:42.861
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so could I have
used a 2D or two dimensional

07:32:42.861 --> 07:32:47.091
array to solve the problem
earlier of having just one array?

07:32:47.091 --> 07:32:51.111
Yes, but one, I would argue
it's less readable, especially

07:32:51.111 --> 07:32:53.001
as I get lots of names and numbers.

07:32:53.001 --> 07:32:56.091
And two, that too is also kind
of relying on the honor system.

07:32:56.091 --> 07:32:59.301
It would be all too easy to omit some
of the square brackets in the two

07:32:59.301 --> 07:33:00.301
dimensional array.

07:33:00.301 --> 07:33:04.371
So I would argue it too is not
as good as introducing a struct.

07:33:04.371 --> 07:33:05.541
More on that down the road.

07:33:05.541 --> 07:33:10.431
Two dimensional arrays just means
arrays of arrays, as you might infer.

07:33:10.431 --> 07:33:12.441
All right, so now that
we have this ability

07:33:12.441 --> 07:33:16.571
to store different types of data
like contacts in a phone book,

07:33:16.571 --> 07:33:18.321
having names and
addresses, let's actually

07:33:18.321 --> 07:33:21.141
take a step back and
consider how we might now

07:33:21.141 --> 07:33:26.211
solve one of the original problems by
actually sorting the information we're

07:33:26.211 --> 07:33:30.291
given in advance and considering,
per our discussion earlier, just how

07:33:30.291 --> 07:33:33.261
costly, how time consuming is
that because that might tip

07:33:33.261 --> 07:33:37.371
the scales in favor of sorting,
then searching, or maybe just

07:33:37.371 --> 07:33:39.321
not sorting and only searching.

07:33:39.321 --> 07:33:42.831
It'll give us a sense of just
how expensive, so to speak,

07:33:42.831 --> 07:33:44.706
sorting something actually is.

07:33:44.706 --> 07:33:46.581
Well, what's the
formulation of this problem?

07:33:46.581 --> 07:33:48.141
It's the same thing as week zero.

07:33:48.141 --> 07:33:49.311
We've got input to sort.

07:33:49.311 --> 07:33:51.481
We want it to be output as sorted.

07:33:51.481 --> 07:33:54.921
So for instance, if we're
taking unsorted input as input,

07:33:54.921 --> 07:33:58.256
we want the sorted output as
the result. More concretely,

07:33:58.256 --> 07:33:59.631
if we've got numbers like these--

07:33:59.631 --> 07:34:04.461
63852741, which are just
randomly arranged numbers--

07:34:04.461 --> 07:34:08.871
we want to get back out 12345678.

07:34:08.871 --> 07:34:10.801
So we just want those
things to be sorted.

07:34:10.801 --> 07:34:12.831
So again, inside of
the black box here is

07:34:12.831 --> 07:34:17.821
going to be one or more algorithms
that actually gets this job done.

07:34:17.821 --> 07:34:20.041
So how might we go about doing this?

07:34:20.041 --> 07:34:23.541
Well, just to vary things a bit
more, I think we have a chance here

07:34:23.541 --> 07:34:25.941
