07:38:11.291 --> 07:38:13.791
perhaps organically, how you
eight would solve this problem.

07:38:13.791 --> 07:38:16.901
So we currently have the numbers
0 through 7 quite out of order.

07:38:16.901 --> 07:38:20.869
Could you go ahead and just
yourselves from 0 through 7?

07:38:20.869 --> 07:38:21.702
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

07:38:25.761 --> 07:38:28.561
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, so what did they just do?

07:38:28.561 --> 07:38:29.061
OK, yes.

07:38:29.061 --> 07:38:30.621
First of all, yes, very well done.

07:38:34.391 --> 07:38:37.398
How would you describe
what they just did?

07:38:37.398 --> 07:38:38.231
Well, let's do this.

07:38:38.231 --> 07:38:40.421
Could you go back into
that order on the screen--

07:38:40.421 --> 07:38:44.681
52741630?

07:38:44.681 --> 07:38:47.801
And could you do exactly
what you just did again?

07:38:47.801 --> 07:38:49.111
Sort yourselves.

07:38:52.401 --> 07:38:53.391
All right, what did--

07:38:53.391 --> 07:38:53.991
OK, yes.

07:38:53.991 --> 07:38:54.831
Well done again.

07:38:58.551 --> 07:39:01.841
All right, so admittedly, there's kind
of a lot going on because each of you,

07:39:01.841 --> 07:39:05.511
except number four, are doing something
in parallel all at the same time.

07:39:05.511 --> 07:39:07.751
And that's not really how
a computer typically works.

07:39:07.751 --> 07:39:11.261
Just like a computer can only look at
one memory location, at one locker,

07:39:11.261 --> 07:39:15.851
at a time, so can a computer only move
one number at a time-- sort of opening

07:39:15.851 --> 07:39:18.078
a locker, checking what's
there, moving it as needed.

07:39:18.078 --> 07:39:21.161
So let's try this more methodically
based on the two audience suggestions.

07:39:21.161 --> 07:39:26.711
If you all could randomize
yourself again to 52741630,

07:39:26.711 --> 07:39:29.123
let's take the second of
those approaches first.

07:39:29.123 --> 07:39:30.581
I'm going to look at these numbers.

07:39:30.581 --> 07:39:33.231
And even though I as the human
can obviously see all the numbers

07:39:33.231 --> 07:39:35.291
and I just kind of have the
intuition for how to fix this,

07:39:35.291 --> 07:39:37.541
we got to be more methodical
because eventually, we've

07:39:37.541 --> 07:39:39.861
got to translate this to
pseudo code and then code.

07:39:39.861 --> 07:39:40.751
So let me see.

07:39:40.751 --> 07:39:43.561
I'm going to search for, as you
proposed, the smallest number.

07:39:43.561 --> 07:39:45.311
And I'm going to start
from left to right.

07:39:45.311 --> 07:39:48.461
I could do it right to left, but left
to right just tends to be convention.

07:39:48.461 --> 07:39:51.441
All right, 5 at this moment is
the smallest number I've seen.

07:39:51.441 --> 07:39:54.179
So I'm going to remember that
in a variable, if you will.

07:39:54.179 --> 07:39:55.721
Now I'm going to take one more step--

07:39:55.721 --> 07:39:56.291
2.

07:39:56.291 --> 07:39:59.621
OK, 2 I'm going to compare to the
variable in mind, obviously smaller.

07:39:59.621 --> 07:40:03.521
I'm going to forget about 5 and only
now remember 2 as the now smallest

07:40:03.521 --> 07:40:04.181
elements.

07:40:04.181 --> 07:40:07.451
7, nope-- I'm going to ignore that
because it's not smaller than the 2

07:40:07.451 --> 07:40:08.171
I have in mind.

07:40:08.171 --> 07:40:11.531
4, 1-- OK, I'm going to
update the variable in mind

07:40:11.531 --> 07:40:12.791
because that's indeed smaller.

07:40:12.791 --> 07:40:15.491
Now obviously, we the humans
know that's getting pretty small.

07:40:15.491 --> 07:40:16.541
Maybe it's the end.

07:40:16.541 --> 07:40:19.991
I have to check all values to see
if there's something even smaller

07:40:19.991 --> 07:40:22.796
because 6 is not, 3 is not, but 0 is.

07:40:22.796 --> 07:40:23.921
And what's your name again?

07:40:23.921 --> 07:40:24.791
AUDIENCE: Celeste.

07:40:24.791 --> 07:40:25.631
DAVID J. MALAN: Celeste.

07:40:25.631 --> 07:40:32.321
Where should Celeste or number 0 go
according to this proposed algorithm?

07:40:32.321 --> 07:40:33.951
All right, I'm seeing a lot of this.

07:40:33.951 --> 07:40:37.181
So at the beginning of the array,
so before doing this for real,

07:40:37.181 --> 07:40:38.921
let's have you pop out in front.

07:40:38.921 --> 07:40:42.461
And could you all shift
and make room for Celeste?

07:40:42.461 --> 07:40:46.391
Is this a good idea to have all
of them move or equivalently

07:40:46.391 --> 07:40:48.911
move everything in the array
to make room for Celeste

07:40:48.911 --> 07:40:51.281
and number 0 over there?

07:40:51.281 --> 07:40:52.031
No, probably not.

07:40:52.031 --> 07:40:53.239
That felt like a lot of work.

07:40:53.239 --> 07:40:56.561
And even though it happened pretty
quickly, that's like seven steps

07:40:56.561 --> 07:40:58.401
to happen just to move her in place.

07:40:58.401 --> 07:41:00.941
So what would be marginally
smarter perhaps--

07:41:00.941 --> 07:41:03.321
a little more efficient, perhaps?

07:41:03.321 --> 07:41:03.821
What's that?

07:41:03.821 --> 07:41:04.271
AUDIENCE: Swapping.

07:41:04.271 --> 07:41:04.851
DAVID J. MALAN: Swapping.

07:41:04.851 --> 07:41:05.893
What do you mean by swap?

07:41:05.893 --> 07:41:07.414
AUDIENCE: Replacing swaps.

07:41:07.414 --> 07:41:08.831
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, replace two values.

07:41:08.831 --> 07:41:12.451
So if you want to go back to where
you were, one step Over, number 5,

07:41:12.451 --> 07:41:13.921
he's not in the right place.

07:41:13.921 --> 07:41:15.151
He's got to move eventually.

07:41:15.151 --> 07:41:15.859
So you know what?

07:41:15.859 --> 07:41:18.661
If that's where Celeste belongs,
why don't we just swap 5 and 0?

07:41:18.661 --> 07:41:21.286
So if you want to go ahead and
exchange places with each other.

07:41:21.286 --> 07:41:22.591
Notice what's just happened.

07:41:22.591 --> 07:41:25.781
The problem I'm trying to
solve has gotten smaller.

07:41:25.781 --> 07:41:28.381
Instead of being size
8, now it's size 7.

07:41:28.381 --> 07:41:31.411
Now granted, I moved 5 to
another wrong location.

07:41:31.411 --> 07:41:33.301
But if these numbers
started off randomly,

07:41:33.301 --> 07:41:37.151
it doesn't really matter where 5 goes
until we get him into the right place.

07:41:37.151 --> 07:41:38.401
So I think we've improved.

07:41:38.401 --> 07:41:41.791
And now if I go back, my loop
is sort of coming back around.

07:41:41.791 --> 07:41:46.051
I can ignore Celeste and make this
a seven step problem and not eight

07:41:46.051 --> 07:41:47.791
because I know she's in the right place.

07:41:47.791 --> 07:41:49.201
2 seems to be the smallest.

07:41:49.201 --> 07:41:50.011
I'll remember that.

07:41:50.011 --> 07:41:51.661
Not 7, not 4--

07:41:51.661 --> 07:41:53.431
1 seems to be the smallest.

07:41:53.431 --> 07:41:57.961
Now I know as a human this
should be my next smallest.

07:41:57.961 --> 07:42:02.461
But why, intuitively, should
I keep going, do you think?

07:42:02.461 --> 07:42:05.311
I can't sort of optimize as a
human and just say, number 1,

07:42:05.311 --> 07:42:07.121
let's get you into the right place.

07:42:07.121 --> 07:42:08.971
I still want to check the whole array.

07:42:08.971 --> 07:42:09.691
Why?

07:42:09.691 --> 07:42:10.231
Yeah.

07:42:10.231 --> 07:42:12.838
AUDIENCE: Perhaps there's another 1.

07:42:12.838 --> 07:42:14.671
DAVID J. MALAN: Maybe there's
another 1, and that

07:42:14.671 --> 07:42:16.391
could be another problem altogether.

07:42:16.391 --> 07:42:17.131
Other thoughts?

07:42:17.131 --> 07:42:17.631
Yeah.

07:42:17.631 --> 07:42:18.819
AUDIENCE: Could be another 0

07:42:18.819 --> 07:42:20.611
DAVID J. MALAN: There could
be another 0 indeed,

07:42:20.611 --> 07:42:22.921
but I did go through
the list once, right?

07:42:22.921 --> 07:42:24.331
And I kind of know there isn't.

07:42:24.331 --> 07:42:24.931
Your thoughts?

07:42:24.931 --> 07:42:27.661
AUDIENCE: You don't know that
every value is represented.

07:42:27.661 --> 07:42:31.748
So maybe there's a [INAUDIBLE] You
just don't know what kind of data

07:42:31.748 --> 07:42:32.581
you're working with.

07:42:32.581 --> 07:42:34.961
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, I don't
necessarily know what is there.

07:42:34.961 --> 07:42:39.301
And honestly, I only stipulated earlier
that I'm using one variable in my mind.

07:42:39.301 --> 07:42:42.541
I could use two and remember the
two smallest elements I've seen.

07:42:42.541 --> 07:42:44.491
I could use three variables, four.

07:42:44.491 --> 07:42:47.801
But then I'm going to start to use
a lot of space in addition to time.

07:42:47.801 --> 07:42:51.241
So if I've stipulated that I only have
one variable to solve this problem,

07:42:51.241 --> 07:42:53.551
I don't know anything
more about these elements

07:42:53.551 --> 07:42:55.759
because the only thing I'm
remembering at this moment

07:42:55.759 --> 07:42:57.851
is number 1 is the
smallest element I've seen.

07:42:57.851 --> 07:42:58.651
So I'm going to keep going.

07:42:58.651 --> 07:42:59.071
6?

07:42:59.071 --> 07:42:59.431
Nope.

07:42:59.431 --> 07:42:59.851
3?

07:42:59.851 --> 07:43:00.151
Nope.

07:43:00.151 --> 07:43:00.571
5?

07:43:00.571 --> 07:43:01.071
Nope.

07:43:01.071 --> 07:43:03.031
OK, I know that number
1, and your name was--

07:43:03.031 --> 07:43:03.739
AUDIENCE: Hannah.

07:43:03.739 --> 07:43:06.211
DAVID J. MALAN: --Hannah is
the next smallest element.

07:43:06.211 --> 07:43:08.701
I could have everyone move
over to make room, but nope.

07:43:08.701 --> 07:43:09.421
2?

07:43:09.421 --> 07:43:11.341
You know, even though you're
so close to where I want you,

07:43:11.341 --> 07:43:13.531
I'm just going to keep it
simple and swap you two.

07:43:13.531 --> 07:43:15.931
So granted, I've made the
problem a little worse.

07:43:15.931 --> 07:43:19.561
But on average, I could get
lucky too and just pop number 2

07:43:19.561 --> 07:43:20.641
into the right place.

07:43:20.641 --> 07:43:22.571
Now let me just accelerate this.

07:43:22.571 --> 07:43:26.911
I can now ignore Hannah and Celeste,
making the problem size 6 instead of 8.

07:43:26.911 --> 07:43:28.231
So it's getting smaller.

07:43:28.231 --> 07:43:29.551
7 is the smallest.

07:43:29.551 --> 07:43:30.811
Nope, now 4 is--

07:43:30.811 --> 07:43:32.281
2 is the smallest.

07:43:32.281 --> 07:43:34.831
Still 2, still 2, still 2.

07:43:34.831 --> 07:43:37.921
So let's go ahead and swap 2 and 7.

07:43:37.921 --> 07:43:40.591
And now I'll just kind of
orchestrate it verbally.

07:43:40.591 --> 07:43:42.311
4, you're about to have to do something.

07:43:42.311 --> 07:43:46.111
So we now have 4, 7, 6 3, 5.

07:43:46.111 --> 07:43:48.841
OK, 3-- could you swap with 4?

07:43:48.841 --> 07:43:52.036
All right, now we have 7, 6, 4, 5.

07:43:52.036 --> 07:43:54.781
OK, 4, could you swap with 7?

07:43:54.781 --> 07:43:57.541
Now we have 6, 7, 5.

07:43:57.541 --> 07:43:59.791
5, could you swap with 6?

07:43:59.791 --> 07:44:01.196
And now we have 7, 6.

07:44:01.196 --> 07:44:02.521
6, would you swap at 7?

07:44:02.521 --> 07:44:04.051
And now perhaps round of applause.

07:44:04.051 --> 07:44:05.341
They've sorted themselves.

07:44:05.341 --> 07:44:09.611
OK, hang on there one minute.

07:44:09.611 --> 07:44:11.491
So we'll do this one other approach.

07:44:11.491 --> 07:44:14.581
And my God, that felt so much
slower than the first approach,

07:44:14.581 --> 07:44:17.401
but that's, one, because I was
kind of providing a long voiceover.

07:44:17.401 --> 07:44:22.051
But two, we were doing one thing at a
time whereas the first time, you guys

07:44:22.051 --> 07:44:25.471
had the luxury of moving
like eight different CPUs--

07:44:25.471 --> 07:44:28.051
brains, if you will-- were all
operating at the same time.

07:44:28.051 --> 07:44:29.501
And computers like that exist.

07:44:29.501 --> 07:44:32.254
If you have a computer with
multiple cores, so to speak,

07:44:32.254 --> 07:44:34.171
that's like having a
computer that technically

07:44:34.171 --> 07:44:35.851
can do multiple things at once.

07:44:35.851 --> 07:44:38.831
But software typically, at least
as we've written it thus far,

07:44:38.831 --> 07:44:40.556
can only do one thing at a time.

07:44:40.556 --> 07:44:42.431
So in a bit, we'll add
up all of these steps.

07:44:42.431 --> 07:44:44.223
But for now, let's take
one other approach.

07:44:44.223 --> 07:44:46.501
If you all could reorder
yourselves like that--

07:44:46.501 --> 07:44:51.301
52741630-- let's take
the other approach that

07:44:51.301 --> 07:44:54.971
was recommended by just fixing small
problems and see where this gets us.

07:44:54.971 --> 07:44:57.091
So we're back in the original order.

07:44:57.091 --> 07:44:59.173
5 and 2 are clearly out of order.

07:44:59.173 --> 07:44:59.881
So you know what?

07:44:59.881 --> 07:45:01.711
Let's just bite this problem off now.

07:45:01.711 --> 07:45:03.451
5 and 2, could you swap?

07:45:03.451 --> 07:45:04.891
Now let me take a next step.

07:45:04.891 --> 07:45:06.691
5 and 7, I think you're OK.

07:45:06.691 --> 07:45:09.361
There's a gap, yes, but that
might not be a big deal.

07:45:09.361 --> 07:45:10.721
7 and 4-- problem.

07:45:10.721 --> 07:45:12.781
Let's have you swap.

07:45:12.781 --> 07:45:15.511
OK, 7 and 1, let's have you swap.

07:45:15.511 --> 07:45:18.481
7 and 6, let's have you swap.

07:45:18.481 --> 07:45:20.191
7 and 3, you swap.

07:45:20.191 --> 07:45:22.051
7 and 0, you swap.

07:45:22.051 --> 07:45:23.851
Now let me pause for just a moment.

07:45:23.851 --> 07:45:25.151
Still not sorted.

07:45:25.151 --> 07:45:26.831
So I'm clearly not done.

07:45:26.831 --> 07:45:29.701
But have I improved the problem?

07:45:29.701 --> 07:45:32.461
Right, I can't see-- like
before, I can't optimize like

07:45:32.461 --> 07:45:34.571
before because 0 is obviously not here.

07:45:34.571 --> 07:45:38.521
So unless they're still way back there,
so it's not like I've gone from 8 steps

07:45:38.521 --> 07:45:40.661
to 7 to 6 just yet.

07:45:40.661 --> 07:45:42.408
But have I made any improvements?

07:45:42.408 --> 07:45:42.991
AUDIENCE: Yes.

07:45:42.991 --> 07:45:43.616
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes.

07:45:43.616 --> 07:45:45.841
In what sense is this improved?

07:45:45.841 --> 07:45:50.411
What's a concrete thing you
could point to is better?

07:45:50.411 --> 07:45:50.911
Yeah.

07:45:50.911 --> 07:45:52.471
AUDIENCE: Sorted the highest number.

07:45:52.471 --> 07:45:55.051
DAVID J. MALAN: I've sorted the
highest number, which is indeed 7.

07:45:55.051 --> 07:45:59.761
And conversely, if you prefer, Celeste
is one step closer to the beginning.

07:45:59.761 --> 07:46:04.331
Now worst case, Celeste is going to
have to move one step on each iteration.

07:46:04.331 --> 07:46:06.901
So I might need to do this
thing like n total times

07:46:06.901 --> 07:46:08.576
to move her all the way over.

07:46:08.576 --> 07:46:09.701
But that might work out OK.

07:46:09.701 --> 07:46:10.871
Let me see.

07:46:10.871 --> 07:46:12.341
2 and 5, you're good.

07:46:12.341 --> 07:46:13.841
5 and 4, swap you.

07:46:13.841 --> 07:46:15.731
5 and 1, let's swap you.

07:46:15.731 --> 07:46:16.931
5 and 6, you're good.

07:46:16.931 --> 07:46:18.911
6 and 3, let's swap you.

07:46:18.911 --> 07:46:21.011
6 and 0, let's swap you.

07:46:21.011 --> 07:46:22.601
6 and 7, you're good.

07:46:22.601 --> 07:46:23.741
And I think now--

07:46:23.741 --> 07:46:25.871
notice that the high
values, as you noted,

07:46:25.871 --> 07:46:28.511
are sort of bubbling up, if you
will, to the end of the list.

07:46:28.511 --> 07:46:29.891
2 and 4, you're good.

07:46:29.891 --> 07:46:31.181
4 and 1, let's swap.

07:46:31.181 --> 07:46:32.441
4 and 5, good.

07:46:32.441 --> 07:46:33.791
5 and 3, swap.

07:46:33.791 --> 07:46:36.141
5 and 0, swap.

07:46:36.141 --> 07:46:37.731
5, 6, 7, of course, are good.

07:46:37.731 --> 07:46:40.536
So now you can sort of see
the problem resolving itself.

07:46:40.536 --> 07:46:42.161
And let's just do this part now faster.

07:46:42.161 --> 07:46:44.501
2 and 1, 2 and 4.

07:46:44.501 --> 07:46:48.521
OK, 4 and 3, 4 and 0.

07:46:48.521 --> 07:46:53.238
All right, now 1 and 2,
2, and 3, and 0, and good.

07:46:53.238 --> 07:46:54.821
So we do have some optimization there.

07:46:54.821 --> 07:46:57.221
We don't need to keep going
because those all are sorted.

07:46:57.221 --> 07:46:58.271
1 and 2, you're good.

07:46:58.271 --> 07:47:01.091
2 and 0, all right, done.

07:47:01.091 --> 07:47:04.691
1 and 0-- and big round
of applause in closing.

07:47:04.691 --> 07:47:09.251
OK, so thank you all.

07:47:09.251 --> 07:47:11.501
We need the puppets back,
but you can keep the shirts.

07:47:11.501 --> 07:47:13.201
Thank you for volunteering here.

07:47:13.201 --> 07:47:16.171
Feel free to make your
way exits left or right.

07:47:16.171 --> 07:47:18.121
And let's see if,
thanks to our volunteers

07:47:18.121 --> 07:47:24.361
here, we can't now formalize a little
bit what we did on both passes here.

07:47:24.361 --> 07:47:28.531
I claim that the first algorithm
our volunteers kindly acted out

07:47:28.531 --> 07:47:30.091
is what's called selection sort.

07:47:30.091 --> 07:47:35.341
And as the name implied, we selected
the smallest elements again and again

07:47:35.341 --> 07:47:37.891
and again, working our
way from left to right,

07:47:37.891 --> 07:47:42.461
putting Celeste into the right place,
and then continuing with everyone else.

07:47:42.461 --> 07:47:45.421
So selection sort, as
it's formally called,

07:47:45.421 --> 07:47:48.361
can be described, for instance,
with this pseudo code here--

07:47:48.361 --> 07:47:51.271
4i from 0 to n minus 1.

07:47:51.271 --> 07:47:52.711
And again, why this?

07:47:52.711 --> 07:47:54.781
This is just how talk about arrays.

07:47:54.781 --> 07:47:59.281
The left end is 0, the right end
is n minus 1 where in this case,

07:47:59.281 --> 07:48:01.081
n happened to be eight people.

07:48:01.081 --> 07:48:03.061
So that's 0 through 7.

07:48:03.061 --> 07:48:06.601
So for i from 0 to n
minus 1, what did I do?

07:48:06.601 --> 07:48:11.761
I found the smallest number between
numbers bracket i and numbers bracket

07:48:11.761 --> 07:48:13.171
n minus 1.

07:48:13.171 --> 07:48:15.391
It's a little cryptic at
first glance, but this

07:48:15.391 --> 07:48:18.871
is just a very pseudo
code-like way of saying

07:48:18.871 --> 07:48:22.321
find the smallest element
among all eight volunteers

07:48:22.321 --> 07:48:27.481
because if i starts at 0 and n minus
1 never changes because there's always

07:48:27.481 --> 07:48:31.591
8, 8 people, so 8 minus
1 is 7, this first

07:48:31.591 --> 07:48:34.681
says find the smallest number
between numbers bracket 0

07:48:34.681 --> 07:48:37.711
and numbers bracket 7, if you will.

07:48:37.711 --> 07:48:38.911
Then what do I do?

07:48:38.911 --> 07:48:42.091
Swap the smallest number
with numbers bracket i.

07:48:42.091 --> 07:48:45.571
So that's how we got Celeste from
over here all the way over there.

07:48:45.571 --> 07:48:47.701
We just swapped those two values.

07:48:47.701 --> 07:48:50.201
What then happens next
in this pseudo code?

07:48:50.201 --> 07:48:52.261
i, of course, goes from 0 to 1.

07:48:52.261 --> 07:48:54.421
And that's the technical
way of saying now

07:48:54.421 --> 07:48:58.171
find the smallest element among
the 7 remaining volunteers,

07:48:58.171 --> 07:49:01.931
ignoring Celeste this time because she
was already in the correct location.

07:49:01.931 --> 07:49:04.321
So the problem went
from size 8 to size 7.

07:49:04.321 --> 07:49:07.861
And if we repeat, size 6,
5, 4, 3, 2, 1, until boom,

07:49:07.861 --> 07:49:10.181
it's all done at the very end.

07:49:10.181 --> 07:49:13.561
So this is just one way of
expressing in pseudo code what

07:49:13.561 --> 07:49:17.401
we did a little more organically
and a formalization of what someone

07:49:17.401 --> 07:49:19.781
volunteered out in the audience.

07:49:19.781 --> 07:49:24.661
So if we consider, then, the
efficiency of this algorithm,

07:49:24.661 --> 07:49:27.091
maybe abstracting it away
now as a bunch of doors

07:49:27.091 --> 07:49:31.321
where the left most again is always
0, the right most is always n minus 1,

07:49:31.321 --> 07:49:34.711
or equivalently, the second to last
is n minus 2, the third to last

07:49:34.711 --> 07:49:38.911
is n minus 3 where n might
be 8 or anything else,

07:49:38.911 --> 07:49:43.981
how do we think about or quantify
the running time of selection sort?

07:49:43.981 --> 07:49:46.591
Big O of what?

07:49:46.591 --> 07:49:49.591
I mean, that was a lot
of steps to be adding up.

07:49:49.591 --> 07:49:53.491
It's probably more than n, right,
because I went through the list

07:49:53.491 --> 07:49:54.391
again and again.

07:49:54.391 --> 07:49:59.101
It was like n plus n
minus 1 plus n minus 2.

07:49:59.101 --> 07:50:01.441
Any instincts here?

07:50:01.441 --> 07:50:04.981
We got like the whole
team in the orchestra now.

07:50:04.981 --> 07:50:09.541
Let me propose we think about it this
way with just a bit of formula, say.

07:50:09.541 --> 07:50:13.711
So the first time, I had to
look at n different volunteers.

07:50:13.711 --> 07:50:17.491
n was 8 in this case, but generically,
I looked at all eight numbers

07:50:17.491 --> 07:50:19.591
in order to decide who was the smallest.

07:50:19.591 --> 07:50:21.631
And sure enough, Celeste
was at the very end.

07:50:21.631 --> 07:50:23.464
She happened to be all
the way to the right.

07:50:23.464 --> 07:50:27.871
But I only knew that once I looked
at all 8 or all n volunteers.

07:50:27.871 --> 07:50:30.241
So that took me n steps first.

07:50:30.241 --> 07:50:33.631
But once the list was swapped
into the right place, then

07:50:33.631 --> 07:50:37.651
my problem with size n minus 1,
and I had n minus 1 other people

07:50:37.651 --> 07:50:38.681
to look through.

07:50:38.681 --> 07:50:40.231
So that's n minus 1 steps.

07:50:40.231 --> 07:50:44.186
Then after that, it's n minus 2 plus
n minus 3 plus n minus 4 plus dot dot

07:50:44.186 --> 07:50:45.631
dot until I had one final step.

07:50:45.631 --> 07:50:48.821
And it's obvious that I only
have one human left to consider.

07:50:48.821 --> 07:50:51.541
So we might wave our hands at
this with a little ellipsis

07:50:51.541 --> 07:50:54.761
and just say dot dot dot
plus 1 for the final step.

07:50:54.761 --> 07:50:56.251
Now what does this actually equal?

07:50:56.251 --> 07:50:57.841
Well, this is where you
might think back on, like,

07:50:57.841 --> 07:50:59.761
your high school math
or physics textbook that

07:50:59.761 --> 07:51:03.001
has a little cheat sheet at the end
that shows these kinds of recurrences.

07:51:03.001 --> 07:51:05.851
That happens to work
out mathematically to be

07:51:05.851 --> 07:51:09.481
n times n plus 1 all divided by 2.

07:51:09.481 --> 07:51:13.051
That's just what that recurrence,
that series, actually adds up to.

07:51:13.051 --> 07:51:15.661
So if you take on faith that
that math is correct, let's

07:51:15.661 --> 07:51:19.891
just now multiply this
out mathematically.

07:51:19.891 --> 07:51:26.281
That's n squared plus n divided by 2 or
n squared divided by 2 plus n over 2.

07:51:26.281 --> 07:51:29.251
And here's where we're starting
to get annoyingly into the weeds.

07:51:29.251 --> 07:51:34.441
Like, honestly, as n gets really
large, like a million doors or integers

07:51:34.441 --> 07:51:39.301
or a billion web pages in Google search
engine, honestly, which of these terms

07:51:39.301 --> 07:51:41.761
is going to matter the
most mathematically

07:51:41.761 --> 07:51:43.561
if n is a really big number?

07:51:43.561 --> 07:51:46.201
Is n squared divided by
2 the dominant factor,

07:51:46.201 --> 07:51:48.621
or is n divided by 2
the dominant factor?

07:51:48.621 --> 07:51:50.356
AUDIENCE: n squared.

07:51:50.356 --> 07:51:51.481
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, n squared.

07:51:51.481 --> 07:51:53.651
I mean, no matter what n
is-- and the bigger it is,

07:51:53.651 --> 07:51:56.941
the bigger raising it to
the power 2 is going to be.

07:51:56.941 --> 07:51:57.691
So you know what?

07:51:57.691 --> 07:52:00.631
Let's just wave our hands at this
because at the end of the day,

07:52:00.631 --> 07:52:04.141
as n gets really large, the dominant
factor is indeed that first one.

07:52:04.141 --> 07:52:04.891
And you know what?

07:52:04.891 --> 07:52:08.651
Even the divided 2, as I claimed earlier
with our two phone book examples, where

07:52:08.651 --> 07:52:11.321
the two straight lines if you
keep zooming out essentially

07:52:11.321 --> 07:52:16.121
looked the same when n is large enough,
let's just call this on the order of n

07:52:16.121 --> 07:52:16.851
squared.

07:52:16.851 --> 07:52:21.491
So that is to say a computer scientist
would describe bubble sort as taking

07:52:21.491 --> 07:52:24.221
on the order of n squared steps.

07:52:24.221 --> 07:52:25.871
That's an oversimplification.

07:52:25.871 --> 07:52:28.961
If we really added it up, it's
actually this many steps-- n

07:52:28.961 --> 07:52:30.971
squared divided by 2 plus n over 2.

07:52:30.971 --> 07:52:34.781
But again, if we want to just be able
to generally compare two algorithms'

07:52:34.781 --> 07:52:38.171
performance, I think it's going to
suffice if we look at that highest

07:52:38.171 --> 07:52:44.081
order term to get a sense of what the
algorithm feels like, if you will,

07:52:44.081 --> 07:52:46.901
or what it even looks like graphically.

07:52:46.901 --> 07:52:50.531
All right, so with that said,
we might describe bubble sort

07:52:50.531 --> 07:52:52.151
as being in big O--

07:52:52.151 --> 07:52:56.061
sorry, selection sort as
being in big O of n squared.

07:52:56.061 --> 07:53:01.391
But what if we consider now the
best case scenario-- an opportunity

07:53:01.391 --> 07:53:03.431
to talk about a lower bound?

07:53:03.431 --> 07:53:07.666
In the best case, how many
steps does selection sort take?

07:53:07.666 --> 07:53:09.041
Well, here, we need some context.

07:53:09.041 --> 07:53:11.681
Like, what does it mean to be
the best case or the worst case

07:53:11.681 --> 07:53:13.451
when it comes to sorting?

07:53:13.451 --> 07:53:16.961
Like, what could you imagine meaning
the best possible scenario when you're

07:53:16.961 --> 07:53:20.012
trying to sort a bunch of numbers?

07:53:20.012 --> 07:53:21.461
I got the whole crew here again.

07:53:21.461 --> 07:53:21.761
Yeah.

07:53:21.761 --> 07:53:23.411
AUDIENCE: They would already be sorted.

07:53:23.411 --> 07:53:24.731
DAVID J. MALAN: All right, they're
already sorted, right?

07:53:24.731 --> 07:53:28.391
I can't really imagine a better scenario
than I have to sort some numbers,

07:53:28.391 --> 07:53:30.401
but they're already sorted for me.

07:53:30.401 --> 07:53:35.531
But does this algorithm
leverage that fact in practice?

07:53:35.531 --> 07:53:38.471
Even if all of our humans
had lined up from 0 to 7,

07:53:38.471 --> 07:53:41.291
I'm pretty sure I would have
pretty naively started here.

07:53:41.291 --> 07:53:43.031
And yes, Celeste happens to be here.

07:53:43.031 --> 07:53:47.736
But I only know she needs to be here
once I've looked at all eight people.

07:53:47.736 --> 07:53:50.361
And then I would have realized,
well, that was a waste of time.

07:53:50.361 --> 07:53:51.641
I can leave Celeste be.

07:53:51.641 --> 07:53:53.861
But then what would I have done?

07:53:53.861 --> 07:53:57.221
I would have ignored her position
because we've solved one problem.

07:53:57.221 --> 07:54:00.621
I would have done the same thing now
for seven people, then six people.

07:54:00.621 --> 07:54:03.681
So every time I walk through,
I'm not doing much useful work.

07:54:03.681 --> 07:54:06.221
But I am doing those
comparisons because I

07:54:06.221 --> 07:54:09.861
don't know until I do the work that
the people were in the right order.

07:54:09.861 --> 07:54:14.801
So this would seem to imply that
the omega notation, the best case

07:54:14.801 --> 07:54:18.101
scenario, even, a lower bound on the
running time would be what, then?

07:54:18.101 --> 07:54:20.201
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:54:20.201 --> 07:54:21.831
DAVID J. MALAN: A little louder?

07:54:21.831 --> 07:54:22.771
AUDIENCE: N squared.

07:54:22.771 --> 07:54:24.611
DAVID J. MALAN: It's still
going to be n squared,

07:54:24.611 --> 07:54:30.181
in fact, because the code I'm giving
myself doesn't leverage or benefit

07:54:30.181 --> 07:54:34.621
from any of that scenario because
it just mindlessly continues

07:54:34.621 --> 07:54:36.131
to do this again and again.

07:54:36.131 --> 07:54:41.371
So in this case, yes, I would claim that
the omega notation for selection sort

07:54:41.371 --> 07:54:43.191
is also big O of n squared.

07:54:43.191 --> 07:54:44.941
So those are the kinds
of numbers to beat.

07:54:44.941 --> 07:54:48.121
It seems like the upper bound
and lower bound of selection

07:54:48.121 --> 07:54:50.491
sort are indeed n squared.

07:54:50.491 --> 07:54:52.741
And so we can also describe
selection sort, therefore,

07:54:52.741 --> 07:54:54.078
as being in theta of n squared.

07:54:54.078 --> 07:54:56.911
That's the first algorithm we've
had the chance to describe that in,

07:54:56.911 --> 07:54:59.011
which is to say that it's kind of slow.

07:54:59.011 --> 07:55:00.956
I mean, maybe other
algorithms are slower,

07:55:00.956 --> 07:55:02.581
but this isn't the best starting point.

07:55:02.581 --> 07:55:03.731
Can we do better?

07:55:03.731 --> 07:55:07.291
Well, there's a reason that I guided us
to doing the second algorithm second.

07:55:07.291 --> 07:55:09.791
Even though you verbally proposed
them in a different order,

07:55:09.791 --> 07:55:12.961
this second algorithm we did is
generally known as bubble sort.

07:55:12.961 --> 07:55:15.571
And I deliberately used
that word a bit ago,

07:55:15.571 --> 07:55:19.291
saying the big values are
bubbling their way up to the right

07:55:19.291 --> 07:55:22.351
to kind of capture the fact that,
indeed, this algorithm works

07:55:22.351 --> 07:55:22.981
differently.

07:55:22.981 --> 07:55:25.231
But let's consider if
it's better or worse.

07:55:25.231 --> 07:55:28.321
So here, we have pseudo
code for bubble sort.

07:55:28.321 --> 07:55:30.291
You could write this
too in different ways.

07:55:30.291 --> 07:55:32.911
But let's consider what
we did on the stage.

07:55:32.911 --> 07:55:36.211
We repeated the following
n minus 1 times.

07:55:36.211 --> 07:55:39.751
We initialized at least, even though
I didn't verbalize it this way,

07:55:39.751 --> 07:55:44.791
a variable like i from 0
to n minus 2, n minus 2.

07:55:44.791 --> 07:55:46.171
And then I asked this question.

07:55:46.171 --> 07:55:52.831
If numbers bracket i and numbers
bracket i plus 1 are out of order,

07:55:52.831 --> 07:55:54.611
then swap them.

07:55:54.611 --> 07:55:56.941
So again, I just did it more
intuitively by pointing,

07:55:56.941 --> 07:55:59.311
but this would be a way,
with a bit of pseudo code,

07:55:59.311 --> 07:56:00.508
to describe what's going on.

07:56:00.508 --> 07:56:03.091
But notice that I'm doing something
a little differently here.

07:56:03.091 --> 07:56:07.091
I'm iterating from if
equals 0 to n minus 2.

07:56:07.091 --> 07:56:07.591
Why?

07:56:07.591 --> 07:56:11.101
Well, if I'm comparing two
things, left hand and right hand,

07:56:11.101 --> 07:56:13.051
I'd still want to start at 0.

07:56:13.051 --> 07:56:15.811
But I don't want to go
all the way to n minus 1

07:56:15.811 --> 07:56:19.409
because then, I'd be going past
the boundary of my array, which

07:56:19.409 --> 07:56:19.951
would be bad.

07:56:19.951 --> 07:56:22.441
I want to make sure that my
left hand-- i, if you will--

07:56:22.441 --> 07:56:27.181
stops at n minus 2 so that when
I plus 1 in my pseudo code,

07:56:27.181 --> 07:56:29.971
I'm looking at the last two
elements, not the last element

07:56:29.971 --> 07:56:31.261
and then pass the boundary.

07:56:31.261 --> 07:56:33.094
That's actually a common
programming mistake

07:56:33.094 --> 07:56:34.981
that we'll undoubtedly
soon make by going

07:56:34.981 --> 07:56:37.241
beyond the boundaries of your array.

07:56:37.241 --> 07:56:43.891
So this pseudo code, then, allows me to
say compare every one again and again

07:56:43.891 --> 07:56:46.091
and swap them if they're out of order.

07:56:46.091 --> 07:56:50.551
Why do I repeat the whole
thing n minus 1 times?

07:56:50.551 --> 07:56:55.861
Like, why does it not suffice
just to do this loop here?

07:56:55.861 --> 07:56:59.341
Think what happened with Celeste.

07:56:59.341 --> 07:57:03.841
Why do I repeat this whole
thing n minus 1 times?

07:57:03.841 --> 07:57:04.591
Yeah, in the back?

07:57:04.591 --> 07:57:12.216
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:57:12.216 --> 07:57:14.591
DAVID J. MALAN: Indeed, and I think
if I can recap accurately,

07:57:14.591 --> 07:57:16.001
think back to Celeste again.

07:57:16.001 --> 07:57:18.491
And I'm sorry to keep calling
on you as our number 0.

07:57:18.491 --> 07:57:22.031
Each time through bubble
sort, she only moved one step.

07:57:22.031 --> 07:57:25.641
And so in total, if there's n
locations, at the end of the day,

07:57:25.641 --> 07:57:30.291
she needs to move n minus 1 steps to get
0 all the way to where it needs to be.

07:57:30.291 --> 07:57:34.691
And so this inner loop, if you
will, where we're iterating using i,

07:57:34.691 --> 07:57:37.061
that just fixes some of the problems.

07:57:37.061 --> 07:57:40.511
But it doesn't fix all of the problems
until we do that same logic again

07:57:40.511 --> 07:57:42.021
and again and again.

07:57:42.021 --> 07:57:45.771
And so how might we quantify the
running time of this algorithm?

07:57:45.771 --> 07:57:49.031
Well, one way to see it is to just
literally look at the pseudo code.

07:57:49.031 --> 07:57:53.531
The outer loop repeats n
minus 1 times by definition.

07:57:53.531 --> 07:57:54.851
It literally says that.

07:57:54.851 --> 07:58:00.321
The inner loop, the for loop,
also iterates n minus 1 times.

07:58:00.321 --> 07:58:00.821
Why?

07:58:00.821 --> 07:58:02.901
Because it's going from 0 to n minus 2.

07:58:02.901 --> 07:58:06.941
And if that's hard to think about,
that's the same thing is 1 to n minus 1

07:58:06.941 --> 07:58:09.681
if you just add 1 to
both ends of the formula.

07:58:09.681 --> 07:58:14.271
So that means you're doing n
minus 1 things n minus 1 times.

07:58:14.271 --> 07:58:16.901
So I literally multiply how
many times the outer loop

07:58:16.901 --> 07:58:20.321
is running by how many times the
inner loop is running, which gives me

07:58:20.321 --> 07:58:23.681
sort of FOIL method n minus 1 squared.

07:58:23.681 --> 07:58:25.661
And I could multiply
that whole thing out.

07:58:25.661 --> 07:58:28.691
Well, let's consider this just
a little more methodically here.

07:58:28.691 --> 07:58:32.743
If I have n minus 1 on the
outer, n minus 1 on the inner--

07:58:32.743 --> 07:58:33.951
let's go ahead and FOIL this.

07:58:33.951 --> 07:58:37.541
So n squared minus n
minus n plus 1, combine

07:58:37.541 --> 07:58:40.931
like terms-- n squared minus 2n plus 1.

07:58:40.931 --> 07:58:45.831
And now which of these terms is clearly
going to be dominant, so to speak?

07:58:45.831 --> 07:58:46.331
The--

07:58:46.331 --> 07:58:46.811
AUDIENCE: N squared.

07:58:46.811 --> 07:58:48.081
DAVID J. MALAN: --the n squared.

07:58:48.081 --> 07:58:50.621
So yes, even though
minus 2n is a good thing

07:58:50.621 --> 07:58:53.021
because it's subtracting off
some of the time required,

07:58:53.021 --> 07:58:56.051
plus 1 is not that big a thing,
there's such drops in the bucket when

07:58:56.051 --> 07:58:58.481
n gets really large, like
in the millions or billions,

07:58:58.481 --> 07:59:02.871
certainly, that bubble sort 2
is on the order of n squared.

07:59:02.871 --> 07:59:05.471
It's not the same exactly
as selection sort.

07:59:05.471 --> 07:59:07.391
But as n gets big,
honestly, we're barely

07:59:07.391 --> 07:59:09.891
going to be able to notice
the difference most likely.

07:59:09.891 --> 07:59:13.551
And so it too might be said to
be on the order of n squared.

07:59:13.551 --> 07:59:18.891
And if we consider now the lower
bound on bubble sort's running time,

07:59:18.891 --> 07:59:23.081
here's where things get
potentially interesting.

07:59:23.081 --> 07:59:28.811
What might you claim is the running
time of bubble sort in the best case?

07:59:28.811 --> 07:59:32.591
And the best case, I claim, is when
the numbers are already sorted.

07:59:32.591 --> 07:59:35.081
Is our pseudo code going
to take that into account?

07:59:35.081 --> 07:59:36.803
AUDIENCE: N

07:59:36.803 --> 07:59:37.511
DAVID J. MALAN: OK, n.

07:59:37.511 --> 07:59:38.711
Why do you propose n?

07:59:38.711 --> 07:59:43.404
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

07:59:43.404 --> 07:59:45.071
DAVID J. MALAN: Yes, and that's the key word.

07:59:45.071 --> 07:59:49.511
To summarize, in bubble sort, I do have
to minimally make one pass because if I

07:59:49.511 --> 07:59:52.121
don't look at all n elements,
that I'm theoretically

07:59:52.121 --> 07:59:53.621
just guessing if it's sorted or not.

07:59:53.621 --> 07:59:55.841
Like, I obviously
intuitively have to look

07:59:55.841 --> 07:59:58.751
at every element to decide yay
or nay, it's in the right order.

07:59:58.751 --> 08:00:01.541
And my original pseudo code,
though, is pretty naive.

08:00:01.541 --> 08:00:07.001
It's just going to blindly go back and
forth n minus 1 times again and again,

08:00:07.001 --> 08:00:08.171
and that's going to add up.

08:00:08.171 --> 08:00:10.121
But what if I add a
bit of an optimization

08:00:10.121 --> 08:00:12.371
that you might have glimpsed
on the slide a moment ago

08:00:12.371 --> 08:00:15.971
where if I compare two people and I
don't swap them, compare two people,

08:00:15.971 --> 08:00:18.911
don't swap them, and I go all the
way through the list comparing

08:00:18.911 --> 08:00:22.481
every pair of adjacent
people, and I make no swaps,

08:00:22.481 --> 08:00:25.181
it would be kind of not
just naive but stupid

08:00:25.181 --> 08:00:28.721
to do that same process again
because if the humans have not moved,

08:00:28.721 --> 08:00:31.511
I'm not going to make
any different decisions.

08:00:31.511 --> 08:00:34.101
I'm going to do nothing
again, nothing again.

08:00:34.101 --> 08:00:37.511
So at that point, it would be stupid,
very inefficient, to go back and forth

08:00:37.511 --> 08:00:38.571
and back and forth.

08:00:38.571 --> 08:00:42.431
So if I modify our pseudo code with
just an additional if condition,

08:00:42.431 --> 08:00:44.411
I bet we can speed this up.

08:00:44.411 --> 08:00:50.561
Inside of that same pseudo code, what
if I say, hey, if no swaps, quit?

08:00:50.561 --> 08:00:54.371
Like quit, prematurely before
the loops are finished running.

08:00:54.371 --> 08:00:57.741
One of the loops has gone
through per the indentation here.

08:00:57.741 --> 08:01:00.551
But if I do a loop from
left to right and I

08:01:00.551 --> 08:01:03.041
have made no swaps, which you
can think of as just being

08:01:03.041 --> 08:01:06.611
one other variable that's plus plusing
as I go keeping, track of how many

08:01:06.611 --> 08:01:07.181
swaps--

08:01:07.181 --> 08:01:09.101
if I've made no swaps
from left to right,

08:01:09.101 --> 08:01:11.601
I'm not going to make any swaps
the next time around either.

08:01:11.601 --> 08:01:14.391
So let's just quit at that point.

08:01:14.391 --> 08:01:16.991
And that is to say in the
best case, if you will,

08:01:16.991 --> 08:01:20.771
when the list is already sorted,
the omega notation for bubble sort

08:01:20.771 --> 08:01:25.821
might indeed be omega of n
if you add that optimization

08:01:25.821 --> 08:01:28.421
so as to short circuit
all of that inefficient

08:01:28.421 --> 08:01:34.191
looping to do it only as
many times as is necessary.

08:01:34.191 --> 08:01:36.641
Let me pause to see if
there's any questions here.

08:01:36.641 --> 08:01:37.553
Yeah.

08:01:37.553 --> 08:01:46.399
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] to optimize the
running time for all cases possible?

08:01:46.399 --> 08:01:47.441
DAVID J. MALAN: Good question.

08:01:47.441 --> 08:01:53.051
If the running time of selection sort
and bubble sort are both in big O

08:01:53.051 --> 08:01:58.841
of n squared but selection sort is in
omega of n squared while bubble sort is

08:01:58.841 --> 08:02:01.661
in omega of n, which sounds better--

08:02:01.661 --> 08:02:04.871
I think if I may, should we
just always use bubble sort?

08:02:04.871 --> 08:02:09.041
Yes if we think that we
might benefit over time

08:02:09.041 --> 08:02:13.611
from a lot of good case
scenarios or best case scenarios.

08:02:13.611 --> 08:02:15.701
However, the goal at
hand in just a bit is

08:02:15.701 --> 08:02:17.841
going to be to do even
better than both of these.

08:02:17.841 --> 08:02:19.691
So hold that question
further for a moment.

08:02:19.691 --> 08:02:20.191
Yeah.

08:02:20.191 --> 08:02:25.718
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] n minus 1?

08:02:25.718 --> 08:02:26.301
DAVID J. MALAN: No.

08:02:26.301 --> 08:02:27.441
So yes, good question.

08:02:27.441 --> 08:02:31.251
So I say omega of n, but is it
technically omega of n minus 1?

08:02:31.251 --> 08:02:34.471
Maybe, but again, we're
throwing away lower order terms.

08:02:34.471 --> 08:02:38.031
And that's an advantage because we're
not comparing things ever so precisely.

08:02:38.031 --> 08:02:41.541
Just like I plotted with the
green and yellow and red chart,

08:02:41.541 --> 08:02:44.341
I just want to get a sense of
the shape of these algorithms

08:02:44.341 --> 08:02:47.691
so that when n gets really
large, which of these choices

08:02:47.691 --> 08:02:49.546
is going to matter the most?

08:02:49.546 --> 08:02:51.921
At the end of the day, it's
actually perfectly reasonable

08:02:51.921 --> 08:02:53.821
to use selection sort
or bubble sort if you

08:02:53.821 --> 08:02:56.571
don't have that much data because
they're going to be pretty fast.

08:02:56.571 --> 08:02:58.881
My God, our computers
nowadays are 1 gigahertz,

08:02:58.881 --> 08:03:02.801
2 gigahertz, 1 billion things per
second, 2 billion things per second.

08:03:02.801 --> 08:03:05.301
But if we have large data sets,
as we will later in the term

08:03:05.301 --> 08:03:07.831
and as you might in the real world,
that the Googles of the world,

08:03:07.831 --> 08:03:09.891
then you're going to want
to be more thoughtful.

08:03:09.891 --> 08:03:11.601
And that's where we're going today.

08:03:11.601 --> 08:03:14.451
All right, so let's actually see
this visualized a little bit.

08:03:14.451 --> 08:03:16.371
In a moment, I'm going
to change screens here

08:03:16.371 --> 08:03:21.711
to open up what is a little
visualization tool that will give us

08:03:21.711 --> 08:03:25.281
a sense of how these things actually
work and look at a faster rate

08:03:25.281 --> 08:03:27.181
than our humans are able
to do here on stage.

08:03:27.181 --> 08:03:31.941
So here is another visualization of a
bunch of numbers, an array of numbers.

08:03:31.941 --> 08:03:35.031
Short bars mean small numbers,
tall bars mean big numbers.

08:03:35.031 --> 08:03:37.281
So instead of having the
numbers on their torsos here,

08:03:37.281 --> 08:03:42.051
we just have bars that are small or tall
based on the magnitude of the number.

08:03:42.051 --> 08:03:44.991
Let me go ahead, and I
preconfigured this in advance

08:03:44.991 --> 08:03:46.411
to operate somewhat quickly.

08:03:46.411 --> 08:03:49.911
Let's go ahead and do selections
sort by clicking this button.

08:03:49.911 --> 08:03:52.581
And you'll see some pink bars flying by.

08:03:52.581 --> 08:03:56.001
And that's like me walking
left and right, left and right,

08:03:56.001 --> 08:03:58.501
to select the next smallest number.

08:03:58.501 --> 08:04:01.941
And so what you'll see happening on
the left of this array of numbers

08:04:01.941 --> 08:04:04.881
is Celeste, if you will, and
all of the other smaller numbers

08:04:04.881 --> 08:04:08.391
are appearing on the left while
we continue to solve the remaining

08:04:08.391 --> 08:04:10.171
problems to the right.

08:04:10.171 --> 08:04:13.538
So again, we no longer have to
touch the smaller numbers here.

08:04:13.538 --> 08:04:16.371
So that's why the problem is getting
smaller and smaller and smaller

08:04:16.371 --> 08:04:17.151
over time.

08:04:17.151 --> 08:04:20.781
But you can notice now
visually, look at how many times

08:04:20.781 --> 08:04:22.281
we're retracing our steps.

08:04:22.281 --> 08:04:25.071
This is why things
that are n squared tend

08:04:25.071 --> 08:04:29.191
to be frowned upon if avoidable because
I'm touching the same elements again

08:04:29.191 --> 08:04:29.691
and again.

08:04:29.691 --> 08:04:31.971
When I was walking through, I
kept pointing at the same humans

08:04:31.971 --> 08:04:32.721
again and again.

08:04:32.721 --> 08:04:34.021
And that adds up.

08:04:34.021 --> 08:04:37.111
So let's see if bubble sort looks
or feels a little different.

08:04:37.111 --> 08:04:40.549
Let me re-randomize the thing, and let
me now click Bubble Sort at the top.

08:04:40.549 --> 08:04:43.341
And as you might infer, there's
other sorting algorithms out there,

08:04:43.341 --> 08:04:44.633
not all of which we'll look at.

08:04:44.633 --> 08:04:46.101
But here's bubble sort.

08:04:46.101 --> 08:04:48.931
Same pink coloration, but it's
doing something different.

08:04:48.931 --> 08:04:52.221
It's two pink bars going through
again and again comparing

08:04:52.221 --> 08:04:53.911
the adjacent numbers.

08:04:53.911 --> 08:04:57.411
And you'll see that the largest
numbers are indeed bubbling the way up

08:04:57.411 --> 08:05:02.481
to the right, but the smaller
numbers, like our number 0 was,

08:05:02.481 --> 08:05:04.648
is only slowly making its way over.

08:05:04.648 --> 08:05:05.481
Here's a comparable.

08:05:05.481 --> 08:05:06.561
Here's the number one.

08:05:06.561 --> 08:05:09.261
And it's going to take a while
to get all the way to the left.

08:05:09.261 --> 08:05:12.921
And here too, notice how
many times the same bars

08:05:12.921 --> 08:05:16.951
are becoming pink, how many times the
algorithm is retracing and retracing

08:05:16.951 --> 08:05:17.451
its steps.

08:05:17.451 --> 08:05:17.961
Why?

08:05:17.961 --> 08:05:21.501
Because it's only solving one
problem at a time on each pass.

08:05:21.501 --> 08:05:25.078
And each time we do that, we're stepping
through practically the whole array.

08:05:25.078 --> 08:05:28.161
And now granted, I could speed this
up even further if I really wanted to,

08:05:28.161 --> 08:05:32.481
but my God, this is only, what, like
50 or 60 elements, something like that?

08:05:32.481 --> 08:05:33.501
This is slow.

08:05:33.501 --> 08:05:36.681
Like, this is what n squared
looks like and feels like.

08:05:36.681 --> 08:05:38.931
And now I'm just trying to
come up with words to say

08:05:38.931 --> 08:05:40.641
until we get to the finish line here.

08:05:40.641 --> 08:05:43.611
Like, this would be annoying if
this is the speed of sorting,

08:05:43.611 --> 08:05:47.494
and this is why I sort of secretly
sorted the numbers for Rave in advance

08:05:47.494 --> 08:05:49.911
because it would have taken
us an annoying number of steps

08:05:49.911 --> 08:05:51.571
to get that in place for her.

08:05:51.571 --> 08:05:54.531
So those two algorithms are n squared.

08:05:54.531 --> 08:05:56.626
Can we do, in fact, better?

08:05:56.626 --> 08:05:59.751
Well, to save the best algorithm for
last, let's take a shorter five minute

08:05:59.751 --> 08:06:00.251
break here.

08:06:00.251 --> 08:06:04.771
And when we come back, we'll
do even better than n squared.

08:06:04.771 --> 08:06:06.841
All right.

08:06:06.841 --> 08:06:11.541
So the challenge at hand is to
do better than selection sort

08:06:11.541 --> 08:06:14.631
and better than bubble sort
and ideally not just marginally

08:06:14.631 --> 08:06:16.821
better but fundamentally better.

08:06:16.821 --> 08:06:20.331
Just like in week zero, that third
and final divide and conquer algorithm

08:06:20.331 --> 08:06:23.221
was sort of fundamentally
faster than the other two.

08:06:23.221 --> 08:06:26.311
So can we do better than something
on the order of n squared?

08:06:26.311 --> 08:06:28.671
Well, I bet we can if
we start to approach

08:06:28.671 --> 08:06:30.441
the problem a little differently.

08:06:30.441 --> 08:06:32.563
The sorts we've done
thus far, generally known

08:06:32.563 --> 08:06:34.521
as comparison sorts--
and that kind of captures

08:06:34.521 --> 08:06:38.361
the reality that we were doing a huge
number of comparisons again and again.

08:06:38.361 --> 08:06:41.721
And you kind of saw that in the vertical
bars that were going pink as everything

08:06:41.721 --> 08:06:43.216
was being compared again and again.

08:06:43.216 --> 08:06:45.591
But there's this programming
technique, and it's actually

08:06:45.591 --> 08:06:48.501
a mathematical technique
known as recursion

08:06:48.501 --> 08:06:50.311
that we've actually seen before.

08:06:50.311 --> 08:06:53.361
And this is a building
block or a mental model

08:06:53.361 --> 08:06:56.661
we can bring to bear on the problem
to solve the sorting problem

08:06:56.661 --> 08:06:58.101
sort of fundamentally differently.

08:06:58.101 --> 08:07:00.981
But first, let's look at it
in a more familiar context.

08:07:00.981 --> 08:07:07.551
A little bit ago, I proposed this pseudo
code for the binary search algorithm.

08:07:07.551 --> 08:07:10.491
And notice that what was
interesting about this code,

08:07:10.491 --> 08:07:14.331
even though I didn't call it out at the
time, it's kind of cyclically defined.

08:07:14.331 --> 08:07:17.091
Like, I claim this is
an algorithm for search,

08:07:17.091 --> 08:07:21.111
and yet it seems a little unfair
that I'm using the verb search

08:07:21.111 --> 08:07:23.321
inside of the algorithm for search.

08:07:23.321 --> 08:07:26.081
It's like an English sort of
defining a word by using the word.

08:07:26.081 --> 08:07:28.211
Normally, you shouldn't
really get away with that.

08:07:28.211 --> 08:07:30.791
But there's something
interesting about this technique

08:07:30.791 --> 08:07:35.471
here because even though this
whole thing is a search algorithm

08:07:35.471 --> 08:07:40.871
and I'm using my own algorithm to
search the left half or the right half,

08:07:40.871 --> 08:07:42.881
the key feature here
that doesn't normally

08:07:42.881 --> 08:07:46.031
happen in English when you
define a word in terms of a word

08:07:46.031 --> 08:07:49.661
is that when I search the left
half or search the right half, yes,

08:07:49.661 --> 08:07:51.171
I'm doing the same thing.

08:07:51.171 --> 08:07:52.451
I'm using the same algorithm.

08:07:52.451 --> 08:07:55.421
But the problem is, by
definition, half as large.

08:07:55.421 --> 08:07:58.541
So this isn't going to be a
cyclical argument in the same way.

08:07:58.541 --> 08:08:02.111
This approach, by using
search within search

08:08:02.111 --> 08:08:05.651
is going to whittle the problem down
and down and down until hopefully,

08:08:05.651 --> 08:08:08.541
one door or no doors remains.

08:08:08.541 --> 08:08:11.081
And so recursion is a
programming technique

08:08:11.081 --> 08:08:14.291
whereby a function calls itself.

08:08:14.291 --> 08:08:18.581
And we haven't seen this yet in C, and
we haven't seen this really in Scratch.

08:08:18.581 --> 08:08:22.483
But in C, you can have
a function call itself.

08:08:22.483 --> 08:08:24.191
And the form that
takes is like literally

08:08:24.191 --> 08:08:28.541
using the function's name inside of
the function's implementation itself.

08:08:28.541 --> 08:08:32.671
We've actually seen an opportunity
for this once before too.

08:08:32.671 --> 08:08:33.671
Think back to week zero.

08:08:33.671 --> 08:08:35.921
Here's that same pseudo code
for searching for someone

08:08:35.921 --> 08:08:37.541
in an actual, physical phone book.

08:08:37.541 --> 08:08:40.061
And notice these yellow lines here.

08:08:40.061 --> 08:08:44.171
We described those in week zero
as inducing a loop, a cycle.

08:08:44.171 --> 08:08:48.671
And this is a very procedural approach,
if you will, because lines 8 and 11

08:08:48.671 --> 08:08:50.831
are very mechanically,
if you will, telling

08:08:50.831 --> 08:08:54.731
me to go back to line three to
do this kind of looping thing.

08:08:54.731 --> 08:08:59.111
But really, what that's doing in the
binary search algorithm for the phone

08:08:59.111 --> 08:09:04.481
book is it's just telling me to search
the left half or search the right half.

08:09:04.481 --> 08:09:08.261
I'm doing it more mechanically
again by sort of telling myself

08:09:08.261 --> 08:09:09.851
what line number to go back to.

08:09:09.851 --> 08:09:12.761
But that's equivalent to just telling
myself go search the left half,

08:09:12.761 --> 08:09:15.311
search the right half, the
key thing being the left

08:09:15.311 --> 08:09:18.081
have and the right half are
smaller than the original problem.

08:09:18.081 --> 08:09:21.991
It would be a bug if I just said search
the phone book, search the phone book,

08:09:21.991 --> 08:09:23.741
because obviously, you
never get anywhere.

08:09:23.741 --> 08:09:25.901
But if you search the
half, the half, the half,

08:09:25.901 --> 08:09:27.831
problem gets smaller and smaller.

08:09:27.831 --> 08:09:34.541
So let's reformulate week zero's phone
book code to be not procedural as here

08:09:34.541 --> 08:09:39.501
but recursive whereby in
this search algorithm,

08:09:39.501 --> 08:09:42.941
AKA binary search, formerly
called divide and conquer, I'm

08:09:42.941 --> 08:09:46.301
going to literally use also
the keyword search here.

08:09:46.301 --> 08:09:48.311
Notice among the benefits
of doing this is it

08:09:48.311 --> 08:09:51.159
kind of tightens the code up,
makes it a little more succinct,

08:09:51.159 --> 08:09:53.201
even though that's kind
of a fringe benefit here.

08:09:53.201 --> 08:09:56.711
But it's an elegant
way too of describing

08:09:56.711 --> 08:10:01.571
a problem by just having
a function use itself

08:10:01.571 --> 08:10:05.691
to solve a smaller puzzle at hand.

08:10:05.691 --> 08:10:08.741
So let's now consider a
familiar problem, a smaller

08:10:08.741 --> 08:10:11.493
version than the one you've dabbled
with-- this sort of pyramid,

08:10:11.493 --> 08:10:12.701
this half pyramid from Mario.

08:10:12.701 --> 08:10:15.431
And let's throw away the parts
that aren't that interesting

08:10:15.431 --> 08:10:19.781
and just consider how we might, up
until now, implement this in C code,

08:10:19.781 --> 08:10:21.881
this left aligned pyramid, if you will.

08:10:21.881 --> 08:10:28.991
Let me go over here, and let me create
a file called-- how about iteration.c?

08:10:28.991 --> 08:10:32.441
And in this file, I'm going to
go ahead and include cs50.h.

08:10:32.441 --> 08:10:36.251
And I'm going to include stdio.h.

08:10:36.251 --> 08:10:41.801
And the goal at hand is to implement in
C a little program that just prints out

08:10:41.801 --> 08:10:43.641
this and exactly this pyramid.

08:10:43.641 --> 08:10:46.474
So no get string or any of that--
we're just going to keep it simple

08:10:46.474 --> 08:10:49.761
and print exactly this
pyramid of height 4 here.

08:10:49.761 --> 08:10:50.931
So how might I do this?

08:10:50.931 --> 08:10:55.306
Well, let me go ahead, and in main,
let me first ask the user for--

08:10:55.306 --> 08:10:56.931
well, we'll go ahead and generalize it.

08:10:56.931 --> 08:10:58.764
Let's go ahead and ask
the user for heights.

08:10:58.764 --> 08:11:00.611
We're using getint as before.

08:11:00.611 --> 08:11:02.861
And I'll store that in a
variable called height.

08:11:02.861 --> 08:11:05.111
And then let me go ahead
and simply call the function

08:11:05.111 --> 08:11:06.771
draw passing in that height.

08:11:06.771 --> 08:11:09.401
So for the moment, let me
assume that someone somewhere

08:11:09.401 --> 08:11:10.991
has implemented a draw function.

08:11:10.991 --> 08:11:14.171
And this, then, is the
entirety of my program.

08:11:14.171 --> 08:11:17.331
All right, unfortunately, C does
not come with a draw function.

08:11:17.331 --> 08:11:19.181
So let me go ahead and invent one.

08:11:19.181 --> 08:11:20.661
It doesn't need to return a value.

08:11:20.661 --> 08:11:23.211
It just needs to print
something-- so-called side effect.

08:11:23.211 --> 08:11:27.821
So I'm going to define a function
called draw that takes as input an int.

08:11:27.821 --> 08:11:30.641
I'll call it n for number, but
I could call it anything I want.

08:11:30.641 --> 08:11:32.211
And inside of this.

08:11:32.211 --> 08:11:37.601
I'm going to go ahead and print out a
left aligned pyramid like this from top

08:11:37.601 --> 08:11:38.351
to bottom.

08:11:38.351 --> 08:11:42.071
The salient features here are that this
is a pyramid, at least in this example,

08:11:42.071 --> 08:11:43.181
of height four.

08:11:43.181 --> 08:11:46.601
And now in height four, the
first row has one brick.

08:11:46.601 --> 08:11:47.831
The second row has two.

08:11:47.831 --> 08:11:48.761
The third has three.

08:11:48.761 --> 08:11:49.631
The fourth has four.

08:11:49.631 --> 08:11:52.461
That's a nice pattern that I
can probably represent in code.

08:11:52.461 --> 08:11:53.571
So how might I do this?

08:11:53.571 --> 08:11:55.751
Well, how about 4 int i gets--

08:11:55.751 --> 08:11:57.311
let me do it the old school way--

08:11:57.311 --> 08:11:58.091
1.

08:11:58.091 --> 08:12:02.891
And then i is less than or equal to n.

08:12:02.891 --> 08:12:04.541
And then i plus plus--

08:12:04.541 --> 08:12:08.171
so I'm going from 1 to 4 just
to keep myself sane here.

08:12:08.171 --> 08:12:11.051
And then inside of this
loop, what do I want to do?

08:12:11.051 --> 08:12:12.921
Well, let me keep it
conventional, in fact.

08:12:12.921 --> 08:12:16.331
Let me just change this to
be the more conventional 0

08:12:16.331 --> 08:12:20.531
to n even though it might not be
as intuitive because now on row 0,

08:12:20.531 --> 08:12:21.911
I want one brick.

08:12:21.911 --> 08:12:24.326
On row 1, I want two
bricks, dot dot dot.

08:12:24.326 --> 08:12:25.661
On row 3, I want four.

08:12:25.661 --> 08:12:26.891
So it's kind of offset now.

08:12:26.891 --> 08:12:28.601
But I'm being more conventional.

08:12:28.601 --> 08:12:32.801
So on each row, how many
bricks do I want to print?

08:12:32.801 --> 08:12:34.241
Well, I think I want to do this.

08:12:34.241 --> 08:12:40.061
For int j, for instance, common to
use j after if you have a nested loop,

08:12:40.061 --> 08:12:47.291
let's start j at 0 and do this
so long as is less than i plus 1

08:12:47.291 --> 08:12:48.821
and then do j plus plus.

08:12:48.821 --> 08:12:50.861
So why i plus 1?

08:12:50.861 --> 08:12:55.031
Well, again, when I equals 0, that's
the first row, and I want one brick.

08:12:55.031 --> 08:12:57.011
When i equals 1, that's the second row.

08:12:57.011 --> 08:12:57.791
I want two bricks.

08:12:57.791 --> 08:13:00.611
And dot dot dot, when i
is 3, I want four bricks.

08:13:00.611 --> 08:13:03.821
So again, I have to add 1 to i
to get the total number of bricks

08:13:03.821 --> 08:13:05.481
that I want to print to the screen.

08:13:05.481 --> 08:13:10.181
So inside of this nested for loop,
I'm going to do printf of a hash

08:13:10.181 --> 08:13:13.331
with no new line.

08:13:13.331 --> 08:13:17.473
I'm going to save the new
line for about here instead.

08:13:17.473 --> 08:13:19.181
All right, the last
thing I'm going to do

08:13:19.181 --> 08:13:22.911
is copy and paste the prototype
at the top of the file.

08:13:22.911 --> 08:13:23.981
So that I can call this.

08:13:23.981 --> 08:13:27.089
And again, this is of
now week one, week two.

08:13:27.089 --> 08:13:29.381
Wouldn't necessarily come to
your mind as quickly as it

08:13:29.381 --> 08:13:32.741
might to mine after all this practice,
but this is something reminiscent

08:13:32.741 --> 08:13:35.591
of what you yourself did
already for Mario-- printing out

08:13:35.591 --> 08:13:38.691
a pyramid that hopefully in a
moment is going to look like this.

08:13:38.691 --> 08:13:40.731
So let me go back to my code.

08:13:40.731 --> 08:13:44.921
Let me run make iteration, and
let me do dot slash iteration.

08:13:44.921 --> 08:13:46.901
I'll type in 4, and voila.

08:13:46.901 --> 08:13:50.201
Seems to be correct, and let's assume
it's going to work for other inputs

08:13:50.201 --> 08:13:50.861
as well.

08:13:50.861 --> 08:13:51.671
Oh, thank you.

08:13:56.131 --> 08:13:59.821
So this is indeed an example
of iteration-- doing something

08:13:59.821 --> 08:14:01.141
again and again.

08:14:01.141 --> 08:14:02.521
And it's very procedural.

08:14:02.521 --> 08:14:05.521
Like, I literally have a function
called draw that does this thing.

08:14:05.521 --> 08:14:09.931
But I can think about implementing
draw in a somewhat different way that's

08:14:09.931 --> 08:14:11.071
kind of clever.

08:14:11.071 --> 08:14:13.081
And it's not strictly
necessary for this problem

08:14:13.081 --> 08:14:15.721
because this problem honestly
is not that complicated

08:14:15.721 --> 08:14:17.748
to solve once you have
practice under your belt.

08:14:17.748 --> 08:14:20.581
Certainly the first time around,
probably significantly challenging.

08:14:20.581 --> 08:14:23.611
But now that you kind of
associate, OK, row one

08:14:23.611 --> 08:14:26.371
with one brick, row two with two
bricks, it kind of comes together

08:14:26.371 --> 08:14:27.701
with these for loops.

08:14:27.701 --> 08:14:30.131
But how else could we
think about this problem?

08:14:30.131 --> 08:14:33.301
Well, this physical structure,
these bricks, in some sense

08:14:33.301 --> 08:14:39.341
is a recursive structure, a structure
that's defined in terms of itself.

08:14:39.341 --> 08:14:40.721
Now what do I mean by that?

08:14:40.721 --> 08:14:45.961
Well, if I were to ask you the question,
what does a pyramid of height 4

08:14:45.961 --> 08:14:49.081
look like, you would point,
of course, to this picture.

08:14:49.081 --> 08:14:55.531
But you could also kind of
cleverly say to me, well,

08:14:55.531 --> 08:15:00.601
it's actually a pyramid of
height 3 plus 1 additional row.

08:15:00.601 --> 08:15:02.598
And here's that cyclical
argument, right?

08:15:02.598 --> 08:15:05.431
Kind of obnoxious to do typically
in English or in a spoken language

08:15:05.431 --> 08:15:07.561
because you're defining one
thing in terms of itself.

08:15:07.561 --> 08:15:08.769
What's a pyramid of height 4?

08:15:08.769 --> 08:15:12.541
Well, it's a pyramid of
height 3 plus 1 more row.

08:15:12.541 --> 08:15:15.301
But we can kind of leverage
this logic in code.

08:15:15.301 --> 08:15:16.951
Well, what's a pyramid of height 3?

08:15:16.951 --> 08:15:19.231
Well, it's a pyramid of
height 2 plus 1 more row.

08:15:19.231 --> 08:15:21.301
Fine, what's a pyramid of height 2?

08:15:21.301 --> 08:15:23.731
Well, it's a pyramid of
height 1 plus 1 more row.

08:15:23.731 --> 08:15:26.731
And then hopefully, this process
ends, and it does because notice,

08:15:26.731 --> 08:15:29.351
the pyramid is getting
smaller and smaller.

08:15:29.351 --> 08:15:32.971
So you're not going to have this
sort of silly back and forth with me

08:15:32.971 --> 08:15:36.911
infinitely many times because when
we finally get to the base case,

08:15:36.911 --> 08:15:38.491
the end of the pyramid, fine.

08:15:38.491 --> 08:15:39.961
What is a pyramid of height 1?

08:15:39.961 --> 08:15:42.991
Well, it's a pyramid of no
height plus one more row.

08:15:42.991 --> 08:15:45.631
And at that point, things
just get negative--

08:15:45.631 --> 08:15:46.411
no pun intended.

08:15:46.411 --> 08:15:48.396
Things just would otherwise go negative.

08:15:48.396 --> 08:15:49.771
And so you can just kind of stop.

08:15:49.771 --> 08:15:52.291
The base case is when
there is no more pyramid.

08:15:52.291 --> 08:15:56.441
So there's a way to draw a line in the
sand and say, stop, no more arguments.

08:15:56.441 --> 08:16:00.541
But this idea of defining a physical
structure in terms of itself

08:16:00.541 --> 08:16:06.541
or code in terms of itself actually lets
us do some interesting new algorithms.

08:16:06.541 --> 08:16:08.521
Let me go back to my code here.

08:16:08.521 --> 08:16:14.161
Let me go ahead and create one
final file here called recursion.c

08:16:14.161 --> 08:16:20.461
that leverages this idea of this
built-in self-referential nature.

08:16:20.461 --> 08:16:22.441
Let me include cs50.h.

08:16:22.441 --> 08:16:26.101
Let me go ahead and include
standardio.h, int main void.

08:16:26.101 --> 08:16:30.361
And then inside of main, I'm going
to do the exact same thing-- int

08:16:30.361 --> 08:16:34.831
height equals get int,
asking the user for height.

08:16:34.831 --> 08:16:38.231
And then I'm going to go ahead
and call draw passing in height.

08:16:38.231 --> 08:16:39.881
So that's going to stay the same.

08:16:39.881 --> 08:16:45.941
I even am going to make my prototype
the same-- void draw int n semicolon.

08:16:45.941 --> 08:16:48.181
And now I'm going to
implement void down here

08:16:48.181 --> 08:16:49.951
with that same prototype, of course.

08:16:49.951 --> 08:16:52.831
But the code now is going
to be a little different.

08:16:52.831 --> 08:16:54.431
What am I going to do here?

08:16:54.431 --> 08:16:59.971
Well, first of all, if you ask
me to draw a pyramid of height n,

08:16:59.971 --> 08:17:02.941
I'm going to be kind of a wise
ass here and say, well, just

08:17:02.941 --> 08:17:05.371
draw a pyramid of n minus 1--

08:17:05.371 --> 08:17:06.123
done.

08:17:06.123 --> 08:17:08.581
All right, but there's still
a little more work to be done.

08:17:08.581 --> 08:17:13.021
What happens after I print or
draw a pyramid of height n minus 1

08:17:13.021 --> 08:17:17.701
according to our structural
definition a moment ago?

08:17:17.701 --> 08:17:22.831
What remains after drawing a pyramid
of height n minus 1 or 3, specifically?

08:17:22.831 --> 08:17:25.201
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

08:17:25.201 --> 08:17:26.761
We need one more row of hashes.

08:17:26.761 --> 08:17:28.111
OK, so I can do that, right?

08:17:28.111 --> 08:17:29.574
I'm OK with the single loops.

08:17:29.574 --> 08:17:30.991
There's no nesting necessary here.

08:17:30.991 --> 08:17:35.371
I'm just going to do this-- for
int i get 0, i is less than n,

08:17:35.371 --> 08:17:37.546
which is the height that's
passed in, i plus plus.

08:17:37.546 --> 08:17:39.421
And then inside of this
loop, I'm very simply

08:17:39.421 --> 08:17:41.101
going to print out a single hash.

08:17:41.101 --> 08:17:45.011
And then down here, I'm going to
print out a new line at the very end.

08:17:45.011 --> 08:17:45.961
So that's good, right?

08:17:45.961 --> 08:17:48.081
I might not be as comfortable
with nested loops.

08:17:48.081 --> 08:17:49.081
This is nice and simple.

08:17:49.081 --> 08:17:52.021
What does this loop do
here on line 17 through 20?

08:17:52.021 --> 08:17:57.901
It literally prints n hashes by
counting from i equals 0 on up to

08:17:57.901 --> 08:17:59.491
but not through n.

08:17:59.491 --> 08:18:02.291
So that's sort of week one style syntax.

08:18:02.291 --> 08:18:05.101
But this is kind of trippy
now because I've somehow

08:18:05.101 --> 08:18:09.841
boiled down the implementation of
draw into printing a row after just

08:18:09.841 --> 08:18:11.611
drawing the thing above it.

08:18:11.611 --> 08:18:15.611
But this is problematic as
is because in this case,

08:18:15.611 --> 08:18:22.261
my drawer function, notice, is always
going to call the draw function forever

08:18:22.261 --> 08:18:23.251
in some sense.

08:18:23.251 --> 08:18:28.561
But ideally, when do I want
this cyclical process to stop?

08:18:28.561 --> 08:18:32.371
When do I want to not call draw anymore?

08:18:32.371 --> 08:18:35.658
Yeah, when n is 1, right?

08:18:35.658 --> 08:18:37.741
When I get to the top of
the pyramid, when n is 1,

08:18:37.741 --> 08:18:40.081
or heck, when the pyramids
all gone and n equals 0.

08:18:40.081 --> 08:18:42.421
I can pick any line in
the sand, so long as it's

08:18:42.421 --> 08:18:44.051
sort of at the end of the process.

08:18:44.051 --> 08:18:45.841
Then I don't want to call draw anymore.

08:18:45.841 --> 08:18:48.211
So maybe what I should do is this.

08:18:48.211 --> 08:18:54.811
If n equals equals 0, there's
really nothing to draw.

08:18:54.811 --> 08:18:58.701
So I'm just going to go
ahead and return like this.

08:18:58.701 --> 08:19:01.281
Otherwise, I'm going
to go ahead and draw

08:19:01.281 --> 08:19:04.599
n minus 1 rows and then one more row.

08:19:04.599 --> 08:19:06.141
And I could express this differently.

08:19:06.141 --> 08:19:08.901
I could do something like this,
which would be equivalent.

08:19:08.901 --> 08:19:13.851
I could say something like if n
is greater than or equal to 0,

08:19:13.851 --> 08:19:15.823
then go ahead and draw the row.

08:19:15.823 --> 08:19:17.031
But I like it this way first.

08:19:17.031 --> 08:19:18.948
For now, I'm going to
go with the original way

08:19:18.948 --> 08:19:22.101
just to ask a simple question and
then just bail out of the function

08:19:22.101 --> 08:19:23.331
if n equals 0.

08:19:23.331 --> 08:19:26.101
And heck, just to be
super safe, just in case

08:19:26.101 --> 08:19:28.401
the user types in a
negative number, let me also

08:19:28.401 --> 08:19:31.341
just check if n is a negative number,
also, just return immediately.

08:19:31.341 --> 08:19:32.571
Don't do anything.

08:19:32.571 --> 08:19:35.851
I'm not returning a value because
again, the function is void.

08:19:35.851 --> 08:19:38.041
It doesn't need or have a return value.

08:19:38.041 --> 08:19:40.221
So just saying return suffices.

08:19:40.221 --> 08:19:45.021
But if n equals 1 or 2
or 3 or anything higher,

08:19:45.021 --> 08:19:50.481
it is reasonable to draw a pyramid of
slightly shorter height like, instead

08:19:50.481 --> 08:19:55.041
of 4, 3, and then go ahead
and print one more row.

08:19:55.041 --> 08:20:00.811
So this is an example now of code
that calls itself within itself.

08:20:00.811 --> 08:20:02.511
Draw is calling draw.

08:20:02.511 --> 08:20:07.281
But this so-called base case
ensures, this conditional ensures,

08:20:07.281 --> 08:20:09.051
that we're not going to do this forever.

08:20:09.051 --> 08:20:11.661
Otherwise, we literally would
do this infinitely many times,

08:20:11.661 --> 08:20:14.691
and something bad is
probably going to happen.

08:20:14.691 --> 08:20:18.591
All right, let me go ahead and
compile this code-- make recursion.

08:20:18.591 --> 08:20:22.611
OK, no syntax errors-- dot slash
recursion, Enter, height of 4,

08:20:22.611 --> 08:20:24.951
and voila.

08:20:24.951 --> 08:20:28.761
If only because some of you have run
into this issue accidentally already,

08:20:28.761 --> 08:20:32.781
let me get rid of the base case
here, and let me recompile the code.

08:20:32.781 --> 08:20:34.341
Make recursion.

08:20:34.341 --> 08:20:36.511
Oh, and actually, now
it's actually catching it.

08:20:36.511 --> 08:20:39.411
So the compiler is smart
enough here to realize

08:20:39.411 --> 08:20:42.831
that all paths through this
function will call itself.

08:20:42.831 --> 08:20:45.661
AKA, It's going to loop forever.

08:20:45.661 --> 08:20:47.451
So let me do the first thing.

08:20:47.451 --> 08:20:49.911
Suppose I only check for n equaling 0.

08:20:49.911 --> 08:20:53.751
Let me go ahead and recompile
this code with make recursion.

08:20:53.751 --> 08:20:56.391
And now let me just be
kind of uncooperative.

08:20:56.391 --> 08:21:00.561
When I run this program, still
works for 4, still works for 0.

08:21:00.561 --> 08:21:03.471
What if I do like negative 100?

08:21:03.471 --> 08:21:07.281
Have any of you experienced a
segmentation fault or core dump?

08:21:07.281 --> 08:21:08.601
OK, so no shame in this.

08:21:08.601 --> 08:21:13.341
Like, this means I have somehow
touched memory that I shouldn't have.

08:21:13.341 --> 08:21:17.271
And in short, I actually called
this function thousands of times

08:21:17.271 --> 08:21:19.911
accidentally, it would seem
now, until the program just

08:21:19.911 --> 08:21:22.774
bailed on me because I eventually
touched memory in the computer

08:21:22.774 --> 08:21:23.691
that I shouldn't have.

08:21:23.691 --> 08:21:25.501
That'll make even more sense next week.

08:21:25.501 --> 08:21:27.001
But for now, it's simply a bug.

08:21:27.001 --> 08:21:28.791
And I can avoid that
bug in this context,

08:21:28.791 --> 08:21:33.261
probably not your own pset context,
by just making sure we don't even

08:21:33.261 --> 08:21:35.701
allow for negative numbers at all.

08:21:35.701 --> 08:21:38.061
So with this building
block in place, what

08:21:38.061 --> 08:21:41.781
can we now do in terms of
those same numbers to sort?

08:21:41.781 --> 08:21:44.751
Well, it turns out there's a
sorting algorithm called merge sort.

08:21:44.751 --> 08:21:46.341
And there's bunches of others too.

08:21:46.341 --> 08:21:51.284
But merge sort is a nice one to discuss
because it fundamentally, we hope,

08:21:51.284 --> 08:21:53.451
is going to do better than
selection sort and bubble

08:21:53.451 --> 08:21:55.851
sort that is better than n squared.

08:21:55.851 --> 08:21:58.599
But the catch is it's a
little harder to think about.

08:21:58.599 --> 08:22:01.641
In fact, I'll act it out myself with
just these numbers on the shelf here

08:22:01.641 --> 08:22:05.721
rather than humans because recursion
in general takes a little bit of effort

08:22:05.721 --> 08:22:08.061
to wrap your mind around,
typically a bit of practice.

08:22:08.061 --> 08:22:10.269
But I'll see if we can't
walk through it methodically

08:22:10.269 --> 08:22:12.621
enough such that this comes to light.

08:22:12.621 --> 08:22:16.821
So here's the pseudo code I propose
for this algorithm called merge sort.

08:22:16.821 --> 08:22:20.001
In the spirit of recursion,
this sorting algorithm

08:22:20.001 --> 08:22:25.381
literally calls itself by using
the verb sort in its pseudo code.

08:22:25.381 --> 08:22:26.961
So how does merge sort work?

08:22:26.961 --> 08:22:30.381
It sort of obnoxiously says, well, if
you want to sort all of these things,

08:22:30.381 --> 08:22:33.081
go sort the left half, then
go sort the right half,

08:22:33.081 --> 08:22:34.791
and then merge the two together.

08:22:34.791 --> 08:22:36.043
Now obnoxious in what sense?

08:22:36.043 --> 08:22:38.751
Well, if I just asked you to sort
something and you just tell me,

08:22:38.751 --> 08:22:40.431
well, go sort that
thing and then go sort

08:22:40.431 --> 08:22:43.098
that thing, what was the point
of asking you in the first place?

08:22:43.098 --> 08:22:45.441
But the key is that
each of these lines is

08:22:45.441 --> 08:22:48.241
sorting a smaller piece of the problem.

08:22:48.241 --> 08:22:50.811
So eventually, we'll be
able to pare this down

08:22:50.811 --> 08:22:54.771
into something that doesn't go on
forever because in fact, in merge sort,

08:22:54.771 --> 08:22:56.481
there's a base case too.

08:22:56.481 --> 08:22:59.301
There's a scenario where we
just check, wait a minute,

08:22:59.301 --> 08:23:01.881
if there's only one
number to sort, that's it.

08:23:01.881 --> 08:23:03.831
Quit then because you're all done.

08:23:03.831 --> 08:23:06.951
So there has to be this base
case in any use of recursion

08:23:06.951 --> 08:23:11.451
to make sure that you don't
mindlessly call yourself forever.

08:23:11.451 --> 08:23:13.741
You've got to stop at some point.

08:23:13.741 --> 08:23:16.921
So let's focus on the
third of these steps.

08:23:16.921 --> 08:23:21.689
What does it mean to merge two
lists, two halves of a list,

08:23:21.689 --> 08:23:23.481
just because this is
apparently going to be

08:23:23.481 --> 08:23:25.651
a key ingredient-- so
here, for instance,

08:23:25.651 --> 08:23:28.251
are two halves of a list of size 8.

08:23:28.251 --> 08:23:31.371
We have the numbers 2-- and I'll call
it out if you're at a bad angle--

08:23:31.371 --> 08:23:36.561
2457 and 0136.

08:23:36.561 --> 08:23:41.071
Notice that the left half at the
moment, 2457, is already sorted,

08:23:41.071 --> 08:23:45.361
and the right half, 0136,
is also sorted as well.

08:23:45.361 --> 08:23:48.231
So that's a good thing because
it means that theoretically, I've

08:23:48.231 --> 08:23:49.641
sorted the left half already.

08:23:49.641 --> 08:23:51.981
I've sorted the right half
already before we began.

08:23:51.981 --> 08:23:54.051
I just need to merge these two halves.

08:23:54.051 --> 08:23:56.161
What does it mean to sort two halves?

08:23:56.161 --> 08:23:57.911
Well, for the sake of
discussion, I'm just

08:23:57.911 --> 08:24:03.701
going to turn over most of the numbers
except for the first numbers in each

08:24:03.701 --> 08:24:04.721
of these halves.

08:24:04.721 --> 08:24:07.751
There's two halves here, left and right.

08:24:07.751 --> 08:24:09.821
At the moment, I'm
only going to consider

08:24:09.821 --> 08:24:13.893
the leftmost element of each half--
that is, the one on the left here

08:24:13.893 --> 08:24:15.101
and the one on the left here.

08:24:15.101 --> 08:24:18.161
How do I merge these two lists together?

08:24:18.161 --> 08:24:22.901
Well, if I look at 2 and I look at 0,
which one should presumably come first?

08:24:22.901 --> 08:24:23.863
The smaller one.

08:24:23.863 --> 08:24:25.571
So I'm going to grab
the 0, and I'm going

08:24:25.571 --> 08:24:28.511
to put it into its own place
on this new shelf here.

08:24:28.511 --> 08:24:34.661
And now I'm going to consider,
as part of my iteration,

08:24:34.661 --> 08:24:37.631
the beginning of this list and
the new beginning of this list.

08:24:37.631 --> 08:24:39.491
So I'm now comparing 2 and 1.

08:24:39.491 --> 08:24:40.571
Which one's smaller?

08:24:40.571 --> 08:24:42.761
I'm going to go ahead and grab the 1.

08:24:42.761 --> 08:24:45.491
Now I'm going to compare the
beginning of the left list

08:24:45.491 --> 08:24:47.801
and the new beginning of
the right list, 2 and 3.

08:24:47.801 --> 08:24:49.379
Of course, it's 2.

08:24:49.379 --> 08:24:51.671
Now I'm going to compare the
beginning of the left list

08:24:51.671 --> 08:24:53.651
and the beginning of
the right list, 4 and 3.

08:24:53.651 --> 08:24:55.871
It's of course 3.

08:24:55.871 --> 08:24:58.611
Now I'm going to compare the 4
against the beginning and end,

08:24:58.611 --> 08:25:00.191
it turns out, of the second list--

08:25:00.191 --> 08:25:01.349
4, of course.

08:25:01.349 --> 08:25:03.641
Now I'm going to compare the
beginning of the left list

08:25:03.641 --> 08:25:05.183
and the beginning of the right list--

08:25:05.183 --> 08:25:06.791
5, of course.

08:25:06.791 --> 08:25:09.371
I'm realizing this is not going
to end well because I left

08:25:09.371 --> 08:25:10.781
too much distance between the numbers.

08:25:10.781 --> 08:25:12.698
But that has nothing to
do with the algorithm.

08:25:12.698 --> 08:25:14.201
7 is the beginning of the left list.

08:25:14.201 --> 08:25:15.821
6 is the beginning of the right list.

08:25:15.821 --> 08:25:17.321
It's, of course, 6.

08:25:17.321 --> 08:25:20.771
And at the risk of
knocking all of these over,

08:25:20.771 --> 08:25:27.671
if I now make room for this
element, we have hopefully

08:25:27.671 --> 08:25:34.761
sorted the whole thing by having merged
together the two halves of the list.

08:25:34.761 --> 08:25:36.401
So in short-- thank you.

08:25:40.809 --> 08:25:43.101
I'm a little worried that's
just getting sarcastic now,

08:25:43.101 --> 08:25:48.231
but we now have merged two half lists.

08:25:48.231 --> 08:25:51.791
We haven't done the guts of the
algorithm yet-- sort the left half

08:25:51.791 --> 08:25:52.791
and sort the right half.

08:25:52.791 --> 08:25:55.521
But I claim that that
is how mechanically you

08:25:55.521 --> 08:25:57.321
merge two sorted halves.

08:25:57.321 --> 08:25:59.421
You keep looking at the
beginning of each list,

08:25:59.421 --> 08:26:02.001
and you just kind of
weave them together based

08:26:02.001 --> 08:26:05.431
on which one belongs
first based on its size.

08:26:05.431 --> 08:26:07.821
So if you agree that
that was a reasonable way

08:26:07.821 --> 08:26:12.381
to merge two lists together,
let's go ahead and focus lastly

08:26:12.381 --> 08:26:15.621
on what it means to
actually sort the left half

08:26:15.621 --> 08:26:17.798
and sort the right half of
a whole bunch of numbers.

08:26:17.798 --> 08:26:19.881
And for this, I'm going
to go ahead and order them

08:26:19.881 --> 08:26:21.681
in this seemingly random order.

08:26:21.681 --> 08:26:24.561
And I just have a little cheat
sheet above so that I don't mess up.

08:26:24.561 --> 08:26:26.691
And I'm going to start at
the very top this time.

08:26:26.691 --> 08:26:29.751
And hopefully, these will
not fall down at any point.

08:26:29.751 --> 08:26:35.661
But I'm just deliberately putting
them in this random order, 5274.

08:26:35.661 --> 08:26:38.064
And then we have 1630--

08:26:38.064 --> 08:26:41.361
1630.

08:26:41.361 --> 08:26:43.701
Hopefully this won't fall over.

08:26:43.701 --> 08:26:48.351
Here is now an array of
size 8 with eight integers.

08:26:48.351 --> 08:26:49.651
And I want to sort this.

08:26:49.651 --> 08:26:52.693
I could use selection sort and just
go back and forth and back and forth.

08:26:52.693 --> 08:26:55.461
I could use bubble sort and just
compare pairs, pairs, pairs.

08:26:55.461 --> 08:26:58.191
But those are going to be on
the order of big O of n squared.

08:26:58.191 --> 08:27:00.361
My hope is to do
fundamentally better here.

08:27:00.361 --> 08:27:02.121
So let's see if we can do better.

08:27:02.121 --> 08:27:04.161
All right, so let me
look now at my code.

08:27:04.161 --> 08:27:05.851
I'll keep it on the screen.

08:27:05.851 --> 08:27:07.671
How do I implement merge sort?

08:27:07.671 --> 08:27:09.664
Well, if there's only
one number, I quit.

08:27:09.664 --> 08:27:10.581
There's obviously not.

08:27:10.581 --> 08:27:12.631
There's eight numbers,
so that's not applicable.

08:27:12.631 --> 08:27:14.964
I'm going to go ahead and
sort the left half of numbers.

08:27:14.964 --> 08:27:16.821
All right, here's the left half--

08:27:16.821 --> 08:27:18.831
5274.

08:27:18.831 --> 08:27:21.591
Do I sort an array of size 4?

08:27:21.591 --> 08:27:24.531
Well, here's where the
recursion kicks in.

08:27:24.531 --> 08:27:26.511
How do you sort a list of size 4?

08:27:26.511 --> 08:27:28.671
Well, there's the pseudo
code on the board.

08:27:28.671 --> 08:27:31.851
I sort the left half
of the list of size 4.

08:27:31.851 --> 08:27:33.561
So here we go.

08:27:33.561 --> 08:27:34.911
I have a list of size 4.

08:27:34.911 --> 08:27:35.751
How do I sort it?

08:27:35.751 --> 08:27:37.041
I sort the left half.

08:27:37.041 --> 08:27:38.841
All right, now I have a list of size 2.

08:27:38.841 --> 08:27:41.041
How do I sort this?

08:27:41.041 --> 08:27:42.961
Well, sort the left half.

08:27:42.961 --> 08:27:43.881
So here we go.

08:27:43.881 --> 08:27:45.381
Here's a list of size 1.

08:27:45.381 --> 08:27:48.357
How do I sort this?

08:27:48.357 --> 08:27:50.001
I think it's done, right?

08:27:50.001 --> 08:27:50.841
That's quit, right?

08:27:50.841 --> 08:27:52.371
If only one number, I'm done.

08:27:52.371 --> 08:27:53.594
The 5 is sorted.

08:27:53.594 --> 08:27:55.011
All right, what was the next step?

08:27:55.011 --> 08:27:56.541
You have to now rewind in time.

08:27:56.541 --> 08:28:00.871
I just sorted the left half of
the left half of the left half.

08:28:00.871 --> 08:28:02.331
What do I now sort?

08:28:02.331 --> 08:28:05.211
The right half, which is 2.

08:28:05.211 --> 08:28:06.391
This is one element.

08:28:06.391 --> 08:28:07.101
So I'm done.

08:28:07.101 --> 08:28:11.421
So now at this point in the story,
I have sorted, sort of idiotically--

08:28:11.421 --> 08:28:14.211
the 5 assorted, and the 2 is sorted.

08:28:14.211 --> 08:28:18.771
But what's the third and final step
of this phase of the algorithm?

08:28:18.771 --> 08:28:20.011
Merge the two together.

08:28:20.011 --> 08:28:21.961
So here's the left,
here's the right list.

08:28:21.961 --> 08:28:23.211
How do I merge these together?

08:28:23.211 --> 08:28:25.891
I compare the lists,
and I put the two there.

08:28:25.891 --> 08:28:27.951
I only have the [? 5 ?]
left, and I do that.

08:28:27.951 --> 08:28:30.351
So now we see some visible progress.

08:28:30.351 --> 08:28:31.551
But again, let's rewind.

08:28:31.551 --> 08:28:32.581
How did we get here?

08:28:32.581 --> 08:28:37.221
We started to sort the left half of
the left half of the left half, then

08:28:37.221 --> 08:28:38.061
the right half.

08:28:38.061 --> 08:28:39.261
And now where are we?

08:28:39.261 --> 08:28:42.451
We've just sorted the left
half of the left half.

08:28:42.451 --> 08:28:45.501
So what comes after sorting
the left half of anything?

08:28:45.501 --> 08:28:46.053
Right half.

08:28:46.053 --> 08:28:48.261
All right, here's the sort
of same nonsensical thing.

08:28:48.261 --> 08:28:49.821
Here's a list of size 2.

08:28:49.821 --> 08:28:51.051
Let's sort the left half.

08:28:51.051 --> 08:28:51.708
Done.

08:28:51.708 --> 08:28:52.791
Let's sort the right half.

08:28:52.791 --> 08:28:53.481
Done.

08:28:53.481 --> 08:28:54.531
What's the third step?

08:28:54.531 --> 08:28:55.951
Merge them together.

08:28:55.951 --> 08:28:59.241
So that's the 4, and that's the 7.

08:28:59.241 --> 08:29:00.471
What have I now done?

08:29:00.471 --> 08:29:05.431
In total, I've now sorted the
left half of the original thing.

08:29:05.431 --> 08:29:07.453
So what happens next?

08:29:07.453 --> 08:29:08.661
Wait a minute, wait a minute.

08:29:08.661 --> 08:29:09.921
I have not done that.

08:29:09.921 --> 08:29:10.911
What have I done?

08:29:10.911 --> 08:29:13.581
I have sorted the left
half of the left half,

08:29:13.581 --> 08:29:16.711
and I've sorted the right
half of the left half.

08:29:16.711 --> 08:29:19.071
What do I now need to do lastly?

08:29:19.071 --> 08:29:21.001
Merge those two lists together.

08:29:21.001 --> 08:29:22.923
So again, I put my
finger on the beginning

08:29:22.923 --> 08:29:24.631
of this list, the
beginning of this list.

08:29:24.631 --> 08:29:26.721
And if you want, I'll do the same
thing when I merged last time

08:29:26.721 --> 08:29:28.191
to be clear what I'm comparing.

08:29:28.191 --> 08:29:30.801
2 and 4-- the 2 obviously comes first.

08:29:30.801 --> 08:29:32.581
What comes next?

08:29:32.581 --> 08:29:34.771
Well, the 4 comes next.

08:29:34.771 --> 08:29:36.031
What comes next?

08:29:36.031 --> 08:29:40.281
The 5 comes next and then
lastly, of course, the 7.

08:29:40.281 --> 08:29:44.371
Notice that the 2457 are now sorted.

08:29:44.371 --> 08:29:47.163
So the original left half is sorted.

08:29:47.163 --> 08:29:49.371
And I'll do the rest a little
faster because, my God,

08:29:49.371 --> 08:29:50.746
this feels like it takes forever.

08:29:50.746 --> 08:29:52.791
But I bet we're on to something here.

08:29:52.791 --> 08:29:54.681
What step remains next?

08:29:54.681 --> 08:29:56.931
I've just sorted the left
half of the original.

08:29:56.931 --> 08:29:58.641
Sort the right half of the original.

08:29:58.641 --> 08:29:59.661
How do I sort this?

08:29:59.661 --> 08:30:02.241
I sort the left half of the right half.

08:30:02.241 --> 08:30:03.411
How do I sort this?

08:30:03.411 --> 08:30:05.721
I sort the left half of the left half.

08:30:05.721 --> 08:30:06.681
Done.

08:30:06.681 --> 08:30:08.721
I sort the right half of the left half.

08:30:08.721 --> 08:30:09.621
Done.

08:30:09.621 --> 08:30:11.751
Now I merge the two together.

08:30:11.751 --> 08:30:14.551
The 1 comes first, the 6 comes next.

08:30:14.551 --> 08:30:18.741
Now I sort the right
half of the right half.

08:30:18.741 --> 08:30:19.551
What do I do?

08:30:19.551 --> 08:30:20.961
Sort the left half.

08:30:20.961 --> 08:30:21.561
Done.

08:30:21.561 --> 08:30:22.561
Sort the right half.

08:30:22.561 --> 08:30:23.061
Done.

08:30:23.061 --> 08:30:23.781
What do I do?

08:30:23.781 --> 08:30:25.591
Merge them together.

08:30:25.591 --> 08:30:27.441
So that's the third step of that phase.

08:30:27.441 --> 08:30:32.121
Now where are we in the stor-- oh
my God, where are we in the story?

08:30:32.121 --> 08:30:36.591
We have sorted the left
half of the right half

08:30:36.591 --> 08:30:38.541
and the right half of the right half.

08:30:38.541 --> 08:30:40.121
What comes next?

08:30:40.121 --> 08:30:40.624
Merge.

08:30:40.624 --> 08:30:42.791
So I'm going to compare,
and I'm going to move those

08:30:42.791 --> 08:30:44.951
down just to make clear
what I'm comparing,

08:30:44.951 --> 08:30:46.661
the beginning of both sublists.

08:30:46.661 --> 08:30:47.471
What comes first?

08:30:47.471 --> 08:30:48.971
Of course, the 0.

08:30:48.971 --> 08:30:51.471
What comes next?

08:30:51.471 --> 08:30:52.911
What comes next?

08:30:52.911 --> 08:30:54.701
The 1.

08:30:54.701 --> 08:30:56.091
What comes next?

08:30:56.091 --> 08:30:57.371
The 3.

08:30:57.371 --> 08:30:59.291
And then lastly comes the 6.

08:30:59.291 --> 08:31:01.241
All right, where are we in the story?

08:31:01.241 --> 08:31:03.581
We've now sorted the
left half of the original

08:31:03.581 --> 08:31:05.141
and the right half of the original.

08:31:05.141 --> 08:31:06.791
What step remains?

08:31:06.791 --> 08:31:07.391
Merge.

08:31:07.391 --> 08:31:09.431
All right, so I'm going
to make the same point.

08:31:09.431 --> 08:31:12.051
And this is actually
literally what we did earlier

08:31:12.051 --> 08:31:16.121
because I deliberately demoed those
original numbers in this order, 2

08:31:16.121 --> 08:31:16.991
and a 0.

08:31:16.991 --> 08:31:18.371
This comes out first.

08:31:18.371 --> 08:31:19.661
What comes next?

08:31:19.661 --> 08:31:20.591
2 and 1.

08:31:20.591 --> 08:31:22.251
The 1 comes out next.

08:31:22.251 --> 08:31:23.811
What comes next?

08:31:23.811 --> 08:31:25.281
The 2 comes next.

08:31:25.281 --> 08:31:26.391
What comes next?

08:31:26.391 --> 08:31:27.771
The 3 comes next.

08:31:27.771 --> 08:31:28.821
What comes next?

08:31:28.821 --> 08:31:31.511
The 4.

08:31:31.511 --> 08:31:32.951
What comes after that?

08:31:32.951 --> 08:31:35.081
The 5.

08:31:35.081 --> 08:31:36.281
What comes after that?

08:31:36.281 --> 08:31:37.061
The 6.

08:31:37.061 --> 08:31:41.121
And lastly-- this is when
we run out of memory--

08:31:41.121 --> 08:31:45.371
the 7 over there is actually in place.

08:31:45.371 --> 08:31:47.751
OK.

08:31:47.751 --> 08:31:50.228
OK, so admittedly, a
little harder to explain,

08:31:50.228 --> 08:31:52.311
and honestly, it gets a
little trippy because it's

08:31:52.311 --> 08:31:55.341
so easy to forget about
where you are in the story

08:31:55.341 --> 08:31:58.041
because we're constantly
diving into the algorithm

08:31:58.041 --> 08:31:59.611
and then backing back out of it.

08:31:59.611 --> 08:32:02.331
But in code, we could
express this pretty correctly

08:32:02.331 --> 08:32:05.361
and, it turns out, pretty
efficiently because what

08:32:05.361 --> 08:32:09.021
I was doing, even though it's
longer when I do it verbally,

08:32:09.021 --> 08:32:12.621
I was touching these elements a
minimal amount of times, right?

08:32:12.621 --> 08:32:15.891
I wasn't going back and forth, back
and forth in front of the shelf

08:32:15.891 --> 08:32:16.851
again and again.

08:32:16.851 --> 08:32:21.601
I was deliberately only ever merging
the smallest elements in each list.

08:32:21.601 --> 08:32:24.561
So every time we merge, even
though I was doing it quickly,

08:32:24.561 --> 08:32:28.731
my fingers were only touching
each of the elements once.

08:32:28.731 --> 08:32:34.513
And how many times did we divide,
divide, divide in half the list?

08:32:34.513 --> 08:32:36.471
Well, we started with
all of the elements here,

08:32:36.471 --> 08:32:37.679
and there were eight of them.

08:32:37.679 --> 08:32:41.221
And then we moved them
1, 2, 3 positions.

08:32:41.221 --> 08:32:47.781
So the height of this visualization,
if you will, is actually log n, right?

08:32:47.781 --> 08:32:50.641
If I started with 8, turns
out if you do the arithmetic,

08:32:50.641 --> 08:32:54.741
this is log n height
because 2 to the 3 is 8.

08:32:54.741 --> 08:32:57.111
But for now, just trust
that this is a log n height.

08:32:57.111 --> 08:32:58.641
And how wide is the shelf?

08:32:58.641 --> 08:33:02.631
Well, it's of width n because
there's n elements any time

08:33:02.631 --> 08:33:03.831
they were on the shelf.

08:33:03.831 --> 08:33:08.098
So technically, I was kind of
cheating this algorithm because this

08:33:08.098 --> 08:33:09.681
is the first time I've needed shelves.

08:33:09.681 --> 08:33:13.371
With the human examples, we just had the
humans, and that's it, and only eight

08:33:13.371 --> 08:33:13.881
of them.

08:33:13.881 --> 08:33:16.561
Here, I was sort of using
more and more memory.

08:33:16.561 --> 08:33:19.131
In fact, I was using like
four times as much memory

08:33:19.131 --> 08:33:21.291
even though that was just
for visualization's sake.

08:33:21.291 --> 08:33:25.401
Merge sort actually requires that you
have some spare space, an empty array

08:33:25.401 --> 08:33:28.406
to move the elements into when
you're merging them together.

08:33:28.406 --> 08:33:31.281
But if I really wanted and if I
didn't have this shelf or this shelf,

08:33:31.281 --> 08:33:33.951
honestly, I could have just gone back
and forth between the two shelves.

08:33:33.951 --> 08:33:35.611
That would have been sufficient.

08:33:35.611 --> 08:33:40.551
So merge sort uses more memory
for this merging process,

08:33:40.551 --> 08:33:43.461
but the advantage of
using more memory is

08:33:43.461 --> 08:33:49.041
that the total running time, if you can
perhaps infer from that math, is what?

08:33:49.041 --> 08:33:51.921
The big O notation for
merge sort, it turns out,

08:33:51.921 --> 08:33:54.891
is actually going to be n times log n.

08:33:54.891 --> 08:33:57.411
And even if you're a little
rusty still on your logarithms,

08:33:57.411 --> 08:34:02.751
we saw in week zero and again
today that log n is smaller than n.

08:34:02.751 --> 08:34:04.011
That's a good thing.

08:34:04.011 --> 08:34:05.241
Binary search was log n.

08:34:05.241 --> 08:34:07.611
That's faster than linear
search, which was n.

08:34:07.611 --> 08:34:13.191
So n times log n is, of course,
smaller than n times n or n squared.

08:34:13.191 --> 08:34:16.191
So it's sort of lower on this little
cheat sheet that I've been drawing,

08:34:16.191 --> 08:34:19.711
which is to suggest that it's running
time is indeed better or faster.

08:34:19.711 --> 08:34:22.701
And in fact, if we consider
the best case running time,

08:34:22.701 --> 08:34:27.006
turns out it's not quite as good
as bubble sort with omega of n,

08:34:27.006 --> 08:34:29.631
where you can just sort of abort
if you realize, wait a minute,

08:34:29.631 --> 08:34:30.681
I've done no work.

08:34:30.681 --> 08:34:35.211
Merge sort, you actually have to do that
work to get to the finish line anyway.

08:34:35.211 --> 08:34:40.531
So it's actually in omega and
ultimately theta of n log n as well.

08:34:40.531 --> 08:34:42.591
So again, a trade off
there because if you

08:34:42.591 --> 08:34:44.901
happen to have a data set
that is very often sorted,

08:34:44.901 --> 08:34:47.026
honestly, you might want
to stick with bubble sort.

08:34:47.026 --> 08:34:49.851
But in the general case,
where the data is unsorted,

08:34:49.851 --> 08:34:53.181
n log n as sounding
better than n squared.

08:34:53.181 --> 08:34:55.191
Well, what does it
actually look or feel like?

08:34:55.191 --> 08:34:58.591
Give me a moment to just change
over to our visualization here.

08:34:58.591 --> 08:35:02.571
And we'll see with this example
what merge sort looks like

08:35:02.571 --> 08:35:04.531
depicted with now these vertical bars.

08:35:04.531 --> 08:35:07.221
So same algorithm, but instead
of my numbers on shelves,

08:35:07.221 --> 08:35:12.331
here is a random array
of numbers being sorted.

08:35:12.331 --> 08:35:14.481
And you can see it being
done half at a time.

08:35:14.481 --> 08:35:18.081
And you see sort of remnants
of the previous bars.

08:35:18.081 --> 08:35:19.881
Actually, that was unfair.

08:35:19.881 --> 08:35:21.861
Let me zoom out here.

08:35:21.861 --> 08:35:26.391
Let me zoom out so you can
actually see the height here.

08:35:26.391 --> 08:35:29.091
Let me go ahead and randomize
this again and run merge sort.

08:35:29.091 --> 08:35:29.671
There we go.

08:35:29.671 --> 08:35:34.461
Now you can see the second array and
where the values are going temporarily.

08:35:34.461 --> 08:35:38.491
And even though this one looks way
more cryptic visualization-wise,

08:35:38.491 --> 08:35:40.531
it does seem to be moving faster.

08:35:40.531 --> 08:35:44.031
And it seems to be merging halves
together, and boom, it's done.

08:35:44.031 --> 08:35:48.351
So let's actually see, in conclusion,
what these algorithms compare to

08:35:48.351 --> 08:35:51.441
and consider that moving forward
as we write more and more code,

08:35:51.441 --> 08:35:54.711
the goal is, again, not just to be
correct but to be well-designed.

08:35:54.711 --> 08:35:58.181
And one measure of design is
going to indeed be efficiency.

08:35:58.181 --> 08:36:02.481
So here we have, in final, a
visualization of three algorithms--

08:36:02.481 --> 08:36:05.361
selection sort, bubble
sort, and merge sort--

08:36:05.361 --> 08:36:06.811
from top to bottom.

08:36:06.811 --> 08:36:09.981
And let's see what these algorithms
might look or sound like here.

08:36:09.981 --> 08:36:12.216
Oh, if we can dim the
lights for dramatic effect--

08:36:16.521 --> 08:36:20.331
selection's on top, bubble on
bottom, merge in the middle.

08:36:20.331 --> 08:37:17.681
[MUSIC PLAYING]

08:37:17.681 --> 08:37:55.361
