10:47:08.512 --> 10:47:12.232
Well, x is presumably at some address.

10:47:12.232 --> 10:47:15.712
Maybe it's like 0x123, as always.

10:47:15.712 --> 10:47:18.832
What then does A get
when I'm using this code?

10:47:18.832 --> 10:47:22.492
The value of A is 0x123.

10:47:22.492 --> 10:47:23.752
What is the value of B?

10:47:23.752 --> 10:47:27.022
Maybe y is that 0x456.

10:47:27.022 --> 10:47:28.012
What goes in B?

10:47:28.012 --> 10:47:33.642
Well, I'm going to put 0x456,
and the what am I going to do?

10:47:33.642 --> 10:47:35.832
Based on these three
lines of code, I'm going

10:47:35.832 --> 10:47:40.032
to store in tmp whatever is at the
address in A. What is the address in A?

10:47:40.032 --> 10:47:43.062
That's this thing here, so
I'm going to put 1 in tmp.

10:47:43.062 --> 10:47:45.612
Line two-- I'm going to go to B--

10:47:45.612 --> 10:47:48.492
all right, B is 456, so
I'm going to B and I'm

10:47:48.492 --> 10:47:53.292
going to store 2 at whatever is
at location A, and at location A

10:47:53.292 --> 10:47:56.572
is 123, so that's this,
so what am I going to do?

10:47:56.572 --> 10:47:59.262
I'm going to change this 1 to a 2.

10:47:59.262 --> 10:48:01.992
Last line of code-- get the
value of tmp, which is 1,

10:48:01.992 --> 10:48:07.092
and then put it at whatever the
location B is, so B, 456, go there

10:48:07.092 --> 10:48:11.652
and change it to be the value
of tmp, tmp, which puts 1 here.

10:48:11.652 --> 10:48:12.882
That's it for the code.

10:48:12.882 --> 10:48:14.442
There's still no return value.

10:48:14.442 --> 10:48:17.742
swap returns, which means
these three temporary variables

10:48:17.742 --> 10:48:19.452
are garbage values now.

10:48:19.452 --> 10:48:21.832
They can be reused by
subsequent function calls

10:48:21.832 --> 10:48:26.452
but now, I've actually
swapped the values of x and y.

10:48:26.452 --> 10:48:30.402
Which is to say what came as naturally
as the real world here for Mariana

10:48:30.402 --> 10:48:33.882
is not quite as simply
done in C because again,

10:48:33.882 --> 10:48:36.222
functions are isolated from each other.

10:48:36.222 --> 10:48:39.502
You can pass in values but you
get copies of those values.

10:48:39.502 --> 10:48:44.052
If you want one function to affect the
value of a variable somewhere else,

10:48:44.052 --> 10:48:47.382
you have to 1, understand
what's going on but 2,

10:48:47.382 --> 10:48:50.332
pass things in as by a pointer here.

10:48:50.332 --> 10:48:53.922
So if I go back to my code here,
I need to make a few changes now.

10:48:53.922 --> 10:48:56.022
Let me get rid of these extra printf's.

10:48:56.022 --> 10:48:58.752
Let me go in and add all these stars.

10:48:58.752 --> 10:49:02.772
So I'm dereferencing these
actual addresses here and here,

10:49:02.772 --> 10:49:05.182
and I've got to make one more change.

10:49:05.182 --> 10:49:11.742
How do I now call swap if swap is
expecting an int star and an int star?

10:49:11.742 --> 10:49:14.802
That is, the address of an int
and the address of another int.

10:49:14.802 --> 10:49:17.292
What do I change on line 11 here?

10:49:17.292 --> 10:49:19.592
Yeah.

10:49:19.592 --> 10:49:21.344
Sorry, a little louder.

10:49:21.344 --> 10:49:25.592
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

10:49:25.592 --> 10:49:28.412
DAVID J. MALAN: Sorry,
the address of operator.

10:49:28.412 --> 10:49:33.092
So up here on line 11, we do
ampersand x and ampersand y.

10:49:33.092 --> 10:49:36.362
So that yes, we're technically
passing in a copy of a value,

10:49:36.362 --> 10:49:39.242
but this time the copy we're passing
in is technically an address,

10:49:39.242 --> 10:49:42.632
and as soon as we have an address, just
like when I held up the fuzzy finger--

10:49:42.632 --> 10:49:45.932
the foamy finger-- I can point at
that address, I can go to that address

10:49:45.932 --> 10:49:49.922
and actually get a value from the
mailbox or put a value into the mailbox

10:49:49.922 --> 10:49:52.182
if I even want.

10:49:52.182 --> 10:49:56.912
So let's cross our fingers
now and do make swap, Enter.

10:49:56.912 --> 10:49:58.082
Oh my God, so many mistakes.

10:49:58.082 --> 10:50:00.242
Oh, I didn't remember
to change my prototype,

10:50:00.242 --> 10:50:03.782
so let me go way up here and
add two more stars because I

10:50:03.782 --> 10:50:05.162
made that change already.

10:50:05.162 --> 10:50:10.322
Make swap, ./swap, and viola--
now I have actually swapped.

10:50:10.322 --> 10:50:11.102
Thank you.

10:50:14.652 --> 10:50:15.192
Thank you.

10:50:15.192 --> 10:50:17.022
The two values.

10:50:17.022 --> 10:50:19.852
All right, so what more can we do here?

10:50:19.852 --> 10:50:24.822
Well, let me consider
that all this time we've

10:50:24.822 --> 10:50:29.052
been deliberately using
GetString and GetInt and GetFloat

10:50:29.052 --> 10:50:30.472
and so forth, but for a reason.

10:50:30.472 --> 10:50:33.430
These aren't just training wheels
for the sake of making things easier,

10:50:33.430 --> 10:50:36.432
they're actually in place
to make your code safer.

10:50:36.432 --> 10:50:40.872
And to illustrate this, let me go
ahead and open up one other file here.

10:50:40.872 --> 10:50:45.222
How about a file called scanf.c.

10:50:45.222 --> 10:50:48.252
It turns out that the old
school way-- the way in C,

10:50:48.252 --> 10:50:52.512
really, of getting user input,
is via functions like scanf,

10:50:52.512 --> 10:50:56.112
and let me go ahead and include
stdio.h, int main(void),

10:50:56.112 --> 10:50:59.802
and without using the CS50 library at
all for strings or for any of those

10:50:59.802 --> 10:51:00.972
get functions.

10:51:00.972 --> 10:51:03.522
Let me give myself an int called x.

10:51:03.522 --> 10:51:07.437
Let me just print out what the value of
x is, even though it's going to be a--

10:51:07.437 --> 10:51:10.722
or rather, ask the user for
the value by asking them for x.

10:51:10.722 --> 10:51:14.142
And I'm going to use a function
called scanf that's going to scan

10:51:14.142 --> 10:51:20.712
in an integer using %i, and I'm going
to store whatever the human types

10:51:20.712 --> 10:51:22.667
in at this location.

10:51:22.667 --> 10:51:25.542
And then I'm going to go ahead and,
just so we can see what happened,

10:51:25.542 --> 10:51:29.592
I'm going to print out with %i
whatever the human typed in as follows.

10:51:29.592 --> 10:51:32.682
All right, so line eight
is week 1 style code.

10:51:32.682 --> 10:51:36.352
Line five and six is week 1 style code.

10:51:36.352 --> 10:51:41.772
So the curiosity today is this new line.
scanf is another function in stdio.h,

10:51:41.772 --> 10:51:43.332
and notice what I'm doing.

10:51:43.332 --> 10:51:46.032
I'm using the same syntax
that I use for printf,

10:51:46.032 --> 10:51:49.452
which is kind of a little clue-- a
format code to tell scanf what it is I

10:51:49.452 --> 10:51:52.392
want to scan in, that is, read
from the human's keyboard--

10:51:52.392 --> 10:51:55.932
and I'm telling it where to put
whatever the human typed in.

10:51:55.932 --> 10:51:59.682
I can't just say x, because we run into
the same darn problem as with swap.

10:51:59.682 --> 10:52:02.172
I have to give a little
breadcrumb to the variable

10:52:02.172 --> 10:52:05.472
where I want scanf to
put the human's integer.

10:52:05.472 --> 10:52:08.902
And so this just tells the
computer to get an int.

10:52:08.902 --> 10:52:11.142
This is what you would have
had to type, essentially,

10:52:11.142 --> 10:52:14.052
in week 1 just to get
an int from the user,

10:52:14.052 --> 10:52:16.902
and there's a whole bunch of
things that can go wrong still,

10:52:16.902 --> 10:52:20.292
but that's the cryptic syntax we
would have had to show you in week 1.

10:52:20.292 --> 10:52:22.242
Let me go ahead and make scanf here--

10:52:22.242 --> 10:52:25.302
oops-- user error.

10:52:25.302 --> 10:52:27.252
Put the semicolon in the wrong place.

10:52:27.252 --> 10:52:29.142
Make scanf, Enter.

10:52:29.142 --> 10:52:30.642
Oh my God.

10:52:30.642 --> 10:52:32.037
Non void doesn't return a value.

10:52:35.732 --> 10:52:37.952
Oh, thank you.

10:52:37.952 --> 10:52:38.582
Strike two.

10:52:38.582 --> 10:52:39.212
OK.

10:52:39.212 --> 10:52:40.502
Make scanf.

10:52:40.502 --> 10:52:41.192
There we go.

10:52:41.192 --> 10:52:42.332
OK, so scanf--

10:52:42.332 --> 10:52:45.312
I'm going to type in a number like
50 and it just prints it back out.

10:52:45.312 --> 10:52:49.542
So that is the traditional way of
implementing something like GetInt.

10:52:49.542 --> 10:52:53.012
The problem, though, is when you
start to get into strings, things

10:52:53.012 --> 10:52:54.482
get dangerous quickly.

10:52:54.482 --> 10:52:56.650
Let me delete all of
this and give myself

10:52:56.650 --> 10:52:59.192
a string s, although wait a
minute-- we don't call it strings

10:52:59.192 --> 10:53:02.252
anymore-- char star to store a string.

10:53:02.252 --> 10:53:06.092
Then let me go ahead and just prompt the
user for a string, using just printf.

10:53:06.092 --> 10:53:10.892
Then let me go ahead and use scanf, ask
them for a string this time with %s,

10:53:10.892 --> 10:53:13.572
and store it at that address.

10:53:13.572 --> 10:53:16.112
Then let me go ahead and print
out whatever the human typed

10:53:16.112 --> 10:53:19.002
in just by using the same notation.

10:53:19.002 --> 10:53:24.152
So here, line five is the same thing
as string s, but we've taken back

10:53:24.152 --> 10:53:26.552
that layer today so it's char star s.

10:53:26.552 --> 10:53:31.352
This is just week one this is
just week one, line seven is new.

10:53:31.352 --> 10:53:37.172
scanf will also read from the human's
keyboard a string and store it at s.

10:53:37.172 --> 10:53:39.002
But that's OK, because s is an address.

10:53:39.002 --> 10:53:41.912
It's correct not to do the ampersand.

10:53:41.912 --> 10:53:42.812
It's not necessary.

10:53:42.812 --> 10:53:47.432
A string is and has always
been a char star, a.k.a string.

10:53:47.432 --> 10:53:49.452
The problem, though, arises as follows--

10:53:49.452 --> 10:53:51.772
if I do make scanf--

10:53:51.772 --> 10:53:53.272
oh my God, what did I do wrong--

10:53:53.272 --> 10:53:55.792
I can't-- OK, we have certain
defenses in place with make.

10:53:55.792 --> 10:54:02.242
Let me do clang of scanf.c, an
output of program called scanf.

10:54:02.242 --> 10:54:05.199
All right, so I'm overriding
some of our pedagogical defenses

10:54:05.199 --> 10:54:06.532
that we have in place with make.

10:54:06.532 --> 10:54:11.122
Let me now run scanf of this version,
Enter, and let me type in something

10:54:11.122 --> 10:54:15.702
like, how about hi again.

10:54:15.702 --> 10:54:18.522
So it didn't even store something
and it weirdly printed out null.

10:54:18.522 --> 10:54:22.182
This time it's in lowercase,
but that is somewhat related.

10:54:22.182 --> 10:54:26.922
What did I fundamentally
do wrong though, here?

10:54:26.922 --> 10:54:29.052
Why is this getting
more and more dangerous?

10:54:29.052 --> 10:54:30.832
And let me illustrate
the point even more.

10:54:30.832 --> 10:54:34.102
What if I type in not just something
like hello, which also doesn't work.

10:54:34.102 --> 10:54:39.942
What if I do like, hellooooo and
make a really long string, Enter--

10:54:39.942 --> 10:54:41.232
that still works.

10:54:41.232 --> 10:54:43.552
Can I do this again?

10:54:43.552 --> 10:54:45.452
Let's try again.

10:54:45.452 --> 10:54:48.632
Right, a really long,
unexpectedly long string.

10:54:48.632 --> 10:54:50.492
This is the nondeterminism kicking in.

10:54:50.492 --> 10:54:51.212
Enter.

10:54:51.212 --> 10:54:51.782
All right, damn it.

10:54:51.782 --> 10:54:53.615
I was trying to trigger
a segmentation fault

10:54:53.615 --> 10:54:56.852
but it wouldn't, but
the point still remains.

10:54:56.852 --> 10:55:01.542
It's still not working, but what's
the essence of why this isn't working,

10:55:01.542 --> 10:55:03.212
and it's not storing my actual input?

10:55:03.212 --> 10:55:04.092
Yeah.

10:55:04.092 --> 10:55:06.027
AUDIENCE: Do you have to make a space?

10:55:06.027 --> 10:55:07.902
DAVID J. MALAN: We have
to make space for it.

10:55:07.902 --> 10:55:11.142
So what we're missing here is
malloc, or something like that.

10:55:11.142 --> 10:55:14.102
So I could do that, I could
do something like this.

10:55:14.102 --> 10:55:16.802
Well, let the human type in
at least a three letter word

10:55:16.802 --> 10:55:20.942
so I could do malloc of 3
plus 1 for the null character.

10:55:20.942 --> 10:55:25.322
So let me give them four characters,
and let me go ahead and do make scanf--

10:55:25.322 --> 10:55:26.282
whoops.

10:55:26.282 --> 10:55:28.442
Nope, sorry. clang, I have to--

10:55:28.442 --> 10:55:29.082
nope.

10:55:29.082 --> 10:55:29.582
Dammit.

10:55:29.582 --> 10:55:36.172
Oh, include stdlib.h-- there we go.

10:55:36.172 --> 10:55:39.197
That gives me malloc, now I'm
going to recompile this with clang,

10:55:39.197 --> 10:55:42.322
now I'm going to rerun it, and now I'm
going to type in my first thing, hi.

10:55:42.322 --> 10:55:43.702
That now works.

10:55:43.702 --> 10:55:47.422
And let me get a little aggressive now
and type in hello, which is too long.

10:55:47.422 --> 10:55:49.462
Still works, but I'm getting lucky.

10:55:49.462 --> 10:55:53.032
Let me try a hellooooooo.

10:55:53.032 --> 10:55:55.356
Damn it, that still works, too.

10:55:55.356 --> 10:55:56.452
Sort of.

10:55:56.452 --> 10:55:58.651
But it actually-- not quite.

10:55:58.651 --> 10:56:00.772
There's some weirdness
going on there already.

10:56:00.772 --> 10:56:02.372
It turns out I can also do this.

10:56:02.372 --> 10:56:05.751
I could actually just say
char star four and give myself

10:56:05.751 --> 10:56:07.042
an array of four characters.

10:56:07.042 --> 10:56:08.462
Let me try this one more time.

10:56:08.462 --> 10:56:12.022
So let me rerun clang ./scanf.

10:56:12.022 --> 10:56:16.821
Hellooooooo, clearly exceeding
the four characters--

10:56:16.821 --> 10:56:17.452
there we go.

10:56:17.452 --> 10:56:18.441
Thank you, all right.

10:56:22.182 --> 10:56:24.703
So the point here, though, is
if we hadn't given you GetInt,

10:56:24.703 --> 10:56:27.161
you would have had to use the
scanf thing-- not a huge deal

10:56:27.161 --> 10:56:28.432
because it seemed to work.

10:56:28.432 --> 10:56:31.682
But if we hadn't given you GetString you
would have had to do stuff like this,

10:56:31.682 --> 10:56:34.842
knowing about malloc already or
knowing about strings being erased,

10:56:34.842 --> 10:56:36.911
and even now there's a danger.

10:56:36.911 --> 10:56:41.112
If the human types in five letters,
six letters, 100 letters-- this code,

10:56:41.112 --> 10:56:44.862
like with the Hello input, will
probably just crash, which is bad.

10:56:44.862 --> 10:56:46.842
So GetString also has
this functionality built

10:56:46.842 --> 10:56:49.151
in where we have a
fancy loop inside such

10:56:49.151 --> 10:56:53.682
that we allocate using malloc as
many bytes as you physically type in,

10:56:53.682 --> 10:56:55.632
and we use malloc
essentially every keystroke.

10:56:55.632 --> 10:57:00.462
The moment you type in h-e-l-l-o, we're
laying the tracks as we go and we keep

10:57:00.462 --> 10:57:04.932
allocating more and more memory so that
we theoretically will never crash with

10:57:04.932 --> 10:57:07.661
GetString even though
it's this easy to crack--

10:57:07.661 --> 10:57:10.812
this easy to crash your code
using scanf if you again

10:57:10.812 --> 10:57:13.482
did it without the help of a library.

10:57:13.482 --> 10:57:15.539
So where are we all going with this?

10:57:15.539 --> 10:57:17.622
Well, let me show you a
few final examples that'll

10:57:17.622 --> 10:57:19.962
pave the way for what
will be problem set four.

10:57:19.962 --> 10:57:23.122
Let me go ahead and open
up from today's code--

10:57:23.122 --> 10:57:25.241
which is available on
the course's website--

10:57:25.241 --> 10:57:32.202
for instance, a program like
this, called phonebook.c,

10:57:32.202 --> 10:57:34.901
and I'm just going to give
you a quick tour of it,

10:57:34.901 --> 10:57:37.863
that you'll see more details on in
the context of p-set four itself.

10:57:37.863 --> 10:57:40.571
We're going to introduce a few
new functions you're going to see.

10:57:40.571 --> 10:57:43.812
You're going to see a function called
fopen, which stands for file open,

10:57:43.812 --> 10:57:47.203
and it takes two arguments-- the
name of a file to open like a CSV

10:57:47.203 --> 10:57:50.411
that you might manipulate in Excel or
Google Spreadsheets or the like-- comma

10:57:50.411 --> 10:57:55.212
separated values, and then something
like A for append, R for read,

10:57:55.212 --> 10:57:58.151
W for write, depending on whether
you want to add to the file,

10:57:58.151 --> 10:58:00.682
just open it up, or change it.

10:58:00.682 --> 10:58:03.192
We're going to introduce
you to a file pointer.

10:58:03.192 --> 10:58:05.032
You'll see that capital file--

10:58:05.032 --> 10:58:07.632
which is a little bit
unconventional-- capital file is

10:58:07.632 --> 10:58:10.482
a pointer to an actual file
on the computer's hard drive

10:58:10.482 --> 10:58:13.001
so that you can actually access
something like a CSV file,

10:58:13.001 --> 10:58:14.352
or heck, even images.

10:58:14.352 --> 10:58:16.661
And we're going to see
down below that you're also

10:58:16.661 --> 10:58:20.411
going to have the ability to write
files as well, or print to files.

10:58:20.411 --> 10:58:24.342
You'll see functions like
printf printf for file printf.

10:58:24.342 --> 10:58:29.472
Or fwrite-- file write-- which now that
you will begin to understand pointers,

10:58:29.472 --> 10:58:33.312
you'll have the ability to
actually not only read files--

10:58:33.312 --> 10:58:36.831
text files, images, other
things-- but also write them out.

10:58:36.831 --> 10:58:42.282
In fact for instance, just as a teaser
here, JPEGs will be one of the things

10:58:42.282 --> 10:58:44.682
we focus on this week where
we give you a forensic image

10:58:44.682 --> 10:58:47.352
and your goal is to
recover as many photographs

10:58:47.352 --> 10:58:51.012
from this forensic image of a
digital camera as you possibly can.

10:58:51.012 --> 10:58:54.432
And the way you're going to do
that is by knowing in advance

10:58:54.432 --> 10:58:58.932
that every JPEG in the world starts
with these three bytes, written

10:58:58.932 --> 10:59:01.161
in hexadecimal, but these three numbers.

10:59:01.161 --> 10:59:03.882
And so in fact, just as
a teaser, let me open up

10:59:03.882 --> 10:59:07.062
an example you'll see on the
course's website for today.

10:59:07.062 --> 10:59:09.797
If I scroll through here,
you'll see a program

10:59:09.797 --> 10:59:11.422
that does a little something like this.

10:59:11.422 --> 10:59:13.572
And again, more on this--

10:59:13.572 --> 10:59:15.762
if we could hit the button--

10:59:15.762 --> 10:59:16.402
there we go.

10:59:16.402 --> 10:59:21.582
So here we have the notion of a byte
we're going to create for ourselves.

10:59:21.582 --> 10:59:24.462
We'll see a data type called byte,
which is a common convention.

10:59:24.462 --> 10:59:25.702
This gives me three bytes.

10:59:25.702 --> 10:59:28.035
And you're going to learn
about a function called fread,

10:59:28.035 --> 10:59:31.932
which reads from a file some number
of bytes-- for instance, three bytes.

10:59:31.932 --> 10:59:33.702
We might then use code like this.

10:59:33.702 --> 10:59:37.362
If bytes bracket zero
equals equals 0xFF and bytes

10:59:37.362 --> 10:59:43.122
bracket 1 equals 0xD8 and bytes bracket
2 equals 0xFF, all three of those

10:59:43.122 --> 10:59:47.842
bytes I just claimed represent a
JPEG, you'll see an output like this.

10:59:47.842 --> 10:59:51.172
Let me go ahead and run
this program as follows.

10:59:51.172 --> 10:59:55.282
Let me copy jpeg.c into my
directory from today's distribution.

10:59:55.282 --> 11:00:03.432
Let me do make jpeg, and let me run
jpeg on a file which is available online

11:00:03.432 --> 11:00:07.202
called lecture.jpeg, and I
claim yes, it's possibly a JPEG.

11:00:07.202 --> 11:00:08.202
Well, what is that file?

11:00:08.202 --> 11:00:11.842
Let me open it up for us, called
lecture.jpeg, and here, for instance,

11:00:11.842 --> 11:00:15.942
is that same photo with which we began
class, namely implemented as a JPEG.

11:00:15.942 --> 11:00:18.072
But what we're also
going to do this week

11:00:18.072 --> 11:00:22.992
is start to implement our own sort
of filters a la Instagram, whereby

11:00:22.992 --> 11:00:26.262
we might take images and actually
run them through a program that

11:00:26.262 --> 11:00:28.280
creates different versions thereof.

11:00:28.280 --> 11:00:30.072
For instance, using a
different file format

11:00:30.072 --> 11:00:33.862
called BMP, which essentially lays out
all of its pixels from left to right,

11:00:33.862 --> 11:00:35.262
top to bottom, in a grid.

11:00:35.262 --> 11:00:36.822
You're going to see a struct--

11:00:36.822 --> 11:00:38.862
a data struct in C that's
way more complicated

11:00:38.862 --> 11:00:40.992
than the candidate
structure from the past,

11:00:40.992 --> 11:00:43.227
or the person structure
from the past, that

11:00:43.227 --> 11:00:45.852
looks like this, which is just
a whole bunch more values in it,

11:00:45.852 --> 11:00:47.769
but we'll walk you through
these in the p-set.

11:00:47.769 --> 11:00:49.782
And we might take a
photograph like this and ask

11:00:49.782 --> 11:00:52.242
you to run a few different
filters on it a la Instagram,

11:00:52.242 --> 11:00:55.872
like a black and white filter,
or grayscale, a sepia filter

11:00:55.872 --> 11:00:59.892
to give it some old school feel, or
a reflection like this to invert it,

11:00:59.892 --> 11:01:02.482
or blur it, even in this way.

11:01:02.482 --> 11:01:05.472
And just to end on a note
here, I have a version

11:01:05.472 --> 11:01:08.982
of this code ready to go that doesn't
implement all of those filters,

11:01:08.982 --> 11:01:11.712
it just implements one filter initially.

11:01:11.712 --> 11:01:14.412
Let me go ahead and just ready
this on my computer here.

11:01:14.412 --> 11:01:16.467
I'm going to go into my
own version of filter

11:01:16.467 --> 11:01:18.342
and you'll see a few
files that will give you

11:01:18.342 --> 11:01:21.982
a tour of this coming week
in bitmap.h, for instance,

11:01:21.982 --> 11:01:26.872
is a version of this structure that
I claimed existed a moment ago.

11:01:26.872 --> 11:01:34.722
And let me show you this file here,
helpers.c, in which there is a function

11:01:34.722 --> 11:01:38.412
called filter that I've already
implemented in advance today.

11:01:38.412 --> 11:01:41.472
But the ones we give you for the piece
that won't already be implemented,

11:01:41.472 --> 11:01:43.847
this function called filter
takes the height of an image,

11:01:43.847 --> 11:01:46.942
the width of an image, and
a two dimensional array.

11:01:46.942 --> 11:01:49.932
So rows and columns
of pixels, and then I

11:01:49.932 --> 11:01:53.772
have a loop like this that iterates over
all of the pixels in an image from top

11:01:53.772 --> 11:01:55.402
to bottom, left to right.

11:01:55.402 --> 11:01:57.372
And then notice what
I'm going to do here.

11:01:57.372 --> 11:02:00.552
I'm going to change the blue
value to be zero in this case,

11:02:00.552 --> 11:02:02.962
and the green value to
be zero in this case.

11:02:02.962 --> 11:02:03.702
But why?

11:02:03.702 --> 11:02:07.452
Well, the image I have
here in mind is this one,

11:02:07.452 --> 11:02:10.242
whereby we have this
hidden image that simply

11:02:10.242 --> 11:02:13.512
has old school style-- a
secret message embedded in it.

11:02:13.512 --> 11:02:16.722
And if you don't happen to have in
your dorm one of these secret decoder

11:02:16.722 --> 11:02:18.942
glasses that essentially
make everything red--

11:02:18.942 --> 11:02:21.817
getting rid of the green in the
world and the blue in the world--

11:02:21.817 --> 11:02:24.192
you can actually-- I'm actually
probably the only one who

11:02:24.192 --> 11:02:26.472
can read this right
now-- see what message

11:02:26.472 --> 11:02:28.752
is hidden behind all of this red noise.

11:02:28.752 --> 11:02:34.482
But if using my code written here in
helpers.c I get rid of all the blue

11:02:34.482 --> 11:02:37.182
in the picture and I get rid of
all the green in the picture,

11:02:37.182 --> 11:02:39.792
essentially implementing
the idea of this filter--

11:02:39.792 --> 11:02:42.612
this red filter where you only see red--

11:02:42.612 --> 11:02:45.862
well, let's go ahead and
compile this program.

11:02:45.862 --> 11:02:50.832
Make filter, run ./filter
on this hidden message.bmp.

11:02:50.832 --> 11:02:53.892
I'm going to save it in a
new file called message.bmp,

11:02:53.892 --> 11:02:56.832
and with one final flourish
we're going to open up

11:02:56.832 --> 11:03:00.732
message.bmp, which is the result
of having put on these glasses,

11:03:00.732 --> 11:03:03.882
and hopefully now you
too will see what I see.

11:03:12.892 --> 11:03:14.292
All right, that's it for CS50!

11:03:14.292 --> 11:03:17.482
We'll see you next time.

11:03:17.482 --> 11:04:35.862
[MUSIC PLAYING]

11:04:35.862 --> 11:04:37.482
SPEAKER 1: All right.

11:04:37.482 --> 11:04:39.312
This is CS 50.

11:04:39.312 --> 11:04:42.222
And this is already week 5,
which means this is actually

11:04:42.222 --> 11:04:44.722
our last week in C together.

11:04:44.722 --> 11:04:48.552
In fact, in just a few days'
time, what has looked like this

11:04:48.552 --> 11:04:50.972
and much more cryptic
than this perhaps, is

11:04:50.972 --> 11:04:53.472
going to be distilled into
something much simpler next week.

11:04:53.472 --> 11:04:55.632
When we transition to a
language called Python.

11:04:55.632 --> 11:04:59.952
And with Python, we'll still have our
conditionals, and loops, and functions,

11:04:59.952 --> 11:05:00.655
and so forth.

11:05:00.655 --> 11:05:03.822
But a lot of the low-level plumbing
that you might have been wrestling with,

11:05:03.822 --> 11:05:06.502
struggling with, frustrated by,
over the past couple of weeks,

11:05:06.502 --> 11:05:08.802
especially, now that
we've introduced pointers.

11:05:08.802 --> 11:05:11.682
And it feels like you probably
have to do everything yourself.

11:05:11.682 --> 11:05:14.542
In Python, and in a lot
of higher level languages

11:05:14.542 --> 11:05:16.932
so to speak-- more modern,
more recent languages,

11:05:16.932 --> 11:05:20.022
you'll be able to do so much more
with just single lines of code.

11:05:20.022 --> 11:05:23.022
And indeed, we're going to start
leveraging libraries, all the more code

11:05:23.022 --> 11:05:24.462
that other people wrote.

11:05:24.462 --> 11:05:27.642
Frameworks, which is collections of
libraries that other people wrote.

11:05:27.642 --> 11:05:31.092
And on top of all that, will you be
able to make even better, grander, more

11:05:31.092 --> 11:05:34.692
impressive projects, that actually solve
problems of particular interest to you.

11:05:34.692 --> 11:05:37.582
Particularly, by way of
your own final project.

11:05:37.582 --> 11:05:41.082
So last week though, in week 4,
recall that we focused on memory.

11:05:41.082 --> 11:05:43.692
And we've been treating this
memory inside of your computer

11:05:43.692 --> 11:05:45.042
is like a canvas, right.

11:05:45.042 --> 11:05:48.252
At the end of the day, it's just
zeros and ones, or bytes, really.

11:05:48.252 --> 11:05:51.382
And it's really up to you
what you do with those bytes.

11:05:51.382 --> 11:05:54.882
And how you interconnect them, how
you represent information on them.

11:05:54.882 --> 11:05:56.960
And arrays, were like
one of the simplest ways.

11:05:56.960 --> 11:05:58.752
We started playing
around with that memory.

11:05:58.752 --> 11:06:00.642
Just contiguous chunks of memory.

11:06:00.642 --> 11:06:01.782
Back-to-back, to back.

11:06:01.782 --> 11:06:04.512
But let's consider, for a
moment, some of the problems that

11:06:04.512 --> 11:06:06.102
pretty quickly arise with arrays.

11:06:06.102 --> 11:06:09.672
And then, today focus on what more
generally are called data structures.

11:06:09.672 --> 11:06:14.592
Using your computer's memory as
a much more versatile canvas,

11:06:14.592 --> 11:06:16.862
to create even
two-dimensional structures.

11:06:16.862 --> 11:06:18.612
To represent information,
and, ultimately,

11:06:18.612 --> 11:06:20.692
to solve more interesting problems.

11:06:20.692 --> 11:06:22.272
So here's an array of size 3.

11:06:22.272 --> 11:06:24.072
Maybe, the size of 3 integers.

11:06:24.072 --> 11:06:26.320
And suppose that this
is inside of a program.

11:06:26.320 --> 11:06:29.112
And at this point in the story,
you've got 3 numbers in it already.

11:06:29.112 --> 11:06:30.522
1, 2 and 3.

11:06:30.522 --> 11:06:34.559
And suppose, whatever the context,
you need to now add a fourth number

11:06:34.559 --> 11:06:35.142
to this array.

11:06:35.142 --> 11:06:36.432
Like, the number 4.

11:06:36.432 --> 11:06:39.449
Well, instinctively, where
should the number 4 go?

11:06:39.449 --> 11:06:41.532
If this is your computer's
memory and we currently

11:06:41.532 --> 11:06:43.241
have this array 1, 2, 3, from what.

11:06:43.241 --> 11:06:44.592
Left to right.

11:06:44.592 --> 11:06:47.822
Where should the number 4
just, perhaps, naively go.

11:06:47.822 --> 11:06:48.822
Yeah, what do you think?

11:06:48.822 --> 11:06:49.902
AUDIENCE: Replace number 1.

11:06:49.902 --> 11:06:50.412
SPEAKER 1: Sorry?

11:06:50.412 --> 11:06:51.312
AUDIENCE: Replace number 1.

11:06:51.312 --> 11:06:52.062
SPEAKER 1: Oh, OK.

11:06:52.062 --> 11:06:53.502
So you could replace number 1.

11:06:53.502 --> 11:06:55.377
I don't really like
that, though, because I'd

11:06:55.377 --> 11:06:56.772
like to keep number 1 around.

11:06:56.772 --> 11:06:58.062
But that's an option.

11:06:58.062 --> 11:06:59.812
But I'm losing, of course, information.

11:06:59.812 --> 11:07:02.272
So what else could I do if
I want to add the number 4.

11:07:02.272 --> 11:07:02.772
Over there?

11:07:02.772 --> 11:07:04.147
AUDIENCE: On the right side of 3.

11:07:04.147 --> 11:07:04.814
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

11:07:04.814 --> 11:07:06.954
So, I mean, it feels like
if there's some ordering

11:07:06.954 --> 11:07:09.162
to these, which seems kind
of a reasonable inference,

11:07:09.162 --> 11:07:11.262
that it probably belongs
somewhere over here.

11:07:11.262 --> 11:07:14.742
But recall last week, as we started
poking around a computer's memory,

11:07:14.742 --> 11:07:16.612
there's other stuff
potentially going on.

11:07:16.612 --> 11:07:20.232
And if fill that in, ideally, we'd
want to just plop the number 4 here.

11:07:20.232 --> 11:07:22.062
If we're maintaining this kind of order.

11:07:22.062 --> 11:07:24.462
But recall in the context
of your computer's memory,

11:07:24.462 --> 11:07:25.902
there might be other stuff there.

11:07:25.902 --> 11:07:28.414
Some of these garbage
values that might be usable,

11:07:28.414 --> 11:07:30.372
but we don't really know
or care what they are.

11:07:30.372 --> 11:07:31.962
As represented by Oscar here.

11:07:31.962 --> 11:07:34.992
But there might actually
be useful data in use.

11:07:34.992 --> 11:07:38.382
Like, if your program has not
just a few integers in this array,

11:07:38.382 --> 11:07:40.512
but also a string that
says like, "Hello, world."

11:07:40.512 --> 11:07:46.572
It could be that your computer has
plopped the H-E-L-L-O W-O-R-L-D right

11:07:46.572 --> 11:07:47.692
after this array.

11:07:47.692 --> 11:07:48.192
Why?

11:07:48.192 --> 11:07:50.442
Well, maybe, you created the
array in one line of code

11:07:50.442 --> 11:07:52.092
and filled it with 1, 2, 3.

11:07:52.092 --> 11:07:54.492
Maybe the next line of
code used GET-STRING.

11:07:54.492 --> 11:07:57.712
Or maybe just hard coded a string
in your code for "Hello, world."

11:07:57.712 --> 11:08:00.459
And so you painted yourself
into a corner, so to speak.

11:08:00.459 --> 11:08:03.042
Now I think you might claim,
well, let's just overwrite the H.

11:08:03.042 --> 11:08:04.992
But that's problematic
for the same reasons.

11:08:04.992 --> 11:08:06.712
We don't want to do that.

11:08:06.712 --> 11:08:09.612
So where else could the 4 go?

11:08:09.612 --> 11:08:12.852
Or how do we solve this problem
if we want to add a number,

11:08:12.852 --> 11:08:14.562
and there's clearly memory available.

11:08:14.562 --> 11:08:17.952
Because those garbage values are junk
that we don't care about anymore.

11:08:17.952 --> 11:08:20.082
So we could certainly reuse those.

11:08:20.082 --> 11:08:23.722
Where could the 4, and
perhaps this whole array, go?

11:08:23.722 --> 11:08:24.222
OK.

11:08:24.222 --> 11:08:26.052
So I'm hearing we could
move it somewhere.

11:08:26.052 --> 11:08:27.885
Maybe, replace some of
those garbage values.

11:08:27.885 --> 11:08:29.902
And honestly, we have a lot of options.

11:08:29.902 --> 11:08:32.142
We could use any of these
garbage values up here.

11:08:32.142 --> 11:08:34.882
We could use any of these down
here, or even further down.

11:08:34.882 --> 11:08:38.442
The point is there is plenty
of memory available as

11:08:38.442 --> 11:08:41.892
indicated by these Oscars, where
we could put 4, maybe even, 5,

11:08:41.892 --> 11:08:43.272
6 or more integers.

11:08:43.272 --> 11:08:46.452
The catch is that we
chose poorly early on.

11:08:46.452 --> 11:08:47.532
Or we just got unlucky.

11:08:47.532 --> 11:08:51.168
And 1, 2, 3 ended up back-to-back with
some other data that we care about.

11:08:51.168 --> 11:08:52.251
All right, so that's fine.

11:08:52.251 --> 11:08:55.061
Let's go ahead and assume that
we'll abstract away everything else.

11:08:55.061 --> 11:08:58.227
And we'll plop the new
array in this location here.

11:08:58.227 --> 11:09:00.102
So I'm going to go ahead
and copy the 1 over.

11:09:00.102 --> 11:09:01.002
The 2 over.

11:09:01.002 --> 11:09:01.902
The 3 over.

11:09:01.902 --> 11:09:04.634
And then, ultimately, once
I'm ready to fill the 4,

11:09:04.634 --> 11:09:07.092
I can throw away, essentially,
the old array at this point.

11:09:07.092 --> 11:09:09.102
Because I have it now
entirely in duplicate.

11:09:09.102 --> 11:09:11.242
And I can populate it with the number 4.

11:09:11.242 --> 11:09:11.742
All right.

11:09:11.742 --> 11:09:12.612
So problem solved.

11:09:12.612 --> 11:09:15.582
That is a correct potential
solution to this problem.

11:09:15.582 --> 11:09:16.665
But, what's the trade off?

11:09:16.665 --> 11:09:19.624
And this is something we're going to
start thinking about all the more.

11:09:19.624 --> 11:09:22.302
What's the downside of having
solved this problem in this way?

11:09:22.302 --> 11:09:23.897
Yeah.

11:09:23.897 --> 11:09:25.272
I'm adding a lot of running time.

11:09:25.272 --> 11:09:28.062
It took me a lot of effort to
copy those additional numbers.

11:09:28.062 --> 11:09:29.502
Now, granted, it's a small array.

11:09:29.502 --> 11:09:30.502
3 numbers, who cares.

11:09:30.502 --> 11:09:32.377
It's going to be over
in the blink of an eye.

11:09:32.377 --> 11:09:35.062
But if we start talking
about interesting data sets,

11:09:35.062 --> 11:09:37.672
web application data sets,
mobile app data sets.

11:09:37.672 --> 11:09:41.152
Where you have not just a few, but
maybe a few hundred, few thousand,

11:09:41.152 --> 11:09:43.112
a few million pieces of data.

11:09:43.112 --> 11:09:46.252
This is probably a suboptimal
solution to just, oh,

11:09:46.252 --> 11:09:48.234
move all your data from
one place to another.

11:09:48.234 --> 11:09:49.942
Because who's to say
that we're not going

11:09:49.942 --> 11:09:51.532
to paint ourselves into a new corner.

11:09:51.532 --> 11:09:54.742
And it would feel like you're wasting
all of this time moving stuff around.

11:09:54.742 --> 11:09:58.592
And, ultimately, just costing
yourself a huge amount of time.

11:09:58.592 --> 11:10:01.612
In fact, if we put this now into
the context of our Big O notation

11:10:01.612 --> 11:10:06.532
from a few weeks back, what might
the running time now of Search

11:10:06.532 --> 11:10:07.642
be for an array?

11:10:07.642 --> 11:10:08.752
Let's start simple.

11:10:08.752 --> 11:10:10.912
A throwback a couple of weeks ago.

11:10:10.912 --> 11:10:14.062
If you're using an array, to
recap, what was the running time

11:10:14.062 --> 11:10:17.072
of a Search algorithm in Big O notation?

11:10:17.072 --> 11:10:19.252
So, maybe, in the worst case.

11:10:19.252 --> 11:10:23.032
If you've got n numbers, 3 in this
case or 4, but n more generally.

11:10:23.032 --> 11:10:25.802
Big O of what for Search?

11:10:25.802 --> 11:10:26.302
Yeah.

11:10:26.302 --> 11:10:26.902
What do you think?

11:10:26.902 --> 11:10:27.532
AUDIENCE: Big O of n.

11:10:27.532 --> 11:10:28.582
SPEAKER 1: Big O of n.

11:10:28.582 --> 11:10:30.202
And what's your intuition for that?

11:10:30.202 --> 11:10:31.627
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

11:10:35.969 --> 11:10:36.552
SPEAKER 1: OK.

11:10:36.552 --> 11:10:36.792
Yeah.

11:10:36.792 --> 11:10:39.584
So if we go through each element,
for instance, from left to right,

11:10:39.584 --> 11:10:42.972
then Search is going to take
this a Big O running time.

11:10:42.972 --> 11:10:46.002
If, though, we're talking about
these numbers, specifically.

11:10:46.002 --> 11:10:48.972
And now I'll explicitly stipulate
that, yeah, they're sorted.

11:10:48.972 --> 11:10:50.142
Does that buy us anything?

11:10:50.142 --> 11:10:54.432
What would the Big O notation be
for Searching an array in this case,

11:10:54.432 --> 11:10:56.922
be it of size 3, or 4,
or n, more generally.

11:10:56.922 --> 11:10:57.972
AUDIENCE: Big O of n.

11:10:57.972 --> 11:10:59.772
SPEAKER 1: Big O of, not n, but rather?

11:10:59.772 --> 11:11:00.162
AUDIENCE: Log n.

11:11:00.162 --> 11:11:01.182
SPEAKER 1: Log n, right.

11:11:01.182 --> 11:11:05.190
Because we could use per week zero
binary search on an array like this,

11:11:05.190 --> 11:11:06.732
we'd have to deal with some rounding.

11:11:06.732 --> 11:11:08.922
Because there's not a perfect
number of elements at the moment.

11:11:08.922 --> 11:11:10.332
But you could use binary search.

11:11:10.332 --> 11:11:11.652
Go to the middle roughly.

11:11:11.652 --> 11:11:13.392
And then go left or
right, left or right,

11:11:13.392 --> 11:11:15.142
until you find the
element you care about.

11:11:15.142 --> 11:11:19.302
So Search remains in Big O
of log n when using arrays.

11:11:19.302 --> 11:11:21.132
But what about insertion, now?

11:11:21.132 --> 11:11:23.172
If we start to think
about other operations.

11:11:23.172 --> 11:11:26.862
Like, adding a number to this array,
or adding a friend to your contacts

11:11:26.862 --> 11:11:29.532
app, or Google finding
another page on the internet.

11:11:29.532 --> 11:11:31.992
So insertion happens all the time.

11:11:31.992 --> 11:11:34.812
What's the running time of Insert?

11:11:34.812 --> 11:11:38.112
When it comes to inserting into
an existing array of size n.

11:11:38.112 --> 11:11:40.782
How many steps might that take?

11:11:40.782 --> 11:11:41.652
Big O of n.

11:11:41.652 --> 11:11:42.702
It would be, indeed, n.

11:11:42.702 --> 11:11:43.202
Why?

11:11:43.202 --> 11:11:46.062
Because in the worst case,
where you're out of space,

11:11:46.062 --> 11:11:48.630
you have to allocate, it
would seem, a new array.

11:11:48.630 --> 11:11:50.922
Maybe, taking over some of
the previous garbage values.

11:11:50.922 --> 11:11:52.662
But the catch is, even
though you're only

11:11:52.662 --> 11:11:55.032
inserting one new number,
like the number 4,

11:11:55.032 --> 11:11:58.552
you have to copy over all the darn
existing numbers into the new one.

11:11:58.552 --> 11:12:01.542
So if your original array of
size n, the copying of that

11:12:01.542 --> 11:12:03.412
is going to take Big O of n plus 1.

11:12:03.412 --> 11:12:06.412
But we can throw away the plus 1
because of the math we did in the past.

11:12:06.412 --> 11:12:09.342
So Insert now becomes Big O of n.

11:12:09.342 --> 11:12:11.202
And that might not be ideal.

11:12:11.202 --> 11:12:13.992
Because if you're in the habit
of inserting things frequently,

11:12:13.992 --> 11:12:16.362
that could start to add
up, and add up, and add up.

11:12:16.362 --> 11:12:19.302
And this is why computer programs,
and websites, and mobile apps

11:12:19.302 --> 11:12:20.472
could be slow.

11:12:20.472 --> 11:12:23.482
If you're not being mindful
of these trade offs.

11:12:23.482 --> 11:12:27.492
So what about, just for good
measure, Omega notation.

11:12:27.492 --> 11:12:28.752
And maybe, the best case.

11:12:28.752 --> 11:12:31.242
Well just to recap
here, we could get lucky

11:12:31.242 --> 11:12:33.534
