11:23:43.012 --> 11:23:44.500
No leaks are possible.

11:23:44.500 --> 11:23:47.542
So this is perhaps the best output
you can see from a tool like Valgrind.

11:23:47.542 --> 11:23:50.432
I used the heap, but I freed
all the memory as well.

11:23:50.432 --> 11:23:52.112
So there were 2 fixes needed there.

11:23:52.112 --> 11:23:52.612
All right.

11:23:52.612 --> 11:23:56.392
Any questions then on this array-based
approach, the first of which

11:23:56.392 --> 11:23:59.012
is statically allocating
an array, so to speak.

11:23:59.012 --> 11:24:00.712
By just hard coding the number 3.

11:24:00.712 --> 11:24:04.672
The second version now is
dynamically allocating the array,

11:24:04.672 --> 11:24:06.862
using not the stack but the heap.

11:24:06.862 --> 11:24:10.282
But, it too, suffers from the
slowness we described earlier,

11:24:10.282 --> 11:24:12.772
of having to copy all those
values from one to the other.

11:24:12.772 --> 11:24:13.272
OK.

11:24:13.272 --> 11:24:14.665
A hand was over here.

11:24:14.665 --> 11:24:17.340
AUDIENCE: Why do you not
have to free the TMP?

11:24:17.340 --> 11:24:18.382
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

11:24:18.382 --> 11:24:20.302
Why did I not have to free the TMP?

11:24:20.302 --> 11:24:22.612
I essentially did eventually.

11:24:22.612 --> 11:24:27.842
Because TMP was pointing
at the chunk of 4 integers.

11:24:27.842 --> 11:24:33.292
But on line 33 here,
I assigned list to be

11:24:33.292 --> 11:24:36.062
identical to what TMP was pointing at.

11:24:36.062 --> 11:24:40.655
And so, when I finally freed the list,
that was the same thing as freeing TMP.

11:24:40.655 --> 11:24:43.822
In fact, if I wanted to, I could say
free TMP here and it would be the same.

11:24:43.822 --> 11:24:45.562
But conceptually, it's wrong.

11:24:45.562 --> 11:24:49.612
Because at this point in the story, I
should be freeing the actual list, not

11:24:49.612 --> 11:24:50.722
that temporary variable.

11:24:50.722 --> 11:24:52.822
But they were the same at
that point in the story.

11:24:52.822 --> 11:24:53.322
Yeah.

11:24:53.322 --> 11:24:55.360
AUDIENCE: Is [? the line ?] part of it?

11:24:55.360 --> 11:24:56.402
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

11:24:56.402 --> 11:24:58.832
And long story short,
everything we're doing thus far

11:24:58.832 --> 11:25:00.302
is still in the world of arrays.

11:25:00.302 --> 11:25:02.192
The only distinction
we're making is that

11:25:02.192 --> 11:25:08.702
in version 1, when I said int list
[3], that was an array of fixed size.

11:25:08.702 --> 11:25:12.632
So-called statically allocated
on the stack, as per last week.

11:25:12.632 --> 11:25:16.382
This version now is still dealing with
arrays, but I'm flexing my muscles

11:25:16.382 --> 11:25:18.462
and using dynamic memory allocation.

11:25:18.462 --> 11:25:20.980
So that I can still use an
array per the first pictures

11:25:20.980 --> 11:25:22.022
we started talking about.

11:25:22.022 --> 11:25:24.552
But I can at least grow
the array if I want.

11:25:24.552 --> 11:25:28.472
So we haven't even now solved this, even
better in a sense, with linked lists.

11:25:28.472 --> 11:25:29.562
That's going to come next.

11:25:29.562 --> 11:25:30.062
Yeah.

11:25:30.062 --> 11:25:34.412
AUDIENCE: How are you able to free
list and then still make list?

11:25:34.412 --> 11:25:37.202
SPEAKER 1: How am I able to free list?

11:25:37.202 --> 11:25:41.792
I freed the original address of list.

11:25:41.792 --> 11:25:44.702
I, then, changed what list is storing.

11:25:44.702 --> 11:25:47.552
I'm moving its arrow to
a new chunk of memory.

11:25:47.552 --> 11:25:51.032
And that is perfectly reasonable
for me to now manipulate

11:25:51.032 --> 11:25:54.662
because now list is pointing
at the same value of TMP.

11:25:54.662 --> 11:26:00.092
And TMP is what was given the return
value of malloc, the second time.

11:26:00.092 --> 11:26:02.262
So that chunk of memory is valid.

11:26:02.262 --> 11:26:05.702
So these are just squares
on the board, right.

11:26:05.702 --> 11:26:07.452
There's just pointers inside of them.

11:26:07.452 --> 11:26:09.369
So what I'm technically
saying is, and I'm not

11:26:09.369 --> 11:26:11.522
pointing I'm not freeing
list per se, I am

11:26:11.522 --> 11:26:16.142
freeing the chunk of memory that begins
at the address currently in list.

11:26:16.142 --> 11:26:21.542
Therefore, if a few lines later, I
change what the address is in list.

11:26:21.542 --> 11:26:25.562
Totally reasonable to then touch that
memory, and eventually free it later.

11:26:25.562 --> 11:26:27.872
Because you're not freeing
the variable per se,

11:26:27.872 --> 11:26:30.272
you're freeing the
address in the variable.

11:26:30.272 --> 11:26:31.112
Good distinction.

11:26:31.112 --> 11:26:31.622
All right.

11:26:31.622 --> 11:26:37.232
So let me back up here and
now make one final edit.

11:26:37.232 --> 11:26:41.672
So let's finish this with
one final improvement here.

11:26:41.672 --> 11:26:44.642
Because it turns out,
there's a somewhat better way

11:26:44.642 --> 11:26:48.092
to actually resize an array
as we've been doing here.

11:26:48.092 --> 11:26:52.510
And there's another function in stdlib
that's called realloc, for re-allocate.

11:26:52.510 --> 11:26:55.052
And I'm just going to go in and
make a little bit of a change

11:26:55.052 --> 11:26:58.060
here so that I can do the following.

11:26:58.060 --> 11:26:59.852
Let me go ahead and
first comment this now,

11:26:59.852 --> 11:27:02.802
just so we can keep track of what's
been going on this whole time.

11:27:02.802 --> 11:27:09.452
So dynamically allocate
an array of size 3.

11:27:09.452 --> 11:27:14.132
Assign 3 numbers to that array.

11:27:14.132 --> 11:27:15.812
Time passes.

11:27:15.812 --> 11:27:21.122
Allocate new array of size 4.

11:27:21.122 --> 11:27:26.942
Copy numbers from old
array into new array.

11:27:26.942 --> 11:27:31.652
And add fourth number to new array.

11:27:31.652 --> 11:27:33.377
Free old array.

11:27:36.332 --> 11:27:41.942
Remember, if you will, new array
using my same list variable.

11:27:41.942 --> 11:27:46.442
And now, print new array.

11:27:46.442 --> 11:27:48.752
Free new array.

11:27:48.752 --> 11:27:49.742
Hopefully, that helps.

11:27:49.742 --> 11:27:53.012
And we'll post this code online after
2, which tells a more explicit story.

11:27:53.012 --> 11:27:56.702
So it turns out that we can reduce
some of the labor involved with this.

11:27:56.702 --> 11:27:59.462
Not so much with the printing
here, but with this copying.

11:27:59.462 --> 11:28:01.742
Turns out c does have a
function called realloc,

11:28:01.742 --> 11:28:07.062
that can actually handle the resizing
of an array for you, as follows.

11:28:07.062 --> 11:28:09.182
I'm going to scroll up
to where I previously

11:28:09.182 --> 11:28:12.302
allocated a new array of size 4.

11:28:12.302 --> 11:28:19.502
And I'm instead going to say this,
resize old array to be of size 4.

11:28:19.502 --> 11:28:21.959
Now, previously this wasn't
necessarily possible.

11:28:21.959 --> 11:28:23.792
Because recall that we
had painted ourselves

11:28:23.792 --> 11:28:25.625
into a corner with the
example on the screen

11:28:25.625 --> 11:28:28.472
where "Hello, world" happened to
be right after the original array.

11:28:28.472 --> 11:28:29.892
But let me do this.

11:28:29.892 --> 11:28:32.822
Let me use realloc, for re-allocate.

11:28:32.822 --> 11:28:36.122
And pass in not just the size
of memory we want this time,

11:28:36.122 --> 11:28:39.812
but also the address
that we want to resize.

11:28:39.812 --> 11:28:43.422
Which, again, is this array called list.

11:28:43.422 --> 11:28:43.922
All right.

11:28:43.922 --> 11:28:46.812
The code thereafter is
pretty much the same.

11:28:46.812 --> 11:28:50.682
But what I don't need to do is this.

11:28:50.682 --> 11:28:54.002
So realloc is a pretty handy
function that will do the following.

11:28:54.002 --> 11:28:57.152
If at the very beginning of class,
when we had 1, 2, 3 on the board.

11:28:57.152 --> 11:29:00.492
And someone's instinct was to just plop
the 4 right at the end of the list.

11:29:00.492 --> 11:29:03.242
If there's available memory,
realloc will just do that.

11:29:03.242 --> 11:29:07.682
And boom, it will just grow the array
for you in the computer's memory.

11:29:07.682 --> 11:29:11.642
If, though, it realizes, sorry, there's
already a string like "Hello, world"

11:29:11.642 --> 11:29:14.522
or something else there,
realloc will handle

11:29:14.522 --> 11:29:18.212
the trouble of moving that whole
array from 1 chunk of memory,

11:29:18.212 --> 11:29:20.492
originally, to a new chunk of memory.

11:29:20.492 --> 11:29:26.882
And then realloc will return to you,
the address of that new chunk of memory.

11:29:26.882 --> 11:29:31.032
And it will handle the process
of freeing the old chunk for you.

11:29:31.032 --> 11:29:33.282
So you do not need to do this yourself.

11:29:33.282 --> 11:29:36.612
So in fact, let me go ahead
and get rid of this as well.

11:29:36.612 --> 11:29:41.582
So realloc just condenses, a lot of what
we just did, into a single function.

11:29:41.582 --> 11:29:45.592
Whereby, realloc handles it for you.

11:29:45.592 --> 11:29:46.092
All right.

11:29:46.092 --> 11:29:49.152
So that's the final improvement
on this array-based approach.

11:29:49.152 --> 11:29:51.932
So what now, knowing
what your memory is,

11:29:51.932 --> 11:29:54.882
what can we now do with it that
solves that kind of problem?

11:29:54.882 --> 11:29:56.802
Because the world is
going to get really slow.

11:29:56.802 --> 11:29:59.802
And our apps, and our phones, and our
computers are getting really slow,

11:29:59.802 --> 11:30:04.032
if we're just constantly wasting
time moving things around in memory.

11:30:04.032 --> 11:30:05.892
What could we perhaps do instead?

11:30:05.892 --> 11:30:07.962
Well there's one new
piece of syntax today

11:30:07.962 --> 11:30:11.322
that builds on these 3 pieces
of syntax from the past.

11:30:11.322 --> 11:30:13.182
Recall, that we've
looked at struct, which

11:30:13.182 --> 11:30:16.302
is a keyword in C, that just lets
you invent your own structure.

11:30:16.302 --> 11:30:19.542
Your own variable, if you will,
in conjunction with typedef.

11:30:19.542 --> 11:30:23.682
Which lets you say a person has a name
and a number, or something like that.

11:30:23.682 --> 11:30:26.142
Or a candidate has a name
and some number of votes.

11:30:26.142 --> 11:30:30.522
You can encapsulate multiple pieces of
data inside of just one using struct.

11:30:30.522 --> 11:30:34.642
What did we use the Dot Notation
for now, a couple of times?

11:30:34.642 --> 11:30:37.950
What does the Dot operator do in C?

11:30:37.950 --> 11:30:39.242
AUDIENCE: Access the structure.

11:30:39.242 --> 11:30:39.632
SPEAKER 1: Perfect.

11:30:39.632 --> 11:30:41.682
To access the field
inside of a structure.

11:30:41.682 --> 11:30:43.807
So if you've got a person
with a name and a number,

11:30:43.807 --> 11:30:46.832
you could say something like
person.name or person.number,

11:30:46.832 --> 11:30:48.992
if person is the name
of one such variable.

11:30:48.992 --> 11:30:51.332
Star, of course, we've
seen now in a few ways.

11:30:51.332 --> 11:30:55.022
Like way back in week 1, we
saw it as like, multiplication.

11:30:55.022 --> 11:30:58.232
Last week, we began to see it
in the context of pointers,

11:30:58.232 --> 11:31:00.452
whereby, you use it
to declare a pointer.

11:31:00.452 --> 11:31:03.042
Like, int* p, or something like that.

11:31:03.042 --> 11:31:05.522
But we also saw it in
one other context, which

11:31:05.522 --> 11:31:08.862
was like the opposite, which
was the dereference operator.

11:31:08.862 --> 11:31:10.754
Which says if this is
an address, that is

11:31:10.754 --> 11:31:13.712
if this is a variable like a pointer,
and you put a star in front of it

11:31:13.712 --> 11:31:17.462
then with no int or no char,
no data type in front of it.

11:31:17.462 --> 11:31:19.352
That means go to that address.

11:31:19.352 --> 11:31:22.782
And it dereferences the pointer
and goes to that location.

11:31:22.782 --> 11:31:25.202
So it turns out that using
these 3 building blocks,

11:31:25.202 --> 11:31:28.242
you can actually start to now use
your computer's memory almost any way

11:31:28.242 --> 11:31:28.742
you want.

11:31:28.742 --> 11:31:31.202
And even next week, when
we transition to Python,

11:31:31.202 --> 11:31:33.842
and you start to get a
lot of features for free.

11:31:33.842 --> 11:31:36.032
Like a single line of
code will just do so much

11:31:36.032 --> 11:31:40.652
more in Python than it does in C. It
boils down to those basic primitives.

11:31:40.652 --> 11:31:42.542
And just so you've seen it already.

11:31:42.542 --> 11:31:47.252
It turns out that it's so
common in C to use this operator

11:31:47.252 --> 11:31:51.272
to go inside of a structure and
this operator to go to an address,

11:31:51.272 --> 11:31:53.732
that there's shorthand
notation for it, a.k.a.

11:31:53.732 --> 11:31:54.932
syntactic sugar.

11:31:54.932 --> 11:31:56.577
That literally looks like an arrow.

11:31:56.577 --> 11:31:58.952
So recall last week, I was in
the habit of pointing, even

11:31:58.952 --> 11:32:00.152
with the big foam finger.

11:32:00.152 --> 11:32:04.502
This arrow notation, a
hyphen and an angled bracket,

11:32:04.502 --> 11:32:11.432
denotes going to an address and
looking at a field inside of it.

11:32:11.432 --> 11:32:13.722
But we'll see this in
practice in just a bit.

11:32:13.722 --> 11:32:16.592
So what might be the
solution, now, to this problem

11:32:16.592 --> 11:32:20.102
we saw a moment ago whereby, we had
painted ourselves into a corner.

11:32:20.102 --> 11:32:23.382
And our memory, a few moments
ago, looked like this.

11:32:23.382 --> 11:32:28.202
We could just copy the whole existing
array to a new location, add the 4,

11:32:28.202 --> 11:32:29.492
and go about our business.

11:32:29.492 --> 11:32:33.332
What would another, perhaps
better solution longer term

11:32:33.332 --> 11:32:38.627
be, that doesn't require
constantly moving stuff around?

11:32:38.627 --> 11:32:40.502
Maybe hang in there for
your instincts if you

11:32:40.502 --> 11:32:44.682
know the buzz phrase we're looking for
from past experience, hang in there.

11:32:44.682 --> 11:32:47.282
But if we want to avoid
moving the 1, 2, and the 3,

11:32:47.282 --> 11:32:49.982
but we still want to be able
to add endless amounts of data.

11:32:49.982 --> 11:32:51.462
What could we do?

11:32:51.462 --> 11:32:51.962
Yeah.

11:32:51.962 --> 11:32:54.872
So maybe create some kind
of list using pointers that

11:32:54.872 --> 11:32:56.852
just point at a new location, right.

11:32:56.852 --> 11:32:59.972
In an ideal world, even
though this piece of memory

11:32:59.972 --> 11:33:02.912
is being used by this h in
the string "Hello, world",

11:33:02.912 --> 11:33:05.462
maybe we could somehow use
a pointer from last week.

11:33:05.462 --> 11:33:09.812
Like an arrow, that says after the
3, oh I don't know, go down over here

11:33:09.812 --> 11:33:11.522
to this location in memory.

11:33:11.522 --> 11:33:15.792
And you just stitch together
these integers in memory

11:33:15.792 --> 11:33:17.822
so that each one leads to the next.

11:33:17.822 --> 11:33:21.182
It's not necessarily the case
that it's literally back-to-back.

11:33:21.182 --> 11:33:23.432
That would have the
downside, it would seem,

11:33:23.432 --> 11:33:24.992
of costing us a little bit of space.

11:33:24.992 --> 11:33:27.602
Like a pointer, which recall,
takes up some amount of space.

11:33:27.602 --> 11:33:29.882
Typically 8 bytes or 64 bits.

11:33:29.882 --> 11:33:33.482
But I don't have to copy potentially
a huge amount of data just

11:33:33.482 --> 11:33:34.922
to add one more number.

11:33:34.922 --> 11:33:36.760
And so these things do have a name.

11:33:36.760 --> 11:33:38.552
And indeed, these things
are what generally

11:33:38.552 --> 11:33:42.302
would be called a linked list.

11:33:42.302 --> 11:33:44.822
A linked list captures
exactly that intuition

11:33:44.822 --> 11:33:46.542
of linking together things in memory.

11:33:46.542 --> 11:33:48.012
So let's take a look at an example.

11:33:48.012 --> 11:33:49.804
Here's a computer's
memory in the abstract.

11:33:49.804 --> 11:33:52.622
Suppose that I'm trying
to create an array.

11:33:52.622 --> 11:33:55.682
Let's generalize it as
a list, now, of numbers.

11:33:55.682 --> 11:33:57.362
An array has a very specific meaning.

11:33:57.362 --> 11:34:00.092
It's memory that's contiguous,
back, to back, to back.

11:34:00.092 --> 11:34:03.722
At the end of the day, I as the
programmer, just care about the data--

11:34:03.722 --> 11:34:05.822
1, 2, 3, 4, and so forth.

11:34:05.822 --> 11:34:09.782
I don't really care how it's stored.

11:34:09.782 --> 11:34:12.092
I don't care how it's stored
when I'm writing the code,

11:34:12.092 --> 11:34:13.925
I just wanted to work
at the end of the day.

11:34:13.925 --> 11:34:16.052
So suppose that I first
insert my number 1.

11:34:16.052 --> 11:34:19.592
And, who knows, it ends up,
up there at location, 0X123,

11:34:19.592 --> 11:34:20.802
for the sake of discussion.

11:34:20.802 --> 11:34:21.302
All right.

11:34:21.302 --> 11:34:23.552
Maybe there's something already here.

11:34:23.552 --> 11:34:25.592
And heck, maybe there's
something already here,

11:34:25.592 --> 11:34:28.577
but there's plenty of other options
for where this thing can go.

11:34:28.577 --> 11:34:30.452
And suppose that, for
the sake of discussion,

11:34:30.452 --> 11:34:32.285
the first available
spot for the next number

11:34:32.285 --> 11:34:38.094
happens to be over here at location
0X456, for the sake of discussion.

11:34:38.094 --> 11:34:40.052
So that's where I'm going
to plop the number 2.

11:34:40.052 --> 11:34:41.552
And where might the number 3 end up?

11:34:41.552 --> 11:34:44.342
Oh I don't know, maybe
down over there at 0X789.

11:34:44.342 --> 11:34:48.512
The point being, I don't know
what is, or really care about,

11:34:48.512 --> 11:34:50.672
everything else that's
in the computer's memory.

11:34:50.672 --> 11:34:54.722
I just care that there are at
least 3 locations available where

11:34:54.722 --> 11:34:57.782
I can put my 1, my 2, and my 3.

11:34:57.782 --> 11:35:01.502
But the catch is, now that
we're not using an array,

11:35:01.502 --> 11:35:05.852
we can't just naively assume that
you just add 1 to an index and boom,

11:35:05.852 --> 11:35:06.992
you're at the next number.

11:35:06.992 --> 11:35:10.442
Add 2 to an index, and boom
you're at the next, next number.

11:35:10.442 --> 11:35:14.852
Now you have to leave these little
breadcrumbs, or use the arrow notation,

11:35:14.852 --> 11:35:17.162
to lead from one to the other.

11:35:17.162 --> 11:35:19.352
And sometimes, it might be
close, a few bytes away.

11:35:19.352 --> 11:35:23.292
Maybe, it's a whole gigabyte away
in an even bigger computer's memory.

11:35:23.292 --> 11:35:25.022
So how might I do this?

11:35:25.022 --> 11:35:30.252
Like where do these pointers
go, as you proposed?

11:35:30.252 --> 11:35:30.752
All right.

11:35:30.752 --> 11:35:32.822
All I have access to here are bytes.

11:35:32.822 --> 11:35:34.892
I've already stored the
1, the 2, and the 3.

11:35:34.892 --> 11:35:37.262
So what more should I do?

11:35:37.262 --> 11:35:37.962
OK, yeah.

11:35:37.962 --> 11:35:40.852
So let me, you put the pointers
right next to these numbers.

11:35:40.852 --> 11:35:44.892
So let me at least plan ahead, so that
when I ask the computer like malloc,

11:35:44.892 --> 11:35:47.952
recall from last week, for some
memory, I don't just ask it now

11:35:47.952 --> 11:35:49.857
for space for just the number.

11:35:49.857 --> 11:35:51.732
Let me start getting
into the habit of asking

11:35:51.732 --> 11:35:56.832
malloc for enough space for the number
and a pointer to another such number.

11:35:56.832 --> 11:35:59.542
So it's a little more aggressive
of me to ask for more memory.

11:35:59.542 --> 11:36:00.822
But I'm planning ahead.

11:36:00.822 --> 11:36:02.622
And here is an example of a trade off.

11:36:02.622 --> 11:36:06.402
Almost any time in CS, when you start
using more space, you can save time.

11:36:06.402 --> 11:36:10.662
Or if you try to conserve space,
you might have to lose time.

11:36:10.662 --> 11:36:12.162
It's being that trade off there.

11:36:12.162 --> 11:36:14.392
So how might I solve this?

11:36:14.392 --> 11:36:15.942
Well let me abstract this away.

11:36:15.942 --> 11:36:19.057
And either next to or below, I'm
just drawing it vertically, just

11:36:19.057 --> 11:36:20.182
for the sake of discussion.

11:36:20.182 --> 11:36:22.152
So the arrows are a bit prettier.

11:36:22.152 --> 11:36:25.062
I've asked malloc for
now twice as much space,

11:36:25.062 --> 11:36:27.072
it would seem, than I previously needed.

11:36:27.072 --> 11:36:31.017
But I'm going to use this second chunk
of memory to refer to the next number.

11:36:31.017 --> 11:36:33.642
And I'm going to use this chunk
of memory to refer to the next,

11:36:33.642 --> 11:36:35.452
essentially, stitching
this thing together.

11:36:35.452 --> 11:36:37.512
So what should go in this first box?

11:36:37.512 --> 11:36:41.082
Well, I claim the number, 0X456.

11:36:41.082 --> 11:36:43.782
And it's written in hex because
it represents a memory address.

11:36:43.782 --> 11:36:47.802
But this is the equivalent of drawing
an arrow from one to the other.

11:36:47.802 --> 11:36:51.552
As a little check here, what
should go in this second box

11:36:51.552 --> 11:36:55.422
if the goal is to stitch these
together in order 1, 2, 3?

11:36:55.422 --> 11:36:57.594
Feel free to just shout this out.

11:36:57.594 --> 11:36:59.052
AUDIENCE: 0X789.

11:36:59.052 --> 11:37:00.472
SPEAKER 1: OK, that worked well.

11:37:00.472 --> 11:37:01.397
So 0X789, indeed.

11:37:01.397 --> 11:37:04.272
And you can't do that with the hands
because I can't count that fast.

11:37:04.272 --> 11:37:08.512
So 0X789 should go here because that's
like a little breadcrumb to the next.

11:37:08.512 --> 11:37:11.772
And then, we don't really have
terribly many possibilities here.

11:37:11.772 --> 11:37:14.442
This has to have a value, right.

11:37:14.442 --> 11:37:19.312
Because at the end of the day, it's
got to use its 64 bits in some way.

11:37:19.312 --> 11:37:22.652
So what value should go here,
if this is the end of this list?

11:37:22.652 --> 11:37:23.652
AUDIENCE: 0.

11:37:23.652 --> 11:37:25.752
SPEAKER 1: So it could be 0X123.

11:37:25.752 --> 11:37:29.532
The implication being that
it would be a cyclical list.

11:37:29.532 --> 11:37:32.052
Which is OK, but
potentially problematic.

11:37:32.052 --> 11:37:36.102
If any of you have accidentally
lost control over your code space

11:37:36.102 --> 11:37:39.162
because you had an infinite loop,
this would seem a very easy way

11:37:39.162 --> 11:37:43.812
to give yourself the accidental
probability of an infinite loop.

11:37:43.812 --> 11:37:46.398
What might be simpler than
that and ward that off?

11:37:46.398 --> 11:37:47.072
AUDIENCE: Null.

11:37:47.072 --> 11:37:47.987
SPEAKER 1: Say again?

11:37:47.987 --> 11:37:48.612
AUDIENCE: Null.

11:37:48.612 --> 11:37:50.322
SPEAKER 1: So just the null character.

11:37:50.322 --> 11:37:53.022
Not N-U-L, confusingly, which
is at the end of strings.

11:37:53.022 --> 11:37:56.032
But N-U-L-L, as we
introduced it last week.

11:37:56.032 --> 11:37:58.062
Which is the same as 0x0.

11:37:58.062 --> 11:38:00.882
So this is just a special value
that programmers decades ago

11:38:00.882 --> 11:38:04.992
decided that if you store the address
0, that's not a valid address.

11:38:04.992 --> 11:38:07.902
There's never going to be
anything useful at 0x0.

11:38:07.902 --> 11:38:11.082
Therefore, it's a sentinel
value, just a special value,

11:38:11.082 --> 11:38:12.282
that indicates that's it.

11:38:12.282 --> 11:38:14.352
There's nowhere further to go.

11:38:14.352 --> 11:38:17.952
It's OK to come back to your
suggestion of making a cyclical list.

11:38:17.952 --> 11:38:19.872
But we'd better be
smart enough to, maybe,

11:38:19.872 --> 11:38:23.862
remember where did the list start
so that you can detect cycles.

11:38:23.862 --> 11:38:26.422
If you start looping around
in this structure, otherwise.

11:38:26.422 --> 11:38:26.922
All right.

11:38:26.922 --> 11:38:29.122
But these addresses, who really
cares at the end of the day

11:38:29.122 --> 11:38:30.402
if we abstract this away.

11:38:30.402 --> 11:38:32.302
It really just now looks like this.

11:38:32.302 --> 11:38:35.260
And indeed, this is how most anyone
would draw this on a whiteboard

11:38:35.260 --> 11:38:36.552
if having a discussion at work.

11:38:36.552 --> 11:38:38.344
Talking about what data
structure we should

11:38:38.344 --> 11:38:40.272
use to solve some problem
in the real world.

11:38:40.272 --> 11:38:42.522
We don't care generally
about the addresses.

11:38:42.522 --> 11:38:45.112
We care that in code we can access them.

11:38:45.112 --> 11:38:48.072
But in terms of the concept
alone this would be, perhaps,

11:38:48.072 --> 11:38:49.721
the right way to think about this.

11:38:49.721 --> 11:38:51.679
All right, let me pause
here and see if there's

11:38:51.679 --> 11:38:55.902
any questions on this idea of creating
a linked list in memory by just storing,

11:38:55.902 --> 11:39:00.022
not just the numbers like 1,
2, 3, but twice as much data.

11:39:00.022 --> 11:39:02.592
So that you have little
breadcrumbs in the form of pointers

11:39:02.592 --> 11:39:05.992
that can lead you from one to the next.

11:39:05.992 --> 11:39:08.156
Any questions on these linked lists?

11:39:11.612 --> 11:39:12.212
Any questions?

11:39:12.212 --> 11:39:12.712
No?

11:39:12.712 --> 11:39:13.422
All right.

11:39:13.422 --> 11:39:13.922
Oh, yeah.

11:39:13.922 --> 11:39:14.913
Over here.

11:39:14.913 --> 11:39:19.507
AUDIENCE: So does this takes
time more memory than an array?

11:39:19.507 --> 11:39:21.632
SPEAKER 1: This does take
more memory than an array

11:39:21.632 --> 11:39:24.181
because I now need space
for these pointers.

11:39:24.181 --> 11:39:28.152
And to be clear, I technically
didn't really draw this to scale.

11:39:28.152 --> 11:39:31.082
Thus far, in the class, we've
generally thought about integers

11:39:31.082 --> 11:39:33.992
like, 1, 2 and 3, as
being 4 bytes, or 32 bits.

11:39:33.992 --> 11:39:37.022
I made the claim last week that
on modern computer's pointers

11:39:37.022 --> 11:39:40.052
tend to be 8 bytes or 64 bits.

11:39:40.052 --> 11:39:42.762
So, technically, this box should
actually be a little bigger.

11:39:42.762 --> 11:39:44.462
It was just going to look a
little stupid in the picture.

11:39:44.462 --> 11:39:45.812
So I abstracted it away.

11:39:45.812 --> 11:39:48.812
But, indeed, you're using
more space as a result.

11:39:48.812 --> 11:39:50.269
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

11:39:50.269 --> 11:39:51.602
SPEAKER 1: Oh, how does-- sorry.

11:39:51.602 --> 11:39:55.452
How does the computer identify
useful data from used data?

11:39:55.452 --> 11:39:58.262
So, for instance, garbage
values or non-garbage values.

11:39:58.262 --> 11:40:00.902
For now, think of that
as the job of malloc.

11:40:00.902 --> 11:40:04.292
So when you ask malloc for memory,
as we started to last week,

11:40:04.292 --> 11:40:07.472
malloc keeps track of the
addresses of the memory

11:40:07.472 --> 11:40:10.442
it has handed to as valid values.

11:40:10.442 --> 11:40:12.932
The other type of memory you
use, not just from the heap.

11:40:12.932 --> 11:40:15.872
Because recall we briefly
discussed that malloc uses space

11:40:15.872 --> 11:40:18.872
from the heap, which was drawn at the
top of the picture, pointing down.

11:40:18.872 --> 11:40:22.702
There's also stack memory, which is
where all of your local variables go.

11:40:22.702 --> 11:40:25.202
And where all of the memory
used by individual functions go.

11:40:25.202 --> 11:40:27.535
And that was drawn in the
picture is working its way up.

11:40:27.535 --> 11:40:30.302
That's just an artist's
rendition of direction.

11:40:30.302 --> 11:40:33.662
The compiler, essentially,
will also help

11:40:33.662 --> 11:40:37.350
keep track of which values are
valid or not inside of the stack.

11:40:37.350 --> 11:40:39.392
Or really the underlying
code that you've written

11:40:39.392 --> 11:40:40.725
will keep track of that for you.

11:40:40.725 --> 11:40:43.692
So it's managed for you at that point.

11:40:43.692 --> 11:40:44.192
All right.

11:40:44.192 --> 11:40:44.792
Good question.

11:40:44.792 --> 11:40:46.522
Sorry it took me a bit to catch on.

11:40:46.522 --> 11:40:48.692
So let's now translate
this to actual code.

11:40:48.692 --> 11:40:52.262
How could we implement this idea
of, let's call these things nodes.

11:40:52.262 --> 11:40:53.642
And that's a term of our NCS.

11:40:53.642 --> 11:40:57.692
Whenever you have some data structure
that encapsulates information, node,

11:40:57.692 --> 11:41:00.429
N-O-D-E, is the generic term for that.

11:41:00.429 --> 11:41:02.262
So each of these might
be said to be a node.

11:41:02.262 --> 11:41:03.312
Well, how can we do this?

11:41:03.312 --> 11:41:06.104
Well a couple of weeks ago, we saw
how we could represent something

11:41:06.104 --> 11:41:07.742
like a student or a candidate.

11:41:07.742 --> 11:41:12.422
And a student, or rather a person,
we said has a name and a number.

11:41:12.422 --> 11:41:14.162
And we used a few pieces of syntax here.

11:41:14.162 --> 11:41:17.372
One, we use the struct keyword,
which gives us a data structure.

11:41:17.372 --> 11:41:21.902
We use typedef, which defines the
name person to be our new data

11:41:21.902 --> 11:41:24.332
type representing that whole structure.

11:41:24.332 --> 11:41:26.432
So we probably have the
right ingredients here

11:41:26.432 --> 11:41:28.982
to build up this thing called a node.

11:41:28.982 --> 11:41:32.102
And just to be clear, what should
go inside of one of these nodes,

11:41:32.102 --> 11:41:32.917
do we think?

11:41:32.917 --> 11:41:35.042
It's not going to be a name
or a number, obviously.

11:41:35.042 --> 11:41:39.732
But what should a node have in
terms of those fields, perhaps?

11:41:39.732 --> 11:41:40.232
Yeah?

11:41:40.232 --> 11:41:41.107
AUDIENCE: [? Data. ?]

11:41:41.107 --> 11:41:44.082
SPEAKER 1: So a number like a
number and a pointer in some form.

11:41:44.082 --> 11:41:46.332
So let's translate this to actual code.

11:41:46.332 --> 11:41:51.092
So let's rename person to node
to capture this notion here.

11:41:51.092 --> 11:41:52.347
And the number is easy.

11:41:52.347 --> 11:41:54.222
If it's just going to
be an int, that's fine.

11:41:54.222 --> 11:41:56.462
We can just say int number,
or int n, or whatever

11:41:56.462 --> 11:41:58.862
you want to call that particular field.

11:41:58.862 --> 11:42:00.554
The next one is a little non-obvious.

11:42:00.554 --> 11:42:02.762
And this is where things
get a little weird at first,

11:42:02.762 --> 11:42:05.312
but, in retrospect, it
should all fit together.

11:42:05.312 --> 11:42:11.112
Let me propose that, ideally, we
would say something like node* next.

11:42:11.112 --> 11:42:13.412
And I could call the word
next anything I want.

11:42:13.412 --> 11:42:17.592
Next just means what comes after
me is the notion I'm using it at.

11:42:17.592 --> 11:42:19.982
So a lot of CS people would
just use next to represent

11:42:19.982 --> 11:42:21.362
the name of this pointer.

11:42:21.362 --> 11:42:22.742
But there's a catch here.

11:42:22.742 --> 11:42:25.922
C and C compilers are
pretty naive, recall.

11:42:25.922 --> 11:42:29.142
They only look at code top
to bottom, left to right.

11:42:29.142 --> 11:42:31.322
And any time they encounter
a word they have never

11:42:31.322 --> 11:42:32.995
seen before, bad things happen.

11:42:32.995 --> 11:42:34.412
Like, you can't compile your code.

11:42:34.412 --> 11:42:36.402
You get some cryptic
error message or the like.

11:42:36.402 --> 11:42:39.392
And that seems to be
about to happen here.

11:42:39.392 --> 11:42:42.452
Because if the compiler is reading
this code from top to bottom,

11:42:42.452 --> 11:42:44.822
it's going to say, oh,
inside of this struct

11:42:44.822 --> 11:42:46.622
should be a variable called next.

11:42:46.622 --> 11:42:48.482
Which is of type node*.

11:42:48.482 --> 11:42:49.682
What the heck is a node?

11:42:49.682 --> 11:42:52.952
Because it literally does
not find out until 2 lines

11:42:52.952 --> 11:42:55.202
later, after that semicolon.

11:42:55.202 --> 11:42:57.812
So the way to avoid this, which
we haven't quite seen before,

11:42:57.812 --> 11:43:02.702
is that you can temporarily name this
whole thing up here, struct node.

11:43:02.702 --> 11:43:08.042
And then, down here inside of the
data structure, you say struct node*.

11:43:08.042 --> 11:43:09.692
And then, you leave the rest alone.

11:43:09.692 --> 11:43:14.102
This is a workaround this is
possible because now you're

11:43:14.102 --> 11:43:17.222
teaching the compiler, from
the first line, that here comes

11:43:17.222 --> 11:43:19.442
a data structure called struct node.

11:43:19.442 --> 11:43:22.902
Down here, you're shortening the name
of this whole thing to just node.

11:43:22.902 --> 11:43:23.402
Why?

11:43:23.402 --> 11:43:26.485
It's just a little more convenient
than having to write struct everywhere.

11:43:26.485 --> 11:43:30.242
But you do have to write struct
node* inside of the data structure.

11:43:30.242 --> 11:43:33.212
But that's OK because it's
already come into existence

11:43:33.212 --> 11:43:35.374
now, as of that first line of code.

11:43:35.374 --> 11:43:37.082
So that's the only
fundamental difference

11:43:37.082 --> 11:43:40.382
between what we did last week
with a person or a candidate.

11:43:40.382 --> 11:43:45.372
We just now have to use this
struct workaround, syntactically.

11:43:45.372 --> 11:43:45.872
All right.

11:43:45.872 --> 11:43:46.652
Yeah, question.

11:43:46.652 --> 11:43:50.492
AUDIENCE: So [INAUDIBLE] have like
right next to the [INAUDIBLE] point

11:43:50.492 --> 11:43:51.452
to another [INAUDIBLE].

11:43:51.452 --> 11:43:56.552
SPEAKER 1: Why is the next variable
a struct node* pointer and not an int

11:43:56.552 --> 11:43:58.632
star pointer, for instance?

11:43:58.632 --> 11:44:01.352
So think about the picture
we are trying to draw.

11:44:01.352 --> 11:44:05.222
Technically, yes, each of these
arrows I deliberately drew

11:44:05.222 --> 11:44:06.722
is pointing at the number.

11:44:06.722 --> 11:44:07.982
But that's not alone.

11:44:07.982 --> 11:44:10.802
They need to point at the
whole data structure in memory.

11:44:10.802 --> 11:44:13.082
Because the computer,
ultimately, and the compiler,

11:44:13.082 --> 11:44:16.952
in turn, needs to know that this
chunk of memory is not just an int.

11:44:16.952 --> 11:44:18.522
It is a whole node.

11:44:18.522 --> 11:44:21.852
Inside of a node is a number
and also another pointer.

11:44:21.852 --> 11:44:24.252
So when you draw these
arrows, it would be

11:44:24.252 --> 11:44:26.862
incorrect to point at just the number.

11:44:26.862 --> 11:44:29.239
Because that throws
away information that

11:44:29.239 --> 11:44:31.572
would leave the compiler
wondering, OK, I'm at a number.

11:44:31.572 --> 11:44:32.682
Where the heck is the pointer?

11:44:32.682 --> 11:44:34.932
You have to tell it that
it's pointing at a whole node

11:44:34.932 --> 11:44:38.339
so it knows a few bytes away
is that corresponding pointer.

11:44:38.339 --> 11:44:38.922
Good question.

11:44:38.922 --> 11:44:40.665
Yeah.

11:44:40.665 --> 11:44:42.112
AUDIENCE: How do you [INAUDIBLE].

11:44:42.112 --> 11:44:43.445
SPEAKER 1: Really good question.

11:44:43.445 --> 11:44:46.732
It would seem that just as
copying the array earlier

11:44:46.732 --> 11:44:49.942
required twice as much memory,
because we copied from old to new.

11:44:49.942 --> 11:44:52.612
So, technically, twice as much
plus 1 for the new number.

11:44:52.612 --> 11:44:56.002
Here, too, it looks like we're
using twice as much memory, also.

11:44:56.002 --> 11:44:58.882
And to my comment earlier, it's
even more than twice as much memory

11:44:58.882 --> 11:45:02.752
because these pointers are 8 bytes, and
not just 4 bytes like a typical integer

11:45:02.752 --> 11:45:03.352
is.

11:45:03.352 --> 11:45:04.762
The differences are these.

11:45:04.762 --> 11:45:08.392
In the context of the array, you
were using that memory temporarily.

11:45:08.392 --> 11:45:10.232
So, yes, you needed
twice as much memory.

11:45:10.232 --> 11:45:13.082
But then you were quickly
freeing the original array.

11:45:13.082 --> 11:45:16.372
So you weren't consuming long-term,
more memory than you might need.

11:45:16.372 --> 11:45:19.772
The difference here, too, is
that, as we'll see in a moment,

11:45:19.772 --> 11:45:23.152
it turns out it's going to be
relatively quick for me, potentially,

11:45:23.152 --> 11:45:25.102
to insert new numbers in here.

11:45:25.102 --> 11:45:28.102
Because I'm not going to have
to do a huge amount of copying.

11:45:28.102 --> 11:45:31.282
And even though I might still have
to follow all of these arrows, which

11:45:31.282 --> 11:45:33.562
is going to take some
amount of time, I'm

11:45:33.562 --> 11:45:36.952
not going to have to be asking for
more memory, freeing more memory.

11:45:36.952 --> 11:45:40.672
And certain operations in the computer,
anything involving asking for or giving

11:45:40.672 --> 11:45:42.482
back memory, tends to be slower.

11:45:42.482 --> 11:45:44.340
So we get to avoid
that situation as well.

11:45:44.340 --> 11:45:46.132
There's going to be
some downsides, though.

11:45:46.132 --> 11:45:47.182
This is not all upside.

11:45:47.182 --> 11:45:51.242
But we'll see in a bit just what some
of those trade offs actually are.

11:45:51.242 --> 11:45:51.742
All right.

11:45:51.742 --> 11:45:56.222
So from here, if we go back to the
structure in code as we left it,

11:45:56.222 --> 11:45:59.302
let's start to now build up a
linked list with some actual code.

11:45:59.302 --> 11:46:03.682
How do you go about, in C,
representing a linked list in code?

11:46:03.682 --> 11:46:06.262
Well, at the moment, it would
actually be as simple as this.

11:46:06.262 --> 11:46:09.412
You declare a variable,
called list, for instance.

11:46:09.412 --> 11:46:12.452
That itself stores
the address of a node.

11:46:12.452 --> 11:46:13.492
That's what node* means.

11:46:13.492 --> 11:46:14.702
The address of a node.

11:46:14.702 --> 11:46:17.362
So if you want to store
a linked list in memory,

11:46:17.362 --> 11:46:19.879
you just create a variable
called list, or whatever else.

11:46:19.879 --> 11:46:21.712
And you just say that
this variable is going

11:46:21.712 --> 11:46:25.912
to be pointing at the first node in a
list, wherever it happens to end up.

11:46:25.912 --> 11:46:29.752
Because malloc is ultimately going
to be the tool that we use just to go

11:46:29.752 --> 11:46:33.752
get at any one particular
node in memory.

11:46:33.752 --> 11:46:34.252
All right.

11:46:34.252 --> 11:46:36.172
So let's actually do
this in pictorial form.

11:46:36.172 --> 11:46:39.172
When you write a line of
code, like I just did here--

11:46:39.172 --> 11:46:43.162
and I do not initialize it to
anything with the assignment operator,

11:46:43.162 --> 11:46:44.212
an equal sign.

11:46:44.212 --> 11:46:48.202
It does exist in memory as a box,
as I'll draw it here, called list.

11:46:48.202 --> 11:46:50.912
But I've deliberately
drawn Oscar inside of it.

11:46:50.912 --> 11:46:51.412
Why?

11:46:51.412 --> 11:46:53.112
To connote what exactly?

11:46:53.112 --> 11:46:54.112
AUDIENCE: Garbage value.

11:46:54.112 --> 11:46:55.445
SPEAKER 1: It's a garbage value.

11:46:55.445 --> 11:46:59.882
I have been allocated the
variable in memory, called list.

11:46:59.882 --> 11:47:03.952
Which is going to give me 64 bits
or 8 bytes somewhere drawn here

11:47:03.952 --> 11:47:04.952
with this box.

11:47:04.952 --> 11:47:07.702
But if I myself have not
used the assignment operator,

11:47:07.702 --> 11:47:11.312
it's not going to get magically
initialized to any particular address

11:47:11.312 --> 11:47:11.812
for me.

11:47:11.812 --> 11:47:13.952
It's not going to even give me a node.

11:47:13.952 --> 11:47:18.632
This is literally just going to be an
address of a future node that exists.

11:47:18.632 --> 11:47:20.242
So what would be a solution here?

11:47:20.242 --> 11:47:23.242
Suppose that I'm beginning
to create my linked list,

11:47:23.242 --> 11:47:24.772
but I don't have any nodes yet.

11:47:24.772 --> 11:47:28.784
What would be a sensible thing to
initialize the list to, perhaps?

11:47:28.784 --> 11:47:29.604
AUDIENCE: Null.

11:47:29.604 --> 11:47:30.562
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, again.

11:47:30.562 --> 11:47:31.320
AUDIENCE: To null.

11:47:31.320 --> 11:47:32.612
SPEAKER 1: So just null, right.

11:47:32.612 --> 11:47:34.342
When in doubt with
pointers, generally it's

11:47:34.342 --> 11:47:36.092
a good thing to
initialize things to null,

11:47:36.092 --> 11:47:37.642
so at least it's not a garbage value.

11:47:37.642 --> 11:47:38.902
It's a known value.

11:47:38.902 --> 11:47:39.900
Invalid, yes.

11:47:39.900 --> 11:47:41.692
But it's a special
value you can then check

11:47:41.692 --> 11:47:43.622
for with a conditional, or the like.

11:47:43.622 --> 11:47:47.602
So this might be a better
way to create a linked list,

11:47:47.602 --> 11:47:51.602
even before you've inserted any
numbers into the thing itself.

11:47:51.602 --> 11:47:52.102
All right.

11:47:52.102 --> 11:47:55.317
So after that, how can we go about
adding something to this linked list?

11:47:55.317 --> 11:47:56.692
So now the story looks like this.

11:47:56.692 --> 11:47:59.632
Oscar is gone because inside
of this box is all zero bits.

11:47:59.632 --> 11:48:03.532
Just because it's nice and clean, and
this represents an empty linked list.

11:48:03.532 --> 11:48:08.072
Well, if I want to add the number 1
to this linked list, what could I do?

11:48:08.072 --> 11:48:10.072
Well, perhaps I could
start with code like this.

11:48:10.072 --> 11:48:11.782
Borrowing inspiration from last week.

11:48:11.782 --> 11:48:16.402
Let's ask malloc for enough
space for the size of a node.

11:48:16.402 --> 11:48:20.542
And this gets to your question earlier,
like, what is it I'm manipulating here?

11:48:20.542 --> 11:48:23.842
I don't just need space for an int and
I don't just need space for a pointer.

11:48:23.842 --> 11:48:24.922
I need space for both.

11:48:24.922 --> 11:48:27.632
And I gave that thing a name, node.

11:48:27.632 --> 11:48:30.412
So size of node figures out
and does the arithmetic for me.

11:48:30.412 --> 11:48:32.872
And gives me back the
right number of bytes.

11:48:32.872 --> 11:48:36.412
This, then, stores the address
of that chunk of memory

11:48:36.412 --> 11:48:38.362
in what I'll temporarily called n.

11:48:38.362 --> 11:48:40.642
Just to represent a generic new node.

11:48:40.642 --> 11:48:42.352
And it's of type node*.

11:48:42.352 --> 11:48:45.562
Because just like last week when I
asked malloc for enough space for an int

11:48:45.562 --> 11:48:47.842
and I stored it in an int* pointer.

11:48:47.842 --> 11:48:50.242
This week, if I'm asking
for memory for a node,

11:48:50.242 --> 11:48:52.822
I'm storing it in a node* pointer.

11:48:52.822 --> 11:48:56.002
So technically, nothing new
there except for this new term

11:48:56.002 --> 11:48:58.502
of art in data structure called node.

11:48:58.502 --> 11:48:59.002
All right.

11:48:59.002 --> 11:49:00.352
So what does that do for me?

11:49:00.352 --> 11:49:03.142
It essentially draws a
picture like this in memory.

11:49:03.142 --> 11:49:07.172
I still have my list variable from
my previous line of code initialize

11:49:07.172 --> 11:49:07.672
to null.

11:49:07.672 --> 11:49:09.130
And that's why I've drawn it blank.

11:49:09.130 --> 11:49:11.542
I also now have a
temporary variable called

11:49:11.542 --> 11:49:15.052
n, which I initialize to
the return value of malloc.

11:49:15.052 --> 11:49:17.132
Which gave me one of
these nodes in memory.

11:49:17.132 --> 11:49:19.612
But I've drawn it having
garbage values, too,

11:49:19.612 --> 11:49:21.332
because I don't know what int is there.

11:49:21.332 --> 11:49:22.790
I don't know what pointer is there.

11:49:22.790 --> 11:49:27.082
It's garbage values because malloc does
not magically initialize memory for me.

11:49:27.082 --> 11:49:28.732
There is another function for that.

11:49:28.732 --> 11:49:31.582
But malloc alone just says,
sure, use this chunk of memory.

11:49:31.582 --> 11:49:33.392
Deal with whatever is there.

11:49:33.392 --> 11:49:36.382
So how can I go about
initializing this to known values?

11:49:36.382 --> 11:49:40.922
Well, suppose I want to insert the
number 1 and then, leave it at that.

11:49:40.922 --> 11:49:44.694
A list of size 1, I could
do something like this.

11:49:44.694 --> 11:49:47.402
And this is where you have to
think back to some of these basics.

11:49:47.402 --> 11:49:51.542
My conditional here is asking the
question if n does not equal null.

11:49:51.542 --> 11:49:54.692
So that is, if malloc
gave me valid memory,

11:49:54.692 --> 11:49:58.172
and I don't have to quit altogether
because my computer's out of memory.

11:49:58.172 --> 11:50:02.072
If n does not equal null, but
is equal to valid address,

11:50:02.072 --> 11:50:03.552
I'm going to go ahead and do this.

11:50:03.552 --> 11:50:06.302
And this is cryptic looking syntax now.

11:50:06.302 --> 11:50:09.632
But does someone want to take a stab
at translating this inside line of code

11:50:09.632 --> 11:50:13.862
to English, in some sense?

11:50:13.862 --> 11:50:18.002
How might you explain what that
inner line of code is doing? *n.

11:50:18.002 --> 11:50:20.612
number equals 1.

11:50:20.612 --> 11:50:22.837
Let me go further back.

11:50:22.837 --> 11:50:23.959
Nope?

11:50:23.959 --> 11:50:24.542
OK, over here.

11:50:24.542 --> 11:50:25.254
Yeah.

11:50:25.254 --> 11:50:26.492
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

11:50:26.492 --> 11:50:27.284
SPEAKER 1: Perfect.

11:50:27.284 --> 11:50:29.642
The place that n is pointing
to, set it equal to 1.

11:50:29.642 --> 11:50:33.542
Or using the vernacular of going
there, go to the address in n

11:50:33.542 --> 11:50:35.962
and set it's number field to 1.

11:50:35.962 --> 11:50:37.962
However you want to think
about it, that's fine.

11:50:37.962 --> 11:50:40.412
But the * again is the
dereference operator here.

11:50:40.412 --> 11:50:42.212
And we're doing the
parentheses, which we

11:50:42.212 --> 11:50:45.722
haven't needed to do before because we
haven't dealt with pointers and data

11:50:45.722 --> 11:50:47.492
structures together until today.

11:50:47.492 --> 11:50:49.862
This just means go there first.

11:50:49.862 --> 11:50:52.202
And then once you're
there, go access number.

11:50:52.202 --> 11:50:54.312
You don't want to do one
thing before the other.

11:50:54.312 --> 11:50:56.372
So this is just enforcing
order of operations.

11:50:56.372 --> 11:50:58.782
The parentheses just like
in grade school math.

11:50:58.782 --> 11:50:59.282
All right.

11:50:59.282 --> 11:51:00.692
So this line of code is cryptic.

11:51:00.692 --> 11:51:01.464
It's ugly.

11:51:01.464 --> 11:51:03.422
It's not something most
people easily remember.

11:51:03.422 --> 11:51:07.232
Thankfully, there's that syntactic
sugar that simplifies this line of code

11:51:07.232 --> 11:51:08.339
to just this.

11:51:08.339 --> 11:51:10.172
And this, even though
it's new to you today,

11:51:10.172 --> 11:51:12.302
should eventually feel
a little more familiar.

11:51:12.302 --> 11:51:15.692
Because this now is shorthand
notation for saying, start at n.

11:51:15.692 --> 11:51:17.892
Go there as by following the arrow.

11:51:17.892 --> 11:51:20.012
And when you get there,
change the number field.

11:51:20.012 --> 11:51:22.202
In this case, to 1.

11:51:22.202 --> 11:51:24.722
So most people would not
write code like this.

11:51:24.722 --> 11:51:25.512
It's just ugly.

11:51:25.512 --> 11:51:26.912
It's a couple extra keystrokes.

11:51:26.912 --> 11:51:30.782
This just looks more like the artist's
renditions we've been talking about.

11:51:30.782 --> 11:51:35.012
And how most CS people would think about
pointers as really just being arrows

11:51:35.012 --> 11:51:36.192
in some form.

11:51:36.192 --> 11:51:36.692
All right.

11:51:36.692 --> 11:51:37.775
So what have we just done?

11:51:37.775 --> 11:51:42.132
The picture now, after setting number to
1, looks a little something like this.

11:51:42.132 --> 11:51:43.922
So there's still one step missing.

11:51:43.922 --> 11:51:46.202
And that's, of course, to
initialize, it would seem,

11:51:46.202 --> 11:51:50.562
the pointer in this new node
to something known like null.

11:51:50.562 --> 11:51:52.217
So I bet we could do this like this.

11:51:52.217 --> 11:51:54.092
With a different line
of code, I'm just going

11:51:54.092 --> 11:52:00.362
to say if n does not equal null,
then set n's next field to null.

11:52:00.362 --> 11:52:04.022
Or more pedantically, go
to n, follow the arrow,

11:52:04.022 --> 11:52:07.922
and then update the next field
that you find there to equal null.

11:52:07.922 --> 11:52:10.172
And again, this is just
doing some nice bookkeeping.

11:52:10.172 --> 11:52:13.352
Technically speaking,
we might not need to set

11:52:13.352 --> 11:52:16.392
this to null if we're going to keep
adding more and more numbers to it.

11:52:16.392 --> 11:52:19.592
But I'm doing it step-by-step so
that I have a very clean picture.

11:52:19.592 --> 11:52:23.282
And there's no bugs in
my code at this point.

11:52:23.282 --> 11:52:24.752
But I'm still not done.

11:52:24.752 --> 11:52:27.212
There's one last thing I'm
going to have to do here.

11:52:27.212 --> 11:52:32.432
If the goal, ultimately, was to insert
the number 1 into my linked list,

11:52:32.432 --> 11:52:36.342
what's the last step I
should, perhaps, do here?

11:52:36.342 --> 11:52:37.532
Just been English is fine.

11:52:37.532 --> 11:52:38.032
Yeah.

11:52:38.032 --> 11:52:40.742
AUDIENCE: Set the pointer value to null.

11:52:40.742 --> 11:52:41.492
SPEAKER 1: Yes.

11:52:41.492 --> 11:52:45.452
I now need to update the actual
variable, that represents my linked

11:52:45.452 --> 11:52:48.512
list, to point at this brand new node.

11:52:48.512 --> 11:52:52.799
That is now perfectly initialized as
having an integer and a null pointer.

11:52:52.799 --> 11:52:54.882
Yeah, technically, this
is already pointing there.

11:52:54.882 --> 11:52:57.572
But I describe this deliberately
earlier as being temporary.

11:52:57.572 --> 11:53:02.102
I just needed this to get it back from
malloc and clean things up, initially.

11:53:02.102 --> 11:53:04.712
This is the long term
variable I care about.

11:53:04.712 --> 11:53:06.962
So I'm going to want to do
something simple like this.

11:53:06.962 --> 11:53:09.002
List equals n.

11:53:09.002 --> 11:53:11.345
And this seems a little
weird that list equals n.

11:53:11.345 --> 11:53:13.262
But again, think about
what's inside this box.

11:53:13.262 --> 11:53:15.470
At the moment this is null
because there is no linked

11:53:15.470 --> 11:53:17.012
list at the beginning of our story.

11:53:17.012 --> 11:53:21.392
N is the address of the beginning, and
it turns out, end of our linked list.

11:53:21.392 --> 11:53:24.782
So it stands to reason that
if you set list equal to n,

11:53:24.782 --> 11:53:27.662
that has the effect of
copying this address up here.

11:53:27.662 --> 11:53:30.765
Or really just copying the
arrow into that same location

11:53:30.765 --> 11:53:32.432
so that now the picture looks like this.

11:53:32.432 --> 11:53:35.822
And heck, if this was a temporary
variable, it will eventually go away.

11:53:35.822 --> 11:53:37.352
And now, this is the picture.

11:53:37.352 --> 11:53:39.512
So an annoying number
of steps, certainly,

11:53:39.512 --> 11:53:42.002
to walk through verbally like this.

11:53:42.002 --> 11:53:44.162
But it's just malloc to
give yourself a node,

11:53:44.162 --> 11:53:49.412
initialize the 2 fields inside of
it, update the linked list, and boom,

11:53:49.412 --> 11:53:50.252
you're on your way.

11:53:50.252 --> 11:53:52.392
I didn't have to copy anything.

11:53:52.392 --> 11:53:55.614
I just had to insert
something in this case.

11:53:55.614 --> 11:53:58.322
Let me pause here to see if there's
any questions on those steps.

11:53:58.322 --> 11:54:02.272
And we'll see before long it all
in context with some larger code.

11:54:02.272 --> 11:54:06.447
AUDIENCE: So if the
statements [INAUDIBLE]..

11:54:06.447 --> 11:54:07.072
SPEAKER 1: Yes.

11:54:07.072 --> 11:54:10.492
I drew them separately just
for the sake of the voiceover

11:54:10.492 --> 11:54:12.502
of doing each thing very methodically.

11:54:12.502 --> 11:54:14.572
In real code, as we'll
transition to now,

11:54:14.572 --> 11:54:16.702
I could have and should
have just done it

11:54:16.702 --> 11:54:20.482
all inside of one conditional after
checking if n is not equal to null.

11:54:20.482 --> 11:54:22.792
I could set number to a value like 1.

11:54:22.792 --> 11:54:25.897
And I could set the pointer
itself to something like null.

11:54:25.897 --> 11:54:26.512
All right.

11:54:26.512 --> 11:54:30.082
Well let's translate, then,
this into some similar code

11:54:30.082 --> 11:54:34.822
that allows us to build up a linked
list now using code similar in spirit

11:54:34.822 --> 11:54:35.632
to before.

11:54:35.632 --> 11:54:37.382
But now, using this new primitive.

11:54:37.382 --> 11:54:39.622
So I'm going to go
back into VS Code here.

11:54:39.622 --> 11:54:42.952
I'm going to go ahead now and delete
the entirety of this old version that

11:54:42.952 --> 11:54:44.752
was entirely array-based.

11:54:44.752 --> 11:54:49.952
And now, inside of my main function,
I'm going to go ahead and first do this.

11:54:49.952 --> 11:54:53.662
I'm going to first give
myself a list of size 0.

11:54:53.662 --> 11:54:56.092
And I'm going to call that node* list.

11:54:56.092 --> 11:54:59.092
And I'm going to initialize that
to null, as we proposed earlier.

11:54:59.092 --> 11:55:02.242
But I'm also now going to have to
take the additional step of defining

11:55:02.242 --> 11:55:03.452
what this node is.

11:55:03.452 --> 11:55:06.982
So recall that I might do something
like typedef, struct node.

11:55:06.982 --> 11:55:09.802
Inside of this struct node, I'm
going to have a number, which

11:55:09.802 --> 11:55:11.492
I'll call number of type int.

11:55:11.492 --> 11:55:13.642
And I'm going to have
a structure called node

11:55:13.642 --> 11:55:16.952
with a * that says the next
pointer is called next.

11:55:16.952 --> 11:55:20.632
And I'm going to call this whole
thing, more succinctly, node,

11:55:20.632 --> 11:55:22.312
instead of struct node.

11:55:22.312 --> 11:55:25.402
Now as an aside, for those of you
wondering what the difference really

11:55:25.402 --> 11:55:27.082
is between struct and node.

11:55:27.082 --> 11:55:29.932
Technically, I could
do something like this.

11:55:29.932 --> 11:55:33.442
Not use typedef and not
use the word node alone.

11:55:33.442 --> 11:55:37.162
This syntax here would actually
create for me a new data

11:55:37.162 --> 11:55:40.312
type called, verbosely, struct node.

11:55:40.312 --> 11:55:42.922
And I could use this throughout
my code saying struct node.

11:55:42.922 --> 11:55:43.942
Struct node.

11:55:43.942 --> 11:55:45.322
That just gets a little tedious.

11:55:45.322 --> 11:55:48.197
And it would be nicer just to refer
to this thing more simplistically

11:55:48.197 --> 11:55:49.232
as a node.

11:55:49.232 --> 11:55:51.712
So what typedef has
been doing for us is it,

11:55:51.712 --> 11:55:55.252
again, lets us invent our own
word that's even more succinct.

11:55:55.252 --> 11:55:58.522
And this just has the effect
now of calling this whole thing

11:55:58.522 --> 11:56:02.242
node without the need, subsequently, to
keep saying struct all over the place.

11:56:02.242 --> 11:56:03.652
Just FYI.

11:56:03.652 --> 11:56:04.162
All right.

11:56:04.162 --> 11:56:07.532
So now that this thing exists in
main, let's go ahead and do this.

11:56:07.532 --> 11:56:10.252
Let's add a number to list.

11:56:10.252 --> 11:56:12.922
And to do this, I'm going to
give myself a temporary variable.

11:56:12.922 --> 11:56:14.822
I'll call it n for consistency.

11:56:14.822 --> 11:56:18.022
I'm going to use malloc to
give myself the size of a node,

11:56:18.022 --> 11:56:19.562
just like in our slides.

11:56:19.562 --> 11:56:21.022
And then, I'm going to
do a little safety check.

11:56:21.022 --> 11:56:23.952
If n equals equals null, I'm going
to do the opposite of the slides.

11:56:23.952 --> 11:56:25.702
I'm just going to quit
out of this program

11:56:25.702 --> 11:56:28.442
because there's nothing useful
to be done at this point.

11:56:28.442 --> 11:56:31.052
But most likely my computer is
not going to run out of memory.

11:56:31.052 --> 11:56:34.232
So I'm going to assume we can keep
going with some of the logic here.

11:56:34.232 --> 11:56:38.872
If n does not equal null, and that
is it's a valid memory address,

11:56:38.872 --> 11:56:40.852
I'm going to say n []--

11:56:40.852 --> 11:56:42.412
I'm going to build this up backwards.

11:56:42.412 --> 11:56:44.189
Well let's do.

11:56:44.189 --> 11:56:45.772
That's OK, let's go ahead and do this.

11:56:45.772 --> 11:56:48.082
N [number] equals 1.

11:56:48.082 --> 11:56:52.972
And then n [arrow next] equals null.

11:56:52.972 --> 11:56:59.902
And now, update list to point
to new node, list equals n.

11:56:59.902 --> 11:57:02.062
So at this point in the
story, we've essentially

11:57:02.062 --> 11:57:06.812
constructed what was that first
picture, which looks like this.

11:57:06.812 --> 11:57:11.362
This is the corresponding code via
which we built up this node in memory.

11:57:11.362 --> 11:57:14.342
Suppose now, we want to add
the number 2 to the list.

11:57:14.342 --> 11:57:15.562
So let's do this again.

11:57:15.562 --> 11:57:20.032
Add a number to list.

11:57:20.032 --> 11:57:21.392
How might I do this?

11:57:21.392 --> 11:57:23.812
Well, I don't need to
redeclare n because I can use

11:57:23.812 --> 11:57:25.592
the same temporary variables before.

11:57:25.592 --> 11:57:30.792
So this time, I'm just going to say n
equals malloc and the size of a node.

11:57:30.792 --> 11:57:32.542
I'm, again, going to
have my safety check.

11:57:32.542 --> 11:57:36.772
So if n equals equals null, then let's
just quit out of this altogether.

11:57:36.772 --> 11:57:41.302
But, I have to be a
little more careful now.

11:57:41.302 --> 11:57:43.642
Technically speaking,
what do I still need

11:57:43.642 --> 11:57:48.022
to do before I quit out of my
program to be really proper?

11:57:48.022 --> 11:57:51.362
Free the memory that did
succeed a little higher up.

11:57:51.362 --> 11:57:56.762
So I think it suffices to free what
is now called list, way at the top.

11:57:56.762 --> 11:57:57.262
All right.

11:57:57.262 --> 11:58:03.742
Now, if all was well, though, let's
go ahead and say n [number] equals 2.

11:58:03.742 --> 11:58:09.322
And now, n [arrow next] equals null.

11:58:09.322 --> 11:58:12.382
And now, let's go ahead
and add it to the list.

11:58:12.382 --> 11:58:20.392
If I go ahead and do
list arrow next equals n,

11:58:20.392 --> 11:58:24.142
I think what we've just done is
build up the equivalent, now,

11:58:24.142 --> 11:58:27.142
of this in the computer's memory.

11:58:27.142 --> 11:58:29.662
By going to the list
field's next field, which

11:58:29.662 --> 11:58:33.562
is synonymous with the 1
nodes, bottom-most box.

11:58:33.562 --> 11:58:37.022
And store the address of what was n,
which a moment ago looked like this.

11:58:37.022 --> 11:58:39.872
And I'm just throwing away, in the
picture, the temporary variable.

11:58:39.872 --> 11:58:40.372
All right.

11:58:40.372 --> 11:58:42.362
One last thing to do.

11:58:42.362 --> 11:58:47.569
Let me go down here and say, add
a number to list, n equals malloc.

11:58:47.569 --> 11:58:48.652
Let's do it one more time.

11:58:48.652 --> 11:58:49.822
Size of node.

11:58:49.822 --> 11:58:52.762
And clearly, in a real program, we
might want to start using a loop.

11:58:52.762 --> 11:58:56.542
And do this dynamically or a function
because it's a lot of repetition now.

11:58:56.542 --> 11:58:59.602
But just to go through the
syntax here, this is fine.

11:58:59.602 --> 11:59:03.182
If n equals equals null, out
of memory for some reason.

11:59:03.182 --> 11:59:09.132
Let's return 1, but we
should free the list itself

11:59:09.132 --> 11:59:12.932
and even the second node, list [next].

11:59:12.932 --> 11:59:16.212
But I've deliberately done this poorly.

11:59:16.212 --> 11:59:16.712
All right.

11:59:16.712 --> 11:59:18.722
This is a little more subtle now.

11:59:18.722 --> 11:59:22.052
And let me get rid of the highlighting
just so it's a little more visible.

11:59:22.052 --> 11:59:26.372
If n happens to equal equal
null, and something really just

11:59:26.372 --> 11:59:32.522
went wrong they're out of memory,
why am I freeing 2 addresses now?

11:59:32.522 --> 11:59:35.252
And again, it's not that I'm
freeing those variables per se.

11:59:35.252 --> 11:59:39.102
I'm freeing the addresses
at in those variables.

11:59:39.102 --> 11:59:41.372
But there's also a
bug with my code here.

11:59:41.372 --> 11:59:43.772
And it's subtle.

11:59:43.772 --> 11:59:45.062
Let me ask more pointedly.

11:59:45.062 --> 11:59:49.165
This line here, 43, what is
that freeing specifically?

11:59:49.165 --> 11:59:49.832
Can I go to you?

11:59:49.832 --> 11:59:52.382
AUDIENCE: You're freeing list 2 times.

11:59:52.382 --> 11:59:54.122
SPEAKER 1: I'm freeing, not so.

11:59:54.122 --> 11:59:54.632
That's OK.

11:59:54.632 --> 11:59:56.222
I'm not freeing list 2 times.

11:59:56.222 --> 11:59:59.012
Technically, I'm freeing
list once and list next once.

11:59:59.012 --> 12:00:01.082
But let me just ask the
more explicit question.

12:00:01.082 --> 12:00:03.902
What am I freeing with
line 43 at the moment?

12:00:03.902 --> 12:00:06.902
Which node?

12:00:06.902 --> 12:00:08.412
I think node number 1.

12:00:08.412 --> 12:00:08.912
Why?

12:00:08.912 --> 12:00:10.922
Because if 1 is at the
beginning of the list,

12:00:10.922 --> 12:00:14.012
list contains the address
of that number 1 node.

12:00:14.012 --> 12:00:15.762
And so this frees that node.

12:00:15.762 --> 12:00:18.732
This line of code, you might
think now intuitively, OK,

12:00:18.732 --> 12:00:21.092
it's probably freeing the node number 2.

12:00:21.092 --> 12:00:22.022
But this is bad.

12:00:22.022 --> 12:00:22.892
And this is subtle.

12:00:22.892 --> 12:00:24.602
Valgrind might help you catch this.

12:00:24.602 --> 12:00:27.002
But by eyeing it, it's
not necessarily obvious.

12:00:27.002 --> 12:00:31.472
You should never touch memory
that you have already freed.

12:00:31.472 --> 12:00:34.412
And so, the fact that I did
in this order, very bad.

12:00:34.412 --> 12:00:37.112
Because I'm telling the
operating system, I don't know.

12:00:37.112 --> 12:00:39.632
I don't need the list address anymore.

12:00:39.632 --> 12:00:40.892
Do with it what you want.

12:00:40.892 --> 12:00:43.142
And then, literally one line later,
you're saying, wait a minute.

12:00:43.142 --> 12:00:45.212
Let me actually go to
that address for a moment

12:00:45.212 --> 12:00:47.882
and look at the next
field of that first node.

12:00:47.882 --> 12:00:48.702
It's too late.

12:00:48.702 --> 12:00:51.192
You've already given up
control over the node.

12:00:51.192 --> 12:00:54.212
So it's an easy fix in
this case, logically.

12:00:54.212 --> 12:00:56.852
But we should be freeing
the second node first

12:00:56.852 --> 12:01:00.542
and then the first one
so that we're doing it

12:01:00.542 --> 12:01:02.522
in, essentially, reverse order.

12:01:02.522 --> 12:01:04.439
And again, Valgrind would
help you catch that.

12:01:04.439 --> 12:01:07.064
But that's the kind of thing one
needs to be careful about when

12:01:07.064 --> 12:01:08.082
touching memory at all.

12:01:08.082 --> 12:01:10.592
You cannot touch memory
after you freed it.

12:01:10.592 --> 12:01:12.452
But here is my last step.

12:01:12.452 --> 12:01:17.972
Let me go ahead and update
the number field of n to be 3.

12:01:17.972 --> 12:01:20.982
The next node of n to be null.

12:01:20.982 --> 12:01:22.772
And then, just like
in the slide earlier,

12:01:22.772 --> 12:01:28.502
I think I can do list
next, next equals n.

12:01:28.502 --> 12:01:32.372
And that has the effect now of
building up in the computer's memory,

12:01:32.372 --> 12:01:34.472
essentially, this data structure.

12:01:34.472 --> 12:01:35.372
Very manually.

12:01:35.372 --> 12:01:36.302
Very pedantically.

12:01:36.302 --> 12:01:38.342
Like, in a better world, we'd
have a loop and some functions

12:01:38.342 --> 12:01:39.902
that are automating this process.

12:01:39.902 --> 12:01:44.162
But, for now, we're doing it just
to play around with the syntax.

12:01:44.162 --> 12:01:48.902
So at this point, unfortunately,
suppose I want to print the numbers.

12:01:48.902 --> 12:01:53.672
It's no longer as easy as int
i equals 0, i less than 3, i++.

12:01:53.672 --> 12:02:00.902
Because you cannot just
do something like this.

12:02:00.902 --> 12:02:06.002
Because pointer arithmetic
no longer comes into play

12:02:06.002 --> 12:02:10.232
when it's you, who are stitching
together the data structure in memory.

12:02:10.232 --> 12:02:12.932
In all of our past examples
with arrays, you've

12:02:12.932 --> 12:02:16.302
been trusting that all of the bytes in
the array are back, to back, to back.

12:02:16.302 --> 12:02:19.015
So it's perfectly reasonable for
the compiler and the computer

12:02:19.015 --> 12:02:21.932
to just figure out, oh, well if you
want [0], that's at the beginning.

12:02:21.932 --> 12:02:23.612
[1], it's one location over.

12:02:23.612 --> 12:02:25.592
[2], it's one location over.

12:02:25.592 --> 12:02:28.512
This is way less obvious now.

12:02:28.512 --> 12:02:32.132
Because even though you might want to
go to the first element in the linked

12:02:32.132 --> 12:02:36.752
list, or the second, or the third, you
can't just jump to those arithmetically

12:02:36.752 --> 12:02:38.072
by doing a bit of math.

12:02:38.072 --> 12:02:41.522
Instead, you have to
follow all of those arrows.

12:02:41.522 --> 12:02:44.822
So with linked lists, you can't use
this square bracket notation anymore

12:02:44.822 --> 12:02:47.792
because one node might be here,
over here, over here, over here.

12:02:47.792 --> 12:02:51.032
You can't just use some simple offset.

12:02:51.032 --> 12:02:53.822
So I think our code is going
to have to be a little fancier.

12:02:53.822 --> 12:02:57.302
And this might look scary at
first, but it's just an application

12:02:57.302 --> 12:02:59.642
of some of the basic definitions here.

12:02:59.642 --> 12:03:06.962
Let me do a for-loop that actually
uses a node* variable initialized

12:03:06.962 --> 12:03:08.612
to the list itself.

12:03:08.612 --> 12:03:13.262
I'm going to keep doing this, so
long as TMP does not equal null.

12:03:13.262 --> 12:03:15.842
And on each iteration
of this loop, I'm going

12:03:15.842 --> 12:03:20.582
to update TMP to be
whatever TMP arrow next is.

12:03:20.582 --> 12:03:23.192
And I'll remind you in a moment
and explain in more detail.

12:03:23.192 --> 12:03:27.212
But when I print something here
with printf, I can still use %i.

12:03:27.212 --> 12:03:29.522
Because it's still a number
at the end of the day.

12:03:29.522 --> 12:03:34.122
But what I want to print out is the
number in this temporary variable.

12:03:34.122 --> 12:03:36.514
So maybe the ugliest
for-loop we've ever seen.

12:03:36.514 --> 12:03:38.972
Because it's mixing, not just
the idea of a for-loop, which

12:03:38.972 --> 12:03:40.982
itself was a bit cryptic weeks ago.

12:03:40.982 --> 12:03:43.507
But now, I'm using pointers
instead of integers.

12:03:43.507 --> 12:03:45.632
But I'm not violating the
definition of a for-loop.

12:03:45.632 --> 12:03:48.422
Recall that a for-loop has 3
main things in parentheses.

12:03:48.422 --> 12:03:50.282
What do you want to initialize first?

12:03:50.282 --> 12:03:53.222
What condition do you want to
keep checking again and again?

12:03:53.222 --> 12:03:56.922
And what update do you want to make
on every iteration of the loop?

12:03:56.922 --> 12:03:59.342
So with that basic
definition in mind, this

12:03:59.342 --> 12:04:01.832
is giving me a temporary
variable called TMP

12:04:01.832 --> 12:04:04.002
that is initialized to
the beginning of the loop.

12:04:04.002 --> 12:04:07.592
So it's like pointing my
finger at the number 1 node.

12:04:07.592 --> 12:04:11.012
Then, I'm asking the question,
does TMP not equal null?

12:04:11.012 --> 12:04:13.652
Well, hopefully, not because
I'm pointing at a valid node

12:04:13.652 --> 12:04:15.192
that is the number 1 node.

12:04:15.192 --> 12:04:17.012
So, of course, it
doesn't equal null yet.

12:04:17.012 --> 12:04:19.512
Null won't be until we get
to the end of the list.

12:04:19.512 --> 12:04:21.012
So what do I do?

12:04:21.012 --> 12:04:22.742
I started this TMP variable.

12:04:22.742 --> 12:04:27.752
I follow the arrow and go to
the number field they're in.

12:04:27.752 --> 12:04:28.832
What do I then do?

12:04:28.832 --> 12:04:32.492
The for-loop says,
change TMP to be whatever

12:04:32.492 --> 12:04:36.572
is at TMP, by following the arrow
and grabbing the next field.

12:04:36.572 --> 12:04:39.742
That, then, has the result of being
checked against this conditional.

12:04:39.742 --> 12:04:42.242
No, of course, it doesn't equal
null because the second node

12:04:42.242 --> 12:04:43.532
is the number 2 node.

12:04:43.532 --> 12:04:45.402
Null is still at the very end.

12:04:45.402 --> 12:04:47.192
So I print out the number 2.

12:04:47.192 --> 12:04:51.152
Next step, I update TMP one more
time to be whatever is next.

12:04:51.152 --> 12:04:53.712
That, then, does not yet equal null.

12:04:53.712 --> 12:04:55.952
So I go ahead and print
out the number 3 node.

12:04:55.952 --> 12:05:01.602
Then one last time, I update TMP to
be whatever TMP is in the next field.

12:05:01.602 --> 12:05:05.462
But after 1, 2, 3, that
last next field is null.

12:05:05.462 --> 12:05:09.272
And so, I break out of
this for-loop altogether.

12:05:09.272 --> 12:05:12.212
So if I do this in
pictorial form, all we're

12:05:12.212 --> 12:05:15.782
doing, if I now use my finger
to represent the TMP variable.

12:05:15.782 --> 12:05:19.562
I initialize TMP to be whatever
list is, so it points here.

12:05:19.562 --> 12:05:22.262
That's obviously not null
so I print out whatever

12:05:22.262 --> 12:05:26.582
is that TMP, follow the arrow
in number, and I print that out.

12:05:26.582 --> 12:05:28.772
Then I update TMP to point here.

12:05:28.772 --> 12:05:30.559
Then I update TMP to point here.

12:05:30.559 --> 12:05:31.892
Then I update TMP to point here.

12:05:31.892 --> 12:05:32.642
Wait, that's null.

12:05:32.642 --> 12:05:34.962
The for-loop ends.

12:05:34.962 --> 12:05:39.152
So, again, admittedly much more cryptic
than our familiar int i equals 0,

12:05:39.152 --> 12:05:40.092
and so forth.

12:05:40.092 --> 12:05:46.337
But it's just a different
utilization of the for-loop syntax.

12:05:46.337 --> 12:05:46.837
Yes.

12:05:46.837 --> 12:05:50.622
AUDIENCE: How does it happen that
you're always printing out the numbers.

12:05:50.622 --> 12:05:52.500
Because it seems to me that addresses-

12:05:52.500 --> 12:05:53.542
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

12:05:53.542 --> 12:05:56.542
How is it that I'm actually printing
numbers and not printing out

12:05:56.542 --> 12:05:57.922
addresses instead.

12:05:57.922 --> 12:05:59.602
The compiler is helping me here.

12:05:59.602 --> 12:06:02.212
Because I taught it, in the
very beginning of my program,

12:06:02.212 --> 12:06:02.842
what a node is.

12:06:02.842 --> 12:06:05.212
Which looks like this here.

12:06:05.212 --> 12:06:08.992
The compiler knows that a node has
a number of fields and a next field

12:06:08.992 --> 12:06:10.912
down here, in the for-loop.

12:06:10.912 --> 12:06:16.892
Because I'm iterating using a node*
pointer, and not an int* pointer,

12:06:16.892 --> 12:06:19.642
the compiler knows that any
time I'm pointing at something,

12:06:19.642 --> 12:06:21.422
I'm pointing at the whole node.

12:06:21.422 --> 12:06:24.502
Doesn't matter where specifically in
the rectangle I'm pointing per se.

12:06:24.502 --> 12:06:26.692
It's, ultimately, pointing
at the whole node itself.

12:06:26.692 --> 12:06:30.802
And the fact that I, then, use
TMP arrow number means, OK,

12:06:30.802 --> 12:06:31.972
adjust your finger slightly.

12:06:31.972 --> 12:06:35.992
So you're literally pointing at the
number field and not the next field.

12:06:35.992 --> 12:06:40.402
So that's sufficient information for
the computer to distinguish the 2.

12:06:40.402 --> 12:06:41.042
Good question.

12:06:41.042 --> 12:06:44.212
Other questions then
on this approach here.

12:06:44.212 --> 12:06:45.524
Yeah, in the back.

12:06:45.524 --> 12:06:46.762
AUDIENCE: How would you--

12:06:46.762 --> 12:06:51.322
SPEAKER 1: How would I use a for-loop
to add elements to a linked list?

12:06:51.322 --> 12:06:56.122
You will do something like this,
if I may, in problem set 5.

12:06:56.122 --> 12:06:59.212
We will give you some of the
scaffolding for doing this.

12:06:59.212 --> 12:07:02.182
But in this coming weeks materials
will we guide you to that.

12:07:02.182 --> 12:07:04.775
But let me not spoil it just yet.

12:07:04.775 --> 12:07:05.692
Fair question, though.

12:07:05.692 --> 12:07:06.192
Yeah.

12:07:06.192 --> 12:07:08.559
AUDIENCE: So I had a
question about line 49.

12:07:08.559 --> 12:07:09.142
SPEAKER 1: OK.

12:07:09.142 --> 12:07:11.160
AUDIENCE: Is line 49
possible in line 43?

12:07:11.160 --> 12:07:12.202
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

12:07:12.202 --> 12:07:15.382
Is line 49 acceptable, even
if we freed it earlier.

12:07:15.382 --> 12:07:18.082
We didn't free it in line
43, in this case, right.

12:07:18.082 --> 12:07:22.282
You can only reach line 49,
if n does not equal null.

12:07:22.282 --> 12:07:24.472
And you do not return on line 45.

12:07:24.472 --> 12:07:25.342
So that's safe.

12:07:25.342 --> 12:07:29.662
I was only doing those freeing, if I
knew on line 45 that I'm out of here

12:07:29.662 --> 12:07:31.102
anyway, at that point.

12:07:31.102 --> 12:07:31.882
Good question.

12:07:31.882 --> 12:07:32.512
And, yeah.

12:07:32.512 --> 12:07:33.887
AUDIENCE: I had a quick question.

12:07:33.887 --> 12:07:36.862
Is TMP [INAUDIBLE].

12:07:36.862 --> 12:07:40.132
SPEAKER 1: Correct You're asking
about TMP, because it's in a for-loop,

12:07:40.132 --> 12:07:41.840
does that mean you
don't have to free it?

12:07:41.840 --> 12:07:44.242
You never have to free pointers, per se.

12:07:44.242 --> 12:07:49.042
You should only free addresses that
were returned to you by malloc.

12:07:49.042 --> 12:07:51.412
So I haven't finished
the program, to be fair.

12:07:51.412 --> 12:07:53.362
But you're not freeing variables.

12:07:53.362 --> 12:07:55.222
You're not freeing like, fields.

12:07:55.222 --> 12:07:58.352
You are freeing specific
addresses, whatever they may be.

12:07:58.352 --> 12:08:01.252
So the last thing, and I
was stalling on showing this

12:08:01.252 --> 12:08:02.932
because it too is a little cryptic.

12:08:02.932 --> 12:08:06.052
Here is how you can free,
now, a whole linked list.

12:08:06.052 --> 12:08:08.724
In the world of arrays,
recall, it was so easy.

12:08:08.724 --> 12:08:09.682
You just say free list.

12:08:09.682 --> 12:08:11.402
You return 0 and you're done.

12:08:11.402 --> 12:08:12.622
Not with a linked list.

12:08:12.622 --> 12:08:14.482
Because, again, the
computer doesn't know

12:08:14.482 --> 12:08:17.182
what you have stitched together
using all of these pointers

12:08:17.182 --> 12:08:18.622
all over the computer's memory.

12:08:18.622 --> 12:08:20.662
You need to follow those arrows.

12:08:20.662 --> 12:08:23.402
So one way to do this
would be as follows.

12:08:23.402 --> 12:08:28.402
While the list itself is not null,
so while there's a list to be freed.

12:08:28.402 --> 12:08:29.722
What do I want to do?

12:08:29.722 --> 12:08:32.454
I'm going to give myself a
temporary variable called TMP again.

12:08:32.454 --> 12:08:34.912
And it's a different TMP because
it's in a different scope.

12:08:34.912 --> 12:08:38.692
It's inside of the while loop instead
the for-loop, a few lines earlier.

12:08:38.692 --> 12:08:44.122
I am going to initialize TMP to
be the address of the next node.

12:08:44.122 --> 12:08:46.642
Just so I can get one
step ahead of things.

12:08:46.642 --> 12:08:47.932
Why am I doing this?

12:08:47.932 --> 12:08:51.812
Because now, I can boldly
free the list itself,

12:08:51.812 --> 12:08:53.452
which does not mean the whole list.

12:08:53.452 --> 12:08:56.152
Again, I'm freeing the
address in list, which

12:08:56.152 --> 12:08:58.892
is the address of the number 1 node.

12:08:58.892 --> 12:08:59.872
That's what list is.

12:08:59.872 --> 12:09:02.462
It's just the address
of the number 1 node.

12:09:02.462 --> 12:09:05.362
So if I first use TMP
to point out the number

12:09:05.362 --> 12:09:10.792
2 slightly in the middle of the picture,
then it is safe for me on line 61,

12:09:10.792 --> 12:09:12.772
at the moment, to free list.

12:09:12.772 --> 12:09:15.352
That is the address of the first node.

12:09:15.352 --> 12:09:19.642
Now I'm going to say, all right, once
I freed the first node in the list,

12:09:19.642 --> 12:09:24.562
I can update the list
itself to be literally TMP.

12:09:24.562 --> 12:09:26.602
And now, the loop repeats.

12:09:26.602 --> 12:09:27.932
So what's happening here?

12:09:27.932 --> 12:09:33.622
If you think about this picture, TMP
is initially pointing at not the list,

12:09:33.622 --> 12:09:35.032
but list arrow next.

12:09:35.032 --> 12:09:38.422
So TMP, represented by my right hand
here, is pointing at the number 2.

12:09:38.422 --> 12:09:43.012
Totally safe and reasonable to
free now the list itself a.k.a.

12:09:43.012 --> 12:09:44.632
the address of the number 1 node.

12:09:44.632 --> 12:09:47.362
That has the effect of just
throwing away the number 1 node,

12:09:47.362 --> 12:09:50.152
telling the computer you can
reuse that memory for you.

12:09:50.152 --> 12:09:53.632
The last line of code I wrote
updated list to point at the number

12:09:53.632 --> 12:09:58.042
2, at which point my loop proceeded
to do the exact same thing again.

12:09:58.042 --> 12:10:01.072
And only once my finger is
literally pointing at nowhere,

12:10:01.072 --> 12:10:03.832
the null symbol, will the
loop, by nature of a while

12:10:03.832 --> 12:10:06.472
loop as I'll toggle back to, break out.

12:10:06.472 --> 12:10:09.112
And there's nothing more to be freed.

12:10:09.112 --> 12:10:12.172
So again, what you'll see,
ultimately, in problem set 5,

12:10:12.172 --> 12:10:16.172
more on that later, is an opportunity
to play around with just this syntax.

12:10:16.172 --> 12:10:17.212
But also these ideas.

12:10:17.212 --> 12:10:20.062
But again, even though the syntax
is admittedly pretty cryptic,

12:10:20.062 --> 12:10:23.782
we're still using basics like
these for-loops or while loops.

12:10:23.782 --> 12:10:27.442
We're just starting to now
follow explicit addresses rather

12:10:27.442 --> 12:10:31.222
than letting the computer do
all of the arithmetic for us,

12:10:31.222 --> 12:10:33.117
as we previously benefited from.

12:10:33.117 --> 12:10:36.242
At the very end of this thing, I'm
going to return 0 as though all is well.

12:10:36.242 --> 12:10:39.722
And I think, then, we're good to go.

12:10:39.722 --> 12:10:40.222
All right.

12:10:40.222 --> 12:10:43.442
Questions on this linked list code now?

12:10:43.442 --> 12:10:46.192
And again, we'll walk through this
again in the coming weeks spec.

12:10:46.192 --> 12:10:46.692
Yeah.

12:10:46.692 --> 12:10:51.095
AUDIENCE: Can you explain the while
loop [INAUDIBLE] starts in other ways?

12:10:51.095 --> 12:10:51.762
SPEAKER 1: Sure.

12:10:51.762 --> 12:10:55.432
Can we explain this while loop
here for freeing the list.

12:10:55.432 --> 12:10:58.062
So notice that, first, I'm just
asking the obvious question.

12:10:58.062 --> 12:10:58.902
Is the list null?

12:10:58.902 --> 12:11:02.872
Because if it is, there's
no work to be done.

12:11:02.872 --> 12:11:06.942
However, while the list is not
null, according to line 58,

12:11:06.942 --> 12:11:08.022
what do we want to do?

12:11:08.022 --> 12:11:12.402
I want to create a temporary variable
that points at the same thing

12:11:12.402 --> 12:11:15.022
that list arrow next is pointing at.

12:11:15.022 --> 12:11:16.242
So what does that mean?

12:11:16.242 --> 12:11:17.742
Here is list.

12:11:17.742 --> 12:11:21.172
List arrow next is whatever
this thing is here.

12:11:21.172 --> 12:11:23.952
So if my right hand represents
the temporary variable,

12:11:23.952 --> 12:11:27.952
I'm literally pointing at the
same thing as the list is itself.

12:11:27.952 --> 12:11:31.122
The next line of code,
recall, was free the list.

12:11:31.122 --> 12:11:33.882
And unlike, in our world of
arrays, like half an hour

12:11:33.882 --> 12:11:36.582
ago where that just meant
free the whole darn list,

12:11:36.582 --> 12:11:41.172
you now have taken over control over the
computer's memory with a linked list,

12:11:41.172 --> 12:11:43.032
in ways that you didn't with the array.

12:11:43.032 --> 12:11:46.332
The computer knew how to free
the whole array because you

12:11:46.332 --> 12:11:48.162
malloc the whole thing at once.

12:11:48.162 --> 12:11:52.062
You are now mallocing the
linked list one node at a time.

12:11:52.062 --> 12:11:54.912
And the operating system does
not keep track of for you

12:11:54.912 --> 12:11:56.292
where all these nodes are.

12:11:56.292 --> 12:11:59.952
So when you free list,
you are literally freeing

12:11:59.952 --> 12:12:03.912
the value of the list variable,
which is just this first node here.

12:12:03.912 --> 12:12:07.302
Then my last line of code, which I'll
flip back to in a second, updates

12:12:07.302 --> 12:12:11.982
list to now ignore the
free memory and point at 2.

12:12:11.982 --> 12:12:14.562
And the story then repeats.

12:12:14.562 --> 12:12:17.982
So, again, it's just a
very pedantic way of using

12:12:17.982 --> 12:12:21.942
this new syntax of star notation,
and the arrow notation, and the like,

12:12:21.942 --> 12:12:25.902
to do the equivalent of walking
down all of these arrows.

12:12:25.902 --> 12:12:28.122
Following all of these breadcrumbs.

12:12:28.122 --> 12:12:31.422
But it does take admittedly
some getting used to.

12:12:31.422 --> 12:12:33.927
Syntax, you only have to do one week.

12:12:33.927 --> 12:12:35.802
But, again, next week
in Python will we begin

12:12:35.802 --> 12:12:37.632
to abstract a lot of
this complexity away.

12:12:37.632 --> 12:12:39.502
But none of this
complexity is going away.

12:12:39.502 --> 12:12:42.252
It's just that someone else, the
authors of Python for instance,

12:12:42.252 --> 12:12:44.390
will have automated this stuff for us.

12:12:44.390 --> 12:12:46.182
The goal this week is
to understand what it

12:12:46.182 --> 12:12:49.462
is we're going to get for
free, so to speak, next week.

12:12:49.462 --> 12:12:49.962
All right.

12:12:49.962 --> 12:12:54.292
Questions on these length lists.

12:12:54.292 --> 12:12:54.792
All right.

12:12:54.792 --> 12:12:55.932
Just, yeah, in the back.

12:12:55.932 --> 12:12:58.746
AUDIENCE: So are the while
loops strictly necessary

12:12:58.746 --> 12:13:00.210
for the freeing [INAUDIBLE].

12:13:00.210 --> 12:13:01.252
SPEAKER 1: Fair question.

12:13:01.252 --> 12:13:03.835
Let me summarize as, could we
have freed this with a for-loop?

12:13:03.835 --> 12:13:04.761
Absolutely.

12:13:04.761 --> 12:13:06.112
It just is a matter of style.

12:13:06.112 --> 12:13:09.152
It's a little more elegant to do it
in a while loop, according to me.

12:13:09.152 --> 12:13:11.154
But other people will
reasonably disagree.

12:13:11.154 --> 12:13:13.862
Anything you can do with a while
loop you can do with a for-loop,

12:13:13.862 --> 12:13:14.872
and vise versa.

12:13:14.872 --> 12:13:17.211
Do while loops, recall,
are a little different.

12:13:17.211 --> 12:13:19.854
But they will always
do at least one thing.

12:13:19.854 --> 12:13:22.312
But for-loops and while loops
behave the same in this case.

12:13:22.312 --> 12:13:23.435
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

12:13:23.435 --> 12:13:24.102
SPEAKER 1: Sure.

12:13:24.102 --> 12:13:25.482
Other questions?

12:13:25.482 --> 12:13:27.881
All right, well let's just
vary things a little bit here.

12:13:27.881 --> 12:13:29.964
Just to see what some of
the pitfalls might now be

12:13:29.964 --> 12:13:31.722
without getting into the weeds of code.

12:13:31.722 --> 12:13:35.711
Indeed, we'll try to save some of
that for problem set 5's exploration.

12:13:35.711 --> 12:13:40.002
But instead, let's imagine that we
want to create a list here of our own.

12:13:40.002 --> 12:13:43.182
I can offer, in exchange for a
few volunteers, some foam fingers

12:13:43.182 --> 12:13:45.099
to bring to the next game, perhaps.

12:13:45.099 --> 12:13:46.932
Could we get maybe just
one volunteer first?

12:13:46.932 --> 12:13:47.591
Come on up.

12:13:47.591 --> 12:13:50.591
You will be our linked
list from the get go.

12:13:50.591 --> 12:13:51.395
What's your name?

12:13:51.395 --> 12:13:52.062
AUDIENCE: Pedro.

12:13:52.062 --> 12:13:54.322
SPEAKER 1: Pedro, come on up.

12:13:54.322 --> 12:13:55.572
All right, thank you to Pedro.

12:13:55.572 --> 12:13:58.662
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:13:58.662 --> 12:14:00.662
And if you want to just
stand roughly over here.

12:14:00.662 --> 12:14:03.211
But you are a null pointer so
just point sort of at the ground,

12:14:03.211 --> 12:14:04.412
as though you're pointing at 0.

12:14:04.412 --> 12:14:04.912
All right.

12:14:04.912 --> 12:14:07.509
So Pedro is our linked list
of size 0, which pictorially

12:14:07.509 --> 12:14:10.801
might look a little something like this
for consistency with our past pictures.

12:14:10.801 --> 12:14:15.482
Now suppose that we want to go ahead
and malloc, oh, how about the number 2.

12:14:15.482 --> 12:14:17.682
Can we get a volunteer
to be on camera here?

12:14:17.682 --> 12:14:18.182
OK.

12:14:18.182 --> 12:14:19.349
You jumped out of your seat.

12:14:19.349 --> 12:14:21.890
Do you want to come up?

12:14:21.890 --> 12:14:23.682
OK, you really want
the foam finger, I say.

12:14:23.682 --> 12:14:23.852
All right.

12:14:23.852 --> 12:14:24.932
Round of applause, sure.

12:14:24.932 --> 12:14:30.172
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:14:30.172 --> 12:14:30.717
OK.

12:14:30.717 --> 12:14:31.592
And what's your name?

12:14:31.592 --> 12:14:32.452
AUDIENCE: Caleb.

12:14:32.452 --> 12:14:32.912
SPEAKER 1: Say again?

12:14:32.912 --> 12:14:33.242
AUDIENCE: Caleb.

12:14:33.242 --> 12:14:33.512
SPEAKER 1: Halen?

12:14:33.512 --> 12:14:34.244
AUDIENCE: Caleb.

12:14:34.244 --> 12:14:34.952
SPEAKER 1: Caleb.

12:14:34.952 --> 12:14:36.252
Caleb, sorry.

12:14:36.252 --> 12:14:36.752
All right.

12:14:36.752 --> 12:14:39.272
So here is your number
2 for your number field.

12:14:39.272 --> 12:14:40.502
And here is your pointer.

12:14:40.502 --> 12:14:43.597
And come on, let's say that there
was room for Caleb like, right there.

12:14:43.597 --> 12:14:44.222
That's perfect.

12:14:44.222 --> 12:14:46.962
So Caleb got malloced,
if you will, over here.

12:14:46.962 --> 12:14:51.287
So now if we want to insert Caleb and
the number 2 into this linked list,

12:14:51.287 --> 12:14:52.412
well what do we need to do?

12:14:52.412 --> 12:14:53.822
I already initialized you to 2.

12:14:53.822 --> 12:14:55.802
And pointing as you
are to the ground means

12:14:55.802 --> 12:14:58.112
you're initialized to
null for your next field.

12:14:58.112 --> 12:14:59.882
Pedro, what you should you-- perfect.

12:14:59.882 --> 12:15:01.202
What should Pedro do.

12:15:01.202 --> 12:15:02.102
That's fine, too.

12:15:02.102 --> 12:15:03.677
So Pedro is now pointing at the list.

12:15:03.677 --> 12:15:05.802
So now our list looks a
little something like this.

12:15:05.802 --> 12:15:07.022
So far, so good.

12:15:07.022 --> 12:15:07.652
All is well.

12:15:07.652 --> 12:15:10.152
So the first couple of these
will be pretty straightforward.

12:15:10.152 --> 12:15:13.662
Let's insert one more, if anyone
really wants another foam finger.

12:15:13.662 --> 12:15:15.162
Here, how about right in the middle.

12:15:15.162 --> 12:15:16.352
Come on down.

12:15:16.352 --> 12:15:19.160
And just in anticipation, how
about let's malloc someone else.

12:15:19.160 --> 12:15:20.702
OK, your friends are pointing at you.

12:15:20.702 --> 12:15:22.832
Do you want to come
down too, preemptively?

12:15:22.832 --> 12:15:25.334
This is a pool of memory, if you will.

12:15:25.334 --> 12:15:26.042
What's your name?

12:15:26.042 --> 12:15:26.612
AUDIENCE: Hannah.

12:15:26.612 --> 12:15:27.362
SPEAKER 1: Hannah.

12:15:27.362 --> 12:15:28.082
All right, Hanna.

12:15:28.082 --> 12:15:28.922
You are number 4.

12:15:28.922 --> 12:15:30.662
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:15:30.662 --> 12:15:32.292
And hang there for just a moment.

12:15:32.292 --> 12:15:32.792
All right.

12:15:32.792 --> 12:15:34.352
So we've just malloced Hannah.

12:15:34.352 --> 12:15:37.622
And Hannah, how about Hannah,
suppose you ended up over there

12:15:37.622 --> 12:15:39.282
in just some random location.

12:15:39.282 --> 12:15:39.782
All right.

12:15:39.782 --> 12:15:43.442
So what should we now do, if the
goal is to keep these things sorted?

12:15:43.442 --> 12:15:44.042
How about?

12:15:44.042 --> 12:15:46.020
So Pedro, do you have
to update yourself?

12:15:46.020 --> 12:15:46.562
AUDIENCE: No.

12:15:46.562 --> 12:15:46.892
SPEAKER 1: No.

12:15:46.892 --> 12:15:47.392
All right.

12:15:47.392 --> 12:15:48.782
Caleb, what do you have to do?

12:15:48.782 --> 12:15:49.282
OK.

12:15:49.282 --> 12:15:52.174
And Hannah what should you be doing?

12:15:52.174 --> 12:15:55.382
I would, it's just for you for now, so
point at the ground representing null.

12:15:55.382 --> 12:15:55.882
OK.

12:15:55.882 --> 12:15:58.772
So, again demonstrating the fact
that, unlike in past weeks where

12:15:58.772 --> 12:16:01.292
we had our nice, clean array
back, to back, to back,

12:16:01.292 --> 12:16:03.862
contiguously, these guys are
deliberately all over the stage.

12:16:03.862 --> 12:16:04.862
So let's malloc another.

12:16:04.862 --> 12:16:06.494
How about number 5.

12:16:06.494 --> 12:16:07.202
What's your name?

12:16:07.202 --> 12:16:07.922
AUDIENCE: Jonathan.

12:16:07.922 --> 12:16:08.402
SPEAKER 1: Jonathan.

12:16:08.402 --> 12:16:09.235
All right, Jonathan.

12:16:09.235 --> 12:16:10.922
You are our number 5.

12:16:10.922 --> 12:16:12.737
And pick your favorite place in memory.

12:16:12.737 --> 12:16:13.682
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:16:13.682 --> 12:16:14.182
OK.

12:16:16.302 --> 12:16:16.802
All right.

12:16:16.802 --> 12:16:19.030
So Jonathan's now over there.

12:16:19.030 --> 12:16:20.072
And Hannah is over there.

12:16:20.072 --> 12:16:21.929
So 5, we want to point
Hannah at number 5.

12:16:21.929 --> 12:16:23.762
So you, of course, are
going to point there.

12:16:23.762 --> 12:16:25.137
And where should you be pointing?

12:16:25.137 --> 12:16:26.982
Down to represent null, as well.

12:16:26.982 --> 12:16:27.482
OK.

12:16:27.482 --> 12:16:29.035
So pretty straightforward.

12:16:29.035 --> 12:16:30.702
But now things get a little interesting.

12:16:30.702 --> 12:16:33.482
And here, we'll use a chance
to, without the weeds of code,

12:16:33.482 --> 12:16:36.572
point out how order of operations
is really going to matter.

12:16:36.572 --> 12:16:40.802
Suppose that I next want to
allocate say, the number 1.

12:16:40.802 --> 12:16:42.992
And I want to insert the
number 1 into this list.

12:16:42.992 --> 12:16:43.492
Yes.

12:16:43.492 --> 12:16:45.102
This is what the code would look like.

12:16:45.102 --> 12:16:48.662
But if we act this out-- could
we get one more volunteer?

12:16:48.662 --> 12:16:50.472
How about on the end
there in the sweater.

12:16:50.472 --> 12:16:50.972
Yeah.

12:16:50.972 --> 12:16:52.262
Come on down.

12:16:52.262 --> 12:16:53.432
We have, what's your name?

12:16:53.432 --> 12:16:54.332
AUDIENCE: Lauren.

12:16:54.332 --> 12:16:54.782
SPEAKER 1: Lauren.

12:16:54.782 --> 12:16:55.022
OK.

12:16:55.022 --> 12:16:56.132
Lauren, come on down.

12:16:56.132 --> 12:17:01.457
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:17:01.457 --> 12:17:03.332
And how about, Lauren,
why don't you go right

12:17:03.332 --> 12:17:04.952
in here in front, if you don't mind.

12:17:04.952 --> 12:17:06.152
Here is your number.

12:17:06.152 --> 12:17:07.262
Here is your pointer.

12:17:07.262 --> 12:17:09.332
So I've initialized
Lauren to the number 1.

12:17:09.332 --> 12:17:11.942
And your pointer will be
null, pointing at the ground.

12:17:11.942 --> 12:17:14.485
Where do you belong if we're
maintaining sorted order?

12:17:14.485 --> 12:17:15.902
Looks like right at the beginning.

12:17:15.902 --> 12:17:18.402
What should happen here?

12:17:18.402 --> 12:17:18.902
OK.

12:17:18.902 --> 12:17:23.582
So Pedro has presumed
to point now at Lauren.

12:17:23.582 --> 12:17:27.812
But how do you know where to point?

12:17:27.812 --> 12:17:28.982
AUDIENCE: He's number 2.

12:17:28.982 --> 12:17:30.882
SPEAKER 1: Pedro's undoing
what he did a moment ago.

12:17:30.882 --> 12:17:31.862
So this was deliberate.

12:17:31.862 --> 12:17:35.232
And that was perfect that Pedro
presumed to point immediately at Lauren.

12:17:35.232 --> 12:17:35.732
Why?

12:17:35.732 --> 12:17:39.432
You literally just orphaned all of these
folks, all of these chunks of memory.

12:17:39.432 --> 12:17:39.932
Why?

12:17:39.932 --> 12:17:44.282
Because if Pedro was our only variable
pointing at that chunk of memory,

12:17:44.282 --> 12:17:47.282
this is the danger of using pointers,
and dynamic memory allocation,

12:17:47.282 --> 12:17:48.662
and building your own data structures.

12:17:48.662 --> 12:17:50.620
The moment you point
temporarily, if you could,

12:17:50.620 --> 12:17:53.972
to Lauren, I have no idea
where he's pointing to.

12:17:53.972 --> 12:17:58.742
I have no idea how to get back to Caleb,
or Hannah, or anyone else on stage.

12:17:58.742 --> 12:17:59.522
So that was bad.

12:17:59.522 --> 12:18:00.792
So you did undo it.

12:18:00.792 --> 12:18:01.772
So that's good.

12:18:01.772 --> 12:18:03.782
I think we need Lauren
to make a decision first.

12:18:03.782 --> 12:18:04.892
Who should you point at?

12:18:04.892 --> 12:18:05.132
AUDIENCE: Caleb.

12:18:05.132 --> 12:18:06.302
SPEAKER 1: So pointing at Caleb.

12:18:06.302 --> 12:18:06.602
Why?

12:18:06.602 --> 12:18:09.185
Because you're pointing at
literally who Pedro is pointing at.

12:18:09.185 --> 12:18:10.972
Pedro, now what are you safe to do?

12:18:10.972 --> 12:18:11.472
Good.

12:18:11.472 --> 12:18:13.212
So order of operations there matters.

12:18:13.212 --> 12:18:17.312
And if we had just done this line
of code in red here, list equals n.

12:18:17.312 --> 12:18:20.222
That was like Pedro's first
instinct, bad things happen.

12:18:20.222 --> 12:18:22.182
And we orphaned the rest of the list.

12:18:22.182 --> 12:18:25.832
But if we think through it logically and
do this, as Lauren did for us, instead,

12:18:25.832 --> 12:18:29.322
we've now updated the list to look
a little something more like this.

12:18:29.322 --> 12:18:30.392
Let's do one last one.

12:18:30.392 --> 12:18:32.967
We got one more foam finger
here for the number 3.

12:18:32.967 --> 12:18:33.842
How about on the end?

12:18:33.842 --> 12:18:34.342
Yeah.

12:18:34.342 --> 12:18:35.672
You want to come down.

12:18:35.672 --> 12:18:36.332
All right.

12:18:36.332 --> 12:18:37.382
One final volunteer.

12:18:37.382 --> 12:18:43.492
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:18:43.492 --> 12:18:43.992
All right.

12:18:43.992 --> 12:18:44.867
And what's your name?

12:18:44.867 --> 12:18:45.712
AUDIENCE: Miriam.

12:18:45.712 --> 12:18:45.912
SPEAKER 1: I'm sorry?

12:18:45.912 --> 12:18:46.422
AUDIENCE: Miriam.

12:18:46.422 --> 12:18:46.962
SPEAKER 1: Miriam.

12:18:46.962 --> 12:18:47.232
All right.

12:18:47.232 --> 12:18:48.342
So here is your number 3.

12:18:48.342 --> 12:18:49.217
Here is your pointer.

12:18:49.217 --> 12:18:52.852
If you want to go maybe in the middle of
the stage in a random memory location.

12:18:52.852 --> 12:18:56.752
So here, too, the goal is
to maintain sorted order.

12:18:56.752 --> 12:19:01.882
So let's ask the audience, who or what
number should point at whom first here?

12:19:01.882 --> 12:19:04.392
So we don't screw up and
orphan some of the memory.

12:19:04.392 --> 12:19:07.722
And if we do orphan memory, this is
what's called, again per last week,

12:19:07.722 --> 12:19:08.592
a memory leak.

12:19:08.592 --> 12:19:10.902
Your Mac, your PC, your
phone can start to slow down

12:19:10.902 --> 12:19:14.092
if you keep asking for memory but
never give it back or lose track of it.

12:19:14.092 --> 12:19:15.912
So we want to get this right.

12:19:15.912 --> 12:19:17.622
Who should point at whom?

12:19:17.622 --> 12:19:18.852
Or what number?

12:19:18.852 --> 12:19:19.794
Say again.

12:19:19.794 --> 12:19:20.502
AUDIENCE: 3 to 4.

12:19:20.502 --> 12:19:22.182
SPEAKER 1: 3 should point at 4.

12:19:22.182 --> 12:19:25.572
So 3, do you want to point at 4.

12:19:25.572 --> 12:19:27.282
And not, so, OK, good.

12:19:27.282 --> 12:19:32.442
And how did you know,
Miriam, whom to point at?

12:19:32.442 --> 12:19:33.480
AUDIENCE: Copying Caleb.

12:19:33.480 --> 12:19:34.272
SPEAKER 1: Perfect.

12:19:34.272 --> 12:19:35.632
OK, so copying Caleb.

12:19:35.632 --> 12:19:36.132
Why?

12:19:36.132 --> 12:19:39.702
Because if you look at where this
list is currently constructed,

12:19:39.702 --> 12:19:42.552
and you can cheat on the board
here, 2 is pointing to 4.

12:19:42.552 --> 12:19:46.122
If you point at whoever Caleb,
number 2, is pointing out,

12:19:46.122 --> 12:19:48.942
that, indeed, leads you
to Hannah for number 4.

12:19:48.942 --> 12:19:53.082
So now what's the next step
to stitch this together?

12:19:53.082 --> 12:19:54.702
Our voice in the crowd.

12:19:54.702 --> 12:19:55.632
AUDIENCE: 2 to 3.

12:19:55.632 --> 12:19:56.742
SPEAKER 1: 2 to 3.

12:19:56.742 --> 12:19:57.792
So, 2 to 3.

12:19:57.792 --> 12:20:00.385
So Caleb, I think it's now
safe for you to decouple.

12:20:00.385 --> 12:20:02.302
Because someone is already
pointing at Hannah.

12:20:02.302 --> 12:20:03.427
We haven't orphaned anyone.

12:20:03.427 --> 12:20:05.322
So now, if we follow
the breadcrumbs, we've

12:20:05.322 --> 12:20:10.352
got Pedro leading to 1,
to 2, to 3, to 4, to 5.

12:20:10.352 --> 12:20:12.852
We need the numbers back, but
you can keep the foam fingers.

12:20:12.852 --> 12:20:15.019
Thank you to our volunteers here.

12:20:15.019 --> 12:20:15.852
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

12:20:15.852 --> 12:20:16.352
Thank you.

12:20:16.352 --> 12:20:17.742
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

12:20:17.742 --> 12:20:20.739
SPEAKER 1: You can just
put the numbers here.

12:20:20.739 --> 12:20:21.572
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

12:20:21.572 --> 12:20:22.739
SPEAKER 1: Thank you to all.

12:20:22.739 --> 12:20:26.682
So this is only to say that when you
start looking at the code this week

12:20:26.682 --> 12:20:29.245
and in the problem set, it's
going to be very easy to lose

12:20:29.245 --> 12:20:30.662
sight of the forest for the trees.

12:20:30.662 --> 12:20:32.702
Because the code does get really dense.

12:20:32.702 --> 12:20:37.722
But the idea is, again, really do bubble
up to these higher level descriptions.

12:20:37.722 --> 12:20:40.782
And if you think about data
structures at this level.

12:20:40.782 --> 12:20:42.899
If you go off in program
after a class like CS50

12:20:42.899 --> 12:20:45.482
and your whiteboarding something
with a friend or a colleague,

12:20:45.482 --> 12:20:48.512
most people think at
and talk at this level.

12:20:48.512 --> 12:20:51.032
And they just assume that,
yeah, if we went back and looked

12:20:51.032 --> 12:20:54.372
at our textbooks or class notes, we
could figure out how to implement this.

12:20:54.372 --> 12:20:56.222
But the important stuff
is the conversation.

12:20:56.222 --> 12:20:57.602
And the idea is up here.

12:20:57.602 --> 12:21:02.562
Even though, via this week, will we
get some practice with the actual code.

12:21:02.562 --> 12:21:06.572
So when it comes to analyzing
an algorithm like this,

12:21:06.572 --> 12:21:08.642
let's consider the following.

12:21:08.642 --> 12:21:15.962
What might be now the running time of
operations like searching and inserting

12:21:15.962 --> 12:21:17.582
into a linked list?

12:21:17.582 --> 12:21:19.292
We talked about arrays earlier.

12:21:19.292 --> 12:21:22.292
And we had some binary search
possibilities still, as soon

12:21:22.292 --> 12:21:23.132
as it's an array.

12:21:23.132 --> 12:21:26.312
But as soon as we have a linked list,
these arrows, like our volunteers,

12:21:26.312 --> 12:21:27.662
could be anywhere on stage.

12:21:27.662 --> 12:21:29.370
And so you can't just
assume that you can

12:21:29.370 --> 12:21:32.162
jump arithmetically to the middle
element, to the middle element,

12:21:32.162 --> 12:21:32.982
to the middle one.

12:21:32.982 --> 12:21:36.572
You pretty much have to follow all
of these breadcrumbs again and again.

12:21:36.572 --> 12:21:39.362
So how might that inform what we see?

12:21:39.362 --> 12:21:41.077
Well, consider this too.

12:21:41.077 --> 12:21:43.952
Even though I keep drawing all these
pictures with all of the numbers

12:21:43.952 --> 12:21:44.462
exposed.

12:21:44.462 --> 12:21:46.254
And all of us humans
in the room can easily

12:21:46.254 --> 12:21:49.842
spot where the 1 is, where the 2 is,
where the 3 is, the computer, again,

12:21:49.842 --> 12:21:54.092
just like with our lockers and arrays,
can only see one location at a time.

12:21:54.092 --> 12:21:57.992
And the key thing with a linked
list is that the only address

12:21:57.992 --> 12:22:01.892
we've fundamentally been remembering
is what Pedro represented a moment ago.

12:22:01.892 --> 12:22:05.472
He was the link to all
of the other nodes.

12:22:05.472 --> 12:22:07.472
And, in turn, each
person led to the next.

12:22:07.472 --> 12:22:12.132
But without Pedro, we would have lost
some of, or all of, the linked list.

12:22:12.132 --> 12:22:14.432
So when you start with
a linked list, if you

12:22:14.432 --> 12:22:18.212
want to find an element as via
search, you have to do it linearly.

12:22:18.212 --> 12:22:19.682
Following all of the arrows.

12:22:19.682 --> 12:22:21.692
Following all of the
pointers on the stage

12:22:21.692 --> 12:22:23.822
in order to get to the node in question.

12:22:23.822 --> 12:22:27.182
And only once you hit null can
you conclude, yep, it was there.

12:22:27.182 --> 12:22:28.982
Or no, it was not.

12:22:28.982 --> 12:22:31.922
So given that if a
computer, essentially,

12:22:31.922 --> 12:22:36.452
can only see the number 1, or the number
2, or the number 3, or the number 4,

12:22:36.452 --> 12:22:39.752
or the number 5, one
at a time, how might we

12:22:39.752 --> 12:22:43.172
think about the running time of search?

12:22:43.172 --> 12:22:45.092
And it is indeed Big O of n.

12:22:45.092 --> 12:22:45.892
But why is that?

12:22:45.892 --> 12:22:48.392
Well, in the worst case, the
number you might be looking for

12:22:48.392 --> 12:22:49.962
is all the way at the end.

12:22:49.962 --> 12:22:53.192
And so, obviously, you're going to
have to search all of the n elements.

12:22:53.192 --> 12:22:55.425
And I drew these things
with boxes on top of them.

12:22:55.425 --> 12:22:57.842
Because, again, even though
you and I can immediately see,

12:22:57.842 --> 12:23:00.092
where the 5 is for
instance, the computer

12:23:00.092 --> 12:23:03.962
can only figure that out by starting
at the beginning and going there.

12:23:03.962 --> 12:23:05.882
So there, too, is another trade off.

12:23:05.882 --> 12:23:09.512
It would seem that, overnight,
we have lost the ability

12:23:09.512 --> 12:23:14.672
to do a very powerful algorithm from
week 0 known as binary search, right.

12:23:14.672 --> 12:23:15.302
It's gone.

12:23:15.302 --> 12:23:19.292
Because there's no way in this
picture to jump mathematically

12:23:19.292 --> 12:23:21.857
to the middle node, unless
you remember where it is.

12:23:21.857 --> 12:23:23.732
And then, remember where
every other node is.

12:23:23.732 --> 12:23:25.524
And at that point,
you're back to an array.

12:23:25.524 --> 12:23:29.862
Linked list, by design, only
remember the next node in the list.

12:23:29.862 --> 12:23:30.362
All right.

12:23:30.362 --> 12:23:32.852
How about something like insert?

12:23:32.852 --> 12:23:35.672
In the worst case,
perhaps, how many steps

12:23:35.672 --> 12:23:38.822
might it take to insert
something into a linked list?

12:23:38.822 --> 12:23:40.480
Someone else.

12:23:40.480 --> 12:23:41.022
Someone else.

12:23:41.022 --> 12:23:41.522
Yeah.

12:23:41.522 --> 12:23:42.542
AUDIENCE: N squared.

12:23:42.542 --> 12:23:42.962
SPEAKER 1: Say again?

12:23:42.962 --> 12:23:43.802
AUDIENCE: N squared.

12:23:43.802 --> 12:23:44.372
SPEAKER 1: N squared.

12:23:44.372 --> 12:23:45.714
Fortunately, it's not that bad.

12:23:45.714 --> 12:23:46.922
It's not as bad as n squared.

12:23:46.922 --> 12:23:49.202
That typically means
doing n things, n times.

12:23:49.202 --> 12:23:53.742
And I think we can stay under
that, but not a bad thought.

12:23:53.742 --> 12:23:54.242
Yeah.

12:23:54.242 --> 12:23:55.314
AUDIENCE: Is it n?

12:23:55.314 --> 12:23:56.522
SPEAKER 1: Why would it be n?

12:23:56.522 --> 12:24:00.269
AUDIENCE: Because the [INAUDIBLE].

12:24:00.269 --> 12:24:00.852
SPEAKER 1: OK.

12:24:00.852 --> 12:24:03.132
So to summarize, you're proposing n.

12:24:03.132 --> 12:24:04.995
Because to find where
the thing goes, you

12:24:04.995 --> 12:24:06.912
have to traverse,
potentially, the whole list.

12:24:06.912 --> 12:24:09.702
Because if I'm inserting the
number 6 or the number 99,

12:24:09.702 --> 12:24:12.252
that numerically
belongs at the very end,

12:24:12.252 --> 12:24:15.312
I can only find its location
by looking for all of them.

12:24:15.312 --> 12:24:16.850
At this point, though, in the term.

12:24:16.850 --> 12:24:18.642
And really, at this
point in the story, you

12:24:18.642 --> 12:24:22.072
should start to question these very
simplistic questions, to be honest.

12:24:22.072 --> 12:24:25.842
Because the answer is almost
always going to depend, right.

12:24:25.842 --> 12:24:28.462
If I've just got a link to
list that looks like this,

12:24:28.462 --> 12:24:31.722
the first question back to
someone asking this question

12:24:31.722 --> 12:24:34.782
would be, well does the list
need to be sorted, right?

12:24:34.782 --> 12:24:37.174
I've drawn it as sorted
and it might imply as much.

12:24:37.174 --> 12:24:39.132
So that's a reasonable
assumption to have made.

12:24:39.132 --> 12:24:41.802
But if I don't care about
maintaining sorted order,

12:24:41.802 --> 12:24:45.672
I could actually insert into a
linked list in constant time.

12:24:45.672 --> 12:24:46.212
Why?

12:24:46.212 --> 12:24:49.110
I could just keep inserting into
the beginning, into the beginning,

12:24:49.110 --> 12:24:49.902
into the beginning.

12:24:49.902 --> 12:24:51.792
And even though the
list is getting longer,

12:24:51.792 --> 12:24:55.752
the number of steps required to insert
something between the first element

12:24:55.752 --> 12:24:57.702
is not growing at all.

12:24:57.702 --> 12:25:00.222
You just keep inserting.

12:25:00.222 --> 12:25:02.382
If you want to keep it
sorted though, yes, it's

12:25:02.382 --> 12:25:03.792
going to be, indeed, Big O of n.

12:25:03.792 --> 12:25:05.322
But again, these kinds
of, now, assumptions

12:25:05.322 --> 12:25:06.530
are going to start to matter.

12:25:06.530 --> 12:25:09.222
So let's for the sake of
discussion say it's Big O of n,

12:25:09.222 --> 12:25:11.142
if we do want to maintain sorted order.

12:25:11.142 --> 12:25:14.292
But what about in the
case of not caring.

12:25:14.292 --> 12:25:16.110
It might indeed be a Big O of 1.

12:25:16.110 --> 12:25:19.152
And now these are the kinds of decisions
that will start to leave to you.

12:25:19.152 --> 12:25:20.682
What about in the best case here?

12:25:20.682 --> 12:25:22.722
If we're thinking about
Big Omega notation,

12:25:22.722 --> 12:25:25.114
then, frankly, we could just
get lucky in the best case.

12:25:25.114 --> 12:25:27.822
And the element we're looking for
happens to be at the beginning.

12:25:27.822 --> 12:25:32.052
Or heck, we just blindly insert to the
beginning irrespective of the order

12:25:32.052 --> 12:25:33.982
that we want to keep things in.

12:25:33.982 --> 12:25:34.482
All right.

12:25:34.482 --> 12:25:39.900
So besides then, how can we
improve further on this design?

12:25:39.900 --> 12:25:41.442
We don't need to stop at linked list.

12:25:41.442 --> 12:25:43.572
Because, honestly, it's
not been a clear win.

12:25:43.572 --> 12:25:46.422
Like, linked list allow us
to use more of our memory

12:25:46.422 --> 12:25:49.912
because we don't need massive
growing chunks of contiguous memory.

12:25:49.912 --> 12:25:50.782
So that's a win.

12:25:50.782 --> 12:25:54.792
But they still require Big O of
n time to find the end of it,

12:25:54.792 --> 12:25:56.112
if we care about order.

12:25:56.112 --> 12:25:59.352
We're using at least twice as
much memory for the darn pointer.

12:25:59.352 --> 12:26:01.602
So that seems like a sidestep.

12:26:01.602 --> 12:26:03.582
It's not really a step forward.

12:26:03.582 --> 12:26:05.322
So can we do better?

12:26:05.322 --> 12:26:09.639
Here's where we can now accelerate the
story by just stipulating that, hey,

12:26:09.639 --> 12:26:11.472
even if you haven't
used this technique yet,

12:26:11.472 --> 12:26:15.612
we would seem to have an ability to
stitch together pieces of memory just

12:26:15.612 --> 12:26:16.602
using pointers .

12:26:16.602 --> 12:26:19.002
And anything you could
imagine drawing with arrows,

12:26:19.002 --> 12:26:21.622
you can implement, it
would seem, in code.

12:26:21.622 --> 12:26:24.102
So what if we leverage
a second dimension.

12:26:24.102 --> 12:26:26.619
Instead of just stringing
together things laterally,

12:26:26.619 --> 12:26:28.452
left to right, essentially,
even though they

12:26:28.452 --> 12:26:30.102
were bouncing around on the screen.

12:26:30.102 --> 12:26:33.252
What if we start to leverage a
second dimension here, so to speak.

12:26:33.252 --> 12:26:36.882
And build more interesting
structures in the computer's memory.

12:26:36.882 --> 12:26:39.672
Well it turns out that
in a computer's memory,

12:26:39.672 --> 12:26:42.612
we could create a tree,
similar to a family tree.

12:26:42.612 --> 12:26:46.362
If you've ever seen or draw on a family
tree with grandparents, and parents,

12:26:46.362 --> 12:26:47.652
and siblings, and so forth.

12:26:50.442 --> 12:26:53.652
So inverted branch of a
tree that grows, typically

12:26:53.652 --> 12:26:56.532
when it's drawn, downward instead
of upward like a typical tree.

12:26:56.532 --> 12:26:59.022
But that's something we could
translate into code as well.

12:26:59.022 --> 12:27:02.722
Specifically, let's do something
called a binary search tree.

12:27:02.722 --> 12:27:04.602
Which is a type of tree.

12:27:04.602 --> 12:27:07.152
And what I mean by
this is the following.

12:27:07.152 --> 12:27:07.962
Notice this.

12:27:07.962 --> 12:27:10.842
This is an example of an
array from like week 2,

12:27:10.842 --> 12:27:12.232
when we first talked about those.

12:27:12.232 --> 12:27:13.932
And we had the lockers on stage.

12:27:13.932 --> 12:27:19.962
And recall that what was nice
about an array, if 1, it's sorted.

12:27:19.962 --> 12:27:23.022
And 2, all of its numbers
are indeed contiguous,

12:27:23.022 --> 12:27:25.012
which is by definition an array.

12:27:25.012 --> 12:27:26.752
We can just do some simple math.

12:27:26.752 --> 12:27:31.462
For instance, if there are 7 elements
in this array, and we do 7 divided by 2,

12:27:31.462 --> 12:27:31.962
that's what?

12:27:31.962 --> 12:27:34.812
3 and 1/2, round down
through truncation, that's 3.

12:27:34.812 --> 12:27:36.162
0, 1, 2, 3.

12:27:36.162 --> 12:27:39.415
That gives me the middle element,
arithmetically, in this thing.

12:27:39.415 --> 12:27:41.832
And even though I have to be
careful about rounding, using

12:27:41.832 --> 12:27:45.912
simple arithmetic, I can very quickly,
with a single line of code or math,

12:27:45.912 --> 12:27:48.372
find for you the middle of the
left half, of the left half,

12:27:48.372 --> 12:27:49.664
of the right half, or whatever.

12:27:49.664 --> 12:27:50.962
That's the power of arrays.

12:27:50.962 --> 12:27:52.902
And that's what gave us binary search.

12:27:52.902 --> 12:27:54.422
And how did binary search work?

12:27:54.422 --> 12:27:55.672
Well, we looked at the middle.

12:27:55.672 --> 12:27:57.312
And then, we went left or right.

12:27:57.312 --> 12:28:02.562
And then, we went left or right again,
implied by this color scheme here.

12:28:02.562 --> 12:28:07.692
Wouldn't it be nice if we
somehow preserved the new upsides

12:28:07.692 --> 12:28:10.520
today of dynamic memory
allocation, giving ourselves

12:28:10.520 --> 12:28:13.062
the ability to just add another
element, add another element,

12:28:13.062 --> 12:28:14.232
add another element.

12:28:14.232 --> 12:28:16.782
But retain the power of binary search.

12:28:16.782 --> 12:28:21.582
Because log of n was much better than
n, certainly for large data sets, right.

12:28:21.582 --> 12:28:24.462
Even the phone book
demonstrated as much weeks ago.

12:28:24.462 --> 12:28:28.492
So what if I draw this same
picture in 2 dimensions.

12:28:28.492 --> 12:28:32.442
And I preserve the color scheme,
just so it's obvious what came where.

12:28:32.442 --> 12:28:35.982
What are these things look like now?

12:28:35.982 --> 12:28:38.532
Maybe, like, things we
might now call nodes, right.

12:28:38.532 --> 12:28:42.512
A node is just a generic term
for like, storing some data.

12:28:42.512 --> 12:28:45.682
What if the data these nodes
are storing are numbers.

12:28:45.682 --> 12:28:47.212
So still integers.

12:28:47.212 --> 12:28:51.342
But what if we connected these
cleverly, like an old family tree.

12:28:51.342 --> 12:28:56.712
Whereby, every node has not one
pointer now, but as many as 2.

12:28:56.712 --> 12:28:59.812
Maybe 0, like in the leaves
at the bottom are in green.

12:28:59.812 --> 12:29:02.932
But other nodes on the interior
might have as many as 2.

12:29:02.932 --> 12:29:04.732
Like having 2 children, so to speak.

12:29:04.732 --> 12:29:06.902
And indeed, the vernacular
here is exactly that.

12:29:06.902 --> 12:29:08.812
This would be called
the root of the tree.

12:29:08.812 --> 12:29:11.752
Or this would be a parent,
with respect to these children.

12:29:11.752 --> 12:29:14.392
The green ones would be
grandchildren, respect to these.

12:29:14.392 --> 12:29:19.012
The green ones would be siblings
with respect to each other.

12:29:19.012 --> 12:29:19.852
And over there, too.

12:29:19.852 --> 12:29:22.144
So all the same jargon you
might use in the real world,

12:29:22.144 --> 12:29:25.402
applies in the world of data
structures and CS trees.

12:29:25.402 --> 12:29:30.292
But this is interesting because I think
we could build this now, this data

12:29:30.292 --> 12:29:32.782
structure in the computer's memory.

12:29:32.782 --> 12:29:33.322
How?

12:29:33.322 --> 12:29:37.522
Well, suppose that we defined
a node to be no longer just

12:29:37.522 --> 12:29:39.592
this, a number in a next field.

12:29:39.592 --> 12:29:42.352
What if we give ourselves
a bit more room here?

12:29:42.352 --> 12:29:47.212
And give ourselves a pointer called
left and another one called right.

12:29:47.212 --> 12:29:49.562
Both of which is a
pointer to a struct node.

12:29:49.562 --> 12:29:53.512
So same idea as before, but now we
just make sure we think of these things

12:29:53.512 --> 12:29:56.692
as pointing this way and
this way, not just this way.

12:29:56.692 --> 12:29:58.762
Not just a single direction, but 2.

12:29:58.762 --> 12:30:02.662
So you could imagine, in code, building
something up like this with a node.

12:30:02.662 --> 12:30:06.052
That creates, in essence,
this diagram here.

12:30:06.052 --> 12:30:07.732
But why is this compelling?

12:30:07.732 --> 12:30:09.772
Suppose I want to find the number 3.

12:30:09.772 --> 12:30:12.322
I want to search for the
number 3 in this tree.

12:30:12.322 --> 12:30:15.682
It would seem, just like Pedro was
the beginning of our linked list,

12:30:15.682 --> 12:30:18.572
in the world of trees,
the root, so to speak,

12:30:18.572 --> 12:30:20.572
is the beginning of your data structure.

12:30:20.572 --> 12:30:26.212
You can retain and remember this entire
tree just by pointing at the root node,

12:30:26.212 --> 12:30:26.752
ultimately.

12:30:26.752 --> 12:30:29.812
One variable can hang
on to this whole tree.

12:30:29.812 --> 12:30:32.002
So how can I find the number 3?

12:30:32.002 --> 12:30:36.142
Well, if I look at the root node and
the number I'm looking for is less than.

12:30:36.142 --> 12:30:37.732
Notice, I can go this way.

12:30:37.732 --> 12:30:40.052
Or if it's greater
than, I can go this way.

12:30:40.052 --> 12:30:42.232
So I preserve that
property of the phone book,

12:30:42.232 --> 12:30:44.482
or just assorted array in general.

12:30:44.482 --> 12:30:45.802
What's true over here?

12:30:45.802 --> 12:30:48.810
If I'm looking for 3, I can
go to the right of the 2

12:30:48.810 --> 12:30:50.602
because that number is
going to be greater.

12:30:50.602 --> 12:30:53.162
If I go left, it's going
to be smaller instead.

12:30:53.162 --> 12:30:55.912
And here's an example
of actually recursion.

12:30:55.912 --> 12:30:59.572
Recursion in a physical sense
much like the Mario's pyramid.

12:30:59.572 --> 12:31:01.732
Which was recursively to find.

12:31:01.732 --> 12:31:02.782
Notice this.

12:31:02.782 --> 12:31:04.732
I claim this whole thing is a tree.

12:31:04.732 --> 12:31:08.272
Specifically, a binary search
tree, which means every node

12:31:08.272 --> 12:31:11.362
has 2, or maybe 1, or maybe 0 children.

12:31:11.362 --> 12:31:12.592
But no more than 2.

12:31:12.592 --> 12:31:14.212
Hence the bi in binary.

12:31:14.212 --> 12:31:19.642
And it's the case that every left
child is smaller than the root.

12:31:19.642 --> 12:31:22.612
And every right child
is larger than the root.

12:31:22.612 --> 12:31:25.582
That definition certainly
works for 2, 4, and 6.

12:31:25.582 --> 12:31:30.412
But it also works recursively for
every sub tree, or branch of this tree.

12:31:30.412 --> 12:31:32.392
Notice, if you think
of this as the root,

12:31:32.392 --> 12:31:34.462
it is indeed bigger
than this left child.

12:31:34.462 --> 12:31:36.562
And it's smaller than this right child.

12:31:36.562 --> 12:31:39.082
And if you look even at
the leaves, so to speak.

12:31:39.082 --> 12:31:40.492
The grandchildren here.

12:31:40.492 --> 12:31:44.169
This root node is bigger than
its left child, if it existed.

12:31:44.169 --> 12:31:45.502
So it's a meaningless statement.

12:31:45.502 --> 12:31:47.692
And it's less than its right child.

12:31:47.692 --> 12:31:50.482
Or it's not greater than, certainly,
so that's meaningless too.

12:31:50.482 --> 12:31:54.242
So we haven't violated the definition
even for these leaves, as well.

12:31:54.242 --> 12:31:57.712
And so, now, how many steps does
it take to find in the worst case

12:31:57.712 --> 12:32:02.062
any number in a binary
search tree, it would seem?

12:32:02.062 --> 12:32:04.012
So it seems 2, literally.

12:32:04.012 --> 12:32:05.882
And the height of this
thing is actually 3.

12:32:05.882 --> 12:32:08.632
And so long story short, especially,
if you're a little less comfy

12:32:08.632 --> 12:32:10.792
with your logarithms from yesteryear.

12:32:10.792 --> 12:32:14.602
Log base 2 is the number of times you
can divide something in half, and half,

12:32:14.602 --> 12:32:16.342
and half, until you get down to 1.

12:32:16.342 --> 12:32:19.310
This is like a logarithm
in the reverse direction.

12:32:19.310 --> 12:32:20.602
Here's a whole lot of elements.

12:32:20.602 --> 12:32:22.972
And we're having, we're
having until we get down to 1.

12:32:22.972 --> 12:32:27.125
So the height of this tree, that
is to say, is log base 2 of n.

12:32:27.125 --> 12:32:30.292
Which means that even in the worst case,
the number you're looking for maybe

12:32:30.292 --> 12:32:32.167
it's all the way at the
bottom in the leaves.

12:32:32.167 --> 12:32:32.812
Doesn't matter.

12:32:32.812 --> 12:32:37.702
It's going to take log base 2
of n steps, or log of n steps,

12:32:37.702 --> 12:32:41.312
to find, maximally, any
one of those numbers.

12:32:41.312 --> 12:32:46.102
So, again, binary search is back.

12:32:46.102 --> 12:32:48.117
But we've paid a price, right.

12:32:48.117 --> 12:32:49.492
This isn't a linked list anymore.

12:32:49.492 --> 12:32:50.674
It's a tree.

12:32:50.674 --> 12:32:53.632
But we've gained back binary search,
which is pretty compelling, right.

12:32:53.632 --> 12:32:56.257
That's where the whole class
began, on making that distinction.

12:32:56.257 --> 12:33:01.502
But what price have we paid to retain
binary search in this new world.

12:33:01.502 --> 12:33:02.002
Yeah.

12:33:04.552 --> 12:33:06.532
It's no longer sorted
left to right, but this

12:33:06.532 --> 12:33:09.502
is a claim sorted, according to
the binary search tree definition.

12:33:09.502 --> 12:33:13.492
Where, again, left child
is smaller than root.

12:33:13.492 --> 12:33:15.922
And right child is greater than root.

12:33:15.922 --> 12:33:19.342
So it is sorted, but it's sorted in
a 2-dimensional sense, if you will.

12:33:19.342 --> 12:33:20.392
Not just 1.

12:33:20.392 --> 12:33:22.742
But another price paid?

12:33:22.742 --> 12:33:24.152
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] nodes now.

12:33:24.152 --> 12:33:24.944
SPEAKER 1: Exactly.

12:33:24.944 --> 12:33:29.312
Every node now needs not one
number, but 2, 3 pieces of data.

12:33:29.312 --> 12:33:31.112
A number and now 2 pointers.

12:33:31.112 --> 12:33:32.867
So, again, there's that trade off again.

12:33:32.867 --> 12:33:34.742
Where, well, if you want
to save time, you've

12:33:34.742 --> 12:33:37.562
got to give something if
you start giving space.

12:33:37.562 --> 12:33:40.029
And you start using more
space, you can speed up time.

12:33:40.029 --> 12:33:40.862
Like, you've got it.

12:33:40.862 --> 12:33:42.122
There's always a price paid.

12:33:42.122 --> 12:33:47.882
And it's very often in space, or time,
or complexity, or developer time,

12:33:47.882 --> 12:33:49.512
the number of bugs you have to solve.

12:33:49.512 --> 12:33:51.542
I mean, all of these
are finite resources

12:33:51.542 --> 12:33:53.315
that you have to juggle them on.

12:33:53.315 --> 12:33:55.982
So if we consider now the code
with which we can implement this,

12:33:55.982 --> 12:33:57.602
here might be the node.

12:33:57.602 --> 12:34:00.552
And how might we actually
use something like this?

12:34:00.552 --> 12:34:03.002
Well, let's take a look at,
maybe, one final program.

12:34:03.002 --> 12:34:07.122
And see here, before we transition
to higher level concepts, ultimately.

12:34:07.122 --> 12:34:11.552
Let me go ahead here and let me just
open a program I wrote here in advance.

12:34:11.552 --> 12:34:15.692
So let me, in a moment, copy
over file called tree.c.

12:34:15.692 --> 12:34:18.550
Which we'll have on
the course's websites.

12:34:18.550 --> 12:34:20.342
And I'll walk you
through some of the logic

12:34:20.342 --> 12:34:25.272
here that I've written for tree.c.

12:34:25.272 --> 12:34:25.772
All right.

12:34:25.772 --> 12:34:27.282
So what do we have here first?

12:34:27.282 --> 12:34:31.922
So here is an implementation of
a binary search tree for numbers.

12:34:31.922 --> 12:34:36.342
And as before, I've played around and
I've inserted the numbers manually.

12:34:36.342 --> 12:34:37.772
So what's going on first?

12:34:37.772 --> 12:34:41.612
Here is my definition of a node for a
binary search tree, copied and pasted

12:34:41.612 --> 12:34:44.492
from what I proposed on
the board a moment ago.

12:34:44.492 --> 12:34:47.192
Here are 2 prototypes for
2 functions, that I'll

12:34:47.192 --> 12:34:49.262
show you in a moment,
that allow me to free

12:34:49.262 --> 12:34:52.652
an entire tree, one node at a time.

12:34:52.652 --> 12:34:55.382
And then, also allow me to
print the tree in order.

12:34:55.382 --> 12:34:57.782
So even though they're
not sorted left to right,

12:34:57.782 --> 12:35:00.932
I bet if I'm clever about
what child I print first,

12:35:00.932 --> 12:35:04.152
I can reconstruct the idea of
printing this tree properly.

12:35:04.152 --> 12:35:06.632
So how might I implement
a binary search tree?

12:35:06.632 --> 12:35:07.922
Here's my main function.

12:35:07.922 --> 12:35:10.502
Here is how I might
represent a tree of size 0.

12:35:10.502 --> 12:35:13.442
It's just a null pointer called tree.

12:35:13.442 --> 12:35:15.542
Here's how I might add
a number to that list.

12:35:15.542 --> 12:35:19.562
So here, for instance, is me
malllocing space for a node.

12:35:19.562 --> 12:35:21.692
Storing it in a temporary
variable called n.

12:35:21.692 --> 12:35:23.552
Here is me just doing a safety check.

12:35:23.552 --> 12:35:25.262
Make sure n does not equal null.

12:35:25.262 --> 12:35:29.612
And then, here is me initializing this
node to contain the number 2, first.

12:35:29.612 --> 12:35:32.342
Then, initializing the left
child of that node to be null.

12:35:32.342 --> 12:35:34.992
And the right child of
that null node to be null.

12:35:34.992 --> 12:35:40.152
And then, initializing the tree itself
to be equal to that particular node.

12:35:40.152 --> 12:35:43.322
So at this point in the story, there's
just one rectangle on the screen

12:35:43.322 --> 12:35:46.222
containing the number
2 with no children.

12:35:46.222 --> 12:35:46.722
All right.

12:35:46.722 --> 12:35:49.112
Let's just add manually
to this a little further.

12:35:49.112 --> 12:35:52.262
Let's add another number to the
list, by mallocing another node.

12:35:52.262 --> 12:35:55.622
I don't need to declare n as a node*
because it already exists at this

12:35:55.622 --> 12:35:56.262
point.

12:35:56.262 --> 12:35:58.202
Here's a little safety check.

12:35:58.202 --> 12:36:02.762
I'm going to not bother with my,
let me do this, free memory here.

12:36:02.762 --> 12:36:04.722
Just to be safe.

12:36:04.722 --> 12:36:07.285
Do I want to do this?

12:36:07.285 --> 12:36:09.452
We want a free memory too,
which I've not done here,

12:36:09.452 --> 12:36:11.132
but I'll save that for another time.

12:36:11.132 --> 12:36:13.472
Here, I'm going to
initialize the number to 1.

12:36:13.472 --> 12:36:17.582
I'm going to initialize the children
of this node to null and null.

12:36:17.582 --> 12:36:19.292
And now, I'm going to do this.

12:36:19.292 --> 12:36:23.762
Initialize the tree's
left child to be n.

12:36:23.762 --> 12:36:26.704
So what that's essentially
doing here is if this

12:36:26.704 --> 12:36:29.912
is my root node, the single rectangle
I described a moment ago that currently

12:36:29.912 --> 12:36:32.012
has no children, neither left nor right.

12:36:32.012 --> 12:36:33.962
Here's my new node with the number 1.

12:36:33.962 --> 12:36:36.102
I want it to become the new left child.

12:36:36.102 --> 12:36:39.632
So that line of code on the
screen there, tree left equals n,

12:36:39.632 --> 12:36:44.202
is like stitching these 2 together
with a pointer from 2 to the 1.

12:36:44.202 --> 12:36:44.702
All right.

12:36:44.702 --> 12:36:47.582
The next lines of code,
you can probably guess,

12:36:47.582 --> 12:36:50.042
are me adding another
number to the list.

12:36:50.042 --> 12:36:51.212
Just the number 3.

12:36:51.212 --> 12:36:56.682
So this is a simpler tree with
2, 1, and, 3 respectively.

12:36:56.682 --> 12:36:59.192
And this code, let me wave
my hands, is almost the same.

12:36:59.192 --> 12:37:02.492
Except for the fact that I'm
updating the tree's right child

12:37:02.492 --> 12:37:04.472
to be this new and third node.

12:37:04.472 --> 12:37:07.862
Let's now run the code before
looking at those 2 functions.

12:37:07.862 --> 12:37:11.762
Let me do make tree, ./tree.

12:37:11.762 --> 12:37:12.992
And while I'll 1, 2, 3.

12:37:12.992 --> 12:37:16.412
So it sounds like the data structure
is sorted, to your concern earlier.

12:37:16.412 --> 12:37:18.182
But how did I actually print this?

12:37:18.182 --> 12:37:20.072
And then, eventually,
free the whole thing?

12:37:20.072 --> 12:37:23.462
Well let's look at the
definition of first print tree.

12:37:23.462 --> 12:37:26.432
And this is where
things get interesting.

12:37:26.432 --> 12:37:30.272
Print tree returns nothing
so it's a void function.

12:37:30.272 --> 12:37:36.002
But it takes a pointer to a root element
as its sole argument, node* root.

12:37:36.002 --> 12:37:37.172
Here's my safety check.

12:37:37.172 --> 12:37:39.272
If root equals equals
null, there's obviously

12:37:39.272 --> 12:37:40.592
nothing to print, just return.

12:37:40.592 --> 12:37:42.452
That goes without saying.

12:37:42.452 --> 12:37:44.492
But here's where things
get a little magical.

12:37:44.492 --> 12:37:47.762
Otherwise, print your left child.

12:37:47.762 --> 12:37:50.492
Then print your own number.

12:37:50.492 --> 12:37:53.912
Then, print your right child.

12:37:53.912 --> 12:37:59.182
What is this an example of, even
though it's not mentioned by name here?

12:37:59.182 --> 12:38:00.802
What programming technique here?

12:38:00.802 --> 12:38:01.732
AUDIENCE: Recursion.

12:38:01.732 --> 12:38:02.399
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

12:38:02.399 --> 12:38:05.854
So this is actually perhaps the most
compelling use of recursion, yet.

12:38:05.854 --> 12:38:08.062
It wasn't really that
compelling with the Mario thing

12:38:08.062 --> 12:38:10.192
because we had such an easy
implementation with a for-loop loop

12:38:10.192 --> 12:38:11.032
weeks ago.

12:38:11.032 --> 12:38:15.652
But here is a perfect application of
recursion, where your data structure

12:38:15.652 --> 12:38:17.392
itself is recursive, right.

12:38:17.392 --> 12:38:19.702
If you take any snip
of any branch, it all

12:38:19.702 --> 12:38:22.072
still looks like a tree,
just a smaller one.

12:38:22.072 --> 12:38:23.912
That lends itself to recursion.

12:38:23.912 --> 12:38:28.492
So here is this leap of faith where I
say, print my left tree, or my left sub

12:38:28.492 --> 12:38:31.312
tree, if you will, via
my child at the left.

12:38:31.312 --> 12:38:34.612
Then, I'll print my own root
node here in the middle.

12:38:34.612 --> 12:38:37.222
Then, go ahead and
print my right sub tree.

12:38:37.222 --> 12:38:41.662
And because we have this base case that
makes sure that if the root is null,

12:38:41.662 --> 12:38:44.449
there's nothing to do, you're
not going to recurse infinitely.

12:38:44.449 --> 12:38:47.032
You're not going to call yourself
again, and again, and again,

12:38:47.032 --> 12:38:48.692
infinitely, many times.

12:38:48.692 --> 12:38:52.882
So it works out and prints
the 1, the 2, and the 3.

12:38:52.882 --> 12:38:54.322
And notice what we could do, too.

12:38:54.322 --> 12:38:57.742
If you wanted to print the tree in
reverse order, you could do that.

12:38:57.742 --> 12:39:00.532
Print your right tree
first, the greater element.

12:39:00.532 --> 12:39:01.432
Then, yourself.

12:39:01.432 --> 12:39:02.812
Then, your smaller sub tree.

12:39:02.812 --> 12:39:05.452
And if I do make tree
here and ./tree, well now,

12:39:05.452 --> 12:39:07.582
I've reversed the order of the list.

12:39:07.582 --> 12:39:08.672
And that's pretty cool.

12:39:08.672 --> 12:39:10.422
You can do it with a
for-loop in an array.

12:39:10.422 --> 12:39:13.852
But you can also do it, even with
this 2-dimensional structure.

12:39:13.852 --> 12:39:17.662
Let's lastly look at
this free tree function.

12:39:17.662 --> 12:39:19.642
And this one's almost the same.

12:39:19.642 --> 12:39:22.882
Order doesn't matter in quite the
same way, but it does still matter.

12:39:22.882 --> 12:39:24.502
Here's what I did with free tree.

12:39:24.502 --> 12:39:27.460
Well, if the root of the tree is
null, there's obviously nothing to do.

12:39:27.460 --> 12:39:28.042
Just return.

12:39:28.042 --> 12:39:32.582
Otherwise, go ahead and free your
left child and all of its descendants.

12:39:32.582 --> 12:39:35.572
Then free your right child
and all of its descendants.

12:39:35.572 --> 12:39:37.382
And then, free yourself.

12:39:37.382 --> 12:39:43.172
And again, free literally just
frees the address in that variable.

12:39:43.172 --> 12:39:45.052
It doesn't free the whole darn thing.

12:39:45.052 --> 12:39:47.332
It just frees literally
what's at that address.

12:39:47.332 --> 12:39:51.382
Why was it important that
I did line 72 last, though?

12:39:51.382 --> 12:39:53.932
Why did I free the left
child and the right child

12:39:53.932 --> 12:39:57.455
before I freed myself, so to speak?

12:39:57.455 --> 12:39:58.372
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

12:39:58.372 --> 12:39:59.164
SPEAKER 1: Exactly.

12:39:59.164 --> 12:40:03.622
If you free yourself first, if I had
done incorrectly this line higher up,

12:40:03.622 --> 12:40:08.302
you're not allowed to touch the left
child tree or the right child tree.

12:40:08.302 --> 12:40:10.832
Because the memory address is
no longer valid at that point.

12:40:10.832 --> 12:40:12.772
You would get some
memory error, perhaps.

12:40:12.772 --> 12:40:13.792
The program would crash.

12:40:13.792 --> 12:40:15.472
Valgrind definitely wouldn't like it.

12:40:15.472 --> 12:40:17.542
Bad things would otherwise happen.

12:40:17.542 --> 12:40:19.372
But here, then, is an
example of recursion.

12:40:19.372 --> 12:40:23.842
And again, just a recursive use
of an actual data structure.

12:40:23.842 --> 12:40:26.602
And what's even cooler here
is, relatively speaking,

12:40:26.602 --> 12:40:29.122
suppose we wanted to
search something like this.

12:40:29.122 --> 12:40:33.202
Binary search actually gets pretty
straightforward to implement 2.

12:40:33.202 --> 12:40:33.892
For instance.

12:40:33.892 --> 12:40:38.422
here might be the prototype for a search
function for a binary search tree.

12:40:38.422 --> 12:40:43.402
You give me the root of a tree, and
you give me a number I'm looking for,

12:40:43.402 --> 12:40:47.362
and I can pretty easily now return true
if it's in there or false if it's not.

12:40:47.362 --> 12:40:47.932
How?

12:40:47.932 --> 12:40:49.912
Well, let's first ask a question.

12:40:49.912 --> 12:40:52.877
If tree equals equals null,
then you just return false.

12:40:52.877 --> 12:40:56.002
Because if there's no tree, there's no
number, so it's obviously not there.

12:40:56.002 --> 12:40:57.342
Return false.

12:40:57.342 --> 12:41:04.042
Else if, the number you're looking for
is less than the tree's own number,

12:41:04.042 --> 12:41:06.052
which direction should we go?

12:41:06.052 --> 12:41:06.729
AUDIENCE: Left.

12:41:06.729 --> 12:41:07.562
SPEAKER 1: OK, left.

12:41:07.562 --> 12:41:08.672
How do we express that?

12:41:08.672 --> 12:41:11.782
Well, let's just return the
answer to this question.

12:41:11.782 --> 12:41:15.922
Search the left sub tree,
by way of my left child,

12:41:15.922 --> 12:41:17.452
looking for the same number.

12:41:17.452 --> 12:41:19.732
And you just assume through
the beauty of recursion

12:41:19.732 --> 12:41:22.882
that you're kicking the can
and let yourself figure it out

12:41:22.882 --> 12:41:24.082
with a smaller problem.

12:41:24.082 --> 12:41:26.542
Just that snipped left tree instead.

12:41:26.542 --> 12:41:30.802
Else if, the number you're looking for
is greater than the tree's own number,

12:41:30.802 --> 12:41:32.642
go to the right, as you might infer.

12:41:32.642 --> 12:41:35.542
So I can just return the
answer to this question.

12:41:35.542 --> 12:41:38.632
Search my right sub tree
for that same number.

12:41:38.632 --> 12:41:40.502
And there's a fourth
and final condition.

12:41:40.502 --> 12:41:43.732
What's the fourth scenario we
have to consider, explicitly?

12:41:43.732 --> 12:41:44.242
Yeah.

12:41:44.242 --> 12:41:45.262
AUDIENCE: The number.

12:41:45.262 --> 12:41:47.304
SPEAKER 1: If the number,
itself, is right there.

12:41:47.304 --> 12:41:50.962
So else if, the number I'm looking
for equals the tree's own number,

12:41:50.962 --> 12:41:53.732
then and only then,
should you return true.

12:41:53.732 --> 12:41:55.972
And if you're thinking
quickly here, there's

12:41:55.972 --> 12:41:59.632
an optimization possible,
better design opportunity.

12:41:59.632 --> 12:42:01.132
Think back to even our scratch days.

12:42:01.132 --> 12:42:03.252
What could we do a little better here?

12:42:03.252 --> 12:42:04.192
You're pointing at it.

12:42:04.192 --> 12:42:04.990
AUDIENCE: Else.

12:42:04.990 --> 12:42:05.782
SPEAKER 1: Exactly.

12:42:05.782 --> 12:42:06.622
An else suffices.

12:42:06.622 --> 12:42:09.164
Because if there's logically
only 4 things that could happen,

12:42:09.164 --> 12:42:12.022
you're wasting your time by asking
a fourth gratuitous question.

12:42:12.022 --> 12:42:13.342
And else here suffices.

12:42:13.342 --> 12:42:16.982
So here to, more so than the
Mario example a few weeks ago,

12:42:16.982 --> 12:42:19.582
there's just this elegance
arguably to recursion.

12:42:19.582 --> 12:42:20.332
And that's it.

12:42:20.332 --> 12:42:21.442
This is not pseudocode.

12:42:21.442 --> 12:42:25.432
This is the code for binary
search on a binary search tree.

12:42:25.432 --> 12:42:27.502
And so, recursion tends
to work in lockstep

12:42:27.502 --> 12:42:32.182
with these kinds of data structures
that have this structure to them

12:42:32.182 --> 12:42:33.662
as we're seeing here.

12:42:33.662 --> 12:42:34.162
All right.

12:42:34.162 --> 12:42:39.842
Any questions, then, on binary search
as implemented here with a tree?

12:42:39.842 --> 12:42:40.709
Yeah.

12:42:40.709 --> 12:42:42.657
AUDIENCE: About like third years.

12:42:42.657 --> 12:42:43.631
[INAUDIBLE]

12:42:47.170 --> 12:42:48.212
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

12:42:48.212 --> 12:42:54.172
So when returning a Boolean value, true
and false are values that are defined

12:42:54.172 --> 12:42:57.832
in a library called Standard
Bool, S-T-D-B-O-O-L dot H.

12:42:57.832 --> 12:42:59.962
With a header file that you can use.

12:42:59.962 --> 12:43:06.740
It is the case that true is, it's
not well defined what they are.

12:43:06.740 --> 12:43:08.032
But they would map indeed, yes.

12:43:08.032 --> 12:43:09.442
To 0 and 1, essentially.

12:43:09.442 --> 12:43:11.872
But you should not compare
them explicitly to 0 and 1.

12:43:11.872 --> 12:43:14.872
When you're using true and false, you
should compare them to each other.

12:43:14.872 --> 12:43:18.857
AUDIENCE: I meant if
it's in a code return.

12:43:18.857 --> 12:43:19.732
SPEAKER 1: Oh, sorry.

12:43:19.732 --> 12:43:23.332
So if I am in my own code from
earlier, an avoid function,

12:43:23.332 --> 12:43:25.762
it is totally fine to return.

12:43:25.762 --> 12:43:28.432
You just can't return
something explicitly.

12:43:28.432 --> 12:43:30.202
So return just means that's it.

12:43:30.202 --> 12:43:31.762
Quit out of this function.

12:43:31.762 --> 12:43:33.632
You're not actually
handing back a value.

12:43:33.632 --> 12:43:37.252
So it's a way of short
circuiting the execution.

12:43:37.252 --> 12:43:39.532
If you don't like that,
and some people do frown

12:43:39.532 --> 12:43:44.242
upon having code return from functions
prematurely, you could invert the logic

12:43:44.242 --> 12:43:45.532
and do something like this.

12:43:45.532 --> 12:43:49.222
If the root does not equal
null, do all of these things.

12:43:49.222 --> 12:43:51.502
And then, indent all three
of these lines underneath.

12:43:51.502 --> 12:43:52.972
That's perfectly fine too.

12:43:52.972 --> 12:43:54.772
I happen to write it
the other way just so

12:43:54.772 --> 12:43:58.472
that there was explicitly a base case
that I could point to on the screen.

12:43:58.472 --> 12:44:01.402
Whereas, now, it's
implicitly there for us only.

12:44:01.402 --> 12:44:03.272
But a good observation too.

12:44:03.272 --> 12:44:03.772
All right.

12:44:03.772 --> 12:44:07.442
So let's ask the question as
before about running time of this.

12:44:07.442 --> 12:44:09.412
It would look like
binary search is back.

12:44:09.412 --> 12:44:15.082
And we can now do things in logarithmic
time, but we should be careful.

12:44:15.082 --> 12:44:17.422
Is this a binary search tree?

12:44:17.422 --> 12:44:19.142
Just to be clear.

12:44:19.142 --> 12:44:21.862
And again, a binary
search tree is a tree

12:44:21.862 --> 12:44:28.600
where the root is greater than its left
child and smaller than its right child.

12:44:28.600 --> 12:44:29.392
That's the essence.

12:44:29.392 --> 12:44:30.862
So you're nodding your head.

12:44:30.862 --> 12:44:32.762
You agree?

12:44:32.762 --> 12:44:33.502
I agree.

12:44:33.502 --> 12:44:35.512
So this is a binary search tree.

12:44:35.512 --> 12:44:37.872
Is this a binary search tree?

12:44:37.872 --> 12:44:38.812
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

12:44:38.812 --> 12:44:39.312
OK.

12:44:39.312 --> 12:44:40.342
I'm hearing yeses.

12:44:40.342 --> 12:44:43.192
Or I'm hearing just my delay
changing the vote it would seem.

12:44:43.192 --> 12:44:45.562
So this is one of those trick questions.

12:44:45.562 --> 12:44:47.962
This is a binary search
tree because I've not

12:44:47.962 --> 12:44:50.872
violated the definition
of what I gave you, right.

12:44:50.872 --> 12:44:56.962
Is there any example of a left child
that is greater than its parent?

12:44:56.962 --> 12:44:59.962
Or is there any example of a right
child that's smaller than its parent?

12:44:59.962 --> 12:45:02.379
That's just the opposite way
of describing the same thing.

12:45:02.379 --> 12:45:04.552
No, this is a binary search tree.

12:45:04.552 --> 12:45:07.692
Unfortunately, it also looks like,
albeit at a different axis, what?

12:45:07.692 --> 12:45:08.692
AUDIENCE: A linked list.

12:45:08.692 --> 12:45:09.382
SPEAKER 1: A linked list.

12:45:09.382 --> 12:45:11.452
But you could imagine
this happening, right.

12:45:11.452 --> 12:45:14.122
Suppose that I hadn't been as
thoughtful as I was earlier

12:45:14.122 --> 12:45:17.452
by inserting 2, And then 1, and then 3.

12:45:17.452 --> 12:45:19.642
Which nicely balanced everything out.

12:45:19.642 --> 12:45:22.342
Suppose that instead, because
of what the user is typing in

12:45:22.342 --> 12:45:25.462
or whatever you contrive in your
own code, suppose you insert a 1,

12:45:25.462 --> 12:45:27.742
and then a 2, and then a 3.

12:45:27.742 --> 12:45:30.332
Like, you've created a
problem for yourself.

12:45:30.332 --> 12:45:33.772
Because if we follow the same logic
as before, going left or going right,

12:45:33.772 --> 12:45:38.512
this is how you might implement
a binary search tree accidentally

12:45:38.512 --> 12:45:42.232
if you just blindly keep
following that definition.

12:45:42.232 --> 12:45:44.512
I mean, this would be
better designed as what?

12:45:44.512 --> 12:45:46.972
If we rotated the whole thing around.

12:45:46.972 --> 12:45:48.352
And that's totally fine.

12:45:48.352 --> 12:45:50.542
And those kinds of trees
actually have names.

12:45:50.542 --> 12:45:52.882
There's trees called AVL
trees in computer science.

12:45:52.882 --> 12:45:54.532
There are red-black black
trees in computer science.

12:45:54.532 --> 12:45:56.782
There are other types of
trees that, additionally,

12:45:56.782 --> 12:45:59.992
add some logic that tell you
when you got to pivot the thing,

12:45:59.992 --> 12:46:03.720
and rotate it, and snip off the
root, and fix things in this way.

12:46:03.720 --> 12:46:05.512
But a binary search
tree, in and of itself,

12:46:05.512 --> 12:46:09.152
does not guarantee that it
will be balanced, so to speak.

12:46:09.152 --> 12:46:11.722
And so, if you consider
the worst case scenario

12:46:11.722 --> 12:46:13.342
of even using a binary search tree.

12:46:13.342 --> 12:46:15.442
If you're not smart about
the code you're writing

12:46:15.442 --> 12:46:17.662
and you just blindly
follow this definition,

12:46:17.662 --> 12:46:21.772
you might accidentally create a
crazy, long and stringy binary search

12:46:21.772 --> 12:46:24.532
tree that essentially
looks like a linked list.

12:46:24.532 --> 12:46:26.992
Because you're not even using
any of the left children.

12:46:26.992 --> 12:46:30.232
So unfortunately, the literal
answer to the question

12:46:30.232 --> 12:46:32.962
here is what's the
running time of search?

12:46:32.962 --> 12:46:34.882
Well, hopefully, log n.

12:46:34.882 --> 12:46:37.462
But not if you don't maintain
the balance of the tree.

12:46:37.462 --> 12:46:42.772
Both, in certain search, could actually
devolve into instead of big O of log n,

12:46:42.772 --> 12:46:44.434
literally, big O of n.

12:46:44.434 --> 12:46:46.642
If you don't somehow take
into account, and we're not

12:46:46.642 --> 12:46:48.202
going to do the code for that here.

12:46:48.202 --> 12:46:51.622
It's a higher level thing you
might explore down the road.

12:46:51.622 --> 12:46:55.412
It can devolve into something
that you might not have intended.

12:46:55.412 --> 12:46:57.504
And so, now that we're
talking about 2 dimensions,

12:46:57.504 --> 12:46:59.212
it's really the onus
is on the programmer

12:46:59.212 --> 12:47:01.972
to consider what kinds of
perverse situations might happen.

12:47:01.972 --> 12:47:04.342
Where the thing devolves
into a structure

12:47:04.342 --> 12:47:07.832
that you don't actually
want it to devolve into.

12:47:07.832 --> 12:47:08.332
All right.

12:47:08.332 --> 12:47:09.842
We've got just a few structures to go.

12:47:09.842 --> 12:47:11.422
Let's go ahead and take one
more 5 minute break here.

12:47:11.422 --> 12:47:12.892
When we come back,
we'll talk at this level

12:47:12.892 --> 12:47:14.512
about some final applications of this.

12:47:14.512 --> 12:47:15.992
See you in 5.

12:47:15.992 --> 12:47:17.752
All right.

12:47:17.752 --> 12:47:19.342
So we are back.

12:47:19.342 --> 12:47:22.732
And as promised, we'll operate
now at this higher level.

12:47:22.732 --> 12:47:26.002
Where if we take for granted that, even
though you haven't had an opportunity

12:47:26.002 --> 12:47:28.794
to play with these techniques yet,
you have the ability now in code

12:47:28.794 --> 12:47:30.262
to stitch things together.

12:47:30.262 --> 12:47:33.112
Both in a one dimension
and even 2 dimensions,

12:47:33.112 --> 12:47:35.452
to build things like lists and trees.

12:47:35.452 --> 12:47:37.462
So if we have these building blocks.

12:47:37.462 --> 12:47:40.162
Things like now arrays,
and lists, and trees,

12:47:40.162 --> 12:47:44.272
what if we start to amalgamate
them such that we build things out

12:47:44.272 --> 12:47:46.382
of multiple data structures?

12:47:46.382 --> 12:47:49.842
Can we start to get some of the best
of both worlds by way of, for instance,

12:47:49.842 --> 12:47:51.192
something called a hash table.

12:47:51.192 --> 12:47:55.022
So a hash table is a Swiss
army knife of data structures

12:47:55.022 --> 12:47:56.792
in that it's so commonly used.

12:47:56.792 --> 12:48:01.482
Because it allows you to associate
keys with value, so to speak.

12:48:01.482 --> 12:48:06.542
So, for instance, it allows you to
associate a username with a password.

12:48:06.542 --> 12:48:08.552
Or a name with a number.

12:48:08.552 --> 12:48:11.402
Or anything where you have
to take something as input,

12:48:11.402 --> 12:48:13.782
and get as output a corresponding
piece of information.

12:48:13.782 --> 12:48:16.692
A hash table is often a
data structure of choice.

12:48:16.692 --> 12:48:17.942
And here's what it looks like.

12:48:17.942 --> 12:48:20.282
It's actually looks like
an array, at first glance.

12:48:20.282 --> 12:48:23.472
But for discussion's sake, I've
drawn this array vertically,

12:48:23.472 --> 12:48:24.402
which is totally fine.

12:48:24.402 --> 12:48:26.142
It's still just an array.

12:48:26.142 --> 12:48:31.202
But it allows you, a hash table, to
jump to any of these locations randomly.

12:48:31.202 --> 12:48:32.222
That is instantly.

12:48:32.222 --> 12:48:35.612
So, for instance, there's actually
26 locations in this array.

12:48:35.612 --> 12:48:38.582
Because I want to, for
instance, store initially

12:48:38.582 --> 12:48:41.462
names of people, for instance.

12:48:41.462 --> 12:48:44.135
And wouldn't it be nice if the
person's name starts with A,

12:48:44.135 --> 12:48:45.302
I have a go to place for it.

12:48:45.302 --> 12:48:46.262
Maybe the first box.

12:48:46.262 --> 12:48:48.345
And if it starts with Z,
I put them at the bottom.

12:48:48.345 --> 12:48:50.552
So that I can jump
instantly, arithmetically,

12:48:50.552 --> 12:48:52.952
using a little bit of
Ascii or Unicode fanciness,

12:48:52.952 --> 12:48:56.022
exactly to the location that
they want to they need to go.

12:48:56.022 --> 12:48:58.172
So, for instance, here's
our array 0 index.

12:48:58.172 --> 12:48:59.612
0 through 25.

12:48:59.612 --> 12:49:01.982
If I think of this,
though, as A through Z,

12:49:01.982 --> 12:49:03.852
I'm going to think of
these 26 locations,

12:49:03.852 --> 12:49:07.112
now in the context of a hash table,
is what we'll generally call buckets.

12:49:07.112 --> 12:49:09.492
So buckets into which
you can put values.

12:49:09.492 --> 12:49:13.862
So, for instance, suppose that we
want to insert a value, one name

12:49:13.862 --> 12:49:15.072
into this data structure.

12:49:15.072 --> 12:49:16.742
And that name is say, Albus.

12:49:16.742 --> 12:49:21.462
So Albus starting with A. Albus might
go at the very beginning of this list.

12:49:21.462 --> 12:49:21.962
All right.

12:49:21.962 --> 12:49:23.670
And then, we want to
insert another name.

12:49:23.670 --> 12:49:25.112
This one happens to be Zacharias.

12:49:25.112 --> 12:49:28.172
Starting with Z, so it goes all
the way at the end of this data

12:49:28.172 --> 12:49:29.972
structure in location 25 a.k.a.

12:49:29.972 --> 12:49:30.872
Z.

12:49:30.872 --> 12:49:34.742
And then, maybe a third name like
Hermione, and that goes at location H

12:49:34.742 --> 12:49:36.792
according to that
position in the alphabet.

12:49:36.792 --> 12:49:39.542
So this is great because
in constant time,

12:49:39.542 --> 12:49:43.502
I can insert and conversely
search for any of these names,

12:49:43.502 --> 12:49:45.182
based on the first letter of their name.

12:49:45.182 --> 12:49:47.580
A, or Z, or H, in this case.

12:49:47.580 --> 12:49:50.372
Let's fast forward and assume we
put a whole bunch of other names--

12:49:50.372 --> 12:49:52.382
might look familiar,
into this hash table.

12:49:52.382 --> 12:49:56.592
It's great because every
name has its own location.

12:49:56.592 --> 12:50:00.962
But if you're thinking of names
you don't yet see it on the screen,

12:50:00.962 --> 12:50:03.192
we eventually encounter a
problem with this, right.

12:50:03.192 --> 12:50:06.962
When could something go wrong
using a hash table like this

12:50:06.962 --> 12:50:09.572
if we wanted to insert even more names?

12:50:09.572 --> 12:50:11.772
What's going to eventually happen?

12:50:11.772 --> 12:50:12.272
Yeah.

12:50:12.272 --> 12:50:14.480
There's already someone with
the first letter, right.

12:50:14.480 --> 12:50:17.342
Like I haven't even mentioned
Harry, for instance, or Hagrid.

12:50:17.342 --> 12:50:19.232
And yet, Hermione's
already using that spot.

12:50:19.232 --> 12:50:21.512
So that invites the
question, well, what happens?

12:50:21.512 --> 12:50:25.082
Maybe, if we want to insert Harry
next, do we maybe cheat and put him

12:50:25.082 --> 12:50:26.192
at location I?

12:50:26.192 --> 12:50:28.805
But then if there's a location
I, where do we put them?

12:50:28.805 --> 12:50:31.472
And it just feels like the situation
could very quickly devolve.

12:50:31.472 --> 12:50:34.412
But I've deliberately
drawn this data structure,

12:50:34.412 --> 12:50:37.472
that I claim as a hash
table, in 2 directions.

12:50:37.472 --> 12:50:39.602
An array vertically, here.

12:50:39.602 --> 12:50:42.782
But what might this be hinting
I'm using horizontally,

12:50:42.782 --> 12:50:45.782
even though I'm drawing the rectangles
a little differently from before?

12:50:45.782 --> 12:50:46.574
AUDIENCE: An array.

12:50:46.574 --> 12:50:47.240
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

12:50:47.240 --> 12:50:48.573
Maybe another array, to be fair.

12:50:48.573 --> 12:50:51.740
But, honestly, arrays are such a pain
with the allocating, and reallocating,

12:50:51.740 --> 12:50:52.292
and so forth.

12:50:52.292 --> 12:50:56.082
These look like the beginnings
of a linked list, if you will.

12:50:56.082 --> 12:50:59.672
Where the name is where the number
used to be, even though I'm drawing it

12:50:59.672 --> 12:51:01.682
horizontally now just
for discussion's sake.

12:51:01.682 --> 12:51:05.282
And this seems to be a pointer
that isn't pointing anywhere yet.

12:51:05.282 --> 12:51:10.562
But it looks like the array is 26
pointers, some of which are null,

12:51:10.562 --> 12:51:11.402
that is empty.

12:51:11.402 --> 12:51:14.157
Some of which are pointing at
the first node in a linked list.

12:51:14.157 --> 12:51:16.532
So that's really what a hash
table might be in your mind.

12:51:16.532 --> 12:51:21.310
An amalgam of an array, whose
elements are linked lists.

12:51:21.310 --> 12:51:23.852
And in theory, this gives you
the best of both worlds, right.

12:51:23.852 --> 12:51:26.912
You get random access with
high probability, right.

12:51:26.912 --> 12:51:30.102
You get to jump immediately to the
location you want to put someone.

12:51:30.102 --> 12:51:32.912
But, if you run into this perverse
situation where there's someone

12:51:32.912 --> 12:51:34.352
already there, OK, fine.

12:51:34.352 --> 12:51:37.832
It starts to devolve into a
linked list, but it's at least 26

12:51:37.832 --> 12:51:39.062
smaller length lists.

12:51:39.062 --> 12:51:42.152
Not one massive linked list,
which would be Big O of n.

12:51:42.152 --> 12:51:43.962
And quite slow to solve.

12:51:43.962 --> 12:51:46.112
So if Harry gets inserted in Hagrid.

12:51:46.112 --> 12:51:50.262
Yeah, you have to chain them
together, so to speak, in this way.

12:51:50.262 --> 12:51:53.127
But, at least you've not
painted yourself into a corner.

12:51:53.127 --> 12:51:56.252
And in fact, if we fast forward and
put a whole bunch of familiar names in,

12:51:56.252 --> 12:51:58.602
the data structure
starts to look like this.

12:51:58.602 --> 12:52:00.942
So the chains not terribly long.

12:52:00.942 --> 12:52:03.752
And some of them are actually
of size 0 because there's just

12:52:03.752 --> 12:52:06.632
some unpopular letters of the
alphabet among these names.

12:52:06.632 --> 12:52:08.582
But it seems better than
just putting everyone

12:52:08.582 --> 12:52:11.342
in one big array, or
one big linked list.

12:52:11.342 --> 12:52:15.672
We're trying to balance these trade
offs a little bit in the middle here.

12:52:15.672 --> 12:52:17.892
Well, how might we represent
something like this?

12:52:17.892 --> 12:52:19.622
Here's how we could describe this thing.

12:52:19.622 --> 12:52:22.802
A node in the context of a
linked list could be this.

12:52:22.802 --> 12:52:26.342
I have an array called
word of type char.

12:52:26.342 --> 12:52:30.542
And it's big enough to fit the
longest word in the alphabet plus 1.

12:52:30.542 --> 12:52:32.372
And the plus 1 why, probably?

12:52:32.372 --> 12:52:33.242
AUDIENCE: The null.

12:52:33.242 --> 12:52:34.212
SPEAKER 1: The null character.

12:52:34.212 --> 12:52:37.322
So I'm assuming that longest word
is like a constant defined elsewhere

12:52:37.322 --> 12:52:37.952
in the story.

12:52:37.952 --> 12:52:40.217
And it's something big
like 40, 100, whatever.

12:52:40.217 --> 12:52:43.292
Whatever the longest word
in the Harry Potter universe

12:52:43.292 --> 12:52:45.922
is or the English dictionary is.

12:52:45.922 --> 12:52:51.532
Longest word plus 1 should be sufficient
to store any name in the story here.

12:52:51.532 --> 12:52:53.842
And then, what else does it
each of these nodes have?

12:52:53.842 --> 12:52:57.542
Well it has a pointer to another node.

12:52:57.542 --> 12:52:59.872
So here's how we might
implement the notion of a node

12:52:59.872 --> 12:53:04.192
in the context of storing
not integers, but names.

12:53:04.192 --> 12:53:05.842
Instead, like this.

12:53:05.842 --> 12:53:08.842
But how do we decide what
the hash table itself is?

12:53:08.842 --> 12:53:12.622
Well, if we now have a definition of a
node, we could have a variable in main,

12:53:12.622 --> 12:53:14.992
or even globally, called hash table.

12:53:14.992 --> 12:53:20.392
That itself is an array
of node* pointers.

12:53:20.392 --> 12:53:22.792
That is an array of pointers to nodes.

12:53:22.792 --> 12:53:24.772
The beginnings of linked lists.

12:53:24.772 --> 12:53:26.432
Number of buckets is to me.

12:53:26.432 --> 12:53:28.565
I proposed, verbally, that it be 26.

12:53:28.565 --> 12:53:30.982
But honestly, if you get a lot
of collisions, so to speak.

12:53:30.982 --> 12:53:33.105
A lot of H names trying
to go to the same place.

12:53:33.105 --> 12:53:35.272
Well, maybe, we need to be
smarter and not just look

12:53:35.272 --> 12:53:36.689
at the first letter of their name.

12:53:36.689 --> 12:53:38.282
But, maybe, the first and the second.

12:53:38.282 --> 12:53:42.382
So it's H-A and H-E. But wait, no,
then Harry and Hagrid still collide.

12:53:42.382 --> 12:53:45.322
But we start to at least make
the problem a little less

12:53:45.322 --> 12:53:48.982
impactful by tinkering with
something like the number of buckets

12:53:48.982 --> 12:53:50.362
in a hash table like this.

12:53:50.362 --> 12:53:55.042
But how do we decide where someone
goes in a hash table in this way?

12:53:55.042 --> 12:53:57.382
Well, it's an old school
problem of input and output.

12:53:57.382 --> 12:54:00.742
The input to the problem is going
to be something like the name.

12:54:00.742 --> 12:54:02.782
And the algorithm in
the middle, as of today,

12:54:02.782 --> 12:54:05.212
is going to be something
called a hash function.

12:54:05.212 --> 12:54:07.102
A hash function is
generally something that

12:54:07.102 --> 12:54:10.852
takes as input, a string, a
number, whatever, and produces

12:54:10.852 --> 12:54:13.342
as output a location in our context.

12:54:13.342 --> 12:54:15.232
Like a number 0 through 25.

12:54:15.232 --> 12:54:16.972
Or 0 through 16,000.

12:54:16.972 --> 12:54:19.672
Or whatever the number
of buckets you want is,

12:54:19.672 --> 12:54:23.852
it's going to just tell you where to
put that input at a specific location.

12:54:23.852 --> 12:54:27.682
So, for instance, Albus, according to
the story thus far, gave me back to 0

12:54:27.682 --> 12:54:28.192
as output.

12:54:28.192 --> 12:54:30.052
Zacharias gave me 25.

12:54:30.052 --> 12:54:32.782
So the hash function, in the
middle of that black box,

12:54:32.782 --> 12:54:35.242
is pretty simplistic in this story.

12:54:35.242 --> 12:54:38.842
It's just looking at the Ascii
value, it seems, of the first letter

12:54:38.842 --> 12:54:39.592
in their name.

12:54:39.592 --> 12:54:42.632
And then, subtracting
off what capital A is 65.

12:54:42.632 --> 12:54:46.952
So like doing some math to get
back in number between 0 and 25.

12:54:46.952 --> 12:54:50.092
So that's how we got to
this point in the story.

12:54:50.092 --> 12:54:54.922
And how might we, then, resolve
the problem further and use

12:54:54.922 --> 12:54:56.542
this notion of hashing more generally?

12:54:56.542 --> 12:54:58.417
Well just for demonstration
sake here, here's

12:54:58.417 --> 12:55:00.772
actually some buckets, literally.

12:55:00.772 --> 12:55:03.862
And we've labeled, in advance,
these buckets with the suits

12:55:03.862 --> 12:55:05.282
from a deck of cards.

12:55:05.282 --> 12:55:07.252
So we've got some spades.

12:55:07.252 --> 12:55:12.082
And we've got diamonds here.

12:55:12.082 --> 12:55:15.592
And we've got, what else here?

12:55:15.592 --> 12:55:19.372
Clubs and hearts.

12:55:19.372 --> 12:55:22.074
So we have a deck of cards
here, for instance, right.

12:55:22.074 --> 12:55:24.532
And this is something you,
yourself, might do instinctively

12:55:24.532 --> 12:55:26.902
if you're getting ready to
start playing a game of cards.

12:55:26.902 --> 12:55:29.069
You're just cleaning up or
you want things in order.

12:55:29.069 --> 12:55:31.445
Like, here is literally
a jumbo deck of cards.

12:55:31.445 --> 12:55:33.862
What would be the easiest way
for me to sort these things?

12:55:33.862 --> 12:55:36.570
Well we've got a whole bunch of
sorting algorithms from the past.

12:55:36.570 --> 12:55:39.112
So I could go through like,
here's the 3 of diamonds.

12:55:39.112 --> 12:55:41.362
And I could, here let me
throw this up on the screen.

12:55:41.362 --> 12:55:43.052
Just so, if you're far in back.

12:55:43.052 --> 12:55:45.382
So here's diamonds.

12:55:45.382 --> 12:55:46.372
I could put this here.

12:55:46.372 --> 12:55:47.992
3, 4.

12:55:47.992 --> 12:55:49.612
I could do this in order here.

12:55:49.612 --> 12:55:52.022
But a lot of us, honestly,
if given a deck of cards.

12:55:52.022 --> 12:55:54.772
And you just want to clean
it up and sort it in order,

12:55:54.772 --> 12:55:56.102
you might do things like this.

12:55:56.102 --> 12:55:59.512
Well here's my input, 3 of diamonds,
let's put it in this bucket.

12:55:59.512 --> 12:56:01.252
4 of diamonds, this bucket.

12:56:01.252 --> 12:56:03.122
5 of diamonds, this bucket.

12:56:03.122 --> 12:56:06.982
And if you keep going through the cards,
here's seven of hearts, hearts bucket.

12:56:06.982 --> 12:56:08.692
8's bucket.

12:56:08.692 --> 12:56:10.552
Queen of spades over here.

12:56:10.552 --> 12:56:12.502
And it's still going
to take you 52 steps.

12:56:12.502 --> 12:56:15.502
But at the end of it, you
have hashed all of the cards

12:56:15.502 --> 12:56:17.092
into 4 distinct buckets.

12:56:17.092 --> 12:56:19.972
And now you have problems
of size 13, which

12:56:19.972 --> 12:56:23.512
is a little more tenable than
doing one massive 52 card problem.

12:56:23.512 --> 12:56:25.552
You can now do 4, 13 size problems.

12:56:25.552 --> 12:56:29.272
And so hashing is something that even
you and I might do instinctively.

12:56:29.272 --> 12:56:34.162
Taking as input some card, some name,
and producing as output some location.

12:56:34.162 --> 12:56:39.442
A temporary pile in which you
want to stage things, so to speak.

12:56:39.442 --> 12:56:41.924
But these collisions are inevitable.

12:56:41.924 --> 12:56:44.632
And honestly, if we kept going
through the Harry Potter universe,

12:56:44.632 --> 12:56:47.432
some of these chains would get
longer, and longer and longer.

12:56:47.432 --> 12:56:50.812
Which means that instead of
getting someone's name quickly,

12:56:50.812 --> 12:56:53.660
by searching for them
or inserting them, might

12:56:53.660 --> 12:56:55.202
start taking a decent amount of time.

12:56:55.202 --> 12:56:58.252
So what could we do instead to
resolve situations like this?

12:56:58.252 --> 12:57:01.852
If the problem, fundamentally, is
that the first letter is just too darn

12:57:01.852 --> 12:57:04.869
popular, H, we need
to take in more input.

12:57:04.869 --> 12:57:07.202
Not just the first letter but
maybe the first 2 letters.

12:57:07.202 --> 12:57:10.252
So if we do that, we
can go from A through Z

12:57:10.252 --> 12:57:16.682
to something more extreme like maybe
H-A, H-B, H-C, H-D, H-F, and so forth.

12:57:16.682 --> 12:57:20.152
So that now Harry and Hermione
end up at different locations.

12:57:20.152 --> 12:57:23.072
But, darn it, Hagrid
still collides with Harry.

12:57:23.072 --> 12:57:24.862
So it's better than before.

12:57:24.862 --> 12:57:27.032
The chains aren't quite as long.

12:57:27.032 --> 12:57:28.892
But the problem isn't
fundamentally gone.

12:57:28.892 --> 12:57:32.122
And in this case here, anyone
know how many buckets we just

12:57:32.122 --> 12:57:40.312
increased to, if we now look at not just
a through Z but AA through ZZ, roughly?

12:57:40.312 --> 12:57:41.665
AUDIENCE: 26 squared.

12:57:41.665 --> 12:57:42.332
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

12:57:42.332 --> 12:57:42.922
OK, good.

12:57:42.922 --> 12:57:46.462
So the easy answer to
26 squared are 676.

12:57:46.462 --> 12:57:48.052
So that's a lot more buckets.

12:57:48.052 --> 12:57:50.522
And this is why I only showed
a few of them on the screen.

12:57:50.522 --> 12:57:51.412
So that's a lot more.

12:57:51.412 --> 12:57:54.532
And it spreads things out in particular.

12:57:54.532 --> 12:57:56.122
What if we take this one step further?

12:57:56.122 --> 12:58:01.612
Instead of H-A, we do like H-A-A,
H-A-B, H-A-C, H-Z-Z, and so forth.

12:58:01.612 --> 12:58:03.562
Well now, we have an
even better situation.

12:58:03.562 --> 12:58:05.962
Because Hermoine has her one spot.

12:58:05.962 --> 12:58:07.252
Harry has his one spot.

12:58:07.252 --> 12:58:09.322
Hagrid has his one spot.

12:58:09.322 --> 12:58:11.362
But there's a trade off here.

12:58:11.362 --> 12:58:14.722
The upside is now, arithmetically,
we can find their locations

12:58:14.722 --> 12:58:16.102
in constant time.

12:58:16.102 --> 12:58:17.512
Maybe, technically 3 steps.

12:58:17.512 --> 12:58:21.422
But 3 is constant, no matter how many
other names are in here, it would seem.

12:58:21.422 --> 12:58:24.634
But what's the downside here?

12:58:24.634 --> 12:58:25.342
Sorry, say again.

12:58:25.342 --> 12:58:25.972
AUDIENCE: Memory.

12:58:25.972 --> 12:58:26.722
SPEAKER 1: Memory.

12:58:26.722 --> 12:58:27.772
So significantly more.

12:58:27.772 --> 12:58:33.322
We're now up to 17,576 buckets, which
itself isn't that big a deal, right.

12:58:33.322 --> 12:58:35.222
Computers have a lot
of memory these days.

12:58:35.222 --> 12:58:38.932
But as you can infer,
I can't really think

12:58:38.932 --> 12:58:43.642
of someone whose name started with
H-E-Q, for instance, in the Harry

12:58:43.642 --> 12:58:44.314
Potter universe.

12:58:44.314 --> 12:58:46.522
And if we keep going,
definitely don't know of anyone

12:58:46.522 --> 12:58:49.522
whose name started with
Z-Z-Z or A-A-A. There's

12:58:49.522 --> 12:58:54.872
a lot of not useful combinations
that have to be there mathematically,

12:58:54.872 --> 12:58:58.522
so that you can do a bit of math
and jump to randomly, so to speak,

12:58:58.522 --> 12:58:59.774
the precise location.

12:58:59.774 --> 12:59:01.232
But they're just going to be empty.

12:59:01.232 --> 12:59:04.862
So it's a very sparsely
populated array, so to speak.

12:59:04.862 --> 12:59:08.122
So what does that really mean
for performance, ultimately?

12:59:08.122 --> 12:59:10.882
Well let's consider, again, in
the context of our Big O notation.

12:59:10.882 --> 12:59:14.272
It turns out that a hash
table, technically speaking,

12:59:14.272 --> 12:59:18.352
is still just going to give us
Big O of n in the worst case.

12:59:18.352 --> 12:59:18.952
Why?

12:59:18.952 --> 12:59:21.922
If you have some crazy perverse
case where everyone in the universe

12:59:21.922 --> 12:59:25.432
has a name that starts with A, or
starts with H, or starts with Z,

12:59:25.432 --> 12:59:26.722
you just get really unlucky.

12:59:26.722 --> 12:59:28.599
And your chain is massively long.

12:59:28.599 --> 12:59:30.682
Well then, at that point,
it's just a linked list.

12:59:30.682 --> 12:59:31.599
It's not a hash table.

12:59:31.599 --> 12:59:33.862
It's like the perverse
situation with the tree, where

12:59:33.862 --> 12:59:39.682
if you insert it without any mind for
keeping it balance, it just evolves.

12:59:39.682 --> 12:59:43.882
But there's a difference here
between a theoretical performance

12:59:43.882 --> 12:59:45.502
and an actual performance.

12:59:45.502 --> 12:59:48.772
If you look back at the
the hash table here,

12:59:48.772 --> 12:59:55.372
this is absolutely, in practice, going
to be faster than a single linked list.

12:59:55.372 --> 12:59:58.342
Mathematically, asymptotically,
big O notation, sure.

12:59:58.342 --> 12:59:59.182
It's all the same.

12:59:59.182 --> 13:00:00.112
Big O of n.

13:00:00.112 --> 13:00:03.982
But if what we're really caring about
is real humans using our software,

13:00:03.982 --> 13:00:06.472
there's something to be said
for crafting a data structure.

13:00:06.472 --> 13:00:09.052
That technically, if this data
were uniformly distributed,

13:00:09.052 --> 13:00:12.932
is 26 times faster than
a linked list alone.

13:00:12.932 --> 13:00:18.202
And so, there's this tension too
between systems, types of CS,

13:00:18.202 --> 13:00:19.329
and theoretical CS.

13:00:19.329 --> 13:00:21.412
Where yeah, theoretically,
these are all the same.

13:00:21.412 --> 13:00:24.142
But in practice, for
making real-world software,

13:00:24.142 --> 13:00:29.872
improving this speed by a factor of 26
in this case, let alone 576 or more,

13:00:29.872 --> 13:00:31.652
might actually make a big difference.

13:00:31.652 --> 13:00:33.152
But there's going to be a trade off.

13:00:33.152 --> 13:00:37.022
And that's typically some other
resource like giving up more space.

13:00:37.022 --> 13:00:37.522
All right.

13:00:37.522 --> 13:00:40.582
How about another data
structure we could build.

13:00:40.582 --> 13:00:43.492
Let me fast forward to
something here called a trie.

13:00:43.492 --> 13:00:46.402
So a trie, a weird
name in pronunciation.

13:00:46.402 --> 13:00:49.432
Short for retrieval,
pronounced trie typically.

13:00:49.432 --> 13:00:55.162
A trie is a tree that actually
gives us constant time lookup,

13:00:55.162 --> 13:00:58.522
even for massive data sets.

13:00:58.522 --> 13:00:59.572
What do I mean by this?

13:00:59.572 --> 13:01:04.712
In the world of a trie, you
create a tree out of arrays.

13:01:04.712 --> 13:01:07.042
So we're really getting into
the Frankenstein territory

13:01:07.042 --> 13:01:09.802
of just building things up with
spare parts of data structures

13:01:09.802 --> 13:01:10.982
that we have here.

13:01:10.982 --> 13:01:13.942
But the root of a trie
is, itself, an array.

13:01:13.942 --> 13:01:16.012
For instance, of size 26.

13:01:16.012 --> 13:01:22.282
Where each element in that
trie points to another node,

13:01:22.282 --> 13:01:23.992
which is to say another array.

13:01:23.992 --> 13:01:26.962
And each of those locations in
the array represents a letter

13:01:26.962 --> 13:01:28.402
of the alphabet like A through Z.

13:01:28.402 --> 13:01:32.452
So for instance, if you wanted to store
the names of the Harry Potter universe,

13:01:32.452 --> 13:01:36.532
not in a hash table, not in a linked
list, not in a tree, but in a trie.

13:01:36.532 --> 13:01:41.302
What you would do is hash on every
letter in the person's name one

13:01:41.302 --> 13:01:42.122
at a time.

13:01:42.122 --> 13:01:45.532
So a trie is like a multi-tier
hash table, in a sense.

13:01:45.532 --> 13:01:47.252
Where you first look
at the first letter,

13:01:47.252 --> 13:01:49.960
then the second letter, then the
third, and you do the following.

13:01:49.960 --> 13:01:53.422
For instance, each of these
locations represents a letter A

13:01:53.422 --> 13:01:56.932
through Z. Suppose I wanted to
insert someone's name into this

13:01:56.932 --> 13:02:01.012
that starts with the letter
H, like Hagrid for instance.

13:02:01.012 --> 13:02:03.842
Well, I go to the location
H. I see it's null,

13:02:03.842 --> 13:02:06.922
which means I need to malloc myself
another node or another array.

13:02:06.922 --> 13:02:08.452
And that's depicted here.

13:02:08.452 --> 13:02:12.292
Then, suppose I want to store the
second letter in Hagrid's name,

13:02:12.292 --> 13:02:14.914
an A. So I go to that
location in the second node.

13:02:14.914 --> 13:02:16.372
And I see, OK, it's currently null.

13:02:16.372 --> 13:02:17.414
There's nothing below it.

13:02:17.414 --> 13:02:19.922
So I allocate another node
using malloc or the like.

13:02:19.922 --> 13:02:24.172
And now I have H-A-G. And
I continue this with R-I-D.

13:02:24.172 --> 13:02:27.722
And then, when I get to the
bottom of this person's name,

13:02:27.722 --> 13:02:30.322
I just have to indicate
here in color, but probably

13:02:30.322 --> 13:02:31.762
with a Boolean value or something.

13:02:31.762 --> 13:02:35.672
Like a true value that
says, a name stops here.

13:02:35.672 --> 13:02:41.222
So that it's clear that the person's
name is not H-A, or H-A-G, or H-A-G-R,

13:02:41.222 --> 13:02:45.752
or H-A-G-R-I. It's H-A-G-R-I-D.
And the D is green,

13:02:45.752 --> 13:02:49.082
just to indicate there's like some
other Boolean value that just says, yes.

13:02:49.082 --> 13:02:52.782
This is the node in
which the name stops.

13:02:52.782 --> 13:02:57.722
And if I continue this logic, here's
how I might insert someone like Harry.

13:02:57.722 --> 13:03:00.902
And here's how I might
insert someone like Hermione.

13:03:00.902 --> 13:03:05.492
And what's interesting about the
design here is that some of these names

13:03:05.492 --> 13:03:07.412
share a common prefix.

13:03:07.412 --> 13:03:10.472
Which starts to get compelling
because you're reusing space.

13:03:10.472 --> 13:03:15.392
You're using the same nodes
for names like H-A-G and H-A-R

13:03:15.392 --> 13:03:17.852
because they share H and an A in common.

13:03:17.852 --> 13:03:20.112
And they all share an H in common.

13:03:20.112 --> 13:03:23.822
So you have this data structure
now that, itself, is a tree.

13:03:23.822 --> 13:03:27.572
Each node in the tree
is, itself, an array.

13:03:27.572 --> 13:03:31.172
And we, therefore, might implement
this thing using code like this.

13:03:31.172 --> 13:03:36.677
Every node is containing, I'll
do it in reverse order, an array.

13:03:36.677 --> 13:03:39.302
I'll call it children because
that's what it really represents.

13:03:39.302 --> 13:03:41.612
Up to 26 children for
each of these nodes.

13:03:41.612 --> 13:03:42.912
Size of the alphabet.

13:03:42.912 --> 13:03:45.842
So I might have used just
a constant for number 26,

13:03:45.842 --> 13:03:47.882
to give myself 26
letters of the alphabet.

13:03:47.882 --> 13:03:52.112
And each of those arrays
stores that many node stars.

13:03:52.112 --> 13:03:54.032
That many pointers to another node.

13:03:54.032 --> 13:03:55.502
And here's an example of the Bool.

13:03:55.502 --> 13:03:58.232
This is what I represented in
green on the slide a moment ago.

13:03:58.232 --> 13:04:00.062
I also need another piece of data.

13:04:00.062 --> 13:04:03.002
Just a 0 or 1, a true
or false, that says yes.

13:04:03.002 --> 13:04:08.292
A name stops in this node or it's just
a path to the rest of the person's name.

13:04:08.292 --> 13:04:12.572
But the upside of this is
that the height of this tree

13:04:12.572 --> 13:04:15.572
is only as tall as the
person's longest name.

13:04:15.572 --> 13:04:22.412
H-A-G-R-I-D or H-E-R-M-O-I-N-E. And
notice that no matter how many other

13:04:22.412 --> 13:04:26.222
people are in this data structure,
there's 3 at the moment,

13:04:26.222 --> 13:04:30.632
if there were 3 million, it would
still take me how many steps to search

13:04:30.632 --> 13:04:31.982
for Hermoine?

13:04:31.982 --> 13:04:37.232
H-E-R-M-I-O-N-E. So, 8 steps total.

13:04:37.232 --> 13:04:42.062
No matter if there's 2 other people,
2 million, 10 million other people.

13:04:42.062 --> 13:04:46.142
Because the path to her name
is always on the same path.

13:04:46.142 --> 13:04:51.032
And if you assume that there's a
maximum limit on the length of names

13:04:51.032 --> 13:04:51.902
in the human world.

13:04:51.902 --> 13:04:53.992
Maybe it's 40, 100, whatever.

13:04:53.992 --> 13:04:55.742
Whatever the longest
name in the world is.

13:04:55.742 --> 13:04:56.642
That's constant.

13:04:56.642 --> 13:04:59.112
Maybe it's 40, 100, but that's constant.

13:04:59.112 --> 13:05:02.322
Which is to say that with a
trie, technically speaking,

13:05:02.322 --> 13:05:06.962
it is the case that your lookup
time, Big O of n, a big O notation,

13:05:06.962 --> 13:05:09.002
would be big O of 1.

13:05:09.002 --> 13:05:12.062
It's constant time, because
unlike every other data structure

13:05:12.062 --> 13:05:16.922
we've looked at, with a trie, the amount
of time it takes you to find one person

13:05:16.922 --> 13:05:20.402
or insert one person is
completely independent of how

13:05:20.402 --> 13:05:24.692
many other pieces of data are
already in the data structure.

13:05:24.692 --> 13:05:27.452
And this holds true even if one
name is a prefix of another.

13:05:27.452 --> 13:05:30.855
I don't think there was a Daniel or
Danielle in the Harry Potter universe

13:05:30.855 --> 13:05:31.772
that I could think of.

13:05:31.772 --> 13:05:35.882
But, D-A-N-I-E-L could be one name.

13:05:35.882 --> 13:05:38.470
And, therefore, we have
a true there in green.

13:05:38.470 --> 13:05:40.262
And if there's a longer
name like Danielle.

13:05:40.262 --> 13:05:42.242
Then, you keep going
until you get to the E.

13:05:42.242 --> 13:05:45.032
So you can still have with
a trie, one name that's

13:05:45.032 --> 13:05:47.142
a substring of another name.

13:05:47.142 --> 13:05:49.862
So it's not as though we've
created a problem there.

13:05:49.862 --> 13:05:51.534
That, too, is still possible.

13:05:51.534 --> 13:05:54.242
But at the end of the day, it only
takes a finite number of steps

13:05:54.242 --> 13:05:55.892
to find any of these people.

13:05:55.892 --> 13:05:58.802
And again, that's what's
particularly compelling.

13:05:58.802 --> 13:06:00.880
That you effectively have
constant time lookup.

13:06:00.880 --> 13:06:01.922
So that's amazing, right.

13:06:01.922 --> 13:06:05.635
We've gone through this whole story
for weeks now of like, linear time.

13:06:05.635 --> 13:06:07.052
And then, it went up to n squared.

13:06:07.052 --> 13:06:07.832
And then, log n.

13:06:07.832 --> 13:06:12.912
And now constant time, what's the price
paid for a data structure like this?

13:06:12.912 --> 13:06:16.112
This so-called trie?

13:06:16.112 --> 13:06:17.292
What's the downside here?

13:06:17.292 --> 13:06:19.022
There's got to be a catch.

13:06:19.022 --> 13:06:21.452
And in fact, tries are not
actually used that often,

13:06:21.452 --> 13:06:24.982
amazing as they might sound
on some CS level here.

13:06:24.982 --> 13:06:25.742
AUDIENCE: Memory.

13:06:25.742 --> 13:06:27.002
SPEAKER 1: Memory.

13:06:27.002 --> 13:06:28.217
In what sense?

13:06:28.217 --> 13:06:30.380
AUDIENCE: Much like a [INAUDIBLE].

13:06:30.380 --> 13:06:31.172
SPEAKER 1: Exactly.

13:06:31.172 --> 13:06:33.092
If you're storing all
of these darn arrays

13:06:33.092 --> 13:06:36.352
it's, again, a sparsely
populated data structure.

13:06:36.352 --> 13:06:37.352
And you can see it here.

13:06:37.352 --> 13:06:41.282
Granted there's only 3 names, but most
of those boxes, most of those pointers,

13:06:41.282 --> 13:06:42.972
are going to remain null.

13:06:42.972 --> 13:06:46.022
So this is an incredibly wide
data structure, if you will.

13:06:46.022 --> 13:06:48.522
It uses a huge amount of
memory to store the names.

13:06:48.522 --> 13:06:50.342
But again, you've got to pick a lane.

13:06:50.342 --> 13:06:53.462
Either you're going to minimize space
or you're going to minimize time.

13:06:53.462 --> 13:06:56.722
It's not really possible to get
truly the best of both worlds.

13:06:56.722 --> 13:06:58.772
You have to decide where
the inflection point is

13:06:58.772 --> 13:07:01.592
for the device you're writing
software for, how much memory it has,

13:07:01.592 --> 13:07:02.942
how expensive it is.

13:07:02.942 --> 13:07:06.462
And again, taking all of
these things into account.

13:07:06.462 --> 13:07:08.882
So lastly, let's do one
further abstraction.

13:07:08.882 --> 13:07:12.392
So even higher level to discuss
something that are generally

13:07:12.392 --> 13:07:14.444
known as abstract data structures.

13:07:14.444 --> 13:07:16.152
It turns out we could
spend like all day,

13:07:16.152 --> 13:07:17.732
all week, talking about
different things we

13:07:17.732 --> 13:07:19.182
could build with these data structures.

13:07:19.182 --> 13:07:21.140
But for the most part,
now that we have arrays.

13:07:21.140 --> 13:07:23.912
Now that we have linked lists
or their cousin's trees, which

13:07:23.912 --> 13:07:24.910
are 2-dimensional.

13:07:24.910 --> 13:07:26.702
And beyond that, there's
even graphs, where

13:07:26.702 --> 13:07:29.889
the arrows can go in multiple
directions, not just down, so to speak.

13:07:29.889 --> 13:07:32.222
Now that we have this ability
to stitch things together,

13:07:32.222 --> 13:07:34.272
we can solve all different
types of problems.

13:07:34.272 --> 13:07:38.222
So, for instance, a very
common type of data structure

13:07:38.222 --> 13:07:42.212
to use in a program, or even our
human world, are things called queues.

13:07:42.212 --> 13:07:46.262
A queue being a data structure
like a line outside of a store.

13:07:46.262 --> 13:07:48.332
Where it has what's
called a FIFO property.

13:07:48.332 --> 13:07:49.722
First In, First Out.

13:07:49.722 --> 13:07:52.142
Which is great for fairness,
at least in the human world.

13:07:52.142 --> 13:07:56.282
And if you've ever waited outside
of Tasty Burger, or Salsa Fresca,

13:07:56.282 --> 13:07:58.472
or some other restaurant
nearby, presumably,

13:07:58.472 --> 13:08:01.262
if you're queuing up at
the counter, you want

13:08:01.262 --> 13:08:03.752
them store to maintain a FIFO system.

13:08:03.752 --> 13:08:05.012
First in and first out.

13:08:05.012 --> 13:08:08.642
So that whoever's first in line gets
their food first and gets out first.

13:08:08.642 --> 13:08:12.192
So a queue is actually a
computer science term, too.

13:08:12.192 --> 13:08:14.942
And even if you're still in the
habit of printing things on paper,

13:08:14.942 --> 13:08:17.192
there are things you might
have heard called printer

13:08:17.192 --> 13:08:19.532
queues, which also do things in order.

13:08:19.532 --> 13:08:21.949
The first person to send
their essay to the printer

13:08:21.949 --> 13:08:24.032
should, ideally, be printed
before the last person

13:08:24.032 --> 13:08:26.402
to send their essay to the printer.

13:08:26.402 --> 13:08:28.202
Again, in the interest of fairness.

13:08:28.202 --> 13:08:29.852
But how can you implement a queue?

13:08:29.852 --> 13:08:32.732
Well, you typically have to
implement 2 fundamental operations,

13:08:32.732 --> 13:08:34.292
enqueue and dequeue.

13:08:34.292 --> 13:08:37.392
So adding something to it and
removing something from it.

13:08:37.392 --> 13:08:41.132
And the interesting thing here is
that how do you implement a queue?

13:08:41.132 --> 13:08:44.132
Well in the human world, you would
just have literally physical space

13:08:44.132 --> 13:08:46.772
for humans to line up from left
to right, or right to left.

13:08:46.772 --> 13:08:47.815
Same in a computer.

13:08:47.815 --> 13:08:50.732
Like a printer queue, if you send a
whole bunch of jobs to be printed,

13:08:50.732 --> 13:08:52.832
a whole bunch of essays
or documents, well, you

13:08:52.832 --> 13:08:54.912
need a chunk of memory like an array.

13:08:54.912 --> 13:08:55.412
All right.

13:08:55.412 --> 13:08:57.632
Well, if you use an
array, what's a problem

13:08:57.632 --> 13:09:01.242
that could happen in the world
of printing, for instance?

13:09:01.242 --> 13:09:04.502
If you use an array to store all of
the documents that need to be printed.

13:09:04.502 --> 13:09:05.660
AUDIENCE: It can be filled.

13:09:05.660 --> 13:09:07.202
SPEAKER 1: It could be filled, right.

13:09:07.202 --> 13:09:10.502
So if the programmer decided, HP or
whoever makes the printer decides,

13:09:10.502 --> 13:09:14.162
oh, you can send like a megabyte worth
of documents to this printer at once.

13:09:14.162 --> 13:09:16.212
At some point you might
get an error message,

13:09:16.212 --> 13:09:17.582
which says, sorry out of memory.

13:09:17.582 --> 13:09:18.477
Wait a few minutes.

13:09:18.477 --> 13:09:20.852
Which is maybe a reasonable
solution, but a little annoy.

13:09:20.852 --> 13:09:24.482
Or HP could write code that maybe
dynamically resizes the array

13:09:24.482 --> 13:09:25.152
or so forth.

13:09:25.152 --> 13:09:27.722
But at that point, maybe they
should just use a linked list.

13:09:27.722 --> 13:09:28.652
And they could.

13:09:28.652 --> 13:09:32.372
So there, too, you could
implement the notion of a queue

13:09:32.372 --> 13:09:33.720
using a linked list instead.

13:09:33.720 --> 13:09:35.762
You're going to spend more
memory, but you're not

13:09:35.762 --> 13:09:38.132
going to run out of space in your array.

13:09:38.132 --> 13:09:39.975
Which might be more compelling.

13:09:39.975 --> 13:09:41.642
This happens even in the physical world.

13:09:41.642 --> 13:09:45.122
You go to the store and you start having
to line up outside and down the road.

13:09:45.122 --> 13:09:49.409
And like, for a really busy store,
they run out of space so they make do.

13:09:49.409 --> 13:09:51.992
But in that case, it tends to
be more of an array just because

13:09:51.992 --> 13:09:54.447
of the physical notion
of humans lining up.

13:09:54.447 --> 13:09:56.072
But there's other data structures, too.

13:09:56.072 --> 13:09:59.197
If you've ever gone to the dining hall
and picked up like a Harvard or Yale

13:09:59.197 --> 13:10:04.352
tray, you're typically picking up
the last tray that was just cleaned,

13:10:04.352 --> 13:10:06.212
not the first tray that was cleaned.

13:10:06.212 --> 13:10:06.722
Why?

13:10:06.722 --> 13:10:10.652
Because these cafeteria trays
stack up on top of each other.

13:10:10.652 --> 13:10:13.892
And indeed a stack is another
type of abstract data structure.

13:10:13.892 --> 13:10:16.352
In the physical world, it's
literally something physical

13:10:16.352 --> 13:10:18.512
like a stack of trays.

13:10:18.512 --> 13:10:21.422
Which have what we would
call a LIFO property.

13:10:21.422 --> 13:10:22.942
Last In, First Out.

13:10:22.942 --> 13:10:24.692
So as these things
come out of the washer,

13:10:24.692 --> 13:10:27.002
they're putting the most
recent ones on the top.

13:10:27.002 --> 13:10:30.722
And then you, the human, are probably
taking the most recently cleaned one.

13:10:30.722 --> 13:10:33.182
Which means in the
extreme, no one on campus

13:10:33.182 --> 13:10:36.617
might ever use that very first tray.

13:10:36.617 --> 13:10:38.492
Which is probably fine
in the world of trays,

13:10:38.492 --> 13:10:42.452
but would really be bad in the world of
Tasty Burger lining up for food if LIFO

13:10:42.452 --> 13:10:44.252
were the property being implemented.

13:10:44.252 --> 13:10:46.322
But here, too, it could be an array.

13:10:46.322 --> 13:10:47.432
It could be a linked list.

13:10:47.432 --> 13:10:49.015
And you see this, honestly, every day.

13:10:49.015 --> 13:10:51.242
If you're using Gmail
and your Gmail inbox.

13:10:51.242 --> 13:10:53.762
That is actually a stack,
at least by default,

13:10:53.762 --> 13:10:57.160
where your newest message
last in are the first ones

13:10:57.160 --> 13:10:58.202
at the top of the screen.

13:10:58.202 --> 13:11:00.062
That's a LIFO data structure.

13:11:00.062 --> 13:11:02.192
And it means that you see
your most recent emails.

13:11:02.192 --> 13:11:04.650
But if you have a busy day,
you're getting a lot of emails,

13:11:04.650 --> 13:11:05.912
it might not be a good thing.

13:11:05.912 --> 13:11:08.312
Because now you're ignoring
the people who wrote you

13:11:08.312 --> 13:11:10.622
way earlier in the day or the week.

13:11:10.622 --> 13:11:13.082
So LIFO and FIFO are
just properties that you

13:11:13.082 --> 13:11:15.842
can achieve with these very
specific types of data structures.

13:11:15.842 --> 13:11:17.592
And the parliaments
in the world of stacks

13:11:17.592 --> 13:11:21.452
is to push something onto a
stack or pop something out.

13:11:21.452 --> 13:11:23.642
These are here, for instance,
as an example of why

13:11:23.642 --> 13:11:24.932
might you always wear the same color.

13:11:24.932 --> 13:11:27.192
Well, if you're storing all
of your clothes in a stack,

13:11:27.192 --> 13:11:29.012
you might not ever get
to the different colored

13:11:29.012 --> 13:11:30.452
clothes at the bottom of the list.

13:11:30.452 --> 13:11:35.372
And in fact, to paint this picture,
we have a couple of minute video here.

13:11:35.372 --> 13:11:38.372
Just to paint this here, made
by a faculty member elsewhere.

13:11:38.372 --> 13:11:41.312
Let's go ahead and dim the lights
for just a minute or 2 here.

13:11:41.312 --> 13:11:45.467
So that we can take a look
at Jack learning some facts.

13:11:45.467 --> 13:11:46.092
[VIDEO PLAYING]

13:11:46.092 --> 13:11:48.842
SPEAKER 2: Once upon a time,
there was a guy named Jack.

13:11:48.842 --> 13:11:52.232
When it came to making friends
Jack did not have the knack.

13:11:52.232 --> 13:11:55.202
So Jack went to talk to the
most popular guy he knew.

13:11:55.202 --> 13:11:57.872
He went up to Lou and
asked, what do I do?

13:11:57.872 --> 13:12:00.332
Lou saw that his friend
was really distressed.

13:12:00.332 --> 13:12:03.042
Well, Lou began, just
look how you're dressed.

13:12:03.042 --> 13:12:05.612
Don't you have any clothes
with a different look?

13:12:05.612 --> 13:12:06.692
Yes, said Jack.

13:12:06.692 --> 13:12:08.012
I sure do.

13:12:08.012 --> 13:12:10.202
Come to my house and
I'll showed them to you.

13:12:10.202 --> 13:12:11.492
So they went off the Jack's.

13:12:11.492 --> 13:12:15.182
And Jack showed Lou the box, where he
kept all his shirts, and his pants,

13:12:15.182 --> 13:12:16.232
at his socks.

13:12:16.232 --> 13:12:19.202
Lou said, I see you have
all your clothes in a pile.

13:12:19.202 --> 13:12:21.782
Why don't you wear some
others once in a while?

13:12:21.782 --> 13:12:24.932
Jack said, well, when I
remove clothes and socks,

13:12:24.932 --> 13:12:27.662
I wash them and put
them away in the box.

13:12:27.662 --> 13:12:30.152
Then comes the next
morning and up I hop.

13:12:30.152 --> 13:12:33.392
I go to the box and get
my clothes off the top.

13:12:33.392 --> 13:12:36.002
Lou quickly realized
the problem with Jack.

13:12:36.002 --> 13:12:38.972
He kept clothes, CDs,
and books in a stack.

13:12:38.972 --> 13:12:41.402
When he'd reached for
something to read or to wear,

13:12:41.402 --> 13:12:44.012
he chose a top book or underwear.

13:12:44.012 --> 13:12:46.402
Then when he was done he
would put it right back.

13:12:46.402 --> 13:12:48.982
Back it would go on top of the stack.

13:12:48.982 --> 13:12:51.352
I know the solution,
said a triumphant Lou.

13:12:51.352 --> 13:12:53.992
You need to learn to
start using a queue.

13:12:53.992 --> 13:12:56.782
Lou took Jack's clothes
and hung them in a closet.

13:12:56.782 --> 13:12:59.602
And when he had emptied
the box, he just tossed it.

13:12:59.602 --> 13:13:03.472
Then he said, now Jack, at the end of
the day, put your clothes on the left

13:13:03.472 --> 13:13:04.952
when you put them away.

13:13:04.952 --> 13:13:07.672
Then tomorrow morning when
you see the sunshine, get

13:13:07.672 --> 13:13:10.402
your clothes from the right,
from the end of the line.

13:13:10.402 --> 13:13:13.282
Don't you see, said
Lou, it will be so nice.

13:13:13.282 --> 13:13:16.612
You'll wear everything once
before you wear something twice.

13:13:16.612 --> 13:13:19.552
And with everything in queues
in his closet and shelf,

13:13:19.552 --> 13:13:22.162
Jack started to feel
quite sure of himself.

13:13:22.162 --> 13:13:24.637
All thanks to Lou and
his wonderful queue.

13:13:26.702 --> 13:13:29.702
SPEAKER 1: So just to help you realize
that these things are everywhere.

13:13:29.702 --> 13:13:32.312
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

13:13:32.312 --> 13:13:33.862
Even in our human world.

13:13:33.862 --> 13:13:35.542
If you've ever lined up at this place.

13:13:35.542 --> 13:13:37.462
Anyone recognize this?

13:13:37.462 --> 13:13:40.282
OK, so sweetgreen, little
salad place in the square.

13:13:40.282 --> 13:13:42.172
This is if you order
online or in advance,

13:13:42.172 --> 13:13:44.714
your food ends up according to
the first letter in your name.

13:13:44.714 --> 13:13:46.964
Which actually sounds awfully
reminiscent of something

13:13:46.964 --> 13:13:47.782
like a hash table.

13:13:47.782 --> 13:13:50.842
And in fact, no matter whether
you implement a hash table like we

13:13:50.842 --> 13:13:52.612
did, with an array and linked list.

13:13:52.612 --> 13:13:54.817
Or with 3 shelves like this.

13:13:54.817 --> 13:13:57.802
This is actually an abstract
data type called a dictionary.

13:13:57.802 --> 13:14:01.162
And a dictionary, just like in our
human world, has keys and values.

13:14:01.162 --> 13:14:02.872
Words and their definitions.

13:14:02.872 --> 13:14:07.372
This just has letters of the
alphabet and salads as their value.

13:14:07.372 --> 13:14:09.742
But here, too, there's
a real world constraint.

13:14:09.742 --> 13:14:13.222
In what kind of scenario does
this system at sweetgreen

13:14:13.222 --> 13:14:15.892
devolve into a problem, for instance?

13:14:15.892 --> 13:14:19.582
Because they, too, are using only
finite space, finite storage.

13:14:19.582 --> 13:14:20.572
What could go wrong?

13:14:20.572 --> 13:14:20.842
Yeah.

13:14:20.842 --> 13:14:21.772
AUDIENCE: Run out of space.

13:14:21.772 --> 13:14:22.012
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

13:14:22.012 --> 13:14:23.392
If they run out of space
on the shelf and there's

13:14:23.392 --> 13:14:25.862
a lot of people whose names
start with D, or E, or whatever.

13:14:25.862 --> 13:14:26.782
And so, they just pile up.

13:14:26.782 --> 13:14:29.362
And then, maybe, they kind of
overflow into the E's or the F's.

13:14:29.362 --> 13:14:31.282
And they probably don't
really care because any human

13:14:31.282 --> 13:14:33.772
is going to come by, and just
eyeball it, and figure it out anyway.

13:14:33.772 --> 13:14:36.262
But in the world of a
computer, you're the one coding

13:14:36.262 --> 13:14:38.152
and have to be ever so precise.

13:14:38.152 --> 13:14:41.722
We thought we would lastly
do one final thing here.

13:14:41.722 --> 13:14:45.527
In advance, we prepared a linked
list of sorts in the audience.

13:14:45.527 --> 13:14:47.152
Since this has become a bit of a thing.

13:14:47.152 --> 13:14:50.012
I am starting to represent the
beginning of this linked list.

13:14:50.012 --> 13:14:54.592
And so far as I have a pointer
here with seat location G9.

13:14:54.592 --> 13:14:57.982
Whoever is in G9, would
you mind standing up?

13:14:57.982 --> 13:15:00.652
And what letter is on your sheet there?

13:15:00.652 --> 13:15:01.582
AUDIENCE: F15.

13:15:01.582 --> 13:15:04.132
SPEAKER 1: OK, so you
have S15 and your letter--

13:15:04.132 --> 13:15:04.787
AUDIENCE: F15.

13:15:04.787 --> 13:15:05.662
SPEAKER 1: Say again?

13:15:05.662 --> 13:15:06.352
AUDIENCE: F.

13:15:06.352 --> 13:15:07.162
SPEAKER 1: F15.

13:15:07.162 --> 13:15:09.472
So I see you're holding
a C in your node.

13:15:09.472 --> 13:15:12.982
You are pointing to, if
you could physically, F15.

13:15:12.982 --> 13:15:14.362
F15, what do you hold?

13:15:14.362 --> 13:15:15.262
AUDIENCE: S.

13:15:15.262 --> 13:15:17.872
SPEAKER 1: You have an S. And
who should you be pointing at?

13:15:17.872 --> 13:15:18.652
AUDIENCE: F5.

13:15:18.652 --> 13:15:19.412
SPEAKER 1: F5.

13:15:19.412 --> 13:15:20.722
Could you stand up, F5.

13:15:20.722 --> 13:15:22.432
You're holding a 5, I see.

13:15:22.432 --> 13:15:23.512
What address?

13:15:23.512 --> 13:15:24.502
AUDIENCE: F12.

13:15:24.502 --> 13:15:25.522
SPEAKER 1: F12.

13:15:25.522 --> 13:15:26.302
Big finale.

13:15:26.302 --> 13:15:30.502
F12, if you'd like to stand up holding
a 0 and null, which means that was CS50.

13:15:30.502 --> 13:15:34.022
[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]

13:15:34.022 --> 13:15:34.522
All right.

13:15:34.522 --> 13:15:36.522
We'll see you next time.

