13:17:59.492 --> 13:18:01.322
But there, too, the
past few weeks, we've

13:18:01.322 --> 13:18:04.652
been focusing on functions and loops
and variables, conditionals, and really

13:18:04.652 --> 13:18:06.072
all of those same ideas.

13:18:06.072 --> 13:18:10.232
And so what we begin to do today
is to, one, simplify the language

13:18:10.232 --> 13:18:15.362
we're using, transitioning from C now
to Python, this now being the equivalent

13:18:15.362 --> 13:18:18.722
program in Python, and look
at its relative simplicity,

13:18:18.722 --> 13:18:20.462
but also transitioning
to look at how you

13:18:20.462 --> 13:18:22.322
can implement these
same kinds of features,

13:18:22.322 --> 13:18:23.952
just using a different language.

13:18:23.952 --> 13:18:25.772
So we're going to see
a lot of code today.

13:18:25.772 --> 13:18:29.672
And you won't have nearly as much
practice with Python as you did with C.

13:18:29.672 --> 13:18:32.732
But that's because so many of the
ideas are still going to be with us.

13:18:32.732 --> 13:18:35.102
And, really, it's going to be a
process of figuring out, all right,

13:18:35.102 --> 13:18:35.935
I want to do a loop.

13:18:35.935 --> 13:18:38.282
I know how to do it in C.
How do I do this in Python?

13:18:38.282 --> 13:18:39.512
How do I do the same with conditionals?

13:18:39.512 --> 13:18:41.232
How do I declare
variables, and the like,

13:18:41.232 --> 13:18:43.982
and moving forward, not just in
CS50, but in life in general,

13:18:43.982 --> 13:18:47.282
if you continue programming and learn
some other language after the class,

13:18:47.282 --> 13:18:50.792
if in 5-10 years, there's a new, more
popular language that you pick up,

13:18:50.792 --> 13:18:53.042
it's just going to be a
matter of googling and looking

13:18:53.042 --> 13:18:54.932
at websites like Stack
Overflow and the like,

13:18:54.932 --> 13:18:57.872
to look at just basic building
blocks of programming languages,

13:18:57.872 --> 13:19:01.202
because you already speak,
after these past 6 plus weeks,

13:19:01.202 --> 13:19:04.022
you already speak programming
itself fundamentally.

13:19:04.022 --> 13:19:07.592
All right, so let's do a few quick
comparisons, left and right, of what

13:19:07.592 --> 13:19:09.482
something might have
looked like in Scratch,

13:19:09.482 --> 13:19:11.342
and what it then looked
like in C, but now,

13:19:11.342 --> 13:19:13.292
as of today, what it's going
to look like in Python.

13:19:13.292 --> 13:19:15.375
Then we'll turn our attention
to the command line,

13:19:15.375 --> 13:19:19.032
ultimately, in order to
implement some actual programs.

13:19:19.032 --> 13:19:22.262
So in Scratch, we had
functions like this, say Hello,

13:19:22.262 --> 13:19:23.792
world, a verb or an action.

13:19:23.792 --> 13:19:26.262
In C it looked a little
something like this,

13:19:26.262 --> 13:19:29.672
and a bit of a cryptic mess the
first week, you had the printf,

13:19:29.672 --> 13:19:30.812
you had the double quotes.

13:19:30.812 --> 13:19:32.502
You had the semicolon, the parentheses.

13:19:32.502 --> 13:19:34.945
So there's a lot more syntax
just to do the same thing.

13:19:34.945 --> 13:19:37.862
We're not going to get rid of all
of that syntax now, but as of today,

13:19:37.862 --> 13:19:42.102
in Python, that same statement is going
to look a little something like this.

13:19:42.102 --> 13:19:44.162
And just to perhaps call
out the obvious, what

13:19:44.162 --> 13:19:48.572
is different or, now, simpler
in Python versus C, even

13:19:48.572 --> 13:19:50.162
in this simple example here?

13:19:50.162 --> 13:19:51.067
Yeah.

13:19:51.067 --> 13:19:53.942
AUDIENCE: Now print, instead of
printf would be, something like that.

13:19:53.942 --> 13:19:56.359
DAVID J. MALAN: Good, so it's
now print instead of printf.

13:19:56.359 --> 13:19:57.632
And there's also no semicolon.

13:19:57.632 --> 13:19:59.625
And there's one other
subtlety, over here.

13:19:59.625 --> 13:20:00.542
AUDIENCE: No new line.

13:20:00.542 --> 13:20:02.162
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah,
so no new line, and that

13:20:02.162 --> 13:20:03.632
doesn't mean it's not
going to be printed.

13:20:03.632 --> 13:20:05.924
It just turns out that one
of the differences we'll see

13:20:05.924 --> 13:20:08.162
is that, with print, you
get the new line for free.

13:20:08.162 --> 13:20:11.472
It automatically gets outputted by
default, being sort of a common case.

13:20:11.472 --> 13:20:13.712
But you can override it,
we'll see, ultimately, too.

13:20:13.712 --> 13:20:14.822
How about in Scratch?

13:20:14.822 --> 13:20:18.604
We had multiple functions like
this, that not only said something

13:20:18.604 --> 13:20:20.312
on the screen, but
also asked a question,

13:20:20.312 --> 13:20:23.822
thereby being another function that
returned a value, called answer.

13:20:23.822 --> 13:20:26.252
In C we saw code that
looked a little something

13:20:26.252 --> 13:20:29.942
like this, whereby that first line
declares a variable called answer,

13:20:29.942 --> 13:20:32.312
sets it equal to the
return value of getString,

13:20:32.312 --> 13:20:34.262
one of the functions
from the CS50 library,

13:20:34.262 --> 13:20:37.502
and then the same double quotes
and parentheses and semicolon.

13:20:37.502 --> 13:20:41.912
Then we had this format code
in C that allowed us, with %S,

13:20:41.912 --> 13:20:44.282
to actually print out that same value.

13:20:44.282 --> 13:20:46.922
In Python, this, too, is going
to look a little bit simpler.

13:20:46.922 --> 13:20:49.982
Instead, we're going to have
answer equals getString,

13:20:49.982 --> 13:20:52.592
quote unquote "What's your
name," and then print,

13:20:52.592 --> 13:20:55.392
with a plus sign and a
little bit of new syntax.

13:20:55.392 --> 13:20:58.172
But let's see if we can't just
infer from this example what

13:20:58.172 --> 13:20:59.382
it is that's going on.

13:20:59.382 --> 13:21:02.192
Well, first missing on the left is what?

13:21:02.192 --> 13:21:05.142
To the left of the equal sign,
there's no what this time?

13:21:05.142 --> 13:21:06.392
Feel free to just call it out.

13:21:06.392 --> 13:21:07.212
AUDIENCE: Type.

13:21:07.212 --> 13:21:07.982
DAVID J. MALAN: So there's no type.

13:21:07.982 --> 13:21:10.292
There's no type, like
the word string, which

13:21:10.292 --> 13:21:14.612
even though that was a type in
CS50, every other variable in C

13:21:14.612 --> 13:21:17.959
did we use Int or string or
float, or Bool or something else.

13:21:17.959 --> 13:21:20.042
In Python, there are still
going to be data types,

13:21:20.042 --> 13:21:22.502
today onward, but you,
the programmer, don't

13:21:22.502 --> 13:21:25.564
have to bother telling the
computer what types you're using.

13:21:25.564 --> 13:21:27.272
The computer is going
to be smart enough,

13:21:27.272 --> 13:21:29.762
the language, really, is going to be
smart enough, to just figure it out

13:21:29.762 --> 13:21:30.782
from context.

13:21:30.782 --> 13:21:32.672
Meanwhile, on the right
hand side, getString

13:21:32.672 --> 13:21:34.380
is going to be a
function we'll use today

13:21:34.380 --> 13:21:37.842
and this week, which comes from a
Python version of the CS50 library.

13:21:37.842 --> 13:21:40.892
But we'll also start to take off
those training wheels, so that you'll

13:21:40.892 --> 13:21:44.192
see how to do things without
any CS50 library moving forward,

13:21:44.192 --> 13:21:45.812
using a different function instead.

13:21:45.812 --> 13:21:49.442
As before, no semicolon, but the rest
of the syntax is pretty much the same

13:21:49.442 --> 13:21:49.952
here.

13:21:49.952 --> 13:21:52.535
This starts, of course, to get
a little bit different, though.

13:21:52.535 --> 13:21:54.172
We're using print instead of printf.

13:21:54.172 --> 13:21:57.382
But now, even though this
looks a little cryptic,

13:21:57.382 --> 13:21:59.632
perhaps, if you've never
programmed before CS50,

13:21:59.632 --> 13:22:03.652
what might that plus be doing,
just based on inference here.

13:22:03.652 --> 13:22:04.402
What do you think?

13:22:04.402 --> 13:22:08.242
AUDIENCE: Adding answer
to the string Hello.

13:22:08.242 --> 13:22:11.512
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so adding
answer to the string Hello,

13:22:11.512 --> 13:22:13.552
and adding, so to speak,
not mathematically,

13:22:13.552 --> 13:22:16.102
but in the form of joining
them together, much like we

13:22:16.102 --> 13:22:19.562
saw the joined block in Scratch, or
concatenation was the term of art

13:22:19.562 --> 13:22:20.062
there.

13:22:20.062 --> 13:22:23.332
This plus sign appends,
if you will, whatever's

13:22:23.332 --> 13:22:25.147
in answer to whatever is quoted here.

13:22:25.147 --> 13:22:27.772
And I deliberately left a space
there, so that grammatically it

13:22:27.772 --> 13:22:29.944
looks nice, after the comma as well.

13:22:29.944 --> 13:22:31.402
Now there's another way to do this.

13:22:31.402 --> 13:22:33.652
And it, too, is going to
look cryptic at first glance.

13:22:33.652 --> 13:22:36.032
But it just gets easier and
more convenient over time.

13:22:36.032 --> 13:22:41.102
You can also change this second
line to be this, instead.

13:22:41.102 --> 13:22:42.292
So what's going on here.

13:22:42.292 --> 13:22:45.232
This is actually a relatively new
feature of Python in the past couple

13:22:45.232 --> 13:22:47.542
of years, where now what
you're seeing is, yes,

13:22:47.542 --> 13:22:50.102
a string, between these
same double quotes,

13:22:50.102 --> 13:22:53.597
but this is what Python would
call a format string, or Fstring.

13:22:53.597 --> 13:22:56.722
And it literally starts with the letter
F, which admittedly looks, I think,

13:22:56.722 --> 13:22:57.502
a little weird.

13:22:57.502 --> 13:23:01.222
But that just indicates
that Python should

13:23:01.222 --> 13:23:05.632
assume that anything inside of
curly braces inside of the string

13:23:05.632 --> 13:23:09.082
should be interpolated, so to
speak, which is a fancy term saying,

13:23:09.082 --> 13:23:12.682
substitute the value of
any variables therein.

13:23:12.682 --> 13:23:14.552
And it can do some other things as well.

13:23:14.552 --> 13:23:18.562
So answer is a variable, declared,
of course, on this first line.

13:23:18.562 --> 13:23:22.822
This Fstring, then, says to Python,
print out Hello comma space, and then

13:23:22.822 --> 13:23:24.472
the value of Answer.

13:23:24.472 --> 13:23:28.912
If, by contrast, you
omitted the curly braces,

13:23:28.912 --> 13:23:30.562
just take a guess, what would happen?

13:23:30.562 --> 13:23:33.442
What would the symptom of that
bug be, if you accidentally

13:23:33.442 --> 13:23:36.532
forgot the curly braces, but
maybe still had the F there?

13:23:36.532 --> 13:23:38.272
AUDIENCE: It would print below it, too.

13:23:38.272 --> 13:23:40.822
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, it would literally
print Hello, comma answer, because it's

13:23:40.822 --> 13:23:41.722
going to take you literally.

13:23:41.722 --> 13:23:44.212
So the curly braces just kind
of allow you to plug things in.

13:23:44.212 --> 13:23:45.872
And, again, it looks
a little more cryptic,

13:23:45.872 --> 13:23:47.789
but it's just going to
save us time over time.

13:23:47.789 --> 13:23:50.642
And if any of you programmed in
Java in high school, for instance,

13:23:50.642 --> 13:23:53.152
you saw plus in that context,
too, for concatenation.

13:23:53.152 --> 13:23:56.277
This just kind of makes your code a
little tighter, a little more succinct.

13:23:56.277 --> 13:23:58.252
So it's a convenient
feature now in Python.

13:23:58.252 --> 13:24:00.712
All right, this was an example
in Scratch of a variable,

13:24:00.712 --> 13:24:03.262
setting a variable like
counter equal to 0.

13:24:03.262 --> 13:24:06.982
In C it looked like this, where
you specify the type, the name,

13:24:06.982 --> 13:24:08.752
and then the value, with a semicolon.

13:24:08.752 --> 13:24:11.618
In Python, it's going to look like this.

13:24:11.618 --> 13:24:12.951
And I'll state the obvious here.

13:24:12.951 --> 13:24:15.862
You don't need to mention the
type, just like before with string.

13:24:15.862 --> 13:24:17.552
And you don't need a semicolon.

13:24:17.552 --> 13:24:18.652
So it's a little simpler.

13:24:18.652 --> 13:24:21.527
If you want a variable, just write
it and set it equal to some value.

13:24:21.527 --> 13:24:24.592
But the single equal sign
still behaves the same as in C.

13:24:24.592 --> 13:24:26.962
Suppose we wanted to
increment counter by one.

13:24:26.962 --> 13:24:29.272
In Scratch, we use
this puzzle piece here.

13:24:29.272 --> 13:24:31.772
In C, we could do this, actually,
in a few different ways.

13:24:31.772 --> 13:24:33.922
There was this way, if
counter already exists,

13:24:33.922 --> 13:24:36.502
you just say counter
equals counter plus 1.

13:24:36.502 --> 13:24:41.362
There was the slightly less verbose
way, where you could say, oops, sorry.

13:24:41.362 --> 13:24:42.922
Let me do the first sentence first.

13:24:42.922 --> 13:24:45.212
In Python, that same
thing, as you might guess,

13:24:45.212 --> 13:24:48.682
is actually going to be almost the
same, you just throw away the semicolon.

13:24:48.682 --> 13:24:51.892
And the mathematics are ultimately
the same, copying from right to left,

13:24:51.892 --> 13:24:53.812
via the assignment operator.

13:24:53.812 --> 13:24:56.092
Now, recall, in C, that
we had this shorthand

13:24:56.092 --> 13:24:58.522
notation, which did the same thing.

13:24:58.522 --> 13:25:03.502
In Python, you can similarly do the same
thing, just no need for the semicolon.

13:25:03.502 --> 13:25:05.812
The only step backwards
we're taking, if you

13:25:05.812 --> 13:25:10.312
were a big fan of counter plus
plus, that doesn't exist in Python,

13:25:10.312 --> 13:25:11.147
nor minus minus.

13:25:11.147 --> 13:25:12.022
You just can't do it.

13:25:12.022 --> 13:25:16.732
You have to do the plus equals 1
or plus/minus or minus equals 1

13:25:16.732 --> 13:25:20.242
to achieve that same result. All
right, how about in Python 2?

13:25:20.242 --> 13:25:22.882
Here in Scratch, recall,
was a conditional,

13:25:22.882 --> 13:25:26.512
asking a silly question like is x less
than y, and if so, just say as much.

13:25:26.512 --> 13:25:30.502
In C, that looked a little
something like this, printf and if

13:25:30.502 --> 13:25:33.832
with the parentheses, the curly
braces, the semicolon, and all of that.

13:25:33.832 --> 13:25:37.132
In Python, this is going to get a
little more pleasant to type, too.

13:25:37.132 --> 13:25:39.842
It's going to be just this.

13:25:39.842 --> 13:25:42.982
And if someone wants to call out
some of the obvious changes here,

13:25:42.982 --> 13:25:46.887
what has been simplified now in Python
for a conditional, it would seem?

13:25:46.887 --> 13:25:48.262
Yeah, what's missing, or changed?

13:25:48.262 --> 13:25:48.872
AUDIENCE: Braces.

13:25:48.872 --> 13:25:49.927
DAVID J. MALAN: So no curly braces.

13:25:49.927 --> 13:25:51.262
AUDIENCE: Colon is back.

13:25:51.262 --> 13:25:51.892
DAVID J. MALAN: I'm sorry?

13:25:51.892 --> 13:25:53.032
AUDIENCE: Using the colon instead.

13:25:53.032 --> 13:25:55.115
DAVID J. MALAN: And we're
using the colon instead.

13:25:55.115 --> 13:25:57.142
So I got rid of the
curly braces in Python.

13:25:57.142 --> 13:25:58.715
But I'm using a colon instead.

13:25:58.715 --> 13:26:00.632
And even though this is
a single line of code,

13:26:00.632 --> 13:26:04.972
so long as you indent subsequent
lines along with the printf,

13:26:04.972 --> 13:26:09.352
that's going to imply that everything,
if the if condition is true,

13:26:09.352 --> 13:26:13.492
should be executed below it, until you
start to un-indent and start writing

13:26:13.492 --> 13:26:14.992
a different line of code altogether.

13:26:14.992 --> 13:26:17.522
So indentation in Python is important.

13:26:17.522 --> 13:26:21.622
So this is among the reasons
we've emphasized axes like style,

13:26:21.622 --> 13:26:23.362
just how well styled your code is.

13:26:23.362 --> 13:26:25.882
And honestly, we've seen,
certainly, in office hours,

13:26:25.882 --> 13:26:28.522
and you've seen in your own code,
sort of a tendency sometimes

13:26:28.522 --> 13:26:31.552
to be a little lax when it
comes to indentation, right?

13:26:31.552 --> 13:26:34.192
If you're one of those folks
who likes to indent everything

13:26:34.192 --> 13:26:37.732
on the left hand side of the window,
yeah, it might compile and run.

13:26:37.732 --> 13:26:41.392
But it's not particularly
readable by you or anyone else.

13:26:41.392 --> 13:26:45.112
Python actually addresses this
by just requiring indentation,

13:26:45.112 --> 13:26:46.312
when logically needed.

13:26:46.312 --> 13:26:50.572
So Python is going to force you to start
inventing properly now, if that's been,

13:26:50.572 --> 13:26:53.202
perhaps, a tendency otherwise.

13:26:53.202 --> 13:26:54.142
What else is missing?

13:26:54.142 --> 13:26:55.572
Well, we have no semicolon here.

13:26:55.572 --> 13:26:57.672
Of course, it's print instead of printf.

13:26:57.672 --> 13:27:00.342
But otherwise, those seem to
be the primary differences.

13:27:00.342 --> 13:27:02.202
What about something larger in Scratch?

13:27:02.202 --> 13:27:05.334
If an if-else block, like
this, you can perhaps

13:27:05.334 --> 13:27:06.792
guess what it's going to look like.

13:27:06.792 --> 13:27:10.062
In C it looks like this, curly
braces semicolons, and so forth.

13:27:10.062 --> 13:27:14.052
In Python, it's going to now
look like this, almost the same,

13:27:14.052 --> 13:27:15.342
but indentation is important.

13:27:15.342 --> 13:27:16.482
The colons are important.

13:27:16.482 --> 13:27:19.332
And there's one other difference
that's now again visible here,

13:27:19.332 --> 13:27:21.192
but we didn't call it out a second ago.

13:27:21.192 --> 13:27:24.282
What else is different in Python
versus C for these conditionals?

13:27:24.282 --> 13:27:24.993
Yeah.

13:27:24.993 --> 13:27:27.642
AUDIENCE: You don't have any
parentheses around the condition.

13:27:27.642 --> 13:27:28.222
DAVID J. MALAN: Perfect.

13:27:28.222 --> 13:27:30.612
We don't have any parentheses
around the condition,

13:27:30.612 --> 13:27:32.232
the Boolean expression itself.

13:27:32.232 --> 13:27:33.089
And why not?

13:27:33.089 --> 13:27:34.422
Well, it's just simpler to type.

13:27:34.422 --> 13:27:35.472
It's less to type.

13:27:35.472 --> 13:27:36.972
You can still use parentheses.

13:27:36.972 --> 13:27:39.072
And, in fact, you might
want to or need to,

13:27:39.072 --> 13:27:43.992
if you want to combine thoughts and
do this and that, or this or that.

13:27:43.992 --> 13:27:47.442
But by default, you no longer need
or should have those parentheses.

13:27:47.442 --> 13:27:48.672
Just say what you mean.

13:27:48.672 --> 13:27:50.962
Lastly, with conditionals,
we had something like this,

13:27:50.962 --> 13:27:53.292
an if else if else statement.

13:27:53.292 --> 13:27:55.362
In C, it looked a little
something like this.

13:27:55.362 --> 13:27:57.402
In Python, it's going to
get really tighter now.

13:27:57.402 --> 13:28:02.352
It's just if, and this is the
curiosity, elif x greater than y.

13:28:02.352 --> 13:28:07.632
So it's not else if, it's literally
one keyword, elif, and the colons

13:28:07.632 --> 13:28:09.837
remain now on each of the three lines.

13:28:09.837 --> 13:28:11.212
But the indentation is important.

13:28:11.212 --> 13:28:13.002
And if we did want to
do multiple things,

13:28:13.002 --> 13:28:16.760
we could just indent below each
of these conditionals, as well.

13:28:16.760 --> 13:28:18.552
All right, let me pause
there first, to see

13:28:18.552 --> 13:28:21.012
if there's any questions on
these syntactic differences.

13:28:21.012 --> 13:28:21.769
Yeah.

13:28:21.769 --> 13:28:24.054
AUDIENCE: My thought is
maybe like, it's good,

13:28:24.054 --> 13:28:27.682
though, does it matter if there's
this in between thing like that, but

13:28:27.682 --> 13:28:28.692
and why.

13:28:28.692 --> 13:28:31.572
DAVID J. MALAN: In between,
between what and what?

13:28:31.572 --> 13:28:34.942
AUDIENCE: So like the left-hand
side and like the right side spaces?

13:28:34.942 --> 13:28:38.352
DAVID J. MALAN: Ah, good
question, is Python sensitive

13:28:38.352 --> 13:28:40.272
to spaces and where they go?

13:28:40.272 --> 13:28:42.912
Sometimes no, sometimes
yes, is the short answer.

13:28:42.912 --> 13:28:46.602
Stylistically, though, you should be
practicing what we're preaching here,

13:28:46.602 --> 13:28:50.787
whereby you do have spaces to the
left and right of binary operators,

13:28:50.787 --> 13:28:52.662
that they're called,
something like less than

13:28:52.662 --> 13:28:54.870
or greater than is a binary
operator, because there's

13:28:54.870 --> 13:28:57.102
two operands to the left
and to the right of them.

13:28:57.102 --> 13:29:00.162
And in fact, in Python,
more so than the world of C,

13:29:00.162 --> 13:29:02.862
there's actually formal
style conventions.

13:29:02.862 --> 13:29:07.209
Not only within CS50 have we had a
style guide on the course's website,

13:29:07.209 --> 13:29:10.542
for instance, that just dictates how you
should write your code so that it looks

13:29:10.542 --> 13:29:11.467
like everyone else's.

13:29:11.467 --> 13:29:13.842
In the Python community, they
take this one step further,

13:29:13.842 --> 13:29:17.782
and there's an actual standard whereby
you don't have to adhere to it,

13:29:17.782 --> 13:29:20.832
but generally speaking, in the real
world, someone would reprimand you,

13:29:20.832 --> 13:29:23.622
would reject your code, if you're trying
to contribute it to another project,

13:29:23.622 --> 13:29:25.252
if you don't adhere to these standards.

13:29:25.252 --> 13:29:28.212
So while you could be lax
with some of this white space,

13:29:28.212 --> 13:29:29.382
do make things readable.

13:29:29.382 --> 13:29:33.297
And that's Python theme, for the
code to be as readable as possible.

13:29:33.297 --> 13:29:35.922
All right, so let's take a look
at a couple of other constructs

13:29:35.922 --> 13:29:37.882
before transitioning
to some actual code.

13:29:37.882 --> 13:29:40.632
This, of course, in Scratch
was a loop, meowing forever.

13:29:40.632 --> 13:29:44.862
In C, the closest we could get was
doing something while true, because true

13:29:44.862 --> 13:29:45.622
never changes.

13:29:45.622 --> 13:29:48.582
So it's sort of a simple way
of just saying do this forever.

13:29:48.582 --> 13:29:51.462
In Python, it's pretty
much the same thing,

13:29:51.462 --> 13:29:53.262
but a couple of small differences here.

13:29:53.262 --> 13:29:55.122
The parentheses are gone.

13:29:55.122 --> 13:29:56.120
The colon is there.

13:29:56.120 --> 13:29:57.162
The indentation is there.

13:29:57.162 --> 13:30:00.785
No semicolon, and there's
one other subtle difference.

13:30:00.785 --> 13:30:01.452
What do you see?

13:30:01.452 --> 13:30:02.442
AUDIENCE: True is capitalized?

13:30:02.442 --> 13:30:04.525
DAVID J. MALAN: True is
capitalized, just because.

13:30:04.525 --> 13:30:07.092
Both true and false are
Boolean values in Python.

13:30:07.092 --> 13:30:09.672
But you've got to start
capitalizing them, just because.

13:30:09.672 --> 13:30:11.562
All right, how about a
loop like this, where

13:30:11.562 --> 13:30:14.982
you repeat something a finite number
of times, like meowing three times.

13:30:14.982 --> 13:30:17.572
In C, we could do this
a few different ways.

13:30:17.572 --> 13:30:21.312
There's this very mechanical way,
where you initialize a variable like i

13:30:21.312 --> 13:30:22.092
to zero.

13:30:22.092 --> 13:30:25.872
You then use a while loop and
check if i is less than 3,

13:30:25.872 --> 13:30:27.709
the total number of
times you want to meow.

13:30:27.709 --> 13:30:29.292
Then you print what you want to print.

13:30:29.292 --> 13:30:32.892
You increment i using this syntax,
or the longer, more verbose syntax,

13:30:32.892 --> 13:30:34.402
with plus equals or whatnot.

13:30:34.402 --> 13:30:36.732
And then you do it again
and again and again.

13:30:36.732 --> 13:30:40.692
In Python, you can do it
functionally the same way, same idea,

13:30:40.692 --> 13:30:42.102
slightly different syntax.

13:30:42.102 --> 13:30:44.712
You just don't bother saying
what type of variable you want.

13:30:44.712 --> 13:30:47.560
Python will infer from the fact
that there's a 0 right there.

13:30:47.560 --> 13:30:48.852
You don't need the parentheses.

13:30:48.852 --> 13:30:49.782
You do need the colon.

13:30:49.782 --> 13:30:51.282
You do need the indentation.

13:30:51.282 --> 13:30:54.432
You can't do the i plus plus, but
you can do this other technique,

13:30:54.432 --> 13:30:56.622
as we could have done in C, as well.

13:30:56.622 --> 13:30:58.842
How else might we do this, though, too?

13:30:58.842 --> 13:31:01.062
Well. it turns out in
C, we could do something

13:31:01.062 --> 13:31:04.752
like this, which, again, sort
of cryptic at first glance,

13:31:04.752 --> 13:31:07.692
became perhaps more familiar,
where you have initialization,

13:31:07.692 --> 13:31:11.442
a conditional, and then an update
that you do after each iteration.

13:31:11.442 --> 13:31:14.472
In Python, there isn't really an analog.

13:31:14.472 --> 13:31:17.022
There is no analog in
Python, where you have

13:31:17.022 --> 13:31:19.902
the parentheses and the multiple
semicolons in the same line.

13:31:19.902 --> 13:31:23.532
Instead, there is a for loop, but
it's meant to read a little more

13:31:23.532 --> 13:31:27.072
like English, for i in 0, 1, and 2.

13:31:27.072 --> 13:31:31.302
So we'll see in a bit, these square
brackets represent an array, now

13:31:31.302 --> 13:31:33.612
to be called a list in Python.

13:31:33.612 --> 13:31:37.812
So lists in Python are more like
link lists than they are arrays.

13:31:37.812 --> 13:31:38.902
More on that soon.

13:31:38.902 --> 13:31:42.732
So this just means for i and the
following list of three values.

13:31:42.732 --> 13:31:46.342
And on each iteration of this loop,
Python automatically, for you,

13:31:46.342 --> 13:31:47.772
it first sets i to zero.

13:31:47.772 --> 13:31:49.362
Then it sets i to one.

13:31:49.362 --> 13:31:54.402
Then it sets i to two, so that you
effectively do things three times.

13:31:54.402 --> 13:31:57.972
But this doesn't necessarily scale,
as I've drawn it on the board.

13:31:57.972 --> 13:32:01.662
Suppose you took this
at face value as the way

13:32:01.662 --> 13:32:05.502
you iterate some number of times
in Python, using a for loop.

13:32:05.502 --> 13:32:10.004
At what point does this approach
perhaps get bad, or bad design?

13:32:10.004 --> 13:32:11.712
Let me give folks just
a moment to think.

13:32:11.712 --> 13:32:12.937
Yeah, in back.

13:32:12.937 --> 13:32:15.604
AUDIENCE: If you don't know how
many times, last time, you know,

13:32:15.604 --> 13:32:17.605
you've got the link in there.

13:32:17.605 --> 13:32:20.022
DAVID J. MALAN: Sure, if you
don't know how many times you

13:32:20.022 --> 13:32:23.982
want to loop or iterate, you can't
really create a hard-coded list

13:32:23.982 --> 13:32:25.272
like that, of 0, 1, 2.

13:32:25.272 --> 13:32:26.845
Other thoughts?

13:32:26.845 --> 13:32:29.512
AUDIENCE: So you want to say raise
a large number of allowances.

13:32:29.512 --> 13:32:32.262
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, if you're
iterating a large number of times,

13:32:32.262 --> 13:32:34.162
this list is going to
get longer and longer,

13:32:34.162 --> 13:32:36.454
and you're just kind of
stupidly going to be typing out

13:32:36.454 --> 13:32:40.182
like comma 3, comma 4, comma 5, comma
dot dot dot, comma 99, comma 100.

13:32:40.182 --> 13:32:42.682
I mean, your code would start
to look atrocious, eventually.

13:32:42.682 --> 13:32:44.032
So there is a better way.

13:32:44.032 --> 13:32:46.881
In Python, there is a function,
or technically a type,

13:32:46.881 --> 13:32:51.052
called range, that essentially magically
gives you back a range of values

13:32:51.052 --> 13:32:54.121
from 0 on up to, but
not through a value.

13:32:54.121 --> 13:32:58.131
So the effect of this line of
code, for i in the following range,

13:32:58.131 --> 13:33:01.006
essentially hands you back
a list of three values,

13:33:01.006 --> 13:33:02.881
thereby letting you do
something three times.

13:33:02.881 --> 13:33:05.589
And if you want to do something
99 times instead, you, of course,

13:33:05.589 --> 13:33:07.097
just change the 3 to a 99.

13:33:07.097 --> 13:33:07.597
Question.

13:33:07.597 --> 13:33:11.612
AUDIENCE: Is there a way to start
the beginning point of that range

13:33:11.612 --> 13:33:15.932
at a number or an integer that's higher
than zero, or is there never a really

13:33:15.932 --> 13:33:16.982
any point to do so?

13:33:16.982 --> 13:33:18.062
DAVID J. MALAN: A really
good question, can

13:33:18.062 --> 13:33:19.962
you start counting at a higher number.

13:33:19.962 --> 13:33:23.432
So not 0, which is the implied default,
but something larger than that.

13:33:23.432 --> 13:33:28.082
Yes, so it turns out the range function
takes multiple arguments, not just one

13:33:28.082 --> 13:33:31.520
but maybe two or even three, that
allows you to customize this behavior.

13:33:31.520 --> 13:33:33.062
So you can customize where it begins.

13:33:33.062 --> 13:33:34.442
You can customize the increment.

13:33:34.442 --> 13:33:36.234
By default, it's one,
but if you want to do

13:33:36.234 --> 13:33:39.104
every two values, for like evens
or odds, you could do that as well,

13:33:39.104 --> 13:33:40.062
and a few other things.

13:33:40.062 --> 13:33:42.452
And before long, we'll take a
look at some Python documentation

13:33:42.452 --> 13:33:45.332
that will become your authoritative
source for answers like that.

13:33:45.332 --> 13:33:47.312
Like, what can this function do.

13:33:47.312 --> 13:33:51.542
Other questions on this thus far?

13:33:51.542 --> 13:33:56.502
Seeing none, so what else might
we compare and contrast here.

13:33:56.502 --> 13:34:00.842
Well, in the world of C, recall that
we had a whole bunch of built-in data

13:34:00.842 --> 13:34:04.832
types, like these here, Bool and char
and double and float, and so forth,

13:34:04.832 --> 13:34:08.192
string, which happened to
come from the CS50 library.

13:34:08.192 --> 13:34:12.512
But the language C itself certainly
understood the idea of strings,

13:34:12.512 --> 13:34:17.222
because the backslash 0, the support
for %S and printf, that's all native,

13:34:17.222 --> 13:34:19.892
built into C, not a CS50 simplification.

13:34:19.892 --> 13:34:22.142
All we did, and revealed,
as of a couple of weeks

13:34:22.142 --> 13:34:24.572
ago, is that string,
this data type, is just

13:34:24.572 --> 13:34:29.252
a synonym for a typedef for char star,
which is part of the language natively.

13:34:29.252 --> 13:34:32.132
In Python now, this list actually
gets a little shorter, at least

13:34:32.132 --> 13:34:33.965
for these common primitive data types.

13:34:33.965 --> 13:34:36.632
Still going to have bulls, we're
going to have floats, and Ints,

13:34:36.632 --> 13:34:39.122
and we're going to have strings,
but we're going to call them STRs.

13:34:39.122 --> 13:34:41.282
And this is not a CS50
thing from the library,

13:34:41.282 --> 13:34:44.822
STR, S-T-R, is, in fact,
a data type in Python,

13:34:44.822 --> 13:34:48.782
that's going to do a lot more than
strings did for us automatically in C.

13:34:48.782 --> 13:34:53.655
Ints and floats, meanwhile, don't need
the corresponding longs and doubles,

13:34:53.655 --> 13:34:56.072
because, in fact, among the
problems Python solves for us,

13:34:56.072 --> 13:34:58.862
too, Ints can get as big as you want.

13:34:58.862 --> 13:35:01.742
Integer overflow is no
longer going to be an issue.

13:35:01.742 --> 13:35:04.472
Per week 1, the language
solves that for us.

13:35:04.472 --> 13:35:06.312
Floating point
imprecision, unfortunately,

13:35:06.312 --> 13:35:07.712
is still a problem that remains.

13:35:07.712 --> 13:35:11.252
But there are libraries, code that
other people have written, as we briefly

13:35:11.252 --> 13:35:13.532
discussed in weeks past,
that allow you to do

13:35:13.532 --> 13:35:16.772
scientific or financial computing,
using libraries that build

13:35:16.772 --> 13:35:19.147
on top of these data types, as well.

13:35:19.147 --> 13:35:22.022
So there's other data types, too,
in Python, which we'll see actually

13:35:22.022 --> 13:35:25.232
gives us a whole bunch of
more power and capability,

13:35:25.232 --> 13:35:28.022
things called ranges,
like we just saw, lists,

13:35:28.022 --> 13:35:30.602
like I called out verbally,
with the square brackets,

13:35:30.602 --> 13:35:33.422
things called tuples, for
things like x comma y,

13:35:33.422 --> 13:35:36.827
or latitude, longitude,
dictionaries, or Dicts,

13:35:36.827 --> 13:35:40.262
which allow you to store keys and
values, much like our hash tables

13:35:40.262 --> 13:35:43.495
from last time, and then sets in the
mathematical sense, where they filter

13:35:43.495 --> 13:35:46.412
out duplicates for you, and you can
just put a whole bunch of numbers,

13:35:46.412 --> 13:35:50.432
a whole bunch of words or whatnot,
and the language, via this data type,

13:35:50.432 --> 13:35:52.922
will filter out duplicates for you.

13:35:52.922 --> 13:35:56.507
Now there's going to be a few functions
we give you this week and beyond,

13:35:56.507 --> 13:35:59.132
training wheels that we're then
going to very quickly take off,

13:35:59.132 --> 13:36:02.582
just because, as we'll see today, they
just simplify the process of getting

13:36:02.582 --> 13:36:05.727
user input correctly, without
accidentally writing buggy code,

13:36:05.727 --> 13:36:08.852
just when you're trying to get Hello,
World, or something similar, to work.

13:36:08.852 --> 13:36:12.572
And we'll give you functions, not
like, not as long as this list in C,

13:36:12.572 --> 13:36:15.152
but a subset of these,
get float, get Int,

13:36:15.152 --> 13:36:18.182
and get string, that'll
automate the process of getting

13:36:18.182 --> 13:36:21.932
user input in a way that's more
resilient against potential bugs.

13:36:21.932 --> 13:36:23.792
But we'll see what those bugs might be.

13:36:23.792 --> 13:36:26.642
And the way we're going to do
this is similar in spirit to C.

13:36:26.642 --> 13:36:30.902
Instead of doing include,
CS50.h, like we did in C,

13:36:30.902 --> 13:36:33.812
you're going to now
start saying import CS50.

13:36:33.812 --> 13:36:37.082
Python supports,
similar to C, libraries,

13:36:37.082 --> 13:36:38.822
but there aren't header files anymore.

13:36:38.822 --> 13:36:41.612
You just use the name of
the library in Python.

13:36:41.612 --> 13:36:44.972
And if you want to import CS50's
functions, you just say import CS50.

13:36:44.972 --> 13:36:48.992
Or, if you want to be more precise, and
not just import the whole thing, which

13:36:48.992 --> 13:36:52.382
could be slow, if you've got a really
big library with a lot of functionality

13:36:52.382 --> 13:36:56.252
in it, you can be more precise and
say from CS50, import get float.

13:36:56.252 --> 13:37:00.002
From CS50 import get Int,
from CSM 50 import get string,

13:37:00.002 --> 13:37:02.792
or you can just separate
them by commas and import 3

13:37:02.792 --> 13:37:07.072
and only 3 things from a
particular library, like ours.

13:37:07.072 --> 13:37:08.822
But starting today and
onward, we're going

13:37:08.822 --> 13:37:11.972
to start making much more
heavy use of libraries, code

13:37:11.972 --> 13:37:15.092
that other people wrote, so that
we're no longer reinventing the wheel.

13:37:15.092 --> 13:37:18.397
We're not making our own linked lists,
our own trees, our own dictionaries.

13:37:18.397 --> 13:37:20.772
We're going to start standing
on the shoulders of others,

13:37:20.772 --> 13:37:23.642
so that you can get real work
done, so to speak, faster,

13:37:23.642 --> 13:37:28.232
by building your software on
top of others' code as well.

13:37:28.232 --> 13:37:31.632
All right, so that's it for the
syntactic tour of the language,

13:37:31.632 --> 13:37:32.882
and the sort of core features.

13:37:32.882 --> 13:37:34.842
Soon we'll transition
to application thereof.

13:37:34.842 --> 13:37:40.562
But let me pause here to see if there's
any questions on syntax or primitives

13:37:40.562 --> 13:37:46.862
or otherwise, or otherwise.

13:37:46.862 --> 13:37:48.726
Oh, yes, in back.

13:37:48.726 --> 13:37:52.685
AUDIENCE: Why don't Python
have the increment operators.

13:37:52.685 --> 13:37:54.852
DAVID J. MALAN: I'm sorry,
say it again, why doesn't

13:37:54.852 --> 13:37:56.310
Python have what kind of operators?

13:37:56.310 --> 13:37:59.100
AUDIENCE: Why doesn't Python
have the increment operator?

13:37:59.100 --> 13:38:02.142
DAVID J. MALAN: Sorry, someone coughed
when you said something operators.

13:38:02.142 --> 13:38:03.470
AUDIENCE: The increment.

13:38:03.470 --> 13:38:05.262
DAVID J. MALAN: Oh,
the increment operator?

13:38:05.262 --> 13:38:06.929
I'd have to check the history, honestly.

13:38:06.929 --> 13:38:09.432
Python has tended to be a
fairly minimus language.

13:38:09.432 --> 13:38:12.612
And if you can do something one
way, the community, arguably,

13:38:12.612 --> 13:38:16.667
has tended to not give you multiple
ways to do the same thing syntactically.

13:38:16.667 --> 13:38:18.042
There's probably a better answer.

13:38:18.042 --> 13:38:22.362
And I'll see if I can dig in and post
something online, to follow up on that.

13:38:22.362 --> 13:38:26.392
All right, so before we transition
to now writing some actual code,

13:38:26.392 --> 13:38:31.392
let me go ahead and consider exactly
how we're going to write code.

13:38:31.392 --> 13:38:35.292
In the world of C, recall that it's
generally been a 2-step process.

13:38:35.292 --> 13:38:40.752
We create a file called like Hello.c,
and then, step one, make Hello, step 2,

13:38:40.752 --> 13:38:41.922
./Hello.

13:38:41.922 --> 13:38:44.652
Or, if you think back to week
two, when we sort of peeled back

13:38:44.652 --> 13:38:47.622
the layer of what Hello,
of what make was doing,

13:38:47.622 --> 13:38:50.832
you could more verbosely type out
the name of the actual compiler,

13:38:50.832 --> 13:38:54.162
Clang in our case, command line
arguments like dash Oh, Hello,

13:38:54.162 --> 13:38:56.362
to specify what name you want to create.

13:38:56.362 --> 13:38:58.182
And then you can specify the file name.

13:38:58.182 --> 13:39:01.572
And then you can specify what
libraries you want to link in.

13:39:01.572 --> 13:39:03.072
So that was a very verbose approach.

13:39:03.072 --> 13:39:05.452
But it was always a two-step approach.

13:39:05.452 --> 13:39:08.202
And so, even as you've been
doing recent problem sets,

13:39:08.202 --> 13:39:11.922
odds are you've realized that, any time
you want to make a change to your code,

13:39:11.922 --> 13:39:16.182
or make a change to your code
and try and test your code again,

13:39:16.182 --> 13:39:18.882
you're constantly doing those two steps.

13:39:18.882 --> 13:39:22.362
Moving forward in Python,
it's going to become simpler,

13:39:22.362 --> 13:39:24.132
and it's going to be just this.

13:39:24.132 --> 13:39:26.982
The file name is going to change,
but that might go without saying.

13:39:26.982 --> 13:39:31.782
It's going to be something like
Hello.py, P-Y, instead of Hello.c.

13:39:31.782 --> 13:39:34.512
And that's just a convention,
using a different file extension.

13:39:34.512 --> 13:39:37.302
But there's no compilation step per se.

13:39:37.302 --> 13:39:40.692
You jump right to the
execution of your code.

13:39:40.692 --> 13:39:43.722
And so Python, it turns out, is
the name, not only of the language

13:39:43.722 --> 13:39:48.672
we're going to start using, it's also
the name of a program on a Mac, a PC,

13:39:48.672 --> 13:39:52.542
assuming it's been pre-installed,
that interprets the language for you.

13:39:52.542 --> 13:39:56.622
This is to say that Python is generally
described as being interpreted,

13:39:56.622 --> 13:39:57.882
not compiled.

13:39:57.882 --> 13:40:01.692
And by that, I mean you get to skip,
from the programmer's perspective,

13:40:01.692 --> 13:40:02.892
that compilation step.

13:40:02.892 --> 13:40:07.392
There is no manual step in the world of
Python, typically, of writing your code

13:40:07.392 --> 13:40:11.052
and then compiling it to zeros and ones,
and then running the zeros and ones.

13:40:11.052 --> 13:40:13.392
Instead, these kind of
two steps get collapsed

13:40:13.392 --> 13:40:19.092
into the illusion of one, whereby you,
instead, are able to just run the code,

13:40:19.092 --> 13:40:22.722
and let the computer figure
out how to actually convert it

13:40:22.722 --> 13:40:24.762
to something the computer understands.

13:40:24.762 --> 13:40:28.372
And the way we do that is via this
old process, input and output.

13:40:28.372 --> 13:40:30.432
But now, when you have
source code, it's going

13:40:30.432 --> 13:40:33.372
to be passed into an
interpreter, not a compiler.

13:40:33.372 --> 13:40:35.922
And the best analog of this
is just to perhaps point out

13:40:35.922 --> 13:40:38.472
that, in the human world, if
you speak, or don't speak,

13:40:38.472 --> 13:40:42.162
multiple human languages, it can
be a pretty slow process from going

13:40:42.162 --> 13:40:43.792
from one language to another.

13:40:43.792 --> 13:40:46.692
For instance, here are step-by-step
instructions for finding someone

13:40:46.692 --> 13:40:49.062
in a phone book,
unfortunately, in Spanish.

13:40:49.062 --> 13:40:51.882
Unfortunately, if you don't
speak or read Spanish.

13:40:51.882 --> 13:40:53.082
You could figure this out.

13:40:53.082 --> 13:40:55.902
You could run this algorithm, but you're
going to have to do some googling,

13:40:55.902 --> 13:40:58.652
or you're going to have to open
up literal dictionary from Spanish

13:40:58.652 --> 13:40:59.982
to English and convert this.

13:40:59.982 --> 13:41:03.582
And the catch with translating
any language, human or computer

13:41:03.582 --> 13:41:07.372
or otherwise, is that you're going
to pay a price, typically some time.

13:41:07.372 --> 13:41:10.362
And so converting this in
Spanish to this in English

13:41:10.362 --> 13:41:12.882
is just going to take you
longer than if this were already

13:41:12.882 --> 13:41:14.975
in your native language.

13:41:14.975 --> 13:41:17.892
And that's going to be one of the
subtleties with the world of Python.

13:41:17.892 --> 13:41:21.702
Yes, it's a feature that you can
just run the code without having

13:41:21.702 --> 13:41:24.402
to bother compiling it manually first.

13:41:24.402 --> 13:41:25.572
But we might pay a price.

13:41:25.572 --> 13:41:27.337
And things might be a little slower.

13:41:27.337 --> 13:41:28.962
Now, there's ways to chip away at that.

