13:50:43.262 --> 13:50:46.882
So this is a little similar to fopen,
a function in C you might recall.

13:50:46.882 --> 13:50:49.402
Then let me iterate over
every line in the file.

13:50:49.402 --> 13:50:54.322
In Python, this is pretty pleasant,
for line in file colon indent.

13:50:54.322 --> 13:50:59.032
How, now, do I get at the current
word, and then strip off the new line,

13:50:59.032 --> 13:51:02.092
because in this file of
words, 140,000 words,

13:51:02.092 --> 13:51:05.274
there's word backslash n,
word backslash n, all right?

13:51:05.274 --> 13:51:07.732
Well, let me go ahead and get
a word from the current line,

13:51:07.732 --> 13:51:11.362
but strip off, from the right end
of the string, the new line, which

13:51:11.362 --> 13:51:14.062
the Rstrip function
in Python does for me.

13:51:14.062 --> 13:51:18.892
Then let me go ahead and add to my
dictionary, or hash table, that word,

13:51:18.892 --> 13:51:19.552
done.

13:51:19.552 --> 13:51:22.057
Let me go ahead and close
the file for good measure.

13:51:22.057 --> 13:51:24.682
And then let me go ahead and
return true, because all was well.

13:51:24.682 --> 13:51:26.842
That's it for the load
function in Python.

13:51:26.842 --> 13:51:28.102
How about the size function?

13:51:28.102 --> 13:51:31.342
This did not take any arguments, it
just returns the size of the hash table

13:51:31.342 --> 13:51:32.512
or dictionary in Python.

13:51:32.512 --> 13:51:36.502
I can do that by returning the
length of the dictionary in question.

13:51:36.502 --> 13:51:41.182
And then lastly, gone from the
world of Python is malloc and free.

13:51:41.182 --> 13:51:42.612
Memory is managed for you.

13:51:42.612 --> 13:51:45.472
So no matter what I do,
there's nothing to unload.

13:51:45.472 --> 13:51:47.342
The computer will do that for me.

13:51:47.342 --> 13:51:51.382
So I give you, in these functions,
problem set five in Python.

13:51:51.382 --> 13:51:53.542
So, I'm sorry, we made
you write it in C first.

13:51:53.542 --> 13:51:57.142
But the implication now is that,
what are you getting for free,

13:51:57.142 --> 13:51:58.372
in a language like Python?

13:51:58.372 --> 13:52:00.892
Well, encapsulated in
this one line of code

13:52:00.892 --> 13:52:04.792
is much of what you wrote for
problem set five, implementing

13:52:04.792 --> 13:52:07.792
your array for all of your
letters of the alphabet or more,

13:52:07.792 --> 13:52:10.912
all of the linked lists that you
implemented to create chains,

13:52:10.912 --> 13:52:12.452
to store all of those words.

13:52:12.452 --> 13:52:13.582
All of that is happening.

13:52:13.582 --> 13:52:16.612
It's just someone else in the
world wrote that code for you.

13:52:16.612 --> 13:52:19.582
And you can now use it
by way of a dictionary.

13:52:19.582 --> 13:52:22.072
And actually, I can
change this a little bit,

13:52:22.072 --> 13:52:25.192
because add is technically not
