13:54:56.202 --> 13:54:59.982
is better for the programmer,
because it was way faster to write,

13:54:59.982 --> 13:55:02.982
but C is maybe better for the computer,
because it's much faster to run.

13:55:02.982 --> 13:55:04.649
I think that's a reasonable formulation.

13:55:04.649 --> 13:55:05.952
Other opinions?

13:55:05.952 --> 13:55:07.110
Yeah.

13:55:07.110 --> 13:55:09.402
AUDIENCE: I think it depends
on the size of the project

13:55:09.402 --> 13:55:10.432
that you're dealing with.

13:55:10.432 --> 13:55:12.807
So if it's going to be something
that's relatively quick,

13:55:12.807 --> 13:55:15.232
I might not care that it
takes 10 seconds to do it.

13:55:15.232 --> 13:55:17.432
And it could be way faster
to do it with Python.

13:55:17.432 --> 13:55:20.592
Whereas with C, if I'm dealing
with something like a massive data

13:55:20.592 --> 13:55:24.822
set or something huge, then that
time is going to really build up on,

13:55:24.822 --> 13:55:29.262
it might be worth it to put in the
upfront effort and just load it into C,

13:55:29.262 --> 13:55:32.782
so the process continually will run
faster over a longer period of time.

13:55:32.782 --> 13:55:33.952
DAVID J. MALAN: Absolutely,
a really good answer.

13:55:33.952 --> 13:55:36.822
And let me summarize, is it depends
on the workload, if you will.

13:55:36.822 --> 13:55:40.572
If you have a very large
data set, you might

13:55:40.572 --> 13:55:43.650
want to optimize your code to be as
fast and performant as it can be,

13:55:43.650 --> 13:55:45.942
especially if you're running
that code again and again.

13:55:45.942 --> 13:55:47.472
Maybe you're a company like Google.

13:55:47.472 --> 13:55:49.632
People are searching a
huge database all the time.

13:55:49.632 --> 13:55:52.272
You really want to squeeze
every bit of performance

13:55:52.272 --> 13:55:53.744
as you can out of the computer.

13:55:53.744 --> 13:55:56.202
You might want to have someone
smart take a language like C

13:55:56.202 --> 13:55:57.972
and write it at a very low level.

13:55:57.972 --> 13:55:59.022
It's going to be painful.

13:55:59.022 --> 13:55:59.922
They're going to have bugs.

13:55:59.922 --> 13:56:02.672
They're going to have to deal with
memory management and the like.

13:56:02.672 --> 13:56:06.012
But if and when it works correctly, it's
going to be much faster, it would seem.

13:56:06.012 --> 13:56:08.802
By contrast, if you have
a data set that's big,

13:56:08.802 --> 13:56:12.342
and 140,000 words is
not small, but you don't

13:56:12.342 --> 13:56:15.462
want to spend like 5 hours,
10 hours, a week of your time,

13:56:15.462 --> 13:56:17.585
building a spell
checker or a dictionary,

13:56:17.585 --> 13:56:20.502
you can instead leverage a different
language with different libraries

13:56:20.502 --> 13:56:25.212
and build on top of it, in order to
prioritize the human time instead.

13:56:25.212 --> 13:56:27.363
Other thoughts?

13:56:27.363 --> 13:56:29.311
AUDIENCE: Would you,
because with Python,

13:56:29.311 --> 13:56:33.450
doesn't it also like
convert the words, or like

13:56:33.450 --> 13:56:35.061
convert the words, for a lesson?

13:56:35.061 --> 13:56:37.103
When we convert that into
the same version again,

13:56:37.103 --> 13:56:40.670
do we just take that into view?

13:56:40.670 --> 13:56:43.462
DAVID J. MALAN: That's a perfect
segue to exactly the next point we

13:56:43.462 --> 13:56:45.862
wanted to make, which was, is
there something in between?

13:56:45.862 --> 13:56:46.882
And indeed there is.

13:56:46.882 --> 13:56:49.492
I'm oversimplifying what this
language is actually doing.

13:56:49.492 --> 13:56:51.802
It's not as stark a difference
as saying, like, hey,

13:56:51.802 --> 13:56:54.862
Python is four times slower than C.
Like that's not the right takeaway.

13:56:54.862 --> 13:56:57.982
There are absolutely ways that
engineers can optimize languages,

13:56:57.982 --> 13:56:59.752
as they have already done for Python.

13:56:59.752 --> 13:57:02.362
And in fact, I've configured
my settings in such a way

13:57:02.362 --> 13:57:05.299
that I've kind of dramatized
just how big the difference is.

13:57:05.299 --> 13:57:07.132
It is going to be slower,
Python, typically,

13:57:07.132 --> 13:57:08.452
than the equivalent C program.

13:57:08.452 --> 13:57:10.462
But it doesn't have
to be as big of a gap

13:57:10.462 --> 13:57:14.242
as it is here, because, indeed, among
the features you can turn on in Python

13:57:14.242 --> 13:57:16.642
is to save some intermediate results.

13:57:16.642 --> 13:57:19.882
Technically speaking, yes,
Python is interpreting

13:57:19.882 --> 13:57:23.212
Dictionary.py and these
other files, translating them

13:57:23.212 --> 13:57:24.725
from one language to another.

13:57:24.725 --> 13:57:27.892
But that doesn't mean it has to do that
every darn time you run the program.

13:57:27.892 --> 13:57:33.542
As you propose, you can save, or cache,
C-A-C-H-E, the results of that process.

13:57:33.542 --> 13:57:36.962
So that the second time and the third
time are actually notably faster.

13:57:36.962 --> 13:57:39.952
And, in fact, Python itself, the
interpreter, the most popular version

13:57:39.952 --> 13:57:42.502
thereof, itself is
actually implemented in C.

13:57:42.502 --> 13:57:45.812
So you can make sure that your
interpreter is as fast as possible.

13:57:45.812 --> 13:57:47.872
And what then is maybe
the high level takeaway?

13:57:47.872 --> 13:57:50.842
Yes, if you are going to try to
squeeze every bit of performance

13:57:50.842 --> 13:57:54.232
out of your code, and
maybe code is constrained.

13:57:54.232 --> 13:57:55.672
Maybe you have very small devices.

13:57:55.672 --> 13:57:57.292
Maybe it's like a watch nowadays.

13:57:57.292 --> 13:58:02.842
Or maybe it's a sensor that's installed
in some small format in an appliance,

13:58:02.842 --> 13:58:06.232
or in infrastructure, where you
don't have much battery life

13:58:06.232 --> 13:58:08.152
and you don't have much
size, you might want

13:58:08.152 --> 13:58:10.232
to minimize just how
much work is being done.

13:58:10.232 --> 13:58:13.265
And so the faster the code runs,
and the better it's going to be,

13:58:13.265 --> 13:58:14.932
if it's implemented something low level.

13:58:14.932 --> 13:58:18.832
So C is still very commonly used
for certain types of applications.

13:58:18.832 --> 13:58:22.102
But, again, if you just want
to solve real world problems,

13:58:22.102 --> 13:58:26.362
and get real work done, and your time
is just as, if not more, valuable

13:58:26.362 --> 13:58:28.522
than the device you're
running it on, long term,

13:58:28.522 --> 13:58:31.880
you know what, Python is among the
most popular languages as well.

13:58:31.880 --> 13:58:34.672
And frankly, if I were implementing
a spell checker moving forward,

13:58:34.672 --> 13:58:36.232
I'm probably starting with Python.

13:58:36.232 --> 13:58:38.065
And I'm not going to
waste time implementing

13:58:38.065 --> 13:58:41.452
all of that low-level stuff, because
the whole point of using newer,

13:58:41.452 --> 13:58:45.982
modern languages is to use abstractions
that other people have created for you.

13:58:45.982 --> 13:58:49.432
And by abstraction, I mean something
like the dictionary function,

13:58:49.432 --> 13:58:51.892
that just gives you a
dictionary, or hash table,

13:58:51.892 --> 13:58:55.747
or the equivalent version that I
used, which in this case was a set.

13:58:55.747 --> 13:58:59.242
All right, any questions,
then, on Python thus far?

13:59:02.252 --> 13:59:03.232
No, all right.

13:59:03.232 --> 13:59:04.232
Oh, yeah, in the middle.

13:59:04.232 --> 13:59:06.442
AUDIENCE: Could you
compile the Python code,

13:59:06.442 --> 13:59:11.132
or is there some, I'd imagine that
with the audience that can happen,

13:59:11.132 --> 13:59:14.702
but it feels like if you can just
come up with a Python compiler,

13:59:14.702 --> 13:59:16.615
that would give you the
best of both worlds.

13:59:16.615 --> 13:59:18.782
DAVID J. MALAN: Really good
question or observation,

13:59:18.782 --> 13:59:20.240
could you just compile Python code?

13:59:20.240 --> 13:59:23.702
Yes, absolutely, this idea of
compiling code or interpreting code

13:59:23.702 --> 13:59:26.012
is not native to the language itself.

13:59:26.012 --> 13:59:28.932
It tends to be native to the
conventions that we humans use.

13:59:28.932 --> 13:59:31.252
So you could actually
write an interpreter for C

13:59:31.252 --> 13:59:34.502
that would read it top to bottom, left
to right, converting it to, on the fly,

13:59:34.502 --> 13:59:38.162
something the computer understands, but
historically that's not been the case.

13:59:38.162 --> 13:59:40.082
C is generally a compiled language.

13:59:40.082 --> 13:59:41.192
But it doesn't have to be.

13:59:41.192 --> 13:59:44.532
What Python nowadays is actually
doing is what you described earlier.

13:59:44.532 --> 13:59:46.742
It technically is, sort
of unbeknownst to us,

13:59:46.742 --> 13:59:50.492
compiling the code, technically
not into 0's and 1's, technically

13:59:50.492 --> 13:59:54.032
into something called byte code,
which is this intermediate step that

13:59:54.032 --> 13:59:58.032
just doesn't take as much time as it
would to recompile the whole thing.

13:59:58.032 --> 14:00:00.899
And this is an area of research
for computer scientists working

14:00:00.899 --> 14:00:03.482
in programming languages, to
improve these kinds of paradigms.

14:00:03.482 --> 14:00:04.022
Why?

14:00:04.022 --> 14:00:07.262
Well, honestly, for you and I, the
programmer, it's just much easier to,

14:00:07.262 --> 14:00:10.322
one, run the code and not worry
about the stupid second step

14:00:10.322 --> 14:00:11.622
of compiling it all the time.

14:00:11.622 --> 14:00:12.122
Why?

14:00:12.122 --> 14:00:14.742
It's literally half as many
steps for me, the human.

14:00:14.742 --> 14:00:17.022
And that's a nice thing to optimize for.

14:00:17.022 --> 14:00:20.852
And ultimately, too, you might
want all of the fancy features that

14:00:20.852 --> 14:00:22.442
come with these other languages.

14:00:22.442 --> 14:00:24.482
So you should really
just be fine-tuning how

14:00:24.482 --> 14:00:28.322
you can enable these features, as
opposed to shying away from them here.

14:00:28.322 --> 14:00:31.112
And, in fact, the only time
I personally ever use C

14:00:31.112 --> 14:00:34.472
is from like September to October
of every year, during CS50.

14:00:34.472 --> 14:00:36.872
Almost every other month
do I reach for Python,

14:00:36.872 --> 14:00:40.212
or another language called JavaScript,
to actually get real work done,

14:00:40.212 --> 14:00:44.162
which is not to impugn C. It's just that
those other languages tend to be better

14:00:44.162 --> 14:00:47.552
fits for the amount of time I have to
allocate, and the types of problems

14:00:47.552 --> 14:00:48.427
that I want to solve.

