14:06:50.142 --> 14:06:52.932
As an aside, suppose I had
taken a different approach

14:06:52.932 --> 14:06:56.030
to importing the whole CS50 library,
functionally, it's the same.

14:06:56.030 --> 14:06:58.072
You're not to notice any
performance impact here.

14:06:58.072 --> 14:06:59.212
It's a small library.

14:06:59.212 --> 14:07:02.202
But notice what does not
work now, whereas it did work

14:07:02.202 --> 14:07:07.632
in C. Python of Calculator.py, Enter,
we see our first traceback deliberately

14:07:07.632 --> 14:07:08.212
here.

14:07:08.212 --> 14:07:10.092
So a traceback is just
a term of art that

14:07:10.092 --> 14:07:13.732
says, here is a trace back
through all of the functions

14:07:13.732 --> 14:07:14.772
that just got executed.

14:07:14.772 --> 14:07:16.692
In the world of C, you
might call this a stack

14:07:16.692 --> 14:07:19.459
trace, stack being the operative word.

14:07:19.459 --> 14:07:21.792
Recall that when we talked
about the stack and the heap,

14:07:21.792 --> 14:07:24.599
the stack, like a stack of trays,
was all of the functions that

14:07:24.599 --> 14:07:26.182
might get called, one after the other.

14:07:26.182 --> 14:07:30.852
We had main, we had swap, then swap went
away, and then main finished, recall.

14:07:30.852 --> 14:07:34.542
So here's a trace back of all of the
functions or code that got executed.

14:07:34.542 --> 14:07:37.402
There's not really any functions
other than my file itself.

14:07:37.402 --> 14:07:38.872
Otherwise there'd be more detail.

14:07:38.872 --> 14:07:42.102
But even though it's a little cryptic,
we can perhaps infer from the output

14:07:42.102 --> 14:07:46.482
here, name error, so something related
to the name of something, name, getInt

14:07:46.482 --> 14:07:47.472
is not defined.

14:07:47.472 --> 14:07:50.712
And this of course, happens
on line 3 over there.

14:07:50.712 --> 14:07:52.042
All right, so why is that?

14:07:52.042 --> 14:07:55.692
Well, Python essentially
allows us to namespace

14:07:55.692 --> 14:07:58.272
our functions that come from libraries.

14:07:58.272 --> 14:08:01.812
There was a problem in C. If
you were using the CS50 library,

14:08:01.812 --> 14:08:03.702
and thus had access
to getInt, getString,

14:08:03.702 --> 14:08:06.372
and so forth, you could
not use another library

14:08:06.372 --> 14:08:08.112
that had the same function names.

14:08:08.112 --> 14:08:10.032
They would collide, and
the compiler would not

14:08:10.032 --> 14:08:12.552
know how to link them
together correctly.

14:08:12.552 --> 14:08:18.042
In Python, and other languages
like JavaScript, and in Java,

14:08:18.042 --> 14:08:21.792
you have support for effectively
what would be called namespaces.

14:08:21.792 --> 14:08:26.892
You can isolate variables and
function names to their own namespace,

14:08:26.892 --> 14:08:29.112
like their own container in memory.

14:08:29.112 --> 14:08:32.082
And what this means is,
if you import all of CS50,

14:08:32.082 --> 14:08:36.252
you have to say that the getInt you
want is inside the CS50 library.

14:08:36.252 --> 14:08:39.702
So just like with the image
blurring, and the image edges

14:08:39.702 --> 14:08:44.952
before, where I had to specify image dot
and image filter dot, similarly here,

14:08:44.952 --> 14:08:48.492
am I specifying with a dot operator,
albeit a little differently, that I

14:08:48.492 --> 14:08:50.932
want CS50.getInt in both places.

14:08:50.932 --> 14:08:54.642
And now if I rerun Python
of Calculator.py, 1 and 2,

14:08:54.642 --> 14:08:56.382
now we're back in business.

14:08:56.382 --> 14:08:57.312
Which one is better?

14:08:57.312 --> 14:09:01.312
Generally speaking, it depends
on just how many functions

14:09:01.312 --> 14:09:02.562
you're using from the library.

14:09:02.562 --> 14:09:05.562
If you're using a whole bunch of
functions, just import the whole thing.

14:09:05.562 --> 14:09:09.855
If you're only using maybe one
or two, import them line by line.

14:09:09.855 --> 14:09:12.272
All right, so let's go ahead
and make a little tweak here.

14:09:12.272 --> 14:09:15.439
Let's get rid of this library
and take this training wheel off,

14:09:15.439 --> 14:09:18.272
too, as quickly as we introduced
it, though for the problems set six

14:09:18.272 --> 14:09:20.832
you'll be able to use all
of these same functions.

14:09:20.832 --> 14:09:24.632
Suppose I get rid of this, and
I just use the input function,

14:09:24.632 --> 14:09:28.232
just like I did by
replacing getString earlier.

14:09:28.232 --> 14:09:31.232
Let me go ahead now and run
this version of the code.

14:09:31.232 --> 14:09:37.486
Python of Calculator.py, OK,
how about 1 plus 2 equals 3.

14:09:37.486 --> 14:09:39.182
Huh.

14:09:39.182 --> 14:09:41.852
All right, obviously wrong, incorrect.

14:09:41.852 --> 14:09:46.412
Can anyone explain what just
happened, based on instincts?

14:09:46.412 --> 14:09:47.412
What just happened here.

14:09:47.412 --> 14:09:47.912
Yeah.

14:09:47.912 --> 14:09:49.142
AUDIENCE: You want an answer?

14:09:49.142 --> 14:09:50.267
DAVID J. MALAN: Sure, yeah.

14:09:50.267 --> 14:09:54.452
AUDIENCE: Say you have a number
of strings that don't have Ints,

14:09:54.452 --> 14:09:57.842
so you would part with them and
say, printing one, two, better.

14:09:57.842 --> 14:10:01.172
DAVID J. MALAN: Exactly, Python
is interpreting, or treating,

14:10:01.172 --> 14:10:03.332
both x and y as strings,
which is actually

14:10:03.332 --> 14:10:05.642
what the input function
returns by default.

14:10:05.642 --> 14:10:08.672
And so plus is now being interpreted
as concatenation, as we defined it

14:10:08.672 --> 14:10:09.182
earlier.

14:10:09.182 --> 14:10:12.302
So x plus y isn't x
plus y mathematically,

14:10:12.302 --> 14:10:15.002
but in terms of string
joining, just like in Scratch.

14:10:15.002 --> 14:10:18.212
So that's why we're getting
12, or really one two,

14:10:18.212 --> 14:10:19.562
which isn't itself a number.

14:10:19.562 --> 14:10:20.702
It, too, is another string.

14:10:20.702 --> 14:10:22.472
So we somehow need to convert things.

14:10:22.472 --> 14:10:25.562
And we didn't have this
ability quite as easily in C.

14:10:25.562 --> 14:10:29.192
We did have like the A to i
function, ASCII to integer,

14:10:29.192 --> 14:10:30.792
which did allow you to do this.

14:10:30.792 --> 14:10:35.912
The analog in Python is actually just
to do a cast, a typecast, using Int.

14:10:35.912 --> 14:10:39.272
So just like in C, you
can use the keyword Int,

14:10:39.272 --> 14:10:41.022
but you use it a little differently.

14:10:41.022 --> 14:10:45.822
Notice that I'm not doing parenthesis
Int close parenthesis before the value.

14:10:45.822 --> 14:10:47.532
I'm using Int as a function.

14:10:47.532 --> 14:10:49.952
So indeed, in Python, Int is a function.

14:10:49.952 --> 14:10:53.132
Float is a function, that
you can pass values into,

14:10:53.132 --> 14:10:54.792
to do this kind of conversion.

14:10:54.792 --> 14:10:58.532
So now, if I run Python
of Calculator.py, 1 and 2,

14:10:58.532 --> 14:11:01.952
now we're back in business,
and getting the answer of 3.

14:11:01.952 --> 14:11:03.762
But there's kind of a catch here.

14:11:03.762 --> 14:11:04.952
There's always going to be a trade-off.

14:11:04.952 --> 14:11:07.082
Like that sounds amazing that
it just works in this way.

14:11:07.082 --> 14:11:08.972
We can throw away the
CS50 library already.

14:11:08.972 --> 14:11:13.652
But what if the user accidentally
types, or maliciously types in,

14:11:13.652 --> 14:11:15.557
like a cat, instead of a number.

14:11:15.557 --> 14:11:17.432
Damn, well, there's one
of these trace backs.

14:11:17.432 --> 14:11:19.302
Like, now my program has crashed.

14:11:19.302 --> 14:11:21.864
This is similar in spirit
to the kinds of segfaults

14:11:21.864 --> 14:11:23.072
that you might have had in C.

14:11:23.072 --> 14:11:24.362
But they're not segfaults per se.

14:11:24.362 --> 14:11:26.029
It doesn't necessarily relate to memory.

14:11:26.029 --> 14:11:31.812
This time it relates to actual
runtime values, not being as expected.

14:11:31.812 --> 14:11:34.772
So this time it's not a name
error, it's a value error,

14:11:34.772 --> 14:11:39.102
invalid literal for Int with
base 10 quote unquote "cat."

14:11:39.102 --> 14:11:43.322
So, again, it's written for sort
of a programmer, more than sort

14:11:43.322 --> 14:11:46.172
of a typical person, because it's
pretty arcane, the language here.

14:11:46.172 --> 14:11:47.422
But let's try to interpret it.

14:11:47.422 --> 14:11:51.384
Invalid literal, a literal is just
something someone typed for Int, which

14:11:51.384 --> 14:11:52.842
is the function name, with base 10.

14:11:52.842 --> 14:11:54.692
It's just defaulting to decimal numbers.

14:11:54.692 --> 14:11:56.937
Cat is apparently not a decimal number.

14:11:56.937 --> 14:11:59.562
It doesn't look like it, therefore
it can't be treated like it.

14:11:59.562 --> 14:12:01.452
Therefore, there's a value error.

14:12:01.452 --> 14:12:03.272
So what can we do?

14:12:03.272 --> 14:12:06.722
Unfortunately, you would have
to somehow catch this error.

14:12:06.722 --> 14:12:08.972
And the only way to do
that in Python really

14:12:08.972 --> 14:12:11.492
is by way of another
feature that C did not have,

14:12:11.492 --> 14:12:13.922
namely, what are called exceptions.

14:12:13.922 --> 14:12:18.602
An exception is exactly what just
happened, name error, value error.

14:12:18.602 --> 14:12:22.112
They are things that can go wrong
when your Python code is running,

14:12:22.112 --> 14:12:27.192
that aren't necessarily going to be
detected until you run your code.

14:12:27.192 --> 14:12:32.762
So in Python, and in JavaScript, and in
Java, and other more modern languages,

14:12:32.762 --> 14:12:35.762
there's this ability to
actually try to do something,

14:12:35.762 --> 14:12:37.537
except if something goes wrong.

14:12:37.537 --> 14:12:39.662
And in fact, I'm going to
introduce a bit of syntax

14:12:39.662 --> 14:12:42.079
here, even though we won't
have to use this much just yet.

14:12:42.079 --> 14:12:46.502
Instead of just blindly converting
x to an Int, let me go ahead

14:12:46.502 --> 14:12:48.492
and try to do that.

14:12:48.492 --> 14:12:51.902
And if there's an exception,
go ahead and say something

14:12:51.902 --> 14:12:58.802
like print, that is not an Int.

14:12:58.802 --> 14:13:02.060
And then I'm going to do
something like exit, right there.

14:13:02.060 --> 14:13:03.602
And let me go ahead and do this here.

14:13:03.602 --> 14:13:07.892
Let me try to get y, except
if there's an exception.

14:13:07.892 --> 14:13:12.519
Then let me go ahead and say, again,
that is not an Int exclamation point.

14:13:12.519 --> 14:13:14.852
And then I'm going to exit
from there to, otherwise I'll

14:13:14.852 --> 14:13:16.382
go ahead and print x plus y.

14:13:16.382 --> 14:13:22.982
If I run Python of
Calculator.py now, whoops, oh,

14:13:22.982 --> 14:13:25.202
forgot my close quote, sorry.

14:13:25.202 --> 14:13:31.082
All right, so close quote, Python of
Calculator.py, 1 and 2 still work.

14:13:31.082 --> 14:13:34.322
But if I try to type in
something wrong like cat, now

14:13:34.322 --> 14:13:35.832
it actually detects the error.

14:13:35.832 --> 14:13:38.372
So what is the CS50
library in Python doing?

14:13:38.372 --> 14:13:42.122
It's actually doing that try and accept
for you, because suffice it to say,

14:13:42.122 --> 14:13:45.062
otherwise your programs for
something simple, like a calculator,

14:13:45.062 --> 14:13:46.422
start to get longer and longer.

14:13:46.422 --> 14:13:49.682
So we factored that kind of
logic out to the CS50 getInt

14:13:49.682 --> 14:13:51.212
function and get float function.

14:13:51.212 --> 14:13:55.305
But underneath the hood, they're
essentially doing this, try except,

14:13:55.305 --> 14:13:56.972
but they're being a little more precise.

14:13:56.972 --> 14:14:00.972
They're detecting a specific error,
and they are doing it in a loop,

14:14:00.972 --> 14:14:03.572
so that these functions will
get executed again and again.

14:14:03.572 --> 14:14:07.232
In fact, the best way to do this is to
say except if there's a value error,

14:14:07.232 --> 14:14:10.600
then print that error
message out to the user.

14:14:10.600 --> 14:14:13.392
And again, let's not get too into
the weeds here with this feature.

14:14:13.392 --> 14:14:15.282
We've already put into the CS50 library.

14:14:15.282 --> 14:14:17.582
But that's why, for instance,
we bootstrap things,

14:14:17.582 --> 14:14:20.942
by just using these
functions out of the box.

14:14:20.942 --> 14:14:24.132
All right, let's do something
more with our calculator here.

14:14:24.132 --> 14:14:25.532
How about this.

14:14:25.532 --> 14:14:28.412
In the world of C, we
had another version

14:14:28.412 --> 14:14:33.512
of this code, which actually
did some division by way of--

14:14:33.512 --> 14:14:38.202
which actually did division of
numbers, not just the addition herein.

14:14:38.202 --> 14:14:42.512
So let me go ahead and close the C
version, and let's focus only on Python

14:14:42.512 --> 14:14:44.464
now, doing some of these
same lines of codes.

14:14:44.464 --> 14:14:46.172
But I'm going to go
ahead and just assume

14:14:46.172 --> 14:14:48.662
that the user is going to
cooperate and use proper input.

14:14:48.662 --> 14:14:52.832
So from CS50, import getInt, that
will deal with any errors for me.

14:14:52.832 --> 14:15:00.162
X gets getInt, ask the user
for an Int x, y equals getInt,

14:15:00.162 --> 14:15:01.692
ask the user for an Int y.

14:15:01.692 --> 14:15:03.532
And then, let's go ahead and do this.

14:15:03.532 --> 14:15:07.632
Let's declare a variable called
z, set it equal to x divided by y.

14:15:07.632 --> 14:15:09.372
Then let's go ahead and print z.

14:15:09.372 --> 14:15:13.762
Still no need for a format string, I
can just print out the variable's value.

14:15:13.762 --> 14:15:15.762
Let me go ahead and run
Python of Calculator.py.

14:15:15.762 --> 14:15:20.172
Let me do 1, 10, and I get 0.1.

14:15:20.172 --> 14:15:25.782
What did I get in C,
though, if you think back.

14:15:25.782 --> 14:15:28.598
What would we have happened in C?

14:15:28.598 --> 14:15:29.942
AUDIENCE: Zero?

14:15:29.942 --> 14:15:32.162
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, we
would have gotten zero in C.

14:15:32.162 --> 14:15:34.520
But why, in C, when you
divide one Int by another,

14:15:34.520 --> 14:15:36.437
and those Ints are like
1 and 10 respectively?

14:15:36.437 --> 14:15:38.199
AUDIENCE: It'll give
you an integer back.

14:15:38.199 --> 14:15:39.782
DAVID J. MALAN: It will give you what?

14:15:39.782 --> 14:15:40.865
AUDIENCE: An integer back.

14:15:40.865 --> 14:15:44.432
DAVID J. MALAN: It will give you an
integer back, and, unfortunately, 0.1,

14:15:44.432 --> 14:15:46.382
the integer part of it is indeed zero.

14:15:46.382 --> 14:15:48.492
So this was an example of truncation.

14:15:48.492 --> 14:15:51.062
So truncation was an
issue in C. But it would

14:15:51.062 --> 14:15:53.972
seem as though this is no
longer a problem in Python,

14:15:53.972 --> 14:15:57.812
insofar as the division operator
actually handles that for us.

14:15:57.812 --> 14:16:00.752
As an aside, if you want the old
behavior, because it actually

14:16:00.752 --> 14:16:03.542
is sometimes useful for
rounding or flooring values,

14:16:03.542 --> 14:16:06.092
you can actually use two slashes.

14:16:06.092 --> 14:16:08.142
And now you get the C behavior.

14:16:08.142 --> 14:16:10.232
So that now 1 divided by 10 is zero.

14:16:10.232 --> 14:16:12.752
So you don't give up that
capability, but at least it

14:16:12.752 --> 14:16:14.132
does a more sensible default.

14:16:14.132 --> 14:16:17.552
Most people, especially new programmers,
when dividing one value by another,

14:16:17.552 --> 14:16:20.522
would want to get 0.1,
not 0, for reasons

14:16:20.522 --> 14:16:22.622
that indeed we had to explain weeks ago.

14:16:22.622 --> 14:16:26.462
But what about another problem we
had with the world of floats before,

14:16:26.462 --> 14:16:28.562
whereby there is imprecision?

14:16:28.562 --> 14:16:31.502
Let me go ahead and, somewhat
cryptically, print out the value of z

14:16:31.502 --> 14:16:32.382
as follows.

14:16:32.382 --> 14:16:34.862
I'm going to format
it using an f-string.

14:16:34.862 --> 14:16:39.242
And I'm going to go ahead and format,
not just z, because this is essentially

14:16:39.242 --> 14:16:39.972
the same thing.

14:16:39.972 --> 14:16:43.142
Notice this, if I do Python
of Calculator.py, 1 and 10,

14:16:43.142 --> 14:16:46.292
I get, by default, just
one significant digit.

14:16:46.292 --> 14:16:50.442
But if I use this syntax in Python,
which we won't have to use often,

14:16:50.442 --> 14:16:53.072
I can actually do in
C like I did before,

14:16:53.072 --> 14:16:56.172
50 significant digits
after the decimal point.

14:16:56.172 --> 14:17:00.542
So now let me rerun Python
of Calculator.py 1 and 10,

14:17:00.542 --> 14:17:03.512
and let's see if floating point
imprecision is still with us.

14:17:03.512 --> 14:17:04.802
Unfortunately, it is.

14:17:04.802 --> 14:17:07.472
And you can see as much here,
the f-string, the format string,

14:17:07.472 --> 14:17:10.512
is just showing us now 50 digits
instead of the default one.

14:17:10.512 --> 14:17:12.632
So we've not solved all problems.

14:17:12.632 --> 14:17:15.367
But we have solved at least some.

14:17:15.367 --> 14:17:18.242
All right, before we pivot away from
a mere calculator, any questions

14:17:18.242 --> 14:17:21.872
now on syntax or concepts or the like?

14:17:21.872 --> 14:17:22.592
Yeah.

14:17:22.592 --> 14:17:25.842
AUDIENCE: Do you think
the double slash you get

14:17:25.842 --> 14:17:28.459
has merit, how do you comment on that?

14:17:28.459 --> 14:17:29.792
DAVID J. MALAN: How do you what?

14:17:29.792 --> 14:17:30.750
Oh, how do you comment.

14:17:30.750 --> 14:17:33.932
Really good question, if you're
using double slash for division

14:17:33.932 --> 14:17:36.392
with flooring or truncation,
like I described,

14:17:36.392 --> 14:17:38.372
how do you do a comment in Python.

14:17:38.372 --> 14:17:39.902
This is a comment.

14:17:39.902 --> 14:17:42.452
And the convention is actually
to use a complete sentence,

14:17:42.452 --> 14:17:43.995
like with a capital T here.

14:17:43.995 --> 14:17:46.412
You don't need a period unless
there's multiple sentences.

14:17:46.412 --> 14:17:49.362
And technically, it should be above
the line of code by convention.

14:17:49.362 --> 14:17:51.642
So you would use a hash symbol instead.

14:17:51.642 --> 14:17:52.602
Good question.

14:17:52.602 --> 14:17:53.942
I haven't seen those yet.

14:17:53.942 --> 14:17:57.272
All right, let's go ahead and make
something else here, how about.

14:17:57.272 --> 14:17:59.952
Let me go ahead and
open up, for instance,

14:17:59.952 --> 14:18:05.612
an example called Points1.c,
which we saw a few weeks back.

14:18:05.612 --> 14:18:10.052
And let me go ahead on the other side
and create a file called Points.py.

14:18:10.052 --> 14:18:13.412
This was a program, recall, that
asked the user how many points they

14:18:13.412 --> 14:18:15.910
lost on the first assignment.

14:18:15.910 --> 14:18:17.702
And then it went ahead
and just printed out

14:18:17.702 --> 14:18:20.312
whether they lost fewer points
than me, because I lost two,

14:18:20.312 --> 14:18:23.639
if you recall the photo, more points
than me, or the same points as me.

14:18:23.639 --> 14:18:26.222
Let me go ahead and zoom out so
we can see a bit more of this.

14:18:26.222 --> 14:18:30.730
And let me now, on the top right here,
go about implementing this in Python.

14:18:30.730 --> 14:18:33.272
So I want to first prompt the
user for some number of points.

14:18:33.272 --> 14:18:37.062
So from CS50 let's import getInt,
so it handles the error-checking.

14:18:37.062 --> 14:18:39.932
Let's then do points
equals getInt, and ask

14:18:39.932 --> 14:18:43.952
the user, how many points
did you lose, question mark.

14:18:43.952 --> 14:18:48.512
Then let's go ahead and say, if points
less than two, which was my value,

14:18:48.512 --> 14:18:52.322
print, you lost fewer points than me.

14:18:52.322 --> 14:18:59.792
Otherwise, if it's else if points
greater than 2, go ahead and print,

14:18:59.792 --> 14:19:03.592
you lost more points than me.

14:19:03.592 --> 14:19:07.322
Else let's go ahead and handle
the final scenario, which is you

14:19:07.322 --> 14:19:11.122
lost the same number of points as me.

14:19:11.122 --> 14:19:15.752
Before I run this, does anyone want to
point out a mistake I've already made?

14:19:15.752 --> 14:19:16.252
Yeah.

14:19:16.252 --> 14:19:17.912
AUDIENCE: Else if has to be elif.

14:19:17.912 --> 14:19:21.212
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so else if in
C is actually now elif in Python.

14:19:21.212 --> 14:19:22.302
It's a single word.

14:19:22.302 --> 14:19:26.312
So let me change this to elif, and now
cross my fingers, Python of Points.py,

14:19:26.312 --> 14:19:29.852
suppose you lost three
points on some assignment.

14:19:29.852 --> 14:19:31.712
You lost more points than my two.

14:19:31.712 --> 14:19:34.330
If you only lost one point,
you lost fewer points than me.

14:19:34.330 --> 14:19:35.372
So the logic is the same.

14:19:35.372 --> 14:19:37.562
But notice the code is much tighter.

14:19:37.562 --> 14:19:41.222
In 10 total lines, we did in
what was 24 lines, because we've

14:19:41.222 --> 14:19:42.872
thrown away a lot of the syntax.

14:19:42.872 --> 14:19:44.892
The curly braces are
no longer necessary.

14:19:44.892 --> 14:19:46.752
The parentheses are
gone, the semicolons.

14:19:46.752 --> 14:19:50.192
So this is why it just tends to
be more pleasant pretty quickly,

14:19:50.192 --> 14:19:52.832
using a language like this.

14:19:52.832 --> 14:19:55.292
All right, let's do
one other example here.

14:19:55.292 --> 14:19:59.522
In C, recall that we were able to
determine the parity of some number,

14:19:59.522 --> 14:20:01.112
if something is even or odd.

14:20:01.112 --> 14:20:05.522
Well, in Python, let me go ahead
and create a file called Parity.py,

14:20:05.522 --> 14:20:09.332
and let's look for a moment
at the C version at left.

14:20:09.332 --> 14:20:13.202
Here was the code in C that we used
to determine the parity of a number.

14:20:13.202 --> 14:20:16.322
And, really, the key
takeaway from all these lines

14:20:16.322 --> 14:20:17.812
was just the remainder operator.

14:20:17.812 --> 14:20:19.062
And that one is still with us.

14:20:19.062 --> 14:20:21.520
So this is a simple demonstration,
just to make that point,

14:20:21.520 --> 14:20:25.292
if in Python, I want to determine
whether a number is even or odd.

14:20:25.292 --> 14:20:29.672
Well, let's go ahead and from CS50,
import getInt, then let's go ahead

14:20:29.672 --> 14:20:35.132
and get a number like n from the user,
using getInt, and ask them for n.

14:20:35.132 --> 14:20:40.742
And then let's go ahead and say,
if n percent sign 2 equals 0,

14:20:40.742 --> 14:20:44.792
then let's go ahead and
print quote unquote "Even."

14:20:44.792 --> 14:20:50.275
Else let's go ahead and print
out Odd, but before I run this,

14:20:50.275 --> 14:20:53.192
anyone want to instinctively, even
though we've not talked about this,

14:20:53.192 --> 14:20:55.532
point out a mistake here?

14:20:55.532 --> 14:20:56.332
What I did wrong?

14:20:56.332 --> 14:20:57.332
AUDIENCE: Double equals.

14:20:57.332 --> 14:20:58.957
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so double equals.

14:20:58.957 --> 14:21:02.372
Again, so even though some of the stuff
is changing, some of the same ideas

14:21:02.372 --> 14:21:02.952
are the same.

14:21:02.952 --> 14:21:05.042
So this, too, should
be a double equal sign,

14:21:05.042 --> 14:21:07.142
because I'm comparing for equality here.

14:21:07.142 --> 14:21:08.675
And why is this the right math?

14:21:08.675 --> 14:21:10.592
Well, if you divide a
number by 2, it's either

14:21:10.592 --> 14:21:12.812
going to have 0 or 1 as a remainder.

14:21:12.812 --> 14:21:15.552
And that's going to determine
if it's even or odd for us.

14:21:15.552 --> 14:21:18.722
So let's run Python of Parity.py,
type in a number like 50,

14:21:18.722 --> 14:21:21.182
and hopefully we get, indeed, even.

14:21:21.182 --> 14:21:23.432
So again, same idea, but now
we're down to eight lines

14:21:23.432 --> 14:21:25.082
of code instead of the 20.

14:21:25.082 --> 14:21:27.332
Well, let's now do something
a little more interactive

14:21:27.332 --> 14:21:31.202
and a little representative of tools
that actually ask the user questions.

14:21:31.202 --> 14:21:36.842
In C, recall that we had this
agreement program, Agree.c.

14:21:36.842 --> 14:21:40.802
And then let's go ahead and implement
a corresponding version in Python,

14:21:40.802 --> 14:21:42.392
in a file called Agree.py.

14:21:42.392 --> 14:21:45.092
And let's look at the C version first.

14:21:45.092 --> 14:21:47.222
On the left, we used get char here.

14:21:47.222 --> 14:21:49.712
And then we used the
double vertical bars

14:21:49.712 --> 14:21:52.952
to check if C is equal to
capital Y or lowercase y.

14:21:52.952 --> 14:21:55.022
And then we did the
same thing for n for no.

14:21:55.022 --> 14:22:00.902
And so let's go over here and
let's do from CS50, import get--

14:22:00.902 --> 14:22:03.092
OK, get char is not a thing.

14:22:03.092 --> 14:22:05.612
And this here is another
difference with Python.

14:22:05.612 --> 14:22:09.032
There is no data type for
individual characters.

14:22:09.032 --> 14:22:11.162
You have strings, STRs,
and, honestly, those

14:22:11.162 --> 14:22:13.142
are fine, because if
you have a STR that's

14:22:13.142 --> 14:22:15.482
just one character, for
all intents and purposes,

14:22:15.482 --> 14:22:17.232
it is just a single character.

14:22:17.232 --> 14:22:18.482
So it's just a simplification.

14:22:18.482 --> 14:22:19.722
You don't have to think as much.

14:22:19.722 --> 14:22:22.180
You don't have to worry about
double quotes, single quotes.

14:22:22.180 --> 14:22:25.872
In fact, in Python, you can use
double quotes or single quotes,

14:22:25.872 --> 14:22:27.452
so long as you're consistent.

14:22:27.452 --> 14:22:29.492
So long as you're
consistent, the single quotes

14:22:29.492 --> 14:22:32.192
do not mean something
different, like they do in C.

14:22:32.192 --> 14:22:34.862
So I'm going to go ahead
and use getString here,

14:22:34.862 --> 14:22:37.742
although, strictly speaking, I
could just use the input function,

14:22:37.742 --> 14:22:39.002
as we saw before.

14:22:39.002 --> 14:22:43.772
I'm going to get a string from the
user that asks them this, getString,

14:22:43.772 --> 14:22:47.079
quote unquote, "Do you agree," like a
little checkbox or interactive prompt,

14:22:47.079 --> 14:22:50.162
where you have to say yes or no, you
want to agree to the following terms,

14:22:50.162 --> 14:22:51.102
or whatnot.

14:22:51.102 --> 14:22:54.632
And then let's translate the
conditionals to Python, now, too.

14:22:54.632 --> 14:23:02.372
So if S equals equals quote-unquote
"Y," or S equals equals lowercase y,

14:23:02.372 --> 14:23:08.702
let's go ahead and print out agreed,
just like in C, elif S equals

14:23:08.702 --> 14:23:12.062
equals N or S equals equals little n.

14:23:12.062 --> 14:23:14.580
Let's go ahead, then,
and print out not agreed.

14:23:14.580 --> 14:23:17.372
And you can already see, perhaps,
one of the differences here, too.

14:23:17.372 --> 14:23:20.222
Is Python a little more
English-like, in that

14:23:20.222 --> 14:23:24.132
you just literally use the English word
or, instead of the two vertical bars.

14:23:24.132 --> 14:23:26.892
But it's ultimately
doing the same thing.

14:23:26.892 --> 14:23:29.912
Can we simplify this code a bit, though.

14:23:29.912 --> 14:23:31.862
This would be a little
annoying if we wanted

14:23:31.862 --> 14:23:34.322
to add support, not just
for big Y and little y,

14:23:34.322 --> 14:23:40.752
but Yes or big Yes or little yes or
big Y, lowercase e, capital S, right?

14:23:40.752 --> 14:23:43.652
There's a lot of permutations
of Y-E-S or just y,

14:23:43.652 --> 14:23:45.242
that we ideally should tolerate.

14:23:45.242 --> 14:23:47.992
Otherwise, the user is going to
have to type exactly what we want,

14:23:47.992 --> 14:23:49.292
which isn't very user-friendly.

14:23:49.292 --> 14:23:51.572
Any intuition for how
we could logically,

14:23:51.572 --> 14:23:54.792
even if you don't know how to
do it in code, make this better?

14:23:54.792 --> 14:23:55.292
Yeah.

14:23:55.292 --> 14:23:58.057
AUDIENCE: Write way over
the list, and then up,

14:23:58.057 --> 14:23:59.432
it's like the things in the list.

14:23:59.432 --> 14:24:03.572
DAVID J. MALAN: Nice, yeah, we saw an
example of a list before, just 0, 1, 2.

14:24:03.572 --> 14:24:06.421
Why don't we take that same
idea and ask a similar question.

14:24:06.421 --> 14:24:11.341
If S is in the following list
of values, Y or little y,

14:24:11.341 --> 14:24:15.122
or heck, let me add to the list
now, yes, or maybe all capital YES.

14:24:15.122 --> 14:24:17.301
And it's going to get a
little annoying, admittedly,

14:24:17.301 --> 14:24:20.272
but this is still better than the
alternative, with all the or's.

14:24:20.272 --> 14:24:22.162
I could do things like
this, and so forth.

14:24:22.162 --> 14:24:24.262
There's a whole bunch more permutations.

14:24:24.262 --> 14:24:26.992
But let's leave this alone,
and let me just go into here

14:24:26.992 --> 14:24:33.801
and change this to, if S is in the
following list of N or little n or no,

14:24:33.801 --> 14:24:36.982
and I won't do as, let's just not
worry about the weird capitalizations

14:24:36.982 --> 14:24:38.122
there, for now.

14:24:38.122 --> 14:24:39.322
Let's go ahead and run this.

14:24:39.322 --> 14:24:42.472
Python of Agree.py, do I agree?

14:24:42.472 --> 14:24:45.262
Y. OK, how about yes?

14:24:45.262 --> 14:24:46.881
All right, how about big Yes.

14:24:46.881 --> 14:24:48.372
OK, that does not seem to work.

14:24:48.372 --> 14:24:50.872
Notice it did not say agreed,
and it did not say not agreed.

14:24:50.872 --> 14:24:51.932
It didn't detect it.

14:24:51.932 --> 14:24:53.702
So how can I do this?

14:24:53.702 --> 14:24:57.292
Well, you know what I could
do, what I don't really

14:24:57.292 --> 14:24:58.762
need the uppercase and lowercase.

14:24:58.762 --> 14:25:00.711
Let me tighten this
list up a little bit.

14:25:00.711 --> 14:25:04.162
And why don't I just
force S to be lowercase.

14:25:04.162 --> 14:25:07.522
S.lower, recall, whether
it's one character or more,

14:25:07.522 --> 14:25:10.702
is a function built into
STRs now, strings in Python,

14:25:10.702 --> 14:25:12.472
that forces the whole
thing to lowercase.

14:25:12.472 --> 14:25:13.972
So now, watch what I can do.

14:25:13.972 --> 14:25:19.222
Python of Agree.py, little y,
that works, big Y, that works.

14:25:19.222 --> 14:25:24.362
Big Yes, that works, big Y,
little e, big S, that also works.

14:25:24.362 --> 14:25:27.432
So we've now handled, in one fell
swoop, a whole bunch more logic.

14:25:27.432 --> 14:25:29.432
