14:47:55.832 --> 14:47:58.292
can have two types of
arguments, or parameters.

14:47:58.292 --> 14:48:01.682
Some arguments are positional, which
is the fancy way of saying it's

14:48:01.682 --> 14:48:03.212
a comma separated list of arguments.

14:48:03.212 --> 14:48:06.062
And that's what we did all the time
in C. Something comma, something

14:48:06.062 --> 14:48:08.187
comma, something, we did
it in printf all the time,

14:48:08.187 --> 14:48:10.502
and in other functions that
took multiple arguments.

14:48:10.502 --> 14:48:14.402
In Python, you have, not
only positional arguments,

14:48:14.402 --> 14:48:18.182
where you just separate them by commas,
to give one or two or three or more

14:48:18.182 --> 14:48:19.172
arguments.

14:48:19.172 --> 14:48:22.742
There are also named arguments,
which looks weird but is

14:48:22.742 --> 14:48:24.662
helpful for reasons like this.

14:48:24.662 --> 14:48:27.422
If you read the
documentation, you will see

14:48:27.422 --> 14:48:31.262
that there is a named argument
that Python accepts, called end.

14:48:31.262 --> 14:48:34.202
And if you set that
equal to something, that

14:48:34.202 --> 14:48:36.722
will be used as the end
of every line, instead

14:48:36.722 --> 14:48:39.272
of the default, which the
documentation will also say

14:48:39.272 --> 14:48:41.222
is quote unquote backslash n.

14:48:41.222 --> 14:48:45.522
So this line here has no effect
on my logic at the moment.

14:48:45.522 --> 14:48:49.802
But if I change it to just quote
unquote, essentially overriding

14:48:49.802 --> 14:48:54.992
the default new line character, and
now run Mario again, now I get all four

14:48:54.992 --> 14:48:55.800
on the same line.

14:48:55.800 --> 14:48:57.092
There's a bit of a bug, though.

14:48:57.092 --> 14:49:00.132
My prompt is not meant
to be on the same line.

14:49:00.132 --> 14:49:02.162
So I can fix that by
just printing nothing.

14:49:02.162 --> 14:49:05.162
But, really, it's not nothing,
because you get the new line for free.

14:49:05.162 --> 14:49:09.452
So let me run Python of
Mario.py again, and now we

14:49:09.452 --> 14:49:12.662
have what I intended in the first
place, which was a little something that

14:49:12.662 --> 14:49:13.692
looked like this.

14:49:13.692 --> 14:49:17.432
And this is just one example
of an argument that has a name.

14:49:17.432 --> 14:49:19.802
But this is a common
paradigm in Python 2,

14:49:19.802 --> 14:49:22.772
to not just separate things by
commas, but to be very specific,

14:49:22.772 --> 14:49:27.332
because the print function might take
5, 10, even 20 different arguments.

14:49:27.332 --> 14:49:31.150
And my God, if you had to
enumerate like 10 or 20 commas,

14:49:31.150 --> 14:49:32.192
you're going to screw up.

14:49:32.192 --> 14:49:34.109
You're going to get
things in the wrong order.

14:49:34.109 --> 14:49:37.122
Named arguments allow you to
be resilient against that.

14:49:37.122 --> 14:49:39.212
So you only specify
arguments by name, and it

14:49:39.212 --> 14:49:42.526
doesn't matter what order they are in.

14:49:42.526 --> 14:49:46.682
All right, any questions, then, on
