15:00:32.342 --> 15:00:35.362
But now the program's a little
more dynamic, which is nice.

15:00:35.362 --> 15:00:37.462
But there's other
syntax I could use here.

15:00:37.462 --> 15:00:40.852
Just so you've seen it, Python does
have some neat syntactic tricks,

15:00:40.852 --> 15:00:43.372
whereby, if you don't
want to do scores.append,

15:00:43.372 --> 15:00:47.812
you can actually say scores
plus equals this score.

15:00:47.812 --> 15:00:52.252
So you can actually concatenate
lists together in Python 2.

15:00:52.252 --> 15:00:54.862
Just as we used plus to
join two strings together,

15:00:54.862 --> 15:00:57.922
you can use plus to
join two lists together.

15:00:57.922 --> 15:01:00.562
The catch is, you need
to put the one score I'm

15:01:00.562 --> 15:01:03.292
