15:07:00.412 --> 15:07:04.302
Let's do Python of Argv.py,
Enter, Hello, world.

15:07:04.302 --> 15:07:08.002
What if I do Argv.py
David at the command line.

15:07:08.002 --> 15:07:09.252
Now I get Hello, David.

15:07:09.252 --> 15:07:11.202
So there's one curiosity here.

15:07:11.202 --> 15:07:15.897
Python is not included in
argv, whereas in C, dot

15:07:15.897 --> 15:07:18.462
slash whatever was the first thing.

15:07:18.462 --> 15:07:22.032
If the analog in Python is that
the name of your Python program

15:07:22.032 --> 15:07:26.322
is the first thing, in bracket 0,
which is why David is in bracket 1,

15:07:26.322 --> 15:07:32.262
the word Python does not appear in
the argv list, just to be clear.

15:07:32.262 --> 15:07:34.512
But otherwise, the
idea of these arguments

15:07:34.512 --> 15:07:36.905
is exactly the same as before.

15:07:36.905 --> 15:07:39.072
And in fact, what you can
do, which is kind of cool,

15:07:39.072 --> 15:07:42.252
is, because argv is a list,
you can do things like this.

15:07:42.252 --> 15:07:47.412
For arg in argv, go ahead
and print out each argument.

15:07:47.412 --> 15:07:49.512
So instead of using a
for loop and i and all

15:07:49.512 --> 15:07:53.742
of this, if I do Python of argv Enter,
it just writes the program's name.

15:07:53.742 --> 15:07:58.482
If I do Python of argv Foo,
it puts Argv.py and Foo.

15:07:58.482 --> 15:08:03.042
If I do, sorry, if I do Foo and
bar, those words all print out.

15:08:03.042 --> 15:08:05.292
If I do Foobar baz, those print out too.

15:08:05.292 --> 15:08:08.352
And Foo and bar or baz are like
a mathematician's x and y and z

15:08:08.352 --> 15:08:11.722
for computer scientists, when you
just need some placeholder words.

15:08:11.722 --> 15:08:12.942
So this is just nice.

15:08:12.942 --> 15:08:16.542
It reads a little more like English, and
a for loop is just much more concise,

15:08:16.542 --> 15:08:20.052
allows you to iterate very quickly
when you want something like that.

15:08:20.052 --> 15:08:22.692
Suppose I only wanted the real
words that the human typed

15:08:22.692 --> 15:08:23.772
after the program's name.

15:08:23.772 --> 15:08:26.982
Like, suppose I want to ignore Argv.py.

15:08:26.982 --> 15:08:30.162
I mean I could do something
hackish like this.

15:08:30.162 --> 15:08:35.627
If arg equals Argv.py,
I could just ignore,

15:08:35.627 --> 15:08:37.002
you know, let's invert the logic.

15:08:37.002 --> 15:08:39.052
I could do this, for instance.

15:08:39.052 --> 15:08:41.622
So if the arg does not
equal the program name,

15:08:41.622 --> 15:08:44.412
then go ahead and print out the word.

15:08:44.412 --> 15:08:46.362
So I get Foobar and baz only.

15:08:46.362 --> 15:08:50.922
Or, this is what's kind of neat
about Python 2, let me undo that.

15:08:50.922 --> 15:08:54.922
And let me just take a slice of
the array of the list instead.

15:08:54.922 --> 15:08:59.332
So it turns out, if argv is
a list, I can actually say,

15:08:59.332 --> 15:09:03.582
you know what, go into that list,
start at element 1, instead of 0,

15:09:03.582 --> 15:09:05.722
and then go all the way to the end.

15:09:05.722 --> 15:09:08.322
And we have not seen this
syntax in C. But this

15:09:08.322 --> 15:09:10.932
is a way of slicing a list in Python.

15:09:10.932 --> 15:09:12.342
So now watch what happens.

15:09:12.342 --> 15:09:17.382
If I run Python of
Argv.py, Foo bar baz Enter,

15:09:17.382 --> 15:09:21.252
I get only a subset of the
list, starting at position 1,

15:09:21.252 --> 15:09:23.414
going all of the way to the end.

15:09:23.414 --> 15:09:25.122
And you can even do
kind of the opposite.

15:09:25.122 --> 15:09:27.852
If, for whatever reason, you
want to ignore the last element,

15:09:27.852 --> 15:09:33.552
you can say colon, we
could say colon negative 1,

15:09:33.552 --> 15:09:36.082
and use a negative number,
which we've not seen before,

15:09:36.082 --> 15:09:38.992
which slices off the end
of the list, as well.

15:09:38.992 --> 15:09:42.522
So there's some syntactic tricks
that tend to be powerful in Python 2,

15:09:42.522 --> 15:09:46.662
even if at first glance, you might
not need them for typical things.

