15:27:05.832 --> 15:27:08.562
Well, let me go ahead here
and implement a program

15:27:08.562 --> 15:27:12.622
in a file called Hogwarts.py,
that processes this data.

15:27:12.622 --> 15:27:14.802
So in Hogwarts.py, let's
just write a program

15:27:14.802 --> 15:27:17.962
that now reads a CSV, in
this case not a phone book,

15:27:17.962 --> 15:27:19.932
but everyone's sorting hat information.

15:27:19.932 --> 15:27:21.972
And I'm going to go
ahead and Import CSV.

15:27:21.972 --> 15:27:25.182
And suppose I want to answer a
reasonable question, ignoring

15:27:25.182 --> 15:27:28.992
the fact that Google's GUI or graphical
user interface, can do this for me.

15:27:28.992 --> 15:27:31.842
I just want to count up who's
going to be in which house.

15:27:31.842 --> 15:27:36.162
So let me give myself a dictionary
called houses, that's initially empty,

15:27:36.162 --> 15:27:37.302
with curly braces.

15:27:37.302 --> 15:27:39.312
And let me pre-create a few keys.

15:27:39.312 --> 15:27:44.022
Let me say Gryffindor is
going to be initialized to 0,

15:27:44.022 --> 15:27:48.342
Hufflepuff will be initialized
to 0 as well, Ravenclaw

15:27:48.342 --> 15:27:49.722
will be initialized to 0.

15:27:49.722 --> 15:27:53.292
And finally, Slytherin
will be initialized to 0.

15:27:53.292 --> 15:27:56.472
So here's another example of
a dictionary, or a hash table,

15:27:56.472 --> 15:27:58.662
just being a very
general-purpose piece of data.

15:27:58.662 --> 15:28:00.282
You can have keys and values.

15:28:00.282 --> 15:28:01.992
The keys, in this case, are the houses.

15:28:01.992 --> 15:28:05.022
The values are initially zero,
but I'm going to use this,

15:28:05.022 --> 15:28:10.122
instead of like four separate variables,
to keep track of everyone's answer

15:28:10.122 --> 15:28:11.252
to this form.

15:28:11.252 --> 15:28:12.252
So I'm going to do this.

15:28:12.252 --> 15:28:19.702
With opening Hogwarts.csv, in read mode,
not append, I don't want to change it.

15:28:19.702 --> 15:28:22.962
I just want to read it, as
file as my variable name.

15:28:22.962 --> 15:28:26.052
Let's go ahead and create
a reader this time,

15:28:26.052 --> 15:28:31.232
that is using the reader function in
the CSV library, by opening that file.

15:28:31.232 --> 15:28:33.732
I'm going to go ahead and ignore
the first line of the file,

15:28:33.732 --> 15:28:36.792
because, recall, that the first
line is just timestamp and house.

15:28:36.792 --> 15:28:37.972
I want to get the real data.

15:28:37.972 --> 15:28:40.062
So this next function
is just a little trick

15:28:40.062 --> 15:28:43.252
for ignoring the first line of the file.

15:28:43.252 --> 15:28:44.322
Then let's do this.

15:28:44.322 --> 15:28:48.702
For every other row in the
reader, that is line by line,

15:28:48.702 --> 15:28:51.942
get the current person's house,
which is in row bracket 1.

15:28:51.942 --> 15:28:54.735
This is what the CSV reader
library is doing for us.

15:28:54.735 --> 15:28:56.652
It's handling all of the
reading of this file.

15:28:56.652 --> 15:29:00.282
It figures out where the comma is,
and, for every row in the file,

15:29:00.282 --> 15:29:02.772
it hands you back a list of size 2.

15:29:02.772 --> 15:29:07.612
In bracket 0 is the time stamp,
in bracket 1 is the house name.

15:29:07.612 --> 15:29:11.352
So, in my code, I can say
house equals row bracket 1.

15:29:11.352 --> 15:29:13.492
I don't care about the time
stamp for this program.

15:29:13.492 --> 15:29:17.592
And then let's go into my dictionary
called houses, plural, index

15:29:17.592 --> 15:29:23.892
into it at the house location, by
its name, and increment that 0 to 1.

15:29:23.892 --> 15:29:26.802
And now, at the end
of this block of code,

15:29:26.802 --> 15:29:29.562
that has the effect of iterating
over every line of the file,

15:29:29.562 --> 15:29:31.992
updating my dictionary
in four different places,

15:29:31.992 --> 15:29:35.712
based on whether someone typed
Gryffindor or Slytherin or anything

15:29:35.712 --> 15:29:36.222
else.

15:29:36.222 --> 15:29:40.332
And notice that I'm using the name of
the house to index into my dictionary,

15:29:40.332 --> 15:29:44.022
to essentially go up to this little
cheat sheet and change the 0 to a 1,

15:29:44.022 --> 15:29:46.542
the 1 to a 2, the 2 to
a 3, instead of having

15:29:46.542 --> 15:29:48.522
like four separate
variables, which would just

15:29:48.522 --> 15:29:50.592
be much more annoying to maintain.

15:29:50.592 --> 15:29:52.812
Down at the bottom, let's
just print out the results.

15:29:52.812 --> 15:29:56.142
For each house in those
houses, iterating over

15:29:56.142 --> 15:29:58.272
the keys they're in
by default in Python,

15:29:58.272 --> 15:30:01.152
let's go ahead and print
out an f-string that says,

15:30:01.152 --> 15:30:05.982
the current house has the current count.

15:30:05.982 --> 15:30:11.592
And count will be the result of indexing
into houses, for that given house.

15:30:11.592 --> 15:30:13.332
And let me close my quote.

15:30:13.332 --> 15:30:18.462
So let's run this to summarize
the data, Hogwarts.py, 140 of you

15:30:18.462 --> 15:30:22.722
answered Gryffindor, 54 Hufflepuff,
72 Ravenclaw, and 80 of you Slytherin.

15:30:22.722 --> 15:30:25.092
And that's just my now way
of code, and this is, oh,

15:30:25.092 --> 15:30:28.749
my God, so much easier than C, to
actually analyze data in this way.

15:30:28.749 --> 15:30:32.082
And one of the reasons that Python is so
popular for data science and analytics,

15:30:32.082 --> 15:30:36.432
more generally, is that it's actually
really easy to manipulate data, and run

15:30:36.432 --> 15:30:37.462
analytics like this.

15:30:37.462 --> 15:30:38.892
And let me clean this up slightly.

15:30:38.892 --> 15:30:41.682
It's a little annoying that
I just have to know and trust

15:30:41.682 --> 15:30:46.932
that the house name is in bracket
1 and timestamp is in bracket 0.

15:30:46.932 --> 15:30:47.962
Let's clean this up.

15:30:47.962 --> 15:30:53.052
There's something called a
Dictionary Reader in the CSV library

15:30:53.052 --> 15:30:54.402
that I can use instead.

15:30:54.402 --> 15:30:58.992
Capital D, capital R, this means
I can throw away this next thing,

15:30:58.992 --> 15:31:01.422
because what a dictionary
reader does is it

15:31:01.422 --> 15:31:05.412
still returns to me every row from
the file, one after the other,

15:31:05.412 --> 15:31:09.082
but it doesn't just give me a list
of size 2 representing each row.

15:31:09.082 --> 15:31:10.482
It gives me a dictionary.

15:31:10.482 --> 15:31:15.522
And it uses, as the keys in that
dictionary, timestamp and house,

15:31:15.522 --> 15:31:17.982
for every row in the
file, which is just to say

15:31:17.982 --> 15:31:20.472
it makes my code a little
more readable, because instead

15:31:20.472 --> 15:31:23.112
of doing this little
trickery, bracket 1,

15:31:23.112 --> 15:31:26.022
I can say quote unquote "Bracket
House" with a capital H,

15:31:26.022 --> 15:31:28.882
because it's capitalized
in the Google Form itself.

15:31:28.882 --> 15:31:31.320
So the code now is
just minorly different,

15:31:31.320 --> 15:31:34.362
but it's way more resilient, especially
if I'm using Google Spreadsheets,

15:31:34.362 --> 15:31:36.912
and I'm moving the columns around
or doing something like that,

15:31:36.912 --> 15:31:38.495
where the numbers might get messed up.

15:31:38.495 --> 15:31:41.782
Now I can run this on Hogwarts.py
again, and I get the same answers.

15:31:41.782 --> 15:31:46.482
But I now don't have to worry about
where those individual columns are.

15:31:46.482 --> 15:31:51.402
All right, any questions on
