15:47:37.492 --> 15:47:40.852
Sometimes I see the genre
like comedy, comedy, comedy,

15:47:40.852 --> 15:47:45.212
but sometimes it's like crime, comma,
drama, or action, comma, crime, comma,

15:47:45.212 --> 15:47:45.892
drama.

15:47:45.892 --> 15:47:47.812
And those things are quoted.

15:47:47.812 --> 15:47:49.522
And yet, I didn't do any quotes.

15:47:49.522 --> 15:47:51.142
You probably didn't type any quotes.

15:47:51.142 --> 15:47:55.522
Where are those quotes
coming from in this CSV file?

15:47:55.522 --> 15:47:56.992
Why are they there if we infer?

15:47:56.992 --> 15:47:57.827
Yeah?

15:47:57.827 --> 15:48:00.682
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

15:48:00.682 --> 15:48:03.650
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah, so you
have a corner case, if you will.

15:48:03.650 --> 15:48:05.692
Because if you're using
commas, as you described,

15:48:05.692 --> 15:48:09.622
to separate your data into what
are effectively columns, well,

15:48:09.622 --> 15:48:12.352
you've painted yourself into
a corner if your actual data

15:48:12.352 --> 15:48:13.920
has commas in it itself.

15:48:13.920 --> 15:48:16.462
So what Google has done, what
Microsoft does, what Apple does

15:48:16.462 --> 15:48:19.672
is, they quote any strings
of text that themselves

15:48:19.672 --> 15:48:23.902
have commas so that these are
now English grammatical commas,

15:48:23.902 --> 15:48:26.072
not CSV specific commas.

15:48:26.072 --> 15:48:28.742
So it's a way of escaping
your data, if you will.

15:48:28.742 --> 15:48:31.412
And escaping just means to call
out a symbol in a special way

15:48:31.412 --> 15:48:33.979
so it's not misinterpreted
as something else.

15:48:33.979 --> 15:48:35.812
All right, so this is
