How could I start to keep that around?

16:01:45.862 --> 16:01:47.187
AUDIENCE: Use a dictionary.

16:01:47.187 --> 16:01:49.312
DAVID J. MALAN: We could
use a dictionary, and how?

16:01:49.312 --> 16:01:50.602
Elaborate on that.

16:01:50.602 --> 16:01:51.477
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

16:01:51.477 --> 16:01:53.435
DAVID J. MALAN: Perfect,
really good instincts.

16:01:53.435 --> 16:01:55.192
Using a dictionary,
insofar as it lets us

16:01:55.192 --> 16:01:58.642
store keys and values, that is,
associate something with something

16:01:58.642 --> 16:01:59.192
else.

16:01:59.192 --> 16:02:01.822
This is why a dictionary
or hash tables more

16:02:01.822 --> 16:02:04.972
generally are such a useful,
practical data structure.

16:02:04.972 --> 16:02:08.462
Because they just let you remember
stuff in some kind of structured way.

16:02:08.462 --> 16:02:11.212
So if the keys are going
to be the titles I've seen,

16:02:11.212 --> 16:02:15.202
the values could be the number of
times I've seen each of those titles.

16:02:15.202 --> 16:02:19.650
And so it's kind of like just
having a two-column table on paper.

16:02:19.650 --> 16:02:22.192
For instance, if I were going
to do this on a piece of paper,

16:02:22.192 --> 16:02:24.682
I might just have two
columns here, where

16:02:24.682 --> 16:02:29.932
maybe this is the title that I've
seen, and this is the count over here.

16:02:29.932 --> 16:02:33.592
This is, in effect, a
dictionary in Python.

16:02:33.592 --> 16:02:36.832
It's two columns, keys on the
left, values on the right.

16:02:36.832 --> 16:02:38.962
And this, if I can implement
in code, will actually

16:02:38.962 --> 16:02:42.922
allow me to store this data, and
then maybe do some simple arithmetic

16:02:42.922 --> 16:02:44.792
to figure out which is the most popular.

16:02:44.792 --> 16:02:45.722
So let's do this.

16:02:45.722 --> 16:02:49.582
Let me go ahead and change my titles
to not be a list, not be a set.

16:02:49.582 --> 16:02:54.502
Let's have it be a dictionary instead,
either doing this, or more succinctly,

16:02:54.502 --> 16:02:58.942
two curly braces that are empty gives
me an empty dictionary automatically.

16:02:58.942 --> 16:03:00.202
What do I now want to do?

16:03:00.202 --> 16:03:02.782
I think most of my
code can stay the same.

16:03:02.782 --> 16:03:06.202
But down here, I don't want
to just blindly add titles

16:03:06.202 --> 16:03:07.522
to the data structure.

16:03:07.522 --> 16:03:10.402
I somehow need to keep
track of the count.

16:03:10.402 --> 16:03:14.032
And unfortunately, if I just
do this-- let's do titles,

16:03:14.032 --> 16:03:18.922
bracket, title, plus equals 1.

16:03:18.922 --> 16:03:21.472
This is a reasonable
first attempt at this.

16:03:21.472 --> 16:03:22.762
Because what am I doing?

16:03:22.762 --> 16:03:28.072
If titles is a dictionary and I want
to look up the current title therein,

16:03:28.072 --> 16:03:30.862
the syntax for that, like before,
is titles, bracket, and then

16:03:30.862 --> 16:03:34.222
the key you want to use to
