16:06:05.132 --> 16:06:06.962
And I'm not seeing any of the counts.

16:06:06.962 --> 16:06:09.122
Let me just quickly add
the counts, and there's

16:06:09.122 --> 16:06:10.872
a couple of ways I could do this.

16:06:10.872 --> 16:06:18.242
I could, say, print out the title, and
then, maybe, let's do something like--

16:06:18.242 --> 16:06:22.562
how about just, comma,
titles, bracket, title?

16:06:22.562 --> 16:06:24.362
So I'm going to print
two things at once,

16:06:24.362 --> 16:06:26.942
both the current title
in the dictionary,

16:06:26.942 --> 16:06:29.642
and whatever its value
is by indexing into it.

16:06:29.642 --> 16:06:31.482
Let me increase my Terminal window.

16:06:31.482 --> 16:06:35.762
Let me run Python of
favorites.py, Enter, and OK.

16:06:35.762 --> 16:06:37.250
Huh.

16:06:37.250 --> 16:06:39.422
Huh.

16:06:39.422 --> 16:06:42.902
None of you said a whole
lot of TV shows, it seems.

16:06:42.902 --> 16:06:47.032
What's the logical error here?

16:06:47.032 --> 16:06:50.802
What did I do wrong if I
look back at my code here?

16:06:50.802 --> 16:06:51.302
Yeah?

16:06:51.302 --> 16:06:52.942
Why so many 0s?

16:06:52.942 --> 16:06:54.832
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

16:06:54.832 --> 16:06:55.832
DAVID J. MALAN: Exactly.

16:06:55.832 --> 16:07:00.482
To summarize, I initialized the
count to 0 the first time I saw it,

16:07:00.482 --> 16:07:03.842
but I should have initialized it at
least to 1 because I just saw it.

16:07:03.842 --> 16:07:05.562
Or I should change my code a bit.

16:07:05.562 --> 16:07:08.222
So for instance, if I go back
in here, the simplest fix

16:07:08.222 --> 16:07:11.732
is probably to initialize to 1,
because on this iteration of the loop,

16:07:11.732 --> 16:07:14.552
obviously, I'm seeing this
title for the very first time.

16:07:14.552 --> 16:07:16.922
Or I could change my logic a little bit.

16:07:16.922 --> 16:07:18.812
I could do something like this instead.

16:07:18.812 --> 16:07:24.182
If the current title is not in titles,
then I could initialize it to 0.

16:07:24.182 --> 16:07:28.202
And then I could get rid of
the else, and now blindly index

16:07:28.202 --> 16:07:30.242
into the titles dictionary.

16:07:30.242 --> 16:07:34.442
Because now, on line 11, I
can trust that lines 9 and 10

16:07:34.442 --> 16:07:37.382
took care of the initialization
for me if need be.

16:07:37.382 --> 16:07:38.370
Which one is better?

16:07:38.370 --> 16:07:38.912
I don't know.

16:07:38.912 --> 16:07:42.492
This one's a little nicer, maybe
because it's one line fewer.

16:07:42.492 --> 16:07:45.812
But I think both approaches are
perfectly reasonable and well-designed.

16:07:45.812 --> 16:07:47.762
But the key thing, no
pun intended, is that we

16:07:47.762 --> 16:07:52.442
have to make sure the key exists
before we presume to actually incrue.

16:07:52.442 --> 16:07:54.152
Oh, this is wrong.

16:07:54.152 --> 16:07:55.712
This is incorrect code.

16:07:55.712 --> 16:07:58.382
What did I do wrong?

16:07:58.382 --> 16:07:59.132
OK, yes.

16:07:59.132 --> 16:07:59.742
There we go.

16:07:59.742 --> 16:08:03.032
So otherwise, everyone would have
liked this show once, and no matter

16:08:03.032 --> 16:08:04.532
how many people said the same thing.

16:08:04.532 --> 16:08:06.579
Now the code is as it should be.

16:08:06.579 --> 16:08:08.912
So let me go ahead and open
up my Terminal window again.

16:08:08.912 --> 16:08:13.052
Let me run Python of favorites.py,
and now we see more reasonable counts.

16:08:13.052 --> 16:08:14.492
Some shows weren't that popular.

16:08:14.492 --> 16:08:16.172
There's just 1s and maybe 2s.

16:08:16.172 --> 16:08:21.912
But I bet if we sort these things we
can start to see a little more detail.

16:08:21.912 --> 16:08:23.232
So how else can we do this?

16:08:23.232 --> 16:08:29.862
Well, turns out, when dealing
with a dictionary like this--

16:08:29.862 --> 16:08:32.502
let's go ahead and just
sort the titles themselves.

16:08:32.502 --> 16:08:37.472
So let's reintroduce the sorted function
as I did before, but no other changes.

16:08:37.472 --> 16:08:40.077
Let me go ahead now and
run Python of favorites.py.

16:08:40.077 --> 16:08:42.452
Now it's just a little easier
to wrap your mind around it

16:08:42.452 --> 16:08:43.910
because at least it's alphabetical.

16:08:43.910 --> 16:08:47.942
But it's not sorted by
value, it's sorted by key.

16:08:47.942 --> 16:08:51.512
But sure enough, if we scroll
down, there's something down here,

16:08:51.512 --> 16:08:54.544
for instance, like,
let's see, The Office.

16:08:54.544 --> 16:08:56.252
That's definitely
going to be a contender

16:08:56.252 --> 16:08:58.202
for most popular, 15 responses.

16:08:58.202 --> 16:09:01.202
But let's see what's actually
going to bubble up to the top.

16:09:01.202 --> 16:09:06.212
Unfortunately, the sorted function
only sorts dictionaries by keys

16:09:06.212 --> 16:09:09.752
by default, not by values.

16:09:09.752 --> 16:09:12.842
But it turns out, in Python,
if you read the documentation

16:09:12.842 --> 16:09:14.852
for the sorted function,
you can actually

16:09:14.852 --> 16:09:19.922
pass in other arguments that
tell it how to sort things.

16:09:19.922 --> 16:09:22.772
For instance, if I want to
do things in reverse order,

16:09:22.772 --> 16:09:27.482
I can add a second parameter to
the sorted function called reverse.

16:09:27.482 --> 16:09:28.652
And it's a named parameter.

16:09:28.652 --> 16:09:30.962
You literally say,
reverse equals true, so

16:09:30.962 --> 16:09:34.172
that the position of it in the
comma-separated list doesn't matter.

16:09:34.172 --> 16:09:37.104
If I now rerun this after
increasing my Terminal window,

16:09:37.104 --> 16:09:39.062
you'll see now that it's
in the opposite order.

16:09:39.062 --> 16:09:41.672
Now adventure and Anne
with an E is at the bottom

16:09:41.672 --> 16:09:43.752
of the output instead of the top.

16:09:43.752 --> 16:09:52.187
How can I tell it to sort
by values instead of by key?

16:09:52.187 --> 16:09:53.562
Well, let's go ahead and do this.

16:09:53.562 --> 16:09:56.282
Let me go ahead and define a function.

16:09:56.282 --> 16:09:58.412
I'm just going to call it
f to keep things simple.

16:09:58.412 --> 16:10:01.532
And this f function is going
to take a title as input.

16:10:01.532 --> 16:10:06.482
And given a given title, it's going
to return the value of that title.

16:10:06.482 --> 16:10:09.902
So actually, maybe a better name
for this would be get value,

16:10:09.902 --> 16:10:12.162
and/or we could come up
with something else as well.

16:10:12.162 --> 16:10:14.642
The purpose of the get
value function, to be clear,

16:10:14.642 --> 16:10:19.542
is to take it as input a title and
then return the corresponding value.

16:10:19.542 --> 16:10:20.802
Why is this useful?

16:10:20.802 --> 16:10:23.102
Well, it turns out that the
sorted function in Python,

16:10:23.102 --> 16:10:27.212
according to its documentation,
also takes a key parameter,

16:10:27.212 --> 16:10:31.202
where you can pass in, crazy
enough, the name of a function

16:10:31.202 --> 16:10:36.992
that it will use in order to determine
what it should sort by, by the key,

16:10:36.992 --> 16:10:41.892
or by the value, or in other cases,
even other types of data as well.

16:10:41.892 --> 16:10:44.732
So there's a curiosity here,
though, that's very deliberate.

16:10:44.732 --> 16:10:46.892
Key is the name of the
parameter, just like reverse

16:10:46.892 --> 16:10:48.434
was the name of this other parameter.

16:10:48.434 --> 16:10:51.452
The value of it, though,
is not a function call.

16:10:51.452 --> 16:10:52.922
It's a function name.

16:10:52.922 --> 16:10:55.862
Notice I am not doing
this, no parentheses.

16:10:55.862 --> 16:11:00.742
I'm instead passing in get value,
the function I wrote, by its name.

16:11:00.742 --> 16:11:03.242
And this is a feature of Python
and certain other languages.

16:11:03.242 --> 16:11:06.452
Just like variables, you can
actually pass whole functions

16:11:06.452 --> 16:11:10.902
around so that they can be called
for you later on by someone else.

16:11:10.902 --> 16:11:14.222
So what this means is that the
sorted function written by Python,

16:11:14.222 --> 16:11:16.752
they didn't know what you're
going to want to sort by today.

16:11:16.752 --> 16:11:21.152
But if you provide them with a function
called get value, or anything else, now

16:11:21.152 --> 16:11:23.342
their sorted function
will use that function

16:11:23.342 --> 16:11:27.182
to determine, OK, if you don't want to
sort by the key of the dictionary, what

16:11:27.182 --> 16:11:28.292
do you want to sort by?

16:11:28.292 --> 16:11:31.142
This is going to tell
it to sort by the value

16:11:31.142 --> 16:11:34.092
by returning the specific
value we care about.

16:11:34.092 --> 16:11:37.922
So let me go ahead now and rerun this
after increasing my Terminal, Python

16:11:37.922 --> 16:11:39.962
of favorites.py, Enter.

16:11:39.962 --> 16:11:42.452
Here we have now an example
of all of the titles you all

16:11:42.452 --> 16:11:47.522
typed in, albeit forced to uppercase
and with any whitespace thrown out.

16:11:47.522 --> 16:11:50.075
And now, The Office is
an easy win over Friends,

16:11:50.075 --> 16:11:52.742
versus Community, versus Game of
Thrones, Breaking Bad, and then

16:11:52.742 --> 16:11:55.082
a lot of variants thereafter.

16:11:55.082 --> 16:11:57.022
So there's a lot of steps to go through.

16:11:57.022 --> 16:11:58.897
This isn't that bad once
you've done it once,

16:11:58.897 --> 16:12:00.814
and you know what these
functions are, and you

16:12:00.814 --> 16:12:02.212
know that these parameters exist.

16:12:02.212 --> 16:12:03.352
But it's a lot of work.

16:12:03.352 --> 16:12:07.432
That's 17 lines of code
just to analyze a CSV file

16:12:07.432 --> 16:12:10.762
that you all created by way of
those Google Form submissions.

16:12:10.762 --> 16:12:13.702
But it took me a lot of work just
to get simple answers out of it.

16:12:13.702 --> 16:12:15.619
And indeed, that's going
to be among the goals

16:12:15.619 --> 16:12:18.262
for today, ultimately, is, how
can we just make this easier?

16:12:18.262 --> 16:12:20.137
It's one thing to learn
new things in Python,

16:12:20.137 --> 16:12:22.642
but if we can avoid writing
code, or this much code,

16:12:22.642 --> 16:12:24.182
that's going to be a good thing.

16:12:24.182 --> 16:12:26.362
And so one other technique
we can introduce here

16:12:26.362 --> 16:12:28.912
that does allow us to
write a little less code

16:12:28.912 --> 16:12:31.072
is, we can actually get
rid of this function.

16:12:31.072 --> 16:12:34.582
It turns out, in Python, if you
just need to make a function

16:12:34.582 --> 16:12:37.282
but it's going to be used and
then essentially thrown away,

16:12:37.282 --> 16:12:40.132
it's not something you're going
to be reusing in multiple places--

16:12:40.132 --> 16:12:42.892
it's not like a library function
that you want to keep around--

16:12:42.892 --> 16:12:45.022
you can actually just do this.

16:12:45.022 --> 16:12:48.772
You can change the value
of this key parameter

16:12:48.772 --> 16:12:51.292
to be what's called a
lambda function, which

16:12:51.292 --> 16:12:54.382
is a fancy way of saying a function
that technically has no name.

16:12:54.382 --> 16:12:56.212
It's an anonymous function.

16:12:56.212 --> 16:12:57.742
Why does it have no name?

16:12:57.742 --> 16:13:00.872
Well, it's kind of stupid that
I invented this name on line 13.

16:13:00.872 --> 16:13:04.012
I used it on line 16, and
then I never again used it.

16:13:04.012 --> 16:13:07.862
If there's only being used in one place,
why bother giving it a name at all?

16:13:07.862 --> 16:13:10.342
So if you instead, in
Python, say lambda,

16:13:10.342 --> 16:13:13.192
and then type out the
name of the parameter

16:13:13.192 --> 16:13:15.472
you want this anonymous
function to take,

16:13:15.472 --> 16:13:19.802
you can then say, go ahead
and return this value.

16:13:19.802 --> 16:13:22.372
Now let's notice the
inconsistencies here.

16:13:22.372 --> 16:13:25.192
When you use this special lambda
keyword that says, hey Python,

16:13:25.192 --> 16:13:28.192
give me an anonymous function,
a function with no name,

16:13:28.192 --> 16:13:31.952
it then says, Python, this anonymous
function will take one parameter.

16:13:31.952 --> 16:13:34.041
Notice there's no parentheses.

16:13:34.041 --> 16:13:35.842
And that's deliberate, if confusing.

16:13:35.842 --> 16:13:38.251
It just tightens things up a little bit.

16:13:38.251 --> 16:13:42.112
Notice that there's no return keyword,
which similarly tightens things

16:13:42.112 --> 16:13:44.122
up a bit, albeit inconsistently.

16:13:44.122 --> 16:13:47.992
But this line of code
I've just highlighted

16:13:47.992 --> 16:13:51.771
is actually identical in
functionality to this.

16:13:51.771 --> 16:13:53.662
But it throws away the word [INAUDIBLE].

16:13:53.662 --> 16:13:55.192
It throws away the word get value.

16:13:55.192 --> 16:13:58.852
It throws away the parentheses, and
it throws away the return keyword just

16:13:58.852 --> 16:14:00.021
to tighten things up.

16:14:00.021 --> 16:14:02.632
And it's well suited
for a problem like this

16:14:02.632 --> 16:14:05.302
where I just want to pass in
a tiny little function that

16:14:05.302 --> 16:14:06.432
does something useful.

16:14:06.432 --> 16:14:08.182
But it's not something
I'm going to reuse.

16:14:08.182 --> 16:14:10.442
It doesn't need multiple
lines to take up space.

16:14:10.442 --> 16:14:12.501
It's just a nice, elegant one liner.

16:14:12.501 --> 16:14:14.182
That's all a lambda function does.

16:14:14.182 --> 16:14:17.251
It allows you to create an anonymous
function right then and there.

16:14:17.251 --> 16:14:22.552
And then the function you're passing it
to, like sorted, will use it as before.

16:14:22.552 --> 16:14:26.152
Indeed, if I run Python of favorites.py
after growing my Terminal window,

16:14:26.152 --> 16:14:28.142
the result is exactly the same.

16:14:28.142 --> 16:14:31.702
