16:20:17.872 --> 16:20:22.492
But a feature most of you have probably
never noticed, or cared about, or used,

16:20:22.492 --> 16:20:24.832
is this thing called a
regular expression, where

16:20:24.832 --> 16:20:26.782
you can actually define a pattern.

16:20:26.782 --> 16:20:30.172
And I could actually reimplement that
same idea by doing something like this.

16:20:30.172 --> 16:20:36.412
I can say, let the user type in anything
represented by .star, then an at sign,

16:20:36.412 --> 16:20:41.942
then something else, then a
literal period, then, for instance,

16:20:41.942 --> 16:20:43.022
something else.

16:20:43.022 --> 16:20:45.292
So it's very cryptic,
admittedly, at first glance.

16:20:45.292 --> 16:20:48.772
But this means any
character 0 more times.

16:20:48.772 --> 16:20:51.502
This means any character 0 more times.

16:20:51.502 --> 16:20:54.067
This means a literal
period, because apparently

16:20:54.067 --> 16:20:57.502
dot means any character in
the context of these patterns.

16:20:57.502 --> 16:21:01.412
Then this thing means any
character 0 more times.

16:21:01.412 --> 16:21:04.012
So I should actually be
a little more nitpicky.

16:21:04.012 --> 16:21:06.872
You don't want 0 or more times,
you want 1 or more times.

16:21:06.872 --> 16:21:10.682
So this with the plus means
any character 1 or more time.

16:21:10.682 --> 16:21:12.362
So there has to be something there.

16:21:12.362 --> 16:21:16.972
And I think I want the same thing
here 1 or more times, 1 or more times.

16:21:16.972 --> 16:21:21.502
Or heck, if I want to restrict this
form in some sense to edu addresses,

16:21:21.502 --> 16:21:24.862
I could change that last
thing to literally .edu.

16:21:24.862 --> 16:21:26.912
And so long story short,
even though this looks,

16:21:26.912 --> 16:21:31.762
I'm sure, pretty cryptic, there's
this mini language built into Python,

16:21:31.762 --> 16:21:35.242
and JavaScript, and Java, and other
languages that allows you to express

16:21:35.242 --> 16:21:37.772
patterns in a standardized way.

16:21:37.772 --> 16:21:41.272
And this pattern is actually something
we can implement in code, too.

16:21:41.272 --> 16:21:43.492
And let me switch back to
Python for a second just

16:21:43.492 --> 16:21:45.262
to do the same kind of idea.

16:21:45.262 --> 16:21:48.292
Let me toggle back to my code here.

16:21:48.292 --> 16:21:52.072
Let me put up, for instance, a
summary of what it is you can do.

16:21:52.072 --> 16:21:58.372
And here's just a quick summary
of some of the available symbols.

16:21:58.372 --> 16:22:04.672
A period may represent any character.
.star or .asterisks means 0 or more

16:22:04.672 --> 16:22:05.312
characters.

16:22:05.312 --> 16:22:08.362
So the dot means anything,
so it can be A or nothing.

16:22:08.362 --> 16:22:09.502
It can be B or nothing.

16:22:09.502 --> 16:22:14.872
It can be A, B, A, B, C. It can be any
combination of 0 or more characters.

16:22:14.872 --> 16:22:18.202
Change that to a plus and you now
express one or more characters.

16:22:18.202 --> 16:22:21.202
Question mark means
something is optional.

16:22:21.202 --> 16:22:24.712
Caret symbol means start matching at
the beginning of the user's input.

16:22:24.712 --> 16:22:30.442
Dollar sign means stop matching
at the end of the user's input.

16:22:30.442 --> 16:22:32.552
So we won't play with
all of these just now.

16:22:32.552 --> 16:22:36.812
But let me go over here and
actually tackle this Office problem.

16:22:36.812 --> 16:22:40.792
Let me go ahead and import a new library
called the regular expression library,

16:22:40.792 --> 16:22:42.622
import re.

16:22:42.622 --> 16:22:45.902
And then, down here, let me say this.

16:22:45.902 --> 16:22:50.422
If re.search, this pattern.

16:22:50.422 --> 16:22:55.552
Let's just search for Office, quote,
unquote, in the current title.

16:22:55.552 --> 16:22:58.072
