16:25:53.512 --> 16:25:56.242
and uppercase the thing again.

16:25:56.242 --> 16:26:01.162
And now, inside of my loop, I
could say something like this.

16:26:01.162 --> 16:26:08.002
If the current row's title after
stripping whitespace and forcing

16:26:08.002 --> 16:26:12.262
it to uppercase, too, equals
the user's title, then, go ahead

16:26:12.262 --> 16:26:14.782
and maybe increment a counter.

16:26:14.782 --> 16:26:16.502
So I still need that counter back.

16:26:16.502 --> 16:26:21.952
So let me go ahead and define this
maybe in here, counter equals 0.

16:26:21.952 --> 16:26:24.062
And then, at the very
end of this program,

16:26:24.062 --> 16:26:26.392
let me go ahead and print
out just the popularity

16:26:26.392 --> 16:26:28.382
of whatever the human typed in.

16:26:28.382 --> 16:26:31.372
So again, the only difference is
I'm asking the human for some input

16:26:31.372 --> 16:26:32.062
this time.

16:26:32.062 --> 16:26:34.492
I'm initializing my
counter to 0, then I'm

16:26:34.492 --> 16:26:38.002
searching for their
title in the CSV file

16:26:38.002 --> 16:26:41.152
by doing the same massaging of the
data by forcing it to uppercase

16:26:41.152 --> 16:26:43.912
and getting rid of the whitespace.

16:26:43.912 --> 16:26:47.122
So now, when I run Python
of favorites.py, Enter,

16:26:47.122 --> 16:26:55.372
I could type in the office all lowercase
even, and now we're down to 13.

16:26:55.372 --> 16:27:00.282
13, why?

16:27:00.282 --> 16:27:02.042
Oh, that's correct.

16:27:02.042 --> 16:27:05.982
Because I'm the one that went in and
removed those The keywords a bit ago.

16:27:05.982 --> 16:27:08.372
If we fixed those, we
would be back up to 15.

16:27:08.372 --> 16:27:12.992
If we added support for The V
Office, we would be up to 16 as well.

16:27:12.992 --> 16:27:15.692
All right, any questions then
on these various manipulations?

16:27:15.692 --> 16:27:17.525
And if you're feeling
like, oh, my god, this

16:27:17.525 --> 16:27:20.442
is so much Python code just to do
simple things, that's the point.

16:27:20.442 --> 16:27:22.502
And indeed, even though
it's a powerful language

16:27:22.502 --> 16:27:26.012
and can solve these kinds of problems,
we had to write almost 20 lines of code

16:27:26.012 --> 16:27:28.812
just to ask a single question like this.

16:27:28.812 --> 16:27:32.462
But any questions on how we did
this, or on any of these building

16:27:32.462 --> 16:27:36.092
blocks along the way?

16:27:36.092 --> 16:27:37.232
Anything here?

16:27:37.232 --> 16:27:37.732
No?

16:27:37.732 --> 16:27:37.942
All right.

16:27:37.942 --> 16:27:38.642
That was a lot.

16:27:38.642 --> 16:27:40.142
Let's take a five-minute break here.

16:27:40.142 --> 16:27:42.572
When we come back, we'll do it better.

16:27:42.572 --> 16:27:43.822
So we are back.

16:27:43.822 --> 16:27:45.952
And the rest of today
is ultimately about, how

16:27:45.952 --> 16:27:50.182
can we store, and manipulate,
and change, and retrieve data

16:27:50.182 --> 16:27:53.432
more efficiently than we might
by just writing raw code?

16:27:53.432 --> 16:27:56.782
This isn't to say that you shouldn't
use Python to do the kinds of things

16:27:56.782 --> 16:27:57.622
that we just did.

16:27:57.622 --> 16:28:02.362
And in fact, it might be super common
if you're getting a lot of messy input

16:28:02.362 --> 16:28:04.415
from users that you might
want to clean it up.

16:28:04.415 --> 16:28:07.582
And maybe the best way to do that is
to write a program so that step-by-step

16:28:07.582 --> 16:28:09.712
you can make all of the
requisite changes and fixes

16:28:09.712 --> 16:28:12.865
like we did with The Office,
for instance, again and again,

16:28:12.865 --> 16:28:15.532
and reuse that code, especially
if more and more submissions are

16:28:15.532 --> 16:28:16.492
coming through.

16:28:16.492 --> 16:28:18.892
But another theme of
today, ultimately, is

16:28:18.892 --> 16:28:22.980
that sometimes there are different,
if not better tools for the same job.

16:28:22.980 --> 16:28:24.772
And in fact, now at
this point in the term,

16:28:24.772 --> 16:28:27.652
as we begin to introduce not
just Python, but in a moment

16:28:27.652 --> 16:28:31.462
a language called SQL, and next
week, a language called JavaScript,

16:28:31.462 --> 16:28:34.492
and the week after that, synthesizing
a whole lot of these languages

16:28:34.492 --> 16:28:37.762
together is to just kind
of paint a picture of how

16:28:37.762 --> 16:28:41.242
you might decide what the trade-offs are
between using this tool, or this tool,

16:28:41.242 --> 16:28:42.172
or this other tool.

16:28:42.172 --> 16:28:45.112
Because undoubtedly you can
solve problems moving forward

16:28:45.112 --> 16:28:48.002
in many different ways
with many different tools.

16:28:48.002 --> 16:28:50.362
So let's give you another
tool, one with which

16:28:50.362 --> 16:28:53.512
you can implement a proper
relational database.

16:28:53.512 --> 16:28:56.392
What we just saw in
the form of CSV files

16:28:56.392 --> 16:28:59.152
are what we might call
flat-file databases.

16:28:59.152 --> 16:29:02.842
Again, just a very simple file, flat
in that there's no hierarchy to it.

16:29:02.842 --> 16:29:04.612
It's just rows and columns.

16:29:04.612 --> 16:29:09.622
And that is all ultimately
storing ASCII or Unicode text.

16:29:09.622 --> 16:29:12.742
A relational database, though,
is something that's actually

16:29:12.742 --> 16:29:16.192
closer to a proper spreadsheet program.

16:29:16.192 --> 16:29:18.782
A CSV is an individual
sheet, if you will,

16:29:18.782 --> 16:29:20.602
from a spreadsheet when you export it.

16:29:20.602 --> 16:29:22.802
If you had multiple
sheets in a spreadsheet,

16:29:22.802 --> 16:29:24.937
you would have to export multiple CSVs.

16:29:24.937 --> 16:29:26.812
And that gets annoying
quickly in code if you

16:29:26.812 --> 16:29:29.332
have to open up this CSV,
this CSV, all of which

16:29:29.332 --> 16:29:32.422
represent different sheets or
tabs in a proper spreadsheet.

16:29:32.422 --> 16:29:36.862
A relational database is more
like a spreadsheet program

16:29:36.862 --> 16:29:39.982
that you, a programmer,
now can interact with.

16:29:39.982 --> 16:29:41.482
You can write data to it.

16:29:41.482 --> 16:29:45.022
You can read data from it, and you
can have multiple sheets, a.k.a.,

16:29:45.022 --> 16:29:47.492
tables storing all of your data.

16:29:47.492 --> 16:29:49.582
So whereas Excel and numbers
in Google spreadsheet

16:29:49.582 --> 16:29:52.432
are meant to be reused really by humans
with their mouse and their keyboard,

16:29:52.432 --> 16:29:55.192
clicking, and pointing, and
manipulating things graphically,

16:29:55.192 --> 16:29:57.502
a relational database
using a language called

16:29:57.502 --> 16:30:02.662
SQL is one in which the programmer
has similar capabilities,

16:30:02.662 --> 16:30:04.342
but doing so in code.

16:30:04.342 --> 16:30:08.062
Specifically, using a language
called SQL, and at a scale

16:30:08.062 --> 16:30:11.012
that's much grander
than spreadsheets alone.

16:30:11.012 --> 16:30:13.762
In fact, if you try on your Mac
or PC to open a spreadsheet that's

16:30:13.762 --> 16:30:16.432
got tens of thousands
of rows, it'll probably

16:30:16.432 --> 16:30:20.122
work fine, hundreds of thousands
of rows, millions of rows, no way.

16:30:20.122 --> 16:30:22.342
At some point your Mac or
PC is going to struggle

16:30:22.342 --> 16:30:24.472
to open particularly large data sets.

16:30:24.472 --> 16:30:26.962
And that, too, is where
proper databases come

16:30:26.962 --> 16:30:29.482
into play and proper
languages for databases come

16:30:29.482 --> 16:30:31.462
into play, when it's all about scale.

16:30:31.462 --> 16:30:34.732
And indeed, most any mobile app or
web app today that you or someone else

16:30:34.732 --> 16:30:38.762
might write should probably plan
on lots of data if it's successful.

16:30:38.762 --> 16:30:41.072
So we need the right
tools for that problem.

16:30:41.072 --> 16:30:44.452
So fortunately, even though we're
about to learn yet another language,

16:30:44.452 --> 16:30:49.702
it only does four things fundamentally,
known by this silly acronym, CRUD.

16:30:49.702 --> 16:30:53.212
SQL, this language for
databases, supports the ability

16:30:53.212 --> 16:30:57.742
to create data, read data,
update data, and delete data.

16:30:57.742 --> 16:30:58.762
That's it.

16:30:58.762 --> 16:31:02.032
There's a few more keywords that
exist in this language called SQL

16:31:02.032 --> 16:31:03.092
that we'll soon see.

16:31:03.092 --> 16:31:04.800
But at the end of the
day, even if you're

16:31:04.800 --> 16:31:07.522
starting to feel like this
is a lot very quickly,

16:31:07.522 --> 16:31:10.282
it all boils down to these
four basic operations.

16:31:10.282 --> 16:31:12.982
And the four commands
in SQL, if you will,

16:31:12.982 --> 16:31:17.122
functions in a sense that implement
those four ideas happen to be these.

16:31:17.122 --> 16:31:19.612
They're almost the same but
with some slight variance.

16:31:19.612 --> 16:31:24.622
The ability to create or insert data
is the C. The ability to select data

16:31:24.622 --> 16:31:26.422
is the R, or read.

16:31:26.422 --> 16:31:27.472
Update is the same.

16:31:27.472 --> 16:31:30.390
Delete is the same, but drop
is also a keyword as well.

16:31:30.390 --> 16:31:32.182
So we'll see these and
a few other keywords

16:31:32.182 --> 16:31:35.752
in SQL that, at the end of the day, just
allow you to create, read, and update

16:31:35.752 --> 16:31:39.652
data using verbs, if
you will, like these.

16:31:39.652 --> 16:31:43.372
So to do that, what's
the syntax going to be?

16:31:43.372 --> 16:31:45.632
Well, we won't get into the
weeds too quickly on this.

16:31:45.632 --> 16:31:47.992
But here's a representative
syntax of how

16:31:47.992 --> 16:31:51.052
you can create using this
language called SQL, in your very

16:31:51.052 --> 16:31:53.362
own database, a brand new table.

16:31:53.362 --> 16:31:56.252
This is so easy in Excel, and Google
Spreadsheets, and Apple Numbers.

16:31:56.252 --> 16:31:58.252
You want a new sheet, you
click the plus button.

16:31:58.252 --> 16:31:59.032
You get a new tab.

16:31:59.032 --> 16:32:00.832
You give it a name,
and boom, you're done.

16:32:00.832 --> 16:32:05.392
In the world of programming, though, if
you want to create the analogue of that

16:32:05.392 --> 16:32:08.782
spreadsheet in the computer's memory,
you create something called a table,

16:32:08.782 --> 16:32:13.762
like a sheet, that has a name, and then
in parentheses has one or more columns.

16:32:13.762 --> 16:32:17.332
But unlike Google Spreadsheets,
and Apple Numbers, and Excel,

16:32:17.332 --> 16:32:20.415
you have to decide as the
programmer what types of data

16:32:20.415 --> 16:32:22.582
you're going to be storing
in each of these columns.

16:32:22.582 --> 16:32:24.772
Now even though Excel,
and Google Spreadsheets,

16:32:24.772 --> 16:32:28.652
and Numbers does allow you to format
or present data in different ways,

16:32:28.652 --> 16:32:33.022
it's not strongly typed data like it
is, for instance, when we were using C.

16:32:33.022 --> 16:32:35.542
And heck, even in Python
there's underlying data types.

16:32:35.542 --> 16:32:37.500
Even if you don't have
to type them explicitly,

16:32:37.500 --> 16:32:40.242
databases are going to want to
know, are you storing integers?

16:32:40.242 --> 16:32:41.982
Are you storing real numbers or floats?

16:32:41.982 --> 16:32:42.972
Are you storing text?

16:32:42.972 --> 16:32:43.482
Why?

16:32:43.482 --> 16:32:46.302
Because especially as your
data scales, the more hints

16:32:46.302 --> 16:32:49.752
you give the database about your
data, the more performance it can be,

16:32:49.752 --> 16:32:52.842
the faster it can help you
get at and store that data.

16:32:52.842 --> 16:32:54.644
So types are about to
be important again,

16:32:54.644 --> 16:32:57.102
but there's not going to be
that many of them, fortunately.

16:32:57.102 --> 16:32:59.982
Now how can I go about converting,
for instance, some real data,

16:32:59.982 --> 16:33:02.832
like that from you,
my favorites.csv file,

16:33:02.832 --> 16:33:04.782
into a proper relational database?

16:33:04.782 --> 16:33:07.992
Well, it turns out that
using SQL I can do this

16:33:07.992 --> 16:33:10.602
in VS Code on my own Mac,
or PC, or in the cloud

16:33:10.602 --> 16:33:13.797
here by just importing
the CSV into a database.

16:33:13.797 --> 16:33:15.672
We'll see eventually
how to do this manually.

16:33:15.672 --> 16:33:17.964
For now, I'm going to use
more of an automated process.

16:33:17.964 --> 16:33:20.022
So let me go over to VS Code here.

16:33:20.022 --> 16:33:22.512
Let me type ls to see
where we left off before.

16:33:22.512 --> 16:33:26.347
I had two files favorites.csv, which
I downloaded from Google Spreadsheets.

16:33:26.347 --> 16:33:27.972
Recall that I made a couple of changes.

16:33:27.972 --> 16:33:31.392
We deleted a couple of Thes
from the file for The Office.

16:33:31.392 --> 16:33:33.942
But this is the same file
as before, and then we

16:33:33.942 --> 16:33:36.552
have favorites.py, which
we'll set aside for now.

16:33:36.552 --> 16:33:40.212
I'm going to go ahead now
and run a command SQLite3.

16:33:40.212 --> 16:33:43.362
So in the world of
relational databases, there's

16:33:43.362 --> 16:33:48.372
many different products out there,
many different software that

16:33:48.372 --> 16:33:50.712
implements the SQL language.

16:33:50.712 --> 16:33:51.942
Microsoft has their own.

16:33:51.942 --> 16:33:55.422
There's something called MySQL
that's been very popular for years.

16:33:55.422 --> 16:33:57.462
Facebook, for instance,
used it early on.

16:33:57.462 --> 16:34:00.372
PostgreSQL, Microsoft
Access Server, Oracle,

16:34:00.372 --> 16:34:02.300
and maybe a whole bunch
of other product names

16:34:02.300 --> 16:34:04.092
you might have encountered
over time, which

16:34:04.092 --> 16:34:08.322
is to say there's many different
types of tools, and servers,

16:34:08.322 --> 16:34:10.332
and software in which you can use SQL.

16:34:10.332 --> 16:34:13.122
We're going to use a very lightweight
version of the SQL language

16:34:13.122 --> 16:34:14.712
today called SQLite.

16:34:14.712 --> 16:34:17.022
This is the version of
SQL that's generally

16:34:17.022 --> 16:34:19.362
used on iPhones and
Android devices these days.

16:34:19.362 --> 16:34:22.272
If you download an app that stores
data like your own contacts,

16:34:22.272 --> 16:34:24.342
typically is stored using SQLite.

16:34:24.342 --> 16:34:28.052
Because it's fairly lightweight,
but you can still store hundreds,

16:34:28.052 --> 16:34:31.152
thousands, even tens of
thousands of pieces of data

16:34:31.152 --> 16:34:33.312
even using this lightweight
version thereof.

16:34:33.312 --> 16:34:36.132
SQLite3 is like version 3 of this tool.

16:34:36.132 --> 16:34:41.682
We're going to go ahead and run SQLite3
with a file called favorites.db.

16:34:41.682 --> 16:34:45.462
It's conventional in the world of
SQLite to name your file something.db.

16:34:45.462 --> 16:34:47.832
I'm going to create a
database called favorites.db.

16:34:47.832 --> 16:34:52.352
Once I'm inside of the program, now I'm
going to go ahead and enter CSV Mode.

16:34:52.352 --> 16:34:54.102
Again, not something
you have to memorize,

16:34:54.102 --> 16:34:55.810
just something you
can look up as needed.

16:34:55.810 --> 16:34:59.442
And then, I'm going to
import favorites.csv

16:34:59.442 --> 16:35:05.602
into a table, that is, a sheet, if
you will, called favorites as well.

16:35:05.602 --> 16:35:09.372
Now I'm going to hit Enter and I'm
going to go ahead and exit the program

16:35:09.372 --> 16:35:10.992
altogether and type ls.

16:35:10.992 --> 16:35:13.512
Now I have three files
in my current directory--

16:35:13.512 --> 16:35:17.472
the CSV file, the Python file
from before, and now favorites.db.

16:35:17.472 --> 16:35:21.522
But if I did this right, all of the
data you all typed into the CSV file

16:35:21.522 --> 16:35:25.182
has now been loaded into a proper
database where I can now use

16:35:25.182 --> 16:35:28.522
this SQL language to access it instead.

16:35:28.522 --> 16:35:33.072
So let's go ahead again and run SQLite3
of favorites.db, which now exists.

16:35:33.072 --> 16:35:35.982
And now, at the SQLite
prompt I can start

16:35:35.982 --> 16:35:38.502
to play around and
see what this data is.

16:35:38.502 --> 16:35:41.952
For instance, I can
look, by typing .schema,

16:35:41.952 --> 16:35:44.704
at what the schema is of
my data, what's the design.

16:35:44.704 --> 16:35:47.412
Now no thought was put into the
design of this data at the moment

16:35:47.412 --> 16:35:49.242
because I automated the whole process.

16:35:49.242 --> 16:35:52.092
Once we start creating
our own databases we'll

16:35:52.092 --> 16:35:55.092
give more thought to the data
types and the columns that we have.

16:35:55.092 --> 16:35:59.562
But we can see what SQLite
presumed I wanted just

16:35:59.562 --> 16:36:01.872
by importing the data by default.

16:36:01.872 --> 16:36:06.462
What the import command did for me a
moment ago is essentially the syntax.

16:36:06.462 --> 16:36:09.852
It automated the process of creating
a table, if it doesn't exist,

16:36:09.852 --> 16:36:11.112
called favorites.

16:36:11.112 --> 16:36:14.322
And then notice, in parentheses
it gave me three columns--

16:36:14.322 --> 16:36:18.702
timestamp, title, and genres, which
were inferred, obviously, from the CSV.

16:36:18.702 --> 16:36:21.342
All three of which have
been decreed to be text.

16:36:21.342 --> 16:36:24.522
Again, once we're more comfortable
we'll create our own tables,

16:36:24.522 --> 16:36:26.352
choose our own types and column names.

16:36:26.352 --> 16:36:28.692
But for now, I just automated
the whole process just

16:36:28.692 --> 16:36:33.462
to get us started by using this
built-in import command as well.

16:36:33.462 --> 16:36:34.152
All right.

16:36:34.152 --> 16:36:36.972
So what now can I begin to do?

16:36:36.972 --> 16:36:42.252
Well, if I wanted to, for instance,
start playing around with data therein,

16:36:42.252 --> 16:36:44.937
I might execute a couple
of different commands.

16:36:48.342 --> 16:36:53.763
Let me find the right one here--
one of which would be select.

16:36:53.763 --> 16:36:56.952
Select being one of our
most versatile tools

16:36:56.952 --> 16:36:58.522
to select data from this database.

16:36:58.522 --> 16:37:01.062
So if I have these three
columns here-- timestamp,

16:37:01.062 --> 16:37:04.362
title, and genres, suppose I
want to select all of the titles.

16:37:04.362 --> 16:37:09.132
Doing that earlier in Python
required importing the CSV library,

16:37:09.132 --> 16:37:14.082
opening the file, creating a reader or
a DictReader, iterating over every row,

16:37:14.082 --> 16:37:16.842
adding every title to a dictionary
or just printing it out,

16:37:16.842 --> 16:37:17.652
and dot, dot, dot.

16:37:17.652 --> 16:37:20.512
There was a dozen or so lines
of code when we first began.

16:37:20.512 --> 16:37:22.182
Now, how about this?

16:37:22.182 --> 16:37:26.562
Select title from
favorites, semicolon, done.

16:37:26.562 --> 16:37:30.912
So now, with this particular
language, the output is very textual

16:37:30.912 --> 16:37:34.272
and it's simulating what it looks like
if it were more graphical by creating

16:37:34.272 --> 16:37:36.212
this table, so to speak.

16:37:36.212 --> 16:37:39.422
Select title from
favorites is a distillation

16:37:39.422 --> 16:37:42.872
in a different language called
SQL of all the lines of code

16:37:42.872 --> 16:37:46.082
I wrote early on when we first
started playing with favorites.py.

16:37:46.082 --> 16:37:50.882
SQL is therefore optimized for
reading, and creating, and updating,

16:37:50.882 --> 16:37:52.842
and ultimately, deleting data.

16:37:52.842 --> 16:37:56.042
So here's perhaps a better tool
for the job once you have the data.

16:37:56.042 --> 16:37:59.372
Tossing it into a more
powerful, versatile format

16:37:59.372 --> 16:38:02.570
might allow you now to get
more work done more quickly

16:38:02.570 --> 16:38:04.112
without having to reinvent the wheel.

16:38:04.112 --> 16:38:06.852
Someone else has figured out
how to select data like this.

16:38:06.852 --> 16:38:09.102
What more can I do here?

16:38:09.102 --> 16:38:12.392
Well, let me go ahead and pull
up, in a moment, just a little bit

16:38:12.392 --> 16:38:14.732
of a cheat sheet here.

16:38:14.732 --> 16:38:19.182
Give me one second to find this.

16:38:19.182 --> 16:38:23.432
So suppose I want to now select
data a little more powerfully.

16:38:23.432 --> 16:38:25.562
So here's what I just
did in a canonical way.

16:38:25.562 --> 16:38:27.062
So select typically works like this.

16:38:27.062 --> 16:38:31.202
You select columns from a
specific table, semicolon.

16:38:31.202 --> 16:38:33.602
Unfortunately, stupid
semicolons are back.

16:38:33.602 --> 16:38:38.052
Select columns from table then, is
the generic form of what I just did.

16:38:38.052 --> 16:38:42.464
More specifically, I selected one
column called title from favorites.

16:38:42.464 --> 16:38:43.922
Favorites is the name of the table.

16:38:43.922 --> 16:38:45.032
Semicolon ends my thought.

16:38:45.032 --> 16:38:48.782
Suppose I wanted to get two things, like
the genres that each of you inputted.

16:38:48.782 --> 16:38:53.762
I could instead do select title,
comma, genres from favorites,

16:38:53.762 --> 16:38:55.562
and then, a semicolon, and Enter.

16:38:55.562 --> 16:38:57.452
It's going to look a
little ugly on my screen

16:38:57.452 --> 16:38:59.012
because some of these titles and--

16:38:59.012 --> 16:39:02.642
OK, one of you really went
all out with Community.

16:39:02.642 --> 16:39:06.002
You can see that it's just
wrapping in an ugly way,

16:39:06.002 --> 16:39:08.642
but it's just now
showing me two columns.

16:39:08.642 --> 16:39:12.182
If we scroll up to the very top
again, the left most of one,

16:39:12.182 --> 16:39:13.622
Black Mirror went all out, too.

16:39:13.622 --> 16:39:14.492
Thank you.

16:39:14.492 --> 16:39:17.342
And now, OK, we're going to
have to clean some of these up.

16:39:17.342 --> 16:39:19.457
Game of Thrones, good comedy, yes.

16:39:22.892 --> 16:39:24.822
Keep going, keep going, keep going.

16:39:24.822 --> 16:39:28.212
So now we've selected two of
the columns that we care about.

16:39:28.212 --> 16:39:28.712
There it is.

16:39:28.712 --> 16:39:31.722
OK, so it's crazy wide because
of all of those genres.

16:39:31.722 --> 16:39:34.477
But it allows me to select
exactly the data I want.

16:39:34.477 --> 16:39:37.352
Let's go back to the titles, though,
and perhaps start playing around

16:39:37.352 --> 16:39:39.092
with some modifiers here.

16:39:39.092 --> 16:39:43.652
For instance, it turns out, using
SQL there's a lot of functionality

16:39:43.652 --> 16:39:45.242
built into the language.

16:39:45.242 --> 16:39:48.185
You've got a lot of functions, similar
to Excel or Google Spreadsheets

16:39:48.185 --> 16:39:49.352
where you can have formulas.

16:39:49.352 --> 16:39:51.662
SQL provides you with some
of the same heuristics that

16:39:51.662 --> 16:39:55.692
allow you to apply operations
like these on entire columns.

16:39:55.692 --> 16:39:58.262
For instance, you can take
averages, count the total,

16:39:58.262 --> 16:40:01.352
get the distinct values, force
things to lowercase, uppercase, min,

16:40:01.352 --> 16:40:02.562
and max, and so forth.

16:40:02.562 --> 16:40:04.952
So let's try distinct, for instance.

16:40:04.952 --> 16:40:08.792
Let me go back to my Terminal,
and let's say, select,

16:40:08.792 --> 16:40:14.102
how about the distinct titles
from the favorites table?

16:40:14.102 --> 16:40:14.855
Enter.

16:40:14.855 --> 16:40:16.772
I didn't bother selecting
the genres because I

16:40:16.772 --> 16:40:18.105
want it to be a little prettier.

16:40:18.105 --> 16:40:23.432
And you can see here that we
have just the distinct titles,

16:40:23.432 --> 16:40:25.889
except for issues of formatting.

16:40:25.889 --> 16:40:27.722
So whitespace is going
to be an issue again.

16:40:27.722 --> 16:40:29.555
Capitalization is going
to be a thing again.

16:40:29.555 --> 16:40:30.572
So there's a trade-off.

16:40:30.572 --> 16:40:34.622
One of the things I was doing in Python
was forcing everything to uppercase

16:40:34.622 --> 16:40:36.272
and then getting rid of whitespace.

16:40:36.272 --> 16:40:37.742
But we could combine some of these.

16:40:37.742 --> 16:40:40.682
I could do something like
force every title to uppercase,

16:40:40.682 --> 16:40:41.849
then get the distinct value.

16:40:41.849 --> 16:40:44.724
And that's actually going to get
rid of some of those values as well.

16:40:44.724 --> 16:40:47.202
And again, I did it all in
one simple line that was fast.

16:40:47.202 --> 16:40:49.369
So let me pull up at the
bottom of the screen again.

16:40:49.369 --> 16:40:53.432
I selected distinct upper
titles from favorites,

16:40:53.432 --> 16:40:56.412
and that did everything for
me at once in just one breath.

16:40:56.412 --> 16:40:58.982
Suppose I want to get the total
number of counts of titles.

16:40:58.982 --> 16:41:05.492
How about select count of all
of those titles from favorites?

16:41:05.492 --> 16:41:09.332
Semicolon, Enter, and now
you get back a mini table

16:41:09.332 --> 16:41:13.302
that contains just your
answer, 158 in this case.

16:41:13.302 --> 16:41:15.902
So that's the total
number of, not distinct,

16:41:15.902 --> 16:41:18.032
but total titles that
we had in the file.

16:41:18.032 --> 16:41:21.902
And we could continue to manipulate
the data further using, again,

16:41:21.902 --> 16:41:23.792
functions like these here.

16:41:23.792 --> 16:41:26.892
But there's also additional
filtration we can do.

16:41:26.892 --> 16:41:32.352
We can also qualify our selections by
saying where some condition is true.

16:41:32.352 --> 16:41:35.972
So just as in Scratch, and C, and
Python, you have Boolean expressions,

16:41:35.972 --> 16:41:41.612
you can have the same in SQL as well,
where I can filter my data where

16:41:41.612 --> 16:41:44.732
something is true or false.

16:41:44.732 --> 16:41:46.682
Like allows me to do approximations.

16:41:46.682 --> 16:41:48.842
If I want to get something
that's like The Office

16:41:48.842 --> 16:41:51.662
but not necessarily
T-H-E, space, Office,

16:41:51.662 --> 16:41:54.782
I could do pattern
matching using like here.

16:41:54.782 --> 16:41:58.512
Order by, limit, and grouped by are
other commands I can execute, too.

16:41:58.512 --> 16:42:01.322
So let me go back and do
a couple of these here.

16:42:01.322 --> 16:42:07.952
How about, let me just get, oh, I don't
know, all of the titles from favorites

16:42:07.952 --> 16:42:10.190
but limit it to 10 results.

16:42:10.190 --> 16:42:13.232
That might be one thing that's helpful
to see if you just care about some

16:42:13.232 --> 16:42:15.452
of the data at the top there instead.

16:42:15.452 --> 16:42:21.872
How about, select all of the titles
from favorites, where the title itself

16:42:21.872 --> 16:42:25.052
is like, quote, unquote, "Office?"

16:42:25.052 --> 16:42:28.082
And this will give me only two answers.

16:42:28.082 --> 16:42:32.522
Those are the two rows, recall, that I
mutated by getting rid of the word The.

16:42:32.522 --> 16:42:37.222
Notice that like allows me too
tolerate uppercase and lowercase.

16:42:37.222 --> 16:42:40.222
Because if I instead
just use the equal sign,

16:42:40.222 --> 16:42:46.402
and in SQL a single equal sign
does, in fact, mean equality.

16:42:46.402 --> 16:42:48.712
For comparison's sake,
it's not doing assignment.

16:42:48.712 --> 16:42:51.412
This is not how you assign data in SQL.

16:42:51.412 --> 16:42:53.282
I got back no answers there.

16:42:53.282 --> 16:42:56.962
So indeed, the equal sign
is giving me literal answers

16:42:56.962 --> 16:42:59.332
that searches just for what I typed in.

16:42:59.332 --> 16:43:00.712
How could I get all of these?

16:43:00.712 --> 16:43:04.432
Well, similar in spirit to regular
expressions but not quite as powerful

16:43:04.432 --> 16:43:06.962
in SQL, I could do something like this.

16:43:06.962 --> 16:43:10.672
I can select the title from favorites
where the title is like, quote,

16:43:10.672 --> 16:43:12.052
unquote, "Office."

16:43:12.052 --> 16:43:17.792
But I can add, a bit weirdly, percent
signs to the left and the right.

16:43:17.792 --> 16:43:23.272
So the language SQL supports the
same notion of pattern matching

16:43:23.272 --> 16:43:24.889
but much more limited out of the box.

16:43:24.889 --> 16:43:26.722
If we want more powerful
regular expressions

16:43:26.722 --> 16:43:28.762
we probably do want
to use Python instead.

16:43:28.762 --> 16:43:32.062
But the percent sign here
means 0 or more characters

16:43:32.062 --> 16:43:34.682
on the left, 0 or more
characters on the right.

16:43:34.682 --> 16:43:39.802
So this will just grab any title that
contains O-F-F-I-C-E in it in that

16:43:39.802 --> 16:43:40.442
order.

16:43:40.442 --> 16:43:44.632
And now I get all 16, it would
seem, of those results, again.

16:43:44.632 --> 16:43:45.832
How do I know it's 16?

16:43:45.832 --> 16:43:48.502
Well, I can just get the
count of those titles

16:43:48.502 --> 16:43:51.482
and get back that
answer instead as well.

16:43:51.482 --> 16:43:54.982
So again, it takes some
getting used to, the vocabulary

16:43:54.982 --> 16:43:56.315
and the syntax that you can use.

16:43:56.315 --> 16:43:58.024
There's these building
blocks and others.

16:43:58.024 --> 16:44:00.842
But SQL is really designed, again,
for creating, reading, updating,

16:44:00.842 --> 16:44:01.822
and deleting data.

16:44:01.822 --> 16:44:06.422
For instance, I've never really
been a fan of Friends, for instance.

16:44:06.422 --> 16:44:12.202
So right now if I do select,
how about title from favorites

16:44:12.202 --> 16:44:18.682
where title like, quote, unquote,
Friends with the percent signs?

16:44:18.682 --> 16:44:20.672
We can see that there's
a whole bunch of them.

16:44:20.672 --> 16:44:21.862
That's how many exactly.

16:44:21.862 --> 16:44:23.302
Let's just do a quick count.

16:44:23.302 --> 16:44:25.232
So that's nine of them.

16:44:25.232 --> 16:44:28.642
Well, delete from favorites.

16:44:28.642 --> 16:44:35.842
OK, you and me, delete from favorites,
where title like Friends, Enter.

16:44:35.842 --> 16:44:39.622
Nothing seems to happen,
but bye-bye Friends.

16:44:39.622 --> 16:44:40.402
[APPLAUSE]

16:44:40.402 --> 16:44:41.485
DAVID J. MALAN: Thank you.

16:44:44.452 --> 16:44:46.732
So now we've actually changed the data.

16:44:46.732 --> 16:44:50.452
And this is what's compelling
about a proper database.

16:44:50.452 --> 16:44:54.662
Yes, you could technically write Python
code that not only reads the CSV file,

16:44:54.662 --> 16:44:55.882
but also writes it.

16:44:55.882 --> 16:44:58.162
You can change using quote,
unquote, "A" for append,

16:44:58.162 --> 16:45:01.252
or quote, unquote, "W" for
write, instead of quote, unquote,

16:45:01.252 --> 16:45:02.605
"R" for read alone.

16:45:02.605 --> 16:45:05.272
But it's definitely a little more
involved to do that in Python.

16:45:05.272 --> 16:45:07.592
But with SQL, you can update
the data in real time.

16:45:07.592 --> 16:45:11.092
And if I were actually running a
web application here or a database

16:45:11.092 --> 16:45:13.222
for a mobile app, that
change, theoretically,

16:45:13.222 --> 16:45:15.502
would be reflected everywhere
on your own devices

16:45:15.502 --> 16:45:17.552
if you're somehow talking
to this application.

16:45:17.552 --> 16:45:19.337
So that's the direction we're headed.

16:45:19.337 --> 16:45:20.962
This other thing has been bothering me.

16:45:20.962 --> 16:45:27.982
So select, how about title from
favorites, where title equals,

16:45:27.982 --> 16:45:28.522
what was it?

16:45:28.522 --> 16:45:31.942
The V Office, was it?

16:45:31.942 --> 16:45:33.022
Yeah, it was that one.

16:45:33.022 --> 16:45:37.492
How about we update
favorites by setting title

16:45:37.492 --> 16:45:44.422
equal to The Office, where title
equals quote, unquote, "The V Office"

16:45:44.422 --> 16:45:45.812
semicolon?

16:45:45.812 --> 16:45:47.992
And now, if I select
the same thing again

16:45:47.992 --> 16:45:50.152
I can go up and down with
my arrow keys quickly.

16:45:50.152 --> 16:45:52.432
Now there is no The V Office.

16:45:52.432 --> 16:45:54.542
We've actually changed that value.

16:45:54.542 --> 16:45:55.642
How about genres?

16:45:55.642 --> 16:46:01.222
Select genres from favorites,
where the title is title

16:46:01.222 --> 16:46:04.792
equals Game of Thrones, semicolon.

16:46:04.792 --> 16:46:08.342
These were kind of long, and I
don't really agree with all of that.

16:46:08.342 --> 16:46:14.902
So how about we update favorites,
set genres equal to, sure,

16:46:14.902 --> 16:46:17.992
action, adventure, sure, drama?

16:46:17.992 --> 16:46:19.732
OK, so it's a decent list.

16:46:19.732 --> 16:46:22.042
Fantasy, sure, thriller, war.

16:46:22.042 --> 16:46:26.392
OK, anything really but
comedy, I would say.

16:46:26.392 --> 16:46:28.502
Let's go ahead and hit Enter now.

16:46:28.502 --> 16:46:33.142
And now, if I select genres again, same
query, now we've canonicalized that.

16:46:33.142 --> 16:46:34.502
We've thrown data away.

16:46:34.502 --> 16:46:36.592
So whether or not that
is right is probably

16:46:36.592 --> 16:46:38.362
a bit subjective and argumentative.

16:46:38.362 --> 16:46:42.262
But I have at least cleaned up my
data, which is, again, the U in CRUD.

16:46:42.262 --> 16:46:46.012
Create, read, update, delete,
you can do it that easily.

16:46:46.012 --> 16:46:47.632
Beware using delete.

16:46:47.632 --> 16:46:51.772
Beware worse using drop, whereby
you can drop an entire table.

16:46:51.772 --> 16:46:54.652
But via these kinds of
commands, can we actually now

16:46:54.652 --> 16:46:58.732
manipulate our data much more
rapidly and with single thoughts.

16:46:58.732 --> 16:47:01.732
And in fact, if you're an aspiring
statistician, or data scientist,

16:47:01.732 --> 16:47:05.662
or analyst in the real world, SQL
is such a commonly used language

16:47:05.662 --> 16:47:08.915
because it allows you to really
dive into data quickly, and ask

16:47:08.915 --> 16:47:11.332
questions of the data, and get
back answers quite quickly.

16:47:11.332 --> 16:47:12.872
And this is a simple data set.

16:47:12.872 --> 16:47:17.182
You can do this with much larger
data sets as we soon will, too.

16:47:17.182 --> 16:47:20.392
Or any questions on what
we've seen of SQL thus far?

16:47:20.392 --> 16:47:22.512
Only scratched the
surface, but again, it

16:47:22.512 --> 16:47:28.442
boils down to creating, reading,
updating, and deleting data.

16:47:28.442 --> 16:47:30.252
Questions here?

16:47:30.252 --> 16:47:30.752
All right.

16:47:30.752 --> 16:47:33.162
Well, let's consider
the design of this data.

16:47:33.162 --> 16:47:37.242
Recall that if I do .schema, that
shows me the design of my table,

16:47:37.242 --> 16:47:39.332
the so-called schema of my data.

16:47:39.332 --> 16:47:40.352
This is OK.

16:47:40.352 --> 16:47:42.991
It gets the job done, and frankly,
everything the user typed in

16:47:42.991 --> 16:47:46.951
was arguably text, including the
timestamp, which is the date and time.

16:47:46.951 --> 16:47:49.382
But so the data set
itself is somewhat simple.

16:47:49.382 --> 16:47:54.911
But if we look at the data set itself,
especially genres, let's do this.

16:47:54.911 --> 16:47:57.302
Select genres from favorites.

16:47:57.302 --> 16:47:59.882
And let me point out one other
thing stylistically, too.

16:47:59.882 --> 16:48:04.392
I am very deliberately capitalizing
all of the special SQL keywords,

16:48:04.392 --> 16:48:08.042
and I'm lowercasing all of the
column names and the table names.

16:48:08.042 --> 16:48:11.102
This is a convention, and
honestly, it just helps you read,

16:48:11.102 --> 16:48:14.552
I think, the code when you're
co-mingling your names for columns

16:48:14.552 --> 16:48:17.942
and tables with proper SQL keywords.

16:48:17.942 --> 16:48:23.371
But I could just as easily do
select genres from favorites,

16:48:23.371 --> 16:48:26.491
but again, the SQL specific keywords
don't quite jump out as much.

16:48:26.491 --> 16:48:29.582
So stylistically, we would
recommend this, selecting genres

16:48:29.582 --> 16:48:31.592
from favorites, semicolon.

16:48:31.592 --> 16:48:34.712
So here is where--

16:48:34.712 --> 16:48:36.902
oh.

16:48:36.902 --> 16:48:38.192
OK, that was not intended.

16:48:38.192 --> 16:48:40.562
