16:49:14.422 --> 16:49:17.272
with a SQL database, so
the best we can do here

16:49:17.272 --> 16:49:21.592
is, let's actually get
rid of favorites.db.

16:49:21.592 --> 16:49:27.682
Let's run SQLite of favorites.db
again, which now will be recreated.

16:49:27.682 --> 16:49:29.782
Let me change myself into CSV mode.

16:49:29.782 --> 16:49:35.272
Let me import, into my
favorites table, the CSV file.

16:49:35.272 --> 16:49:39.592
And now, Friends is back,
for better or for worse,

16:49:39.592 --> 16:49:40.852
but so are all of our genres.

16:49:43.432 --> 16:49:46.672
If I now reload the file
and do select, star, from--

16:49:46.672 --> 16:49:47.182
sorry.

16:49:47.182 --> 16:49:51.180
Select genres from favorites,
that was the result I was getting.

16:49:51.180 --> 16:49:53.972
It's much messier, but that's
because some of these are quite long.

16:49:53.972 --> 16:49:55.639
But now we're back to the original data.

16:49:55.639 --> 16:49:58.042
Lesson here, be sure
to back up your work.

16:49:58.042 --> 16:49:58.582
All right.

16:49:58.582 --> 16:50:02.192
So what more can we
now do with this data?

16:50:02.192 --> 16:50:05.822
Well, I don't love the design of the
genres table for a couple of reasons.

16:50:05.822 --> 16:50:08.542
One, we didn't have
any sort of validation,

16:50:08.542 --> 16:50:10.552
but user input is going to be messy.

16:50:10.552 --> 16:50:13.132
There's just a lot of
redundancy in here.

16:50:13.132 --> 16:50:15.062
Let's go ahead and do this.

16:50:15.062 --> 16:50:17.102
Let me select all the
comedies you all typed in.

16:50:17.102 --> 16:50:23.302
So select title from
favorites, where genres equals,

16:50:23.302 --> 16:50:25.462
