scale very well, but this

17:03:57.702 --> 17:03:59.952
is why SQL is especially powerful.

17:03:59.952 --> 17:04:04.762
You can actually compose one
SQL question from multiple ones.

17:04:04.762 --> 17:04:05.782
So let's do this.

17:04:05.782 --> 17:04:09.822
Why don't I select the title
where the ID of the show

17:04:09.822 --> 17:04:13.392
is in the following list of IDs?

17:04:13.392 --> 17:04:20.052
Select show ID from genres, where the
specific genre is, quote, unquote,

17:04:20.052 --> 17:04:20.982
"comedy."

17:04:20.982 --> 17:04:23.892
So I've got two SQL queries.

17:04:23.892 --> 17:04:27.192
One is deliberately nested
inside of parentheses.

17:04:27.192 --> 17:04:30.102
That's going to give me back
that whole list of show IDs.

17:04:30.102 --> 17:04:32.412
But that's exactly what
I want to then look up

17:04:32.412 --> 17:04:36.342
the titles for by selecting title
from shows where the ID of the show

17:04:36.342 --> 17:04:38.722
is in that big, tall list.

17:04:38.722 --> 17:04:44.052
And so now if I hit Enter,
I get back only those shows

17:04:44.052 --> 17:04:47.862
that were somehow flagged as
comedy, whether you in the audience

17:04:47.862 --> 17:04:51.912
checked one box for comedy,
two boxes, or all of the boxes.

17:04:51.912 --> 17:04:54.270
Somehow we teased out
comedy, again, just

17:04:54.270 --> 17:04:56.562
by using that Python script,
which loaded this data not

17:04:56.562 --> 17:04:59.142
into one big table, but instead, two.

17:04:59.142 --> 17:05:01.762
And if we want to clean this
up, let's do a couple of things.

17:05:01.762 --> 17:05:05.922
Let's, outside of the
parentheses, do order by title.

17:05:05.922 --> 17:05:08.892
This is a way of sorting
the data in SQL very easily.

17:05:08.892 --> 17:05:13.482
Now we have a whole list of the
same titles that are now sorted.

17:05:13.482 --> 17:05:17.982
And what was the keyword with which
I could filter out duplicates?

17:05:17.982 --> 17:05:18.792
Yeah, distinct.

17:05:18.792 --> 17:05:19.602
So let's try this.

17:05:19.602 --> 17:05:24.942
Same query, but let's select only the
distinct titles from that whole query.

17:05:24.942 --> 17:05:27.112
And notice, I've very
deliberately done it this way.

17:05:27.112 --> 17:05:28.862
And to this day, any
time I'm using SQL, I

17:05:28.862 --> 17:05:31.529
don't just start at the beginning
and type out my whole thought,

17:05:31.529 --> 17:05:33.282
and just get it right on the first try.

17:05:33.282 --> 17:05:35.952
I very commonly start
with the subquery, if you

17:05:35.952 --> 17:05:38.142
will, the thing in
parentheses, just to get myself

17:05:38.142 --> 17:05:39.942
one step toward what I care about.

17:05:39.942 --> 17:05:40.674
Then I add to it.

17:05:40.674 --> 17:05:41.382
Then I add to it.

17:05:41.382 --> 17:05:43.932
Then I add to it, just like
we've encouraged in Python and C,

17:05:43.932 --> 17:05:47.712
taking baby steps in order to get to
the answer you actually care about,

17:05:47.712 --> 17:05:48.822
like this one now.

17:05:48.822 --> 17:05:51.402
And other than this
mistake, which we didn't

17:05:51.402 --> 17:05:55.692
