17:16:32.622 --> 17:16:33.462
Enter.

17:16:33.462 --> 17:16:34.882
I'm being prompted for a title.

17:16:34.882 --> 17:16:39.132
I'm going to type in The Office and
cross my fingers, and there's that 12.

17:16:39.132 --> 17:16:40.152
Why is it 12?

17:16:40.152 --> 17:16:42.792
Well, there's a typo again
because I re-imported the CSV.

17:16:42.792 --> 17:16:46.612
I had deleted two of the Thes, so
we're back at the original data set.

17:16:46.612 --> 17:16:51.102
So there's 12 total that have,
quote, unquote, "The Office"

17:16:51.102 --> 17:16:53.122
in the title like that.

17:16:53.122 --> 17:16:54.202
So what have we done?

17:16:54.202 --> 17:16:57.162
We've combined some
Python with some SQL,

17:16:57.162 --> 17:17:00.132
but we've relegated all of the
complexity of searching for something,

17:17:00.132 --> 17:17:02.142
the selecting of something,
gotten rid of all

17:17:02.142 --> 17:17:04.902
of the with keyword, the
open keyword, the for loop,

17:17:04.902 --> 17:17:06.942
the reader the DictReader,
and all of that.

17:17:06.942 --> 17:17:11.802
And it's just one line of SQL now,
using the best of both worlds.

17:17:11.802 --> 17:17:17.062
All right, any questions on what we've
just done here or how any of this

17:17:17.062 --> 17:17:17.562
works?

17:17:20.212 --> 17:17:21.552
Any questions here?

17:17:21.552 --> 17:17:22.652
Yeah?

17:17:22.652 --> 17:17:26.412
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

17:17:26.412 --> 17:17:29.755
DAVID J. MALAN: When does this
function return more than one row?

17:17:29.755 --> 17:17:30.672
Was that the question?

17:17:30.672 --> 17:17:31.102
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

17:17:31.102 --> 17:17:31.372
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

17:17:31.372 --> 17:17:33.582
So let's do that by changing
the problem at hand.

17:17:33.582 --> 17:17:36.312
This program was designed just
to select the total count.

17:17:36.312 --> 17:17:41.412
Let's go ahead and
select, for instance, all

17:17:41.412 --> 17:17:46.047
of the ways you all typed in The Office
by selecting the title this time.

17:17:49.452 --> 17:17:53.812
If I do this in SQLite3, let
me go ahead and do this again

17:17:53.812 --> 17:17:55.312
after increasing my Terminal window.

17:17:55.312 --> 17:17:56.262
Let's do it manually.

17:17:56.262 --> 17:18:00.912
Select title from favorites,
where the title is like,

17:18:00.912 --> 17:18:04.177
quote, unquote, "The Office," semicolon.

17:18:04.177 --> 17:18:07.302
I get back all of these different rows,
and we didn't even notice this one.

17:18:07.302 --> 17:18:09.252
There's actually another
little typo in there

17:18:09.252 --> 17:18:12.972
with some capitalization of the
E, and the C, and the E. That

17:18:12.972 --> 17:18:16.182
would be an example of a query
that gives me back therefore

17:18:16.182 --> 17:18:17.422
for multiple rows.

17:18:17.422 --> 17:18:19.332
So let's now change my Python program.

17:18:19.332 --> 17:18:24.882
If I now, in my Python program, do
this, I get back a whole bunch of rows

17:18:24.882 --> 17:18:26.452
containing all of those titles.

17:18:26.452 --> 17:18:31.692
I can now do, for row in rows, I can
print out the current row's title,

17:18:31.692 --> 17:18:34.732
and now manipulate all
of those things together.

17:18:34.732 --> 17:18:36.102
Let me keep both on the screen.

17:18:36.102 --> 17:18:37.662
Let me run Python of favorites.py.

17:18:37.662 --> 17:18:41.662
And that for loop now should
iterate, what, 10 or more times,

17:18:41.662 --> 17:18:43.362
once for each of those titles.

17:18:43.362 --> 17:18:47.352
And indeed, if I type in
The Office again, Enter.

17:18:47.352 --> 17:18:50.232
Whoops.

17:18:50.232 --> 17:18:51.012
Row title.

17:18:51.012 --> 17:18:52.092
What did I do wrong?

17:18:52.092 --> 17:18:55.394
Oh, I should not be renaming
title to counter this time.

17:18:55.394 --> 17:18:57.102
So that's just a dumb
mistake on my part.

17:18:57.102 --> 17:18:58.752
Let me rerun it again.

17:18:58.752 --> 17:19:01.572
And now I should see after
typing in The Office,

17:19:01.572 --> 17:19:03.762
Enter, a whole bunch of The Offices.

17:19:03.762 --> 17:19:05.862
And because I'm using
like, even the missed

17:19:05.862 --> 17:19:08.892
capitalizations are coming through,
because like is case insensitive.

17:19:08.892 --> 17:19:11.232
It doesn't matter if it's
uppercase or lowercase.

17:19:11.232 --> 17:19:15.642
Whereas had I used the equal sign
I would get back only the same ones

17:19:15.642 --> 17:19:17.277
capitalized correctly.

17:19:17.277 --> 17:19:20.592
All right, any questions on this next?

17:19:20.592 --> 17:19:25.042
All right, so let's transition
to a larger, juicier data

17:19:25.042 --> 17:19:26.952
set, and consider some
of the issues that

17:19:26.952 --> 17:19:31.212
arise when actually now using SQL and
skating toward a world in which we're

17:19:31.212 --> 17:19:34.312
using SQL for mobile apps, web
apps, and generally speaking,

17:19:34.312 --> 17:19:36.142
very large data sets.

17:19:36.142 --> 17:19:39.432
So let's start with a larger
data set just like that.

17:19:39.432 --> 17:19:45.142
Give me just a moment to switch screens
over to what we have for you today,

17:19:45.142 --> 17:19:48.312
which is an actual relational
database that we've created out

17:19:48.312 --> 17:19:51.882
of a real-world data set from IMDb.

17:19:51.882 --> 17:19:54.552
So InternetMovieDatabase.com
is a website

17:19:54.552 --> 17:19:57.132
where you can search for TV
shows, and movies, and actors,

17:19:57.132 --> 17:20:00.222
and so forth, all using their
database behind the scenes.

17:20:00.222 --> 17:20:04.872
IMDb wonderfully makes their data
set available as not CSV files,

17:20:04.872 --> 17:20:08.302
but TSV files, tab-separated values.

17:20:08.302 --> 17:20:11.802
And so what we did is, before class
we downloaded those TSV files.

17:20:11.802 --> 17:20:15.642
We wrote a Python program
similar to my favorites8.py file

17:20:15.642 --> 17:20:19.092
earlier that read in
all of those TSV files,

17:20:19.092 --> 17:20:24.162
created some SQL tables
in an IMDb database

17:20:24.162 --> 17:20:28.612
for you in SQLite that has multiple
tables and multiple columns.

17:20:28.612 --> 17:20:32.532
So let's go and wrap our minds around
what's actually in this data set.

17:20:32.532 --> 17:20:36.282
Let me go back to VS Code
here, and in just a moment,

17:20:36.282 --> 17:20:40.602
I'm going to go ahead and copy the
file, which we've named shows.db.

17:20:40.602 --> 17:20:45.852
And I'm going to go ahead and increase
my Terminal and do SQLite3 of shows.db.

17:20:45.852 --> 17:20:48.912
Whenever playing around with a
SQLite database for the first time,

17:20:48.912 --> 17:20:51.822
typing .schema is perhaps a good
place to start to give you a sense

17:20:51.822 --> 17:20:52.752
of what's in there.

17:20:52.752 --> 17:20:54.462
And things just escalated quickly.

17:20:54.462 --> 17:20:56.982
There's a lot in this data
set, because, indeed, there's

17:20:56.982 --> 17:21:01.092
going to be tens of hundreds of
thousands of rows in this data set,

17:21:01.092 --> 17:21:04.572
and also problem set 7, where we'll
look at the movie side of things

17:21:04.572 --> 17:21:06.262
and not just the TV shows.

17:21:06.262 --> 17:21:09.282
So what is the schema that
we have created for you

17:21:09.282 --> 17:21:12.492
from IMDb's actual real-world data?

17:21:12.492 --> 17:21:14.292
One, there's a table called shows.

17:21:14.292 --> 17:21:17.292
And notice we've just added whitespace
by hitting Enter a bunch of times

17:21:17.292 --> 17:21:19.392
to make it a little more
stylistically readable.

17:21:19.392 --> 17:21:23.322
The shows table has an ID
column, a title column, a year,

17:21:23.322 --> 17:21:26.082
and the total number of
episodes for a given show.

17:21:26.082 --> 17:21:31.092
And the types of those columns are
integer, text, numeric, and integer.

17:21:31.092 --> 17:21:33.432
So it turns out there's
actually a few different data

17:21:33.432 --> 17:21:39.192
types that are worth being aware of when
it comes to creating tables themselves.

17:21:39.192 --> 17:21:43.512
In fact, in SQLite there's
five data types, and only five,

17:21:43.512 --> 17:21:46.992
fortunately, one of which is, indeed,
integer, negative or positive,

17:21:46.992 --> 17:21:50.352
numeric, which is kind of a
catchall for dates and times,

17:21:50.352 --> 17:21:52.662
things that are numeric
but are not just integers,

17:21:52.662 --> 17:21:54.852
and not just real numbers, for instance.

17:21:54.852 --> 17:21:58.362
Real number is what we've generally
thought of as float up until now.

17:21:58.362 --> 17:22:00.400
Text, of course, is
just text, but notice

17:22:00.400 --> 17:22:02.442
that you don't have to
worry about how big it is.

17:22:02.442 --> 17:22:04.452
Like in Python, it will size to fit.

17:22:04.452 --> 17:22:07.182
And then there's BLOB, which
is binary large object, which

17:22:07.182 --> 17:22:10.642
is for just raw 0s and 1s, like
for files or things like that.

17:22:10.642 --> 17:22:12.912
But we'll generally use
the other four of these.

17:22:12.912 --> 17:22:16.302
And so, indeed, when we
imported this data for you

17:22:16.302 --> 17:22:21.612
we decided that every show would be
given an ID, which is just an integer.

17:22:21.612 --> 17:22:24.802
Every show has, of course, a
title, which should not be null.

17:22:24.802 --> 17:22:26.662
Otherwise, why is it in the database?

17:22:26.662 --> 17:22:30.172
Every show has a year,
which is numeric according

17:22:30.172 --> 17:22:31.522
to that definition a moment ago.

17:22:31.522 --> 17:22:34.882
And the total number of episodes for
a show is going to be an integer.

17:22:34.882 --> 17:22:38.452
What now is with these primary keys
that we mentioned earlier, too?

17:22:38.452 --> 17:22:43.432
A primary key is the column that
uniquely identifies all of the data.

17:22:43.432 --> 17:22:46.462
In our case, with the
favorites, I automatically

17:22:46.462 --> 17:22:50.092
gave each of your submissions a unique
ID so that even if two or more of you

17:22:50.092 --> 17:22:52.702
typed in The Office,
your submission still

17:22:52.702 --> 17:22:57.652
had a unique identifier, a number
that allowed me to then correlate it

17:22:57.652 --> 17:23:01.672
with your genres, just
as we saw a moment ago.

17:23:01.672 --> 17:23:04.622
In this version of IMDb,
there's also genres.

17:23:04.622 --> 17:23:07.462
But they don't come from
us, they come from IMDb.com.

17:23:07.462 --> 17:23:11.662
And so a genre has a show ID, and
a genre just like our database.

17:23:11.662 --> 17:23:15.232
But these are real-world genres
with a bit more filtration.

17:23:15.232 --> 17:23:19.862
Notice, though, just like my
version, there's a foreign key.

17:23:19.862 --> 17:23:25.312
A foreign key is the appearance
of another table's primary key

17:23:25.312 --> 17:23:27.392
in its own table.

17:23:27.392 --> 17:23:30.442
So when you have a table
like genres, which is somehow

17:23:30.442 --> 17:23:36.262
cross referencing the original shows
table, if shows have a primary key

17:23:36.262 --> 17:23:40.612
called ID, and those same numbers
appear in the genres table

17:23:40.612 --> 17:23:45.592
under the column called show ID, by
definition, show ID is a foreign key.

17:23:45.592 --> 17:23:47.992
It's the same numbers but
it's foreign in the sense

17:23:47.992 --> 17:23:50.602
that the number is being
used in this table,

17:23:50.602 --> 17:23:54.472
even though it's officially defined
primarily in this other table.

17:23:54.472 --> 17:23:57.112
This is what we mean by
relational databases.

17:23:57.112 --> 17:24:02.512
You have multiple tables with some
column in common, numbers typically.

17:24:02.512 --> 17:24:06.202
And those numbers allow you to line
the two tables up in such a way

17:24:06.202 --> 17:24:09.382
that you can reconnect the
shows with their genres,

17:24:09.382 --> 17:24:12.142
just like we did with our
smaller data set a moment ago.

17:24:12.142 --> 17:24:14.392
This logic is extended further.

17:24:14.392 --> 17:24:18.412
Notice that the IMDb database we've
created for you has a stars table,

17:24:18.412 --> 17:24:22.072
like TV show stars, the actors therein.

17:24:22.072 --> 17:24:25.552
And that table, interestingly,
has no mention of people

17:24:25.552 --> 17:24:27.562
and no mention of shows, per se.

17:24:27.562 --> 17:24:31.072
It only has a column called
show ID, which is an integer,

17:24:31.072 --> 17:24:33.562
and a person ID, which is an integer.

17:24:33.562 --> 17:24:39.662
Meanwhile, if we scrolled
down to the bottom,

17:24:39.662 --> 17:24:42.832
you will see a table called people.

17:24:42.832 --> 17:24:48.352
And we have decided in IMDb's world
that every person in the TV show world

17:24:48.352 --> 17:24:52.852
will have a unique identifier that's
a number, a name that's text, a birth

17:24:52.852 --> 17:24:56.842
date, which is numeric, and
then, again, specifying that ID

17:24:56.842 --> 17:25:00.692
is going to be their primary key.

17:25:00.692 --> 17:25:02.282
So what's going on here?

17:25:02.282 --> 17:25:07.982
Well, it turns out that TV stars and
writers are both types of people.

17:25:07.982 --> 17:25:13.072
So using this relational database,
notice the road we're going down.

17:25:13.072 --> 17:25:15.112
We're factoring out commonalities.

17:25:15.112 --> 17:25:17.912
And if a person can be
different things in life,

17:25:17.912 --> 17:25:20.602
well, we're defining them
first and foremost as people.

17:25:20.602 --> 17:25:23.492
And then, notice these two
tables are almost the same.

17:25:23.492 --> 17:25:26.002
The stars table has a show
ID, which is a number,

17:25:26.002 --> 17:25:28.012
and a person ID, which
is a number, which

17:25:28.012 --> 17:25:36.052
allows us via this middleman table, if
you will, to link people with TV shows.

17:25:36.052 --> 17:25:41.422
Similarly, the writers table allows
us to connect shows with people, too,

17:25:41.422 --> 17:25:43.562
by just recording those numbers.

17:25:43.562 --> 17:25:46.322
So if we go into this data
set, let's do the following.

17:25:46.322 --> 17:25:49.682
Let's do select star
from people semicolon.

17:25:49.682 --> 17:25:52.372
So a huge amount of data is coming back.

17:25:52.372 --> 17:25:56.822
This is hundreds of thousands of rows
now based on the ID numbers alone.

17:25:56.822 --> 17:25:59.662
So this is real-world data
now flying across the screen.

17:25:59.662 --> 17:26:03.502
There's a lot of people in the TV show
business, not just actors and writers,

17:26:03.502 --> 17:26:05.122
but others as well.

17:26:05.122 --> 17:26:06.022
It's still going.

17:26:06.022 --> 17:26:07.319
There's a lot of data there.

17:26:07.319 --> 17:26:09.652
So my god, if you had to do
anything manual in this data

17:26:09.652 --> 17:26:12.002
set it's probably not going
to work out very well.

17:26:12.002 --> 17:26:14.932
And actually, we're up to, what,
a million people in this data

17:26:14.932 --> 17:26:17.332
set, plus, which would mean
this probably isn't even

17:26:17.332 --> 17:26:20.932
going to open very well in Excel, or
Google Spreadsheets, or Apple Numbers.

17:26:20.932 --> 17:26:23.042
SQL probably is the
better approach here.

17:26:23.042 --> 17:26:25.702
Let's search for someone
specific, like select star

17:26:25.702 --> 17:26:31.402
from people, where name equals
Steve Carell, for instance, sticking

17:26:31.402 --> 17:26:32.044
with comedies.

17:26:32.044 --> 17:26:33.502
All right, so there's Steve Carell.

17:26:33.502 --> 17:26:39.442
He is person number
136,797, born in 1962.

17:26:39.442 --> 17:26:41.882
And that's as much data as
we have on Steve Carell here.

17:26:41.882 --> 17:26:44.552
How do we figure out what
shows, for instance, he's in?

17:26:44.552 --> 17:26:48.842
Well, let's see, select
star from shows, semicolon.

17:26:48.842 --> 17:26:52.522
There's a crazy number of shows
out there in the IMDb database.

17:26:52.522 --> 17:26:55.492
And you can see it here again
flying across the screen.

17:26:55.492 --> 17:26:58.972
Feels like we're going to have to
employ some techniques in order

17:26:58.972 --> 17:27:02.402
to get at all of Steve Carell's shows.

17:27:02.402 --> 17:27:04.562
So how are we going to do that?

17:27:04.562 --> 17:27:07.072
Well, god, this is a lot of data here.

17:27:07.072 --> 17:27:10.462
And in fact, yeah, we
have, what, 15 million

17:27:10.462 --> 17:27:12.622
shows plus in this data set, too.

17:27:12.622 --> 17:27:15.682
So doing things efficiently is
now going to start to matter.

17:27:15.682 --> 17:27:17.132
So let's actually do this.

17:27:17.132 --> 17:27:18.802
Let me select a specific show.

17:27:18.802 --> 17:27:23.932
Select star from shows where title
equals, quote, unquote, "The Office."

17:27:23.932 --> 17:27:26.262
And there presumably shouldn't
be typos in this data

17:27:26.262 --> 17:27:28.812
because it comes from the
real website IMDb.com.

17:27:28.812 --> 17:27:30.342
Let's get back to show.

17:27:30.342 --> 17:27:33.552
Turns out there's been a lot of
The Offices out in the world.

17:27:33.552 --> 17:27:37.512
The one that started in 2005
is the one that we want,

17:27:37.512 --> 17:27:40.332
presumably the most
popular with 188 episodes.

17:27:40.332 --> 17:27:41.562
How can we get just that?

17:27:41.562 --> 17:27:46.992
Maybe we could do and year
equals, how about 2005?

17:27:46.992 --> 17:27:50.592
All right, so now we've got
back just the ID of The Office

17:27:50.592 --> 17:27:51.552
that we care about.

17:27:51.552 --> 17:27:52.572
And let's do this, too.

17:27:52.572 --> 17:27:55.452
Let me turn on a timer
within SQLite just

17:27:55.452 --> 17:27:57.132
to get a sense of running time now.

17:27:57.132 --> 17:27:58.452
Let me do that again.

17:27:58.452 --> 17:28:01.392
Select star from shows, where
title equals The Office,

17:28:01.392 --> 17:28:03.150
and year equals 2005.

17:28:03.150 --> 17:28:04.192
And let's keep it simple.

17:28:04.192 --> 17:28:05.712
Let's just do titles for now.

17:28:05.712 --> 17:28:06.792
Enter.

17:28:06.792 --> 17:28:08.802
All right, so not terribly long.

17:28:08.802 --> 17:28:12.332
It found it pretty fast, but it looks
like it took how much real time?

17:28:12.332 --> 17:28:15.352
0.02 seconds, not bad for just a title.

17:28:15.352 --> 17:28:18.552
But just to plant a seed, it
turns out that we can probably

17:28:18.552 --> 17:28:19.542
speed even this up.

17:28:19.542 --> 17:28:20.272
Let me do this.

17:28:20.272 --> 17:28:23.562
Let me create something called an
index, which is another use of the C

17:28:23.562 --> 17:28:25.512
in CRUD for creating something.

17:28:25.512 --> 17:28:28.152
And I'm going to call this title index.

17:28:28.152 --> 17:28:32.682
And I'm going to create
it on the shows table,

17:28:32.682 --> 17:28:34.615
specifically on the title column.

17:28:34.615 --> 17:28:37.032
And we'll see in a moment what
this is going to do for me.

17:28:37.032 --> 17:28:38.262
Enter.

17:28:38.262 --> 17:28:42.472
Took a moment, like 0.349 seconds,
to create something called an index.

17:28:42.472 --> 17:28:46.932
But now watch, if I select star from
shows searching for The Office again,

17:28:46.932 --> 17:28:49.572
previously it took me 0.021 seconds.

17:28:49.572 --> 17:28:53.022
Not bad, but now, wow.

17:28:53.022 --> 17:28:56.532
Literally no time at all, or so low
that it wasn't really measurable.

17:28:56.532 --> 17:28:58.812
And I'll do it again just
to get a sense of things.

17:28:58.812 --> 17:29:00.202
Still quite low.

17:29:00.202 --> 17:29:05.362
Now even though 0.021 seconds, not crazy
long, imagine now having a lot of data,

17:29:05.362 --> 17:29:07.902
a lot of users running a real
website or real mobile app.

17:29:07.902 --> 17:29:11.322
Every millisecond we can start to
shave off is going to be compelling.

17:29:11.322 --> 17:29:13.152
So what is it we just did?

17:29:13.152 --> 17:29:17.172
Well, we actually just created
something called an index.

17:29:17.172 --> 17:29:19.302
And this is a nice way
to tie in, now, some

17:29:19.302 --> 17:29:21.762
of our week 5 discussion
of data structures,

17:29:21.762 --> 17:29:23.592
and our week 3 discussion
of running times.

17:29:23.592 --> 17:29:26.622
An index in a database is
some kind of fancy data

17:29:26.622 --> 17:29:31.452
structure that allows the database
to do better than linear search.

17:29:31.452 --> 17:29:35.922
Literally, as you just saw, these
tables are crazy long or tall right now,

17:29:35.922 --> 17:29:37.492
very linear, that is.

17:29:37.492 --> 17:29:39.642
And so when I first
searched for The Office,

17:29:39.642 --> 17:29:43.122
it was literally doing linear search,
top to bottom, looking at as many as,

17:29:43.122 --> 17:29:44.742
what, a million plus rows.

17:29:44.742 --> 17:29:46.212
That's relatively slow.

17:29:46.212 --> 17:29:48.672
It's not that slow, 0.021 seconds.

17:29:48.672 --> 17:29:52.362
But that's relatively slow just
theoretically, algorithmically,

17:29:52.362 --> 17:29:53.922
doing anything linearly.

17:29:53.922 --> 17:29:57.432
But if you instead create
an index using syntax

17:29:57.432 --> 17:30:03.222
like this, which I just did, creating an
index on the title column of the show's

17:30:03.222 --> 17:30:06.562
table, that's like giving the
database a clue in advance saying,

17:30:06.562 --> 17:30:10.002
hey, I know I'm going to search on
this column in this table a lot.

17:30:10.002 --> 17:30:12.772
Do something with data
structures to speed things up.

17:30:12.772 --> 17:30:15.372
And so if you think back to our
discussion of data structures,

17:30:15.372 --> 17:30:17.062
maybe it's using a tree.

17:30:17.062 --> 17:30:21.402
Maybe it's using a trie or a hash
table, some fancier two-dimensional data

17:30:21.402 --> 17:30:25.272
structure is generally going to lift
the data up creating right maybe a tree

17:30:25.272 --> 17:30:26.152
structure.

17:30:26.152 --> 17:30:28.962
So it's just much faster
to find data, especially

17:30:28.962 --> 17:30:31.842
if it's sorting it now
based on title, and not

17:30:31.842 --> 17:30:33.372
just storing it in one long list.

17:30:33.372 --> 17:30:35.562
And in fact, in the world
of relational databases,

17:30:35.562 --> 17:30:37.902
the type of structure that's
often used in a database

17:30:37.902 --> 17:30:39.262
is something called a B-tree.

17:30:39.262 --> 17:30:40.512
It's not a binary tree.

17:30:40.512 --> 17:30:44.382
Different use of the letter B, but it
looks a little something like the trees

17:30:44.382 --> 17:30:45.162
we've seen.

17:30:45.162 --> 17:30:47.022
It's not binary because
some of the nodes

17:30:47.022 --> 17:30:50.232
might have more than
two children or fewer,

17:30:50.232 --> 17:30:53.532
but it's a very wide but
relatively shallow tree.

17:30:53.532 --> 17:30:55.332
It's not very tall.

17:30:55.332 --> 17:30:59.112
And the upside of that is that if
your data is stored in this tree,

17:30:59.112 --> 17:31:01.402
the database can find it more quickly.

17:31:01.402 --> 17:31:06.612
And the reason it took half a second,
a third of a second to build the index

17:31:06.612 --> 17:31:10.602
is because SQLite needed to take
some non-zero amount of time

17:31:10.602 --> 17:31:12.972
to just build up this tree in memory.

17:31:12.972 --> 17:31:17.242
And it has algorithms for doing so based
on alphabetization or other techniques.

17:31:17.242 --> 17:31:20.682
But you spend a bit of time
up front, a third of a second.

17:31:20.682 --> 17:31:22.812
And then thereafter, wow.

17:31:22.812 --> 17:31:25.812
Every subsequent query, if I
keep doing it again and again,

17:31:25.812 --> 17:31:29.382
is going to be crazy
low, 0.000, maybe 0.001.

17:31:29.382 --> 17:31:33.582
But an order of magnitude, a
factor of 10 or 100 faster than it

17:31:33.582 --> 17:31:36.132
previously was earlier.

17:31:36.132 --> 17:31:39.702
So we have these indexes which
allow us to get at data faster.

17:31:39.702 --> 17:31:42.672
But what if we want to
actually get data that's

17:31:42.672 --> 17:31:44.712
now across these multiple tables?

17:31:44.712 --> 17:31:45.582
How can we do that?

17:31:45.582 --> 17:31:48.402
And how might these indices
or indexes help further?

17:31:48.402 --> 17:31:52.242
Well, it turns out there is
a way that we've seen already

17:31:52.242 --> 17:31:54.852
indirectly to join two tables together.

17:31:54.852 --> 17:31:58.752
Previously, when I selected
the ID of The Office,

17:31:58.752 --> 17:32:03.082
and then I searched for it in the other
table using select in a nested query,

17:32:03.082 --> 17:32:05.752
I was joining two tables together.

17:32:05.752 --> 17:32:08.242
And it turns out there's a
couple of ways to do this.

17:32:08.242 --> 17:32:11.892
Let's go ahead now and, for instance,
find all of Steve Carell's TV shows.

17:32:11.892 --> 17:32:14.022
Not just The Office
but all of them, too.

17:32:14.022 --> 17:32:21.657
Unfortunately, if we look at our schema,
shows up here have no mention of TV--

17:32:21.657 --> 17:32:27.202
oh, shows over here has no
mention of the TV stars in them.

17:32:27.202 --> 17:32:30.472
And people have no mention of shows.

17:32:30.472 --> 17:32:34.702
We somehow need to use this
table here to connect the two.

17:32:34.702 --> 17:32:40.162
And this is called a join table, in the
sense that using two integer columns--

17:32:40.162 --> 17:32:43.132
it joins the two tables
together logically.

17:32:43.132 --> 17:32:47.092
And so if you're savvy enough with SQL,
you can do what I did with my hands

17:32:47.092 --> 17:32:51.352
earlier and like recombine
tables by using these common IDs,

17:32:51.352 --> 17:32:52.802
these integers together.

17:32:52.802 --> 17:32:53.672
So let me do this.

17:32:53.672 --> 17:32:58.072
Let me go ahead and figure out,
step-by-step, Steve Carell's shows.

17:32:58.072 --> 17:32:59.312
So how am I going to do this?

17:32:59.312 --> 17:33:04.462
Well, if I select star from people,
where name equals Steve Carell,

17:33:04.462 --> 17:33:06.182
fortunately, there's only one of them.

17:33:06.182 --> 17:33:12.142
So this gives me back his name,
his ID, and his birth year.

17:33:12.142 --> 17:33:14.302
But it's really only his
ID that I care about.

17:33:14.302 --> 17:33:15.022
Why?

17:33:15.022 --> 17:33:20.842
Because in order to get back his shows,
I need to link person ID with show ID.

17:33:20.842 --> 17:33:22.982
So I need to know his ID number.

17:33:22.982 --> 17:33:24.932
So what could I do with this?

17:33:24.932 --> 17:33:29.572
Well, remember the schema
and the stars table.

17:33:29.572 --> 17:33:33.172
I've just gotten, from the
people table, Steve Carell's ID.

17:33:33.172 --> 17:33:38.872
I bet by transitivity I could
now use his person ID, his ID,

17:33:38.872 --> 17:33:41.242
to get back all of his show IDs.

17:33:41.242 --> 17:33:44.512
And then once I've got all of his show
IDs, I can take it one step further

17:33:44.512 --> 17:33:46.672
and get back all of his shows' titles.

17:33:46.672 --> 17:33:50.782
So the answer is actually English
words and not just random, seemingly,

17:33:50.782 --> 17:33:51.632
integers.

17:33:51.632 --> 17:33:52.982
So let me go ahead and do this.

17:33:52.982 --> 17:33:57.082
Let me, again, get Steve
Carell's ID number, but not star.

17:33:57.082 --> 17:33:58.402
Star represents everything.

17:33:58.402 --> 17:34:00.952
It's a wildcard character in SQL.

17:34:00.952 --> 17:34:03.652
Let me just select the
ID of Steve Carell.

17:34:03.652 --> 17:34:06.982
And that gives me back 136,797.

17:34:06.982 --> 17:34:08.782
And it's only giving me back one value.

17:34:08.782 --> 17:34:12.052
The thing called ID is just
the column heading up above.

17:34:12.052 --> 17:34:16.702
Now, suppose I want to
select all of the show IDs

17:34:16.702 --> 17:34:18.802
that Steve Carell is affiliated with.

17:34:18.802 --> 17:34:25.912
Let me select Show ID from stars,
where the person ID in stars

17:34:25.912 --> 17:34:28.822
happens to equal Steve Carell's ID.

17:34:28.822 --> 17:34:32.662
So again, I'm building up my answer in
reverse and taking these baby steps.

17:34:32.662 --> 17:34:36.772
On the right, in parentheses,
I'm getting Steve Carell's ID.

17:34:36.772 --> 17:34:40.672
On the left, I am now
selecting all of the show IDs

17:34:40.672 --> 17:34:44.752
that have some connection with
that person ID in the stars table.

17:34:44.752 --> 17:34:47.192
This answer, too, is not
going to be that illuminating.

17:34:47.192 --> 17:34:50.762
It's just a whole bunch of integers
that have no meaning to me as a human.

17:34:50.762 --> 17:34:53.112
But let's take this one step further.

17:34:53.112 --> 17:34:54.862
And even though my
code is getting long, I

17:34:54.862 --> 17:34:57.262
could hit Enter and format
it nicely, especially

17:34:57.262 --> 17:34:59.002
if I were doing this in a code file.

17:34:59.002 --> 17:35:00.922
But I'm just doing it
interactively for now.

17:35:00.922 --> 17:35:04.762
Let's now select all of the
titles from the shows table,

17:35:04.762 --> 17:35:13.442
where the ID of the show is in
this following previous query.

17:35:13.442 --> 17:35:15.082
So again, the query is getting long.

17:35:15.082 --> 17:35:17.542
But notice, it's the
third and last step.

17:35:17.542 --> 17:35:21.292
Select title from the shows
table, where the ID of the show

17:35:21.292 --> 17:35:23.932
is in the list of all
of the show IDs that

17:35:23.932 --> 17:35:27.382
came back from the stars table
searching for Steve Carell's person ID.

17:35:27.382 --> 17:35:28.822
How did we get that person ID?

17:35:28.822 --> 17:35:30.022
Let me scroll to the end.

17:35:30.022 --> 17:35:36.502
Well, I selected, in my innermost
parentheses, Steve Carell's own ID.

17:35:36.502 --> 17:35:38.782
So now, when I hit Enter, voila.

17:35:38.782 --> 17:35:41.867
I get all of Steve Carell's
TV shows up until now.

17:35:41.867 --> 17:35:44.992
And if I want to tidy this up further,
I can use the same tricks as before.

17:35:44.992 --> 17:35:47.522
Order by title, semicolon.

17:35:47.522 --> 17:35:50.882
Now I've got it all
alphabetized as before.

17:35:50.882 --> 17:35:53.422
So again, with SQL comes
the ability to search--

17:35:53.422 --> 17:35:56.632
I mean, look how quickly
we do this, 0.094 seconds

17:35:56.632 --> 17:35:59.992
to search across three different
tables to get back this answer.

17:35:59.992 --> 17:36:04.162
But my data is now all neatly
designed in individual tables,

17:36:04.162 --> 17:36:07.342
which is going to be important
now that the data set is so large.

17:36:07.342 --> 17:36:09.682
But let me take this one step further.

17:36:09.682 --> 17:36:12.272
Let me go ahead and do this.

17:36:12.272 --> 17:36:16.922
Let me go ahead and point
out that with this query,

17:36:16.922 --> 17:36:20.212
notice that I'm searching on--

17:36:20.212 --> 17:36:24.052
let's say I'm searching
on a person ID here.

17:36:24.052 --> 17:36:27.752
And at the end here, I'm
searching on a name column here.

17:36:27.752 --> 17:36:30.572
So let me actually go ahead and do this.

17:36:30.572 --> 17:36:34.852
Let me go ahead and see
if we can't speed this up.

17:36:34.852 --> 17:36:38.432
This query at the moment
takes 0.092 seconds.

17:36:38.432 --> 17:36:41.272
Let's see if we can't speed this
up further by just quickly creating

17:36:41.272 --> 17:36:44.272
a few more of those B-trees
in the databases memory.

17:36:44.272 --> 17:36:49.582
Create an index called person index, and
I'm going to do this on the stars table

17:36:49.582 --> 17:36:51.292
on the person ID column.

17:36:51.292 --> 17:36:52.192
Enter.

17:36:52.192 --> 17:36:53.830
It's taking a moment, taking a moment.

17:36:53.830 --> 17:36:56.122
That's almost a full second
because that's a big table.

17:36:56.122 --> 17:37:00.392
Let's create another index called
show index on the stars table.

17:37:00.392 --> 17:37:00.892
Why?

17:37:00.892 --> 17:37:03.292
Because I want to search
by the show ID also.

17:37:03.292 --> 17:37:05.122
That was part of my big query.

17:37:05.122 --> 17:37:06.002
Takes a moment.

17:37:06.002 --> 17:37:09.152
OK, just more than
about 2/3 of a second.

17:37:09.152 --> 17:37:11.782
Now let's create one last one,
another index called name index,

17:37:11.782 --> 17:37:14.502
but I could call these things
anything I want, on the people table.

17:37:14.502 --> 17:37:14.902
Why?

17:37:14.902 --> 17:37:16.892
Because I'm also searching
on the name column.

17:37:16.892 --> 17:37:19.072
So in short, I'm
creating indexes on each

17:37:19.072 --> 17:37:22.792
of the columns that are somehow
involved in my search query,

17:37:22.792 --> 17:37:25.022
going from one table to the other.

17:37:25.022 --> 17:37:32.932
Now let's go back to the previous
query, which, recall, took--

17:37:32.932 --> 17:37:35.612
I think I erased it, 0.091.

17:37:35.612 --> 17:37:36.112
All right.

17:37:36.112 --> 17:37:37.987
Well, it was roughly
this order of magnitude.

17:37:37.987 --> 17:37:39.482
We're not seeing the data now.

17:37:39.482 --> 17:37:42.502
But let me go ahead and run
my original big query once.

17:37:42.502 --> 17:37:45.722
And boom, we're down to almost nothing.

17:37:45.722 --> 17:37:48.202
So again, creating
these indexes in memory

17:37:48.202 --> 17:37:52.982
has the effect of rapidly
speeding up our computation time.

17:37:52.982 --> 17:37:56.482
Now if you've ever used, for instance,
the my.harvard course shopping tool

17:37:56.482 --> 17:38:00.472
here on campus, or Yale's analogue, you
might wonder, why is the thing so slow?

17:38:00.472 --> 17:38:04.672
This could be one of the reasons why
large data sets with thousands of rows,

17:38:04.672 --> 17:38:07.012
thousands of courses
tend to be slow, if,

17:38:07.012 --> 17:38:10.222
and I'm only conjecturing, if the
database isn't properly indexed.

17:38:10.222 --> 17:38:12.232
If you're building your
own web application

17:38:12.232 --> 17:38:14.512
and you're finding that users
are waiting and waiting,

17:38:14.512 --> 17:38:17.632
and things are spinning and spinning,
what might be among the problems?

17:38:17.632 --> 17:38:21.112
Well, it could absolutely just be bad
algorithms and bad code that you wrote.

17:38:21.112 --> 17:38:23.612
Or it might be that you
haven't thought about, well,

17:38:23.612 --> 17:38:27.112
what column should be optimized
for searches and filtration

17:38:27.112 --> 17:38:31.047
like I've done here in order
to speed up subsequent queries?

17:38:31.047 --> 17:38:33.172
Again, from the outside
in, we can only conjecture.

17:38:33.172 --> 17:38:36.922
But ultimately, this is
just one of the things that

17:38:36.922 --> 17:38:39.650
explains performance problems as well.

17:38:39.650 --> 17:38:42.442
All right, let's point out just a
couple of final syntactic things,

17:38:42.442 --> 17:38:45.112
and then we'll consider,
bigger picture, some problems

17:38:45.112 --> 17:38:47.452
that might arise in this world.

17:38:47.452 --> 17:38:52.222
If these nested, nested queries
start to get a little much,

17:38:52.222 --> 17:38:54.502
there are other ways,
just so you've seen it,

17:38:54.502 --> 17:38:57.262
that you can execute
similar logic in SQL.

17:38:57.262 --> 17:38:59.752
For instance, if I
know in advance that I

17:38:59.752 --> 17:39:04.732
want to connect Steve Carell to
his show IDs and to their titles,

17:39:04.732 --> 17:39:06.532
we can do something more like this.

17:39:06.532 --> 17:39:17.392
Select title from the people table,
joined with the stars table on people

17:39:17.392 --> 17:39:20.242
ID equals stars.personID.

17:39:20.242 --> 17:39:21.202
So what am I doing?

17:39:21.202 --> 17:39:21.832
New syntax.

17:39:21.832 --> 17:39:25.124
And again, this is not something you'll
have to memorize or ingrain right away.

17:39:25.124 --> 17:39:29.422
But just so you've seen other
approaches, select title from people

17:39:29.422 --> 17:39:30.502
join stars.

17:39:30.502 --> 17:39:35.032
This is an explicit way to say, take
the people table in one hand, the stars

17:39:35.032 --> 17:39:37.372
table in the other hand,
and somehow join them

17:39:37.372 --> 17:39:39.332
as I keep doing with my fingertips here.

17:39:39.332 --> 17:39:40.952
How to join them?

17:39:40.952 --> 17:39:45.712
Join them so that the people, the ID
column in the people table lines up

17:39:45.712 --> 17:39:48.602
with the person ID in the stars table.

17:39:48.602 --> 17:39:50.602
But that's not quite everything.

17:39:50.602 --> 17:39:54.082
I could also say, join
further on the shows table,

17:39:54.082 --> 17:40:00.632
where the stars show ID
equals the shows ID column.

17:40:00.632 --> 17:40:01.882
So what am I doing here?

17:40:01.882 --> 17:40:11.332
That's saying, go further and join
the stars table with the show's table,

17:40:11.332 --> 17:40:14.332
joining the show ID
column with the ID column.

17:40:14.332 --> 17:40:17.092
Again, this starts to get a
little messy to think about.

17:40:17.092 --> 17:40:21.172
But now I can just say, where name
equals, quote, unquote, "Steve Carell."

17:40:21.172 --> 17:40:24.412
I can do in one query what previously
took me three nested queries

17:40:24.412 --> 17:40:25.942
and get back the same answers.

17:40:25.942 --> 17:40:30.722
And I can still add in my order
by title to get back the result.

17:40:30.722 --> 17:40:35.122
And if I do this a little more
neatly, let me type this out a little

17:40:35.122 --> 17:40:36.092
differently.

17:40:36.092 --> 17:40:41.962
Let me type this out by adding a
new line-- ah, I can't do that here.

17:40:41.962 --> 17:40:43.462
I'm going to leave it alone for now.

17:40:43.462 --> 17:40:46.042
We can type it on multiple
lines in other contexts.

17:40:46.042 --> 17:40:49.042
And let me do one last thing.

17:40:49.042 --> 17:40:50.354
Do I want to show that?

17:40:50.354 --> 17:40:52.312
I'm going to show it,
but this is not something

17:40:52.312 --> 17:40:53.782
you should ingrain just yet either.

17:40:53.782 --> 17:40:56.962
Select title from
people, stars, and shows.

17:40:56.962 --> 17:41:00.202
If you know in advance that you want
to do something with all three tables,

17:41:00.202 --> 17:41:03.472
you can just enumerate them,
one table name after the other.

17:41:03.472 --> 17:41:08.839
And then you can say where
people.ID equals stars.personID.

17:41:08.839 --> 17:41:10.672
And now I'm hitting
Enter so that it formats

17:41:10.672 --> 17:41:12.412
a little more readably on my screen.

17:41:12.412 --> 17:41:20.482
And stars.showID equals shows.ID,
and lastly, name equals Steve Carell.

17:41:20.482 --> 17:41:25.072
In short, you specify that you
want to select data from all three

17:41:25.072 --> 17:41:26.032
of these tables.

17:41:26.032 --> 17:41:31.792
And then you tell the database how to
combine foreign keys with primary keys,

17:41:31.792 --> 17:41:35.032
that is, the columns that
have those integers in common.

17:41:35.032 --> 17:41:38.062
If I hit Enter now, I get
the same exact results, ever

17:41:38.062 --> 17:41:41.452
more so if I also add
in an order by title.

17:41:41.452 --> 17:41:43.112
Oops.

17:41:43.112 --> 17:41:43.612
All right.

17:41:43.612 --> 17:41:45.532
That's why I didn't
want to do this earlier.

17:41:45.532 --> 17:41:48.532
I have to go back through my history
multiple times to actually get back

17:41:48.532 --> 17:41:49.982
the multi-line query this time.

17:41:49.982 --> 17:41:50.482
All right.

17:41:50.482 --> 17:41:52.622
That was a lot all at once.

17:41:52.622 --> 17:41:56.707
But this is only to say that, even
as we make the design of the data

17:41:56.707 --> 17:41:59.332
more sophisticated, and we put
some of it over here, some of it

17:41:59.332 --> 17:42:03.472
over here, some of it over here so as to
avoid duplication of data, weird hacks

17:42:03.472 --> 17:42:07.252
like putting commas in the data, we
can still get back all of the answers

17:42:07.252 --> 17:42:09.622
that we might want across
these several tables.

17:42:09.622 --> 17:42:13.922
And using indexes, we can
significantly speed up these processes

17:42:13.922 --> 17:42:17.482
so as to handle 10 times as
many, a 100 times as many users

17:42:17.482 --> 17:42:19.012
on the same actual database.

17:42:19.012 --> 17:42:20.362
There is going to be a downside.

17:42:20.362 --> 17:42:22.882
And thinking back to our
discussion of algorithms and data

17:42:22.882 --> 17:42:27.462
structures in past weeks, what might be
a downside of creating these indexes?

17:42:27.462 --> 17:42:31.452
Because as of now, I created four
separate indexes on the name column,

17:42:31.452 --> 17:42:34.902
the title column, and
some other columns, too.

17:42:34.902 --> 17:42:37.272
Why wouldn't I just go
ahead and index everything

17:42:37.272 --> 17:42:39.732
if it's clearly speeding things up?

17:42:39.732 --> 17:42:41.112
Memory, so space.

17:42:41.112 --> 17:42:44.232
Any time you're starting to benefit
time wise in computer science,

17:42:44.232 --> 17:42:47.292
odds are you're sacrificing
space, or vice versa.

17:42:47.292 --> 17:42:50.742
And probably indexing absolutely
everything is a little dumb

17:42:50.742 --> 17:42:54.772
because you're going to waste way more
space than you might actually need.

17:42:54.772 --> 17:42:56.952
So figuring out where the
right inflection point is

17:42:56.952 --> 17:43:01.752
is part of the process of designing and
just getting better at these things.

17:43:01.752 --> 17:43:06.252
Now unfortunately, a whole lot of
things can go wrong in this world,

17:43:06.252 --> 17:43:10.212
and they continue to in the real
world with people using SQL databases.

17:43:10.212 --> 17:43:12.102
And in fact, here on
out, if you're reading

17:43:12.102 --> 17:43:16.872
something technical about SQL databases,
and websites being hacked in some form,

17:43:16.872 --> 17:43:20.002
and passwords leaking out,
unfortunately, all too often

17:43:20.002 --> 17:43:22.847
it is because of what are
called SQL injection attacks.

17:43:22.847 --> 17:43:24.972
And just to give you a
sense now to counterbalance,

17:43:24.972 --> 17:43:26.930
maybe [INAUDIBLE] enthusiasm
for like, oh, that

17:43:26.930 --> 17:43:28.962
was neat how we can
do things so quickly.

17:43:28.962 --> 17:43:32.022
With great power comes
responsibility in this world, too.

17:43:32.022 --> 17:43:34.662
And so many people introduce
bugs into their code

17:43:34.662 --> 17:43:42.502
by not quite appreciating how it is the
data is getting into your application.

17:43:42.502 --> 17:43:43.732
So what do I mean by that?

17:43:43.732 --> 17:43:46.542
Here, for instance, is a
typical login screen for Yale.

17:43:46.542 --> 17:43:49.122
And here's the analogue for
Harvard where you're prompted,

17:43:49.122 --> 17:43:51.792
every day probably, for your
username and your password,

17:43:51.792 --> 17:43:53.802
your email address and
your password here.

17:43:53.802 --> 17:43:57.762
Suppose, though, that
behind this login page,

17:43:57.762 --> 17:44:00.372
whether Harvard's or Yale's,
there's some website.

17:44:00.372 --> 17:44:03.612
And that website is using
SQL underneath the hood

17:44:03.612 --> 17:44:06.042
to store all of the
Harvard or Yale people's

17:44:06.042 --> 17:44:09.282
usernames, passwords, ID
numbers, courses, transcripts,

17:44:09.282 --> 17:44:10.312
all of that stuff.

17:44:10.312 --> 17:44:12.822
So there's a SQL database
underneath the website.

17:44:12.822 --> 17:44:15.702
Well, what might go
wrong with this process?

17:44:15.702 --> 17:44:18.192
Unfortunately, there's
some special syntax in SQL

17:44:18.192 --> 17:44:19.872
just like there is in C and Python.

17:44:19.872 --> 17:44:22.302
For instance, there are
comments in SQL, too.

17:44:22.302 --> 17:44:26.022
If you do two hyphens, dash,
dash, that's a comment in SQL.

17:44:26.022 --> 17:44:31.512
And if you, the programmer, aren't
sufficiently distrustful of your users,

17:44:31.512 --> 17:44:34.902
such that you defend against
potentially adversarial attacks,

17:44:34.902 --> 17:44:36.502
you might do something like this.

17:44:36.502 --> 17:44:41.412
Suppose that I somewhat
maliciously or curiously log in

17:44:41.412 --> 17:44:44.472
by typing my username,
Malan@harvard.edu, and then maybe

17:44:44.472 --> 17:44:46.332
a single quote and a dash, dash.

17:44:46.332 --> 17:44:47.022
Why?

17:44:47.022 --> 17:44:50.202
Because I'm trying to suss out
if there is a vulnerability here

17:44:50.202 --> 17:44:51.762
to a SQL injection attack.

17:44:51.762 --> 17:44:53.252
Do not do this in general.

17:44:53.252 --> 17:44:56.502
But if I were the owner of the website
trying to see if I've made any mistake,

17:44:56.502 --> 17:45:00.642
I might try using potentially
dangerous characters in my input.

17:45:00.642 --> 17:45:01.632
Dangerous how?

17:45:01.632 --> 17:45:05.682
Because single quote is used for
quoting things in SQL, as we've seen--

17:45:05.682 --> 17:45:07.152
single quotes or double quotes.

17:45:07.152 --> 17:45:10.272
Dash, dash, I claim now,
is used for commenting.

17:45:10.272 --> 17:45:13.302
But let's now imagine what
the code underneath the hood

17:45:13.302 --> 17:45:17.502
might be for something like
Yale's login or Harvard's login.

17:45:17.502 --> 17:45:19.942
What if it's code that looks like this?

17:45:19.942 --> 17:45:21.882
So let me read it from left to right.

17:45:21.882 --> 17:45:26.052
Suppose that they are using something
like CS50's own execute function,

17:45:26.052 --> 17:45:28.572
and they've got some SQL
typed into the website that

17:45:28.572 --> 17:45:32.502
says select star from users,
where username equals this,

17:45:32.502 --> 17:45:34.392
and password equals that.

17:45:34.392 --> 17:45:37.852
And they're plugging in
username and password.

17:45:37.852 --> 17:45:38.949
So what am I doing here?

17:45:38.949 --> 17:45:41.532
Well, when the user types their
username password, hits Enter,

17:45:41.532 --> 17:45:44.262
I probably want to select
that user from my database

17:45:44.262 --> 17:45:46.362
to see if the username
and passwords match.

17:45:46.362 --> 17:45:49.062
So the underlying SQL
might be, select star

17:45:49.062 --> 17:45:51.132
from users, where username
equals question mark,

17:45:51.132 --> 17:45:52.549
and password equals question mark.

17:45:52.549 --> 17:45:53.442
Users is the table.

17:45:53.442 --> 17:45:54.852
One column is username.

17:45:54.852 --> 17:45:56.742
One column is password.

17:45:56.742 --> 17:45:57.342
All right.

17:45:57.342 --> 17:46:02.772
And if we get back one row,
presumably Malan@harvard.edu

17:46:02.772 --> 17:46:04.312
exists with that password.

17:46:04.312 --> 17:46:06.532
We should let him proceed
from there on out.

17:46:06.532 --> 17:46:10.482
So that's some pseudo code, if
you will, for this scenario.

17:46:10.482 --> 17:46:14.922
What if, though, this code is not
as well written as it currently

17:46:14.922 --> 17:46:16.842
is, and isn't using question marks?

17:46:16.842 --> 17:46:20.099
So the question mark syntax
is a fairly common SQL thing,

17:46:20.099 --> 17:46:22.182
where the question marks
are used as placeholders,

17:46:22.182 --> 17:46:24.732
just like in printf, percent S was.

17:46:24.732 --> 17:46:28.242
But this function, db.execute
from CS50's library

17:46:28.242 --> 17:46:30.762
and third-party libraries
as well, is also

17:46:30.762 --> 17:46:33.132
doing some good stuff
with these question marks,

17:46:33.132 --> 17:46:35.172
and defending against
the following attack.

17:46:35.172 --> 17:46:38.262
Suppose that you were not using
a third-party library like ours

17:46:38.262 --> 17:46:41.832
and you were just manually constructing
your SQL queries like this.

17:46:41.832 --> 17:46:45.282
You were to do something like this
instead using an f-string in Python.

17:46:45.282 --> 17:46:47.142
You're comfortable with
format strings now.

17:46:47.142 --> 17:46:50.225
You've gotten into the habit of using
curly braces and plugging in values.

17:46:50.225 --> 17:46:52.572
Suppose that you, the
aspiring programmer,

17:46:52.572 --> 17:46:55.002
is just using techniques
that you've been taught.

17:46:55.002 --> 17:46:58.002
So you have an f-string
with select star from users,

17:46:58.002 --> 17:47:01.812
where username equals, quote,
unquote, "username" in curly braces.

17:47:01.812 --> 17:47:06.612
And password equals, quote,
unquote, "password" in curly braces.

17:47:06.612 --> 17:47:09.612
As of what, two weeks
ago, this was perfectly

17:47:09.612 --> 17:47:14.802
legitimate technique in Python
to plug in values into a string.

17:47:14.802 --> 17:47:18.972
But notice if you are using
single quotes yourself

17:47:18.972 --> 17:47:24.092
and the user has typed in single
quotes to their input, what

17:47:24.092 --> 17:47:25.262
could go wrong here?

17:47:25.262 --> 17:47:29.852
Where are we going with this if you're
just blindly plugging user input

17:47:29.852 --> 17:47:33.692
into your own prepared string of text?

17:47:33.692 --> 17:47:34.572
Yeah?

17:47:34.572 --> 17:47:40.067
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

17:47:40.067 --> 17:47:40.942
DAVID J. MALAN: Yeah.

17:47:40.942 --> 17:47:46.012
Worst case, they could insert what is
actually SQL code into your database

17:47:46.012 --> 17:47:47.152
as follows.

17:47:47.152 --> 17:47:50.482
Generally speaking, if you're using
special syntax like single quotes

17:47:50.482 --> 17:47:52.342
to surround the user's
input, you'd better

17:47:52.342 --> 17:47:54.592
hope that they don't have
an apostrophe in their name.

17:47:54.592 --> 17:47:57.217
Or you better hope that they
don't type a single quote as well.

17:47:57.217 --> 17:48:01.367
Because what if their single quote
finishes your single quote instead,

17:48:01.367 --> 17:48:03.242
and then the rest of
this is somehow ignored?

17:48:03.242 --> 17:48:04.672
Well, let's consider
how this might happen.

17:48:04.672 --> 17:48:05.852
Let me go ahead in here.

17:48:05.852 --> 17:48:08.182
This got a little
blurry here, but let me

17:48:08.182 --> 17:48:10.192
plug in here-- wow, that looks awful.

17:48:10.192 --> 17:48:13.282
Let me fix the red.

17:48:13.282 --> 17:48:15.772
Just change this to white
so it's more readable.

17:48:15.772 --> 17:48:22.072
What happens if the
user does this instead?

17:48:22.072 --> 17:48:24.652
They type in, like I
did into the screenshot,

17:48:24.652 --> 17:48:28.102
'Malan@harvard.edu,'
single quote, dash, dash.

17:48:28.102 --> 17:48:30.412
What has just happened
logically, even though we've

17:48:30.412 --> 17:48:32.242
only just begun with SQL today?

17:48:32.242 --> 17:48:37.492
Well, select star from users, where
username equals Malan@harvard.edu, end

17:48:37.492 --> 17:48:38.662
quote.

17:48:38.662 --> 17:48:42.682
What's bad about the rest of this?

17:48:42.682 --> 17:48:45.094
Dash, dash, I claim,
means a comment, which

17:48:45.094 --> 17:48:47.552
means my color coding is going
to be a little blurry again.

17:48:47.552 --> 17:48:50.482
But everything after the
dash, dash is just ignored.

17:48:50.482 --> 17:48:52.782
The logic, then, of
the SQL query, then, is

17:48:52.782 --> 17:48:56.102
to just say, select
Malan@harvard.edu from the database,

17:48:56.102 --> 17:48:58.832
not even checking the password anymore.

17:48:58.832 --> 17:49:01.522
Therefore, you will get
back at least one row.

17:49:01.522 --> 17:49:06.532
So length of rows will equal 1, and so
presumably the rest of the pseudo code

17:49:06.532 --> 17:49:09.532
logs the user in, gives them
access to my my.harvard account,

17:49:09.532 --> 17:49:10.492
or whatever it is.

17:49:10.492 --> 17:49:15.982
And they've pretended to be me simply
by using a single quote and a dash,

17:49:15.982 --> 17:49:17.787
dash in the username field.

17:49:17.787 --> 17:49:19.912
Again, please don't go
start doing this later today

17:49:19.912 --> 17:49:21.482
on Harvard, Yale, or other websites.

17:49:21.482 --> 17:49:23.012
But it could be as simple as that.

17:49:23.012 --> 17:49:23.512
Why?

17:49:23.512 --> 17:49:25.372
Because the programmer
practiced what they

17:49:25.372 --> 17:49:29.452
were taught, which was just to
use curly braces to plug in,

17:49:29.452 --> 17:49:30.902
in f-strings, values.

17:49:30.902 --> 17:49:33.932
But if you don't understand how the
user's input is going to be used,

17:49:33.932 --> 17:49:37.597
and if you don't distrust your users
fundamentally, for every good person

17:49:37.597 --> 17:49:39.472
out there there's going
to be, unfortunately,

17:49:39.472 --> 17:49:44.722
some adversary who just wants to try
to find fault in your code or hack

17:49:44.722 --> 17:49:45.832
into your data set.

17:49:45.832 --> 17:49:48.322
This is what's known as
a SQL injection attack,

17:49:48.322 --> 17:49:52.342
because the user can type something
that happens to be or look like SQL,

17:49:52.342 --> 17:49:56.452
and trick your database into doing
something it didn't intend to,

17:49:56.452 --> 17:50:00.172
like, for instance, logging the user in.

17:50:00.172 --> 17:50:02.222
Worst case, they could
even do something else.

17:50:02.222 --> 17:50:06.832
Maybe the user types a semicolon, then
the word drop, or the word update.

17:50:06.832 --> 17:50:10.522
You could imagine doing semicolon
update table grades, where

17:50:10.522 --> 17:50:14.482
name equals Malan, and set the
grade equal to A instead of B,

17:50:14.482 --> 17:50:16.012
or something like that.

17:50:16.012 --> 17:50:18.892
The ability to inject
SQL into the database

17:50:18.892 --> 17:50:22.162
means you can do anything you want with
the data set, either constructively,

17:50:22.162 --> 17:50:25.342
or worse, destructively.

17:50:25.342 --> 17:50:28.222
And now, just a quick, little
cartoon that should now make sense.

17:50:34.752 --> 17:50:38.362
OK, to, like, one of us, two of us.

17:50:38.362 --> 17:50:39.742
Awkwardly somewhat funny.

17:50:39.742 --> 17:50:41.902
All right, so let's move
on to one last condition.

17:50:41.902 --> 17:50:44.472
There's one other problem
that can go awry here.

17:50:44.472 --> 17:50:45.722
Oh, and I should explain this.

17:50:45.722 --> 17:50:50.842
So this is an allusion to the son,
Robert, having typed in semicolon.

17:50:50.842 --> 17:50:54.152
The word drop, table, students, and
doing some of the same technique.

17:50:54.152 --> 17:50:56.782
This is humor that only
CS people would understand

17:50:56.782 --> 17:51:00.382
because it's the mom realizing,
oh, her son's doing a SQL injection

17:51:00.382 --> 17:51:01.650
attack onto the database.

17:51:01.650 --> 17:51:04.942
Less funny when you explain it, but once
you notice the syntax, that's all this

17:51:04.942 --> 17:51:06.302
is an allusion to.

17:51:06.302 --> 17:51:06.802
All right.

17:51:06.802 --> 17:51:10.192
So one final threat, now
that you are graduating

17:51:10.192 --> 17:51:14.662
to the world of proper databases
and away from CSV files alone.

17:51:14.662 --> 17:51:17.512
Things can go wrong
when using databases,

17:51:17.512 --> 17:51:21.180
and honestly, even using CSV
files if you have multiple users.

17:51:21.180 --> 17:51:22.972
And thus far, you and
I have had the luxury

17:51:22.972 --> 17:51:25.889
in almost every program we've written
that it's just me using my code.

17:51:25.889 --> 17:51:27.172
It's just you using your code.

17:51:27.172 --> 17:51:30.112
And even if your teaching fellow
or TA is using it, probably

17:51:30.112 --> 17:51:31.402
not at the same time.

17:51:31.402 --> 17:51:36.112
But the world gets interesting if you
start putting your code on phones,

17:51:36.112 --> 17:51:40.072
on websites, such that now you might
have two users literally trying

17:51:40.072 --> 17:51:42.772
to log in at the same time,
literally clicking a button

17:51:42.772 --> 17:51:44.752
at the same, or nearly the same time.

17:51:44.752 --> 17:51:47.722
What happens, then, if
a computer is trying

17:51:47.722 --> 17:51:50.632
to handle requests from two
different people at once,

17:51:50.632 --> 17:51:52.822
as might happen all
the time on a website?

17:51:52.822 --> 17:51:54.952
You might get what are
called race conditions.

17:51:54.952 --> 17:51:58.402
And this is a problem in computing in
general, not just with SQL, not just

17:51:58.402 --> 17:52:02.302
with Python, really just any
time you have shared data,

17:52:02.302 --> 17:52:04.492
like a database, as follows.

17:52:04.492 --> 17:52:08.962
This apparently is one of the
most liked Instagram posts ever.

17:52:08.962 --> 17:52:11.452
It is literally just
a picture of an egg.

17:52:11.452 --> 17:52:12.987
Has anyone clicked on this egg?

17:52:12.987 --> 17:52:13.612
Like, a couple?

17:52:13.612 --> 17:52:14.122
Oh, OK.

17:52:14.122 --> 17:52:14.622
Wow.

17:52:14.622 --> 17:52:15.562
All right, so yes.

17:52:15.562 --> 17:52:19.222
So go search for this photo if you'd
like to add to the likes on Instagram.

17:52:19.222 --> 17:52:21.452
The account is world_record_egg.

17:52:21.452 --> 17:52:24.382
This is just a screenshot of
Instagram of that picture of an egg.

17:52:24.382 --> 17:52:25.882
If you're in the habit
of using Instagram,

17:52:25.882 --> 17:52:28.840
or like any social media site, there's
some equivalent of a like button

17:52:28.840 --> 17:52:30.262
or a heart button these days.

17:52:30.262 --> 17:52:32.242
And that's actually a
really hard problem.

17:52:32.242 --> 17:52:35.392
Such a simple idea to count
the number of likes something

17:52:35.392 --> 17:52:38.332
has, but that means
someone has to click on it.

17:52:38.332 --> 17:52:40.252
Your code has to detect the click.

17:52:40.252 --> 17:52:43.202
Your code has to update the database,
and then do it again and again,

17:52:43.202 --> 17:52:48.082
even if multiple people are perhaps
right now clicking on that same egg.

17:52:48.082 --> 17:52:53.692
And unfortunately, bad things can
happen if two people try to do something

17:52:53.692 --> 17:52:55.882
at the same time on a computer.

17:52:55.882 --> 17:52:57.032
How might this happen?

17:52:57.032 --> 17:53:01.012
So here's some more code, half
pseudocode, half Python code here,

17:53:01.012 --> 17:53:01.922
as follows.

17:53:01.922 --> 17:53:05.572
Suppose that what happens when you,
literally, right now, maybe click

17:53:05.572 --> 17:53:08.812
on the like button on
the Instagram post.

17:53:08.812 --> 17:53:13.102
Suppose that code, like the following,
is executed on Facebook servers.

17:53:13.102 --> 17:53:19.532
db.execute of select likes from
posts where ID equals question mark.

17:53:19.532 --> 17:53:20.032
All right.

17:53:20.032 --> 17:53:22.432
So what am I assuming here?

17:53:22.432 --> 17:53:24.622
I'm assuming that that
photograph has a unique ID.

17:53:24.622 --> 17:53:28.012
It's some big integer, whatever
it was, randomly assigned.

17:53:28.012 --> 17:53:30.472
I'm assuming that when
you click on the heart

17:53:30.472 --> 17:53:33.502
the unique ID is somehow
sent to Instagram servers

17:53:33.502 --> 17:53:36.082
so that their code can call it ID.

17:53:36.082 --> 17:53:39.172
And I'm assuming that Instagram
is using its SQL database

17:53:39.172 --> 17:53:43.132
and selecting, from a posts
table, the current number of likes

17:53:43.132 --> 17:53:46.502
of that egg for that given ID number.

17:53:46.502 --> 17:53:47.002
Why?

17:53:47.002 --> 17:53:50.294
Because I need to know how many likes it
already has if I want to add one to it

17:53:50.294 --> 17:53:51.532
and then update the database.

17:53:51.532 --> 17:53:55.052
I need to select the data, then
I need to update the data here.

17:53:55.052 --> 17:53:55.552
All right.

17:53:55.552 --> 17:53:59.122
So in some Python code here,
let's store, in a variable called

17:53:59.122 --> 17:54:03.292
likes, whatever comes back in the
first row from the likes column.

17:54:03.292 --> 17:54:06.002
Again, this is new syntax
specific to our library,

17:54:06.002 --> 17:54:09.172
but a common way of getting back
first row and the column called

17:54:09.172 --> 17:54:10.142
likes therein.

17:54:10.142 --> 17:54:12.262
So at this point in the
story, likes is storing

17:54:12.262 --> 17:54:14.804
the total number of likes, in
the millions or whatever it is,

17:54:14.804 --> 17:54:15.952
of that particular egg.

17:54:15.952 --> 17:54:17.242
Then I do this.

17:54:17.242 --> 17:54:21.742
Execute update posts,
set the number of likes

17:54:21.742 --> 17:54:25.949
equal to this value, where the
ID of the post equals this value.

17:54:25.949 --> 17:54:27.532
What do I want to update the likes to?

17:54:27.532 --> 17:54:31.882
Whatever likes currently is plus
1, and then plugging in the ID.

17:54:31.882 --> 17:54:33.682
So a simple idea, right?

17:54:33.682 --> 17:54:37.072
I'm checking the value of
the likes, and maybe it's 10.

17:54:37.072 --> 17:54:40.372
I'm changing 10 to 11 and
then updating the table.

17:54:40.372 --> 17:54:43.732
But a problem can arise
if two people have

17:54:43.732 --> 17:54:48.352
clicked on that egg at roughly the
same time, or literally, the same time.

17:54:48.352 --> 17:54:49.682
Why is that?

17:54:49.682 --> 17:54:52.192
Well, in the world of
databases and servers,

17:54:52.192 --> 17:54:56.462
and the Instagrams of the world have
thousands of physical servers nowadays.

17:54:56.462 --> 17:55:00.772
So they can support millions,
billions even, of users nowadays.

17:55:00.772 --> 17:55:02.252
What can go wrong?

17:55:02.252 --> 17:55:05.872
Well, typically code like this
is not what we'll call atomic.

17:55:05.872 --> 17:55:09.772
To be atomic means that it all
executes together or not at all.

17:55:09.772 --> 17:55:14.842
Rather, code typically is executed,
as you might imagine, line by line.

17:55:14.842 --> 17:55:18.452
And if your code is running on a server
that multiple people have access to,

17:55:18.452 --> 17:55:20.872
which is absolutely the case
for an app like Instagram,

17:55:20.872 --> 17:55:23.902
if you and I click on the
heart at roughly the same time,

17:55:23.902 --> 17:55:27.502
for efficiency, the computer,
the server, owned by Instagram,

17:55:27.502 --> 17:55:29.842
might execute this line of code for me.

17:55:29.842 --> 17:55:31.879
Then it might execute
this line of code for you.

17:55:31.879 --> 17:55:34.462
Then this line of code for me,
then this line of code for you,

17:55:34.462 --> 17:55:37.045
then this line of code for me,
then this line of code for you.

17:55:37.045 --> 17:55:41.892
That is to say, our queries might
get intermingled chronologically.

17:55:41.892 --> 17:55:44.712
Because it'd be a little obnoxious
if, when you're using Instagram,

17:55:44.712 --> 17:55:47.382
I'm blocked out while you're
interacting with the site.

17:55:47.382 --> 17:55:50.120
It'd be a lot nicer for efficiency
and fairness if somehow they

17:55:50.120 --> 17:55:52.662
do a little bit of work for me,
a little bit of work for you,

17:55:52.662 --> 17:55:55.882
and back and forth, and back and
forth, equitably on the server.

17:55:55.882 --> 17:55:58.662
So that's what typically happens
by default. These lines of code

17:55:58.662 --> 17:56:00.762
get executed independently.

17:56:00.762 --> 17:56:05.112
And they can happen in alternating
order with other users.

17:56:05.112 --> 17:56:07.151
You can get them combined like this.

17:56:07.151 --> 17:56:11.182
Same order top to bottom, but other
things might happen in between.

17:56:11.182 --> 17:56:15.342
So suppose that the number of
likes at the very beginning was 10.

17:56:15.342 --> 17:56:19.422
And suppose that Carter and I both click
on that egg at roughly the same time.

17:56:19.422 --> 17:56:21.762
And suppose this line of
code gets executed for me,

17:56:21.762 --> 17:56:25.212
and that gives me a value
in likes, ultimately, of 10.

17:56:25.212 --> 17:56:28.632
Suppose, then, that the computer takes
a break from dealing with my request,

17:56:28.632 --> 17:56:31.032
does the same code for
Carter, and gets back

17:56:31.032 --> 17:56:33.252
what value for the
current number of likes?

17:56:33.252 --> 17:56:34.512
Also 10 for Carter.

17:56:34.512 --> 17:56:36.551
Because mine has not been recorded yet.

17:56:36.551 --> 17:56:39.252
At this point in the story,
somewhere in the computer's memory

17:56:39.252 --> 17:56:41.561
there's a likes variable
for me, storing 10.

17:56:41.561 --> 17:56:44.742
There's a likes variable
storing 10 for Carter.

17:56:44.742 --> 17:56:46.632
Then this line of code executes for me.

17:56:46.632 --> 17:56:50.561
It updates the database to be likes
plus 1, which stores 11 in the database.

17:56:50.561 --> 17:56:55.782
Then Carter's code is executed,
updating the same row in the database

17:56:55.782 --> 17:56:59.502
to 11, unfortunately.

17:56:59.502 --> 17:57:03.551
Because his value of likes happened
to be the same value of mine.

17:57:03.551 --> 17:57:06.882
And so the metaphor here, that if we
had a refrigerator on stage we would

17:57:06.882 --> 17:57:10.132
actually act out, is something that was
taught to me years ago in an operating

17:57:10.132 --> 17:57:15.641
systems class, whereby the most similar
analogue in the real world would be

17:57:15.641 --> 17:57:17.712
if you've got a mini
fridge in your dorm room.

17:57:17.712 --> 17:57:23.782
And one of you and your roommates comes
home, opens the fridge, and realizes,

17:57:23.782 --> 17:57:26.502
oh, we're out of milk, was
how the story went in my day.

17:57:26.502 --> 17:57:30.526
So you close the refrigerator, and
you walk across the street, go to CVS,

17:57:30.526 --> 17:57:31.901
and get in line to buy some milk.

17:57:31.901 --> 17:57:33.651
Meanwhile, your roommate comes home.

17:57:33.651 --> 17:57:38.266
They, too, inspect the state of your
refrigerator, a.k.a., a variable,

17:57:38.266 --> 17:57:40.391
open the door, and realizes,
oh, we're out of milk.

17:57:40.391 --> 17:57:41.322
I'll go get more milk.

17:57:41.322 --> 17:57:43.362
Close the fridge, go
across the street, and head

17:57:43.362 --> 17:57:45.401
to maybe a different store,
or the line is long enough

17:57:45.401 --> 17:57:47.193
that you don't see each
other at the store.

17:57:47.193 --> 17:57:51.042
So long story short, you both eventually
get home, open the door, and damn it,

17:57:51.042 --> 17:57:52.901
now there's milk from
your other roommate

17:57:52.901 --> 17:57:56.022
there because you both
made a decision on this

17:57:56.022 --> 17:58:01.151
based on the state of a variable
that you independently examined.

17:58:01.151 --> 17:58:03.042
And you didn't somehow communicate.

17:58:03.042 --> 17:58:05.952
Now in the real world, this
is absolutely solvable.

17:58:05.952 --> 17:58:09.462
How would you fix this or avoid
this problem in the real world?

17:58:09.462 --> 17:58:11.112
Literally, own roommate, own fridge.

17:58:11.112 --> 17:58:13.092
AUDIENCE: Text your
roommate [INAUDIBLE]..

17:58:13.092 --> 17:58:14.092
DAVID J. MALAN: Perfect.

17:58:14.092 --> 17:58:15.824
Let them know, so somehow communicate.

17:58:15.824 --> 17:58:18.282
And in fact, the terminology
here would be multiple threads

17:58:18.282 --> 17:58:20.801
can somehow intercommunicate
by having shared state,

17:58:20.801 --> 17:58:22.572
like the iMessage thread on your phone.

17:58:22.572 --> 17:58:23.532
You could leave a note.

17:58:23.532 --> 17:58:26.832
You could, more dramatically,
lock the refrigerator somehow,

17:58:26.832 --> 17:58:30.912
thereby making the milk
purchasing process atomic.

17:58:30.912 --> 17:58:33.731
The fundamental problem is
that for efficiency, again,

17:58:33.731 --> 17:58:37.032
computers tend to
intermingle logic that needs

17:58:37.032 --> 17:58:41.322
to happen when it's happening across
multiple users just for fairness' sake,

17:58:41.322 --> 17:58:42.522
for scheduling sake.

17:58:42.522 --> 17:58:45.162
You need to make sure that all
three of these lines of code

17:58:45.162 --> 17:58:48.611
execute for me, and then
for Carter, and then for you

17:58:48.611 --> 17:58:51.051
if you want to ensure that
this count is correct.

17:58:51.051 --> 17:58:54.561
And for years, when social media
was first getting off the ground,

17:58:54.561 --> 17:58:56.442
this was a super hard problem.

17:58:56.442 --> 17:58:59.262
Twitter used to go down all
of the time, and tweets,

17:58:59.262 --> 17:59:01.602
and retweets were a thing
that were similarly happening

17:59:01.602 --> 17:59:02.832
with a very high frequency.

17:59:02.832 --> 17:59:04.362
These are hard problems to solve.

17:59:04.362 --> 17:59:06.032
And thankfully, there are solutions.

17:59:06.032 --> 17:59:08.782
And we won't get into the weeds
of how you might use these things,

17:59:08.782 --> 17:59:11.442
but know that there are
solutions in the form of things

17:59:11.442 --> 17:59:14.952
called locks, which I use that
word deliberately with the fridge.

17:59:14.952 --> 17:59:18.912
Software locks can allow you to
protect a variable so no one else can

17:59:18.912 --> 17:59:20.892
look at it until you're done with it.

17:59:20.892 --> 17:59:23.112
There are things called
transactions, which

17:59:23.112 --> 17:59:26.442
allow you to do the equivalent of
sending a message to, or really locking

17:59:26.442 --> 17:59:29.472
out your roommate from accessing
that same variable, too,

17:59:29.472 --> 17:59:32.262
but for slightly less amount of time.

17:59:32.262 --> 17:59:34.222
There are solutions to these problems.

17:59:34.222 --> 17:59:37.992
So for instance, in Python,
the same code now in green

17:59:37.992 --> 17:59:39.702
might look a little something like this.

17:59:39.702 --> 17:59:42.492
When you know that something
has to happen all at once,

17:59:42.492 --> 17:59:46.362
altogether, you first begin a
transaction, and you do your thing,

17:59:46.362 --> 17:59:49.049
and then you commit the
transaction at the very end.

17:59:49.049 --> 17:59:51.132
Here, too, though, there's
going to be a downside.

17:59:51.132 --> 17:59:55.244
Typically, the more you use
transactions in this way,

17:59:55.244 --> 17:59:56.952
potentially the higher
the probability is

17:59:56.952 --> 18:00:00.532
that you're going to box someone out or
make Carter's request a little slower.

18:00:00.532 --> 18:00:01.032
Why?

18:00:01.032 --> 18:00:02.824
Because we can't interact
at the same time.

18:00:02.824 --> 18:00:05.262
Or you might make his request
fail if he tries to update

18:00:05.262 --> 18:00:07.212
something that's already been updated.

18:00:07.212 --> 18:00:10.062
So you generally want to
have as few lines of code

18:00:10.062 --> 18:00:13.182
together in between these transactions
so that you get in and you get out.

18:00:13.182 --> 18:00:16.122
And you go to CVS and you get
back really fast so as to not

18:00:16.122 --> 18:00:17.892
cause these kind of performance things.

18:00:17.892 --> 18:00:20.412
So things indeed
escalated quickly today.

18:00:20.412 --> 18:00:23.614
The original goal was just to solve
problems using a different language

18:00:23.614 --> 18:00:24.822
more effectively than Python.

18:00:24.822 --> 18:00:27.114
But as soon as you have these
more powerful techniques,

18:00:27.114 --> 18:00:28.867
a whole new set of problems arises.

18:00:28.867 --> 18:00:30.492
Takes practice to get comfortable with.

18:00:30.492 --> 18:00:34.512
But ultimately, this is all leading
us toward the introduction next week

18:00:34.512 --> 18:00:37.602
of web programming with HTML,
CSS, and some JavaScript.

18:00:37.602 --> 18:00:40.432
The week after, bringing Python
and SQL back into the mix.

18:00:40.432 --> 18:00:42.282
So that by term's end,
we've really now used

18:00:42.282 --> 18:00:45.102
all of these different languages
for what they're best at.

18:00:45.102 --> 18:00:48.185
And over the next few weeks, the goal
is to make sure you're understanding

18:00:48.185 --> 18:00:51.274
and comfortable with what each of
these things is good and bad for.

18:00:51.274 --> 18:00:52.482
Let's go ahead and wrap here.

18:00:52.482 --> 18:00:53.815
I'll stick around for questions.

18:00:53.815 --> 18:00:56.192
We'll see you next time.

18:00:56.192 --> 18:02:13.732
[MUSIC PLAYING]

18:02:13.732 --> 18:02:15.172
SPEAKER 1: All right.

18:02:15.172 --> 18:02:19.222
This is CS50, and this
is already week 8.

18:02:19.222 --> 18:02:21.705
And if we think back to
the past several weeks now,

18:02:21.705 --> 18:02:24.622
recall that things started pretty
interestingly, pretty interactively,

18:02:24.622 --> 18:02:27.049
in like week 0, when
we were using Scratch,

18:02:27.049 --> 18:02:29.632
because with Scratch we had a
GUI, a graphical user interface.

18:02:29.632 --> 18:02:33.172
So even as we explored variables and
loops and conditionals and all of that,

18:02:33.172 --> 18:02:36.084
you had kind of a fun environment
in which to express those ideas.

18:02:36.084 --> 18:02:37.792
And then in week 1,
we sort of took a lot

18:02:37.792 --> 18:02:41.692
of that away, when we introduced C, and
a terminal window, and a command line,

18:02:41.692 --> 18:02:46.372
because now, all of your programs became
very textual, very keyboard-based,

18:02:46.372 --> 18:02:49.432
and gone was the mouse, the
animations, the menus, and so forth.

18:02:49.432 --> 18:02:51.622
And so now, fast
forward to week 8, we're

18:02:51.622 --> 18:02:54.952
going to bring those kinds of
user interface, UI, elements back,

18:02:54.952 --> 18:02:56.512
in the form of web programming.

18:02:56.512 --> 18:02:58.852
And this goes beyond
just laying out websites.

18:02:58.852 --> 18:03:03.070
This will, to this week and next week,
combine elements of the back-end server

18:03:03.070 --> 18:03:05.362
stuff that we've been doing
for the past several weeks,

18:03:05.362 --> 18:03:07.972
using Python, using
SQL, and now introducing

18:03:07.972 --> 18:03:10.972
a couple of other languages,
on the so-called client side,

18:03:10.972 --> 18:03:13.462
on your own Mac, your own PC,
your own phone, that's going

18:03:13.462 --> 18:03:15.472
to talk to those back-end services.

18:03:15.472 --> 18:03:18.772
So indeed, at this end of
CS50, does everything rather

18:03:18.772 --> 18:03:22.132
come together into a user interface
that's just super familiar.

18:03:22.132 --> 18:03:24.952
All of us are on our phones,
desktops, laptops, every day.

18:03:24.952 --> 18:03:28.252
And increasingly, even the mobile
apps that you all are using

18:03:28.252 --> 18:03:32.512
are implemented, not necessarily
in languages like Swift or Java,

18:03:32.512 --> 18:03:34.642
if you're familiar with
those, but with languages

18:03:34.642 --> 18:03:38.552
called HTML, CSS, and JavaScript,
which we'll focus on here today.

18:03:38.552 --> 18:03:43.372
But before we do that, let's provide a
foundation on which these apps can run,

18:03:43.372 --> 18:03:46.822
because indeed, we'll start to look
underneath the hood of how the internet

18:03:46.822 --> 18:03:51.105
itself works, albeit quickly, so that
we have kind of a mental model for where

18:03:51.105 --> 18:03:54.022
all of this code is running, how you
can troubleshoot issues, and how,

18:03:54.022 --> 18:03:56.752
really, ultimately, after
CS50, you can learn, by just

18:03:56.752 --> 18:03:59.157
poking around other actual websites.

18:03:59.157 --> 18:04:00.532
So the internet, we're all on it.

18:04:00.532 --> 18:04:05.102
Literally, right now, what
is it, in your own words?

18:04:05.102 --> 18:04:07.672
What is the internet?

18:04:07.672 --> 18:04:10.372
It's this utility nowadays, that
we all rather take for granted.

18:04:10.372 --> 18:04:11.455
How would you describe it?

18:04:11.455 --> 18:04:12.655
AUDIENCE: Big storage.

18:04:12.655 --> 18:04:14.572
SPEAKER 1: OK, big
storage, and indeed, that's

18:04:14.572 --> 18:04:18.452
how the cloud is described, which is
kind of an abstraction if you will,

18:04:18.452 --> 18:04:21.772
for a whole lot of wires
and cables and hardware.

18:04:21.772 --> 18:04:24.942
And the internet, other
formulations of the term, how else?

18:04:24.942 --> 18:04:26.692
AUDIENCE: Bunch of
data that we can reach.

18:04:26.692 --> 18:04:28.609
SPEAKER 1: OK, a bunch
of data that we can all

18:04:28.609 --> 18:04:32.242
reach, by way of being interconnected
somehow with wires or wirelessly.

18:04:32.242 --> 18:04:35.002
And so really, the internet,
too, is a hardware thing.

18:04:35.002 --> 18:04:38.812
There's a whole lot of servers out
there, that are somehow interconnected,

18:04:38.812 --> 18:04:41.332
via physical cables, via
internet service providers,

18:04:41.332 --> 18:04:43.382
via wireless connectivity, and the like.

18:04:43.382 --> 18:04:46.162
And once you start to have
networks of networks of networks,

18:04:46.162 --> 18:04:47.362
do you get the internet.

18:04:47.362 --> 18:04:50.332
Indeed, Harvard has its own network
and Yale has its own network,

18:04:50.332 --> 18:04:52.687
and your own home probably
has its own network.

18:04:52.687 --> 18:04:54.562
But once you start
connecting those networks,

18:04:54.562 --> 18:04:58.922
do you get the interconnected network
that is the internet as we now know it?

18:04:58.922 --> 18:05:01.552
So there's this whole
alphabet soup that goes

18:05:01.552 --> 18:05:03.802
with the internet, some of
whose acronyms and terms

18:05:03.802 --> 18:05:04.732
you've probably seen before.

18:05:04.732 --> 18:05:06.532
But let's at least peel
back some of those layers

18:05:06.532 --> 18:05:08.752
and consider what some of
the building blocks are.

18:05:08.752 --> 18:05:11.752
So here's a picture of the internet
before it was known as the internet,

18:05:11.752 --> 18:05:14.182
back in 1969, when it
was something called

18:05:14.182 --> 18:05:17.482
ARPANET, from the Advanced
Research Projects Agency.

18:05:17.482 --> 18:05:20.872
And the intent, originally, was just
to Interconnect a few universities here

18:05:20.872 --> 18:05:25.702
in Utah and California, literally
servers, or computers, in each

18:05:25.702 --> 18:05:27.892
of those areas, somehow
interconnected with wires,

18:05:27.892 --> 18:05:29.617
so that people could
start to share data.

18:05:29.617 --> 18:05:33.182
A year later, it expanded to
include MIT and Harvard and others.

18:05:33.182 --> 18:05:36.202
And now fast forward to
today, you have a huge number

18:05:36.202 --> 18:05:39.322
of systems around the world
that are on this same network.

18:05:39.322 --> 18:05:41.842
And, in fact, if I
just pull up a web page

18:05:41.842 --> 18:05:44.302
here, that's sort of
constantly changing,

18:05:44.302 --> 18:05:48.802
a visualization of the internet as
it might now be today, this here,

18:05:48.802 --> 18:05:52.072
in the abstract, all of these
lines and interconnections

18:05:52.072 --> 18:05:56.009
represent just how interconnected
the world is today.

18:05:56.009 --> 18:05:59.092
And it just means that there's all the
more servers, all the more cabling,

18:05:59.092 --> 18:06:02.822
all of the more hardware giving
us this underlying infrastructure.

18:06:02.822 --> 18:06:07.402
But if we focus, really, on just
these nodes, these individual dots,

18:06:07.402 --> 18:06:11.872
whether back in 1970, or now in 2021,
each of these dots you can think of as,

18:06:11.872 --> 18:06:15.385
yes, a server, but a certain type
of server, namely known as a router.

18:06:15.385 --> 18:06:17.302
And a router, as the
name implies, just routes

18:06:17.302 --> 18:06:21.302
data left to right, top to
bottom, from one point to another.

18:06:21.302 --> 18:06:25.132
And so there's all these servers here
on campus at Harvard, on Yale's campus,

18:06:25.132 --> 18:06:28.652
in Comcast's network, Verizon's
network, your own home network,

18:06:28.652 --> 18:06:31.461
you have your own routers out
there, whose purpose in life

18:06:31.461 --> 18:06:34.277
is to take in data and then
decide, should I send it this way,

18:06:34.277 --> 18:06:36.652
or this way, or this way, so
to speak, assuming there are

18:06:36.652 --> 18:06:38.512
multiple options with multiple cables.

18:06:38.512 --> 18:06:41.692
You, in your home, probably have just
one cable coming in or going out.

18:06:41.692 --> 18:06:46.162
But certainly, if you're a place like
Harvard or Yale or Comcast or the like,

18:06:46.162 --> 18:06:49.222
there's probably a whole
bunch of interconnections

18:06:49.222 --> 18:06:51.892
that the data can then
travel across ultimately.

18:06:51.892 --> 18:06:54.982
So how do we get data
among these routers?

18:06:54.982 --> 18:06:57.652
For instance, if you want
to send an email to someone

18:06:57.652 --> 18:07:00.502
at Stanford, in California,
from here, on the East Coast,

18:07:00.502 --> 18:07:04.772
or if you want to visit
www.stanford.edu, how does your laptop,

18:07:04.772 --> 18:07:08.557
your phone, your desktop, actually
get data from point A to point B?

18:07:08.557 --> 18:07:12.112
Well, essentially, your
laptop or phone knows

18:07:12.112 --> 18:07:16.142
when it boots up at the beginning of
the day, what the local router is, what

18:07:16.142 --> 18:07:17.682
the address of that local router is.

18:07:17.682 --> 18:07:20.492
So if you want to send an
email from my laptop over here,

18:07:20.492 --> 18:07:23.702
my laptop is essentially going to
hand it to the nearest Harvard router.

18:07:23.702 --> 18:07:25.712
And then, from there, I
don't know, I don't care

18:07:25.712 --> 18:07:27.254
how it gets the rest of the distance.

18:07:27.254 --> 18:07:29.822
But hopefully, within some
small number of steps later,

18:07:29.822 --> 18:07:32.462
Harvard's router is going to
send it to maybe Boston's router

18:07:32.462 --> 18:07:34.587
is going to send it to
California's router is going

18:07:34.587 --> 18:07:37.772
to send it to Stanford's router, until
finally it reaches Stanford's email

18:07:37.772 --> 18:07:38.372
server.

18:07:38.372 --> 18:07:41.822
And we can depict this, actually,
how about a bit playfully.

18:07:41.822 --> 18:07:44.072
Thankfully, the course's
staff kindly volunteered

18:07:44.072 --> 18:07:48.792
to create a visualization for
this, using a familiar technology.

18:07:48.792 --> 18:07:52.322
So here we have some of our TFs
and TAs and CAs present and past.

18:07:52.322 --> 18:07:55.472
Let me go ahead and full
screen this window here.

18:07:55.472 --> 18:07:58.392
Give me just a moment to
pull it up on my screen here.

18:07:58.392 --> 18:08:03.182
And we'll consider what happens if we
want to send a packet of information

18:08:03.182 --> 18:08:07.502
from one person or router,
namely Phyllis in this case,

18:08:07.502 --> 18:08:10.562
in the bottom right hand corner,
up to Brian, in this case,

18:08:10.562 --> 18:08:11.832
in the top left hand corner.

18:08:11.832 --> 18:08:14.462
So each of the staff members
here represents exactly one

18:08:14.462 --> 18:08:17.052
of these routers on the internet.

18:08:17.052 --> 18:08:21.552
[MUSIC PLAYING]

18:08:44.922 --> 18:08:46.412
[APPLAUSE]

18:08:46.412 --> 18:08:47.612
The applause is appreciated.

18:08:47.612 --> 18:08:49.892
It actually took us a
significant number of attempts

18:08:49.892 --> 18:08:51.482
to get that ultimately right.

18:08:51.482 --> 18:08:54.632
So when, what was it the
staff were all passing here?

18:08:54.632 --> 18:08:57.715
Here we have just, physically, what
it was the staff were passing around.

18:08:57.715 --> 18:08:59.882
So Phyllis started with an
envelope, inside of which

18:08:59.882 --> 18:09:01.952
was that email, presumably,
on the East Coast,

18:09:01.952 --> 18:09:05.137
and she wanted to send it to Brian on
the West Coast, top left hand corner.

18:09:05.137 --> 18:09:08.012
And so she had all of these different
options, different connections,

18:09:08.012 --> 18:09:11.112
between her and point B, namely Brian.

18:09:11.112 --> 18:09:14.672
She could go up, down, in her case, and
then each of those subsequent routers

18:09:14.672 --> 18:09:17.462
could go up, down, left, or right,
until it finally reaches Brian.

18:09:17.462 --> 18:09:19.442
And long story short,
there's algorithms that

18:09:19.442 --> 18:09:22.812
figure out how you decide
to send a packet up, down,

18:09:22.812 --> 18:09:24.662
left, or right, so to speak.

18:09:24.662 --> 18:09:29.717
But they do so by taking an input, and
in the form of input is this envelope.

18:09:29.717 --> 18:09:32.342
And there's at least a couple of
things on the outside of this,

18:09:32.342 --> 18:09:35.282
because all of these routers and,
in turn, all of our Macs and PCs

18:09:35.282 --> 18:09:38.012
and phones these days,
speak something called

18:09:38.012 --> 18:09:41.132
TCP/IP, a set of
acronyms you've probably

18:09:41.132 --> 18:09:44.012
seen somewhere on your
phone, your Mac or PC,

18:09:44.012 --> 18:09:48.422
in print somewhere, which refers
to two protocols, two conventions,

18:09:48.422 --> 18:09:51.195
that computers use to
inter-communicate these days.

18:09:51.195 --> 18:09:52.112
Now what's a protocol?

18:09:52.112 --> 18:09:54.632
A protocol is like a set
of rules, that you behave.

18:09:54.632 --> 18:09:57.092
In healthier times, I might
extend my hand and someone

18:09:57.092 --> 18:10:00.632
like Carter might extend his hand,
thereby interacting with me, based

18:10:00.632 --> 18:10:03.872
on a human protocol of like
literally physically shaking hands.

18:10:03.872 --> 18:10:07.082
Nowadays, we have mask protocols,
whereby what you need to do

18:10:07.082 --> 18:10:08.592
is wear a mask indoors.

18:10:08.592 --> 18:10:11.642
But that, too, is just a set of rules
that we all follow and adhere to,

18:10:11.642 --> 18:10:13.952
that's somewhere
standardized and documented.

18:10:13.952 --> 18:10:16.712
So computers use protocols
all the time to govern

18:10:16.712 --> 18:10:19.622
how they are sending information
and receiving information.

18:10:19.622 --> 18:10:24.482
And TCP and IP are two such protocols
that standardize this as follows.

18:10:24.482 --> 18:10:27.212
What TCP/IP tells someone
like Phyllis to do,

18:10:27.212 --> 18:10:31.152
if she wants to send an email to Brian,
is put the email in a virtual envelope,

18:10:31.152 --> 18:10:31.902
so to speak.

18:10:31.902 --> 18:10:37.082
But on the outside of that virtual
envelope, put Brian's unique address.

18:10:37.082 --> 18:10:41.132
And I'll describe this as destination
on the middle of the envelope,

18:10:41.132 --> 18:10:44.012
just like in our human world,
you would write the destination

18:10:44.012 --> 18:10:45.332
address on the envelope.

18:10:45.332 --> 18:10:49.202
And then she's going to put her own
source address in the top left hand

18:10:49.202 --> 18:10:51.751
corner, just like you, the
sender, would put your own source

18:10:51.751 --> 18:10:53.172
address in the human world.

18:10:53.172 --> 18:10:56.432
But, instead of these addresses
being like something Kirkland Street,

18:10:56.432 --> 18:11:01.142
Cambridge, Massachusetts 02138, USA,
you probably know that computers

18:11:01.142 --> 18:11:05.642
on the internet have unique addresses
of their own, known as IP addresses.

18:11:05.642 --> 18:11:08.192
And an IP address is
just a numeric identifier

18:11:08.192 --> 18:11:11.462
on the internet, that allows
computers, like Phyllis and Brian,

18:11:11.462 --> 18:11:14.432
to address these envelopes
to and from each other.

18:11:14.432 --> 18:11:16.742
And you've probably seen
the format at some point.

18:11:16.742 --> 18:11:20.312
Typically, the format of IP
addresses is something dot something

18:11:20.312 --> 18:11:22.262
dot something dot something.

18:11:22.262 --> 18:11:24.872
Each of those somethings,
represented here with a hash symbol,

18:11:24.872 --> 18:11:29.852
is a number from 0 through 255.

18:11:29.852 --> 18:11:32.912
And, based on that little
hint, if each of these hashes

18:11:32.912 --> 18:11:36.632
represents a number from 0
to 255, each of those hashes

18:11:36.632 --> 18:11:39.542
is represented with
how many bytes or bits?

18:11:39.542 --> 18:11:43.442
Eight bits or one byte, which is to
say, we can extrapolate from there,

18:11:43.442 --> 18:11:47.072
an IP address must use
32 bits or 4 bytes,

18:11:47.072 --> 18:11:50.642
if we rewind now to some of the
primitives we looked at in week 0.

18:11:50.642 --> 18:11:52.992
And what that means is,
at least at a glance,

18:11:52.992 --> 18:11:57.154
it looks like we have 4 billion some
odd IP addresses available to us.

18:11:57.154 --> 18:11:58.862
Now, unfortunately,
there's a huge number

18:11:58.862 --> 18:12:01.772
of humans in the world these days,
all of whom have, many of whom

18:12:01.772 --> 18:12:05.222
have multiple devices, certainly
in places like this, where you have

18:12:05.222 --> 18:12:08.975
a laptop, and a phone, and you have
other internet of things-type devices,

18:12:08.975 --> 18:12:10.392
all of which need to be addressed.

18:12:10.392 --> 18:12:12.332
So there's another type
of IP address that's

18:12:12.332 --> 18:12:14.012
starting to be used more commonly.

18:12:14.012 --> 18:12:16.412
This is version 4 of IP.

18:12:16.412 --> 18:12:19.232
There's also version 6
which, instead of 32 bits,

18:12:19.232 --> 18:12:23.552
uses 128 bits, which gives us a
crazy number of possible addresses

18:12:23.552 --> 18:12:27.872
for computers, so we can at least handle
all of the additional devices we now

18:12:27.872 --> 18:12:28.812
have today.

18:12:28.812 --> 18:12:31.772
So this is to say, what ultimately
is going on this envelope

18:12:31.772 --> 18:12:35.942
is the destination address, that is
Brian's IP address, and the source

18:12:35.942 --> 18:12:40.022
address, that is Phyllis's IP address,
so that this packet can go from point A

18:12:40.022 --> 18:12:43.052
to point B, and if need
be, back, by just flipping

18:12:43.052 --> 18:12:44.642
the source and the destination.

18:12:44.642 --> 18:12:49.652
But on the internet, you presumably know
that there's not just email servers.

18:12:49.652 --> 18:12:53.192
There's web servers, there's chat
servers, video servers, game servers.

18:12:53.192 --> 18:12:56.192
Like there's all of these different
functions on the internet nowadays.

18:12:56.192 --> 18:12:58.772
And so, when Brian
receives that envelope,

18:12:58.772 --> 18:13:05.432
how does he know it's an email, versus
a web page, versus a Skype call,

18:13:05.432 --> 18:13:07.622
versus something else altogether.

18:13:07.622 --> 18:13:10.772
Well, it turns out that we
can look at the other part

18:13:10.772 --> 18:13:14.282
of this acronym, the TCP in TCP/IP.

18:13:14.282 --> 18:13:17.172
And what TCP allows us
to do, for instance,

18:13:17.172 --> 18:13:18.822
is specify a couple of things.

18:13:18.822 --> 18:13:23.792
One, the type of service whose
data is in this envelope, that is,

18:13:23.792 --> 18:13:26.882
it does this with a numeric identifier.

18:13:26.882 --> 18:13:31.652
And I'm going to go ahead and write down
a colon, and the word port, P-O-R-T.

18:13:31.652 --> 18:13:35.222
And I'm going to write that in the
source address, too, colon and port.

18:13:35.222 --> 18:13:37.382
So technically, now,
what's on this envelope

18:13:37.382 --> 18:13:40.112
is not just the addresses,
but also a unique number

18:13:40.112 --> 18:13:45.182
that represents what kind of service
is being sent from point A to point B,

18:13:45.182 --> 18:13:48.792
whether it's email, or web traffic,
or Skype, or something else.

18:13:48.792 --> 18:13:53.342
These numbers are standardized, and here
are just two of the most common ones,

18:13:53.342 --> 18:13:56.192
not even in the context of email,
but in the context of the web.

18:13:56.192 --> 18:13:59.132
Port 80 is typically used
whenever an envelope contains

18:13:59.132 --> 18:14:03.362
a web page, or a request
therefor, or the number 443,

18:14:03.362 --> 18:14:07.202
when that request is actually
encrypted, using that thing you probably

18:14:07.202 --> 18:14:11.192
know, in URLs, known as HTTPS,
where the S literally means secure.

18:14:11.192 --> 18:14:13.082
More on what the HTTP means later.

18:14:13.082 --> 18:14:16.955
If it's email, the number
might be 25 or 465, or 587.

18:14:16.955 --> 18:14:19.872
These are the kinds of things you
Google if you ultimately care about.

18:14:19.872 --> 18:14:24.272
But if you've ever had to configure,
like, Outlook or even Gmail

18:14:24.272 --> 18:14:26.672
to talk to another account,
you might very well

18:14:26.672 --> 18:14:30.902
have seen these numbers, by typing
in something like SMTP.Gmail.com

18:14:30.902 --> 18:14:33.892
and then a number, which is only to
say these numbers are omnipresent.

18:14:33.892 --> 18:14:35.642
But they're typically
not things you and I

18:14:35.642 --> 18:14:38.582
have to care about, because
servers and computers nowadays

18:14:38.582 --> 18:14:40.812
automate much of this process.

18:14:40.812 --> 18:14:45.212
But that's all it takes, ultimately, for
Phyllis to get this message to Brian.

18:14:45.212 --> 18:14:47.042
But what if it's a really big message?

18:14:47.042 --> 18:14:50.352
If it's a short email, It might
fit perfectly in one single packet,

18:14:50.352 --> 18:14:50.942
so to speak.

18:14:50.942 --> 18:14:54.242
But suppose that Phyllis wants
to send Brian a picture of a cat,

18:14:54.242 --> 18:14:56.582
like this, or worse, a video of a cat.

18:14:56.582 --> 18:15:00.812
It would be kind of inequitable
if no one else could do anything

18:15:00.812 --> 18:15:03.062
on the internet, just
because Phyllis wants

18:15:03.062 --> 18:15:06.602
to send Brian a really big picture,
a really big video of a cat.

18:15:06.602 --> 18:15:10.352
It would be nice if we could kind
of time-share the interconnections,

18:15:10.352 --> 18:15:13.530
across these routers, so that
we can give a little bit of time

18:15:13.530 --> 18:15:15.572
to Phyllis, a little bit
of time to someone else,

18:15:15.572 --> 18:15:18.780
a little bit of time to someone else,
so that eventually, Phyllis' entire cat

18:15:18.780 --> 18:15:19.892
gets through the internet.

18:15:19.892 --> 18:15:25.472
But in terms of fairness, she
doesn't monopolize the bandwidth

18:15:25.472 --> 18:15:27.842
of the network in question.

18:15:27.842 --> 18:15:31.982
And this, then, allows us to
do one other feature of TCP/IP,

18:15:31.982 --> 18:15:35.072
which is fragmentation,
where we can temporarily,

18:15:35.072 --> 18:15:37.952
and Phyllis's computer would
do this automatically, fragment

18:15:37.952 --> 18:15:41.582
the big packet in question,
or the big file in question,

18:15:41.582 --> 18:15:46.562
and then use, not just a single
envelope, but maybe a second, a third,

18:15:46.562 --> 18:15:48.370
and a fourth, or more.

18:15:48.370 --> 18:15:50.162
If we do that, though,
we're probably going

18:15:50.162 --> 18:15:54.032
to need one other piece of information,
just logically, on these envelopes.

18:15:54.032 --> 18:15:57.332
Like, if you were implementing this,
chopping up this picture of a cat

18:15:57.332 --> 18:16:00.302
into four parts, like,
intuitively, what might you

18:16:00.302 --> 18:16:03.912
want to put virtually on the
outside of this envelope now?

18:16:03.912 --> 18:16:04.412
Yeah.

18:16:04.412 --> 18:16:05.259
AUDIENCE: The order.

18:16:05.259 --> 18:16:06.842
SPEAKER 1: The order of them, somehow.

18:16:06.842 --> 18:16:10.472
So probably something like part
one of four, part two of four,

18:16:10.472 --> 18:16:12.162
part three of four, and so forth.

18:16:12.162 --> 18:16:14.792
So I'm going to write one more thing
in like the memo line of the envelope

18:16:14.792 --> 18:16:15.302
here.

18:16:15.302 --> 18:16:17.552
I put some kind of
sequence number, that's

18:16:17.552 --> 18:16:20.192
just a little bit of a
clue to Brian, to know

18:16:20.192 --> 18:16:22.172
in what order to
reassemble these things.

18:16:22.172 --> 18:16:25.202
And even more powerfully
than that, this actually

18:16:25.202 --> 18:16:29.252
gives us this simple primitive of
just using INTs on these envelopes,

18:16:29.252 --> 18:16:30.452
in these packets.

18:16:30.452 --> 18:16:35.882
If Brian receives envelopes like these,
with numbers like these in the memo

18:16:35.882 --> 18:16:39.692
field, what other feature
does TCP apparently

18:16:39.692 --> 18:16:42.542
enable Brian and Phyllis to implement?

18:16:42.542 --> 18:16:44.282
This is a bit subtle.

18:16:44.282 --> 18:16:46.612
But it's not just the
ordering of the packets.

18:16:46.612 --> 18:16:50.702
What else might be useful about
putting numbers on these things,

18:16:50.702 --> 18:16:52.989
might you think?

18:16:52.989 --> 18:16:54.072
What might be useful here?

18:16:54.072 --> 18:16:55.188
Yeah, in back.

18:16:55.188 --> 18:16:56.932
AUDIENCE: How about if you like missed.

18:16:56.932 --> 18:16:58.852
SPEAKER 1: If you missed something
that was intended to be sent,

18:16:58.852 --> 18:16:59.852
if I heard that correct.

18:16:59.852 --> 18:17:04.110
So short answer, exactly, yes, TCP,
because of this simple little integer

18:17:04.110 --> 18:17:06.652
that we're including, can quote
unquote "guarantee" delivery.

18:17:06.652 --> 18:17:07.162
Why?

18:17:07.162 --> 18:17:10.342
Because if Brian receives one
out of four, two out of four,

18:17:10.342 --> 18:17:12.562
four out of four, but
not three out of four,

18:17:12.562 --> 18:17:16.402
he now knows, predictably, that
he needs to ask Phyllis, somehow,

18:17:16.402 --> 18:17:17.912
to resend that packet.

18:17:17.912 --> 18:17:21.802
And so this is why pretty much
always, if you receive an email,

18:17:21.802 --> 18:17:24.412
you either receive the whole
thing, or nothing at all.

18:17:24.412 --> 18:17:27.742
Like sentences and words and
paragraphs should never really

18:17:27.742 --> 18:17:29.362
be missing from an email.

18:17:29.362 --> 18:17:31.479
Or if you download a
photograph on the web,

18:17:31.479 --> 18:17:33.562
it shouldn't just have a
blank hole in the middle,

18:17:33.562 --> 18:17:36.652
just because that packet of
information happened to be lost.

18:17:36.652 --> 18:17:40.852
TCP, if it is the protocol being used to
transmit data from point A to point B,

18:17:40.852 --> 18:17:44.792
ensures that it either all gets there,
or ultimately, none of it at all.

18:17:44.792 --> 18:17:48.112
So this is an important property,
but, just as a teaser there's

18:17:48.112 --> 18:17:49.312
other protocols out there.

18:17:49.312 --> 18:17:52.732
There's something called UDP,
which is an alternative to TCP,

18:17:52.732 --> 18:17:54.472
that doesn't guarantee delivery.

18:17:54.472 --> 18:17:58.072
And just as a taste of why you might
ever not want to guarantee delivery,

18:17:58.072 --> 18:18:03.367
maybe you're watching like a streaming
video, like a sports event online.

18:18:03.367 --> 18:18:05.242
You probably don't
necessarily want the thing

18:18:05.242 --> 18:18:08.477
to buffer and buffer and buffer, just
because you have a slow connection,

18:18:08.477 --> 18:18:10.352
because you're going to
start to miss things.

18:18:10.352 --> 18:18:12.472
And then you're going to be the
only one in the world watching

18:18:12.472 --> 18:18:15.850
the game that ended 20 minutes ago, when
everyone else is sort of up to speed.

18:18:15.850 --> 18:18:18.142
Similarly for a voice call,
it would be really annoying

18:18:18.142 --> 18:18:19.942
if our voice is constantly buffered.

18:18:19.942 --> 18:18:22.679
So UDP might be a good
protocol for making sure

18:18:22.679 --> 18:18:25.762
that, even if the person on the other
end sounds a little crappy, at least

18:18:25.762 --> 18:18:26.992
you can hear them.

18:18:26.992 --> 18:18:29.782
It's not pausing and
resending and resending,

18:18:29.782 --> 18:18:33.962
because that would really slow down
that sort of human interaction.

18:18:33.962 --> 18:18:36.862
So, in short, IP handles the
addressing of these packets,

18:18:36.862 --> 18:18:40.342
and standardizes numbers that every
computer, your own included, gets,

18:18:40.342 --> 18:18:44.542
and TCP handles the standardization
of like what services

18:18:44.542 --> 18:18:50.662
can be used, between points A and
point B. All right, this is great,

18:18:50.662 --> 18:18:54.652
but presumably, when Phyllis
sends a message to Brian,

18:18:54.652 --> 18:18:56.782
she doesn't really know
and probably shouldn't

18:18:56.782 --> 18:18:58.912
care what his IP address is, right?

18:18:58.912 --> 18:19:01.492
These days it's, like, I don't
know most of the phone numbers

18:19:01.492 --> 18:19:02.662
that my friends have.

18:19:02.662 --> 18:19:04.614
I instead look them up in some way.

18:19:04.614 --> 18:19:07.072
And, indeed, when you visit a
website, what do you type in?

18:19:07.072 --> 18:19:10.049
It's typically not something
dot something dot something dot

18:19:10.049 --> 18:19:12.299
something, where each of
those somethings is a number.

18:19:12.299 --> 18:19:14.182
What do you typically
type in to a browser?

18:19:14.182 --> 18:19:15.622
So a domain name, right?

18:19:15.622 --> 18:19:20.031
Something like Stanford.edu,
Harvard.edu, Yale.edu, gmail.com,

18:19:20.031 --> 18:19:22.072
or any other such domain name.

18:19:22.072 --> 18:19:24.622
And so, thankfully,
there's another system

18:19:24.622 --> 18:19:29.551
on the internet, one more acronym for
today, called DNS, domain name system.

18:19:29.551 --> 18:19:33.742
And pretty much every network on the
internet, Harvard's, Yale's, Comcast's,

18:19:33.742 --> 18:19:37.839
your own home network, somewhere,
somehow has a DNS server.

18:19:37.839 --> 18:19:39.922
You probably didn't have
to configure it yourself.

18:19:39.922 --> 18:19:44.342
Someone else did, your campus, your
job, your internet service provider.

18:19:44.342 --> 18:19:48.112
But there is some server connected
somehow to the network you're on,

18:19:48.112 --> 18:19:52.702
via wires or wirelessly, that just
has a really big table in its memory,

18:19:52.702 --> 18:19:55.672
a big spreadsheet, if you
will, or, if you prefer,

18:19:55.672 --> 18:19:59.992
a hash table, that has at least
two columns of keys and values

18:19:59.992 --> 18:20:00.812
respectively.

18:20:00.812 --> 18:20:02.242
Where on the left hand
side is what we'll

18:20:02.242 --> 18:20:04.262
call domain name,
something like Harvard.edu,

18:20:04.262 --> 18:20:08.902
Yale.edu, an IP address on the
right hand side, that is to say,

18:20:08.902 --> 18:20:13.612
a DNS server's purpose in life
is just to translate domain names

18:20:13.612 --> 18:20:14.897
to IP addresses.

18:20:14.897 --> 18:20:17.272
And vice versa, if you want
to go in the other direction,

18:20:17.272 --> 18:20:22.072
and technically, just to be precise, it
translates fully qualified domain names

18:20:22.072 --> 18:20:23.432
to IP addresses.

18:20:23.432 --> 18:20:25.442
And we'll see what those
are in just a moment.

18:20:25.442 --> 18:20:27.652
But again, all of this just
kind of happens magically

18:20:27.652 --> 18:20:29.402
when you turn on your
phone or your laptop

18:20:29.402 --> 18:20:33.292
today, because all of these things
are pre-configured for us nowadays.

18:20:33.292 --> 18:20:38.752
So how can we actually start to
see some of these things in action?

18:20:38.752 --> 18:20:44.912
Well, let's go ahead and poke around,
for instance, at a couple of URLs here.

18:20:44.912 --> 18:20:48.532
Let's see what we can actually do
now with these basic primitives.

18:20:48.532 --> 18:20:52.672
If we now have the ability to
move data from point A to point B,

18:20:52.672 --> 18:20:55.252
and what can be in that envelope
could be, yes, an email,

18:20:55.252 --> 18:20:58.012
but today, onward, it's really
going to be web content.

18:20:58.012 --> 18:21:00.202
There's going to be content
that you're requesting,

18:21:00.202 --> 18:21:01.584
like give me today's home page.

18:21:01.584 --> 18:21:03.292
And there's content
you're sending, which

18:21:03.292 --> 18:21:05.512
would be the contents of
that actual home page.

18:21:05.512 --> 18:21:10.642
And so, just to go one level deeper,
now that we have these packets that

18:21:10.642 --> 18:21:14.602
are getting from point A to
point B using TCP/IP, let's

18:21:14.602 --> 18:21:19.222
put something specific inside of them,
not just an email and a bunch of text,

18:21:19.222 --> 18:21:24.150
but something called HTTP, which
stands for hypertext transfer protocol.

18:21:24.150 --> 18:21:25.942
You've seen this for
decades now, probably,

18:21:25.942 --> 18:21:29.332
in the form of URLs, so much so that you
probably don't even type it nowadays.

18:21:29.332 --> 18:21:31.522
Your browser just adds
it for you automatically,

18:21:31.522 --> 18:21:35.102
and you just type in Harvard.edu,
or Yale.edu, or the like.

18:21:35.102 --> 18:21:38.392
But HTTP is just a final
protocol that we'll

18:21:38.392 --> 18:21:42.412
talk about here, that just
standardizes how web browsers and web

18:21:42.412 --> 18:21:44.612
servers inter-communicate.

18:21:44.612 --> 18:21:47.602
So this is a distinction now
between the internet and the web.

18:21:47.602 --> 18:21:50.002
The internet is really like
the low-level plumbing,

18:21:50.002 --> 18:21:53.092
all of the cables, all of a
technology that just moves packets

18:21:53.092 --> 18:21:57.322
from left to right, right to left, top
to bottom, that gets data from point A

18:21:57.322 --> 18:22:01.942
to point B. You can do anything you
want on top of that internet nowadays,

18:22:01.942 --> 18:22:06.122
email and web and video and chat
and gaming, and all of that.

18:22:06.122 --> 18:22:09.652
So HTTP, or the web,
is just one application

18:22:09.652 --> 18:22:13.695
that is conceptually on top of,
built on top of the internet.

18:22:13.695 --> 18:22:15.862
Once you take for granted
that there is an internet,

18:22:15.862 --> 18:22:17.452
you can do really
interesting things with it,

18:22:17.452 --> 18:22:19.942
just like in our physical world,
once you have electricity,

18:22:19.942 --> 18:22:22.942
you can just assume you can do really
interesting things with that, too,

18:22:22.942 --> 18:22:25.102
without even knowing
or caring how it works.

18:22:25.102 --> 18:22:28.802
But now that you'll be
programming for the web,

18:22:28.802 --> 18:22:32.132
it's useful to understand how
some of these things indeed work.

18:22:32.132 --> 18:22:36.202
So let's take a peek at the
format of the things that

18:22:36.202 --> 18:22:37.402
go inside of these messages.

18:22:37.402 --> 18:22:39.922
These days, it's usually
actually HTTPS that's

18:22:39.922 --> 18:22:42.172
in play, where, again,
the S just means secure.

18:22:42.172 --> 18:22:47.092
More on that later, but the HTTP is
what standardizes what kinds of messages

18:22:47.092 --> 18:22:48.472
go inside of these envelopes.

18:22:48.472 --> 18:22:52.282
And wonderfully, it's just
textual information, typically.

18:22:52.282 --> 18:22:56.992
There is a simple text format
that humans decided on years ago,

18:22:56.992 --> 18:23:00.442
that goes inside of these
envelopes, that tells a browser how

18:23:00.442 --> 18:23:04.762
to request information from a server,
and how to respond from the server

18:23:04.762 --> 18:23:06.392
to that client with information.

18:23:06.392 --> 18:23:12.592
So here's, for instance, a canonical
URL, https://www.example.com.

18:23:12.592 --> 18:23:14.362
What might you see at the end of this?

18:23:14.362 --> 18:23:15.830
You might sometimes see a slash.

18:23:15.830 --> 18:23:18.622
Browsers nowadays kind of simplify
things and don't show it to you.

18:23:18.622 --> 18:23:21.772
But slash, as we'll see, just
represents like the default

18:23:21.772 --> 18:23:24.652
folder, the root of the
web server's hard drive,

18:23:24.652 --> 18:23:26.077
like whatever the base is of it.

18:23:26.077 --> 18:23:32.182
It's like C colon backslash on
Windows, or it's my computer on Mac OS.

18:23:32.182 --> 18:23:34.432
But a URL can have more than that.

18:23:34.432 --> 18:23:36.982
It can have slash path,
where path is just a word,

18:23:36.982 --> 18:23:40.552
or multiple words, that sort of
describe a longer part of the URL.

18:23:40.552 --> 18:23:43.072
That path could actually be
a specific file, we'll see,

18:23:43.072 --> 18:23:45.262
like something called file.html.

18:23:45.262 --> 18:23:48.682
More on HTML in just a bit, or
it can even be slash folder,

18:23:48.682 --> 18:23:52.912
maybe with another slash, or
maybe it can be /folder/file.html.

18:23:52.912 --> 18:23:57.082
Now these days Safari, and even Chrome
to some extent, and other browsers,

18:23:57.082 --> 18:24:00.622
are in the habit of trying to hide
more and more of these details

18:24:00.622 --> 18:24:02.362
from you and me.

18:24:02.362 --> 18:24:05.122
Ultimately, though, it'll
be useful to understand

18:24:05.122 --> 18:24:08.812
what URLs you're at, because
it maps directly to the code,

18:24:08.812 --> 18:24:10.642
that we're ultimately going to write.

18:24:10.642 --> 18:24:13.282
But this is only to say that
all this stuff in yellow

18:24:13.282 --> 18:24:18.322
refers to, presumably, a specific
file and/or folder on the web

18:24:18.322 --> 18:24:20.214
server, on which you're programming.

18:24:20.214 --> 18:24:21.172
All right, what's this?

18:24:21.172 --> 18:24:26.002
Example.com, this is the domain
name, as we described it earlier.

18:24:26.002 --> 18:24:28.622
Example.com is the
so-called domain name.

18:24:28.622 --> 18:24:33.532
This whole thing, www.example.com,
is the fully qualified domain name.

18:24:33.532 --> 18:24:37.432
And what the WW is referring
to is specifically the name

18:24:37.432 --> 18:24:40.292
of a specific server in that domain.

18:24:40.292 --> 18:24:44.842
So back in the day, there was
a www.example.com web server.

18:24:44.842 --> 18:24:48.442
There might have been a
mail.example.com mail server.

18:24:48.442 --> 18:24:51.502
There might have been a
chat.example.com chat server.

18:24:51.502 --> 18:24:56.332
Nowadays, this hostname, or
subdomain, depending on the context,

18:24:56.332 --> 18:24:58.582
can actually refer to a whole
bunch of servers, right?

18:24:58.582 --> 18:25:01.792
When you go to www.facebook.com,
that's not one server,

18:25:01.792 --> 18:25:03.652
that's thousands of servers nowadays.

18:25:03.652 --> 18:25:05.632
So long story short,
there's technology that

18:25:05.632 --> 18:25:08.122
somehow get your data
to one of those servers,

18:25:08.122 --> 18:25:11.492
but this whole thing is what we
meant by fully qualified domain name.

18:25:11.492 --> 18:25:13.822
This thing here, hostname,
in the context of an email

18:25:13.822 --> 18:25:16.702
address it might alternatively
be called a subdomain.

18:25:16.702 --> 18:25:20.062
This thing here, top
level domain, you probably

18:25:20.062 --> 18:25:23.422
know that dot com means commercial,
although anyone can buy it these days.

18:25:23.422 --> 18:25:25.762
Dot org is similar, dot net.

18:25:25.762 --> 18:25:29.182
Some of them are a bit restricted,
dot mil is just for the US military,

18:25:29.182 --> 18:25:31.712
dot edu is just for accredited
educational institutions.

18:25:31.712 --> 18:25:35.212
But there are hundreds, if
not more, top level domains

18:25:35.212 --> 18:25:37.702
nowadays, some more popular than others.

18:25:37.702 --> 18:25:41.542
CS50's tools, for instance, use CS50.io.

18:25:41.542 --> 18:25:44.182
IO sort of connotes input-output.

18:25:44.182 --> 18:25:49.132
It actually belongs, though, to
a small island nation, a country,

18:25:49.132 --> 18:25:55.367
whose country code is .io, and you see
other two letter top level domains that

18:25:55.367 --> 18:25:56.242
are country specific.

18:25:56.242 --> 18:26:00.400
Indeed, it's something.uk,
something.jp, and the like typically

18:26:00.400 --> 18:26:01.192
refer to countries.

18:26:01.192 --> 18:26:04.042
But some of them have been
rather co-opted, .tv as well,

18:26:04.042 --> 18:26:06.932
because they have these
meanings in English as well.

18:26:06.932 --> 18:26:08.992
Lastly, this is what
we'll call the protocol.

18:26:08.992 --> 18:26:13.042
That specifies how the server uses
this URL to get data from point A

18:26:13.042 --> 18:26:16.352
to point B. So what is
inside of this envelope?

18:26:16.352 --> 18:26:18.622
Let's now start poking
around a little bit more.

18:26:18.622 --> 18:26:20.212
What is inside of this envelope?

18:26:20.212 --> 18:26:23.782
It's essentially, for our
purposes today, one of two verbs,

18:26:23.782 --> 18:26:25.792
either GET or POST.

18:26:25.792 --> 18:26:28.612
And if any of you have dabbled
with HTML or made your own website,

18:26:28.612 --> 18:26:30.570
you might have seen some
of these terms before.

18:26:30.570 --> 18:26:34.702
But these two verbs describe
just how to send information

18:26:34.702 --> 18:26:36.952
from you to the server.

18:26:36.952 --> 18:26:39.172
Long story short, more
on this next week,

18:26:39.172 --> 18:26:43.472
GET means put any user input
in the URL, POST means hide it,

18:26:43.472 --> 18:26:46.642
so that things you're searching for,
credit card numbers you're typing in,

18:26:46.642 --> 18:26:49.634
usernames and passwords you're
inputting, don't show up in the URL,

18:26:49.634 --> 18:26:51.592
and are therefore visible
to anyone with access

18:26:51.592 --> 18:26:53.672
to your computer and
your search history,

18:26:53.672 --> 18:26:57.802
but rather they're somehow provided
elsewhere, deeper into that envelope.

18:26:57.802 --> 18:27:00.292
But for now, we'll focus
almost entirely on GET,

18:27:00.292 --> 18:27:03.742
which is perhaps the most common
one that we're always going to use.

18:27:03.742 --> 18:27:05.252
And what we're going to do is this.

18:27:05.252 --> 18:27:07.462
Let me switch over just
to a blank screen here.

18:27:07.462 --> 18:27:11.182
And if we assume that little
old me is this laptop here,

18:27:11.182 --> 18:27:16.192
and I'm connected to the cloud, and
in that cloud is some server that I

18:27:16.192 --> 18:27:20.182
want to request the web page
of, Harvard.edu or Yale.edu,

18:27:20.182 --> 18:27:22.982
it's really going to
be a two-step process.

18:27:22.982 --> 18:27:27.892
There's going to be a request,
that goes from point A to point B,

18:27:27.892 --> 18:27:29.752
and then, hopefully,
the server that hears

18:27:29.752 --> 18:27:34.012
that request is going to reply with
what we'll typically call a response.

18:27:34.012 --> 18:27:37.042
And other terms that are
relevant here, is my laptop

18:27:37.042 --> 18:27:40.642
is the so-called client,
Harvard.edu, Yale.edu, whatever

18:27:40.642 --> 18:27:42.352
it is, is the so-called server.

18:27:42.352 --> 18:27:45.442
And just like in a restaurant, where
you might request something to eat,

18:27:45.442 --> 18:27:47.092
the server might bring it to you.

18:27:47.092 --> 18:27:49.372
It's, again, that kind of
bidirectional relationship.

18:27:49.372 --> 18:27:54.382
One request, one response, for
each such web page we request.

18:27:54.382 --> 18:27:58.042
All right, so what's inside these
envelopes, and what do we actually see?

18:27:58.042 --> 18:28:01.012
Well, this arrow, this line I
just drew from left to right,

18:28:01.012 --> 18:28:05.302
representing the request, technically
looks a little more like this.

18:28:05.302 --> 18:28:08.032
When you visit a web
page, using your browser,

18:28:08.032 --> 18:28:11.452
on your phone, laptop, or desktop,
what's going inside that envelope,

18:28:11.452 --> 18:28:14.794
and the textual message your Mac or PC
or phone is automatically generating,

18:28:14.794 --> 18:28:16.252
looks a little something like this.

18:28:16.252 --> 18:28:20.112
The verb GET, the URL, or rather
the path that you want to get,

18:28:20.112 --> 18:28:22.942
slash represents the
default page on the website.

18:28:22.942 --> 18:28:27.682
HTTP/1.1 is just some mention of
what version of HTTP you're speaking.

18:28:27.682 --> 18:28:31.222
Now we're up to version 2, and
version 3, but 1.1 is quite common.

18:28:31.222 --> 18:28:35.812
And the envelope contains some
mention of the host that was typed in,

18:28:35.812 --> 18:28:37.502
the fully qualified domain name.

18:28:37.502 --> 18:28:42.332
This is because single servers can
actually host many different websites.

18:28:42.332 --> 18:28:46.012
If you're using Squarespace or Wix or
one of these popular hosting websites

18:28:46.012 --> 18:28:49.282
nowadays, you don't get your own
personal server, most likely.

18:28:49.282 --> 18:28:52.652
You're on the same server as
dozens, hundreds of other customers.

18:28:52.652 --> 18:28:56.242
But when your customers,
your users' browsers,

18:28:56.242 --> 18:29:00.472
include a little mention of your
specific, fully qualified domain

18:29:00.472 --> 18:29:02.842
name in the envelope,
Squarespace and Wix just

18:29:02.842 --> 18:29:06.442
know to send it to your web page or
my web page or some other customer

18:29:06.442 --> 18:29:07.142
altogether.

18:29:07.142 --> 18:29:09.032
Dot dot dot, there's
some other stuff there.

18:29:09.032 --> 18:29:12.532
But that's really the essence
of what's in these requests.

18:29:12.532 --> 18:29:16.312
Hopefully, then, when your browser
requests this web page from the server,

18:29:16.312 --> 18:29:17.362
what comes back?

18:29:17.362 --> 18:29:21.952
Well, hopefully, a response
that looks like this, HTTP/1.1,

18:29:21.952 --> 18:29:25.822
so the same version, some
status code, like a number 200,

18:29:25.822 --> 18:29:30.022
and then literally a short phrase like
OK, which means exactly that, like, OK,

18:29:30.022 --> 18:29:31.682
this request was satisfied.

18:29:31.682 --> 18:29:35.062
Then it contains some other
information, like the type of content

18:29:35.062 --> 18:29:35.872
that's coming back.

18:29:35.872 --> 18:29:37.789
And we'll see that this,
too, is standardized.

18:29:37.789 --> 18:29:41.932
Text/HTML means here comes some
HTML, which is just a text language.

18:29:41.932 --> 18:29:48.142
It could instead be image/jpeg
or Image/png, or video/mp4,

18:29:48.142 --> 18:29:51.982
there are these different content
types, otherwise known as MIME types,

18:29:51.982 --> 18:29:54.712
that uniquely identify types
of files, that come back,

18:29:54.712 --> 18:29:57.322
similar in spirit to file
extensions, but a little more

18:29:57.322 --> 18:29:59.232
standardized this way.

18:29:59.232 --> 18:30:00.982
Then there's some more
stuff, dot dot dot.

18:30:00.982 --> 18:30:05.842
But in general, what you see here, are
a familiar pattern, keys and values.

18:30:05.842 --> 18:30:09.652
These keys and values are
otherwise known as HTTP headers.

18:30:09.652 --> 18:30:14.092
And your browser has been sending
these every time you visit a website.

18:30:14.092 --> 18:30:16.422
And, indeed, we can see
this right now ourselves.

18:30:16.422 --> 18:30:20.422
Let me go over, in just a
second, to Chrome on my computer,

18:30:20.422 --> 18:30:23.552
though you can do this kind of
thing with most any browser today.

18:30:23.552 --> 18:30:28.642
I'll go ahead and visit
HTTP://Harvard.edu, Enter.

18:30:28.642 --> 18:30:31.459
And, voila, I'm at Harvard's
home page for today.

18:30:31.459 --> 18:30:32.542
The content often changes.

18:30:32.542 --> 18:30:34.982
But this is what it
looks like right now.

18:30:34.982 --> 18:30:38.002
Well, I typed in the URL, but
notice it changed a little bit.

18:30:38.002 --> 18:30:41.012
It actually sent me to
HTTPS and added the www,

18:30:41.012 --> 18:30:42.682
even though I didn't type that.

18:30:42.682 --> 18:30:46.702
But it turns out we can poke around
at what my browser is actually doing.

18:30:46.702 --> 18:30:47.812
Let me open another page.

18:30:47.812 --> 18:30:50.542
I'm going to start to use incognito
mode this time, not because I

18:30:50.542 --> 18:30:52.500
care that people know
I'm visiting Harvard.edu,

18:30:52.500 --> 18:30:56.472
but because it throws away
any history that I just did.

18:30:56.472 --> 18:30:58.972
So that every request is going
to look like a brand new one,

18:30:58.972 --> 18:31:01.430
and that's just useful
diagnostically, because we're always

18:31:01.430 --> 18:31:02.782
going to see fresh information.

18:31:02.782 --> 18:31:06.082
My browser is not going to remember
what I previously already requested.

18:31:06.082 --> 18:31:09.442
But I'm going to go
up to View, developer,

18:31:09.442 --> 18:31:13.042
developer tools, which is something
that all of you have, if you use Chrome.

18:31:13.042 --> 18:31:15.652
And there's something
analogous for Firefox and Edge

18:31:15.652 --> 18:31:17.632
and Safari and other browsers.

18:31:17.632 --> 18:31:20.302
Developer tools is going to
open up these tabs down here.

18:31:20.302 --> 18:31:23.510
I don't really care what's new, so I'm
going to close the bottom thing there.

18:31:23.510 --> 18:31:26.182
And I'm going to hover over the
Network tab for just a moment.

18:31:26.182 --> 18:31:31.252
And now I'm going to go and
say HTTP://Harvard.edu, so

18:31:31.252 --> 18:31:32.422
the shorter version.

18:31:32.422 --> 18:31:36.022
I'm going to hit Enter,
and a whole bunch of stuff

18:31:36.022 --> 18:31:37.342
just flew across the screen.

18:31:37.342 --> 18:31:38.602
And it's still coming in.

18:31:38.602 --> 18:31:43.702
And if I zoom in down here, my God,
visiting Harvard.edu, still going,

18:31:43.702 --> 18:31:48.082
is downloading, what 17, 18,
19 megabytes, 20 megabytes,

18:31:48.082 --> 18:31:53.182
millions of bytes of information,
over 111 HTTP requests.

18:31:53.182 --> 18:31:56.062
In other words, a bit of a
simplification, but my browser,

18:31:56.062 --> 18:31:59.692
unbeknownst to me, sent one
envelope initially with the request.

18:31:59.692 --> 18:32:01.612
Then the server said,
OK, by the way, there's

18:32:01.612 --> 18:32:05.242
110 other things you need, 112
other things you need to get.

18:32:05.242 --> 18:32:09.442
So my computer went back and forth,
requesting even more content for me.

18:32:09.442 --> 18:32:10.192
Why?

18:32:10.192 --> 18:32:13.582
Well, inside of Harvard's web
page is a whole bunch of images,

18:32:13.582 --> 18:32:16.342
and maybe sound files and
videos and other stuff,

18:32:16.342 --> 18:32:18.742
that all need to be
downloaded and to compose

18:32:18.742 --> 18:32:20.162
what is ultimately the web page.

18:32:20.162 --> 18:32:22.882
But I don't care about like
100 plus of these things.

18:32:22.882 --> 18:32:25.162
Let's focus on the very first one first.

18:32:25.162 --> 18:32:27.712
The very first request
I sent was up here.

18:32:27.712 --> 18:32:30.772
And I'm going to click on this
row, under the Network tab.

18:32:30.772 --> 18:32:33.502
And then I'm going to see a
bit of diagnostic information.

18:32:33.502 --> 18:32:36.742
To an average person using the
web, they needn't care about this,

18:32:36.742 --> 18:32:39.292
just as you probably didn't
care about it until right now.

18:32:39.292 --> 18:32:41.002
And even then, perhaps not.

18:32:41.002 --> 18:32:44.992
But if I scroll down to
request headers, you will see,

18:32:44.992 --> 18:32:48.952
if I click View source, literally
everything that was in the request

18:32:48.952 --> 18:32:51.282
my Mac just sent to Harvard.edu.

18:32:51.282 --> 18:32:56.482
Two of the lines are familiar,
get/http1.1, host:harvard.edu,

18:32:56.482 --> 18:32:59.942
and then other stuff that, for now,
it's not that interesting for us.

18:32:59.942 --> 18:33:03.382
But let's look at the response
that came back from the server.

18:33:03.382 --> 18:33:08.422
I'm going to scroll up now and
see response headers, view source.

18:33:08.422 --> 18:33:09.802
And this is interesting.

18:33:09.802 --> 18:33:11.212
It is not OK.

18:33:11.212 --> 18:33:14.032
There's no 200, there's no word OK.

18:33:14.032 --> 18:33:18.112
Curiously, harvard.edu
has moved permanently.

18:33:18.112 --> 18:33:18.954
What does that mean?

18:33:18.954 --> 18:33:20.662
Well, there's a whole
bunch of stuff here

18:33:20.662 --> 18:33:22.120
that's not that interesting for us.

18:33:22.120 --> 18:33:24.782
But this line, location, is interesting.

18:33:24.782 --> 18:33:28.552
This is an HTTP header, a
standardized key value pair,

18:33:28.552 --> 18:33:32.302
that's part of the HTTP
protocol, that is, conventions.

18:33:32.302 --> 18:33:34.882
And if I highlight just
this one, it's telling me,

18:33:34.882 --> 18:33:38.422
mm-mmm, Harvard is not
at HTTP://Harvard.edu,

18:33:38.422 --> 18:33:44.242
Harvard's website is now, and perhaps
forever, at HTTPS://www.harvard.edu.

18:33:44.242 --> 18:33:47.632
So what's the value here?

18:33:47.632 --> 18:33:50.642
Probably someone at Harvard wants
you to use a secure connection.

18:33:50.642 --> 18:33:53.092
So they redirected you
from HTTP to HTTPS.

18:33:53.092 --> 18:33:57.392
Maybe the marketing people want you to
be at www instead of just Harvard.edu.

18:33:57.392 --> 18:33:57.892
Why?

18:33:57.892 --> 18:34:00.350
Just to standardize things,
but there are technical reasons

18:34:00.350 --> 18:34:03.442
to use a hostname, and not
just the raw domain name.

18:34:03.442 --> 18:34:06.332
And all this other stuff is sort
of uninteresting for our purposes,

18:34:06.332 --> 18:34:11.362
now, because a browser that
receives a 301 response knows,

18:34:11.362 --> 18:34:16.462
by design, by the definition of HTTP,
to automatically redirect the user.

18:34:16.462 --> 18:34:20.065
And that's why, in my browser, all of
this happened in like a split second,

18:34:20.065 --> 18:34:22.732
because I didn't really know or
care about all of those headers.

18:34:22.732 --> 18:34:26.482
But that's why and how I
ended up at this URL here.

18:34:26.482 --> 18:34:29.842
My browser was told to go
elsewhere via that new location.

18:34:29.842 --> 18:34:32.392
And the browser just
followed those breadcrumbs,

18:34:32.392 --> 18:34:35.122
if you will, at which point it
downloaded all of the other images

18:34:35.122 --> 18:34:39.562
and files, and so forth, that
compose this particular page.

18:34:39.562 --> 18:34:40.639
Well, let me zoom out.

18:34:40.639 --> 18:34:42.472
And let me actually go
into VS Code, if only

18:34:42.472 --> 18:34:45.597
because it's a little more pleasant to
do things in just a terminal window,

18:34:45.597 --> 18:34:49.202
without actually using
a full-fledged browser.

18:34:49.202 --> 18:34:51.382
So now let's just use
an equivalent program.

18:34:51.382 --> 18:34:54.352
It's called Curl, for
connecting to a URL, that's

18:34:54.352 --> 18:34:57.262
going to allow me to play with
websites and just see those headers,

18:34:57.262 --> 18:34:59.872
without bothering to download
all the images and text

18:34:59.872 --> 18:35:01.312
and so forth from the website.

18:35:01.312 --> 18:35:03.902
It's going to allow me to
do something like this.

18:35:03.902 --> 18:35:10.942
Let me go ahead and run, for instance,
Curl-I-xget, which is just the command

18:35:10.942 --> 18:35:14.572
line arguments that says
simulate a GET request textually,

18:35:14.572 --> 18:35:15.832
as though you're a browser.

18:35:15.832 --> 18:35:20.662
And let's go to
HTTP://Harvard.edu Enter.

18:35:20.662 --> 18:35:23.834
Now, by way of how Curl, works,
I'm just seeing the headers.

18:35:23.834 --> 18:35:25.792
It didn't bother downloading
the whole website.

18:35:25.792 --> 18:35:28.792
And you see exactly the same
thing, 301 moved permanently.

18:35:28.792 --> 18:35:31.562
Location is, indeed, this one here.

18:35:31.562 --> 18:35:33.202
So that's kind of interesting.

18:35:33.202 --> 18:35:34.882
But let's follow it manually now.

18:35:34.882 --> 18:35:37.682
Let's now do what it's telling me to do.

18:35:37.682 --> 18:35:42.382
Let's go to the location, with
HTTPS and the www and hit Enter.

18:35:42.382 --> 18:35:46.402
And now, what's a good
sign with this output?

18:35:46.402 --> 18:35:47.662
Most of it's irrelevant.

18:35:47.662 --> 18:35:48.832
AUDIENCE: Migrate?

18:35:48.832 --> 18:35:51.742
SPEAKER 1: 200 OK, that
means I'm seeing, presumably,

18:35:51.742 --> 18:35:55.222
if I were using a real browser,
the actual content of the web page.

18:35:55.222 --> 18:35:58.912
Looks like Harvard's version of HTTP
is even newer than the one I'm using.

18:35:58.912 --> 18:36:01.052
It's using HTTP version
2, which is fine.

18:36:01.052 --> 18:36:04.612
But 200 is indeed indicative
of things being OK.

18:36:04.612 --> 18:36:07.822
Well, what if I try
visiting some bogus URL,

18:36:07.822 --> 18:36:14.332
like Harvard.edu, when this file does
not exist, something completely random,

18:36:14.332 --> 18:36:17.332
probably doesn't exist, and hit Enter.

18:36:17.332 --> 18:36:20.992
What do you see now, that's perhaps
familiar, in the real world?

18:36:20.992 --> 18:36:21.989
Yeah.

18:36:21.989 --> 18:36:23.181
AUDIENCE: Error 404.

18:36:23.181 --> 18:36:24.621
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, error 404.

18:36:24.621 --> 18:36:26.601
All of us have seen
this probably endlessly,

18:36:26.601 --> 18:36:29.302
from time to time, when you
screw up by mis-typing a URL,

18:36:29.302 --> 18:36:31.222
or someone deletes the
web page in question.

18:36:31.222 --> 18:36:34.222
But all that is is a
status code that a browser

18:36:34.222 --> 18:36:37.522
is being sent from the server,
that's a little clue as to what

18:36:37.522 --> 18:36:40.384
the actual problem is,
underneath the hood.

18:36:40.384 --> 18:36:42.092
So instead of getting
back, for instance,

18:36:42.092 --> 18:36:45.202
something like OK, or moved permanently,
what I've just gotten back, quite

18:36:45.202 --> 18:36:47.902
simply, is 404 not found.

18:36:47.902 --> 18:36:51.712
Well, it turns out there's
other types of status codes

18:36:51.712 --> 18:36:55.212
that you'll start to see over time,
as you start to program for the web.

18:36:55.212 --> 18:36:56.382
200 is OK.

18:36:56.382 --> 18:36:57.882
301 is moved permanently.

18:36:57.882 --> 18:37:01.422
302, 304, 307 are all similar in spirit.

18:37:01.422 --> 18:37:04.782
They're related to redirecting the
user from one place to another.

18:37:04.782 --> 18:37:08.802
401, 403, unauthorized or forbidden.

18:37:08.802 --> 18:37:11.022
If you ever mess up
your password, or you

18:37:11.022 --> 18:37:13.135
try visiting a URL you're
not supposed to look at,

18:37:13.135 --> 18:37:15.552
you might see one of these
codes, indicating that you just

18:37:15.552 --> 18:37:17.442
don't have authorization for those.

18:37:17.442 --> 18:37:21.672
404 not found, 418, I'm a
teapot, was an April Fool's joke

18:37:21.672 --> 18:37:24.072
by the tech community years ago.

18:37:24.072 --> 18:37:25.332
500 is bad.

18:37:25.332 --> 18:37:27.282
And, unfortunately,
all of you are probably

18:37:27.282 --> 18:37:30.972
on a path now to creating
HTTP 500 errors, once,

18:37:30.972 --> 18:37:33.115
next week, we start writing
code, because all of us

18:37:33.115 --> 18:37:34.032
are going to screw up.

18:37:34.032 --> 18:37:37.632
We're going to have typos, logical
errors, and this is on the horizon,

18:37:37.632 --> 18:37:42.042
just like segfaults were in the world of
C, but solvable with the right skills.

18:37:42.042 --> 18:37:45.254
503 service unavailable, means
maybe the server is overloaded,

18:37:45.254 --> 18:37:46.212
or something like that.

18:37:46.212 --> 18:37:47.722
And there's other codes there.

18:37:47.722 --> 18:37:51.012
But those are perhaps some
of the most common ones.

