18:43:33.652 --> 18:43:37.490
And we have the ability to
specify in those envelopes what

18:43:37.490 --> 18:43:38.782
it is we want from the website.

18:43:38.782 --> 18:43:40.192
We want to get the home page.

18:43:40.192 --> 18:43:41.932
We want to get back the HTML.

18:43:41.932 --> 18:43:43.247
But what is that HTML?

18:43:43.247 --> 18:43:46.372
In fact, we don't yet have the language
with which the web pages themselves

18:43:46.372 --> 18:43:48.494
are written, namely HTML and CSS.

18:43:48.494 --> 18:43:50.702
But let's go ahead and take
a five minute break here.

18:43:50.702 --> 18:43:54.182
And when we come back, we'll
learn those two languages.

18:43:54.182 --> 18:43:56.072
All right, we are back.

18:43:56.072 --> 18:43:58.372
So we've got three
languages to look at today.

18:43:58.372 --> 18:44:00.832
But two of them are not
actually programming languages.

18:44:00.832 --> 18:44:05.152
What makes something a programming
language, like C or Python and SQL,

18:44:05.152 --> 18:44:08.472
is that there are these constructs via
which you can express conditionals.

18:44:08.472 --> 18:44:10.972
You might have variables, you
might have looping constructs.

18:44:10.972 --> 18:44:13.342
You have the ability,
ultimately, to express logic.

18:44:13.342 --> 18:44:16.912
HTML and CSS aren't so much about
logic as they are about structure,

18:44:16.912 --> 18:44:18.442
and the aesthetics of a page.

18:44:18.442 --> 18:44:21.262
And so we're going to create
the skeleton of a web page using

18:44:21.262 --> 18:44:23.190
this pair of languages, HTML and CSS.

18:44:23.190 --> 18:44:24.982
And then toward the
end of the today, we'll

18:44:24.982 --> 18:44:26.872
introduce an actual
programming language,

18:44:26.872 --> 18:44:30.232
that actually is pretty similar
in spirit and syntactically

18:44:30.232 --> 18:44:34.102
to both C and Python, but that's going
to allow us to make these web pages not

18:44:34.102 --> 18:44:38.252
just static, things that you look at,
but interactive applications as well.

18:44:38.252 --> 18:44:42.772
And then next week again, in week 9,
will we reintroduce Python and SQL,

18:44:42.772 --> 18:44:46.252
tie all of this together, so that you
can actually have a browser or a phone

18:44:46.252 --> 18:44:49.222
talking to a back-end
server, and creating

18:44:49.222 --> 18:44:53.152
the experience that you and I now take
for granted for most any app or website

18:44:53.152 --> 18:44:53.812
today.

18:44:53.812 --> 18:44:55.187
Well, let's go ahead and do this.

18:44:55.187 --> 18:44:58.402
Let's quickly whip up something
in this language called HTML.

18:44:58.402 --> 18:44:59.602
I'm in VS Code here.

18:44:59.602 --> 18:45:04.102
I'm going to go ahead and create a
file quite simply called, Hello.html.

18:45:04.102 --> 18:45:07.492
The convention is typically to
end your file names in dot html.

18:45:07.492 --> 18:45:09.784
And I'm going to go ahead
and bang this out real quick.

18:45:09.784 --> 18:45:12.842
But then we'll more slowly step
through what the constructs are herein.

18:45:12.842 --> 18:45:17.482
So I'm going to say doctype
html open bracket html,

18:45:17.482 --> 18:45:22.072
and then notice I'm going to do open
bracket slash html close bracket.

18:45:22.072 --> 18:45:25.672
And I'm leveraging a feature of VS
Code and programming environments more

18:45:25.672 --> 18:45:27.602
generally, to do a bit of autocomplete.

18:45:27.602 --> 18:45:30.832
So you'll see that there's this symmetry
to much of what I'm going to type,

18:45:30.832 --> 18:45:32.632
but I'm not typing all of these things.

18:45:32.632 --> 18:45:37.612
VS Code is automatically generating the
end of my thought for me, if you will.

18:45:37.612 --> 18:45:41.512
Let me go ahead and
say, Open the head tag.

18:45:41.512 --> 18:45:42.832
Open the title tag.

18:45:42.832 --> 18:45:44.932
I'll say something cute
like, Hello, title.

18:45:44.932 --> 18:45:47.662
And then down here, I'm going to
create the body of this web page

18:45:47.662 --> 18:45:49.462
and say something like Hello, body.

18:45:49.462 --> 18:45:53.242
And let me specify at the very top,
that all of this is really in English,

18:45:53.242 --> 18:45:54.592
Lang equals en.

18:45:54.592 --> 18:46:00.562
So at this moment, I have a file in my
VS Code environment called Hello.html.

18:46:00.562 --> 18:46:03.484
VS Code as we're using it,
of course, is cloud-based.

18:46:03.484 --> 18:46:05.692
We're using it in a browser,
even though you can also

18:46:05.692 --> 18:46:07.792
download it and run it on a Mac and PC.

18:46:07.792 --> 18:46:10.492
So we are in this weird
situation where I'm

18:46:10.492 --> 18:46:12.772
using the cloud to
create a web page, and I

18:46:12.772 --> 18:46:17.782
want that web page to also live in
the cloud, that is, on the internet.

18:46:17.782 --> 18:46:21.082
But the thing about VS
Code, or really any website

18:46:21.082 --> 18:46:24.742
that you might use in a browser, by
default that website is using probably

18:46:24.742 --> 18:46:29.362
TCP port number 80 or
TCP port number 443,

18:46:29.362 --> 18:46:32.062
which is HTTP and HTTPS respectively.

18:46:32.062 --> 18:46:34.912
But here I am, sort of
a programmer myself,

18:46:34.912 --> 18:46:39.242
trying to create my own
website on an existing website.

18:46:39.242 --> 18:46:40.732
So it's a bit of a weird situation.

18:46:40.732 --> 18:46:43.222
But that's OK, because
what's nice about TCP

18:46:43.222 --> 18:46:47.572
is that you and I can just pick port
numbers to use and run our own web

18:46:47.572 --> 18:46:49.312
server, on a web server.

18:46:49.312 --> 18:46:51.802
That is, we can control
the environment entirely,

18:46:51.802 --> 18:46:57.712
by just running our own web server
via this command, HTTP-server,

18:46:57.712 --> 18:46:58.732
in my terminal window.

18:46:58.732 --> 18:47:01.445
This is a command that we
preinstalled in VS Code here.

18:47:01.445 --> 18:47:03.112
And you'll notice a pop-up just came up.

18:47:03.112 --> 18:47:05.842
Your application running
on port 8080 is available.

18:47:05.842 --> 18:47:09.172
That's a commonly used TCP port
number, when 80 is already used,

18:47:09.172 --> 18:47:11.932
and 443 is already used,
you can run your own server

18:47:11.932 --> 18:47:14.522
on your own port, 8080 in this case.

18:47:14.522 --> 18:47:18.592
I've opened that tab in advance, and
if I go into another browser tab here,

18:47:18.592 --> 18:47:22.252
here I see a so-called directory
listing of the web server I'm running.

18:47:22.252 --> 18:47:24.322
So I don't see any of my other files.

18:47:24.322 --> 18:47:27.352
I don't see anything
belonging to VS Code itself.

18:47:27.352 --> 18:47:30.562
I only see the file that I've created
in my current directory, called

18:47:30.562 --> 18:47:31.852
Hello.html.

18:47:31.852 --> 18:47:36.232
And so if I click on this file
now, I should see Hello, body.

18:47:36.232 --> 18:47:37.312
I don't see the title.

18:47:37.312 --> 18:47:39.262
But that's because the
title of a web page

18:47:39.262 --> 18:47:41.699
nowadays is typically
embedded in the tab.

18:47:41.699 --> 18:47:44.032
And if I'm full screen in my
browser, there are no tabs.

18:47:44.032 --> 18:47:45.652
So let me minimize the window a bit.

18:47:45.652 --> 18:47:50.032
And now you can see just in this
single browser window, in my own URL

18:47:50.032 --> 18:47:52.382
here, that Hello, body, is
in the top left hand corner.

18:47:52.382 --> 18:47:54.802
And if I zoom in, there's Hello, title.

18:47:54.802 --> 18:47:56.272
So what have I done here?

18:47:56.272 --> 18:48:01.492
I have gone ahead and created
my own web page in HTML,

18:48:01.492 --> 18:48:04.852
in a file called Hello.html.

18:48:04.852 --> 18:48:09.252
And then I have opened up
a web server of my own,

18:48:09.252 --> 18:48:11.592
configured it to listen
on TCP port 8080,

18:48:11.592 --> 18:48:14.982
which just says to the internet, hey,
listen for requests from web browsers,

18:48:14.982 --> 18:48:18.612
not on the standard port number,
80 or 443, listen on 8080.

18:48:18.612 --> 18:48:22.482
And this means I can develop a website
using a web-based tool, like this one

18:48:22.482 --> 18:48:24.852
here, which is
increasingly common today.

18:48:24.852 --> 18:48:28.692
All right, so now let's consider
what it is I actually just typed out.

18:48:28.692 --> 18:48:32.892
HTML is characterized really by just
two features, two vocab words, tags

18:48:32.892 --> 18:48:33.732
and attributes.

18:48:33.732 --> 18:48:36.972
Most of what I just typed were tags,
but there was at least one attribute

18:48:36.972 --> 18:48:37.732
already.

18:48:37.732 --> 18:48:41.872
Here's the same source code that I
typed out in HTML, from top to bottom.

18:48:41.872 --> 18:48:43.122
Let's consider what this is.

18:48:43.122 --> 18:48:46.632
The very first line of
code here, doctype html,

18:48:46.632 --> 18:48:47.892
is the only anomalous one.

18:48:47.892 --> 18:48:51.522
It's the only one that starts with
an open bracket, a less than sign,

18:48:51.522 --> 18:48:52.782
and an exclamation point.

18:48:52.782 --> 18:48:55.392
There's no more exclamation
points thereafter, for now.

18:48:55.392 --> 18:48:58.842
This is the document type declaration,
which is a fancy way of saying,

18:48:58.842 --> 18:49:00.432
it's just got to be there nowadays.

18:49:00.432 --> 18:49:02.974
It's like a little breadcrumb
at the beginning of a file that

18:49:02.974 --> 18:49:08.022
says to the browser, you are about to
see a file written in HTML version 5.

18:49:08.022 --> 18:49:11.000
That line of code has changed
over time, over the years.

18:49:11.000 --> 18:49:13.542
The most recent version of it
is nice and succinct like this,

18:49:13.542 --> 18:49:16.572
and it's just a clue to the
browser as to what version of HTML

18:49:16.572 --> 18:49:18.882
is being used by you, the programmer.

18:49:18.882 --> 18:49:20.782
All right, what comes after that?

18:49:20.782 --> 18:49:23.592
Well, after that, and I've
highlighted two things in yellow,

18:49:23.592 --> 18:49:26.442
this is what we're going to
start calling an open tag,

18:49:26.442 --> 18:49:30.912
or a start tag, open bracket HTML
then something, close bracket,

18:49:30.912 --> 18:49:32.992
is the so-called start or open tag.

18:49:32.992 --> 18:49:35.742
Then the corresponding close
or end tag is down here.

18:49:35.742 --> 18:49:37.092
And it's almost the same.

18:49:37.092 --> 18:49:40.252
You use the same tag number, you
use the same angled brackets.

18:49:40.252 --> 18:49:43.122
But you do add a slash, and
you don't repeat yourself

18:49:43.122 --> 18:49:46.032
with any of the things
called attributes,

18:49:46.032 --> 18:49:47.772
because, what is this thing here?

18:49:47.772 --> 18:49:50.892
Lang equals quote unquote
"en," means the language

18:49:50.892 --> 18:49:53.632
of my page is written
in the English language.

18:49:53.632 --> 18:49:56.772
The humans have standardized
two and three letter codes

18:49:56.772 --> 18:49:59.452
for every human language, right now.

18:49:59.452 --> 18:50:03.132
And so this is just a clue to the
browser for like automatic translation

18:50:03.132 --> 18:50:06.402
and accessibility purposes,
what language the web page

18:50:06.402 --> 18:50:07.332
itself is written in.

18:50:07.332 --> 18:50:10.662
Not the tags, but the words, like
Hello, title and Hello, body,

18:50:10.662 --> 18:50:13.182
which while minimalist,
are indeed in English.

18:50:13.182 --> 18:50:16.142
So when you close a tag, you close
the name of it with the slash

18:50:16.142 --> 18:50:17.112
and the angle brackets.

18:50:17.112 --> 18:50:19.320
You don't repeat the attribute.

18:50:19.320 --> 18:50:21.862
That would just be annoying to
have to type everything again.

18:50:21.862 --> 18:50:23.029
But notice the pattern here.

18:50:23.029 --> 18:50:24.262
It's new syntax.

18:50:24.262 --> 18:50:27.552
But this is another example of
key value pairs in computing.

18:50:27.552 --> 18:50:31.632
The key is Lang, the
value is E-N for English.

18:50:31.632 --> 18:50:34.182
The attribute is called
Lang, the value is

18:50:34.182 --> 18:50:38.142
called, it is E-N. So again,
it's just key value pairs,

18:50:38.142 --> 18:50:39.552
in just yet another context.

18:50:39.552 --> 18:50:41.682
Probably the browser's using a
hash table underneath the hood,

18:50:41.682 --> 18:50:44.807
to keep track of this stuff, like a
two column table, with keys and values.

18:50:44.807 --> 18:50:48.292
Again, humans keep using the same
paradigm in different languages.

18:50:48.292 --> 18:50:49.732
What's inside of that?

18:50:49.732 --> 18:50:52.242
The nesting is important
visually, not to the computer,

18:50:52.242 --> 18:50:54.492
but to us, the humans,
because it implies

18:50:54.492 --> 18:50:55.972
that there's some hierarchy here.

18:50:55.972 --> 18:50:59.782
And, indeed, what is inside
of the HTML tag here?

18:50:59.782 --> 18:51:02.892
Well, we have what
we'll call the head tag.

18:51:02.892 --> 18:51:06.252
The head tag says, hey, browser,
here comes the head of the page.

18:51:06.252 --> 18:51:08.519
And then the body tag
says, hey, browser,

18:51:08.519 --> 18:51:09.852
here comes the body of the page.

18:51:09.852 --> 18:51:13.902
The body is like 99% of the user's
experience, the big rectangular window.

18:51:13.902 --> 18:51:17.532
The head is really just the address
bar and other such stuff at top,

18:51:17.532 --> 18:51:20.292
like the title that we saw a moment ago.

18:51:20.292 --> 18:51:24.732
Just to introduce the vernacular,
then, the HTML tag, otherwise known

18:51:24.732 --> 18:51:29.202
as an element, has two children,
the head child and the body child,

18:51:29.202 --> 18:51:31.962
which is to say that head
and body are now siblings.

18:51:31.962 --> 18:51:35.112
So you can use the same kind of
family tree terminology that we used,

18:51:35.112 --> 18:51:37.422
when talking about trees, weeks ago.

18:51:37.422 --> 18:51:41.802
If we look at the head tag, how
many children does it seem to have?

18:51:41.802 --> 18:51:44.592
I'm seeing one, and,
indeed, at least if we

18:51:44.592 --> 18:51:48.852
ignore all the white space, the
spaces or tabs or new line characters,

18:51:48.852 --> 18:51:51.072
there's just one child, a title element.

18:51:51.072 --> 18:51:55.032
And an element is the terminology that
includes the start tag and the end tag,

18:51:55.032 --> 18:51:56.422
and everything in between.

18:51:56.422 --> 18:51:58.242
So this is the title element.

18:51:58.242 --> 18:52:01.812
And the title element has one
child, which is just pure text,

18:52:01.812 --> 18:52:03.792
otherwise known as a text node.

18:52:03.792 --> 18:52:06.912
Recall, node, from our discussions
of data structures weeks ago.

18:52:06.912 --> 18:52:10.962
If we jump then to the body, which
is the other child of the HTML tag,

18:52:10.962 --> 18:52:14.922
it too has one child, which is just
another chunk of text, a text node,

18:52:14.922 --> 18:52:17.292
that says, quote unquote "Hello, body."

18:52:17.292 --> 18:52:21.042
What's nice about this indentation,
even though the browser technically

18:52:21.042 --> 18:52:25.312
is not going to care, is that it
implies this kind of structure.

18:52:25.312 --> 18:52:28.902
And this is where we connect,
like weeks 5 and now weeks 8, here

18:52:28.902 --> 18:52:33.282
is the tree structure we began to
talk about, even in our world of C.

18:52:33.282 --> 18:52:35.585
It's not a binary tree,
even though this one happens

18:52:35.585 --> 18:52:37.002
to have no more than two children.

18:52:37.002 --> 18:52:40.962
It's an arbitrary tree that can
have 0 or any number of children.

18:52:40.962 --> 18:52:43.842
But if we have a special node
here that refers to the document,

18:52:43.842 --> 18:52:47.082
the root node, so to speak, is
HTML, drawn with a rectangle

18:52:47.082 --> 18:52:48.672
here, just for discussion's sake.

18:52:48.672 --> 18:52:51.522
It has two children, head
and body, also rectangles.

18:52:51.522 --> 18:52:54.852
Head has a title child,
and then it and body

18:52:54.852 --> 18:52:57.862
have text nodes, which I've
drawn with ovals instead.

18:52:57.862 --> 18:53:01.302
Which is only to say that when your
browser, Chrome, Safari, whatever,

18:53:01.302 --> 18:53:03.762
downloads a web page,
opens up that envelope,

18:53:03.762 --> 18:53:06.772
and sees the contents that
have come back from the server,

18:53:06.772 --> 18:53:10.222
it essentially reads the
code that someone wrote,

18:53:10.222 --> 18:53:12.632
the HTML code, top to
bottom, left to right,

18:53:12.632 --> 18:53:16.402
and creates in the browser's
memory, in your Mac or your PC

18:53:16.402 --> 18:53:20.242
or your phone's memory or RAM,
this kind of data structure.

18:53:20.242 --> 18:53:22.132
That's what's going on
underneath the hood.

18:53:22.132 --> 18:53:24.592
And that's why aesthetically,
it's just nice, as a human,

18:53:24.592 --> 18:53:27.802
to indent things stylistically,
because it's very clear then

18:53:27.802 --> 18:53:32.702
to you, and to other programmers,
what the structure actually is.

18:53:32.702 --> 18:53:36.112
So that's it for like
the fundamentals of HTML.

18:53:36.112 --> 18:53:38.452
We'll see a bunch of tags
and a bunch of examples now.

18:53:38.452 --> 18:53:40.682
But HTML is just tags and attributes.

18:53:40.682 --> 18:53:43.432
And it's the kind of thing that
you look them up when you need to.

18:53:43.432 --> 18:53:45.142
Eventually, many of them get ingrained.

18:53:45.142 --> 18:53:47.939
I constantly check the reference
guides or stack overflow

18:53:47.939 --> 18:53:50.272
if I'm trying to figure out,
how do I lay something out.

18:53:50.272 --> 18:53:52.064
It's really just these
building blocks that

18:53:52.064 --> 18:53:55.132
allow you to assemble the
structure of a web page.

18:53:55.132 --> 18:53:58.552
This one is being super simple,
but it's just tags and attributes.

18:53:58.552 --> 18:54:01.102
Any questions on this
framework, before we

18:54:01.102 --> 18:54:05.489
start to add more tags, more
vocabulary, if you will?

18:54:05.489 --> 18:54:06.322
In the middle, yeah.

18:54:06.322 --> 18:54:09.302
AUDIENCE: What would happen
if we put the title tag?

18:54:09.302 --> 18:54:13.092
SPEAKER 1: If we put the hello tag
around body, that's a good question.

18:54:13.092 --> 18:54:13.752
Let's try it.

18:54:13.752 --> 18:54:20.252
So let me actually go to this,
and say open bracket title,

18:54:20.252 --> 18:54:23.462
whoops, sometimes you don't want
it to finish your thought for you.

18:54:23.462 --> 18:54:24.602
But it did that time.

18:54:24.602 --> 18:54:27.032
I've gone ahead and changed the file.

18:54:27.032 --> 18:54:30.362
Let me go and open up, give me a
second to open my terminal window,

18:54:30.362 --> 18:54:34.412
and go back to the URL that has my page.

18:54:34.412 --> 18:54:36.632
Give me a second.

18:54:36.632 --> 18:54:38.192
There's my Hello.html.

18:54:38.192 --> 18:54:39.762
Let me zoom in on this.

18:54:39.762 --> 18:54:41.162
Let me zoom in on this.

18:54:41.162 --> 18:54:45.892
And let me go ahead now
and click on Hello.html.

18:54:45.892 --> 18:54:49.352
And in this case, it looks like
we don't actually see anything.

18:54:49.352 --> 18:54:50.632
So the browser is hiding it.

18:54:50.632 --> 18:54:54.052
Technically speaking, browsers
tend to be pretty generous.

18:54:54.052 --> 18:54:56.332
And half the time, when
you make mistakes in HTML,

18:54:56.332 --> 18:54:58.102
it will display, it might display--

18:54:58.102 --> 18:54:59.437
not display as you intend it.

18:54:59.437 --> 18:55:03.022
It might not display the same on
Macs or PCs or Chrome or on Firefox.

18:55:03.022 --> 18:55:05.212
There is a tool, though,
that we'll see, that

18:55:05.212 --> 18:55:07.042
can help answer this question for you.

18:55:07.042 --> 18:55:11.032
For instance, if I go
to Validator.w3.org,

18:55:11.032 --> 18:55:13.072
W3 is the World Wide
Web Consortium, a group

18:55:13.072 --> 18:55:15.232
of people that standardize
this kind of stuff,

18:55:15.232 --> 18:55:17.632
I can click on Validate
by direct input, and just

18:55:17.632 --> 18:55:21.412
copy paste my sample HTML into
this box, and click Check.

18:55:21.412 --> 18:55:24.052
And I should see,
hopefully, that indeed, it's

18:55:24.052 --> 18:55:25.672
an error, what you proposed that I do.

18:55:25.672 --> 18:55:27.744
The browser just did its
best to do something,

18:55:27.744 --> 18:55:30.952
which was to show me nothing at least,
rather than the incorrect information.

18:55:30.952 --> 18:55:34.492
But if I revert that change, and
let me undo what we just did,

18:55:34.492 --> 18:55:39.412
let me copy my original code back
into this text box, and click Check,

18:55:39.412 --> 18:55:41.832
now you can see, conversely,
my code is now correct.

18:55:41.832 --> 18:55:43.582
And there's automated
tools to check that.

18:55:43.582 --> 18:55:45.874
But we'll encourage you, for
problem sets and projects,

18:55:45.874 --> 18:55:48.832
to use that particular manual tool.

18:55:48.832 --> 18:55:51.622
All right, so let's go ahead
and enhance this a little bit

18:55:51.622 --> 18:55:53.572
by introducing a whole
bunch of tags, just

18:55:53.572 --> 18:55:55.952
to give you a sense of some
of the building blocks here.

18:55:55.952 --> 18:56:01.292
So I'm going to go ahead and create
a new file called Paragraphs.html.

18:56:01.292 --> 18:56:04.292
And I'm just going to do a bunch of
copy/paste just to start things off,

18:56:04.292 --> 18:56:07.432
so I'm not constantly typing all
this darn stuff again and again,

18:56:07.432 --> 18:56:09.412
because I want everything
to be the same here,

18:56:09.412 --> 18:56:12.442
except I'm going to change my title
to be Paragraphs for this demo.

18:56:12.442 --> 18:56:16.029
And inside of the body, I need a
whole bunch of paragraphs of text.

18:56:16.029 --> 18:56:18.112
And I don't really want
to come up with some text.

18:56:18.112 --> 18:56:21.682
So let me go to some random website
here and grab lorem ipsum text,

18:56:21.682 --> 18:56:25.052
which if you're involved in like
student newspaper or just design,

18:56:25.052 --> 18:56:28.582
this is placeholder text, kind of looks
like Latin, but technically isn't.

18:56:28.582 --> 18:56:32.122
Here, though, I have a handy way of
just getting three long paragraphs

18:56:32.122 --> 18:56:33.802
in something that looks like Latin.

18:56:33.802 --> 18:56:36.012
And I've put those,
notice, inside of the body.

18:56:36.012 --> 18:56:37.012
And they're indeed long.

18:56:37.012 --> 18:56:40.112
Look how long the
made-up words here are.

18:56:40.112 --> 18:56:46.252
So let me go now into
my browser tab here.

18:56:46.252 --> 18:56:50.302
Let me reload this page, and you'll
see two files have now appeared,

18:56:50.302 --> 18:56:53.002
Paragraphs.html, which is
my new one, and Hello.html.

18:56:53.002 --> 18:56:58.352
Let me click on Paragraphs.html,
and what clearly seems to be wrong?

18:56:58.352 --> 18:56:58.852
Yeah.

18:56:58.852 --> 18:57:00.172
AUDIENCE: One paragraph.

18:57:00.172 --> 18:57:03.172
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, it's obviously one
massive paragraph, instead of three.

18:57:03.172 --> 18:57:07.762
So that's interesting, but it's just a
little hint as to how pedantic HTML is.

18:57:07.762 --> 18:57:09.142
It will only do what you say.

18:57:09.142 --> 18:57:12.412
And each of these tags tells the
browser to start doing something,

18:57:12.412 --> 18:57:15.802
and then maybe stop doing something,
like, hey, browser, here comes my HTML.

18:57:15.802 --> 18:57:17.677
Hey, browser, here comes
the head of my page.

18:57:17.677 --> 18:57:20.212
Hey, browser, here comes the
title of my page, Hello, title.

18:57:20.212 --> 18:57:22.042
Hey, browser, that's it for the title.

18:57:22.042 --> 18:57:24.662
That's it for the head,
here comes the body tag.

18:57:24.662 --> 18:57:27.412
So it's kind of having this
conversation between the browser,

18:57:27.412 --> 18:57:30.472
between the HTML and the browser,
doing literally what it says.

18:57:30.472 --> 18:57:32.722
So if you want a
paragraph, you're probably

18:57:32.722 --> 18:57:35.482
going to want to use
the P tag for paragraph.

18:57:35.482 --> 18:57:38.642
And I'm going to go ahead
and add this to my code.

18:57:38.642 --> 18:57:41.392
I'm going to keep things neat,
even though the browser won't care,

18:57:41.392 --> 18:57:43.072
by indenting things here.

18:57:43.072 --> 18:57:47.032
Let me create another paragraph
tag here, and close it

18:57:47.032 --> 18:57:49.522
right after that one,
indenting again, and I'm

18:57:49.522 --> 18:57:51.232
keeping everything nice and orderly.

18:57:51.232 --> 18:57:53.402
Let me do one more here.

18:57:53.402 --> 18:57:58.892
Let me indent that, and then let me
add it to the end of my page here.

18:57:58.892 --> 18:58:02.672
So again, a little tedious, but now I
have three paragraphs of text that say,

18:58:02.672 --> 18:58:04.102
hey, browser, start a paragraph.

18:58:04.102 --> 18:58:05.722
Hey, browser, stop that paragraph.

18:58:05.722 --> 18:58:07.682
Start, stop, and so forth.

18:58:07.682 --> 18:58:09.962
Let me go back to the
browser window here.

18:58:09.962 --> 18:58:13.492
Let me hit Command R or
Control R to reload the page.

18:58:13.492 --> 18:58:16.732
And voila, now I have three
cleaner paragraphs, all right?

18:58:16.732 --> 18:58:18.562
So there's a P tag for paragraphs.

18:58:18.562 --> 18:58:20.572
So now we have that
particular building block.

18:58:20.572 --> 18:58:23.839
What if I want to add, for instance,
some headings to this page?

18:58:23.839 --> 18:58:25.672
Well, that's something
that's possible, too.

18:58:25.672 --> 18:58:29.152
Let me go ahead and create a
new file called Headings.html.

18:58:29.152 --> 18:58:31.912
Let me copy and paste
that same code as before.

18:58:31.912 --> 18:58:36.022
But now, let's preface each
paragraph with maybe H1.

18:58:36.022 --> 18:58:38.482
And I'm going to just
write the word one.

18:58:38.482 --> 18:58:41.452
And here I'm going to say H2, two.

18:58:41.452 --> 18:58:44.572
And down here I might say H3, three.

18:58:44.572 --> 18:58:49.185
So this is another tag,
another three tags, H1, H2, H3.

18:58:49.185 --> 18:58:51.352
As you might have inferred
by the file name I chose,

18:58:51.352 --> 18:58:55.342
this just gives you headings, like in
a book, different chapters or sections

18:58:55.342 --> 18:58:57.412
or subsections, or in
an academic paper, you

18:58:57.412 --> 18:59:00.182
have different hierarchies to
the text that you're writing.

18:59:00.182 --> 18:59:04.462
So now that I've added an H1 tag,
and the word one, H2 tag, the word

18:59:04.462 --> 18:59:07.492
two, H3 tag and the word three,
let's go back to the browser,

18:59:07.492 --> 18:59:12.232
reload the page again,
and voila, once the page

18:59:12.232 --> 18:59:17.152
reloads, I'll do it with the
manual button, reload the page.

18:59:17.152 --> 18:59:19.832
Oh, what am I doing wrong?

18:59:19.832 --> 18:59:20.332
Yeah.

18:59:20.332 --> 18:59:21.624
AUDIENCE: Not in headings file.

18:59:21.624 --> 18:59:23.582
SPEAKER 1: Right, I'm
not in the headings file.

18:59:23.582 --> 18:59:24.902
So let me go back a page.

18:59:24.902 --> 18:59:26.422
Now there's Headings.html.

18:59:26.422 --> 18:59:27.602
Let me click on that.

18:59:27.602 --> 18:59:29.302
OK, now we see some evidence of this.

18:59:29.302 --> 18:59:30.922
Again, it's nonsensical content.

18:59:30.922 --> 18:59:33.982
But you can kind of see that
H1 is apparently big and bold,

18:59:33.982 --> 18:59:36.922
H2 is slightly less big, but still bold.

18:59:36.922 --> 18:59:38.752
H3 is the same but a little smaller.

18:59:38.752 --> 18:59:40.367
And it goes all the way down to H6.

18:59:40.367 --> 18:59:42.742
After that, you should probably
reorganize your thoughts.

18:59:42.742 --> 18:59:44.632
But there are six
different hierarchies here,

18:59:44.632 --> 18:59:48.832
as you might use for chapters, sections,
subsections, and so forth, all right?

18:59:48.832 --> 18:59:53.062
So those are headings, as an
HTML tag, in our vocabulary.

18:59:53.062 --> 18:59:59.902
What's a common thing, too, well, let
me go to VS Code again, let me go ahead

18:59:59.902 --> 19:00:04.852
and get some boilerplate here,
create a file called List.html.

19:00:04.852 --> 19:00:07.642
Let's create a simple
list inside of my body,

19:00:07.642 --> 19:00:10.522
and I'll give this a title of List.

19:00:10.522 --> 19:00:13.532
And let me fix the title of this
one to be Headings, as well.

19:00:13.532 --> 19:00:19.172
So in List.html, suppose I want to have
a list of things, foo, bar, and baths,

19:00:19.172 --> 19:00:21.172
they're like a computer
scientist's go-to words,

19:00:21.172 --> 19:00:23.302
just like a mathematician might say xyz.

19:00:23.302 --> 19:00:26.122
Foo, bar, baths is in List.html.

19:00:26.122 --> 19:00:29.992
Let me go back to my
browser, hit the Back button.

19:00:29.992 --> 19:00:33.922
There's List.html, and, hopefully,
I'll see foo, bar, and baths, one

19:00:33.922 --> 19:00:37.892
on each line like a nice little
list, but, of course, I do not.

19:00:37.892 --> 19:00:38.962
And this is not English.

19:00:38.962 --> 19:00:41.002
Chrome thinks it might be Arabic.

19:00:41.002 --> 19:00:44.749
But that's curious, too,
because the Lang attribute

19:00:44.749 --> 19:00:45.832
should be overriding that.

19:00:45.832 --> 19:00:48.024
So Google is trying to override it.

19:00:48.024 --> 19:00:49.732
All right, what's the
obvious explanation

19:00:49.732 --> 19:00:52.762
why we're seeing foo, bar, and
baths on the same line, and not

19:00:52.762 --> 19:00:53.932
three separate ones?

19:00:53.932 --> 19:00:55.342
AUDIENCE: We didn't tell it.

19:00:55.342 --> 19:00:57.009
SPEAKER 1: We didn't tell it to do that.

19:00:57.009 --> 19:00:59.272
So we need paragraph tags,
or maybe something else.

19:00:59.272 --> 19:01:00.922
Turns out there is something else.

19:01:00.922 --> 19:01:04.972
There is a UL tag, for an
unordered list in HTML,

19:01:04.972 --> 19:01:08.597
inside of which you can
have LI tags, for list item,

19:01:08.597 --> 19:01:10.222
inside of which you can put your words.

19:01:10.222 --> 19:01:13.862
So there's my foo, there's
my bar, there's my baths.

19:01:13.862 --> 19:01:16.672
And, again, notice that VS Code
is finishing my thought for me.

19:01:16.672 --> 19:01:21.412
But notice the hierarchy, open
UL, open LI, close LI, open LI,

19:01:21.412 --> 19:01:24.772
close LI, open LI, close LI, close UL.

19:01:24.772 --> 19:01:27.142
So it's sort of done
in reverse order here.

19:01:27.142 --> 19:01:33.172
Let me go back to my browser, reload
the same page, List.html, and voila,

19:01:33.172 --> 19:01:36.382
a default bulleted list, that
still seems to be in Arabic.

19:01:36.382 --> 19:01:38.152
What if I want this list to be numbered?

19:01:38.152 --> 19:01:39.802
Well, you can probably guess.

19:01:39.802 --> 19:01:43.372
If you don't want an unordered list, but
an ordered list, what tag should I use?

19:01:43.372 --> 19:01:44.662
AUDIENCE: OL.

19:01:44.662 --> 19:01:46.642
SPEAKER 1: OL, sure, so let's try that.

19:01:46.642 --> 19:01:49.312
Not always that easy as just
guessing, but in this case,

19:01:49.312 --> 19:01:51.062
OL is going to do the trick.

19:01:51.062 --> 19:01:52.582
Let me go back to my other browser.

19:01:52.582 --> 19:01:55.665
Let me reload the page, and now it's
going to automatically number for me.

19:01:55.665 --> 19:01:58.112
It's a tiny thing, but
this is actually useful

19:01:58.112 --> 19:02:00.452
if you have a very long
list of data, and maybe you

19:02:00.452 --> 19:02:02.612
might add some things in the
middle, the beginning, or the end.

19:02:02.612 --> 19:02:04.945
It would just be annoying to
have to go and renumber it.

19:02:04.945 --> 19:02:07.772
The computer is doing it
for us by, instead, just

19:02:07.772 --> 19:02:10.152
numbering from top to bottom here.

19:02:10.152 --> 19:02:12.212
All right, what about
another type of layout,

19:02:12.212 --> 19:02:14.959
not just paragraphs, not just
lists, but what about tabular data?

19:02:14.959 --> 19:02:17.042
You've got some research
data you want to present,

19:02:17.042 --> 19:02:20.334
some financial data you want to present,
a phone book that you want to present.

19:02:20.334 --> 19:02:23.222
How might we go about laying
out data, a la a table?

19:02:23.222 --> 19:02:26.222
Well, let me create a
file called Table.html,

19:02:26.222 --> 19:02:28.922
and I'll just copy paste
where we started earlier.

19:02:28.922 --> 19:02:31.292
Let me start to close
some of these other files.

19:02:31.292 --> 19:02:34.689
And in Table.html, this is
going to be a bit more HTML,

19:02:34.689 --> 19:02:36.272
but I'm going to go ahead and do this.

19:02:36.272 --> 19:02:40.592
Table and close table, tables
can have table headings.

19:02:40.592 --> 19:02:45.882
So T head is the name of that tag, and
tables can have T bodies, table bodies.

19:02:45.882 --> 19:02:47.162
So I'm going to add that tag.

19:02:47.162 --> 19:02:49.620
And this is a common technique,
sort of start your thought,

19:02:49.620 --> 19:02:52.722
finish your thought, and then go
back and fill in what's in between.

19:02:52.722 --> 19:02:54.302
What do I want to put in this table?

19:02:54.302 --> 19:02:58.392
How about a bunch of names and numbers.

19:02:58.392 --> 19:03:02.352
So, for instance, like left
column name, right column number.

19:03:02.352 --> 19:03:05.642
So let's create a table row,
with what's called the TR tag.

19:03:05.642 --> 19:03:10.382
Let's create a table heading with
the TH tag, and let's say name here.

19:03:10.382 --> 19:03:14.262
Let's create another table
heading called number here.

19:03:14.262 --> 19:03:17.612
And all of that, to be
clear, is in one table row.

19:03:17.612 --> 19:03:21.332
Meanwhile, in the table body,
let me create another table row,

19:03:21.332 --> 19:03:23.102
but this time, it's not a heading.

19:03:23.102 --> 19:03:24.582
Now I'm in the guts of my table.

19:03:24.582 --> 19:03:28.172
Let's do table data, which is synonymous
with like the cell of the table,

19:03:28.172 --> 19:03:30.872
in like an Excel spreadsheet
or Google spreadsheet.

19:03:30.872 --> 19:03:33.722
In this TD, I'm going to
say like Carter's name,

19:03:33.722 --> 19:03:39.482
and then lets grab Carter's number
from our past demo, 617-495-1000.

19:03:39.482 --> 19:03:43.212
Then let's put me into the mix, and
I'll go ahead and copy paste here,

19:03:43.212 --> 19:03:44.342
which often is not good.

19:03:44.342 --> 19:03:47.462
But we'll see that there's a lot
of shared structure with HTML.

19:03:47.462 --> 19:03:53.772
Let me go ahead and do mine,
949-468-2750, and now save this page.

19:03:53.772 --> 19:03:56.192
So we're getting to be
a lot of indentation.

19:03:56.192 --> 19:03:59.712
I'm using four spaces by default.
Some people use two spaces by default.

19:03:59.712 --> 19:04:02.192
So long as you're consistent,
that's considered good style.

19:04:02.192 --> 19:04:04.982
But let me go back to my
browser here, and hit back.

19:04:04.982 --> 19:04:07.232
That then brings me to my
directory listing again.

19:04:07.232 --> 19:04:10.562
Here's Table.html, and this
is not that interesting yet.

19:04:10.562 --> 19:04:13.532
But you can see that there's
two columns, name and number.

19:04:13.532 --> 19:04:18.302
Because it's a table heading, TH,
the browser made it boldfaced for me.

19:04:18.302 --> 19:04:22.112
In there, in the table, are two
rows below that, Carter and David.

19:04:22.112 --> 19:04:25.262
It's a little, oh, I forgot my
number one, sorry about that.

19:04:25.262 --> 19:04:28.197
One and one, it's not the
prettiest table, right?

19:04:28.197 --> 19:04:30.072
I feel like I kind of
want to separate things

19:04:30.072 --> 19:04:32.155
a little more, maybe put
some borders or the like.

19:04:32.155 --> 19:04:36.282
But with HTML alone, I'm really
focusing on the structure alone.

19:04:36.282 --> 19:04:37.902
So we'll make this prettier soon.

19:04:37.902 --> 19:04:41.332
But for now, this is how you
might lay out tabular data.

19:04:41.332 --> 19:04:44.082
All right, let me pause here just
to see if there's any questions.

19:04:44.082 --> 19:04:46.412
But, again, the goal right now
is just to kind of throw at you

19:04:46.412 --> 19:04:49.802
some basic building blocks, that, again,
can be easily looked up in a reference.

19:04:49.802 --> 19:04:53.672
But we're going to start
stylizing these things soon, too.

19:04:53.672 --> 19:04:54.542
Yeah, in the middle.

19:04:54.542 --> 19:04:55.692
AUDIENCE: How to indent?

19:04:55.692 --> 19:04:57.387
SPEAKER 1: How do you indent paragraphs?

19:04:57.387 --> 19:04:58.262
Really good question.

19:04:58.262 --> 19:04:59.822
For that, we'll probably
going to want something

19:04:59.822 --> 19:05:01.309
called CSS, Cascading Style Sheets.

19:05:01.309 --> 19:05:03.392
So let me come back to
that, in just a little bit.

19:05:03.392 --> 19:05:06.782
For the stylization of these things,
beyond the basics, like big and bold,

19:05:06.782 --> 19:05:10.172
we're going to need a
different language altogether.

19:05:10.172 --> 19:05:13.172
All right, well, let's
now create what the web

19:05:13.172 --> 19:05:17.702
is full of, which is like
photographs and images and the like.

19:05:17.702 --> 19:05:22.832
Let me go ahead and create a new file
called Image.html, and let me go ahead

19:05:22.832 --> 19:05:25.782
and change the title
here to be, say, Image.

19:05:25.782 --> 19:05:29.189
And then, in the body of this page,
let's go ahead and put an image.

19:05:29.189 --> 19:05:31.772
The interesting thing about an
image is that it's actually not

19:05:31.772 --> 19:05:35.252
going to have a start tag and an end
tag, because that's kind of illogical.

19:05:35.252 --> 19:05:38.282
Like, how can you start an image
and then eventually finish it?

19:05:38.282 --> 19:05:39.902
It's either there or it isn't.

19:05:39.902 --> 19:05:42.542
So some tags do not have end tags.

19:05:42.542 --> 19:05:49.082
So let me do image, IMG,
source equals Harvard.jpeg.

19:05:49.082 --> 19:05:51.902
And let me go ahead, and,
in my terminal window,

19:05:51.902 --> 19:05:54.302
I actually came with a photo of Harvard.

19:05:54.302 --> 19:05:57.752
Let me grab this for just a second.

19:05:57.752 --> 19:06:01.172
Let me grab Harvard.jpeg and
put it into my directory,

19:06:01.172 --> 19:06:03.822
pretend that I downloaded
that in advance.

19:06:03.822 --> 19:06:06.422
And so I'm referring
to now a file called

19:06:06.422 --> 19:06:12.032
Harvard.jpeg, that apparently is in
the same folder as my Image.html file.

19:06:12.032 --> 19:06:15.212
If this image were on the
internet, like Harvard server,

19:06:15.212 --> 19:06:21.402
I could also say like
HTTPS://www.Harvard.edu/FolderName,

19:06:21.402 --> 19:06:25.562
whatever it is, /Harvard.jpeg, but
if you've in advance uploaded a file

19:06:25.562 --> 19:06:28.772
to your own, the Scode environment,
like I did before class,

19:06:28.772 --> 19:06:32.072
by dragging and dropping this
whole file, this photo of Harvard,

19:06:32.072 --> 19:06:35.402
you can just refer to it
relatively, so to speak.

19:06:35.402 --> 19:06:38.522
This would be the same thing
as saying ./Harvard.jpeg,

19:06:38.522 --> 19:06:41.942
go to the current directory and
get the file called Harvard.jpeg.

19:06:41.942 --> 19:06:43.862
But that's unnecessary to type.

19:06:43.862 --> 19:06:47.462
For accessibility purposes, though,
for someone who's vision-impaired,

19:06:47.462 --> 19:06:51.122
it's ideal if we also give this
an alternative text, something

19:06:51.122 --> 19:06:55.022
like Harvard University,
in the so-called Alt tag,

19:06:55.022 --> 19:06:57.332
and this is so that
screen readers will recite

19:06:57.332 --> 19:06:59.582
what it is the photo is,
for folks who can't see it.

19:06:59.582 --> 19:07:02.072
And if you're just on a slow
connection, sometimes you'll

19:07:02.072 --> 19:07:04.052
see the text of what
you're about to see,

19:07:04.052 --> 19:07:07.602
before the image itself downloads,
especially on a mobile device.

19:07:07.602 --> 19:07:12.242
So let's now go back to my open browser
tab, and let's look in the directory.

19:07:12.242 --> 19:07:16.532
I now have Harvard.jpeg, which I
downloaded in advance, and Image.html.

19:07:16.532 --> 19:07:20.252
Let me click on Image.html,
and here we have

19:07:20.252 --> 19:07:25.172
a really big picture of Memorial
Hall, the building we're currently in.

19:07:25.172 --> 19:07:29.762
Suffice it to say I should probably fix
this and maybe make it only so wide.

19:07:29.762 --> 19:07:33.272
But to do that, we're going to probably
want to use this other language, CSS.

19:07:33.272 --> 19:07:36.842
There are some historical
attributes that you can still

19:07:36.842 --> 19:07:39.284
use to control width and
height, and so forth.

19:07:39.284 --> 19:07:41.492
But we're going to do it
the better way, so to speak,

19:07:41.492 --> 19:07:44.042
with a language designed for just that.

19:07:44.042 --> 19:07:45.482
How about a video, though.

19:07:45.482 --> 19:07:50.402
I also came prepared with,
let me grab another file here,

19:07:50.402 --> 19:07:55.782
let me grab a file called
Halloween.mp4, which is an MPEG file.

19:07:55.782 --> 19:08:02.822
And let me go ahead and change this
now to be a file called Video.html.

19:08:02.822 --> 19:08:04.592
I'll change my title to be Video.

19:08:04.592 --> 19:08:07.982
And let's go ahead and now
introduce another tag, a video tag,

19:08:07.982 --> 19:08:13.172
open bracket video, and then let me go
ahead and close that tag proactively.

19:08:13.172 --> 19:08:17.462
And then inside of the video tag,
you can say the source of the video

19:08:17.462 --> 19:08:22.502
is going to be specifically
Halloween.mp4, the type of this file,

19:08:22.502 --> 19:08:27.512
I know, is Video/mp4, because I looked
up its content type or MIME type.

19:08:27.512 --> 19:08:29.702
And the video tag actually
has a few attributes.

19:08:29.702 --> 19:08:31.712
I can have this thing autoplay.

19:08:31.712 --> 19:08:33.392
I can have it loop forever.

19:08:33.392 --> 19:08:36.602
I can mute it, so that there's no
sound, which is necessary nowadays.

19:08:36.602 --> 19:08:41.382
Most browsers, to prevent ads, don't
autoplay videos, if they have sound.

19:08:41.382 --> 19:08:44.582
So if you mute your video, it
will autoplay, but presumably not

19:08:44.582 --> 19:08:45.602
annoy users.

19:08:45.602 --> 19:08:49.932
And let me set the width of this thing
to be like, oh, 1280 pixels wide.

19:08:49.932 --> 19:08:51.582
But I can make it any size I want.

19:08:51.582 --> 19:08:55.292
So I know this just from having
looked up the syntax for this tag.

19:08:55.292 --> 19:08:57.122
But notice one curiosity.

19:08:57.122 --> 19:08:59.822
Sometimes attributes don't have values.

19:08:59.822 --> 19:09:01.052
They're empty attributes.

19:09:01.052 --> 19:09:04.387
They're just single words,
autoplay, loop, muted,

19:09:04.387 --> 19:09:06.512
and that kind of makes
sense for any attribute that

19:09:06.512 --> 19:09:08.342
really does what it says.

19:09:08.342 --> 19:09:11.372
Like, it doesn't make sense
to say muted equals something.

19:09:11.372 --> 19:09:12.722
Like it's either muted or not.

19:09:12.722 --> 19:09:14.322
The attribute is there or not.

19:09:14.322 --> 19:09:16.362
Similarly, for these others, as well.

19:09:16.362 --> 19:09:19.982
So let me go back to my other browser
tab, reload the directory listing.

19:09:19.982 --> 19:09:24.122
There is both my mp4
and also Video.html,

19:09:24.122 --> 19:09:26.202
which is the web page that embeds it.

19:09:26.202 --> 19:09:29.792
And this is actually a video that was
just on Harvard's website yesterday,

19:09:29.792 --> 19:09:30.942
and it was amazing.

19:09:30.942 --> 19:09:35.222
So we included it in this demo here.

19:09:35.222 --> 19:09:41.792
This is the video that was on
Harvard.edu last night, same photo.

19:09:41.792 --> 19:09:44.010
But you can see here that
an image alone probably

19:09:44.010 --> 19:09:45.302
would not have the same effect.

19:09:45.302 --> 19:09:48.495
This is actually a movie, a small
video file that's now looping.

19:09:48.495 --> 19:09:51.662
Now there's some artifacts here, like
there's a white border around the top.

19:09:51.662 --> 19:09:53.495
I feel like it'd be
nice to fill the screen.

19:09:53.495 --> 19:09:58.032
But again, we'll come back to a language
that can allow us to do exactly that.

19:09:58.032 --> 19:10:00.272
Well, it's not just
videos like this, that you

19:10:00.272 --> 19:10:01.982
might want to put into a web page.

19:10:01.982 --> 19:10:06.284
Let me create another
file called iFrame.html.

19:10:06.284 --> 19:10:09.242
If you've ever poked around with, if
you have your own YouTube account,

19:10:09.242 --> 19:10:12.565
or if you had your own blog or
WordPress site, or Wix or Squarespace,

19:10:12.565 --> 19:10:14.732
you might have been in the
habit of embedding videos

19:10:14.732 --> 19:10:17.672
in websites, using like
embedded YouTube players.

19:10:17.672 --> 19:10:21.362
Well, this is possible, too, using
what's called an inline frame,

19:10:21.362 --> 19:10:22.412
an iFrame.

19:10:22.412 --> 19:10:25.532
And an iFrame is just a tag
that is literally iFrame.

19:10:25.532 --> 19:10:28.960
It has source equals,
and then a URL, and if it

19:10:28.960 --> 19:10:32.252
happens to be a YouTube video, there's
a certain URL format you need to follow,

19:10:32.252 --> 19:10:33.812
per YouTube's documentation.

19:10:33.812 --> 19:10:41.652
So you might do www.youtube.com, embed,
and then here's an ID of a video.

19:10:41.652 --> 19:10:47.192
So this is essentially what we do, if
we want to embed CS50's own lecture

19:10:47.192 --> 19:10:50.402
videos, in the course's website, or
the video player does literally this.

19:10:50.402 --> 19:10:53.912
If I want to allow full screen,
I can add this attribute, too,

19:10:53.912 --> 19:10:56.662
that I know exists, by just
having checked the documentation.

19:10:56.662 --> 19:11:00.721
And if I now go back to my browser
here, reload my directory listing,

19:11:00.721 --> 19:11:02.039
there's iFrame.html.

19:11:02.039 --> 19:11:03.872
It's not going to fill
the screen, because I

19:11:03.872 --> 19:11:05.761
haven't customized the aesthetics yet.

19:11:05.761 --> 19:11:10.112
But it does seem to embed a tiny little
video there for you to play with later,

19:11:10.112 --> 19:11:10.721
if you'd like.

19:11:10.721 --> 19:11:13.801
So we could change the width, change
the height, get rid of that margin,

19:11:13.801 --> 19:11:14.471
and so forth.

19:11:14.471 --> 19:11:19.171
But an iFrame is a way of embedding
someone else's web page in your web

19:11:19.171 --> 19:11:21.962
page, if they allow
it, so as to create all

19:11:21.962 --> 19:11:25.652
the more of an interactive experience
for them on, say, your site.

19:11:25.652 --> 19:11:28.652
All right, well, the web is, of
course, known for things like links.

19:11:28.652 --> 19:11:32.072
Let's go ahead and create
a file called Link.html.

19:11:32.072 --> 19:11:35.582
And if we want to create a web page that
actually links from itself somewhere

19:11:35.582 --> 19:11:40.101
else, let's go ahead and do this,
something very simple like visit

19:11:40.101 --> 19:11:41.480
Harvard.edu period.

19:11:44.551 --> 19:11:48.182
Now, in like Facebook, Instagram, a lot
of websites nowadays, if you just type

19:11:48.182 --> 19:11:50.852
in a domain name, or a
fully qualified domain name,

19:11:50.852 --> 19:11:52.772
it automatically becomes a link.

19:11:52.772 --> 19:11:56.162
That's because those websites have
code in them that automatically

19:11:56.162 --> 19:12:00.551
detects something that looks like a
URL, and turns it into a proper link.

19:12:00.551 --> 19:12:02.452
HTML itself does not do that for you.

19:12:02.452 --> 19:12:06.822
And so if I go back to my web
page here, click on Link.html,

19:12:06.822 --> 19:12:09.852
if you type visit
Harvard.edu period, that's

19:12:09.852 --> 19:12:11.592
all you're literally going to see.

19:12:11.592 --> 19:12:15.192
But instinctively, even if you've
never written HTML before, what should

19:12:15.192 --> 19:12:19.327
we probably do here
to solve this problem?

19:12:19.327 --> 19:12:20.952
What could we do to solve this problem.

19:12:20.952 --> 19:12:22.244
What do I probably want to add.

19:12:22.244 --> 19:12:22.806
Yeah.

19:12:22.806 --> 19:12:24.462
AUDIENCE: Surround your--

19:12:24.462 --> 19:12:27.702
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, so I want to surround
the URL with some kind of link text.

19:12:27.702 --> 19:12:30.285
And you wouldn't necessarily
know this until someone told you,

19:12:30.285 --> 19:12:33.342
or you looked it up, but the tag for
creating a link is somewhat weirdly

19:12:33.342 --> 19:12:35.832
called the A tag for anchor.

19:12:35.832 --> 19:12:39.252
It has an attribute called
HREF for hyper-reference,

19:12:39.252 --> 19:12:42.792
which is like a link in
the virtual world to a URL.

19:12:42.792 --> 19:12:45.882
So let me type in Harvard's
full and proper URL here.

19:12:45.882 --> 19:12:48.102
Then I'm going to close the tag.

19:12:48.102 --> 19:12:53.412
And then I can still say Harvard.edu,
and make that what the human sees.

19:12:53.412 --> 19:12:58.962
But the place they're going to go
should be a full URL protocol and all,

19:12:58.962 --> 19:13:00.972
HTTP or HTTPS, and all.

19:13:00.972 --> 19:13:03.282
Now if I go back here
and reload the page,

19:13:03.282 --> 19:13:05.322
now it automatically gets underlined.

19:13:05.322 --> 19:13:07.092
It happens to be purple by default. Why?

19:13:07.092 --> 19:13:09.292
Because we visited
Harvard.edu a few minutes ago.

19:13:09.292 --> 19:13:12.642
So my browser, by default, is indicating
in purple that I've been there before.

19:13:12.642 --> 19:13:14.592
But now I have a link
that I can click on,

19:13:14.592 --> 19:13:18.882
and if I hover over it but don't click,
you'll see that, in most browsers,

19:13:18.882 --> 19:13:22.842
there's a little clue as to where
you will go if you click subsequently

19:13:22.842 --> 19:13:24.012
on this link.

19:13:24.012 --> 19:13:26.082
And without going too
far down a rabbit hole,

19:13:26.082 --> 19:13:29.472
but to tie together our discussion
of cybersecurity recently,

19:13:29.472 --> 19:13:32.862
what if I were to do
something like this.

19:13:32.862 --> 19:13:37.332
Right now you have the beginnings
of a phishing attack of sorts,

19:13:37.332 --> 19:13:42.282
P-H-I-S-H-I-N-G, whereby you can
create clearly a web page, or, heck,

19:13:42.282 --> 19:13:46.542
even an email using HTML, that tells
the user they're going to go one place,

19:13:46.542 --> 19:13:49.612
but they're really going to
go someplace else altogether.

19:13:49.612 --> 19:13:52.122
And that is the essence of
phishing attacks these days.

19:13:52.122 --> 19:13:55.452
If you've ever gotten a bogus
email pretending to be from PayPal

19:13:55.452 --> 19:13:57.912
or your bank or some
other website, odds are

19:13:57.912 --> 19:14:00.432
they've just written HTML
that says whatever they want,

19:14:00.432 --> 19:14:04.042
but the underlying tags might
do something very different.

19:14:04.042 --> 19:14:06.792
And so having the instinct to look
in the bottom left hand corner,

19:14:06.792 --> 19:14:10.212
or be a little suspicious when you're
just told blindly to click on a link,

19:14:10.212 --> 19:14:13.602
it's this easy to socially
engineer people, that is,

19:14:13.602 --> 19:14:18.342
deceive them, by just saying one
thing and linking to another.

19:14:18.342 --> 19:14:22.572
Well, what if I want to link my page
to another page I already created?

19:14:22.572 --> 19:14:25.422
Well, if I want to link
to that photo of Harvard,

19:14:25.422 --> 19:14:28.722
I can just do HREF = equals quote
unquote and the name of a file,

19:14:28.722 --> 19:14:32.122
in my same account, that
is itself a web page.

19:14:32.122 --> 19:14:35.682
So this is how you can create
relative links, multi-page web

19:14:35.682 --> 19:14:38.272
pages, multi-page websites, yourself.

19:14:38.272 --> 19:14:41.652
So if I now reload this
page, hover over Harvard.edu,

19:14:41.652 --> 19:14:45.192
you'll see in the bottom left
hand corner a very long URL.

19:14:45.192 --> 19:14:48.102
But that's because I'm in code
spaces right now, VS Code,

19:14:48.102 --> 19:14:51.762
and it's appending automatically
to the end of my current URL

19:14:51.762 --> 19:14:53.947
the file name, Image.html.

19:14:53.947 --> 19:14:54.822
But this should work.

19:14:54.822 --> 19:14:57.252
When I click on this, I
go immediately to that

19:14:57.252 --> 19:15:01.152
file we created earlier, with a
crazy, big version of the image.

19:15:01.152 --> 19:15:03.822
But that's just a way
that one page on a website

19:15:03.822 --> 19:15:07.722
can link to another page on a website.

19:15:07.722 --> 19:15:11.712
Let's do one other thing here,
making things more responsive,

19:15:11.712 --> 19:15:14.622
because, in fact, that wasn't a
particularly responsive website.

19:15:14.622 --> 19:15:17.702
Responsive means responding to the
size of the user's device, which

19:15:17.702 --> 19:15:20.202
is so important when someone
might be on a screen like this,

19:15:20.202 --> 19:15:22.032
or on a screen like this these days.

19:15:22.032 --> 19:15:26.892
There are special tags we can use to
tell the browser to modify its display,

19:15:26.892 --> 19:15:28.272
based on the hardware.

19:15:28.272 --> 19:15:32.382
So let me create a file
called Responsive.html.

19:15:32.382 --> 19:15:36.132
I'm going to copy/paste some starting
point here, call this title Responsive.

19:15:36.132 --> 19:15:40.752
And let me go ahead and just grab, let
me grab some of that lorem ipsum text

19:15:40.752 --> 19:15:46.342
from before, just so that we have a
sizable paragraph to play with here.

19:15:46.342 --> 19:15:50.172
And let me go ahead and
grab this text here.

19:15:50.172 --> 19:15:53.632
And I'm just going to paste
this into the body of this page.

19:15:53.632 --> 19:15:54.412
And that's it.

19:15:54.412 --> 19:15:57.762
So I just have a big paragraph,
at the moment, inside of my body.

19:15:57.762 --> 19:15:59.532
Let me go back to my browser.

19:15:59.532 --> 19:16:02.622
Let me open up this file,
called Responsive.html,

19:16:02.622 --> 19:16:05.442
to make the point that
it is not yet responsive.

19:16:05.442 --> 19:16:07.782
Let me go ahead and
click on Responsive.html.

19:16:07.782 --> 19:16:08.962
That looks fine.

19:16:08.962 --> 19:16:12.382
But here's another trick you can do,
using Chrome or Edge or other browsers

19:16:12.382 --> 19:16:12.882
these days.

19:16:12.882 --> 19:16:14.922
You can pretend to be another device.

19:16:14.922 --> 19:16:19.332
Let me go to View, developer,
developer tools again.

19:16:19.332 --> 19:16:21.780
Last time we used this to
use the Network tab, which

19:16:21.780 --> 19:16:24.822
was kind of interesting, because we
could see what the underlying network

19:16:24.822 --> 19:16:25.822
traffic is.

19:16:25.822 --> 19:16:29.112
But notice, we can also click on
this icon, in Chrome, at least,

19:16:29.112 --> 19:16:31.152
that looks like a mobile phone.

19:16:31.152 --> 19:16:36.199
I can turn my laptop into what looks
like a mobile device by clicking this.

19:16:36.199 --> 19:16:39.282
I'm going to click the dot dot dot
menu over here, and just move the dock.

19:16:39.282 --> 19:16:41.502
Instead of on the bottom,
where it might be by default,

19:16:41.502 --> 19:16:43.282
I'm going to move it
to the right hand side.

19:16:43.282 --> 19:16:45.312
So that now on the left,
you see what looks more

19:16:45.312 --> 19:16:47.082
like the shape of a vertical phone.

19:16:47.082 --> 19:16:49.842
And, in fact, if I go
to my dimensions here,

19:16:49.842 --> 19:16:53.742
I'll choose something like
iPhone X, so a few years back.

19:16:53.742 --> 19:16:57.662
Here's what that same website might
look like on an iPhone X. You know,

19:16:57.662 --> 19:17:01.322
that looks pretty damn
small, to be able to read it.

19:17:01.322 --> 19:17:03.842
And that's because the
website has not automatically

19:17:03.842 --> 19:17:07.772
responded to the fairly narrow
dimensions of the iPhone

19:17:07.772 --> 19:17:09.972
in question, or Android
device, or whatnot.

19:17:09.972 --> 19:17:11.412
So let me go ahead and do this.

19:17:11.412 --> 19:17:13.392
Let me go back into my code.

19:17:13.392 --> 19:17:16.382
And let me go into the head of
the page, and for the first time,

19:17:16.382 --> 19:17:18.150
add another tag up here.

19:17:18.150 --> 19:17:20.192
This word is now all over
the internet, but there

19:17:20.192 --> 19:17:23.132
is a metatag that is
called, that allows you

19:17:23.132 --> 19:17:27.010
to specify the name of some kind
of configuration detail here,

19:17:27.010 --> 19:17:28.052
or property, if you will.

19:17:28.052 --> 19:17:31.082
Viewport is the technical term
for the rectangular region

19:17:31.082 --> 19:17:32.742
that the human sees in a browser.

19:17:32.742 --> 19:17:35.492
It's essentially the body of the
page. but only the part the human

19:17:35.492 --> 19:17:37.052
is currently seeing.

19:17:37.052 --> 19:17:39.752
And you can specify the
content of the viewport

19:17:39.752 --> 19:17:41.762
should have an initial scale of 1.

19:17:41.762 --> 19:17:43.652
So it shouldn't be zoomed in or out.

19:17:43.652 --> 19:17:46.292
And the width that the
browser should assume

19:17:46.292 --> 19:17:48.812
should be equal to the device's width.

19:17:48.812 --> 19:17:51.722
These are sort of magical statements
that you just have to know

19:17:51.722 --> 19:17:55.772
or copy/paste or transcribe, that
just express, to the browser,

19:17:55.772 --> 19:17:59.612
assume that the width of the page is the
same thing as the width of the device.

19:17:59.612 --> 19:18:03.452
Don't assume the luxury of a
big laptop or desktop computer.

19:18:03.452 --> 19:18:08.432
Now, making only that change, let
me go back to my pretend iPhone

19:18:08.432 --> 19:18:10.682
here, using Chrome's developer tools.

19:18:10.682 --> 19:18:13.232
Let me reload the page.

19:18:13.232 --> 19:18:18.722
And now, it's not very effective on this
screen, if I were showing you this on,

19:18:18.722 --> 19:18:20.432
is there--

19:18:20.432 --> 19:18:21.702
well, there we go.

19:18:21.702 --> 19:18:22.872
Let's do this.

19:18:22.872 --> 19:18:23.502
There we go.

19:18:23.502 --> 19:18:28.352
So if I zoom in to 100%, this would be
on an actual physical device, much more

19:18:28.352 --> 19:18:30.372
readable than it would
have been a moment ago,

19:18:30.372 --> 19:18:33.039
even though I realized that demo
was not necessarily persuasive.

19:18:33.039 --> 19:18:34.832
But it's as simple as
telling the browser

19:18:34.832 --> 19:18:38.802
to resize the thing to
the width of the page.

19:18:38.802 --> 19:18:41.972
All right, let me pause here to see
if there's any questions, because that

19:18:41.972 --> 19:18:43.592
feels like enough HTML tags.

19:18:43.592 --> 19:18:45.312
We'll add just a couple of more in.

19:18:45.312 --> 19:18:47.402
But for the most part,
like HTML tags are

19:18:47.402 --> 19:18:51.242
things you Google and figure out over
time, just to build up your vocabulary.

19:18:51.242 --> 19:18:54.182
The basic building blocks
are tags, attributes.

19:18:54.182 --> 19:18:55.652
Some attributes have values.

19:18:55.652 --> 19:18:56.462
Some do not.

19:18:56.462 --> 19:19:00.175
And that's sort of the
structure of HTML in essence.

19:19:00.175 --> 19:19:01.592
Questions on any of these, though.

19:19:01.592 --> 19:19:02.330
Yeah.

19:19:02.330 --> 19:19:03.962
AUDIENCE: Do attributes have an order?

19:19:03.962 --> 19:19:05.612
SPEAKER 1: Do attributes have an order?

19:19:05.612 --> 19:19:08.702
No, attributes can be in any
order, from left to right.

19:19:08.702 --> 19:19:11.702
I tend to be a little nit-picky,
and so I alphabetize them,

19:19:11.702 --> 19:19:14.917
if only because then I can easily
spot if something's missing,

19:19:14.917 --> 19:19:16.292
if it's not there alphabetically.

19:19:16.292 --> 19:19:21.952
Most people on the internet
don't seem to do that.

19:19:21.952 --> 19:19:23.182
Yeah, in the middle.

19:19:23.182 --> 19:19:23.962
Version.

19:19:23.962 --> 19:19:24.802
Yeah, good question.

19:19:24.802 --> 19:19:26.812
I mentioned that HTML
is starting to replace

19:19:26.812 --> 19:19:28.852
other languages for user interfaces.

19:19:28.852 --> 19:19:30.292
And it's not just HTML alone.

19:19:30.292 --> 19:19:34.102
It's HTML with CSS, with JavaScript,
both of which we'll get a taste of here

19:19:34.102 --> 19:19:35.212
today.

19:19:35.212 --> 19:19:37.912
That rather has been the
trend for portability,

19:19:37.912 --> 19:19:40.582
and the ability for companies,
for individual programmers,

19:19:40.582 --> 19:19:42.952
to write one version
of an app and have it

19:19:42.952 --> 19:19:47.092
work on Android devices and iPhones
and Macs and PCs, and the like.

19:19:47.092 --> 19:19:48.352
It is very expensive.

19:19:48.352 --> 19:19:51.412
It is very time-consuming to
learn a language like Java

19:19:51.412 --> 19:19:54.322
and write an Android app, learn
another language called Swift

19:19:54.322 --> 19:19:56.992
and make an iOS app, not to
mention make them look and behave

19:19:56.992 --> 19:19:59.124
the same, not to
mention fix a bug in one

19:19:59.124 --> 19:20:00.832
and then remember to
fix it in the other.

19:20:00.832 --> 19:20:05.062
I mean, this is just very painful
and time-consuming and costly.

19:20:05.062 --> 19:20:09.352
So this standardization on
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript,

19:20:09.352 --> 19:20:13.432
even for mobile apps and web apps,
has been increasingly compelling,

19:20:13.432 --> 19:20:16.012
because it solves problems like that.

19:20:16.012 --> 19:20:19.852
All right, so let's go ahead and now do
something that's finally interactive.

19:20:19.852 --> 19:20:22.162
All of these pages thus
far are really just tastes

19:20:22.162 --> 19:20:25.042
of static content, content
that does not change.

19:20:25.042 --> 19:20:27.992
Well, let's go ahead and do this.

19:20:27.992 --> 19:20:31.012
Let me introduce one other
format of URLs, which looks

19:20:31.012 --> 19:20:33.052
a little something like it did before.

19:20:33.052 --> 19:20:36.452
So slash path, but it could
actually be something like this,

19:20:36.452 --> 19:20:40.012
slash path question
mark, key equals value.

19:20:40.012 --> 19:20:42.022
You might not have noticed,
or cared to notice,

19:20:42.022 --> 19:20:44.642
the URLs in your URL bar every day.

19:20:44.642 --> 19:20:46.282
But these things are everywhere.

19:20:46.282 --> 19:20:49.222
Often when you type into a
search engine like Google

19:20:49.222 --> 19:20:52.492
a search query, whatever you
just typed ends up in the URL.

19:20:52.492 --> 19:20:55.099
When you click on a link that
contains some information,

19:20:55.099 --> 19:20:57.682
there might be a question mark,
and then some keys and values.

19:20:57.682 --> 19:21:00.472
There might be an ampersand
and more keys and values.

19:21:00.472 --> 19:21:02.902
Here, again, is that very
common programming paradigm

19:21:02.902 --> 19:21:05.062
of just associating keys with values.

19:21:05.062 --> 19:21:07.022
We can see this as follows.

19:21:07.022 --> 19:21:11.392
Let me actually go to
google.com, in a browser

19:21:11.392 --> 19:21:16.042
here, and let me search for something
the internet is filled with, cats.

19:21:16.042 --> 19:21:20.482
Enter, notice now that my
URL changed from google.com

19:21:20.482 --> 19:21:23.992
to google.com slash
search question mark,

19:21:23.992 --> 19:21:27.082
Q equals cats, ampersand
and then a bunch of stuff

19:21:27.082 --> 19:21:28.582
that I don't understand or know.

19:21:28.582 --> 19:21:33.502
So let's just delete it for now, and
leave it with the essence of that URL.

19:21:33.502 --> 19:21:34.560
And that still works.

19:21:34.560 --> 19:21:37.102
If I zoom out here, years ago
you would get pictures of cats.

19:21:37.102 --> 19:21:41.122
Now you get videos of the movie.

19:21:41.122 --> 19:21:44.332
And then that top query
there, is Cats a bad movie.

19:21:44.332 --> 19:21:46.822
But we can also, of
course, click on Images.

19:21:46.822 --> 19:21:49.522
And there are the
adorable cat, creepy cats.

19:21:49.522 --> 19:21:52.342
All right, this didn't used to
happen when we searched for cats.

19:21:52.342 --> 19:21:57.922
But anyhow, the point is that the URL
changed to include the user's input.

19:21:57.922 --> 19:22:00.562
And this is such a simple,
but such a powerful thing.

19:22:00.562 --> 19:22:04.822
This is how humans
provide input to servers.

19:22:04.822 --> 19:22:07.742
They don't manually create the
URLs, like I sort of just did.

19:22:07.742 --> 19:22:10.792
But when you fill out a form
on the web and you hit Enter,

19:22:10.792 --> 19:22:13.672
typically the URL suddenly
changes to include

19:22:13.672 --> 19:22:16.552
whatever you typed in,
in the URL, assuming

19:22:16.552 --> 19:22:18.892
the form is using the verb GET.

19:22:18.892 --> 19:22:20.332
That's not ideal.

19:22:20.332 --> 19:22:22.124
If you're typing in a
username, a password,

19:22:22.124 --> 19:22:25.332
a credit card information, because you
don't want the next person to sit down

19:22:25.332 --> 19:22:27.952
at your laptop to see literally
everything you typed in,

19:22:27.952 --> 19:22:29.332
saved in your history.

19:22:29.332 --> 19:22:32.062
So there's another verb, POST,
that can hide all of that.

19:22:32.062 --> 19:22:33.772
And it's just sent a little differently.

19:22:33.772 --> 19:22:36.502
But things like this are
typically sent via GET,

19:22:36.502 --> 19:22:39.922
and what that means underneath the
hood is that your browser is just

19:22:39.922 --> 19:22:43.432
making a request like this, Get/search?

19:22:43.432 --> 19:22:47.752
Q equals, whatever you typed in, the
host that you visited, and so forth.

19:22:47.752 --> 19:22:52.822
And hopefully what comes back is a page
full of search results, including cats.

19:22:52.822 --> 19:22:57.962
And what's interesting here now is, if
I go back to VS Code on my own computer,

19:22:57.962 --> 19:23:04.792
and let me go ahead and create a
file called, how about Search.html.

19:23:04.792 --> 19:23:09.682
In Search.html, I'm going to start
with some copy/paste from before,

19:23:09.682 --> 19:23:11.032
change my title to search.

19:23:11.032 --> 19:23:14.612
And in the body of this page, I'm
going to introduce a form tag.

19:23:14.612 --> 19:23:18.532
And in this form tag, I'm going
to have a couple of inputs.

19:23:18.532 --> 19:23:25.252
And the types of inputs are going to
be text, and the type of the input

19:23:25.252 --> 19:23:28.449
is going to be submit.

19:23:28.449 --> 19:23:30.532
And this isn't that
interesting yet, but let's see

19:23:30.532 --> 19:23:32.422
what is happening in the page itself.

19:23:32.422 --> 19:23:34.762
Let me go back to my directory listing.

19:23:34.762 --> 19:23:36.922
Let me click on Search.html.

19:23:36.922 --> 19:23:39.442
I seem to have the beginning
of my own search engine.

19:23:39.442 --> 19:23:40.552
It's not very interesting.

19:23:40.552 --> 19:23:43.202
It's just a text box
and a submit button.

19:23:43.202 --> 19:23:45.002
But let's finish my thoughts here.

19:23:45.002 --> 19:23:49.912
So let's specifically give
this text box a name of Q,

19:23:49.912 --> 19:23:53.752
which, if you roll back to the late '90s
when Larry and Sergey of Google fame

19:23:53.752 --> 19:23:58.042
created Google.com, Q represented query,
the query that the human's typing in.

19:23:58.042 --> 19:24:01.822
So the name of this
text box shall be text,

19:24:01.822 --> 19:24:05.842
shall be Q. The form is
going to use what method?

19:24:05.842 --> 19:24:07.702
Technically it uses GET
by default, but I'll

19:24:07.702 --> 19:24:10.282
be explicit and say method
equals quote unquote "get."

19:24:10.282 --> 19:24:14.632
Stupidly, it's lowercase in HTML, even
though what's in the envelope is indeed

19:24:14.632 --> 19:24:16.852
uppercase, by convention.

19:24:16.852 --> 19:24:21.492
The action of this form, specifically,
would ideally go to my own server.

19:24:21.492 --> 19:24:24.352
But we don't really have time
today to implement Google itself.

19:24:24.352 --> 19:24:28.942
So we're just going to send the
user's request to google.com/search.

19:24:28.942 --> 19:24:31.152
So I'm creating a form,
the action of which

19:24:31.152 --> 19:24:35.712
is to send the data to Google's slash
search path, using the GET method.

19:24:35.712 --> 19:24:41.022
It's going to send an input called Q,
whenever I click this Submit button.

19:24:41.022 --> 19:24:44.082
Let me go back to the
browser, reload the page.

19:24:44.082 --> 19:24:48.912
Nothing seems to have changed yet,
but, if I search for, let me zoom out,

19:24:48.912 --> 19:24:50.352
so we can see the URL bar.

19:24:50.352 --> 19:24:53.952
Right now I'm in Search.html.

19:24:53.952 --> 19:24:57.282
If I zoom out and search for
cats now and click Submit,

19:24:57.282 --> 19:24:59.262
I'm whisked away to google.com.

19:24:59.262 --> 19:25:02.952
But notice that the URL is
parameterized, with those key value

19:25:02.952 --> 19:25:04.572
pairs, that key value pair.

19:25:04.572 --> 19:25:06.432
And I get back a whole
bunch of cat results.

19:25:06.432 --> 19:25:08.772
And I can very easily now
make this a little prettier.

19:25:08.772 --> 19:25:11.802
Right now, it's not ideal that like
the human has to move their cursor

19:25:11.802 --> 19:25:13.117
and click in the box.

19:25:13.117 --> 19:25:15.492
And it's a little obnoxious
that autocomplete is enabled.

19:25:15.492 --> 19:25:17.292
If I don't want to
search for cats anymore,

19:25:17.292 --> 19:25:21.282
well, according to HTML's documentation,
I can say something like this.

19:25:21.282 --> 19:25:25.362
Autocomplete equals off, to turn
off autocomplete, auto focus

19:25:25.362 --> 19:25:28.522
to automatically put the
cursor inside of that text box.

19:25:28.522 --> 19:25:32.292
If I want some explanatory text, I can
put placeholder text like quote unquote

19:25:32.292 --> 19:25:33.522
"query."

19:25:33.522 --> 19:25:35.652
And now if I go back to
this page and reload,

19:25:35.652 --> 19:25:37.462
now it's a little more user-friendly.

19:25:37.462 --> 19:25:39.642
You see query in kind of gray text.

19:25:39.642 --> 19:25:41.472
The cursor is already
there and blinking.

19:25:41.472 --> 19:25:43.002
I don't have to even move my cursor.

19:25:43.002 --> 19:25:46.122
I can search for dogs now, and you
didn't see any autocomplete at all.

19:25:46.122 --> 19:25:48.912
Hit enter to submit, and
now I'm searching for,

19:25:48.912 --> 19:25:51.832
there we go, adorable dogs, instead.

19:25:51.832 --> 19:25:52.762
So what have I done?

19:25:52.762 --> 19:25:56.852
I've implemented the front end of
Google.com, just not the back end.

19:25:56.852 --> 19:25:58.602
To implement the back
end, we're obviously

19:25:58.602 --> 19:26:01.812
going to need like a really big
database, maybe something like SQL.

19:26:01.812 --> 19:26:05.484
We're going to need some code that like
searches the database for dogs or cats,

19:26:05.484 --> 19:26:06.192
or anything else.

19:26:06.192 --> 19:26:08.092
We're going to need Python
for something like that.

19:26:08.092 --> 19:26:09.912
And in fact, that's the direction
we're steering next week,

19:26:09.912 --> 19:26:11.262
when we implement that back end.

19:26:11.262 --> 19:26:14.592
But today it's all about this front end.

19:26:14.592 --> 19:26:20.682
Or any question, then, about forms,
these URL parameters, before we now

19:26:20.682 --> 19:26:24.680
transition to making things look
a little prettier, with CSS?

19:26:24.680 --> 19:26:27.222
And then we'll end by making
things a little more functional,

19:26:27.222 --> 19:26:29.422
with JavaScript.

19:26:29.422 --> 19:26:31.442
Anything at all?

19:26:31.442 --> 19:26:31.942
No?

19:26:31.942 --> 19:26:35.122
All right, so let's start to answer
a couple of the questions that

19:26:35.122 --> 19:26:40.232
came up, by making these pages a
little more aesthetically interesting.

19:26:40.232 --> 19:26:45.872
Let's go ahead now and introduce to
the mix one other language, as follows.

19:26:45.872 --> 19:26:48.562
Let me go ahead and create
a file called Home.html,

19:26:48.562 --> 19:26:51.292
as though I'm making a home
page for the very first time.

19:26:51.292 --> 19:26:53.962
And in this page, I'm going
to give a title of Home.

19:26:53.962 --> 19:26:55.892
And I'm just going to
have like three things.

19:26:55.892 --> 19:27:00.292
First I'm going to have maybe
a paragraph of text up here

19:27:00.292 --> 19:27:03.322
at the top, that says something
welcoming for my home page,

19:27:03.322 --> 19:27:06.742
like my name, John Harvard, for
instance, or John Harvard's home page.

19:27:06.742 --> 19:27:10.072
Then in the middle of the page,
I'm going to have some text like,

19:27:10.072 --> 19:27:12.982
welcome to my home
page exclamation point!

19:27:12.982 --> 19:27:16.042
And at the bottom of the page, I'm
going to have a final paragraph that

19:27:16.042 --> 19:27:19.672
says something like copyright,
the copyright symbol, John

19:27:19.672 --> 19:27:21.172
Harvard, or something like that.

19:27:21.172 --> 19:27:25.432
All right, so it's like a web page
with three different structural areas,

19:27:25.432 --> 19:27:26.722
made with text.

19:27:26.722 --> 19:27:27.982
This isn't that interesting.

19:27:27.982 --> 19:27:32.422
If I open this page called
Home.html, let me go ahead

19:27:32.422 --> 19:27:35.932
and create three quick paragraphs,
a first paragraph for John Harvard.

19:27:35.932 --> 19:27:39.292
Inside the middle, I'm going to say
something like welcome to my home page

19:27:39.292 --> 19:27:40.232
exclamation point!

19:27:40.232 --> 19:27:42.472
And at the bottom,
whoops, at the bottom,

19:27:42.472 --> 19:27:45.652
a little footer that says
something like copyright,

19:27:45.652 --> 19:27:50.332
a little simple copyright
symbol, and John Harvard's name.

19:27:50.332 --> 19:27:52.365
All right, now let me reload the page.

19:27:52.365 --> 19:27:53.032
And there we go.

19:27:53.032 --> 19:27:57.292
It's a very simple, very underwhelming
web page that has three main sections.

19:27:57.292 --> 19:28:00.189
Let's start to now stylize
this in an interesting way,

19:28:00.189 --> 19:28:02.272
so that it's a little more
aesthetically pleasing.

19:28:02.272 --> 19:28:04.252
First, these aren't really paragraphs.

19:28:04.252 --> 19:28:08.105
They're sort of like areas of the page,
divisions, like the header is up here.

19:28:08.105 --> 19:28:09.772
There's like the main part of my screen.

19:28:09.772 --> 19:28:11.522
And then there's the
footer of my screen.

19:28:11.522 --> 19:28:13.814
So paragraphs isn't quite
right, if these aren't really

19:28:13.814 --> 19:28:15.152
paragraphs of texts.

19:28:15.152 --> 19:28:17.722
I might more properly call
them divs or divisions

19:28:17.722 --> 19:28:21.832
of the page, which is a very commonly
used tag in HTML, which just has

19:28:21.832 --> 19:28:24.122
this generic rectangular region to it.

19:28:24.122 --> 19:28:28.192
It does not do anything aesthetically,
no bold facing, no size changes.

19:28:28.192 --> 19:28:32.602
It just creates an invisible
rectangular region, inside of which

19:28:32.602 --> 19:28:34.342
you can start to style the text.

19:28:34.342 --> 19:28:36.262
Or I can take this one step further.

19:28:36.262 --> 19:28:40.462
There's some other tags in HTML,
known as semantic tags, that literally

19:28:40.462 --> 19:28:42.870
have names that describe the
types of your page, which

19:28:42.870 --> 19:28:45.412
is all the more compelling these
days for accessibility, too,

19:28:45.412 --> 19:28:49.882
for screen readers, for search engines,
because now, a screen reader, a search

19:28:49.882 --> 19:28:52.584
engine can realize that footer
is probably a little fluffy.

19:28:52.584 --> 19:28:54.292
The header might be
a little interesting.

19:28:54.292 --> 19:28:57.202
The main part of the page
is probably the juicy part,

19:28:57.202 --> 19:29:01.762
that I want users to be able to search
for or read aloud, substantively.

19:29:01.762 --> 19:29:04.672
So let's start to stylize
this page somehow.

19:29:04.672 --> 19:29:08.482
Let's introduce a style
attribute in HTML,

19:29:08.482 --> 19:29:13.102
inside of which is going to be
text like this, font size colon

19:29:13.102 --> 19:29:16.852
large, text align colon center.

19:29:16.852 --> 19:29:20.182
On Main, I'm going to add a
style attribute and say font size

19:29:20.182 --> 19:29:24.022
medium, text align center.

19:29:24.022 --> 19:29:27.742
And then on the footer, I'm going
to say style equals font size

19:29:27.742 --> 19:29:32.602
small, text align center.

19:29:32.602 --> 19:29:34.232
What's going on here?

19:29:34.232 --> 19:29:36.592
Well, in blue is the
language we promised,

19:29:36.592 --> 19:29:39.112
called CSS, for Cascading Style Sheets.

19:29:39.112 --> 19:29:42.232
We're not really seeing the
Cascading Style Sheet of it yet.

19:29:42.232 --> 19:29:46.192
But in blue here, notice is
another very common paradigm.

19:29:46.192 --> 19:29:48.862
It's different syntax
now, but how would you

19:29:48.862 --> 19:29:52.462
describe what you're
looking at here in blue?

19:29:52.462 --> 19:29:56.902
This is another example of what
kind of programming convention?

19:29:56.902 --> 19:29:57.790
AUDIENCE: Key value.

19:29:57.790 --> 19:30:00.082
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, it's just
more key value pairs, right?

19:30:00.082 --> 19:30:03.032
It'd be nice if the world standardized
how you write key value pairs,

19:30:03.032 --> 19:30:07.260
because we've now seen equal signs
and arrows and colons and semicolons,

19:30:07.260 --> 19:30:07.802
and all this.

19:30:07.802 --> 19:30:10.010
But it's just different
languages, different choices.

19:30:10.010 --> 19:30:13.122
The key here is font-size,
the value is large.

19:30:13.122 --> 19:30:16.922
The other key is text-align,
the colon, the value is center.

19:30:16.922 --> 19:30:20.642
The semicolon just separates
one key value pair from another.

19:30:20.642 --> 19:30:24.482
Just like in the URL, the ampersand
did, in the context of HTTP.

19:30:24.482 --> 19:30:27.392
The designers of CSS
used semicolons instead.

19:30:27.392 --> 19:30:30.149
Strictly speaking, this
semicolon isn't necessary.

19:30:30.149 --> 19:30:32.732
I tend to include it just for
symmetry, but it doesn't matter,

19:30:32.732 --> 19:30:34.322
because there's nothing after that.

19:30:34.322 --> 19:30:36.302
This is a bit of a weird example.

19:30:36.302 --> 19:30:41.342
This is the co-mingling of
CSS inside of JavaScript.

19:30:41.342 --> 19:30:46.682
So as of now, you can use the CSS
language inside of the quote marks

19:30:46.682 --> 19:30:49.442
in the value of a style attribute.

19:30:49.442 --> 19:30:52.542
We did something a little
similarly last two weeks,

19:30:52.542 --> 19:30:57.032
a week plus ago, when we included
some SQL inside of Python.

19:30:57.032 --> 19:30:59.765
So again, languages can kind
of cross barriers together.

19:30:59.765 --> 19:31:01.682
But we're going to clean
this up, because this

19:31:01.682 --> 19:31:04.599
is going to get messy quickly,
certainly for large web pages, the size

19:31:04.599 --> 19:31:07.212
of Harvard's or Yale's, or the like.

19:31:07.212 --> 19:31:09.372
So let's see what this looks like.

19:31:09.372 --> 19:31:13.142
Let me go back to my browser
window here, reload the page.

19:31:13.142 --> 19:31:14.922
And it's not that different.

19:31:14.922 --> 19:31:19.412
But it's indeed centered, and it's
indeed large, medium, and small text.

19:31:19.412 --> 19:31:20.732
And let me make one refinement.

19:31:20.732 --> 19:31:22.772
The copyright symbol
actually can be expressed,

19:31:22.772 --> 19:31:25.022
but there's no key on
my US keyboard here.

19:31:25.022 --> 19:31:30.902
I can actually magically say
ampersand hash 169 semicolon,

19:31:30.902 --> 19:31:33.167
using what's called an HTML entity.

19:31:33.167 --> 19:31:36.992
It turns out there are numeric
codes, with this weird syntax, that

19:31:36.992 --> 19:31:40.352
allow you to specify symbols that
exist in Macs and PCs and phones,

19:31:40.352 --> 19:31:42.362
but that don't exist on most keyboards.

19:31:42.362 --> 19:31:46.052
If I reload the page now, now
it's a proper copyright symbol.

19:31:46.052 --> 19:31:50.222
So minor aesthetic, but it
introduces us to these HTML entities.

19:31:50.222 --> 19:31:53.672
So even if you've never
seen CSS before, you

19:31:53.672 --> 19:31:56.522
can probably find something
kind of dumb about what

19:31:56.522 --> 19:31:58.202
I did here, like poor design.

19:31:58.202 --> 19:32:02.582
It is correct, if my goal was small,
medium, and large, bottom up, what

19:32:02.582 --> 19:32:07.082
looks like a bad design,
perhaps, even if you've never

19:32:07.082 --> 19:32:08.652
seen this language before.

19:32:08.652 --> 19:32:09.152
Yeah.

19:32:09.152 --> 19:32:09.872
AUDIENCE: Same

19:32:09.872 --> 19:32:12.122
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, I've used
the same style three times,

19:32:12.122 --> 19:32:14.942
like copy/paste, or typing the
exact same thing again and again.

19:32:14.942 --> 19:32:17.222
It has rarely been a good thing.

19:32:17.222 --> 19:32:21.692
Well, here's where we can take
advantage of the design of CSS,

19:32:21.692 --> 19:32:24.122
because it supports what
we might call inheritance,

19:32:24.122 --> 19:32:29.462
whereby children inherit the properties,
the key value pairs of their parents

19:32:29.462 --> 19:32:30.572
or ancestors.

19:32:30.572 --> 19:32:32.472
And what that means is, I can do this.

19:32:32.472 --> 19:32:34.232
Let me get rid of this text align.

19:32:34.232 --> 19:32:36.032
Let me get rid of this text align.

19:32:36.032 --> 19:32:37.232
Let me get rid of this one.

19:32:37.232 --> 19:32:40.142
I could get rid of the semicolon,
too, but I'll leave it for now.

19:32:40.142 --> 19:32:46.502
And let me add all of that style
to the parent element, the body,

19:32:46.502 --> 19:32:51.512
so that it sort of cascades down to the
header, the main, and the footer tags

19:32:51.512 --> 19:32:52.332
as well.

19:32:52.332 --> 19:32:54.452
And let me close my quotes there, too.

19:32:54.452 --> 19:32:58.382
Now, if I go back to my browser
and hit reload, nothing changes.

19:32:58.382 --> 19:33:00.242
But it's a little
better designed, right?

19:33:00.242 --> 19:33:03.602
Because if I want to change the text
alignment to maybe be right aligned,

19:33:03.602 --> 19:33:06.492
I can now reload the page, and
voila, now it's over there.

19:33:06.492 --> 19:33:08.952
I change it in one place, not
in three different places.

19:33:08.952 --> 19:33:11.822
So that would seem to be
marginally better design.

19:33:11.822 --> 19:33:14.442
And could we do this
any more differently?

19:33:14.442 --> 19:33:20.252
Well, it's not that elegant that
it's all just in line with my HTML.

19:33:20.252 --> 19:33:22.622
This generally tends to
be bad practice, where

19:33:22.622 --> 19:33:26.012
you co-mingle your HTML and your
CSS, especially since some of you

19:33:26.012 --> 19:33:28.622
might be really good at laying
out the structure of web pages

19:33:28.622 --> 19:33:31.412
and the content and the data, and you
might have a horrible sense of design

19:33:31.412 --> 19:33:32.822
or just not care about the aesthetics.

19:33:32.822 --> 19:33:34.572
You might work with a
designer, an artist,

19:33:34.572 --> 19:33:37.742
who's much better at all of
these fine tunings aesthetically.

19:33:37.742 --> 19:33:41.922
Wouldn't it be nice if you could work
on the HTML, they could work on the CSS.

19:33:41.922 --> 19:33:43.982
And you don't have to
somehow like literally

19:33:43.982 --> 19:33:46.112
edit the same lines
of code as each other.

19:33:46.112 --> 19:33:50.132
Well, just like we can move
stuff into header files in C,

19:33:50.132 --> 19:33:53.895
or packages in Python, we
can do the same in CSS.

19:33:53.895 --> 19:33:55.812
So I'm actually going
to go ahead and do this.

19:33:55.812 --> 19:33:58.382
Let me get rid of all of
these style attributes,

19:33:58.382 --> 19:34:03.842
and let me now start to practice a
convention of not co-mingling CSS

19:34:03.842 --> 19:34:05.222
with my HTML.

19:34:05.222 --> 19:34:10.052
Let me instead move it into the
head of the page, in a style tag,

19:34:10.052 --> 19:34:11.612
instead of an attribute.

19:34:11.612 --> 19:34:14.012
This is one of the rare
examples where there

19:34:14.012 --> 19:34:16.862
are attributes that have the
same names of tags as vice versa.

19:34:16.862 --> 19:34:19.442
It's not very common,
but this one does exist.

19:34:19.442 --> 19:34:22.892
Here's a slightly different syntax for
expressing the same key value pairs.

19:34:22.892 --> 19:34:26.912
If I want to apply CSS properties,
that is, key value pairs,

19:34:26.912 --> 19:34:31.202
to the header of the page, I say
header, and then I use curly braces,

19:34:31.202 --> 19:34:38.102
and inside of those I say
font-size large, text-align center.

19:34:38.102 --> 19:34:42.132
Then, if I want to apply some properties
to the main section of the page,

19:34:42.132 --> 19:34:47.072
I again do font-size, say, medium,
and then I can do text-align center.

19:34:47.072 --> 19:34:49.622
Then, lastly, on the
footer of the page, I

19:34:49.622 --> 19:34:55.022
can assign some properties like
font-size small, and then text-align

19:34:55.022 --> 19:34:57.532
center semicolon.

19:34:57.532 --> 19:35:00.442
And I don't have to do
anything more in my HTML.

19:35:00.442 --> 19:35:03.872
It all just represents
the structure of my page.

19:35:03.872 --> 19:35:06.314
But, because of this style
tag in the head of the page,

19:35:06.314 --> 19:35:08.272
the browser knows in
advance that the moment it

19:35:08.272 --> 19:35:10.852
encounters a header tag, a
main tag, or a footer tag,

19:35:10.852 --> 19:35:14.062
it should apply those
properties, those styles.

19:35:14.062 --> 19:35:17.032
If I reload the page, other
than it being recentered now,

19:35:17.032 --> 19:35:18.112
there's no other changes.

19:35:18.112 --> 19:35:21.172
All we're doing is sort of
iteratively improving the design here.

19:35:21.172 --> 19:35:24.322
But now everything's
in the top of the file.

19:35:24.322 --> 19:35:26.242
But there's still a bad design here.

19:35:26.242 --> 19:35:30.202
What could I now do
that would be smarter?

19:35:30.202 --> 19:35:32.702
Similar problem to before.

19:35:32.702 --> 19:35:33.202
Yeah.

19:35:33.202 --> 19:35:33.952
AUDIENCE: Create it.

19:35:33.952 --> 19:35:35.932
SPEAKER 1: OK, create a
new file with just the CSS.

19:35:35.932 --> 19:35:36.412
I like that.

19:35:36.412 --> 19:35:37.829
Let's go there in just one second.

19:35:37.829 --> 19:35:40.162
But even as we're here,
there's still a redundancy

19:35:40.162 --> 19:35:41.902
we can probably chip away at.

19:35:41.902 --> 19:35:45.232
Yeah, get rid of the text-align center
in three different places, which

19:35:45.232 --> 19:35:47.602
doesn't seem necessary,
and perhaps someone

19:35:47.602 --> 19:35:53.992
else, if I get rid of text-align center,
what should I add to my style tag

19:35:53.992 --> 19:35:56.992
in order to bring it back, but
apply it to everything in the page?

19:35:56.992 --> 19:35:59.822
And the page, if I scroll
down, looks like this, in HTML.

19:35:59.822 --> 19:36:00.322
Yeah.

19:36:00.322 --> 19:36:01.282
AUDIENCE: The body.

19:36:01.282 --> 19:36:02.792
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, so the body tag.

19:36:02.792 --> 19:36:04.712
So let me go ahead and say body.

19:36:04.712 --> 19:36:07.282
And then in here, put text-align center.

19:36:07.282 --> 19:36:10.282
And that, now, if I reload the
page, has no visual effect,

19:36:10.282 --> 19:36:12.142
but it's just better
design, because now I

19:36:12.142 --> 19:36:14.242
factored out that kind of commonality.

19:36:14.242 --> 19:36:16.522
And so, just to make clear
what we've been doing here,

19:36:16.522 --> 19:36:19.492
these are all, again, CSS
properties, these key value pairs.

19:36:19.492 --> 19:36:22.462
And there's different types
of ways of using them.

19:36:22.462 --> 19:36:24.797
And there's this whole taxonomy.

19:36:24.797 --> 19:36:27.922
What we've been doing thus far are what
we're going to call type selectors,

19:36:27.922 --> 19:36:30.172
where the type is the name of a tag.

19:36:30.172 --> 19:36:33.172
And so it turns out there's
other ways, though, to do this.

19:36:33.172 --> 19:36:35.212
And let's head in this direction.

19:36:35.212 --> 19:36:38.782
Let's go ahead and maybe write
our CSS slightly differently,

19:36:38.782 --> 19:36:40.282
because you know what would be nice.

19:36:40.282 --> 19:36:44.512
I bet, after today, once I start
creating other files for my home page,

19:36:44.512 --> 19:36:46.222
or John Harvard's home
page, I might want

19:36:46.222 --> 19:36:48.742
to have centered text on other pages.

19:36:48.742 --> 19:36:52.012
And I might want to have large
text or medium text or small text.

19:36:52.012 --> 19:36:55.012
It'd be nice if I could reuse
these properties again and again,

19:36:55.012 --> 19:36:57.292
and kind of create my
own library, maybe even

19:36:57.292 --> 19:36:59.812
ultimately putting it
in a separate file.

19:36:59.812 --> 19:37:00.562
So let me do this.

19:37:00.562 --> 19:37:04.042
Instead of explicitly applying
text-align center to the body,

19:37:04.042 --> 19:37:07.102
let me create a new
noun, or an adjective,

19:37:07.102 --> 19:37:09.442
rather, for myself, called centered.

19:37:09.442 --> 19:37:12.022
It has to start with a
dot, because what I'm doing

19:37:12.022 --> 19:37:14.782
is inventing my own class, so to speak.

19:37:14.782 --> 19:37:17.662
This has nothing to do with
classes in Java or Python.

19:37:17.662 --> 19:37:20.272
Class here is this aesthetic feature.

19:37:20.272 --> 19:37:22.972
And, actually, let me rename
these, to be dot large,

19:37:22.972 --> 19:37:26.002
dot medium, and dot small.

19:37:26.002 --> 19:37:29.932
What this is doing for me
is it's inventing new words,

19:37:29.932 --> 19:37:33.232
well-named words, that I
can now use in this file,

19:37:33.232 --> 19:37:36.212
or potentially in other web
pages I make, as follows.

19:37:36.212 --> 19:37:39.322
I can now say, if I want
to center the whole body,

19:37:39.322 --> 19:37:41.632
I can say class equals centered.

19:37:41.632 --> 19:37:45.172
On the header tag, I can
say class equals large.

19:37:45.172 --> 19:37:48.222
On the main tag I can
say class equals medium.

19:37:48.222 --> 19:37:50.632
On the footer tag, I can
say class equals small.

19:37:50.632 --> 19:37:53.392
But let me take this one step further.

19:37:53.392 --> 19:37:56.212
As you suggested, why
don't I go ahead now

19:37:56.212 --> 19:37:59.452
and let me actually get rid
of-- let me grab all of the CSS,

19:37:59.452 --> 19:38:01.312
copy it to my clipboard.

19:38:01.312 --> 19:38:08.362
Let me get rid of the style tag here,
and create a new file called Home.css,

19:38:08.362 --> 19:38:13.432
and let me just save all of that same
text in a separate file ending in .css,

19:38:13.432 --> 19:38:15.622
nothing else, no HTML whatsoever.

19:38:15.622 --> 19:38:18.802
But let me go back to my
Home.html page, and this

19:38:18.802 --> 19:38:21.952
is one of the most annoyingly named
tags, because it doesn't really

19:38:21.952 --> 19:38:30.052
mean what it does, Link HREF
Home.css rel equals stylesheet.

19:38:30.052 --> 19:38:33.292
So ideally we would have used the
link tag for links in web pages,

19:38:33.292 --> 19:38:36.112
but this is link in the
sort of conceptual sense.

19:38:36.112 --> 19:38:39.982
We're linking this file to this other
one, so that they work together,

19:38:39.982 --> 19:38:43.612
using this hyper-reference,
Home.css, the relationship

19:38:43.612 --> 19:38:46.282
of that file to this one
is that of stylesheet.

19:38:46.282 --> 19:38:48.472
A stylesheet is a file
containing a whole bunch

19:38:48.472 --> 19:38:52.582
of stylizations, a whole bunch
of properties, as we just did.

19:38:52.582 --> 19:38:54.622
So here, too, it's
underwhelming the effect.

19:38:54.622 --> 19:38:57.002
If I reload the page, nothing changed.

19:38:57.002 --> 19:39:01.882
But now, I not only have
a better design here,

19:39:01.882 --> 19:39:06.922
because I can now use those same classes
in my second page that I might make,

19:39:06.922 --> 19:39:11.212
my third page, my fourth page, my bio,
my resume page, whatever it is I'm

19:39:11.212 --> 19:39:15.022
making on my website here, I
can reuse those styles by just

19:39:15.022 --> 19:39:19.252
including one line of code, instead of
copying and pasting all of that style

19:39:19.252 --> 19:39:21.842
stuff into file after file after file.

19:39:21.842 --> 19:39:24.322
And heck, if the rest
of the world is really

19:39:24.322 --> 19:39:28.197
impressed by my centered class, and
my large and medium and small classes,

19:39:28.197 --> 19:39:31.072
I could bundle this up, let other
people on the internet download it,

19:39:31.072 --> 19:39:35.482
and I have my own library, my own CSS
library, that other people can use.

19:39:35.482 --> 19:39:37.882
Why should you ever invent
a centered class again,

19:39:37.882 --> 19:39:41.332
if I already did it for you,
stupid and small as this one is.

19:39:41.332 --> 19:39:43.192
But it would be nice
now to package this up

19:39:43.192 --> 19:39:47.912
in a way that's usable
by other people as well.

19:39:47.912 --> 19:39:51.352
So this is perhaps the best
design, when it comes to CSS.

19:39:51.352 --> 19:39:56.602
Use classes where you can, use
external stylesheets where you can,

19:39:56.602 --> 19:40:00.972
but don't use the style attribute
where we began, which while explicit,

19:40:00.972 --> 19:40:06.532
starts to get messy quickly,
especially for large files.

19:40:06.532 --> 19:40:08.617
All right, any questions, then, on this.

19:40:11.362 --> 19:40:13.732
No, all right, so
that's class selectors.

19:40:13.732 --> 19:40:16.132
When you specify dot
something, that means

19:40:16.132 --> 19:40:20.212
you're selecting all of the tags in the
page, that have that particular class,

19:40:20.212 --> 19:40:21.662
and applying those properties.

19:40:21.662 --> 19:40:23.829
So there's a couple of
others here, just to give you

19:40:23.829 --> 19:40:25.432
a taste now of what's possible.

19:40:25.432 --> 19:40:29.072
There's so much more that you can
actually do with HTML and CSS together.

19:40:29.072 --> 19:40:33.502
Let me go ahead and open up a few
examples that I did here in advance.

19:40:33.502 --> 19:40:35.392
Let me go ahead and open up VS Code.

19:40:35.392 --> 19:40:43.502
And let me go ahead and copy
my source eight directory.

19:40:43.502 --> 19:40:47.042
Give me one second to grab the source
eight directory for today's lectures,

19:40:47.042 --> 19:40:51.872
so that I can now go into
my browser, go into some

19:40:51.872 --> 19:40:53.822
of the pre-made examples
in source eight,

19:40:53.822 --> 19:40:57.462
and let me open up paragraphs one here.

19:40:57.462 --> 19:41:00.692
So here's something,
it's a little subtle.

19:41:00.692 --> 19:41:04.292
But does anyone notice
how this is stylized?

19:41:04.292 --> 19:41:07.142
This is just some generic
lorem ipsum text again.

19:41:07.142 --> 19:41:12.972
But what's noteworthy
stylistically, a book might do this.

19:41:12.972 --> 19:41:13.472
Yeah?

19:41:13.472 --> 19:41:14.432
AUDIENCE: They're bigger.

19:41:14.432 --> 19:41:15.932
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, the first
paragraph's a little bigger.

19:41:15.932 --> 19:41:16.322
Why?

19:41:16.322 --> 19:41:18.692
Who knows, it's just a stylistic
thing at the beginning of the chapter.

19:41:18.692 --> 19:41:19.982
The first paragraph is bigger.

19:41:19.982 --> 19:41:21.072
How did we do that?

19:41:21.072 --> 19:41:23.562
Well, we can actually explore
this in a couple of ways.

19:41:23.562 --> 19:41:26.102
One, I can obviously go into
VS Code and show you the code.

19:41:26.102 --> 19:41:29.282
But, now, that we're using Chrome and
we're using these developer tools,

19:41:29.282 --> 19:41:30.572
let's again go into them.

19:41:30.572 --> 19:41:33.662
View developer, developer
tools, and now notice,

19:41:33.662 --> 19:41:37.202
let me turn off the mobile feature,
and let me move the dock back

19:41:37.202 --> 19:41:39.752
to the bottom, just so
that it's fully wide.

19:41:39.752 --> 19:41:41.642
We looked at the Network tab before.

19:41:41.642 --> 19:41:44.072
We looked at the mobile button before.

19:41:44.072 --> 19:41:45.972
Now let me click on Elements.

19:41:45.972 --> 19:41:49.652
What's nice about the Elements tab
is you can see a pretty printed

19:41:49.652 --> 19:41:54.752
version of the web page's HTML,
nicely color-coded, syntax highlighted

19:41:54.752 --> 19:41:58.832
for you, so that you can now henceforth
learn from, look at, the source

19:41:58.832 --> 19:42:02.462
code, the HTML source code, of
any web page on the internet.

19:42:02.462 --> 19:42:05.222
Notice that my own web page
here, it's not that interesting.

19:42:05.222 --> 19:42:08.072
There's a bunch of paragraph
tags of lorem ipsum text.

19:42:08.072 --> 19:42:09.482
But notice what I did.

19:42:09.482 --> 19:42:12.452
The very first one, I gave an ID to.

19:42:12.452 --> 19:42:14.852
This is something that you,
as a web designer, can do.

19:42:14.852 --> 19:42:18.452
You can give an ID attribute
to any tag in a page,

19:42:18.452 --> 19:42:20.222
to give it a unique identifier.

19:42:20.222 --> 19:42:22.952
The onus is on you, not to
reuse the word, anywhere else.

19:42:22.952 --> 19:42:24.932
If you reuse it, you've screwed up.

19:42:24.932 --> 19:42:26.402
It's incorrect behavior.

19:42:26.402 --> 19:42:30.362
But I chose an ID of
first, just so that I

19:42:30.362 --> 19:42:34.272
have some way of referring to the
very first paragraph in this file.

19:42:34.272 --> 19:42:37.572
If I look in the head of the
page, and the style tag here,

19:42:37.572 --> 19:42:40.292
notice that I have hash first.

19:42:40.292 --> 19:42:43.412
So just as I use dot for
classes, the world of CSS

19:42:43.412 --> 19:42:46.742
uses a hash symbol to
represent IDs, unique IDs.

19:42:46.742 --> 19:42:51.722
And what this is telling the browser,
whatever element has the first ID,

19:42:51.722 --> 19:42:57.182
F-I-R-S-T, without the hash,
apply font-size larger to it.

19:42:57.182 --> 19:43:00.542
And that's why the first paragraph,
and only the first paragraph,

19:43:00.542 --> 19:43:01.952
is actually stylized.

19:43:01.952 --> 19:43:04.502
If I actually go into
VS Code now, and let

19:43:04.502 --> 19:43:06.122
me go into my source eight directory.

19:43:06.122 --> 19:43:09.002
Let me open up Paragraphs1.html.

19:43:09.002 --> 19:43:10.622
Here is the actual file.

19:43:10.622 --> 19:43:14.522
If I want to change the color of that
first paragraph to green, for instance,

19:43:14.522 --> 19:43:16.352
I can do color colon: green.

19:43:16.352 --> 19:43:19.862
Let me close the developer
tools, reload the page.

19:43:19.862 --> 19:43:22.572
And now that page is green as well.

19:43:22.572 --> 19:43:24.152
You don't have to just use words.

19:43:24.152 --> 19:43:27.272
You can use hexadecimal.

19:43:27.272 --> 19:43:31.082
What was the hex code for green in RGB?

19:43:31.082 --> 19:43:34.692
Like no red, lots of green, no blue.

19:43:34.692 --> 19:43:38.882
So you could do 00 FF 00, using
a hash, which, coincidentally,

19:43:38.882 --> 19:43:41.132
is the same symbol, but it
has nothing to do with IDs.

19:43:41.132 --> 19:43:44.792
This is just how Photoshop and
web pages represent colors.

19:43:44.792 --> 19:43:46.050
Let's go back here and reload.

19:43:46.050 --> 19:43:48.842
It's the same, although it's a
slightly different version of green.

19:43:48.842 --> 19:43:50.252
This is pure green here.

19:43:50.252 --> 19:43:56.132
If I want to change it to red, that
would be, let's see, RGB FF 00 00,

19:43:56.132 --> 19:43:58.082
and here I can go and reload.

19:43:58.082 --> 19:43:59.932
Now it's first paragraph red.

19:43:59.932 --> 19:44:01.682
This actually gets
pretty tedious quickly.

19:44:01.682 --> 19:44:04.622
Like, if you're a web designer trying
to make a website for the first time,

19:44:04.622 --> 19:44:06.962
it actually might be fun
to tinker with the website,

19:44:06.962 --> 19:44:09.422
before you open up your editor
and you start making changes

19:44:09.422 --> 19:44:10.532
and save and reload.

19:44:10.532 --> 19:44:11.772
That's just more steps.

19:44:11.772 --> 19:44:14.612
So notice what you can
do with developer tools,

19:44:14.612 --> 19:44:16.592
too, in Chrome and other browsers.

19:44:16.592 --> 19:44:19.982
When I highlight over this
paragraph, under the Elements tab,

19:44:19.982 --> 19:44:22.502
notice that, one, it
gets highlighted in blue.

19:44:22.502 --> 19:44:24.715
If I move my cursor, it
doesn't get highlighted.

19:44:24.715 --> 19:44:26.132
If I move it, it gets highlighted.

19:44:26.132 --> 19:44:29.642
So it's showing me what
that tag represents.

19:44:29.642 --> 19:44:32.342
But notice over here on
the right, you can also

19:44:32.342 --> 19:44:35.939
see all of the stylizations
of that particular element.

19:44:35.939 --> 19:44:37.022
Some of them are built-in.

19:44:37.022 --> 19:44:40.562
The italicized ones here at the
bottom means user agent stylesheet.

19:44:40.562 --> 19:44:44.772
That means this is what Google makes
all paragraphs look like by default.

19:44:44.772 --> 19:44:48.432
But in non-italicized
here, you see hash first,

19:44:48.432 --> 19:44:50.442
which is my code, that I just changed.

19:44:50.442 --> 19:44:55.982
And if I want to start tinkering with
colors, I can do like 00 00 FF Enter.

19:44:55.982 --> 19:44:57.212
I changed it to blue.

19:44:57.212 --> 19:45:02.102
But notice, if I go back to VS Code, I
didn't change my original VS Code code.

19:45:02.102 --> 19:45:04.142
This is now purely client side.

19:45:04.142 --> 19:45:05.222
And this is a key detail.

19:45:05.222 --> 19:45:08.282
When I drew that picture
earlier of the browser going,

19:45:08.282 --> 19:45:11.252
making a request to the cloud, the
server in the cloud and the response

19:45:11.252 --> 19:45:14.492
coming back, the browser,
your Mac, your PC, your phone,

19:45:14.492 --> 19:45:18.642
has a copy of all the HTML and
CSS, so you can change it here,

19:45:18.642 --> 19:45:20.665
however you actually want.

19:45:20.665 --> 19:45:22.832
And, for instance, you can
do this with any website.

19:45:22.832 --> 19:45:29.012
Let's go, say, on a field trip
here, to how about Stanford.edu.

19:45:29.012 --> 19:45:31.772
So here's Stanford's
website as of today.

19:45:31.772 --> 19:45:34.142
Let's go ahead here
and let's see, there's

19:45:34.142 --> 19:45:36.642
their admissions page,
campus life, and so forth.

19:45:36.642 --> 19:45:41.282
Let me go ahead and view developer
tools on Stanford's page,

19:45:41.282 --> 19:45:45.242
developer tools, elements,
you can see all of their HTML.

19:45:45.242 --> 19:45:48.222
And notice it's collapsed,
so here is their header.

19:45:48.222 --> 19:45:51.122
Here's their main part, and
I'm using my keyboard shortcuts

19:45:51.122 --> 19:45:54.204
to just open and close the tags,
to dive in deeper and deeper.

19:45:54.204 --> 19:45:56.162
Suppose you want to kind
of mess with Stanford,

19:45:56.162 --> 19:45:58.862
you can actually like right
click on any element of a page,

19:45:58.862 --> 19:46:03.122
or control click, Inspect, and that's
going to jump you automatically

19:46:03.122 --> 19:46:07.062
to the tag in the Elements
tab that shows you that link.

19:46:07.062 --> 19:46:11.882
And notice, if I hover over this
LI, notice Stanford's using a list,

19:46:11.882 --> 19:46:13.922
as an unordered list from left to right.

19:46:13.922 --> 19:46:16.255
But it doesn't have to be a
bulleted list top to bottom.

19:46:16.255 --> 19:46:20.202
They've used CSS to change it to be
a list, from news, events, academics,

19:46:20.202 --> 19:46:22.862
research, health care,
campus admission, about.

19:46:22.862 --> 19:46:25.692
Well, so much for
admission, that's gone.

19:46:25.692 --> 19:46:29.912
So now, if I close developer tools,
now it's gone from Stanford's website.

19:46:29.912 --> 19:46:32.972
But, of course, what have I really done.

19:46:32.972 --> 19:46:35.405
I've just like mutated
my own local copy.

19:46:35.405 --> 19:46:37.322
So this is not hacking,
even though this might

19:46:37.322 --> 19:46:38.947
be how they do it in TV and the movies.

19:46:38.947 --> 19:46:40.692
It's still there if I reload the page.

19:46:40.692 --> 19:46:44.602
But it's a wonderfully powerful way
to, one, just iterate quickly, and try

19:46:44.602 --> 19:46:46.352
different things
stylistically, figure out

19:46:46.352 --> 19:46:48.902
how you want to design
something, and two, just learn

19:46:48.902 --> 19:46:50.382
how Stanford did something.

19:46:50.382 --> 19:46:53.340
So, for instance, if I right click
or control click on admission again,

19:46:53.340 --> 19:46:56.832
go to inspect, and let
me go to the LI tag.

19:46:56.832 --> 19:47:00.212
Let me keep going up, up,
up, up, up to the UL tag.

19:47:00.212 --> 19:47:02.322
There's going to be a lot going on here.

19:47:02.322 --> 19:47:06.272
But notice, they have applied
all of these CSS properties

19:47:06.272 --> 19:47:08.702
to that particular UL tag.

19:47:08.702 --> 19:47:11.912
But notice, here, this is
how, it's something like this.

19:47:11.912 --> 19:47:15.872
And we'd have to read more to learn
how this works, list style type none,

19:47:15.872 --> 19:47:18.122
this is how they probably
got rid of the bullets.

19:47:18.122 --> 19:47:19.682
And what you can do is just tinker.

19:47:19.682 --> 19:47:21.390
Like, all right, well,
what does this do?

19:47:21.390 --> 19:47:22.772
Well, let me uncheck it.

19:47:22.772 --> 19:47:26.102
All right, didn't really change
anything, font weights, uncheck this,

19:47:26.102 --> 19:47:26.922
there we go.

19:47:26.922 --> 19:47:30.572
So now the margin is changed, the
padding around it has changed.

19:47:30.572 --> 19:47:31.802
Let's get rid of this.

19:47:31.802 --> 19:47:34.082
We can just start turning
things on and off, just

19:47:34.082 --> 19:47:35.792
to get a sense of how
the web page works.

19:47:35.792 --> 19:47:37.832
I'm not really learning
anything here so far.

19:47:37.832 --> 19:47:43.802
Let me go to the LI here for, let's
go to the admissions one here.

19:47:43.802 --> 19:47:47.432
Margin, there we go, OK.

19:47:47.432 --> 19:47:50.229
So when there's a
display property in CSS,

19:47:50.229 --> 19:47:53.312
that's apparently effectively changing
things from vertical to horizontal,

19:47:53.312 --> 19:47:56.492
if I turn that off, now Stanford's
links all look like this.

19:47:56.492 --> 19:47:57.722
And there are those bullets.

19:47:57.722 --> 19:48:00.842
So again, just default styles,
that they've somehow overridden,

19:48:00.842 --> 19:48:03.782
and a good web designer
just knows ultimately

19:48:03.782 --> 19:48:06.500
how to do these kinds of things.

19:48:06.500 --> 19:48:08.792
All right, how about a couple
of final building blocks,

19:48:08.792 --> 19:48:09.992
before we'll take one more break.

19:48:09.992 --> 19:48:12.617
And then we'll dive in with
JavaScript to manipulate this stuff

19:48:12.617 --> 19:48:13.772
programmatically.

19:48:13.772 --> 19:48:17.432
Let me go ahead and open up,
how about Paragraphs2 here.

19:48:17.432 --> 19:48:21.632
Let me close this tab, let me go
into Paragraphs2, which is premade.

19:48:21.632 --> 19:48:25.052
And this one looks
the same, except, when

19:48:25.052 --> 19:48:27.662
I go ahead and inspect
this first paragraph,

19:48:27.662 --> 19:48:29.822
notice that I was able
to get rid of the ID

19:48:29.822 --> 19:48:32.202
somehow, which is just
to say, there's many,

19:48:32.202 --> 19:48:34.562
many ways to solve
problems in HTML and CSS,

19:48:34.562 --> 19:48:36.422
just like there is in C and Python.

19:48:36.422 --> 19:48:39.422
Let me look in the head and
the style of the page now.

19:48:39.422 --> 19:48:45.812
This is what we might call
another type of selector,

19:48:45.812 --> 19:48:49.382
that allows us to specify
the paragraph tag,

19:48:49.382 --> 19:48:52.112
that itself happens to
be the first child only.

19:48:52.112 --> 19:48:56.002
So you can apply CSS to a very
specific child, namely first child.

19:48:56.002 --> 19:48:58.687
There's also syntax for last
child, if just the first one

19:48:58.687 --> 19:49:00.312
is supposed to look a little different.

19:49:00.312 --> 19:49:02.432
So, here, I've just
gotten out of the business

19:49:02.432 --> 19:49:05.282
of creating my own unique
identifier and, instead, I'm

19:49:05.282 --> 19:49:08.142
using this type of selector as well.

19:49:08.142 --> 19:49:09.282
Well, what more can we do?

19:49:09.282 --> 19:49:14.072
Let me go into another example
here, called Link1.html,

19:49:14.072 --> 19:49:17.412
and here we have a very simple
page that just says visit Harvard.

19:49:17.412 --> 19:49:19.412
But notice it's purple
by default, because we've

19:49:19.412 --> 19:49:21.272
been to Harvard.edu before.

19:49:21.272 --> 19:49:24.332
Let's see if we can't maybe
stylize Harvard's links

19:49:24.332 --> 19:49:25.812
to be a little different.

19:49:25.812 --> 19:49:30.702
Let me go into Link version
2, now, which looks like this.

19:49:30.702 --> 19:49:33.012
And now Harvard is very red.

19:49:33.012 --> 19:49:33.972
How did I do that?

19:49:33.972 --> 19:49:36.452
Well, let me right click
on it, click Inspect,

19:49:36.452 --> 19:49:37.952
and I can start to poke around.

19:49:37.952 --> 19:49:40.382
It looks like my HTML is
not at all noteworthy.

19:49:40.382 --> 19:49:43.952
It's just very simple HTML,
anchor tag with an HREF.

19:49:43.952 --> 19:49:45.692
So let's look at the style.

19:49:45.692 --> 19:49:46.629
Let me zoom out.

19:49:46.629 --> 19:49:48.462
And we can look at it
in two different ways.

19:49:48.462 --> 19:49:51.422
We can literally look at
the style, contents here,

19:49:51.422 --> 19:49:55.472
or we can look at Chrome's
pretty version of it, over here.

19:49:55.472 --> 19:49:59.522
It looks like my style
sheet, in the style tag,

19:49:59.522 --> 19:50:04.002
has changed the color to be red, and the
text decoration, which is a new thing,

19:50:04.002 --> 19:50:06.122
but it's another CSS property, to none.

19:50:06.122 --> 19:50:08.912
Notice, if I turn that
off, links on the internet

19:50:08.912 --> 19:50:10.832
are underlined by
default, which tends to be

19:50:10.832 --> 19:50:13.592
good for familiarity, for
visibility, for accessibility.

19:50:13.592 --> 19:50:18.152
But, if it's very obvious what
is text and what is a link,

19:50:18.152 --> 19:50:21.062
maybe you change text
decoration to none.

19:50:21.062 --> 19:50:25.142
But maybe, watch this, maybe the
link comes, the line comes back

19:50:25.142 --> 19:50:26.612
when you hover over it.

19:50:26.612 --> 19:50:29.162
Well, let's look at how
I did this in style.

19:50:29.162 --> 19:50:34.112
Notice that I have stylization, and I
put my curly braces on the same line

19:50:34.112 --> 19:50:36.572
here, as tends to be convention in CSS.

19:50:36.572 --> 19:50:38.582
Color is red, text decoration is none.

19:50:38.582 --> 19:50:42.962
But, whenever an anchor
tag is hovered over,

19:50:42.962 --> 19:50:47.882
you can change the text decoration
to be back to the default, underline.

19:50:47.882 --> 19:50:51.252
So, again, just little ways of playing
around with the aesthetics of the page,

19:50:51.252 --> 19:50:52.832
once you understand
that, really, there's

19:50:52.832 --> 19:50:54.092
just different types of selectors.

19:50:54.092 --> 19:50:56.132
And you might have to remind
yourself, look them up occasionally,

19:50:56.132 --> 19:50:57.362
as to what the syntax is.

19:50:57.362 --> 19:51:02.552
But it's just another way of scoping
your properties to specific tags.

19:51:02.552 --> 19:51:06.452
Let's look at version 3 of this
here, which adds Yale to the mix.

19:51:06.452 --> 19:51:11.702
If I go to Link3.html, maybe I
want to have Harvard links red,

19:51:11.702 --> 19:51:12.962
Yale links blue.

19:51:12.962 --> 19:51:14.472
How might I have done this?

19:51:14.472 --> 19:51:17.012
Well, let's right click,
and click Inspect.

19:51:17.012 --> 19:51:21.872
And here we might have two links,
with a couple of techniques,

19:51:21.872 --> 19:51:24.632
just to, again, emphasize, you can
do this so many different ways.

19:51:24.632 --> 19:51:30.062
I gave my Harvard link an ID of
Harvard, my Yale link an ID of Yale.

19:51:30.062 --> 19:51:34.802
In my CSS, if we go to the head
of the page, I then did this.

19:51:34.802 --> 19:51:37.442
The tag with the Harvard ID, a.k.a.

19:51:37.442 --> 19:51:41.672
#Harvard, should be red,
#Yale should be blue,

19:51:41.672 --> 19:51:45.572
and then any anchor tag should
have no text decoration,

19:51:45.572 --> 19:51:48.722
unless you hover over it, at which
point it should be underlined.

19:51:48.722 --> 19:51:52.232
And so, if I hover over Harvard,
it's red underlined, Yale,

19:51:52.232 --> 19:51:53.262
it's blue underlined.

19:51:53.262 --> 19:51:56.262
If I want to get rid of the IDs, I
can do this a slightly different way.

19:51:56.262 --> 19:51:57.542
Let me go into Link4.

19:51:57.542 --> 19:52:00.602
Same effect, but notice,
I got rid of the IDs now.

19:52:00.602 --> 19:52:02.282
How else can I express myself?

19:52:02.282 --> 19:52:03.992
Well, let's look at the CSS here.

19:52:03.992 --> 19:52:06.512
The anchor tag has no text
decoration by default,

19:52:06.512 --> 19:52:08.052
unless you're hovering over it.

19:52:08.052 --> 19:52:09.462
And this is kind of cool.

19:52:09.462 --> 19:52:11.882
This is what we would
call, on our list here,

19:52:11.882 --> 19:52:16.802
an attribute selector, where you
select tags using CSS notation,

19:52:16.802 --> 19:52:18.372
based on an attribute.

19:52:18.372 --> 19:52:21.692
So this is saying, go ahead
and find any anchor tag

19:52:21.692 --> 19:52:26.342
who's HREF value happens to
equal this URL, and make it red.

19:52:26.342 --> 19:52:28.327
Do the same for Yale, and make it blue.

19:52:28.327 --> 19:52:31.452
Now, this might not be ideal, because
if there's something after the slash,

19:52:31.452 --> 19:52:33.602
these equal signs don't
work, because if it's

19:52:33.602 --> 19:52:37.202
a different Harvard or different Yale
link, this is a little too precise.

19:52:37.202 --> 19:52:40.412
So let me look at version
5 here, of Link.html.

19:52:40.412 --> 19:52:43.952
Look at this style, and I
did this a little smarter.

19:52:43.952 --> 19:52:44.942
This is new syntax.

19:52:44.942 --> 19:52:46.952
And, again, just the kind
of thing you look up.

19:52:46.952 --> 19:52:54.542
Star equals means, change any anchor
tag who's HREF contains anywhere in it

19:52:54.542 --> 19:52:59.552
Harvard.edu to red, and do the same
thing for Yale, based on star equals.

19:52:59.552 --> 19:53:01.142
So star here connotes wildcard.

19:53:01.142 --> 19:53:04.652
So search for Harvard.edu or
Yale.edu anywhere in the HREF,

19:53:04.652 --> 19:53:07.652
and if it's there, colorize the link.

19:53:07.652 --> 19:53:11.732
And, again, we could do this all
day long, with diminishing returns,

19:53:11.732 --> 19:53:15.012
to actually achieve the same kind
of stylizations in different ways.

19:53:15.012 --> 19:53:17.115
And as projects just
get larger and larger,

19:53:17.115 --> 19:53:19.032
you just have more and
more decisions to make.

19:53:19.032 --> 19:53:21.752
And so you have certain
conventions you start to adopt.

19:53:21.752 --> 19:53:25.172
And, indeed, if I may,
you have the introduction

19:53:25.172 --> 19:53:28.142
of what are called
frameworks, ultimately.

19:53:28.142 --> 19:53:30.512
If you're a full-time
web developer, or you're

19:53:30.512 --> 19:53:33.890
working for a company doing the same,
you might have internal conventions

19:53:33.890 --> 19:53:34.682
that you adhere to.

19:53:34.682 --> 19:53:38.372
For instance, the company might say,
always use classes, don't use IDs.

19:53:38.372 --> 19:53:41.222
Or always use attribute
selectors, or don't use this.

19:53:41.222 --> 19:53:44.222
And it wouldn't be necessarily
as draconian as that.

19:53:44.222 --> 19:53:46.472
But they might have a
style guide of sorts.

19:53:46.472 --> 19:53:49.442
But, what many people, and
many companies, do nowadays,

19:53:49.442 --> 19:53:53.402
is they do not come up with all
of their own CSS properties.

19:53:53.402 --> 19:53:57.242
They start with something off the shelf,
a framework, typically a free and open

19:53:57.242 --> 19:54:00.782
source framework, that just gives
them a lot of pretty stylizations

19:54:00.782 --> 19:54:04.172
for free, just by using
a third party library.

19:54:04.172 --> 19:54:05.912
And one of the most
popular ones nowadays

19:54:05.912 --> 19:54:07.862
is something called
Bootstrap, that CS50 uses

19:54:07.862 --> 19:54:11.162
on all of its websites,
super-popular in industry as well.

19:54:11.162 --> 19:54:17.162
It's at getbootstrap.com, and this
is just to give you a taste of it,

19:54:17.162 --> 19:54:20.612
a website that documents
the library that they offer.

19:54:20.612 --> 19:54:24.752
And there's so much documentation here,
but let me just go to things like,

19:54:24.752 --> 19:54:26.522
how about components.

19:54:26.522 --> 19:54:30.122
It just gives you, out of the
box, the CSS with which you

19:54:30.122 --> 19:54:31.322
can create little alerts.

19:54:31.322 --> 19:54:33.392
If you've ever noticed
on CS50's website,

19:54:33.392 --> 19:54:36.002
little colorful warnings at
the top of the page, or call

19:54:36.002 --> 19:54:37.862
outs, to draw your attention to things.

19:54:37.862 --> 19:54:38.852
How did we do that?

19:54:38.852 --> 19:54:41.492
It's probably a paragraph
tag or a div tag,

19:54:41.492 --> 19:54:43.112
and maybe we changed the font color.

19:54:43.112 --> 19:54:44.732
We changed the background color.

19:54:44.732 --> 19:54:47.282
Or it's a lot of stuff we could
absolutely do from scratch,

19:54:47.282 --> 19:54:49.747
but, you know what,
why would we reinvent

19:54:49.747 --> 19:54:51.372
the wheel if we can just use Bootstrap.

19:54:51.372 --> 19:54:53.252
So, for instance, let
me just scroll down.

19:54:53.252 --> 19:54:57.852
If you've ever seen on CS50's website
a yellow warning alert like this,

19:54:57.852 --> 19:55:00.542
let me just zoom in on this.

19:55:00.542 --> 19:55:03.272
We are just using HTML like this.

19:55:03.272 --> 19:55:06.242
We're using a div tag, which,
again, is an invisible division,

19:55:06.242 --> 19:55:07.812
a rectangular region of the page.

19:55:07.812 --> 19:55:12.812
But we're using classes called alert
and another class called alert warning.

19:55:12.812 --> 19:55:17.462
Those are classes that the
folks at Bootstrap invented.

19:55:17.462 --> 19:55:19.982
They associated certain
text colors and background

19:55:19.982 --> 19:55:23.042
colors and padding and margin
and like other aesthetics with,

19:55:23.042 --> 19:55:25.412
so all we have to do
is use those classes.

19:55:25.412 --> 19:55:28.442
Role equals alert, just makes clear
to like a screen reader that this

19:55:28.442 --> 19:55:30.512
is an alert, that should
probably be recited,

19:55:30.512 --> 19:55:33.602
and whatever's in between
the open tag and close tag,

19:55:33.602 --> 19:55:35.312
is what the human would see.

19:55:35.312 --> 19:55:37.262
How do you use something like Bootstrap?

19:55:37.262 --> 19:55:39.032
Well, you just read the documentation.

19:55:39.032 --> 19:55:44.272
Under Getting Started, there is a
link tag you copy/paste into your own.

19:55:44.272 --> 19:55:45.232
So let me do this.

19:55:45.232 --> 19:55:49.312
So in Table.html, we had code like this.

19:55:49.312 --> 19:55:52.182
Let me actually read Bootstrap's
documentation really fast.

19:55:52.182 --> 19:55:53.426
And they tell me...

19:55:53.426 --> 19:55:55.542
copy/paste this code.

19:55:55.542 --> 19:55:57.552
I'm going to put this
into the head of my page.

19:55:57.552 --> 19:55:59.982
And it's quite long, but
notice, it's a link tag,

19:55:59.982 --> 19:56:03.852
which I used earlier for my
own CSS file, the HREF of which

19:56:03.852 --> 19:56:06.492
is this CDN link, content
delivery network, that's

19:56:06.492 --> 19:56:09.732
referring to a specific version of
Bootstrap that's available on this day.

19:56:09.732 --> 19:56:13.752
And the file that I'm including
is called Bootstrap.min.css.

19:56:13.752 --> 19:56:17.292
This is an actual file I
can visit with my browser.

19:56:17.292 --> 19:56:20.622
If I open this in a separate
tab, this is the CSS

19:56:20.622 --> 19:56:23.532
that Bootstrap has made
freely available to us.

19:56:23.532 --> 19:56:25.222
Crazy long, no white space.

19:56:25.222 --> 19:56:27.222
That's because it's been
minimized, just to not

19:56:27.222 --> 19:56:29.772
waste space by adding lots
of white space and comments.

19:56:29.772 --> 19:56:33.222
But this contains a whole lot,
hundreds, of CSS properties

19:56:33.222 --> 19:56:36.852
that we can reuse, thanks to
classes that they invented.

19:56:36.852 --> 19:56:40.285
If I want to use some JavaScript
code, I can also copy this script tag.

19:56:40.285 --> 19:56:41.952
But we'll come back to that before long.

19:56:41.952 --> 19:56:45.522
Let me now just make a couple
of tweaks to this table.

19:56:45.522 --> 19:56:48.552
If I go into my browser
from before, this

19:56:48.552 --> 19:56:51.462
is what it looked like previously,
where name and number were

19:56:51.462 --> 19:56:54.087
bold, but centered, and then
Carter and David were on the left,

19:56:54.087 --> 19:56:55.504
and the numbers were to the right.

19:56:55.504 --> 19:56:56.202
It's fine.

19:56:56.202 --> 19:56:59.662
It's not that pretty, but it'd be nice
if it were a little prettier than that.

19:56:59.662 --> 19:57:03.102
So if we add Bootstrap into it,
notice one thing happens first,

19:57:03.102 --> 19:57:04.872
when I reload the page.

19:57:04.872 --> 19:57:08.262
No longer are Chrome's
default styles used.

19:57:08.262 --> 19:57:10.482
Now Bootstrap's default
styles are used, which

19:57:10.482 --> 19:57:14.112
is a way of enforcing similarity
across Chrome, Edge, Firefox,

19:57:14.112 --> 19:57:15.462
Safari, and others.

19:57:15.462 --> 19:57:18.042
Notice it went from a
serif font to a sans serif

19:57:18.042 --> 19:57:19.792
