20:05:54.982 --> 20:05:57.952
without even touching raw CSS myself.

20:05:57.952 --> 20:06:00.502
And now here's the value,
then, of a framework.

20:06:00.502 --> 20:06:02.812
You can just start to use
off the shelf functionality

20:06:02.812 --> 20:06:04.622
that someone else created for you.

20:06:04.622 --> 20:06:07.102
But if you want to make
refinements, you don't really

20:06:07.102 --> 20:06:09.832
like the shade of blue that
Bootstrap chose, or the gray button,

20:06:09.832 --> 20:06:11.749
or you want to curve
things a bit more, that's

20:06:11.749 --> 20:06:14.122
where you can create
your own CSS file, and do

20:06:14.122 --> 20:06:16.372
the last mile, sort
of fine tuning things.

20:06:16.372 --> 20:06:17.962
And that tends to be best practice.

20:06:17.962 --> 20:06:21.052
Stand on the shoulders of others as
much as you can, using libraries.

20:06:21.052 --> 20:06:23.552
And then if you really don't
like what the library is doing,

20:06:23.552 --> 20:06:26.302
then use your own skills and
understanding of HTML and CSS

20:06:26.302 --> 20:06:29.552
to refine things a bit further.

20:06:29.552 --> 20:06:33.262
But still, after all of that, all of
these examples we've done thus far

20:06:33.262 --> 20:06:35.752
are still static, other
than the Google one,

20:06:35.752 --> 20:06:37.672
which searches on the real Google.com.

20:06:37.672 --> 20:06:39.532
Let's take a final 5
minute break and we'll

20:06:39.532 --> 20:06:43.282
give you a sense of what we can next
do, next week onward, with JavaScript.

20:06:43.282 --> 20:06:44.822
See you in five.

20:06:44.822 --> 20:06:48.382
All right, so I think
it's fair to say, we're

20:06:48.382 --> 20:06:50.820
about to see our very last language.

20:06:50.820 --> 20:06:52.612
Next week and final
projects are ultimately

20:06:52.612 --> 20:06:55.102
going to be about
synthesizing so many of these.

20:06:55.102 --> 20:06:58.042
Thankfully, this language called
JavaScript is quite similar

20:06:58.042 --> 20:07:00.230
syntactically to both C and Python.

20:07:00.230 --> 20:07:03.022
And, indeed, if you can imagine
doing something in either of those,

20:07:03.022 --> 20:07:05.392
you can probably do it in
some form in JavaScript.

20:07:05.392 --> 20:07:07.742
The most fundamental
difference today, though,

20:07:07.742 --> 20:07:11.122
is that when you have written C
code and Python code thus far,

20:07:11.122 --> 20:07:12.379
you've done it on the server.

20:07:12.379 --> 20:07:14.462
You've done it in the
terminal window environment.

20:07:14.462 --> 20:07:17.812
And when you run the code, it's
running in the cloud on the server.

20:07:17.812 --> 20:07:20.122
The difference now today
with JavaScript is,

20:07:20.122 --> 20:07:23.362
even though you're going to write
it in the cloud using VS Code,

20:07:23.362 --> 20:07:27.982
recall that, when a browser gets
the page containing this code,

20:07:27.982 --> 20:07:32.012
it's going to get a copy of the HTML,
the CSS, and the JavaScript code.

20:07:32.012 --> 20:07:37.012
So JavaScript, that we see today, is
all going to be executed in the browser,

20:07:37.012 --> 20:07:40.732
on users' own Macs, PCs, and
phones, not in the server.

20:07:40.732 --> 20:07:44.062
JavaScript can be used on the server,
using an environment called Node.js.

20:07:44.062 --> 20:07:47.752
It's an alternative to Python or
Ruby or Java or other languages.

20:07:47.752 --> 20:07:51.362
We are using it today client
side, which is a key difference.

20:07:51.362 --> 20:07:53.932
So in Scratch, let's
do this one last time.

20:07:53.932 --> 20:07:56.992
If you wanted to create a variable
in Scratch, set encounter equal to 0.

20:07:56.992 --> 20:07:59.332
In JavaScript, it's
going to look like this.

20:07:59.332 --> 20:08:02.242
You don't specify the type,
but you do use the keyword let,

20:08:02.242 --> 20:08:07.582
and there's a few others as well, that
say let counter equal 0 semicolon.

20:08:07.582 --> 20:08:11.632
If you want to increment that
variable by one, you in JavaScript

20:08:11.632 --> 20:08:14.932
could say something like,
counter equals counter plus 1,

20:08:14.932 --> 20:08:17.512
or you can do it more
succinctly, with plus equals,

20:08:17.512 --> 20:08:20.092
or the plus plus is back in JavaScript.

20:08:20.092 --> 20:08:23.122
You can now say counter
plus plus semicolon again.

20:08:23.122 --> 20:08:26.122
In Scratch, if you wanted to
do a conditional like this,

20:08:26.122 --> 20:08:30.742
asking if x less than y, it looks
pretty much like C. The parentheses are,

20:08:30.742 --> 20:08:32.362
unfortunately, back.

20:08:32.362 --> 20:08:35.692
The curly braces here are back, if you
have multiple statements in particular.

20:08:35.692 --> 20:08:39.952
But, syntactically, it's pretty much
the same as it was for if, for if else,

20:08:39.952 --> 20:08:42.502
and even for it's else if else.

20:08:42.502 --> 20:08:45.622
Unlike Python, it's two
words again, else if.

20:08:45.622 --> 20:08:49.347
So quite, quite like C,
nothing new beyond that.

20:08:49.347 --> 20:08:52.222
If you want to do something forever
in Scratch, you'd use this block.

20:08:52.222 --> 20:08:55.732
In JavaScript, you can do it a few
ways, similar to Python, similar to C,

20:08:55.732 --> 20:08:57.172
you just say while true.

20:08:57.172 --> 20:09:00.652
In JavaScript, Booleans are
lowercase again, just like in C.

20:09:00.652 --> 20:09:02.074
So it's lowercase true.

20:09:02.074 --> 20:09:04.282
If you want to do something
a finite number of times,

20:09:04.282 --> 20:09:08.042
like repeat three times,
looks almost like C as well.

20:09:08.042 --> 20:09:12.452
The only difference, really, is using
the word let here, instead of INT.

20:09:12.452 --> 20:09:15.142
And, again, you'll use let to
create a string, or an INT,

20:09:15.142 --> 20:09:17.362
or any other type of
variable in JavaScript.

20:09:17.362 --> 20:09:21.022
The browser will figure out
what type you mean from context.

20:09:21.022 --> 20:09:24.232
In C we would have said INT instead.

20:09:24.232 --> 20:09:27.952
Ultimately, this language, and that's
it for our tour of JavaScript syntax.

20:09:27.952 --> 20:09:30.502
There's bunches of other
features, but syntactically it's

20:09:30.502 --> 20:09:32.842
going to be that accessible,
relatively speaking.

20:09:32.842 --> 20:09:35.572
The power of JavaScript
running in the user's browser

20:09:35.572 --> 20:09:39.232
is going to be that you can
change this thing in memory.

20:09:39.232 --> 20:09:43.432
Think about most any website, that's
at all interesting today, that you use.

20:09:43.432 --> 20:09:45.772
It's typically very
interactive and dynamic.

20:09:45.772 --> 20:09:49.582
If you're sitting in front of Gmail on
a laptop or desktop with the browser tab

20:09:49.582 --> 20:09:52.142
open, and someone sends you
an email, all of a sudden,

20:09:52.142 --> 20:09:55.402
another row appears in your
inbox, another row, another row.

20:09:55.402 --> 20:09:56.722
How is that implemented?

20:09:56.722 --> 20:09:58.712
Honestly, it could be an HTML table.

20:09:58.712 --> 20:10:00.742
Maybe it's a bunch of
divs top to bottom.

20:10:00.742 --> 20:10:04.072
The point, though, is, you don't
have to hit Command R or Control

20:10:04.072 --> 20:10:06.532
R to reload the page to see more email.

20:10:06.532 --> 20:10:10.072
It automatically appears
every few seconds or minutes.

20:10:10.072 --> 20:10:11.752
How is that working?

20:10:11.752 --> 20:10:14.812
When you visit Gmail.com,
you are downloading not just

20:10:14.812 --> 20:10:18.322
HTML and CSS with your
initial inbox, presumably.

20:10:18.322 --> 20:10:20.392
You're downloading some
JavaScript code, that

20:10:20.392 --> 20:10:24.592
is designed to keep talking every
second, every 10 seconds or something,

20:10:24.592 --> 20:10:28.282
to Gmail servers, and they,
then, are using their code

20:10:28.282 --> 20:10:31.492
to add another element, another
element, another element,

20:10:31.492 --> 20:10:36.772
to the existing DOM, document object
model, which is the fancy term for tree

20:10:36.772 --> 20:10:40.852
in memory that represents HTML,
so that the web page can continue

20:10:40.852 --> 20:10:42.052
to update in real time.

20:10:42.052 --> 20:10:43.232
Google Maps, same thing.

20:10:43.232 --> 20:10:46.102
If you click and drag and drag
and drag, your browser did not

20:10:46.102 --> 20:10:49.352
download the entire world to
your Mac or PC by default.

20:10:49.352 --> 20:10:52.492
It only downloaded what's in your
viewport, the rectangular region.

20:10:52.492 --> 20:10:55.492
But when you click and drag, it's
going to get some more tiles up there,

20:10:55.492 --> 20:10:58.702
some more images, some more
images, as you keep dragging, using

20:10:58.702 --> 20:11:01.312
JavaScript, again, behind the scenes.

20:11:01.312 --> 20:11:04.672
So let's actually use JavaScript
to start interacting with pages.

20:11:04.672 --> 20:11:05.980
How can we do this?

20:11:05.980 --> 20:11:08.272
We can put the JavaScript
code in the head of the page,

20:11:08.272 --> 20:11:12.182
in the body of the page, or even
factor it out to a separate file.

20:11:12.182 --> 20:11:13.132
So let's take a look.

20:11:13.132 --> 20:11:17.602
Here is a new version of
Hello.html, that, during the break,

20:11:17.602 --> 20:11:20.782
I just added a form to, because it'd
be nice if this page didn't just

20:11:20.782 --> 20:11:24.472
say Hello, title, Hello, body, it said,
Hello, David, Hello, Carter, Hello,

20:11:24.472 --> 20:11:25.672
whoever uses it.

20:11:25.672 --> 20:11:28.672
I've got a form that I borrowed
from some of our earlier code,

20:11:28.672 --> 20:11:34.882
and that form has an input whose ID is
name, that also has a submit button.

20:11:34.882 --> 20:11:36.572
But there's no code in this yet.

20:11:36.572 --> 20:11:39.602
So let's add a little bit of
JavaScript code as follows.

20:11:39.602 --> 20:11:43.102
Suppose that, when this form is
submitted, I want to greet the user.

20:11:43.102 --> 20:11:44.282
How can I do that?

20:11:44.282 --> 20:11:47.302
Well, let's do it the
somewhat messy way first.

20:11:47.302 --> 20:11:51.352
I can add an attribute called
on submit to the form element,

20:11:51.352 --> 20:11:56.452
and I can say on submit, call the
function called greet, close quotes.

20:11:56.452 --> 20:11:58.552
Unfortunately, this
function doesn't yet exist.

20:11:58.552 --> 20:11:59.769
But I can make it exist.

20:11:59.769 --> 20:12:01.102
But there's another detail here.

20:12:01.102 --> 20:12:04.852
When the user clicks submit, normally
forms get submitted to the server.

20:12:04.852 --> 20:12:06.382
I don't want to do that today.

20:12:06.382 --> 20:12:10.372
I want to just submit the form to
the browsers, keep on the same page,

20:12:10.372 --> 20:12:13.142
and just print to the screen,
Hello, David, or so forth.

20:12:13.142 --> 20:12:15.862
So I'm also going to go
ahead and say, return false.

20:12:15.862 --> 20:12:20.302
And this is a JavaScript way of telling
the browser, even when the user tries

20:12:20.302 --> 20:12:21.832
to submit the form, return false.

20:12:21.832 --> 20:12:24.172
Like, no, don't let them
actually submit the form.

20:12:24.172 --> 20:12:26.362
But do call this function called greet.

20:12:26.362 --> 20:12:30.952
In the head of my page, I'm going to add
a script tag, wherein the language is

20:12:30.952 --> 20:12:33.682
implicitly JavaScript,
and has no relationship,

20:12:33.682 --> 20:12:37.402
for those of you who took APCS with
Java, just a similarly named language,

20:12:37.402 --> 20:12:41.542
but no relation, I'm going to
name a function called Greet.

20:12:41.542 --> 20:12:44.632
Apparently in JavaScript, the
way you create a function is you

20:12:44.632 --> 20:12:47.362
literally say the word
function instead of Def.

20:12:47.362 --> 20:12:49.702
You don't specify a return type.

20:12:49.702 --> 20:12:54.382
And in this function, I could do
something like this, alert quote

20:12:54.382 --> 20:12:57.802
unquote, how about, Hello, there.

20:12:57.802 --> 20:13:01.502
Initially I'm going to keep it simple,
using a built-in function called alert,

20:13:01.502 --> 20:13:03.032
which is not a good user interface.

20:13:03.032 --> 20:13:04.407
There are better ways to do this.

20:13:04.407 --> 20:13:06.382
But we're doing something simple first.

20:13:06.382 --> 20:13:09.232
Let me now go ahead and
load this page again.

20:13:09.232 --> 20:13:12.562
It still looks as simple as before,
with just a simple text box.

20:13:12.562 --> 20:13:13.942
I'll zoom in to make it bigger.

20:13:13.942 --> 20:13:15.472
I'm going to type my name,
but I think it's going

20:13:15.472 --> 20:13:17.242
to be ignored when I click Submit.

20:13:17.242 --> 20:13:18.832
It just says, Hello, there.

20:13:18.832 --> 20:13:21.112
And this is, again, this
is an ugly user interface.

20:13:21.112 --> 20:13:24.382
It literally says the whole
code space URL of the web page

20:13:24.382 --> 20:13:25.582
is saying this to you.

20:13:25.582 --> 20:13:29.457
It's really just meant for simple
interactions like this, for now.

20:13:29.457 --> 20:13:31.582
All right, let's have it
say Hello, David, somehow.

20:13:31.582 --> 20:13:33.032
Well, how can I do this?

20:13:33.032 --> 20:13:37.222
Well, if this element on the
page was given by me a unique ID,

20:13:37.222 --> 20:13:41.332
it'd be nice if, just like in CSS, I
can go grab the value of that text box,

20:13:41.332 --> 20:13:42.472
using code.

20:13:42.472 --> 20:13:43.972
And I actually can.

20:13:43.972 --> 20:13:45.572
Let me go ahead and do this.

20:13:45.572 --> 20:13:49.462
Let me store, in a variable
called name, the result

20:13:49.462 --> 20:13:54.382
of calling a special function
called document.queryselector.

20:13:54.382 --> 20:13:57.682
This query selector function
is JavaScript's version

20:13:57.682 --> 20:14:00.142
of what we were doing
in CSS, to select nodes,

20:14:00.142 --> 20:14:02.962
using hashes or dots or other syntax.

20:14:02.962 --> 20:14:04.442
It's the same syntax.

20:14:04.442 --> 20:14:09.392
So if I want to select the
element whose unique ID is name,

20:14:09.392 --> 20:14:12.712
I can literally just pass, in
single or double quotes, hash name,

20:14:12.712 --> 20:14:14.212
just like in CSS.

20:14:14.212 --> 20:14:17.032
That gives me the actual
node from the tree.

20:14:17.032 --> 20:14:21.202
It gives me one of these rectangles
from the DOM, the document object model.

20:14:21.202 --> 20:14:24.352
If I actually want to get at
the specific value therein,

20:14:24.352 --> 20:14:27.302
I need to go one step
further and say .value.

20:14:27.302 --> 20:14:29.302
So, similar in spirit
to Python, where we

20:14:29.302 --> 20:14:32.212
saw a lot of dot notation, where
you can go inside an object,

20:14:32.212 --> 20:14:34.192
inside of an object,
that's what's going on.

20:14:34.192 --> 20:14:38.092
Long story short, in JavaScript,
there is a special global variable

20:14:38.092 --> 20:14:41.392
called document, that lets you just do
stuff with the document, the web page

20:14:41.392 --> 20:14:42.022
itself.

20:14:42.022 --> 20:14:44.362
One of those functions
is called query selector.

20:14:44.362 --> 20:14:47.782
That function returns to you
whatever it is you're selecting.

20:14:47.782 --> 20:14:51.032
And dot value means go
inside of that rectangle,

20:14:51.032 --> 20:14:54.272
and grab the actual text
that the human typed in.

20:14:54.272 --> 20:14:58.252
So if I want to now say,
Hello, to that person,

20:14:58.252 --> 20:15:01.012
the syntax is a little
different from C and Python.

20:15:01.012 --> 20:15:03.742
I can use concatenation, which
actually does exist in Python,

20:15:03.742 --> 20:15:04.942
but we didn't use it much.

20:15:04.942 --> 20:15:10.372
I can go ahead and say hello,
quote unquote "Hello," plus name.

20:15:10.372 --> 20:15:13.132
All right, now, if I go
back to the browser window,

20:15:13.132 --> 20:15:16.912
reload the page, to get the latest
version of the code, type in David,

20:15:16.912 --> 20:15:20.032
and click Submit, now
I see, Hello, David.

20:15:20.032 --> 20:15:23.422
Not the best website, but
it does demonstrate how

20:15:23.422 --> 20:15:25.832
I can start to interact with the page.

20:15:25.832 --> 20:15:29.212
But let me stipulate that
this co-mingling of languages

20:15:29.212 --> 20:15:30.662
is never a good thing.

20:15:30.662 --> 20:15:33.172
It's fine to use
classes, but using style

20:15:33.172 --> 20:15:35.345
equals quote unquote and
a whole bunch of CSS,

20:15:35.345 --> 20:15:38.512
that was not going to scale well, once
you have lots and lots of properties.

20:15:38.512 --> 20:15:40.672
Same here, once you
have more and more code,

20:15:40.672 --> 20:15:44.462
you don't want to just put your code
inside of this on submit handler.

20:15:44.462 --> 20:15:45.752
So there's a better way.

20:15:45.752 --> 20:15:48.302
Let's get rid of that
on summit attribute,

20:15:48.302 --> 20:15:50.132
and literally never use it again.

20:15:50.132 --> 20:15:52.412
That was for demonstration's sake only.

20:15:52.412 --> 20:15:53.802
And let's do this.

20:15:53.802 --> 20:15:57.662
Let me move the script tag,
actually, just below the form,

20:15:57.662 --> 20:16:01.592
but still inside the body, so
that the script tag exists only

20:16:01.592 --> 20:16:04.482
after the form tag exists, logically.

20:16:04.482 --> 20:16:08.412
Just like in Python, your code is
read top to bottom, left to right.

20:16:08.412 --> 20:16:10.752
And let me now do this.

20:16:10.752 --> 20:16:13.592
Let me define this function
called Greet, and then

20:16:13.592 --> 20:16:18.902
let me do this, document.queryselector,
let me select the form on the page.

20:16:18.902 --> 20:16:20.232
It doesn't have a unique ID.

20:16:20.232 --> 20:16:21.062
It doesn't need to.

20:16:21.062 --> 20:16:24.302
I can just reference it by name, form,
because there's only one of them.

20:16:24.302 --> 20:16:28.412
And let me call this special
function, add event listener.

20:16:28.412 --> 20:16:31.472
This is a function that
listens for events.

20:16:31.472 --> 20:16:34.202
Now this is actually a term
of art within programming.

20:16:34.202 --> 20:16:37.172
Many different languages
are governed by events.

20:16:37.172 --> 20:16:40.652
And pretty much any user interface is
governed by events, especially phones.

20:16:40.652 --> 20:16:45.265
On phones, you have touches, and you
have drags, and you have long press,

20:16:45.265 --> 20:16:47.432
and you have pinch, and all
of these other gestures.

20:16:47.432 --> 20:16:49.712
On your Mac or PC you
have click, you have drag,

20:16:49.712 --> 20:16:52.982
you have key down, key up, as
you're moving your hands up and down

20:16:52.982 --> 20:16:53.862
on the keyboard.

20:16:53.862 --> 20:16:57.122
This is a non-exhaustive
list of all of the events

20:16:57.122 --> 20:16:59.919
that you can listen for in the
context of web programming.

20:16:59.919 --> 20:17:02.252
And this might be a throwback
to Scratch, where, recall,

20:17:02.252 --> 20:17:04.412
Scratch let you broadcast events.

20:17:04.412 --> 20:17:07.742
And we had the two puppets sort of
talking to one another via Events.

20:17:07.742 --> 20:17:11.612
In the world of web programming, game
programming, any human physical device

20:17:11.612 --> 20:17:14.132
these days, they're
just governed by events.

20:17:14.132 --> 20:17:16.962
And you write code that listens
for these events happening.

20:17:16.962 --> 20:17:18.302
So what do I want to listen for?

20:17:18.302 --> 20:17:21.462
Well, I want to add an event
listener for the Submit event.

20:17:21.462 --> 20:17:26.772
And when that happens, I want to
call the Greet function, like this.

20:17:26.772 --> 20:17:28.802
So this is kind of interesting.

20:17:28.802 --> 20:17:32.432
Thank you, I have my Greet
function as before, no changes.

20:17:32.432 --> 20:17:34.952
But I'm adding one
line of code down here.

20:17:34.952 --> 20:17:38.072
I'm telling the browser to
use document.queryselector

20:17:38.072 --> 20:17:39.332
to select the form.

20:17:39.332 --> 20:17:42.552
Then I'm adding an event listener,
specifically for the Submit event.

20:17:42.552 --> 20:17:44.672
And when that happens, I call Greet.

20:17:44.672 --> 20:17:47.462
Notice I am not using
parentheses after Greet.

20:17:47.462 --> 20:17:49.382
I don't want to call Greet right away.

20:17:49.382 --> 20:17:55.892
I want to tell the browser to call
Greet, when it hears this Submit event.

20:17:55.892 --> 20:18:03.992
Now let me go ahead and deliberately,
I think, trip over something

20:18:03.992 --> 20:18:08.012
here, let me type in my name,
David, submit, and there we go.

20:18:08.012 --> 20:18:09.362
All right, Hello, David.

20:18:09.362 --> 20:18:13.232
All right, but let's now make
this slightly better designed.

20:18:13.232 --> 20:18:16.572
Right now, I'm defining a
function Greet, which is fine.

20:18:16.572 --> 20:18:18.552
But I'm only using it in one place.

20:18:18.552 --> 20:18:21.122
And you might recall, we
stumbled on this in Python,

20:18:21.122 --> 20:18:24.212
where I was like, why are we creating
a special function called get value

20:18:24.212 --> 20:18:26.462
when we're only using
it like one line later?

20:18:26.462 --> 20:18:29.842
And we introduced what type of
function in Python the other day?

20:18:29.842 --> 20:18:30.602
AUDIENCE: Lambda.

20:18:30.602 --> 20:18:33.222
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, so lambda
functions, anonymous functions.

20:18:33.222 --> 20:18:35.612
You can actually do this
in JavaScript as well.

20:18:35.612 --> 20:18:40.002
If I want to define a function all
at once, I can actually do this.

20:18:40.002 --> 20:18:43.662
Let me cut this onto my
clipboard, paste it over here.

20:18:43.662 --> 20:18:45.452
Let me fix all of the alignment.

20:18:45.452 --> 20:18:46.982
Let me get rid of the name.

20:18:46.982 --> 20:18:50.672
And I can actually, now, do this.

20:18:50.672 --> 20:18:52.352
The syntax is a little weird.

20:18:52.352 --> 20:18:56.022
But using now just these four
lines of code, I can do this.

20:18:56.022 --> 20:18:59.532
I can tell the browser to add an
event listener for the Submit event.

20:18:59.532 --> 20:19:03.331
And then when it hears that, call
this function that has no name.

20:19:03.331 --> 20:19:06.151
And unlike Python, this function
can have multiple lines,

20:19:06.151 --> 20:19:07.651
which is actually a nice thing.

20:19:07.651 --> 20:19:08.715
It looks a little weird.

20:19:08.715 --> 20:19:11.132
There's a lot of indentation
in curly braces going on now.

20:19:11.132 --> 20:19:15.452
But you can think of this as just
being, run these two lines of code,

20:19:15.452 --> 20:19:18.149
when the form is submitted.

20:19:18.149 --> 20:19:20.732
But if I want to block the form
from actually being submitted,

20:19:20.732 --> 20:19:21.722
I've got to do one other thing.

20:19:21.722 --> 20:19:24.930
And you would only know this from being
told it or reading the documentation.

20:19:24.930 --> 20:19:28.292
I need to do this
function, prevent default,

20:19:28.292 --> 20:19:32.122
passing in this E argument, which
is a variable that represents

20:19:32.122 --> 20:19:33.872
the event, more on
that another time, that

20:19:33.872 --> 20:19:36.482
just allows us to prevent
whatever the default

20:19:36.482 --> 20:19:39.512
handling of that particular event is.

20:19:39.512 --> 20:19:42.782
So long story short, this is
representative of the type of code

20:19:42.782 --> 20:19:45.962
you might write in JavaScript,
whereby you can actually interact

20:19:45.962 --> 20:19:48.422
with your code, the user's actual form.

20:19:48.422 --> 20:19:50.192
And we can do interesting things, too.

20:19:50.192 --> 20:19:53.532
Built into browsers nowadays
is functionality like this.

20:19:53.532 --> 20:19:57.251
So here's a very simple example, that
has just three buttons in it, one red,

20:19:57.251 --> 20:19:58.382
one green, one blue.

20:19:58.382 --> 20:20:00.751
Well, it turns out using
JavaScript, you can control

20:20:00.751 --> 20:20:02.882
the CSS of a page programmatically.

20:20:02.882 --> 20:20:05.282
I can change the background
of the body of the page

20:20:05.282 --> 20:20:10.382
to red, to green, to blue, just by
listening for clicks on these buttons,

20:20:10.382 --> 20:20:12.482
and then changing CSS properties.

20:20:12.482 --> 20:20:15.272
Just to give you a taste of this,
if I view the page's source,

20:20:15.272 --> 20:20:19.712
similar code here, I can
select the red button by an ID

20:20:19.712 --> 20:20:22.442
that I apparently defined
on it, right up here.

20:20:22.442 --> 20:20:25.872
I can add an event listener, this
time not for submit, but for click.

20:20:25.872 --> 20:20:28.199
And when it's clicked, I
execute this one line of code.

20:20:28.199 --> 20:20:30.241
And this one line of code
we haven't seen before,

20:20:30.241 --> 20:20:33.872
but you can go into the body of
the page, its style property,

20:20:33.872 --> 20:20:36.331
and you can change its
background color to red.

20:20:36.331 --> 20:20:38.852
This is one example of
two different groups

20:20:38.852 --> 20:20:40.562
not talking to one another in advance.

20:20:40.562 --> 20:20:43.911
In CSS, properties that have two
words are usually hyphenated,

20:20:43.911 --> 20:20:45.592
like background-color.

20:20:45.592 --> 20:20:48.501
Unfortunately, in JavaScript, if
you do something dash something,

20:20:48.501 --> 20:20:52.142
that's subtraction, which is
logically nonsensical here.

20:20:52.142 --> 20:20:56.932
So in CSS, you can convert
background-color to, in JavaScript,

20:20:56.932 --> 20:20:59.602
background Color, where
you capitalize the C,

20:20:59.602 --> 20:21:01.852
and you get rid of the minus sign.

20:21:01.852 --> 20:21:03.182
What else can we do here?

20:21:03.182 --> 20:21:05.901
Well, back in the day, there
used to be a blink tag.

20:21:05.901 --> 20:21:07.972
And it's one of the
few historical examples

20:21:07.972 --> 20:21:12.682
of a tag that was removed from HTML,
because in the late '90s, early 2000s,

20:21:12.682 --> 20:21:14.122
this is what the web looked like.

20:21:14.122 --> 20:21:16.177
There was a lot of this kind of stuff.

20:21:16.177 --> 20:21:18.052
There was even a marquee
that would move text

20:21:18.052 --> 20:21:19.510
from left to right over the screen.

20:21:19.510 --> 20:21:21.782
And the web was a very ugly place.

20:21:21.782 --> 20:21:25.071
I will admit, my very first web page
probably used both of these tags.

20:21:25.071 --> 20:21:26.542
But how can we bring it back?

20:21:26.542 --> 20:21:29.692
Well, this is a version of the
blink tag implemented in JavaScript.

20:21:29.692 --> 20:21:30.202
How?

20:21:30.202 --> 20:21:35.722
I wrote some code in this example, that
waits every 500 milliseconds to change

20:21:35.722 --> 20:21:39.472
the CSS of the page to be
visible, invisible, visible,

20:21:39.472 --> 20:21:42.892
invisible, because built into
JavaScript is support for a clock.

20:21:42.892 --> 20:21:45.452
So you can just do something
on some sort of schedule.

20:21:45.452 --> 20:21:47.782
Let me go ahead and open up
this example, autocomplete.

20:21:47.782 --> 20:21:49.251
So let me zoom back out.

20:21:49.251 --> 20:21:53.751
In Autocomplete.html, I whipped up as
an example, that has just a text box,

20:21:53.751 --> 20:21:56.482
but I also grabbed the
dictionary from problem

20:21:56.482 --> 20:22:00.712
set 5 speller, so that if I want
to search for something like Apple,

20:22:00.712 --> 20:22:04.492
this searches that 140,000
words, using JavaScript,

20:22:04.492 --> 20:22:07.380
to create what we know in the
world of the web as autocomplete.

20:22:07.380 --> 20:22:09.172
When you start searching
for something, you

20:22:09.172 --> 20:22:11.632
should start to see words
that start with that phrase.

20:22:11.632 --> 20:22:14.165
And sure enough, if I search
for something like banana,

20:22:14.165 --> 20:22:17.332
here's the three variants of bananas
that appear in that file, and so forth.

20:22:17.332 --> 20:22:18.382
How is that working?

20:22:18.382 --> 20:22:21.812
Just JavaScript, when
it finds matching words,

20:22:21.812 --> 20:22:24.862
it's just updating the DOM, the
tree in the computer's memory,

20:22:24.862 --> 20:22:27.922
to show more and more text, or less.

20:22:27.922 --> 20:22:34.162
And for one final example, this is how
programs like DoorDash and Google Maps

20:22:34.162 --> 20:22:35.872
and Uber Eats and so work.

20:22:35.872 --> 20:22:40.042
You have built into browsers today some
fancy APIs, application programming

20:22:40.042 --> 20:22:44.152
interfaces, whereby you can ask for
information about the user's device.

20:22:44.152 --> 20:22:47.602
For instance, here, I wrote a
program, in Geolocation.html, that's

20:22:47.602 --> 20:22:49.449
apparently asking to know my location.

20:22:49.449 --> 20:22:51.532
All right, let me go ahead
and allow it this time,

20:22:51.532 --> 20:22:54.202
if that's something you're
comfortable with on your own device.

20:22:54.202 --> 20:22:57.202
It's taking a moment, because sometimes
these things take a little while

20:22:57.202 --> 20:22:58.082
to analyze.

20:22:58.082 --> 20:23:02.602
But, hopefully, in just a moment, there
are apparently my GPS coordinates,

20:23:02.602 --> 20:23:06.232
and as a final flourish today, for what
you can do with a little bit of HTML

20:23:06.232 --> 20:23:08.602
for your structure, CSS
for your style, and now

20:23:08.602 --> 20:23:13.372
JavaScript for your logic, which we'll
tie in again next week, let me go ahead

20:23:13.372 --> 20:23:15.982
and search Google for
those GPS coordinates.

20:23:15.982 --> 20:23:21.472
Zoom in here on Google Maps,
and if we zoom in, in, in,

20:23:21.472 --> 20:23:22.462
OK, we're pretty close.

20:23:22.462 --> 20:23:26.122
We're not on that street, but
there, oh, there it is, actually.

20:23:26.122 --> 20:23:27.832
There is the marker it had put for us.

20:23:27.832 --> 20:23:30.119
We're indeed here in Memorial Hall.

20:23:30.119 --> 20:23:32.452
So all that with JavaScript,
but the basic understanding

20:23:32.452 --> 20:23:34.252
of the DOM and the
document object model,

20:23:34.252 --> 20:23:36.482
we'll pick up where
we left off next week.

20:23:36.482 --> 20:23:37.492
And now add a back-end.

20:23:37.492 --> 20:23:37.762
See you next time.

20:23:37.762 --> 20:23:41.234
[MUSIC PLAYING]

20:24:56.272 --> 20:24:57.172
DAVID: All right.

20:24:57.172 --> 20:25:01.222
So this is CS50 and this is
week nine, and this is it

20:25:01.222 --> 20:25:03.472
in terms of programming fundamentals.

20:25:03.472 --> 20:25:06.250
Today, we come rather full circle
with so many of the languages

20:25:06.250 --> 20:25:08.542
that we've been looking at
over the past several weeks.

20:25:08.542 --> 20:25:11.512
And with HTML and CSS
and JavaScript last week,

20:25:11.512 --> 20:25:14.782
we're going to add back into
the mix, Python and SQL.

20:25:14.782 --> 20:25:18.182
And with that, do we have the
ability to program for the web.

20:25:18.182 --> 20:25:20.602
And even though this isn't
the only user interface out

20:25:20.602 --> 20:25:23.692
there, increasingly-- or people
certainly using laptops and desktops

20:25:23.692 --> 20:25:26.722
and a browser to access applications
that people have written,

20:25:26.722 --> 20:25:31.312
but it's also, increasingly, the way
that mobile apps are written as well.

20:25:31.312 --> 20:25:33.532
There are languages
called Swift for iOS,

20:25:33.532 --> 20:25:35.692
there are languages
called Java for Android,

20:25:35.692 --> 20:25:38.512
but coding applications
in both of those language

20:25:38.512 --> 20:25:42.292
means knowing twice as many language,
building twice as many applications,

20:25:42.292 --> 20:25:42.952
potentially.

20:25:42.952 --> 20:25:45.502
So we're increasingly seeing,
for better or for worse,

20:25:45.502 --> 20:25:47.602
that the world is starting
to really standardize,

20:25:47.602 --> 20:25:51.502
at least for the next some number of
years, on HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

20:25:51.502 --> 20:25:55.492
coupled with other languages like
Python and SQL on the so-called backend.

20:25:55.492 --> 20:25:57.930
And so today, we'll tie
all of those together

20:25:57.930 --> 20:26:00.472
and give you the last of the
tools in your toolkit with which

20:26:00.472 --> 20:26:03.052
to tackle final projects to
go off into the real world,

20:26:03.052 --> 20:26:05.962
ultimately, and somehow solve
problems with programming.

20:26:05.962 --> 20:26:11.072
But we need an additional tool today,
and we've sort of outgrown HTTP server.

20:26:11.072 --> 20:26:14.239
This is just a program that
comes on certain computers

20:26:14.239 --> 20:26:16.072
that you can install
for free, happens to be

20:26:16.072 --> 20:26:18.742
written in a language called
JavaScript, but it's a program

20:26:18.742 --> 20:26:22.102
that we've been using to
run a web server in VSCO.

20:26:22.102 --> 20:26:24.932
But you can run it on your own
Mac or PC or anywhere else.

20:26:24.932 --> 20:26:28.702
But all this particular
HTTP server does is

20:26:28.702 --> 20:26:32.512
serve up static content
like HTML files, CSS files,

20:26:32.512 --> 20:26:36.982
JavaScript files, maybe images, maybe
video files, but just static content.

20:26:36.982 --> 20:26:41.362
It has no ability to really interact
with the user beyond simple clicks.

20:26:41.362 --> 20:26:46.562
You can create a web form and serve
it visually using HTTP server,

20:26:46.562 --> 20:26:50.392
but if the human types in input into
a form and click Submit, unless you

20:26:50.392 --> 20:26:53.842
submit it elsewhere to something like
google.com like we did last time,

20:26:53.842 --> 20:26:56.962
it's not actually going to go anywhere
because this server can't actually

20:26:56.962 --> 20:26:59.222
process the requests that are coming in.

20:26:59.222 --> 20:27:02.152
So today, we're going to introduce
another type of server that

20:27:02.152 --> 20:27:06.112
comes with Python that allows
us to not only serve web pages

20:27:06.112 --> 20:27:07.732
but also process user input.

20:27:07.732 --> 20:27:11.482
And recall that all that input is
going to come ultimately from the URL,

20:27:11.482 --> 20:27:14.062
or more deeply inside of
those virtual envelopes.

20:27:14.062 --> 20:27:17.902
So here's the canonical URL we talked
about last week for random website

20:27:17.902 --> 20:27:20.042
like www.example.com.

20:27:20.042 --> 20:27:23.782
And I've highlighted the slash to
connote the root of the web server,

20:27:23.782 --> 20:27:26.302
like the default folder
where, presumably, there's

20:27:26.302 --> 20:27:29.912
a file called index.html
or something else in there.

20:27:29.912 --> 20:27:32.122
Otherwise, you might have
a more explicit mention

20:27:32.122 --> 20:27:34.552
of the actual file named file.html.

20:27:34.552 --> 20:27:37.635
You can have folders, as you probably
gleaned from the most recent problem

20:27:37.635 --> 20:27:38.135
set.

20:27:38.135 --> 20:27:40.222
You can have files in
folders like this, and these

20:27:40.222 --> 20:27:44.092
are all examples of what a programmer
would typically call a path.

20:27:44.092 --> 20:27:45.922
So it might not just
be a single word, it

20:27:45.922 --> 20:27:49.832
might have multiple slashes and multiple
folders and some folders and files.

20:27:49.832 --> 20:27:51.832
But this is just more
generally known as a path.

20:27:51.832 --> 20:27:54.471
But there's another term of our,
that's essentially equivalent,

20:27:54.471 --> 20:27:55.652
that we'll introduce today.

20:27:55.652 --> 20:27:59.062
This is also synonymously
called a route, which is maybe

20:27:59.062 --> 20:28:03.022
a better generic description of what
these things are because it turns out

20:28:03.022 --> 20:28:05.512
they don't have to
map to, that is, refer

20:28:05.512 --> 20:28:08.572
to a specific folder
or a specific file, you

20:28:08.572 --> 20:28:11.095
can come up with your
own routes in a website.

20:28:11.095 --> 20:28:13.762
And just make sure that when the
user visits that, you give them

20:28:13.762 --> 20:28:14.662
a certain website page.

20:28:14.662 --> 20:28:17.370
If they visit something else, you
give them a different web page.

20:28:17.370 --> 20:28:20.242
It doesn't have to map to a very
specific file, as we'll soon see.

20:28:20.242 --> 20:28:23.422
And if you want to get input from
the user, just like Google does,

20:28:23.422 --> 20:28:28.222
like q=cats, you can add a question
mark at the end of this route.

20:28:28.222 --> 20:28:32.062
The key, or the HTTP parameter name
that you want to define for yourself,

20:28:32.062 --> 20:28:35.025
and then equal sum value that,
presumably, the human typed in.

20:28:35.025 --> 20:28:37.192
If you have more of these,
you can put an ampersand,

20:28:37.192 --> 20:28:41.902
and then more key equals value pairs
ampersand, repeat, repeat, repeat.

20:28:41.902 --> 20:28:46.532
The catch, though, is that using the
tools that we had last week alone,

20:28:46.532 --> 20:28:51.682
we don't really have the ability to
parse, that is, to analyze and extract

20:28:51.682 --> 20:28:53.602
things like q equals cats.

20:28:53.602 --> 20:28:56.662
You could have appended question
mark q equals cats or anything else

20:28:56.662 --> 20:29:00.562
to any of URLs in your home
page for problem set eight,

20:29:00.562 --> 20:29:04.222
but it doesn't actually do
anything useful, necessarily,

20:29:04.222 --> 20:29:06.112
unless you use some fancy JavaScript.

20:29:06.112 --> 20:29:09.242
The server is not going to bother
even looking in that for you.

20:29:09.242 --> 20:29:12.142
But today, we're going to
introduce using a bit of Python.

20:29:12.142 --> 20:29:15.982
And in fact, we're going to use a web
server implemented in Python, instead

20:29:15.982 --> 20:29:19.312
of using HTTP server alone,
to automatically, for you,

20:29:19.312 --> 20:29:22.192
look for any key value pairs
after the question mark

20:29:22.192 --> 20:29:25.882
and then hand them to you in
the form of a Python dictionary.

20:29:25.882 --> 20:29:29.782
Recall that a dictionary in Python, a
dict object, is just key value pairs.

20:29:29.782 --> 20:29:33.917
That seems like a perfect fit
for these kinds of parameters.

20:29:33.917 --> 20:29:36.292
And you're not going to have
to write that code yourself.

20:29:36.292 --> 20:29:39.902
It's going to be handed to you by
way of what's called a framework.

20:29:39.902 --> 20:29:41.782
So this will be the
second of two frameworks,

20:29:41.782 --> 20:29:43.324
really, that we look at in the class.

20:29:43.324 --> 20:29:46.012
And a framework is essentially
a bunch of libraries

20:29:46.012 --> 20:29:48.502
that someone else wrote
and a set of conventions,

20:29:48.502 --> 20:29:49.952
therefore, for doing things.

20:29:49.952 --> 20:29:52.762
So those of you who really
started dabbling with Bootstrap

20:29:52.762 --> 20:29:56.442
this past week to make your home
pages prettier and nicely laid out,

20:29:56.442 --> 20:29:57.712
you are using a framework.

20:29:57.712 --> 20:29:58.212
Why?

20:29:58.212 --> 20:30:01.872
Well, you're using libraries, code that
someone else wrote, like all the CSS,

20:30:01.872 --> 20:30:04.962
maybe some of the JavaScript that
the Bootstrap people wrote for you.

20:30:04.962 --> 20:30:08.742
But it's also a framework in the
sense that you have to go all in.

20:30:08.742 --> 20:30:11.262
You have to use Bootstraps
classes, and you

20:30:11.262 --> 20:30:15.582
have to lay out your divs or
your spans or your table tags

20:30:15.582 --> 20:30:17.425
in a sort of Bootstrap-friendly way.

20:30:17.425 --> 20:30:19.842
And it's not too onerous, but
you're following conventions

20:30:19.842 --> 20:30:21.682
that a bunch of humans standardized on.

20:30:21.682 --> 20:30:25.422
So similarly, in the world of
Python, is there another framework

20:30:25.422 --> 20:30:26.802
we're going to start using today.

20:30:26.802 --> 20:30:30.192
And whereas Bootstrap is
used for CSS and JavaScript,

20:30:30.192 --> 20:30:32.232
Flask is going to be used for Python.

20:30:32.232 --> 20:30:34.932
And it just solves a lot
of common problems for us.

20:30:34.932 --> 20:30:38.142
It's going to make it easier
for us to analyze the URLs

20:30:38.142 --> 20:30:40.782
and get key value pairs,
it's going to make it easier

20:30:40.782 --> 20:30:43.872
for us to find files or
images that the human wants

20:30:43.872 --> 20:30:45.312
to see when visiting our website.

20:30:45.312 --> 20:30:48.204
It's even going to make it easier
to send emails automatically,

20:30:48.204 --> 20:30:49.662
like when someone fills out a form.

20:30:49.662 --> 20:30:52.972
You can dynamically, using code,
send them an email as well.

20:30:52.972 --> 20:30:55.272
So Flask, and with it
some related libraries,

20:30:55.272 --> 20:30:58.512
it's just going to make stuff
like that easier for us.

20:30:58.512 --> 20:31:03.102
And to do this, all we have to do
is adhere to some pretty minimalist

20:31:03.102 --> 20:31:05.212
requirements of this framework.

20:31:05.212 --> 20:31:08.412
We're going to have to create a
file for ourselves called app.py,

20:31:08.412 --> 20:31:11.412
this is where our web app or
application is going to live.

20:31:11.412 --> 20:31:15.882
If we have any libraries that we want to
use, the convention in the Python world

20:31:15.882 --> 20:31:19.512
is to have a very simple text
file called requirements.txt

20:31:19.512 --> 20:31:21.852
where you list the names
of those libraries,

20:31:21.852 --> 20:31:26.652
top to bottom, in that text file,
similar in spirit to the include

20:31:26.652 --> 20:31:30.412
or the import statements that we
saw in C and Python, respectively.

20:31:30.412 --> 20:31:33.462
We're going to have a static
folder or static directory, which

20:31:33.462 --> 20:31:36.612
means any files you create that
are not ever going to change,

20:31:36.612 --> 20:31:39.204
like images, CSS files,
JavaScript files,

20:31:39.204 --> 20:31:40.662
they're going to go in this folder.

20:31:40.662 --> 20:31:43.512
And then lastly, any
HTML that you write,

20:31:43.512 --> 20:31:45.912
web pages you want the
human to see, are going

20:31:45.912 --> 20:31:47.752
to go in a folder called templates.

20:31:47.752 --> 20:31:50.712
So this is, again, evidence of
what we mean by a framework.

20:31:50.712 --> 20:31:52.512
Do you have to make a web app like this?

20:31:52.512 --> 20:31:54.702
No, but if you're using
this particular framework,

20:31:54.702 --> 20:31:58.752
this is what people decided
would be the human conventions.

20:31:58.752 --> 20:32:02.962
If you've heard of other frameworks like
Django or asp.net or bunches of others,

20:32:02.962 --> 20:32:06.132
there are just different conventions
out there for creating applications.

20:32:06.132 --> 20:32:10.152
Flask is a very nice
microframework in that that's it.

20:32:10.152 --> 20:32:13.632
All you have to do is adhere to
these pretty minimalist requirements

20:32:13.632 --> 20:32:16.602
to get some code up and running.

20:32:16.602 --> 20:32:19.382
All right, so let's go
ahead and make a web app.

20:32:19.382 --> 20:32:21.132
Let me go ahead and
switch over to VS Code

20:32:21.132 --> 20:32:23.174
here, and let me practice
what I'm preaching here

20:32:23.174 --> 20:32:25.482
by first creating app.py.

20:32:25.482 --> 20:32:29.172
And let's go ahead and create
an application that very simply,

20:32:29.172 --> 20:32:31.462
maybe, says hello to the user.

20:32:31.462 --> 20:32:35.922
So something that, initially, is not all
that dynamic, pretty static, in fact.

20:32:35.922 --> 20:32:37.882
But we'll build on that
as we've always done.

20:32:37.882 --> 20:32:41.862
So in app.py, what I'm going to do
first is exactly the line of code

20:32:41.862 --> 20:32:43.182
I had on the screen earlier.

20:32:43.182 --> 20:32:49.602
From Flask, import Flask, with a capital
F second and a lowercase f first.

20:32:49.602 --> 20:32:52.152
And I'm also going to
preemptively import

20:32:52.152 --> 20:32:56.202
a couple of functions,
render template, and request.

20:32:56.202 --> 20:32:58.122
More on those in just a bit.

20:32:58.122 --> 20:33:00.762
And then below that, I'm going
to say, go ahead and do this.

20:33:00.762 --> 20:33:03.492
Give me a web-- a
variable called app that's

20:33:03.492 --> 20:33:07.692
going to be the result of calling
the Flask function and passing in it

20:33:07.692 --> 20:33:10.162
this weird incantation here, name.

20:33:10.162 --> 20:33:13.572
So we've seen this a few weeks back
when we played around with Python

20:33:13.572 --> 20:33:16.302
and we had that if main thing
at the bottom of the screen.

20:33:16.302 --> 20:33:21.852
For now, just know that __name__
refers to the name of the current file.

20:33:21.852 --> 20:33:26.322
And so this line here, simple as
it is, tells Python, hey, Python,

20:33:26.322 --> 20:33:30.072
turn this file into a Flask application.

20:33:30.072 --> 20:33:33.792
Flask is a function that just figures
out, then, how to do the rest.

20:33:33.792 --> 20:33:37.392
The last thing I'm going to do for this
very simple web application is this.

20:33:37.392 --> 20:33:41.052
I'm going to say that I'm
going to have a function called

20:33:41.052 --> 20:33:42.972
index that takes no arguments.

20:33:42.972 --> 20:33:44.892
And whenever this
function is called, I want

20:33:44.892 --> 20:33:50.322
to return the results of rendering
a template called index.html.

20:33:50.322 --> 20:33:51.277
And that's it.

20:33:51.277 --> 20:33:53.652
So let's assume there's a file
somewhere, haven't created

20:33:53.652 --> 20:33:55.482
it yet, called index.html.

20:33:55.482 --> 20:33:57.702
But render template
means render this file

20:33:57.702 --> 20:34:01.162
that is printed to the
user's screen, so to speak.

20:34:01.162 --> 20:34:03.972
The last thing I'm going to
do is I have to tell Flask

20:34:03.972 --> 20:34:06.262
when to call this index function.

20:34:06.262 --> 20:34:11.928
And so I'm going to tell it to define
a route for, quote unquote, "slash."

20:34:11.928 --> 20:34:13.030
And that's it.

20:34:13.030 --> 20:34:15.072
So let's take a look at
what I just created here.

20:34:15.072 --> 20:34:18.042
This is slightly new syntax, and
it's really the only weirdness

20:34:18.042 --> 20:34:19.812
that we'll have today in Python.

20:34:19.812 --> 20:34:22.512
This is what's known in Python
is what's called a decorator.

20:34:22.512 --> 20:34:24.522
A decorator is a
special type of function

20:34:24.522 --> 20:34:27.072
that modifies, essentially,
another function.

20:34:27.072 --> 20:34:30.112
For our purposes, just know
that on line six this says,

20:34:30.112 --> 20:34:33.462
hey Python, define a route
for slash, the default

20:34:33.462 --> 20:34:35.262
page on my website application.

20:34:35.262 --> 20:34:37.662
The next two lines, seven
and eight, say, hey Python,

20:34:37.662 --> 20:34:40.392
define a function called
index, takes no arguments.

20:34:40.392 --> 20:34:45.282
And the only thing you should ever do is
return render template of quote unquote

20:34:45.282 --> 20:34:47.302
"index.html."

20:34:47.302 --> 20:34:48.412
All right, so that's it.

20:34:48.412 --> 20:34:51.462
So really, the next question,
naturally, should be all right, well,

20:34:51.462 --> 20:34:55.252
what is in index.html?

20:34:55.252 --> 20:34:56.992
Well, let me go ahead and do that next.

20:34:56.992 --> 20:35:00.262
Let me create a directory called
templates, practicing, again,

20:35:00.262 --> 20:35:01.522
what I preached earlier.

20:35:01.522 --> 20:35:04.282
So I'm going to create a new
empty directory called templates,

20:35:04.282 --> 20:35:08.002
I'm going to go and
CD into that directory

20:35:08.002 --> 20:35:11.662
and then do code of index.html.

20:35:11.662 --> 20:35:13.282
So here is going to be my index page.

20:35:13.282 --> 20:35:16.227
And I'm going to do a very
simple web page, doc type HTML.

20:35:16.227 --> 20:35:18.352
I'm just going to borrow
some stuff from last week.

20:35:18.352 --> 20:35:20.602
HTML language equals English.

20:35:20.602 --> 20:35:21.772
I'll close that tag.

