And let's consider what I've done.

21:00:03.542 --> 21:00:05.972
This is starting to look
weird fast, and this is now

21:00:05.972 --> 21:00:09.872
a mix of HTML with templating code.

21:00:09.872 --> 21:00:15.962
Index.html, first line now says, hey,
Flask, this file extends layout.html,

21:00:15.962 --> 21:00:17.072
whatever that is.

21:00:17.072 --> 21:00:20.402
This next line, three through
10, says, hey, Flask, here

21:00:20.402 --> 21:00:23.732
is what I consider my body block to be.

21:00:23.732 --> 21:00:27.362
Plug this into the layout placeholder.

21:00:27.362 --> 21:00:33.612
Therefore, so if I now go back
to layout.html, and layout.html,

21:00:33.612 --> 21:00:35.672
it's almost all HTML by contrast.

21:00:35.672 --> 21:00:38.757
But there is this placeholder, and
if I want to put a default value,

21:00:38.757 --> 21:00:39.632
I could say-- whoops.

21:00:39.632 --> 21:00:41.402
If I want to put a
default value, I could

21:00:41.402 --> 21:00:45.002
put a default value there just in case
some page does not have a body block.

21:00:45.002 --> 21:00:47.172
But in general, that's
not going to be relevant.

21:00:47.172 --> 21:00:50.882
So this is just a placeholder,
albeit a little verbose, that says

21:00:50.882 --> 21:00:54.492
plug in the page-specific
content right here.

21:00:54.492 --> 21:00:58.262
So if I go now into greet.html,
this one's even easier.

21:00:58.262 --> 21:01:01.262
I'm going to cut this content
and get rid of everything else.

21:01:01.262 --> 21:01:06.842
Greet.html 2 is going to extend
layouts, dot HTML extends plural,

21:01:06.842 --> 21:01:13.512
and then I'm going to have my body block
here simply be this one line of code.

21:01:13.512 --> 21:01:15.962
And then I'm going to go
ahead and end that block here.

21:01:15.962 --> 21:01:18.812
These are not HTML tags,
this is not HTML syntax.

21:01:18.812 --> 21:01:22.742
Technically, the syntax we keep
seeing with the curly braces,

21:01:22.742 --> 21:01:28.982
and these now curly braces with percent
signs, is an example of Jinja syntax,

21:01:28.982 --> 21:01:33.812
J-I-N-J-A, which is a language,
that some humans invented,

21:01:33.812 --> 21:01:35.732
for this purpose of templating.

21:01:35.732 --> 21:01:37.952
And the people who
invented Flask decided,

21:01:37.952 --> 21:01:40.052
we're not going to come
up with our own syntax,

21:01:40.052 --> 21:01:44.067
we're going to use these other
people's syntax called Jinja syntax.

21:01:44.067 --> 21:01:46.442
So again, there starts to be
at this point in the course,

21:01:46.442 --> 21:01:50.372
and really in computing, a lot of
sharing, now, of ideas and sharing

21:01:50.372 --> 21:01:50.952
of code.

21:01:50.952 --> 21:01:54.842
So Flask is using this syntax, but
other libraries and other languages

21:01:54.842 --> 21:01:57.032
might also too.

21:01:57.032 --> 21:02:01.832
All right, so now
index.html is half HTML,

21:02:01.832 --> 21:02:04.262
half templating code, Jinja syntax.

21:02:04.262 --> 21:02:08.012
Greet.html is almost all
Jinja syntax, no tags even,

21:02:08.012 --> 21:02:11.582
but because they both
extend layout.html,

21:02:11.582 --> 21:02:14.912
now I think I've improved
the design of this thing.

21:02:14.912 --> 21:02:18.942
If I go back to app.py, none
of this really needs to change.

21:02:18.942 --> 21:02:22.112
I don't change my templates
to mention layout.html,

21:02:22.112 --> 21:02:25.622
that's already implicit in the fact
that we have the extends keyword.

21:02:25.622 --> 21:02:28.742
So now if I go ahead and
open my terminal window,

21:02:28.742 --> 21:02:32.822
go back to the same folder
as app.py and do Flask run,

21:02:32.822 --> 21:02:35.732
all right, my application
is running on port 5000.

21:02:35.732 --> 21:02:39.392
Let me now go back to the /route
in my browser and hit Enter,

21:02:39.392 --> 21:02:40.682
I have this form again.

21:02:40.682 --> 21:02:44.252
And just as a little check, let
me view the source of the page

21:02:44.252 --> 21:02:45.932
that my browser is seeing.

21:02:45.932 --> 21:02:48.362
And there's all of the code.

21:02:48.362 --> 21:02:51.422
No mention of Jinja, no curly
braces, no percent signs.

21:02:51.422 --> 21:02:52.097
It's just HTML.

21:02:52.097 --> 21:02:54.722
It's not quite pretty printed in
the same way, but that's fine.

21:02:54.722 --> 21:02:57.264
Because now, we're starting to
dynamically generate websites.

21:02:57.264 --> 21:03:00.512
And by that, I mean this isn't
quite indented nicely or perfectly.

21:03:00.512 --> 21:03:01.112
That's fine.

21:03:01.112 --> 21:03:03.872
If it's indented in the
source code version,

21:03:03.872 --> 21:03:05.912
doesn't matter what the
browser really sees.

21:03:05.912 --> 21:03:08.402
Let me now go ahead and type
in my name, click Submit.

21:03:08.402 --> 21:03:10.052
I should see, yep, hello, David.

21:03:10.052 --> 21:03:12.152
Let me go ahead and view
the source of this page.

21:03:12.152 --> 21:03:16.212
And we'll see almost the same
thing with what's plugged in there.

21:03:16.212 --> 21:03:19.742
So this is, now, web programming
in the literal sense.

21:03:19.742 --> 21:03:23.042
I did not hard code a page that says
hello comma David, hello comma Carter,

21:03:23.042 --> 21:03:24.032
hello comma Emma.

21:03:24.032 --> 21:03:27.752
I hardcoded a page that has a
template with a placeholder,

21:03:27.752 --> 21:03:31.472
and now I'm using actual
logic, some code in app.py,

21:03:31.472 --> 21:03:37.752
to actually tell the server
what to send to the browser.

21:03:37.752 --> 21:03:42.902
All right, any questions,
then, on where we're at here?

21:03:42.902 --> 21:03:45.272
This is now a web application.

21:03:45.272 --> 21:03:49.052
Simple though it is, it's
no longer just a web site.

21:03:49.052 --> 21:03:49.923
Yeah?

21:03:49.923 --> 21:03:54.522
AUDIENCE: Is what we did just better
for design or for memory [INAUDIBLE]??

21:03:54.522 --> 21:03:57.232
DAVID: It better for
design or for memory?

21:03:57.232 --> 21:03:57.732
Both.

21:03:57.732 --> 21:03:59.922
It's definitely better
for design because, truly,

21:03:59.922 --> 21:04:02.112
if we had a third page,
fourth page, I would really

21:04:02.112 --> 21:04:03.912
start just resorting to copy paste.

21:04:03.912 --> 21:04:07.032
And as you saw with home page,
often, in the head of your page,

21:04:07.032 --> 21:04:10.632
you might want to include some CSS
files like Bootstrap or something else.

21:04:10.632 --> 21:04:13.122
You might want to have
other information up there.

21:04:13.122 --> 21:04:16.754
If you had to upgrade the version of
Bootstrap or you change libraries,

21:04:16.754 --> 21:04:18.462
so you want to change
one of those lines,

21:04:18.462 --> 21:04:21.852
you would literally have to go into
three, four, a hundred different files

21:04:21.852 --> 21:04:23.632
to make one simple change.

21:04:23.632 --> 21:04:25.002
So that's bad design.

21:04:25.002 --> 21:04:26.502
And in terms of memory, yes.

21:04:26.502 --> 21:04:30.732
Theoretically, the server, because
it knows there's this common layout,

21:04:30.732 --> 21:04:33.394
it can theoretically do some
optimizations underneath the hood.

21:04:33.394 --> 21:04:36.102
Flask is probably doing that, but
not in the mode we're using it.

21:04:36.102 --> 21:04:38.022
We're using it in
development mode, which

21:04:38.022 --> 21:04:41.592
means it's typically
reloading things each time.

21:04:41.592 --> 21:04:44.802
Other questions on this application?

21:04:47.972 --> 21:04:48.842
Anything at all?

21:04:48.842 --> 21:04:54.462
All right, so let me ask a question,
not just in terms of the code design.

21:04:54.462 --> 21:04:56.642
What about the implications for privacy?

21:04:56.642 --> 21:05:02.192
Why is this maybe not the best design
for users, how I've implemented this?

21:05:02.192 --> 21:05:04.232
I've used a web form, but--

21:05:04.232 --> 21:05:05.067
Yeah?

21:05:05.067 --> 21:05:06.492
AUDIENCE: For some reason,
you wanted your name.

21:05:06.492 --> 21:05:08.682
So these private people
could just look at the URL.

21:05:08.682 --> 21:05:09.182
DAVID: Yeah.

21:05:09.182 --> 21:05:11.042
I mean, if you have a
nosy sibling or roommate

21:05:11.042 --> 21:05:13.052
and they have access to
your laptop and they just

21:05:13.052 --> 21:05:15.302
go trolling through your
autocomplete or your history,

21:05:15.302 --> 21:05:18.332
like, literally what you typed into
a website is going to be visible.

21:05:18.332 --> 21:05:21.332
Not a big deal if it's your name, but
if it's your password, your credit

21:05:21.332 --> 21:05:23.552
card or anything else
that's mildly sensitive,

21:05:23.552 --> 21:05:26.942
you probably don't want it
ending up in the URL at all

21:05:26.942 --> 21:05:29.402
even if you're in
incognito mode or whatnot.

21:05:29.402 --> 21:05:34.152
You just don't want to expose yourself
or your users to that kind of risk.

21:05:34.152 --> 21:05:35.942
So perhaps, we can do better than that.

21:05:35.942 --> 21:05:38.312
And fortunately, this one
is actually an easy change.

21:05:38.312 --> 21:05:43.502
Let me go into my
index.html where my form is.

21:05:43.502 --> 21:05:48.272
And in my form, I can just change
the method from GET to POST.

21:05:48.272 --> 21:05:50.912
It's still going to send key
value pairs to the server,

21:05:50.912 --> 21:05:52.952
but it's not going to
put them in the URL.

21:05:52.952 --> 21:05:56.257
The upside of which is that we
can assuage this privacy concern,

21:05:56.257 --> 21:05:58.382
but I'm going to have to
make one other change too.

21:05:58.382 --> 21:06:02.852
Because now, if I go ahead and run
Flask again after making that change,

21:06:02.852 --> 21:06:07.332
and I now reload the form to make
sure I have the latest version.

21:06:07.332 --> 21:06:10.172
You should be in the habit
of going to View, Developer,

21:06:10.172 --> 21:06:12.784
View Source, or Developer
Tools just to make sure

21:06:12.784 --> 21:06:15.242
that what you're seeing in your
browser is what you intend.

21:06:15.242 --> 21:06:18.062
And yes, I do see what I wanted.

21:06:18.062 --> 21:06:19.652
Method equals POST now.

21:06:19.652 --> 21:06:22.382
Let me go ahead and type
in David and click Submit.

21:06:22.382 --> 21:06:24.872
Now I get a different error.

21:06:24.872 --> 21:06:28.902
This one is HTTP 405,
method not allowed.

21:06:28.902 --> 21:06:30.002
Why is that?

21:06:30.002 --> 21:06:34.082
Well, in my Flask application, I've
only defined a couple of routes so far.

21:06:34.082 --> 21:06:37.602
One of which is for slash,
then that worked fine.

21:06:37.602 --> 21:06:40.752
One of which is for /greet,
and that used to work fine.

21:06:40.752 --> 21:06:46.302
But apparently, what Flask is doing
is it only supports GET by default.

21:06:46.302 --> 21:06:51.512
So if I want to change this route to
support different methods, I can say,

21:06:51.512 --> 21:06:56.682
quote unquote "POST" inside
of this parameter here.

21:06:56.682 --> 21:07:01.592
So that now, I can actually
support POST, not just GET.

21:07:01.592 --> 21:07:08.642
And if I now restart Flask, so Flask
run, Enter, and I go back to this URL.

21:07:08.642 --> 21:07:11.282
Let me go back one screen
to the form, reload

21:07:11.282 --> 21:07:13.202
the page just to make
sure I have the latest

21:07:13.202 --> 21:07:14.785
even though nothing there has changed.

21:07:14.785 --> 21:07:18.392
Type David and click Submit now,
now I should see hello, world.

21:07:18.392 --> 21:07:25.142
Notice that I'm at the greet route,
but there's no mention of name

21:07:25.142 --> 21:07:28.022
equals anything in the URL.

21:07:28.022 --> 21:07:30.332
All right, so that's an
interesting takeaway.

21:07:30.332 --> 21:07:35.142
It's a simple change, but whereas GET
puts things in the URL, POST does not.

21:07:35.142 --> 21:07:37.382
But it still works so long
as you tweak the backend

21:07:37.382 --> 21:07:41.972
to look as a POST request, which
means look deeper in the envelope.

21:07:41.972 --> 21:07:44.522
It's not going to be as simple
as looking at the URL itself.

21:07:44.522 --> 21:07:46.322
Why shouldn't we just always use POST?

21:07:49.492 --> 21:07:53.752
Why not use POST everywhere?

21:07:53.752 --> 21:07:55.342
Any thoughts?

21:07:55.342 --> 21:07:58.822
Right, because it's obnoxious to
be putting any information in URLs

21:07:58.822 --> 21:08:02.084
if you're leaving these little
breadcrumbs in your history and people

21:08:02.084 --> 21:08:04.042
can poke around and see
what you've been doing.

21:08:07.702 --> 21:08:09.139
Yeah, what do you think?

21:08:09.139 --> 21:08:11.524
AUDIENCE: You're supposed
to duplicate [INAUDIBLE]..

21:08:15.032 --> 21:08:15.532
DAVID: Yeah.

21:08:15.532 --> 21:08:18.792
I mean, if you get rid of GET
requests and put nothing in the URL,

21:08:18.792 --> 21:08:22.194
your history, your autocomplete,
gets pretty less useful.

21:08:22.194 --> 21:08:25.152
Because none of the information is
there for storage, so you can't just

21:08:25.152 --> 21:08:26.569
go through the menu and hit Enter.

21:08:26.569 --> 21:08:28.182
You'd have to re-fill out the form.

21:08:28.182 --> 21:08:30.390
And there's this other
symptom that you can see here.

21:08:30.390 --> 21:08:33.015
Let me zoom out and let
me just reload this page.

21:08:33.015 --> 21:08:34.932
Notice that you'll get
this warning, and it'll

21:08:34.932 --> 21:08:39.552
look different in Safari and Firefox
and Edge and Chrome here, confirm form.

21:08:39.552 --> 21:08:40.342
args

21:08:40.342 --> 21:08:43.932
So your browser might remember what
your inputs were and that's great,

21:08:43.932 --> 21:08:45.712
but just while you're on the page.

21:08:45.712 --> 21:08:50.562
And this is in contrast to GET,
where the state is information.

21:08:50.562 --> 21:08:53.957
Like, key value pairs is
embedded in the URL itself.

21:08:53.957 --> 21:08:56.082
And if you looked at an
email I sent earlier today,

21:08:56.082 --> 21:08:59.277
I deliberately linked
to https://www.google.c

21:08:59.277 --> 21:09:00.882
om/search?q=what+time+is+it.

21:09:06.972 --> 21:09:11.472
This is, by definition, a GET
request when you click on it.

21:09:11.472 --> 21:09:14.862
Because it's going to grab the
information, the key value pair,

21:09:14.862 --> 21:09:18.172
from the URL, send it to Google
server, and it's just going to work.

21:09:18.172 --> 21:09:20.592
And the reason I sent this
via email earlier was I

21:09:20.592 --> 21:09:23.812
wanted people to very quickly be able
to check what is the current time.

21:09:23.812 --> 21:09:27.732
And so I can sort automate the process
of creating a Google search for you,

21:09:27.732 --> 21:09:29.952
but that you induce when
you click that link.

21:09:29.952 --> 21:09:35.382
If Google did not support GET, they
only supported this, the best I could do

21:09:35.382 --> 21:09:38.172
is send you all to this
URL which, unfortunately,

21:09:38.172 --> 21:09:39.762
has no useful information.

21:09:39.762 --> 21:09:42.972
I would have had to add to my
email, by the way, type in the words

21:09:42.972 --> 21:09:44.812
what time is it.

21:09:44.812 --> 21:09:46.522
So it's just bad for usability.

21:09:46.522 --> 21:09:49.722
So there, too, we might have design
when it comes to the low level code,

21:09:49.722 --> 21:09:53.262
but also the design when it comes
to the user experience, or UX,

21:09:53.262 --> 21:09:54.942
as a computer scientist would call it.

21:09:54.942 --> 21:09:58.212
Just in terms of what you want
to optimize for, ultimately.

21:09:58.212 --> 21:10:00.082
So GET and POST both have their roles.

21:10:00.082 --> 21:10:02.582
It depends on what kind of
functionality you want to provide

21:10:02.582 --> 21:10:06.912
and what kind of sensitivity
there might be around it.

21:10:06.912 --> 21:10:10.122
All right, any questions, then, on
this, our first web application?

21:10:10.122 --> 21:10:13.722
Super simple, just gets someone's
name and prints it back out.

21:10:13.722 --> 21:10:16.032
But we now have all
the plumbing with which

21:10:16.032 --> 21:10:19.032
to create really most anything we want.

21:10:21.820 --> 21:10:24.112
All right, let's go ahead
and take a five minute break.

21:10:24.112 --> 21:10:27.952
And when we come back, we'll add to
this some first year intramural sports.

21:10:27.952 --> 21:10:30.202
All right, so we are back.

21:10:30.202 --> 21:10:32.182
And recall that the last
thing we just changed

21:10:32.182 --> 21:10:34.942
was the route to use
POST instead of GET.

21:10:34.942 --> 21:10:37.852
So gone is my name and
any value in the URL.

21:10:37.852 --> 21:10:44.032
But there was a subtle bug or change
here that we didn't call out earlier.

21:10:44.032 --> 21:10:47.062
I did type David into the
form and I did click Submit,

21:10:47.062 --> 21:10:50.452
and yet here it is
saying hello comma world.

21:10:50.452 --> 21:10:53.492
So that seems to be
broken all of a sudden,

21:10:53.492 --> 21:10:56.572
even though we added support for POST.

21:10:56.572 --> 21:10:58.792
But something must be wrong.

21:10:58.792 --> 21:11:01.342
Logically, it must be the case here.

21:11:01.342 --> 21:11:05.462
Intuitively, that if I'm seeing
hello, world, that's the default value

21:11:05.462 --> 21:11:07.162
I gave the name variable.

21:11:07.162 --> 21:11:10.102
It must be that it's
not seeing a key called

21:11:10.102 --> 21:11:14.482
name in request.args, which is this.

21:11:14.482 --> 21:11:16.852
Gives you access to
everything after the URL.

21:11:16.852 --> 21:11:19.472
That's because there's this
other thing we should know about,

21:11:19.472 --> 21:11:21.742
which is not just
request.args but request.form.

21:11:21.742 --> 21:11:26.212
These are horribly named, but
request.args is for GET requests,

21:11:26.212 --> 21:11:28.804
request.form is for POST requests.

21:11:28.804 --> 21:11:31.012
Otherwise, they're pretty
much functionally the same.

21:11:31.012 --> 21:11:33.592
But the onus is on you,
the user or the programmer,

21:11:33.592 --> 21:11:35.832
to make sure you're using the right one.

21:11:35.832 --> 21:11:38.672
So I think if we want
to get rid of the world

21:11:38.672 --> 21:11:41.002
and actually see what
I, the human, typed in,

21:11:41.002 --> 21:11:45.202
I think I can just change
request.args to request.form.

21:11:45.202 --> 21:11:47.782
Still dot get, still
quote unquote "name,"

21:11:47.782 --> 21:11:52.252
and now, if I go ahead and rerun
Flask in my terminal window,

21:11:52.252 --> 21:11:54.777
go back to my browser, go
back to-- and actually,

21:11:54.777 --> 21:11:56.152
I won't even go back to the form.

21:11:56.152 --> 21:11:59.392
I will literally just reload,
Command R or Control R,

21:11:59.392 --> 21:12:02.572
and what this warning is
saying is it's going to submit

21:12:02.572 --> 21:12:05.002
the same information to the website.

21:12:05.002 --> 21:12:08.902
When I click Continue, now I
should see hello comma David.

21:12:08.902 --> 21:12:11.182
So again, you, too, are
going to encounter, probably,

21:12:11.182 --> 21:12:12.802
all these little subtleties.

21:12:12.802 --> 21:12:15.802
But if you focus on, really, the
first principles of last week,

21:12:15.802 --> 21:12:18.682
like what it HTTP, how
does it get request work,

21:12:18.682 --> 21:12:20.787
how does a POST request
work now, you should

21:12:20.787 --> 21:12:22.912
have a lot of the mental
building blocks with which

21:12:22.912 --> 21:12:25.102
to solve problems like these.

21:12:25.102 --> 21:12:28.342
And let me give you one other mental
model, now, for what it is we're doing.

21:12:28.342 --> 21:12:32.662
This framework called Flask is just an
example of many different frameworks

21:12:32.662 --> 21:12:36.262
that all implement the same
paradigm, the same way of thinking

21:12:36.262 --> 21:12:38.482
and the same way of
programming applications.

21:12:38.482 --> 21:12:41.932
And that's known as MVC,
model view controller.

21:12:41.932 --> 21:12:46.432
And here's a very simple diagram
that represents the process that you

21:12:46.432 --> 21:12:48.302
and I have been implementing thus far.

21:12:48.302 --> 21:12:51.292
And actually, this is more than
we've been implementing thus far.

21:12:51.292 --> 21:12:55.124
In app.py is what a programmer
would typically call the controller.

21:12:55.124 --> 21:12:57.082
That's the code you're
writing, this are called

21:12:57.082 --> 21:13:01.132
business logic that makes all of the
decisions, decides what to render,

21:13:01.132 --> 21:13:03.472
what values to show, and so forth.

21:13:03.472 --> 21:13:09.862
In layout.html, index.html, greet.html
is the so-called view templates

21:13:09.862 --> 21:13:12.652
that is the visualizations
that the human actually

21:13:12.652 --> 21:13:14.272
sees, the user interface.

21:13:14.272 --> 21:13:18.562
Those things are dumb, they pretty
much just say plop some values here.

21:13:18.562 --> 21:13:20.992
All of the hard work is done in app.py.

21:13:20.992 --> 21:13:26.002
So controller, AKA app.py, is where
your Python code generally is.

21:13:26.002 --> 21:13:31.492
And in your view is where your HTML and
your Jinja code, your Jinja templating,

21:13:31.492 --> 21:13:35.512
the curly braces, the curly braces
with percent signs, usually is.

21:13:35.512 --> 21:13:39.412
