21:53:09.312 --> 21:53:13.392
If I look at the deregister
request, notice that it's a POST.

21:53:13.392 --> 21:53:16.212
The status code that
eventually came back as 302,

21:53:16.212 --> 21:53:18.642
but let's look at the request itself.

21:53:18.642 --> 21:53:21.372
All the headers there we'll ignore.

21:53:21.372 --> 21:53:25.872
The only thing that
button submits, cleverly,

21:53:25.872 --> 21:53:29.892
is an ID parameter, a key equaling two.

21:53:29.892 --> 21:53:34.152
What does two presumably
represent or map to?

21:53:34.152 --> 21:53:37.062
Where did this two come from?

21:53:37.062 --> 21:53:40.782
It doesn't say Carter, it
doesn't say basketball?

21:53:40.782 --> 21:53:41.330
What is it?

21:53:41.330 --> 21:53:43.122
AUDIENCE: The second
person who registered.

21:53:43.122 --> 21:53:44.532
DAVID: The second
person that registered.

21:53:44.532 --> 21:53:47.682
So those primary keys that we started
talking about a couple of weeks

21:53:47.682 --> 21:53:50.982
ago, why it's useful to be able to
uniquely identify a row in a table,

21:53:50.982 --> 21:53:53.232
here is just one of the reasons why.

21:53:53.232 --> 21:53:58.272
If it suffices for me just to
send the ID number of the person

21:53:58.272 --> 21:54:03.142
I want to delete from the database,
because I can then have code like this.

21:54:03.142 --> 21:54:09.322
If I go into app.py and I look at my
deregister route now, the last of them,

21:54:09.322 --> 21:54:11.172
notice that I got this.

21:54:11.172 --> 21:54:15.642
I first go into the form, and I get
the ID that was submitted, hopefully.

21:54:15.642 --> 21:54:19.872
If there was, in fact, an ID, and
the form wasn't somehow empty,

21:54:19.872 --> 21:54:21.642
I execute this line of code.

21:54:21.642 --> 21:54:24.642
Delete from registrants where
ID equals question mark,

21:54:24.642 --> 21:54:29.202
and then I plug-in that number,
deleting Carter and only Carter.

21:54:29.202 --> 21:54:32.472
And I'm not using his name, because
what if we have two people named Carter,

21:54:32.472 --> 21:54:34.002
two people named Emma or David?

21:54:34.002 --> 21:54:35.602
You don't want to delete both of them.

21:54:35.602 --> 21:54:39.732
That's why these unique
IDs are so, so important.

21:54:39.732 --> 21:54:41.622
And here's another reason why.

21:54:41.622 --> 21:54:45.012
You don't want to store
some things in URLs.

21:54:45.012 --> 21:54:49.752
Suppose we went to this
URL, deregister?ID=3.

21:54:53.262 --> 21:54:58.132
Suppose I, maliciously,
emailed this URL to Emma.

21:54:58.132 --> 21:55:00.132
It doesn't matter so much
what the beginning is,

21:55:00.132 --> 21:55:06.252
but supposed I emailed her this URL,
/deregister?ID=3, and I said, hey,

21:55:06.252 --> 21:55:07.692
Emma, click this.

21:55:07.692 --> 21:55:10.332
And it uses GET instead of POST.

21:55:10.332 --> 21:55:12.327
What did I just trick her into doing?

21:55:15.370 --> 21:55:17.162
What's going to happen
if Emma clicks this?

21:55:17.162 --> 21:55:17.662
Yeah?

21:55:17.662 --> 21:55:19.022
AUDIENCE: Deregistering?

21:55:19.022 --> 21:55:21.522
DAVID: You would trick her
into deregistering herself.

21:55:21.522 --> 21:55:22.022
Why?

21:55:22.022 --> 21:55:24.812
Because if she's logged
into this FroshIMS website,

21:55:24.812 --> 21:55:28.862
and the URL contains her ID just
because I'm being malicious,

21:55:28.862 --> 21:55:31.922
and she clicked on it and
the website is using GET,

21:55:31.922 --> 21:55:34.712
unfortunately, GET URLs
are, again, stateful.

21:55:34.712 --> 21:55:36.782
They have state information in the URLs.

21:55:36.782 --> 21:55:39.532
And in this case, it's enough
to delete the user and boom,

21:55:39.532 --> 21:55:42.144
she would have accidentally
deregistered herself.

21:55:42.144 --> 21:55:43.352
And this is pretty innocuous.

21:55:43.352 --> 21:55:45.872
Suppose that this was
her bank account trying

21:55:45.872 --> 21:55:47.672
to make a withdrawal or a deposit.

21:55:47.672 --> 21:55:50.822
Suppose that this were some
other website, a Facebook URL,

21:55:50.822 --> 21:55:53.339
trying to trick her into
posting something automatically.

21:55:53.339 --> 21:55:55.172
Here, too, is another
consideration when you

21:55:55.172 --> 21:55:59.402
should use POST versus GET,
because GET requests can

21:55:59.402 --> 21:56:04.332
be plugged into emails sent via Slack
messages, text messages, or the like.

21:56:04.332 --> 21:56:06.602
And unless there's a
prompt saying, are you sure

21:56:06.602 --> 21:56:09.752
you want to deregister
yourself, you might blindly

21:56:09.752 --> 21:56:11.852
trick the user into being
vulnerable to what's

21:56:11.852 --> 21:56:14.222
called a cross-site request forgery.

21:56:14.222 --> 21:56:17.402
A fancy way of saying you
trick them into clicking a link

21:56:17.402 --> 21:56:21.242
that they shouldn't have, because
the website was using GET alone.

21:56:21.242 --> 21:56:25.112
All right, any question, then,
