21:57:17.532 --> 21:57:18.552
Oh, this thing.

21:57:18.552 --> 21:57:20.082
OK, sorry.

21:57:20.082 --> 21:57:27.762
This is a URI, it's typical syntax
that's referring to the SQLite

21:57:27.762 --> 21:57:32.352
protocol, so to speak, which means
use SQLite to talk to a file locally.

21:57:32.352 --> 21:57:35.322
:// is just like you and I see in URLs.

21:57:35.322 --> 21:57:37.962
The third slash, essentially,
means current folder.

21:57:37.962 --> 21:57:38.622
That's all.

21:57:38.622 --> 21:57:41.462
So it's a weird curiosity,
but it's typical

21:57:41.462 --> 21:57:43.962
whenever you're referring to a
local file and not one that's

21:57:43.962 --> 21:57:45.252
elsewhere on the internet.

21:57:45.252 --> 21:57:48.312
That's a bit of an oversimplification,
but that's indeed a convention.

