22:03:15.192 --> 22:03:18.682
which takes a list of emails that
should get the confirmation email.

22:03:18.682 --> 22:03:21.042
So in brackets, I just
put the one user's email

22:03:21.042 --> 22:03:24.082
and then mail.send that message.

22:03:24.082 --> 22:03:29.022
So let's scroll back up to see what
message and what mail actually is.

22:03:29.022 --> 22:03:30.562
Mail, I think, we saw.

22:03:30.562 --> 22:03:33.792
Yep, mail is this, which
I have as a variable

22:03:33.792 --> 22:03:36.222
because I followed the
documentation for this library.

22:03:36.222 --> 22:03:41.810
You simply configure your current app
with Mail support, capital M here.

22:03:41.810 --> 22:03:43.602
And if you look up here
now, on line seven,

22:03:43.602 --> 22:03:46.752
here's the new library
from Flask mail I imported.

22:03:46.752 --> 22:03:50.952
Capital Mail, capital Message, so that
I had the ability to create a message

22:03:50.952 --> 22:03:52.452
and send a mail.

22:03:52.452 --> 22:03:55.302
So such a simple thing whether you
want to confirm things for users,

22:03:55.302 --> 22:03:56.832
you want to do password resets.

22:03:56.832 --> 22:04:00.702
It can be this easy to
actually generate emails

22:04:00.702 --> 22:04:03.754
provided you have the requisite
access and software installed.

22:04:03.754 --> 22:04:05.962
And just to make clear that
I did add something here,

22:04:05.962 --> 22:04:09.102
let me open up my requirements.txt
file, and indeed, I

22:04:09.102 --> 22:04:13.812
have both Flask and
Flask-mail ready to go.

22:04:13.812 --> 22:04:16.812
But I ran the command in
advance to actually do that.

22:04:16.812 --> 22:04:22.082
All right, any questions,
then, on these examples here?

22:04:22.082 --> 22:04:22.942
No?

22:04:22.942 --> 22:04:23.442
All right.

22:04:23.442 --> 22:04:29.572
So what other pieces might actually
remain for us let me flip over here.

22:04:29.572 --> 22:04:32.675
It turns out that a key
component of most any web

22:04:32.675 --> 22:04:34.842
application nowadays that
we haven't touched on yet,

22:04:34.842 --> 22:04:38.397
but it'll be one of our final flourishes
today, is the notion of a session.

22:04:38.397 --> 22:04:41.202
And a session is actually
a feature that derives

22:04:41.202 --> 22:04:43.842
from all of the basics we talked
about today and last week,

22:04:43.842 --> 22:04:47.352
and a session is the technical term for
what you and I know as a shopping cart.

22:04:47.352 --> 22:04:50.922
When you go to amazon.com and you start
adding things to your shopping cart,

22:04:50.922 --> 22:04:53.605
they follow you from
page to page to page.

22:04:53.605 --> 22:04:56.022
Heck if you close your browser,
come back to the next day,

22:04:56.022 --> 22:04:59.669
they're typically still your shopping
cart, which is great for Amazon

22:04:59.669 --> 22:05:01.002
because they want your business.

22:05:01.002 --> 22:05:03.712
They don't want you to have to
start from scratch the next day.

22:05:03.712 --> 22:05:07.482
Similarly, when you log
into any website these days,

22:05:07.482 --> 22:05:11.292
even if it's not an e-commerce thing
but it has usernames and passwords,

22:05:11.292 --> 22:05:13.002
you and I are not in
the habit of logging

22:05:13.002 --> 22:05:15.192
into every darn page
we visit on a website.

22:05:15.192 --> 22:05:19.182
Typically, you log in once, and then
for the next hour, day, week, year,

22:05:19.182 --> 22:05:21.162
you stay logged into that website.

22:05:21.162 --> 22:05:25.302
So somehow, the website is
remembering that you have logged in.

22:05:25.302 --> 22:05:27.890
And that is being implemented
by way of this thing called

22:05:27.890 --> 22:05:29.682
a session, and perhaps
a more familiar term

22:05:29.682 --> 22:05:32.789
that you might know as, and
worry about, called cookies.

22:05:32.789 --> 22:05:35.122
Let's go ahead and take one
more five minute break here.

22:05:35.122 --> 22:05:37.414
And when we come back, we'll
look at cookies, sessions,

22:05:37.414 --> 22:05:39.672
and these final features.

22:05:39.672 --> 22:05:40.952
All right.

22:05:40.952 --> 22:05:43.962
So the promise now is that
we're going to implement

22:05:43.962 --> 22:05:47.382
this notion of a session, which is going
to allow us to log users in and keep

22:05:47.382 --> 22:05:50.262
them logged in and even implement
things like a shopping cart.

22:05:50.262 --> 22:05:54.642
And the overarching goal here
is to build an application that

22:05:54.642 --> 22:05:56.142
is, quote unquote, "stateful."

22:05:56.142 --> 22:05:59.322
Again, state refers to information,
and something that's stateful

22:05:59.322 --> 22:06:01.002
remembers information.

22:06:01.002 --> 22:06:05.712
And in this context, the curiosity
is that HTTP is technically

22:06:05.712 --> 22:06:07.272
a stateless protocol.

22:06:07.272 --> 22:06:11.382
Once you visit a URL,
http://something, hit Enter,

22:06:11.382 --> 22:06:14.472
web page is downloaded to
your browser, like that's it.

22:06:14.472 --> 22:06:17.472
You can unplug from the internet,
you can turn off your Wi-Fi,

22:06:17.472 --> 22:06:19.992
but you still have the web page locally.

22:06:19.992 --> 22:06:23.172
And yet we somehow want to make
sure that the next time you

22:06:23.172 --> 22:06:25.822
click on a link on that website,
it doesn't forget who you are.

22:06:25.822 --> 22:06:27.822
Or the next thing you add
to your shopping cart,

22:06:27.822 --> 22:06:29.632
it doesn't forget what
was already there.

22:06:29.632 --> 22:06:32.862
So we somehow want to
make HTTP stateful,

22:06:32.862 --> 22:06:36.192
and we can actually do this using the
building blocks we've seen thus far.

22:06:36.192 --> 22:06:40.902
So concretely, here's a form you might
see occasionally, but pretty rarely,

22:06:40.902 --> 22:06:42.492
when you log into Gmail.

22:06:42.492 --> 22:06:46.422
And I say rarely because most of
you don't log into Gmail frequently,

22:06:46.422 --> 22:06:49.452
you just stay logged in, pretty
much endlessly, in your browser.

22:06:49.452 --> 22:06:52.152
And that's because Google
has made the conscious choice

22:06:52.152 --> 22:06:55.862
to give you a very long session
time, maybe a day, a week,

22:06:55.862 --> 22:06:57.612
a month, a year, because
they don't really

22:06:57.612 --> 22:07:01.272
want to add friction to using their tool
and making you log in every darn day.

22:07:01.272 --> 22:07:04.192
By contrast, there's other
applications on campus,

22:07:04.192 --> 22:07:07.032
including some of the CS50 zone,
that makes you log in every time.

22:07:07.032 --> 22:07:09.102
Because we want to make
sure that it's indeed you

22:07:09.102 --> 22:07:13.042
accessing the site, and not a roommate
or friend or someone maliciously.

22:07:13.042 --> 22:07:16.822
So once you do fill out this
form, how does Google subsequently

22:07:16.822 --> 22:07:20.152
know that you are you, and
when you reload the page even

22:07:20.152 --> 22:07:22.912
or open a second tab for
your same Gmail account,

22:07:22.912 --> 22:07:26.669
how do they know that you're still
David or Carter or Emma or someone else?

22:07:26.669 --> 22:07:29.002
Well, let's look underneath
the hood of what's going on.

22:07:29.002 --> 22:07:32.452
When you log into Gmail,
essentially, you initially

22:07:32.452 --> 22:07:35.182
see a form like this
using a GET request.

22:07:35.182 --> 22:07:37.852
And the website responds
like we saw last week

22:07:37.852 --> 22:07:39.272
with some kind of HTTP response.

22:07:39.272 --> 22:07:41.392
Hopefully 200 OK with the form.

22:07:41.392 --> 22:07:46.222
Meanwhile, the website might
also respond with an HTTP header

22:07:46.222 --> 22:07:49.432
that, last week we didn't care
about, this week, we now do.

22:07:49.432 --> 22:07:53.002
Whenever you visit a website,
it is very commonly the case

22:07:53.002 --> 22:07:56.072
that the website is putting
a cookie on your computer.

22:07:56.072 --> 22:07:58.672
And you may generally know
that cookies can be bad

22:07:58.672 --> 22:08:03.412
and they track you in some way, and
that's both a blessing and a curse.

22:08:03.412 --> 22:08:08.812
Without cookies, you could not implement
things like shopping carts and log-ins

22:08:08.812 --> 22:08:10.342
as we know them today.

22:08:10.342 --> 22:08:12.742
Unfortunately, they can also
be used for ill purposes

22:08:12.742 --> 22:08:16.042
like tracking you on every website and
serving you ads more effectively and so

22:08:16.042 --> 22:08:16.542
forth.

22:08:16.542 --> 22:08:18.472
So with good comes some bad.

22:08:18.472 --> 22:08:21.652
But the basic primitive for
us, the computer scientist,

22:08:21.652 --> 22:08:24.622
boils down to just HTTP headers.

22:08:24.622 --> 22:08:29.512
A cookie is typically a big number,
a big, seemingly random value,

22:08:29.512 --> 22:08:33.742
that a server tells your browser
to store in memory, or even

22:08:33.742 --> 22:08:35.402
longer term, store on disk.

22:08:35.402 --> 22:08:38.942
So you can think of it like a file that
a server is planting on your computer.

22:08:38.942 --> 22:08:43.012
And the promise that HTTP
makes is that if a server sets

22:08:43.012 --> 22:08:45.982
a cookie on your computer,
you will represent

22:08:45.982 --> 22:08:49.982
that same cookie or that same
value on every subsequent request.

22:08:49.982 --> 22:08:51.862
So when you visit the
website like Gmail,

22:08:51.862 --> 22:08:55.612
they plop a cookie on your computer
like this with some session

22:08:55.612 --> 22:08:57.742
equals value, some long random value.

22:08:57.742 --> 22:09:00.072
One, two, three, A, B,
C, something like that.

22:09:00.072 --> 22:09:04.492
And when you then visit another page
on gmail.com or any other website,

22:09:04.492 --> 22:09:09.352
you send the opposite header, not
set cookie, but just cookie colon,

22:09:09.352 --> 22:09:11.372
and you send the exact same value.

22:09:11.372 --> 22:09:13.779
It's similar to going to a
club or an amusement park

22:09:13.779 --> 22:09:15.862
where you pay once, you
go through the gates once,

22:09:15.862 --> 22:09:17.782
you get checked by
security once, and then

22:09:17.782 --> 22:09:22.672
they very often take like a little stamp
and say, OK, now you can come and go.

22:09:22.672 --> 22:09:25.505
And then for you, efficiency-wise,
if you come back later in the day

22:09:25.505 --> 22:09:27.839
or later in the evening, you
can just present your hand.

22:09:27.839 --> 22:09:29.452
You've been stamped, presumably.

22:09:29.452 --> 22:09:32.122
They've already-- you've
already paid, you've

22:09:32.122 --> 22:09:33.662
already been searched or whatnot.

22:09:33.662 --> 22:09:35.632
And so it's this sort
of fast track ticket

22:09:35.632 --> 22:09:37.462
back into the club, back into the park.

22:09:37.462 --> 22:09:41.242
That's essentially what a
cookie is doing for you, whereby

22:09:41.242 --> 22:09:43.822
it's a way of reminding the
website we've already done this,

22:09:43.822 --> 22:09:46.052
you already asked me for
my username and password.

22:09:46.052 --> 22:09:48.532
This is my path to now come and go.

22:09:48.532 --> 22:09:52.522
Now, unlike this hand stamp, which
can be easily copied or transferred

22:09:52.522 --> 22:09:55.132
or duplicated or kept
on over multiple days,

22:09:55.132 --> 22:09:59.942
these cookies are really big, seemingly
random values, letters and numbers.

22:09:59.942 --> 22:10:02.662
So statistically, there's
no way someone else

22:10:02.662 --> 22:10:05.872
is just going to guess your cookie
value and pretend to be you It's just

22:10:05.872 --> 22:10:08.872
very low probability, statistically.

22:10:08.872 --> 22:10:12.712
But this is all it boils down to is this
agreement between browser and server

22:10:12.712 --> 22:10:16.772
to send these values back
and forth in this way.

22:10:16.772 --> 22:10:19.132
So when we actually
translate this, now, to code,

22:10:19.132 --> 22:10:21.232
let's do something like
a simple login app.

22:10:21.232 --> 22:10:24.352
Let me go into a folder I made
in advance today called login.

22:10:24.352 --> 22:10:28.922
And let me code up app.py and
let's take a look in here.

22:10:28.922 --> 22:10:30.472
So what's going on?

22:10:30.472 --> 22:10:31.912
A couple of new things up top.

22:10:31.912 --> 22:10:36.802
If I want to have the ability to
stamp my users hands, virtually,

22:10:36.802 --> 22:10:40.612
and implement sessions, I'm going
to have to import from Flask support

22:10:40.612 --> 22:10:41.552
for sessions.

22:10:41.552 --> 22:10:44.572
So this is another feature you
get for free by using a framework

22:10:44.572 --> 22:10:46.802
and not having to implement
all this yourself.

22:10:46.802 --> 22:10:48.952
And from the Flask
session library, I'm going

22:10:48.952 --> 22:10:51.502
to import Session, capital S. Why?

22:10:51.502 --> 22:10:53.752
I'm going to configure
the session as follows.

22:10:53.752 --> 22:10:56.752
Long story short, there's different
ways to implement sessions.

22:10:56.752 --> 22:11:00.712
The server can store these
cookies in a database, in a file,

22:11:00.712 --> 22:11:03.152
in memory, in RAM, in other places too.

22:11:03.152 --> 22:11:07.982
We are telling it to store these
cookies on the server's hard drive.

22:11:07.982 --> 22:11:11.452
So in fact, whenever you use sessions
as you will for problem set nine,

22:11:11.452 --> 22:11:15.292
you'll actually see a folder
suddenly appear called Flask_session,

22:11:15.292 --> 22:11:17.842
inside of which are the
cookies, essentially,

22:11:17.842 --> 22:11:20.212
for any users or friends
or yourself who've been

22:11:20.212 --> 22:11:22.802
visiting your particular application.

22:11:22.802 --> 22:11:24.562
So I'm setting it to
use the file system,

22:11:24.562 --> 22:11:26.770
and I don't want them to be
permanent because I want,

22:11:26.770 --> 22:11:29.482
when you close your browser,
the session to go away.

22:11:29.482 --> 22:11:32.032
They could be made to be
permanent and last much longer.

22:11:32.032 --> 22:11:34.222
Then I tell my app to support sessions.

22:11:34.222 --> 22:11:35.902
And that's it for now.

22:11:35.902 --> 22:11:39.572
Let's see what this application actually
does before we dissect the code.

22:11:39.572 --> 22:11:44.902
Let me go over to my terminal window,
run Flask run, and then let me go ahead

22:11:44.902 --> 22:11:49.202
and reload my preview URL.

22:11:49.202 --> 22:11:51.502
Give it a second to kick back in.

22:11:51.502 --> 22:11:53.362
Let me go ahead and open my URL.

22:11:53.362 --> 22:11:55.312
Come on.

22:11:55.312 --> 22:11:58.319
Oops, let me go ahead.

22:11:58.319 --> 22:11:59.152
Too long of a break.

22:11:59.152 --> 22:11:59.792
There we go.

22:11:59.792 --> 22:12:02.227
So this website simply has a login form.

22:12:02.227 --> 22:12:04.102
There's no password,
though I could certainly

22:12:04.102 --> 22:12:05.902
add that and check for that too.

22:12:05.902 --> 22:12:07.412
It just asks for your name.

22:12:07.412 --> 22:12:10.072
So I'm going to log in as
myself, David, and click Login.

22:12:10.072 --> 22:12:12.892
And now notice I'm currently
at the /login route.

22:12:12.892 --> 22:12:13.882
But notice this.

22:12:13.882 --> 22:12:16.207
If I try to go to the
default route, just,

22:12:16.207 --> 22:12:18.832
slash, which is where most
websites live by default,

22:12:18.832 --> 22:12:21.772
notice that I magically
get redirected to log in.

22:12:21.772 --> 22:12:24.922
So somehow, my code knows, hey, if
you're not logged in, you're going

22:12:24.922 --> 22:12:26.692
to /login instead.

22:12:26.692 --> 22:12:29.302
Let me type in my name,
David, and click Login.

22:12:29.302 --> 22:12:32.107
And now notice I am back at slash.

22:12:32.107 --> 22:12:35.332
Chrome is sort of annoyingly hiding
it, but this is the same thing as just

22:12:35.332 --> 22:12:36.482
a single slash.

22:12:36.482 --> 22:12:39.232
And now notice it says you
are logged in as David.

22:12:39.232 --> 22:12:39.892
Log out.

22:12:39.892 --> 22:12:43.162
What's cool is notice if I reload
the page, it still knows that.

22:12:43.162 --> 22:12:46.582
If I create a second tab and go to
the same URL, it still knows that.

22:12:46.582 --> 22:12:48.294
I could even--

22:12:48.294 --> 22:12:50.002
I could keep doing
this in multiple tabs,

22:12:50.002 --> 22:12:53.722
it's still going to remember me on both
of them as being logged in as David.

22:12:53.722 --> 22:12:55.052
So how does that work?

22:12:55.052 --> 22:13:00.142
Especially when I click Log Out,
then I get forgotten altogether.

22:13:00.142 --> 22:13:01.792
All right, so let's see how this works.

22:13:01.792 --> 22:13:03.742
And it's some basic building blocks.

22:13:03.742 --> 22:13:07.552
Under my /route, notice I have this.

22:13:07.552 --> 22:13:12.422
If there is no name in the session,
redirect the user to /login.

22:13:12.422 --> 22:13:14.992
So these two lines
together are what implement

22:13:14.992 --> 22:13:19.612
that automatic redirection using
HTTP 301 or 302 automatically.

22:13:19.612 --> 22:13:21.692
It's handled for me
with these two lines.

22:13:21.692 --> 22:13:23.352
Otherwise, show index.html.

22:13:23.352 --> 22:13:25.102
All right, let's go
down that rabbit hole.

22:13:25.102 --> 22:13:26.752
What's in index.html?

22:13:26.752 --> 22:13:29.992
Well, if I look in my--

22:13:29.992 --> 22:13:36.652
let me look in my templates
folder for my login demo and look

22:13:36.652 --> 22:13:40.042
at templates/index.html.

22:13:40.042 --> 22:13:41.632
All right, so what's going on here?

22:13:41.632 --> 22:13:46.222
I extend layout.html,
I have a block body,

22:13:46.222 --> 22:13:47.962
and then I've got some other syntax.

22:13:47.962 --> 22:13:50.504
So we haven't seen this yet,
but it's more Jinja stuff, which

22:13:50.504 --> 22:13:52.222
again, is almost identical to Python.

22:13:52.222 --> 22:13:55.312
If there's a name in
the session variable,

22:13:55.312 --> 22:14:00.082
then literally say you are logged in
as curly braces session bracket name.

22:14:00.082 --> 22:14:04.642
And then notice this, I've got a simple
HTML link to log out via /logout.

22:14:04.642 --> 22:14:07.312
Else, if there is no
name in the session,

22:14:07.312 --> 22:14:11.122
then it apparently says you are not
logged in and it leads me to an HTML

22:14:11.122 --> 22:14:13.612
link to /login and then end diff.

22:14:13.612 --> 22:14:16.222
So again, Jinja does
not rely on indentation.

22:14:16.222 --> 22:14:19.222
Recall the HTML and CSS don't
really care about indentation,

22:14:19.222 --> 22:14:20.452
only the human does.

22:14:20.452 --> 22:14:24.022
But in code with Jinja,
you need these end tags,

22:14:24.022 --> 22:14:26.872
end block, end for, end
if, to make super obvious

22:14:26.872 --> 22:14:29.092
that you're done with that thought.

22:14:29.092 --> 22:14:32.602
So session is just this
magic variable that we now

22:14:32.602 --> 22:14:36.892
have access to because we've
included these two lines of code

22:14:36.892 --> 22:14:41.902
and these that handle that whole
process of stamping every user's hand

22:14:41.902 --> 22:14:43.852
with a different, unique identifier.

22:14:43.852 --> 22:14:46.522
If I made my code space
public and I let all of you

22:14:46.522 --> 22:14:50.032
visit the exact same URL, all of
you would be logged out by default.

22:14:50.032 --> 22:14:52.102
You could all type your
own names individually,

22:14:52.102 --> 22:14:56.092
all log in at the same URL
using different sessions.

22:14:56.092 --> 22:14:59.332
And in fact, I would then see,
if I go into my terminal window

22:14:59.332 --> 22:15:03.992
here and my login directory, notice the
Flask session directory I mentioned.

22:15:03.992 --> 22:15:07.882
And if I CD into that and type ls,
notice that I had two tabs open,

22:15:07.882 --> 22:15:10.552
or actually, I think I
started the server twice.

22:15:10.552 --> 22:15:11.942
I have two files in there.

22:15:11.942 --> 22:15:14.692
I would ultimately have one
file for every one of you.

22:15:14.692 --> 22:15:16.702
And that's what's
beautiful about sessions

22:15:16.702 --> 22:15:21.292
is it creates the illusion
of per user storage.

22:15:21.292 --> 22:15:25.622
Inside of my session is my name,
inside of your session, so to speak,

22:15:25.622 --> 22:15:26.482
is your name.

22:15:26.482 --> 22:15:30.512
And the same is going to apply to
shopping carts, ultimately, as well.

22:15:30.512 --> 22:15:32.212
Let's see how login works here.

22:15:32.212 --> 22:15:36.622
My login route supports both GET and
POST, so I could play around if I want.

22:15:36.622 --> 22:15:41.192
And notice this, this login route
is kind of interesting as follows.

22:15:41.192 --> 22:15:45.395
If the user got to this
route via POST, my inference

22:15:45.395 --> 22:15:47.062
is that they must have submitted a form.

22:15:47.062 --> 22:15:47.562
Why?

22:15:47.562 --> 22:15:50.992
Because that's how I'm going to
design the HTML form in a second.

22:15:50.992 --> 22:15:53.632
And if they did submit
the form via POST,

22:15:53.632 --> 22:15:56.692
I'm going to store, in
the session, at the name

22:15:56.692 --> 22:15:59.092
key, whatever the human's name is.

22:15:59.092 --> 22:16:01.267
And then, I'm going to
redirect them back to slash.

22:16:01.267 --> 22:16:04.352
Otherwise, I'm going to
show them the login form.

22:16:04.352 --> 22:16:05.632
So this is what's cool.

22:16:05.632 --> 22:16:08.962
If I go to this login
form, which lives at,

22:16:08.962 --> 22:16:12.862
literally, slash login, by default,
when you visit a URL like that,

22:16:12.862 --> 22:16:14.722
you're visiting via GET.

22:16:14.722 --> 22:16:16.882
And so that's why I see the form.

22:16:16.882 --> 22:16:18.412
However, notice this.

22:16:18.412 --> 22:16:22.312
The form, very cleverly,
submits to itself,

22:16:22.312 --> 22:16:26.992
like the one route/login submits
to its same self, /login,

22:16:26.992 --> 22:16:29.582
but it uses POST when
you submit the form.

22:16:29.582 --> 22:16:33.712
And this is a nice way of having one
route but for two different types

22:16:33.712 --> 22:16:36.142
of operations or views.

22:16:36.142 --> 22:16:40.652
When I'm just there visiting /login
via URL, it shows me the form.

22:16:40.652 --> 22:16:45.742
But if I submit the form, then this
logic, these three lines, kick in,

22:16:45.742 --> 22:16:49.732
and this just avoids my having to have
both an index route and a greet route,

22:16:49.732 --> 22:16:50.302
for instance.

22:16:50.302 --> 22:16:55.402
I can just have one route that
handles both GET and POST.

22:16:55.402 --> 22:16:56.212
How about logout?

22:16:56.212 --> 22:16:57.182
What does this do?

22:16:57.182 --> 22:16:58.792
Well, it's as simple as this.

22:16:58.792 --> 22:17:01.222
Change whatever name
is in the session to be

22:17:01.222 --> 22:17:05.452
none, which is Python's version of null,
essentially, and then redirect the user

22:17:05.452 --> 22:17:06.082
back to slash.

22:17:06.082 --> 22:17:12.152
Because now, in index.html, I will
not notice a name there anymore.

22:17:12.152 --> 22:17:13.472
This will be false.

22:17:13.472 --> 22:17:16.922
And so I'll tell the user
instead, you are not logged in.

22:17:16.922 --> 22:17:18.375
So like it's--

22:17:18.375 --> 22:17:20.542
I want to say as simple as
this is, though I realize

22:17:20.542 --> 22:17:22.402
this is a bunch of steps involved.

22:17:22.402 --> 22:17:25.582
This is the essence of every
website on the internet that

22:17:25.582 --> 22:17:26.952
has usernames and passwords.

22:17:26.952 --> 22:17:30.202
And we skip the password name step for
that, more on that in problem set nine,

22:17:30.202 --> 22:17:34.612
but this is how every website out
there remembers that you're logged in.

22:17:34.612 --> 22:17:37.222
And how this works,
ultimately, is that as soon

22:17:37.222 --> 22:17:41.002
as you use in Python lines
like this and lines like this,

22:17:41.002 --> 22:17:45.142
Flask takes care of stamping the
virtual hand of all of your users

22:17:45.142 --> 22:17:50.392
and whenever Flask sees the same
cookie coming back from a user,

22:17:50.392 --> 22:17:53.632
it grabs the appropriate
file from that folder,

22:17:53.632 --> 22:17:56.042
loads it into the
session global variable

22:17:56.042 --> 22:18:01.012
so that your code is now unique
to that user and their name.

22:18:01.012 --> 22:18:03.922
Let's do one other
example with sessions here

22:18:03.922 --> 22:18:06.652
that'll show how we might use
these, now, for shopping carts.

22:18:06.652 --> 22:18:09.142
Let me go into the store example here.

22:18:09.142 --> 22:18:10.972
Let me go ahead and
run this thing first.

22:18:10.972 --> 22:18:16.852
If I run store in my same
tab and go back over here,

22:18:16.852 --> 22:18:19.882
we'll see a very ugly
e-commerce site that

22:18:19.882 --> 22:18:21.772
just sells seven different books here.

22:18:21.772 --> 22:18:26.259
But each of these books has a button
via which I can add it to my cart.

22:18:26.259 --> 22:18:28.342
All right, well where are
these books coming from?

22:18:28.342 --> 22:18:29.362
Well, let's poke around.

22:18:29.362 --> 22:18:32.752
Let me go into my terminal window again.

22:18:32.752 --> 22:18:35.992
Let me go into this example,
which is called store,

22:18:35.992 --> 22:18:40.912
and let me open up about
index dot ht-- whoops.

22:18:40.912 --> 22:18:48.382
Let's open up index,
how about, books.html

22:18:48.382 --> 22:18:50.672
is the default one, not index this time.

22:18:50.672 --> 22:18:55.462
So if I look here, notice that
that route that we just saw

22:18:55.462 --> 22:19:00.172
uses a for loop in Jinja to iterate
over a whole bunch of books, apparently,

22:19:00.172 --> 22:19:03.532
and it outputs, in an H2
tag, the title of the book,

22:19:03.532 --> 22:19:05.722
and then another one of these forms.

22:19:05.722 --> 22:19:07.082
So that's interesting.

22:19:07.082 --> 22:19:08.072
Let's go back one step.

22:19:08.072 --> 22:19:11.992
Let's go ahead and open up app.py,
because that must be-- excuse me,

22:19:11.992 --> 22:19:13.432
what's ticking all of this off.

22:19:13.432 --> 22:19:16.852
Notice that this file is
importing session support.

22:19:16.852 --> 22:19:19.852
It's configuring sessions
down here, but it's also

22:19:19.852 --> 22:19:22.292
connecting to a store.db file.

22:19:22.292 --> 22:19:23.842
So it's adding some SQLite.

22:19:23.842 --> 22:19:29.362
And notice this, in my /route,
I'm selecting star from books,

22:19:29.362 --> 22:19:31.642
which is going to give me
a list of dictionaries,

22:19:31.642 --> 22:19:33.652
each of which represents a row of books.

22:19:33.652 --> 22:19:37.882
And I'm going to pass that list of
books into my books.html template, which

22:19:37.882 --> 22:19:41.602
is why this for loop
works the way it does.

22:19:41.602 --> 22:19:43.282
Let's look at this actual database.

22:19:43.282 --> 22:19:48.472
Let me increase my terminal window
and do SQLite of store.db.schema

22:19:48.472 --> 22:19:49.522
will show me everything.

22:19:49.522 --> 22:19:50.882
There's not much there.

22:19:50.882 --> 22:19:55.532
It's a book-- it's a table called
books with two columns, ID and title.

22:19:55.532 --> 22:19:58.552
Let's do select star
from books semicolon.

22:19:58.552 --> 22:20:01.312
There are the seven books,
each of which has a unique ID.

22:20:01.312 --> 22:20:03.242
And you might see where this is going.

22:20:03.242 --> 22:20:06.802
If I go to the UI and I look
at each of these buttons

22:20:06.802 --> 22:20:11.962
for add to cart, just like Amazon might
have, notice that each of these buttons

22:20:11.962 --> 22:20:13.312
is just a form.

22:20:13.312 --> 22:20:15.265
And what's magical here,
just like deregister,

22:20:15.265 --> 22:20:17.182
even though I didn't
highlight it at the time,

22:20:17.182 --> 22:20:19.342
there's another type
of input that allows

22:20:19.342 --> 22:20:23.242
you to specify a value without the
human being able easily to change it.

22:20:23.242 --> 22:20:26.032
Instead of type equals
text or type equals submit,

22:20:26.032 --> 22:20:30.992
type equals hidden will put the value in
the form but not reveal it to the user.

22:20:30.992 --> 22:20:34.102
So that's how I'm saying that
the idea of this book is one,

22:20:34.102 --> 22:20:38.312
the idea of this book is two, the idea
of this book is three, and so forth.

22:20:38.312 --> 22:20:40.882
And each of these forms,
then, will submit, apparently,

22:20:40.882 --> 22:20:45.842
to /cart using POST and that would
seem to be what adds things to cart.

22:20:45.842 --> 22:20:46.592
So let's try this.

22:20:46.592 --> 22:20:48.622
Let me click on one or two of these.

22:20:48.622 --> 22:20:51.592
Let's add the first book, add to cart.

22:20:51.592 --> 22:20:52.642
Here's my cart.

22:20:52.642 --> 22:20:54.922
Notice my route change to /cart.

22:20:54.922 --> 22:20:58.882
All right, let's go back and
let's add the book number two.

22:20:58.882 --> 22:20:59.872
There we have that one.

22:20:59.872 --> 22:21:02.872
And let's skip ahead to the
seventh book, Deathly Hallows,

22:21:02.872 --> 22:21:05.612
and now we have all three books here.

22:21:05.612 --> 22:21:09.292
So what does the cart route do at /cart?

22:21:09.292 --> 22:21:10.042
Well, let's look.

22:21:10.042 --> 22:21:15.352
If I go back to my terminal window,
look at app.py and look at /cart, OK,

22:21:15.352 --> 22:21:17.722
there's a lot going on
here, but let's see.

22:21:17.722 --> 22:21:21.292
So the /cart route
supports both GET or POST,

22:21:21.292 --> 22:21:24.892
which is a nice way to
consolidate things into one URL.

22:21:24.892 --> 22:21:26.792
All right, this is interesting.

22:21:26.792 --> 22:21:30.945
If there is not a, quote
unquote, "cart" key in session,

22:21:30.945 --> 22:21:32.612
we haven't technically seen this syntax.

22:21:32.612 --> 22:21:36.772
But long story short, these lines
here do ensure that the cart exists.

22:21:36.772 --> 22:21:37.982
What do I mean by that?

22:21:37.982 --> 22:21:43.162
It makes sure that there's a cart
key in the session, global variable,

22:21:43.162 --> 22:21:45.742
and it's by default going
to be an empty list.

22:21:45.742 --> 22:21:46.282
Why?

22:21:46.282 --> 22:21:48.282
That just means you have
an empty shopping cart.

22:21:48.282 --> 22:21:55.612
But if the user visits this route via
POST and the user did provide an ID,

22:21:55.612 --> 22:21:59.242
they didn't muck with the form in any
way and try to hack into the website,

22:21:59.242 --> 22:22:02.692
they gave me a valid ID, then
I'm going to use this syntax.

22:22:02.692 --> 22:22:05.542
If session bracket cart is a list--

22:22:05.542 --> 22:22:07.847
recall from a couple of weeks
ago that dot append just

22:22:07.847 --> 22:22:08.972
adds something to the list.

22:22:08.972 --> 22:22:13.202
So I'm going to add the ID to the
list and return the user to cart.

22:22:13.202 --> 22:22:18.612
Otherwise, if the user is at /cart
via GET, implicitly, we just do this.

22:22:18.612 --> 22:22:22.092
Select star from books where ID is in.

22:22:22.092 --> 22:22:24.932
And this might be syntax
you recall from Pset six.

22:22:24.932 --> 22:22:27.542
It lets you look for
multiple IDs all at once,

22:22:27.542 --> 22:22:29.762
because if I have a list of session--

22:22:29.762 --> 22:22:34.232
list of IDs in my cart, I can
get all of those books at once.

22:22:34.232 --> 22:22:36.932
So long story short,
what has happened here?

22:22:36.932 --> 22:22:42.932
I am storing, in the cart, the books
that I myself have added to my cart.

22:22:42.932 --> 22:22:45.792
My browser is sending the same
hand stamp again and again,

22:22:45.792 --> 22:22:48.912
which is how this website knows that
it's me adding these books to my cart

22:22:48.912 --> 22:22:50.702
and not you or not Carter or not Emma.

22:22:50.702 --> 22:22:54.002
Indeed, if all of us visited the
same long URL and I made it public

22:22:54.002 --> 22:22:56.792
and allowed that, then we would
all have our own illusions

22:22:56.792 --> 22:22:58.412
of our own separate carts.

22:22:58.412 --> 22:23:00.902
And each of those carts,
in practice, would just

22:23:00.902 --> 22:23:04.682
be stored in this Flask
session directory on the server

22:23:04.682 --> 22:23:06.482
so that the server
can keep track of each

22:23:06.482 --> 22:23:09.452
of us using, again, these
cookie values that are being

22:23:09.452 --> 22:23:13.182
sent back and forth via these headers.

22:23:13.182 --> 22:23:13.682
All right.

22:23:13.682 --> 22:23:15.992
I know that's a lot,
but again, it's just

22:23:15.992 --> 22:23:19.472
the new Python way of
just leveraging those HTTP

22:23:19.472 --> 22:23:22.122
headers from last week in a clever way.

22:23:22.122 --> 22:23:26.102
Any questions before we look
at one final set of examples?

22:23:26.102 --> 22:23:27.410
Yeah.

22:23:27.410 --> 22:23:31.322
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] understand how
a log in has to do with [INAUDIBLE]??

22:23:31.322 --> 22:23:34.745
How does it use [INAUDIBLE],,
how do you change [INAUDIBLE]??

22:23:34.745 --> 22:23:39.146
Because in order to use a GET
request dot [INAUDIBLE] equals,

22:23:39.146 --> 22:23:43.082
there has to be an
exchange in [INAUDIBLE]..

22:23:43.082 --> 22:23:46.292
DAVID: So I think you're
asking about using the GET

22:23:46.292 --> 22:23:48.462
and POST in the same function.

22:23:48.462 --> 22:23:52.982
So this is just a nice
aesthetic, if you will.

22:23:52.982 --> 22:23:56.432
If I had to have separate
routes for GET and POST, I mean,

22:23:56.432 --> 22:23:59.192
it literally might mean I need
twice as many routes in my file.

22:23:59.192 --> 22:24:01.442
And it just starts to
get a little annoying.

22:24:01.442 --> 22:24:04.182
And these days, too, in
terms of user experience,

22:24:04.182 --> 22:24:08.162
this is maybe only appeals to the
geek in us, but having clean URLs

22:24:08.162 --> 22:24:09.452
is actually a thing.

22:24:09.452 --> 22:24:11.462
You don't want to have
lots of words in the URL,

22:24:11.462 --> 22:24:14.922
it's nice if the URLs are nice and
succinct and canonical, if you will.

22:24:14.922 --> 22:24:21.032
So it's nice if I can centralize all of
my shopping cart functionality in /cart

22:24:21.032 --> 22:24:25.652
only, and not in multiple routes,
one for GET, one for POST.

22:24:25.652 --> 22:24:30.012
It's a little nitpicky of me,
but this is commonly done here.

22:24:30.012 --> 22:24:34.622
So what this code here means is
that this route, this function,

22:24:34.622 --> 22:24:38.222
henceforth will support both
GET requests and POST requests.

22:24:38.222 --> 22:24:42.212
But then I need to distinguish between
whether it's GET or POST coming in.

22:24:42.212 --> 22:24:44.882
Because if it's a GET request,
I want to show the cart.

22:24:44.882 --> 22:24:47.342
If it's a POST request, I
want to update the cart.

22:24:47.342 --> 22:24:51.542
And the simplest way to do that
is just to check this value here.

22:24:51.542 --> 22:24:54.992
In the request variable that we
imported from Flask up above,

22:24:54.992 --> 22:24:57.842
you can check what is the
current type of request.

22:24:57.842 --> 22:25:00.932
Is it a GET, is it a POST, or
is it something else altogether?

22:25:00.932 --> 22:25:02.582
There are other verbs.

22:25:02.582 --> 22:25:07.652
If it's a POST, that must mean, because
I created the web form that uses POST,

22:25:07.652 --> 22:25:10.922
that the user clicked
the Add to Cart button.

22:25:10.922 --> 22:25:16.112
Otherwise, if it's not POST, it's
implicitly going to be logically GET.

22:25:16.112 --> 22:25:20.372
Then, I just want to show the
user the contents of the cart

22:25:20.372 --> 22:25:21.882
and I use these lines instead.

22:25:21.882 --> 22:25:25.682
So it's just one way of avoiding having
two routes for two different HTTP

22:25:25.682 --> 22:25:26.192
verbs.

22:25:26.192 --> 22:25:29.102
You can combine them so long
as you have a check like this.

22:25:29.102 --> 22:25:35.432
If I really wanted to be pedantic, I
could do this elif request.method=get.

22:25:38.012 --> 22:25:40.592
This would be more symmetric,
but it's not really necessary,

22:25:40.592 --> 22:25:43.512
because I know there's
only two possibilities.

22:25:43.512 --> 22:25:46.092
Hope that helps.

22:25:46.092 --> 22:25:48.902
All right, let's do one
final set of examples here

22:25:48.902 --> 22:25:51.092
that's going to tie the
last of these features

22:25:51.092 --> 22:25:53.522
together to something
that you probably see

22:25:53.522 --> 22:25:56.374
quite often in real-world applications.

22:25:56.374 --> 22:25:58.082
And that, for better
or for worse, is now

22:25:58.082 --> 22:26:01.422
going to involve tying back in
some JavaScript from last week.

22:26:01.422 --> 22:26:03.422
The goal at hand of
these examples is not

22:26:03.422 --> 22:26:06.805
to necessarily master how you yourself
would write the Python code, the SQL

22:26:06.805 --> 22:26:09.722
code, the JavaScript code, but just
to give you a mental model for how

22:26:09.722 --> 22:26:11.052
these different languages work.

22:26:11.052 --> 22:26:13.442
So that for final
projects, especially if you

22:26:13.442 --> 22:26:16.922
do want to add JavaScript functionality,
much more interactive user interface,

22:26:16.922 --> 22:26:20.102
you at least have the bare
bones of a mental model for how

22:26:20.102 --> 22:26:22.542
you can tie these languages together.

22:26:22.542 --> 22:26:26.132
Even though our focus, generally,
has been more on Python and SQL

22:26:26.132 --> 22:26:28.322
than on JavaScript from last week.

22:26:28.322 --> 22:26:33.422
Let me go ahead and open up an example
called shows, version zero of this.

22:26:33.422 --> 22:26:35.022
And let me do Flask run.

22:26:35.022 --> 22:26:39.092
And let me go into my URL here and
see what this application looks

22:26:39.092 --> 22:26:44.432
like by default. This has just a simple
query text box with a search box.

22:26:44.432 --> 22:26:47.462
Let's take a look at the HTML
that just got sent to my browser.

22:26:47.462 --> 22:26:49.572
All right, there's not
much going on here at all.

22:26:49.572 --> 22:26:52.682
So there's a form whose
action is /search.

22:26:52.682 --> 22:26:54.482
It's going to submit via GET.

22:26:54.482 --> 22:26:58.902
It's going to use a q parameter, just
like Google it seems, and submit it.

22:26:58.902 --> 22:27:01.872
So this actually looks like the
Google form we did last week.

22:27:01.872 --> 22:27:03.752
So let's see what goes on here.

22:27:03.752 --> 22:27:06.152
Let me search for something like cat.

22:27:06.152 --> 22:27:07.502
Enter.

22:27:07.502 --> 22:27:09.582
OK, so it looks like--

22:27:09.582 --> 22:27:12.042
all right, so this is actually
a somewhat familiar file.

22:27:12.042 --> 22:27:16.232
What I've gone ahead and done is I've
grabbed all of the titles of TV shows

22:27:16.232 --> 22:27:19.052
from a couple of weeks ago
when we first introduced SQL,

22:27:19.052 --> 22:27:21.602
and I loaded them into this
demo so that you can search

22:27:21.602 --> 22:27:23.412
by keyword for any word you want.

22:27:23.412 --> 22:27:24.512
I just searched for cat.

22:27:24.512 --> 22:27:27.662
If we were to do this again, we
would see all the title of TV shows

22:27:27.662 --> 22:27:32.862
that contain D-O-G, dog, as a
substring somewhere and so forth.

22:27:32.862 --> 22:27:34.892
So this is a traditional
way of doing this.

22:27:34.892 --> 22:27:40.502
Just like in Google, it uses
/search?q=cat, q=dog, and so forth.

22:27:40.502 --> 22:27:41.382
How does that work?

22:27:41.382 --> 22:27:45.432
Well, let's just take a
quick look at app.py here.

22:27:45.432 --> 22:27:50.702
Let me go into my zero example
here, show zero, and open up

22:27:50.702 --> 22:27:53.222
app.py and see what's going on.

22:27:53.222 --> 22:27:54.882
All right, very simple.

22:27:54.882 --> 22:27:57.542
Here's the form, that's
how we started today.

22:27:57.542 --> 22:28:00.017
And here is the /search route.

22:28:00.017 --> 22:28:01.142
Well, what's going on here?

22:28:01.142 --> 22:28:02.832
This gets a little interesting.

22:28:02.832 --> 22:28:06.032
So I first select a whole
bunch of shows by doing this.

22:28:06.032 --> 22:28:10.652
Select star from shows, where
title like question mark.

22:28:10.652 --> 22:28:15.332
And then I'm using some
percent signs from SQL

22:28:15.332 --> 22:28:17.792
on both the left and the
right, and I'm plugging

22:28:17.792 --> 22:28:20.212
in whatever the user's input was for q.

22:28:20.212 --> 22:28:22.802
If I didn't use like and
I used equal instead,

22:28:22.802 --> 22:28:25.892
I could get rid of these curly
brace, these percent signs,

22:28:25.892 --> 22:28:29.522
but then it would have to be a show
called cat or called dog as opposed

22:28:29.522 --> 22:28:32.222
to it being like cat or like dog.

22:28:32.222 --> 22:28:35.792
This whole line returns to me a
list of dictionaries, each of which

22:28:35.792 --> 22:28:38.682
represents a show in the database.

22:28:38.682 --> 22:28:41.972
And then, I'm passing all of those
shows to a template called search.html.

22:28:41.972 --> 22:28:45.772
So let's just follow that
breadcrumb, let's open up shows dot--

22:28:45.772 --> 22:28:48.152
sorry, search.html.

22:28:48.152 --> 22:28:50.332
All right, so this is
where templating gets cool.

22:28:50.332 --> 22:28:53.032
So I just passed back hundreds
of results, potentially,

22:28:53.032 --> 22:28:56.512
but the only thing I'm
outputting is an unordered list

22:28:56.512 --> 22:29:00.592
and using a Jinja for
loop and li tag containing

22:29:00.592 --> 22:29:02.507
the titles of each of those shows.

22:29:02.507 --> 22:29:04.882
And just to prove that this
is indeed a familiar data set

22:29:04.882 --> 22:29:09.802
and I actually simplified it a bit,
if I look at shows.db with SQLite,

22:29:09.802 --> 22:29:14.092
I threw away all the other stuff like
ratings and actors and everyone else

22:29:14.092 --> 22:29:18.832
and I just have, for instance,
select star from shows

22:29:18.832 --> 22:29:21.202
limit 10, just so we can see 10 of them.

22:29:21.202 --> 22:29:23.572
There's 10 of the shows
from that database.

22:29:23.572 --> 22:29:25.982
So that's all that's
in the database itself.

22:29:25.982 --> 22:29:29.572
So it would look like this is a
pretty vanilla web application.

22:29:29.572 --> 22:29:31.607
It uses GET, it submits
it to the server,

22:29:31.607 --> 22:29:33.982
the server spits out a response,
and that response, then,

22:29:33.982 --> 22:29:38.752
looks like this, which is a huge
number of li tags, one for each cat

22:29:38.752 --> 22:29:40.462
or one for each dog match.

22:29:40.462 --> 22:29:44.132
But everything else
comes from a layout.html.

22:29:44.132 --> 22:29:46.772
All the stuff at the
top and at the bottom.

22:29:46.772 --> 22:29:50.782
All right, so these days, though, we're
in the habit of seeing autocomplete.

22:29:50.782 --> 22:29:53.482
And you start typing something
and you don't have to hit Submit,

22:29:53.482 --> 22:29:56.357
you don't have to click a button,
you don't have to go to a new page.

22:29:56.357 --> 22:29:58.712
Web applications, nowadays,
are much more dynamic.

22:29:58.712 --> 22:30:01.892
So let's take a look at this
version one of this thing.

22:30:01.892 --> 22:30:07.792
Let me go into shows one
and close my previous tabs

22:30:07.792 --> 22:30:09.902
and run Flask run in here.

22:30:09.902 --> 22:30:14.602
And it's almost the same thing, but
watch the behavior change a little bit.

22:30:14.602 --> 22:30:16.625
I'm reloading the form,
there's no button now.

22:30:16.625 --> 22:30:18.292
So gone is the need for a submit button.

22:30:18.292 --> 22:30:20.312
I want to implement autocomplete now.

22:30:20.312 --> 22:30:22.912
So let's go ahead and
type in C. OK, there's

22:30:22.912 --> 22:30:25.882
every show that starts
with C. A, there's

22:30:25.882 --> 22:30:27.862
every show that has C-A in it, rather.

22:30:27.862 --> 22:30:31.102
T, there's every show with C-A-T in it.

22:30:31.102 --> 22:30:34.912
I can start it again and do dog,
but notice how instantaneous it was.

22:30:34.912 --> 22:30:38.812
And notice my URL never changed,
there's no /search route,

22:30:38.812 --> 22:30:40.012
and it's just immediate.

22:30:40.012 --> 22:30:43.652
With every keystroke, it is
searching again and again and again.

22:30:43.652 --> 22:30:46.282
That's a nice UX, user experience,
because it's immediate.

22:30:46.282 --> 22:30:48.532
This is what users are
used to these days.

22:30:48.532 --> 22:30:53.392
But if I look at the source code
here, notice that in the source code,

22:30:53.392 --> 22:30:58.615
there's just an empty UL by default but
there is some fancy JavaScript code.

22:30:58.615 --> 22:31:00.032
So let's see what's going on here.

22:31:00.032 --> 22:31:03.512
This JavaScript code
is doing the following.

22:31:03.512 --> 22:31:06.472
Let me zoom in a little bit more.

22:31:06.472 --> 22:31:10.854
This JavaScript code is first
selecting, with query selector,

22:31:10.854 --> 22:31:13.562
which you used this past week,
quote unquote "input, " all right,

22:31:13.562 --> 22:31:15.502
so that's just getting the text box.

22:31:15.502 --> 22:31:19.137
Then it's adding an event listener
to that input for the input event.

22:31:19.137 --> 22:31:21.262
We didn't talk about this
last week, but literally,

22:31:21.262 --> 22:31:23.362
when you provide any
kind of input by typing,

22:31:23.362 --> 22:31:27.862
by pasting, by any other
user interface mechanism,

22:31:27.862 --> 22:31:29.672
it triggers an event called input.

22:31:29.672 --> 22:31:31.732
So similar to key press or key up.

22:31:31.732 --> 22:31:35.542
I then have a function, no worries
about this async function for now.

22:31:35.542 --> 22:31:37.244
Then what do I do inside of this?

22:31:37.244 --> 22:31:39.202
All right, so this is
new, and this is the part

22:31:39.202 --> 22:31:41.872
that let's just focus on the
ideas and not the syntax.

22:31:41.872 --> 22:31:43.762
JavaScript, nowadays,
comes with a function

22:31:43.762 --> 22:31:48.052
called fetch that allows you to
GET or POST information to a server

22:31:48.052 --> 22:31:49.862
without reloading the whole page.

22:31:49.862 --> 22:31:52.372
You can sort of secretly
do it inside of the page.

22:31:52.372 --> 22:31:53.692
What do I want to fetch?

22:31:53.692 --> 22:31:59.032
slash search question mark q equals
whatever the value of that input is.

22:31:59.032 --> 22:32:03.082
When I get back a response, I want
to get the text of that response

22:32:03.082 --> 22:32:05.332
and store it in a variable called shows.

22:32:05.332 --> 22:32:08.002
And I'm deliberately bouncing
around, ignoring special words

22:32:08.002 --> 22:32:11.362
like await and await here, but for
now, just focus on what came back.

22:32:11.362 --> 22:32:14.602
A response came back from the
server, I'm getting the text from it,

22:32:14.602 --> 22:32:16.432
storing it in a variable called shows.

22:32:16.432 --> 22:32:17.692
What am I then doing?

22:32:17.692 --> 22:32:22.462
I'm using query selector to select
my UL, which is empty by default,

22:32:22.462 --> 22:32:26.722
and I'm changing its inner HTML
to be equal to the shows that

22:32:26.722 --> 22:32:28.352
came back from the server.

22:32:28.352 --> 22:32:29.572
So let's poke around.

22:32:29.572 --> 22:32:32.402
Here's where, again, developer
tools are quite powerful.

22:32:32.402 --> 22:32:36.652
Let me go ahead and reload this
page to get rid of everything.

22:32:36.652 --> 22:32:39.952
And let me now open up inspect.

22:32:39.952 --> 22:32:43.292
Let me go to the Network tab and
let's just sniff the traffic going

22:32:43.292 --> 22:32:44.542
between my browser and server.

22:32:44.542 --> 22:32:49.222
I'm going to search for C. Notice that
immediately triggered an HTTP request

22:32:49.222 --> 22:32:51.292
to /search?q=c.

22:32:54.202 --> 22:32:57.812
So I didn't even finish my cat
thought, but notice what came back.

22:32:57.812 --> 22:33:02.662
A bunch of response headers, but let's
actually click on the raw response.

22:33:02.662 --> 22:33:07.312
This is literally the response from the
server, just a whole bunch of li tags.

22:33:07.312 --> 22:33:10.372
No UL, no HTML, no
title, no body, nothing.

22:33:10.372 --> 22:33:11.452
Just li tags.

22:33:11.452 --> 22:33:12.962
And we can actually simulate this.

22:33:12.962 --> 22:33:17.122
Let me manually go to
that same URL, q=c, Enter.

22:33:17.122 --> 22:33:19.132
We are just going to get back--

22:33:19.132 --> 22:33:20.572
whoops, sorry.

22:33:20.572 --> 22:33:25.432
slash search q equals c, we are
just going to get back this stuff,

22:33:25.432 --> 22:33:27.892
which if I view source, it's
not even a complete web page.

22:33:27.892 --> 22:33:31.312
The browser is trying to show it to me
as a complete web page with bullets,

22:33:31.312 --> 22:33:33.862
but it's really just partial HTML.

22:33:33.862 --> 22:33:36.472
But that's perfect,
because this is literally

22:33:36.472 --> 22:33:39.382
what I essentially want my
Python code to copy paste

22:33:39.382 --> 22:33:42.472
into the otherwise empty UL tag.

22:33:42.472 --> 22:33:46.822
And that's what this JavaScript
code then, here, is doing.

22:33:46.822 --> 22:33:51.112
Once it gets back that response from the
server, it's using these lines of code

22:33:51.112 --> 22:33:56.132
to plug all of those li's
into the UL after the fact.

22:33:56.132 --> 22:33:58.492
Again, changing the so-called dom.

22:33:58.492 --> 22:34:01.462
But there's a slightly better way
to do this because, honestly, this

22:34:01.462 --> 22:34:02.932
is not the best design.

22:34:02.932 --> 22:34:07.252
Because if you've got a hundred shows or
more, you're sending all of these tags

22:34:07.252 --> 22:34:08.212
unnecessarily.

22:34:08.212 --> 22:34:10.852
Why do I need to send all
of these stupid HTML tags?

22:34:10.852 --> 22:34:13.952
Why don't I just create those
when I'm ready to create them?

22:34:13.952 --> 22:34:15.682
Well, here's the final flourish.

22:34:15.682 --> 22:34:18.232
Whenever making a web
application nowadays,

22:34:18.232 --> 22:34:22.402
where client and server keep talking
to one another, Google Maps does this,

22:34:22.402 --> 22:34:25.192
Gmail does this, literally
every cool application

22:34:25.192 --> 22:34:27.532
nowadays you load the
page once and then it

22:34:27.532 --> 22:34:29.302
keeps on interacting
with you without you

22:34:29.302 --> 22:34:31.972
reloading or having to change the URL.

22:34:31.972 --> 22:34:36.472
Let's actually use a format called
JSON, JavaScript Object Notation, which

22:34:36.472 --> 22:34:39.562
is to say there's just a
better, more efficient, better

22:34:39.562 --> 22:34:42.152
designed way to send that same data.

22:34:42.152 --> 22:34:46.432
I'm going to go into shows
two now and do Flask run.

22:34:46.432 --> 22:34:48.172
And I'm going to go
back to my page here.

22:34:48.172 --> 22:34:52.522
The user interface is exactly the same,
and it still works exactly the same.

22:34:52.522 --> 22:34:56.552
Here's C, C-A, C-A-T, and so forth.

22:34:56.552 --> 22:34:59.032
But let's see what's coming back now.

22:34:59.032 --> 22:35:09.152
If I go to /search?q=cat, Enter, notice
that I get this crazy-looking syntax.

22:35:09.152 --> 22:35:12.622
But the fact that it's so
compact is actually a good thing.

22:35:12.622 --> 22:35:16.372
This is actually going to-- let
me format it a little nicer, well,

22:35:16.372 --> 22:35:17.452
or a little worse.

22:35:17.452 --> 22:35:20.752
This is what's called
JavaScript Object Notation.

22:35:20.752 --> 22:35:26.542
In JavaScript, an angle-- a square
bracket means here comes an array.

22:35:26.542 --> 22:35:32.542
In JavaScript, a curly bracket says
here comes an object, AKA, a dictionary.

22:35:32.542 --> 22:35:35.602
And you might recall from--

22:35:35.602 --> 22:35:36.782
did we do?

22:35:36.782 --> 22:35:42.772
Yes, sort of recall that you can now
have keys and values in JavaScript

22:35:42.772 --> 22:35:45.122
notation using colons like this.

22:35:45.122 --> 22:35:48.712
So long story short, cryptic
as this is to you and me

22:35:48.712 --> 22:35:52.072
and not very human friendly,
it's very machine friendly.

22:35:52.072 --> 22:35:55.792
Because for every
title in that database,

22:35:55.792 --> 22:36:01.562
I get back its ID and its title, its
ID and its title, its ID and its title.

22:36:01.562 --> 22:36:05.632
And this is a very generic format that
an API, an application programming

22:36:05.632 --> 22:36:07.102
interface, might return to you.

22:36:07.102 --> 22:36:09.142
And this is how APIs, nowadays, work.

22:36:09.142 --> 22:36:13.882
You get back very raw textual
data in this format, JSON format,

22:36:13.882 --> 22:36:17.602
and then you can write code that
actually programmatically turns

22:36:17.602 --> 22:36:22.292
that JSON data into any language
you want, for instance, HTML.

22:36:22.292 --> 22:36:25.252
So here's the third and final
version of this program.

22:36:25.252 --> 22:36:27.352
I, again, select my input.

22:36:27.352 --> 22:36:29.632
I, again, listen for input.

22:36:29.632 --> 22:36:32.452
I then, when I get input,
call this function.

22:36:32.452 --> 22:36:38.992
I fetch slash search q equals whatever
that input was, C or C-A or C-A-T.

22:36:38.992 --> 22:36:41.955
I then wait for the response,
but instead of getting text,

22:36:41.955 --> 22:36:44.872
I'm calling this other function that
comes with JavaScript these days,

22:36:44.872 --> 22:36:47.092
called JSON, that just parses that.

22:36:47.092 --> 22:36:51.232
It turns it into a dictionary for me,
or really a list of dictionaries for me,

22:36:51.232 --> 22:36:53.572
and stores it in a
variable called shows.

22:36:53.572 --> 22:36:57.832
And this is where you start to see the
convergence of HTML with JavaScript.

22:36:57.832 --> 22:37:01.162
Let me initialize a variable called
HTML to nothing, quote unquote,

22:37:01.162 --> 22:37:03.962
using single quotes, but I
could also use double quotes.

22:37:03.962 --> 22:37:06.232
This is JavaScript syntax for a loop.

22:37:06.232 --> 22:37:10.102
Let me iterate over every
ID in the show's list

22:37:10.102 --> 22:37:13.882
that I just got back in the server,
that big chunk of JSON data.

22:37:13.882 --> 22:37:19.192
Let me create a variable called
Title that's equal to the shows--

22:37:19.192 --> 22:37:21.652
the title of the show at that ID.

22:37:21.652 --> 22:37:23.662
But for reasons we'll
come back to, let me

22:37:23.662 --> 22:37:25.582
replace a couple of scary characters.

22:37:25.582 --> 22:37:31.672
Then let me dynamically add to this
variable, an li tag, the actual title,

22:37:31.672 --> 22:37:33.232
and a close li tag.

22:37:33.232 --> 22:37:35.992
And then very lastly,
after this for loop,

22:37:35.992 --> 22:37:42.082
let me update the ULs in our HTML to
be the HTML I just created on the fly.

22:37:42.082 --> 22:37:44.662
So in short, don't worry
too much about the syntax

22:37:44.662 --> 22:37:47.120
because you won't need to use
this unless you start playing

22:37:47.120 --> 22:37:49.232
with more advanced features quite soon.

22:37:49.232 --> 22:37:51.202
But what we're doing is,
with JavaScript, we're

22:37:51.202 --> 22:37:54.122
creating a bigger and bigger
and bigger string of HTML

22:37:54.122 --> 22:37:57.562
containing all of the open brackets,
the li tags, the closed brackets,

22:37:57.562 --> 22:38:00.922
but we're just grabbing the
raw data from the server.

22:38:00.922 --> 22:38:02.932
And so in fact in
problem set nine, you're

22:38:02.932 --> 22:38:06.652
going to use a real world third
party API, application programming

22:38:06.652 --> 22:38:08.092
interface, for which you sign up.

22:38:08.092 --> 22:38:10.852
The data you're going to get
back from that API is not

22:38:10.852 --> 22:38:14.302
going to be show titles, but
actually stock quotes and stocks

22:38:14.302 --> 22:38:17.042
ticker symbols and the prices of last--

22:38:17.042 --> 22:38:19.072
at which stocks were
last bought or sold,

22:38:19.072 --> 22:38:21.880
and you're going to get that
data back in JSON format.

22:38:21.880 --> 22:38:24.922
And you're going to write a bit of
code that's then going to convert that

22:38:24.922 --> 22:38:27.992
to the requisite HTML on the page.

22:38:27.992 --> 22:38:31.282
So the final result here is
literally the kind of autocomplete

22:38:31.282 --> 22:38:33.652
that you and I see and
take for granted every day,

22:38:33.652 --> 22:38:35.812
and that's ultimately how it works.

22:38:35.812 --> 22:38:39.442
HTML and CSS are used to present
the data, your so-called view.

22:38:39.442 --> 22:38:43.862
Python might be used to send or
get the data on the backend server.

22:38:43.862 --> 22:38:46.132
And then lastly, JavaScript
is going to be used

22:38:46.132 --> 22:38:48.519
to make things dynamic and interactive.

22:38:48.519 --> 22:38:50.602
So I know that's a whole
bunch of building blocks,

22:38:50.602 --> 22:38:53.394
but the whole point of problem set
nine to tie everything together,

22:38:53.394 --> 22:38:55.912
set the stage for hopefully a
very successful final project.

22:38:55.912 --> 22:38:58.162
Why don't we go ahead and
wrap up there, and we'll see

22:38:58.162 --> 22:39:01.372
you one last time next week for emoji.

